// Chronicle text and piece manifest. Server-side only: files under /api
// prefixed with _ are never served as static assets.
module.exports = {"parts":[{"part":1,"element":"Fire","chronicles":[{"n":1,"letterTitle":"Memory Lapse","narrator":"Norah Aven","numword":"One","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Legend of the Cobbogothians","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"8a25594f03a541408fce5b10c1fa9b23"},{"so":2,"title":"Text Message #1","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a69640a29eb24b5cbe2c81946ff24d41"},{"so":3,"title":"Memory Lapse","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Perhaps I should begin by introducing myself. My name is Norah Aven Lukens. I know “Aven” is a strange name, but Uncle Jack’s mother, Aven Aegissida, was Icelandic, and the mountain aven is Iceland’s national flower, so there you go. Uncle Jack said he gave me the name not only to honor his mother but to help me remember. When I asked him what he wanted me to remember, all he said was that there would come a time in my life when I would want to know who I am. When that time came, I should go back to the beginning where it all began. I always wondered what he meant by that. Now I know.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was raised by my Uncle Jack from the time I was a baby. He’s not really my uncle—more on that later—but he is the leading archaeologist at Harvard University. They call him the Myth Chaser because, well, he chases myths. Very original, I know. But it does have a sort of immortalizing ring to it, doesn’t it? I’m proud of him; always have been. I’ve included his most famous myth with this letter in case you want to see his work for yourself. He discovered The Legend of the Cobbogothians in the ’70s, before I was born. He found it carved into cave walls on his granddad Aegissida’s farm in Iceland. All you need to know is that the caves are important. Don’t forget about the caves.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only someone had told me sooner.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It makes the most sense to start this chronicle on the day I left boarding school. It’s not where the story begins, of course, but you’ll understand soon enough why I begin there. I was heading back to Boston, and believe me, I was more excited than your average 17-year-old to be finished with high school. Dunstan Academy isn’t your regular prep boarding school, see. Dunstan is a “special” school for “special” kids, which is just a nice way of saying “kids with some serious issues.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"How I ended up there in the first place isn’t important right now. Let’s just say I’d been “seeing things” for a while, things I wasn’t supposed to be seeing, things that shouldn’t be real, couldn’t be real, not in the world I was familiar with. Yet that didn’t stop me from seeing them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first thing I remember from that day is waking up on a public transit bus, of all places, to the driver announcing Belmont Center as our next stop. I don’t think I need to tell you I was feeling pretty freaked out. My panic all came down to one very important fact: I couldn’t remember how I got there. Ever experienced that before? Yeah, well, it’s not a great feeling. Thankfully, I was already under the impression I was a little crazy, so yeah…there was that comfort.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, when I stepped off the bus, I should’ve known something was wrong by the look the driver gave me—by the fact that he asked me with genuine concern if I’d be all right. I was too distracted by Uncle Jack’s text message, which I’d only just seen, letting me know at the last minute that James Riley was going to pick me up from the bus stop. Do you have anxiety at all? If you do, you know how awful it feels when you don’t have time to prepare for something you know will be excruciatingly awkward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know, I know. Why was it such a big deal, Nor? He’s just a guy. But the fact is, James Riley wasn’t and never had been “just a guy.” Not to me, anyway. To me, he’d always been THE guy. So, of course, of all people, Uncle Jack chose James Riley to pick me up and take me home.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the irony of this situation to make any sense to you, you need to understand that Uncle Jack spent the better part of the previous summer doing everything in his power to keep James and me apart. He even sent me off to boarding school for nine hellish months to “put some distance between us.” Why the sudden change of heart? And did he even consider how I might feel about it? I mean, I hadn’t heard from James in those nine months. Not even once. I spent the first three months being devastated, the next three being livid, and the last three trying to forget (unsuccessfully) that James Riley even existed. The last thing I wanted was to have him waiting to greet me the moment I got home.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I did what any semi-sane person would do when confronted with a really awkward situation. I ran across the street to hide in the bathroom until all possibility of running into James had passed. I could make my own way home.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew I’d have to face James sooner or later. We were neighbors, after all. But I preferred it be on my terms, when I wasn’t still wondering why in the world I couldn’t, for the life of me, remember getting on that bus.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The bathroom didn’t offer much clarity either. In fact, it only increased my confusion and terror. Almost the moment I was through the door and could see my reflection in the mirror, I discovered I not only couldn’t remember how I’d gotten on that bus, but judging from the state I was in from head to toe, there was obviously a whole lot more I couldn’t remember. I was a disaster! I mean, I’m not the cleanest person in the world—I’m not one of those people with OCD who can’t stand to have even one hair out of place—but I am pretty neat. What I saw staring back at me from the bathroom mirror, however, was ANYTHING but neat. Seriously, my hair was full of leaves and mud. There was even some blood in my hair—which it definitely was, because there’s no mistaking blood on platinum hair, my friend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That wasn’t all! My face was caked with dirt—or dried mud, really—as apparently I’d been crying at some point. There was more blood at my throat, and after a lot of panicked scrubbing with those cheap, brown, sandpapery towels they have in public restrooms, I soon discovered the blood had come from a thin cut just above my collarbone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then there was the bracelet. I don’t know what your experience has been with this kind of thing—this could happen to you all the time—but I personally have never had a completely unrecognizable piece of jewelry randomly show up on my wrist. Until it did. This wasn’t just a tiny little friendship bracelet—one of those barely there contraptions. This was a full-on, rather expensive-looking cuff. Silver, with a lot of strange engravings and empty stone settings in it, except for the two settings that did have stones. You know what, I’ll just draw it for you here so you’ll know exactly what I’m talking about.","i":false},{"t":"img","src":"/chronicle-art/norah-bracelet.svg","alt":"Norah's drawing of the bracelet"},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I’m standing in the bathroom, freaking out about my face, and trying to fix my hair when an episode starts to hit me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They always come the same way. First, my vision starts to go; then the dizziness comes. The next thing I know, I’m seeing something—something impossible. My therapist is the one who calls them episodes. I prefer to call them visions because they kind of play out like mini movies, mini movies I happen to be trapped in and experience things in that could never in a million years happen in real life. I even have my own guide in these visions. She’s a tall, red-haired woman with odd violet eyes and a face that looks strangely familiar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now, I’m not going to go into details about what I saw—you’ll find out soon enough—but to make a long story short, I woke up shaking on the disgusting bathroom floor with my hand full of a wad of sopping, soapy paper towels. Visions have caused me to collapse in weirder places, believe me, and with witnesses. It’s always the worst when there are witnesses. But this one seemed different somehow. For one thing, there weren’t any supernatural elements to it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, I was still the only person in the bathroom, so I pulled myself together, finished cleaning up, and walked out to the curb, where I ran right into a frantic James Riley, who’d been, to quote him directly, “Lookin’ up and down every street from here to Cabot’s” for me. Why on earth had he gone to Cabot’s? Did he think the first thing I’d do when I got home was go for ice cream…by myself?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ll admit I did feel a little guilty for worrying him so much, but how was I supposed to know he still cared enough to worry about me? He obviously didn’t care enough to write or call for the past nine months. I tried to avoid making eye contact with him for as long as possible, which is pretty hard when you’re the only two people around. But what do you say to someone you once knew as well as you knew yourself—and who knew you the same way? Especially after there had been nothing said between us since what happened last summer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the time I finally got up enough nerve to really look at James, my looking quickly turned to staring—gaping, really—at the extreme difference nine months had made in him. Seriously, James had always been big. In fact, he looked like a Viking, if I’m being honest, with his flaming red hair, especially when he started growing facial hair his junior year of high school. And let’s not forget that James had always been really, well, muscular for a guy his age. But a year ago you could still tell he was a kid, you know? Now he looked like a man, chiseled jaw and all. Which was probably why they agreed to hire him at the Belmont Fire Station—not because of his chiseled jaw, of course, but his size. I mean, at 19, he was Belmont’s youngest fireman on record, and potentially their largest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We couldn’t just stand there gawking at each other forever, so James grabbed my bags and shoved them in his trunk. After opening the door for me, he paused a second, like he wanted to say something, but he just mumbled something that sounded like, “Good to have you back, Nor.” And that was it. To say the drive home was as awkward as I had imagined it would be was the understatement of the century.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only I’d known what was waiting for me at home, I would’ve dragged that drive out for hours. I would have told James all about Dunstan and the awful last nine months, would have asked him why he never wrote to me. I would have even breeched the extremely painful topic of Gram Riley’s death, just to buy back those hours, those minutes, even those seconds before heading home. Because then, in my mind at least, Uncle Jack would still be alive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that isn’t what happened. Instead, James and I drove in silence, neither of us saying a word. When we turned onto Barbara Road, the police were swarming the big house on the hill—invading the only home I’d ever known. As I stepped out of the car, I already knew they were waiting for me, waiting to inform me the only family I’d ever known was dead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I fainted when they told me. Maybe you’ll think that was a bit much? After all, Uncle Jack and I were never that close. It’s hard to get close to someone who’s never around. But that’s not why I fainted, and it wasn’t because I was having another vision or anything either. I fainted because of the vision I’d had in the bathroom earlier. I didn’t tell you before because I didn’t want to scare you, but the scene the red-haired lady showed me in the restroom was the exact scene I saw at home—of Uncle Jack’s death. This, dear reader, was the first time the red-haired lady showed me a vision that came true.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Somehow, I already knew it wouldn’t be the last.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah Aven"}]},{"n":2,"letterTitle":"The Secret Language of Jack","narrator":"James","numword":"Two","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Secret Language of Jack","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Have you ever woken up one day and realized you’ve fallen for someone and have no idea when or how it happened? It just happened. I’m not talking about the puppy-dog kind of “I’ll-be-over-you-next-week-or-next-month” kind of love kids in high school experience all the time. I’ve felt that before, and it’s powerful in its way. What I’m talking about is the kind of love that punches you in the gut and brings you to your knees—the kind that makes you realize you need that person the way fire needs air.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It changes you, you know? You go from being your own man to suddenly being her man. She doesn’t even have to reciprocate; you’re that far gone. Standing there at the curb in Belmont Center, waiting for Norah Aven Lukens to finally look me in the eye after nine months of being MIA, brought it all back. I’m lucky I managed to stay on my feet when she finally looked at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The accusation in those pale green eyes cut so deep it could have filleted the meat off my bones. She had every right to be angry and even more right to be hurt and mistrusting of me. I saw her run into the bathroom once she got off the bus. I knew Norah well enough to know she was trying to avoid riding home with me. I understood, of course. If I was seeing things with the same limited perspective Dr. Lukens had given her, I’d be trying to avoid myself too.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Name’s James Riley. I’ve lived on the same street as the Lukens for ten years now. The first time I ever saw Norah was the day Gram and I moved in next door. Norah was hanging out in that old cherry tree next to her bedroom window. I knew she was spying on me. I was ten; she was eight, and it was cute. She’s always been cute. And smart. And funny. But never in an obvious way, you know. All the best things about her took time to discover. I was lucky enough to have ten years since then discovering Norah Aven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d always cared about her—been protective of her—in a big brother kind of way. Then, without any warning, it just changed. One day, I woke up caring about her in a way that, quite frankly, scared the living daylights out of me. Suddenly she was everything I never knew I needed in life. I was only 18 at the time, for crying out loud. I had plans. I fought my feelings for all I was worth. Then one day, I didn’t want to anymore. Of course, that was also the day Dr. Lukens decided to start acting like a true guardian to Nor. Then all hell broke loose.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The night I picked Norah up on Belmont Center, I was feeling that same scary protectiveness again, especially when we got to her house, found out Dr. Lukens was dead, and old Detective Narson pounced on her with all sorts of questions: “Where were you when the murder happened? Why didn’t your uncle attend your graduation? Why do you look so strange? I hear you got sent away to that nutjob school because you’ve been seeing things?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ll be honest up front—I’m not a huge fan of Narson. He’s always rubbed me the wrong way. He’s been showing up at fires, throwing his insignificant weight around like he owns the place. This time, the guy wasn’t even using the little tact he did have. For crying out loud, not only did Nor just find out her uncle was dead, but she didn’t take it real well. I finally had to step in and tell him to back off so she could gather her wits a little.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not that it did any good. Unfortunately, I directed her to the french doors that look out on the patio—the one place in the Lukens’ house that would only upset her more. I was kicking myself the moment she looked out onto the back porch and saw the blackened mess that used to be her old cherry tree and the tree house platform Dr. Lukens built for her one summer. All I could think was, “Stellar move, James.” In my defense, how was I supposed to know the doc wouldn’t have it cleaned up by now?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When she asked what happened, I wanted to tell her the truth, I really did. But I couldn’t. Not only because I promised her uncle I never would, but because what happened to it was the event that changed everything between us last summer. To tell her the truth would bring on a lot more questions I’d been dying to answer for the past nine months, and I was pretty sure the temptation would be too much to handle if she asked me now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know, you’re probably thinking Dr. Lukens is dead now, so what’s the harm? The harm is I’ve always been a man of my word, and I wasn’t about to change that now—not when the one person I cared about more than anything in the world’s safety depended on it. The answers to those questions would drag her into a situation way more confusing and dangerous than the one she found herself in now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I told her the tree caught fire the night before she left for Dunstan. I told her it had burned all night while she was unconscious with her very first episode—one of her vision thingies. It was part of the truth, at least. I hoped she wouldn’t remember the rest of that night so I wouldn’t have to lie to her. One of my greatest ambitions in life is to never lie to Norah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, before she could ask any more questions, Narson interrupted our conversation. This time, he asked her to follow him. I don’t know if Norah realized what she was doing when she slid her hand into mine, but boy, it felt good to touch her again. It felt even better to imagine she needed and wanted me around again, even if it was just for moral support.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed her and Narson to Dr. Lukens’ library. Honestly, I would’ve followed her anywhere just to keep holding her hand. The library was a disaster. There were more books on the floor than on the built-in shelves that lined the walls. I asked Narson if his people had made the mess. Forensics was all over the place. He glared at me and said they’d found it this way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After leading Norah into the room where her uncle’s body had obviously been found and seeing her take those slow, deep breaths she always takes when she’s on the verge of a panic attack, I told Narson he’d better get to the point already.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He must have seen the warning signs too because he did get to the point. Straight to the point. He motioned, and one of his people came forward with a large envelope. Narson dumped the contents onto Dr. Lukens’ desk, and he asked Nor if she had any idea what it was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Norah knew exactly what it was, and so did I. We’d both seen her uncle scribbling in the little black books often enough. She told Narson the book was one of her uncle’s research journals—where he kept his field notes from his various archaeological digs and “myth chaser” escapades that had constantly taken him away from Norah since she was ten. Honestly, aside from a string of half-baked nannies wandering in and out of her life, the girl had practically raised herself. It was no wonder she had some anxiety issues.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Narson wanted more answers, though. He opened the journal to a random page and asked Norah if she was sure it was just a research journal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To the average Joe, the writing in Dr. Lukens’ journals looks like nothing more than a bunch of weird swirly doodles with a few sketches here and there. But that was the point. Dr. Lukens had always been a bit paranoid when it came to his research. It wasn’t until last summer that I finally understood why. That’s why he kept all his notes in his very own “secret language.” Weird? Definitely. But like I said, last summer was pretty…enlightening.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But my girl Norah lived in the same house as Dr. Lukens, and she happened to have a real knack for picking up languages of any kind. She’s kind of a prodigy, if I’m being honest. So it was no big shocker when she told Narson she could read every word of her uncle Jack’s secret language.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If the detective was surprised, he didn’t show it. All the guy did was hand her the journal and order her to have it translated for him by the next afternoon.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Like I said, the guy liked to throw his weight around. I kinda wanted to punch him right then, but the thing is, I was under specific instructions to make sure Norah got the journal in the first place. There was stuff in there she needed to know if we had any hope of protecting her from what she didn’t know, so I kept my mouth shut and waited to see what she would do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well, my girl simply asked the detective, why? Why did he need it translated and why so soon?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because it holds the answer to your uncle’s death,” he simply said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Honestly, as much as I dislike the guy, I really couldn’t have said it better myself.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"so":2,"title":"Code Key","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"21c799b5ba3540f5939b9e13d13889f5"},{"so":3,"title":"Secret Message #1","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"58110b3bcb0c45d29e522a5f3447713a"},{"so":4,"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You have officially been entrusted with the key to Dr. Jack A. Lukens' secret language. Do not throw it away! There will be secret messages throughout these chronicles. Only one who holds the key will be able to decipher them.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":3,"letterTitle":"The Blue-eyed Man","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Three","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Blue-eyed Man","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If I could describe the terror that takes over when the lights go out and I’m hostage to whatever “vision” the red-haired lady wants to show me, I would. But let’s just say it’s like watching a horror movie, only unlike a horror movie, I can’t look away. I can’t turn it off or walk out of the room or even cover my eyes. I’m trapped, and I must watch whatever is going to unfold. It wouldn’t be so bad if my visions were occasionally happy or fantastical, but they’re never pleasant. In fact, my visions are always disturbing in a horror movie kind of way, with all the supernatural elements you’d expect to find there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sure, I mentally remind myself it’s not real, that it’ll be over soon, and it’s just my broken, messed up mind’s way of doing some serious processing of a lifetime’s worth of subconscious abandonment issues. But that’s never very comforting.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yes, I was abandoned as a baby by my real parents. Yes, Uncle Jack was gone a lot on his myth chases, and he spent most of the time he was here shut up in his library scribbling away in his journals. But I also have some really good memories being shut up in his library with him while he told me countless legends about the ancient Cobbogothians he’d uncovered throughout his career. I mean, I don’t think he ever really saw me as his daughter, but all things considered, it wasn’t as bad as where I could have ended up if he hadn’t taken me in. I mean, he obviously valued me enough to teach me his secret language, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Detective Narson finally finished interrogating me the night my uncle was found, James led me from my house—now a crime scene—across the street to his house. Nothing had changed between us just because Uncle Jack was dead, but I was grateful James was stepping in for old time’s sake and giving me a place to stay for the night, rather than letting Detective Narson put me in a jail cell just to “keep me safe,” as he put it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After showing me to the guest room, James left me to get settled in for the night. I was exhausted, and my mind was racing. I needed time to process everything that had happened since coming home. I didn’t spend any time unpacking or even undressing. I just slipped off my sneakers, curled up on the bed, and closed my eyes, losing myself in my tangled thoughts.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before James and I left, Detective Narson told us he’d discovered Uncle Jack’s research journal out in the mailbox. It was in an envelope addressed to me. I’d only just realized the journal was most likely the graduation present Uncle Jack hinted at in his text message.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They wouldn’t know until an autopsy was done, but Detective Narson said there was no reason to suspect foul play in Uncle Jack’s death. In fact, he even said there were several indications that the mess in the library had been created by Uncle Jack himself and not an intruder. So what on earth had caused his death? Had he been sick and didn’t think to tell me? Did he keep it from me because I was already in such bad shape mentally?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I heard a knock on the door, I told James to come in. He sat an extra blanket on the end of the bed and asked how I was. I don’t know what I was expecting to see when he looked at me, maybe a little pity, some fear, or even resentment because of last summer? Even though I was unconscious when it happened, I was the one who abandoned him without any explanation. But I didn’t see any of those things in his eyes. All I saw was understanding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when it hit me. How could he not understand? Just last summer, James had gone through losing Gram Riley—the only family he’d ever known.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James picked the blanket back up and came toward me. He told me to lie back down, and when I did, he proceeded to tuck me in, just like I’m sure Gram used to tuck him in when he was a little boy. It was sweet, and I had to smile a little, despite myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After he finished tucking me in, he kissed my forehead and took my hand. He didn’t look at me, just looked at my hand for a long time. Then, referring to my bracelet—remember the silver cuff I discovered in the bathroom earlier—asked if I was comfortable or if I wanted to take it off. I shrugged, so James slid it off my hand and set it on the nightstand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew something was wrong the instant my hand dropped back to the bed. The lights started to dim, tunnel vision set in, and I was going into vision mode for the second time that day. And in front of James, which was so not cool. It was totally going to freak him out!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Even as the thought crossed my mind and the darkness gave way to my latest mini-horror film, I was surprised to feel James move onto the bed beside me, his arms going around me. The last thing I heard were the comforting words he whispered in my ear, “Don’t worry, Nor, I’ve got you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was running in the patch of woods at Dunstan. It was something I started doing to get off the sleep meds my therapist had me on. I found that if I ran long and hard, I was tired enough to sleep without them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I waited for the red-haired lady to show up and start directing the show as usual, but so far there was no sign of her. As I continued to run, I began to hear something in the trees above me. It was a strange noise, like the opening and closing of a large paper bag. I looked up but couldn’t see anything except leaves and branches.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Soon I entered a small clearing in the woods. I heard the noise again, just behind me. I stopped, and it stopped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Suddenly there were footsteps. I turned just in time to catch sight of a man—well, a sort of man. He was all man except for the strange bat-like wings quickly gathering in behind him, where I assumed they were vanishing into his shoulder blades. His eyes were yellow and his skin waxy and pale, and he looked at me with an animalistic gleam in his eyes. My mind knew I’d seen something like him before—a drawing or picture somewhere. Maybe in a different vision? I even knew what he was called: a dogril.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I heard myself speak the word and knew I needed to run—needed to get myself as far away from this bat-like humanoid creature as possible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was able to turn and go, but before I could get far, he lunged for me, one of his claws catching me just above my collar bone. I could feel the sting of it as he scratched my skin. Tears welled as terror threatened to choke me. I scrambled on hands and knees into a patch of underbrush.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was sure the creature would come after me—that he’d get me this time. Instead, I heard him hollering something from farther away. I stopped and turned back, just in time to see him stumble forward as though he’d been pushed by an invisible force.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lylend,” he shouted. “Show yourself!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched in horror as, in the very next moment, the air between us split open, and a man—a very large, hooded man—stepped out of nowhere.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The creature was beginning to transform back into its bat-winged form—the dogril—when the hooded man lifted a hand and shot a giant bolt of lightning out of his palm, and the creature was gone. Just like that, he was. Vaporized by lightning.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was on my feet and running toward Dunstan. I glanced over my shoulder once, then slammed into something, falling back onto the earth, gasping from having the air knocked out of me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could regain my breath, the large, hooded man hoisted me onto my feet. He clasped something onto my wrist. Then his vivid blue eyes—glowing eyes—found mine, and he told me I needed to run because there were more dogrils after me, and I wouldn’t be safe until I was back in Boston. He instructed me to gather my things at the school and get on the bus without delay—that the bracelet he’d put on my wrist would protect me until I reached home.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As my sight began to clear and I found myself back in James’ guest room, his arms tight around me, I realized something was very wrong. What I had just seen wasn’t a vision or even a premonition. I know it sounds crazy—believe me, I really do—but I was remembering, remembering something that had actually happened earlier this afternoon. I knew it as certainly as I knew James was right there beside me. Besides, wasn’t the cut just above my collar bone proof—and the bracelet now lying on the nightstand beside me? Not only that, but now that I was fully awake, I knew precisely where I’d seen that drawing of the dogril before and how I knew what it was called.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I broke free of James’ arms and hurried to my duffel on the floor. When I found Uncle Jack’s journal, I shuffled through the familiar pages until I found the drawing I was looking for of the dogril. Marking the page was an old newspaper clipping I’d never seen before and a note from Uncle Jack. The note was written in his secret language, and it was addressed to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I have included them for you to read. Go ahead and make what you will of them. Before my story is finished, you’ll discover if your conclusions are right or not.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"News: Truth in Icelandic Myth","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"5ac8d78df8e04681ba20ee9aace45e52"},{"so":3,"title":"Journal: Dogril Diagram","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f2c653ce0c0442d5a2260aa2299b3c65"},{"so":4,"title":"Secret Message #2","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"8d860bd7505c4b19ae6c0cea9868e4a0"},{"so":5,"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Norah,","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I'm sure you have many questions for me right now. I'm sorry I can't be there to answer them, but perhaps this journal can. Study it carefully, Norah Aven. It's the best preparation I can offer for what's to come.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Uncle Jack","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":4,"letterTitle":"The Crystal City","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Four","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Crystal City","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You can probably already tell I’m in love with James Riley. If I’m being honest, I’ve been in love with him since the day he moved in across the street. That love was a little girl’s infatuation, though. The love that happened between us last summer was something else entirely. It was like we were suddenly the same person. When he hurt, I hurt. If I was happy, he was happy. Even our thoughts were in sync. I suppose that’s why Uncle Jack finally decided to get involved and put a stop to things.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But our history didn’t change the fact that I wasn’t going to tell James what I’d just seen in my vision. I wasn’t going to share Uncle Jack’s journal with him either, not until I was sure what Uncle Jack was trying to prepare me for. Detective Narson thought it contained the key to Uncle Jack’s death, and maybe it did, but now it was clear that’s not why Uncle Jack left it to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Did Uncle Jack want me to finish his life’s work? Was leaving me his journal his way of telling me it was his dying wish? Was he “preparing me” for all the grueling work it would take to finally finish his research?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The answers to those questions remained to be seen. However, what I did unexpectedly discover is that James had wanted to contact me over the past nine months, but Uncle Jack had forbidden him from doing it—said contact from the outside world could potentially slow my healing process. Being near James again, I was already feeling more like myself than I had since last summer—even with the two vision episodes happening in one day—so I was pretty sure that was a serious load of cra—er, malarky. It was probably just Uncle Jack’s excuse to keep James and me apart, if possible. But James and I would have to talk about that later.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After assuring him for the tenth time that it was all right—that of course I understood and didn’t blame him for anything—I finally convinced James to leave so I could get some sleep. As soon as I heard his footsteps heading down the hall, however, I locked the door and made my way back to the bed with Uncle Jack’s journal. Now it was time to get to work.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All I found at first were notes, sketches, and diagrams—things I’d seen countless times before when Uncle Jack had shown them to me while telling one of his stories. Before I reached the end of the journal, however, I came across a few very strange things. The first was a series of regular, dated, diary-style entries written by Uncle Jack in the 90s. The second was that Uncle Jack’s secret language wasn’t originally his after all; he’d created it using hieroglyphs he’d found in Iceland—in the twelve Caves of Aegissida. The third was the fact that several pages had been torn out of the journal just after the diary entries. (For the record, Uncle Jack never would have destroyed any of his research.) And fourth was that Uncle Jack had left another hidden message for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was one other thing—something I didn’t discover until I’d read everything else. It was a hidden compartment in the back of the journal, a sort of pocket where a very peculiar, hand-drawn map had been hidden. The back of the map was covered with notes in Uncle Jack’s handwriting.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ve included copies of each with this chronicle. I wanted to include a few of the diagrams as well, but unfortunately, I only got the journal entries translated before I heard someone talking just outside my window. I quickly shut off my lamp and crept to the window. It didn’t take long to recognize it was James. The clock on my nightstand showed it was just after 2:00 in the morning. I thought maybe he was having trouble sleeping too, but then I realized James wasn’t alone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t mean to eavesdrop at first. Promise. But when they mentioned my name, Uncle Jack’s death, and his journal, and then talked about wanting to get the journal away from me, I couldn’t help it. The longer I listened, the more confused and scared I got. All I could think to myself was maybe, just maybe, I didn’t know James Riley as well as I thought I did.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: Uncle Jack's Journal Pages","kind":"webp","pages":8,"id":"53d111d56a0a4de88f8d4a3bf9677e16"},{"so":3,"title":"Map of Crystal City","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"06141c75d618480dac962bae5e45c6e4"},{"so":4,"title":"Secret Message #3","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2e05310a1faf4f418dc4bf8e9ff38793"},{"so":5,"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Norah,","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I'm afraid my hands are tied and prevent me from being able to tell you more outright. However, go see our good friend Samir Anju when you've made all you can of this journal. He will help you stay on the right track.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Be careful who you trust from now on.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":5,"letterTitle":"The Secret Life of James Riley","narrator":"James","numword":"Five","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Secret Life of James Riley","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If only I’d known the damage a single unguarded conversation could do. But how was I supposed to know Norah was listening? Yeah, I had a visitor that night. He came around to my house. And, yes, he had some pretty important questions to ask about Norah, Dr. Lukens’ death, and the journal. But he also had some pretty important information to give me in exchange.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew Norah hadn’t told me everything she’d seen in her vision because almost the moment she’d come back to herself, she practically ran across the room to shuffle through her uncle’s journal. But it was okay because things were still pretty fragile between us. It was going to take time to rebuild trust. I hoped I had made some progress with her when I told her about Dr. Lukens forbidding me to contact her while she was at Dunstan, but it was obvious her mind was wrapped up in something else. Or maybe she just didn’t want to forgive me yet, which I could understand, all things considered. I mean, I did tell her I loved her right before everything happened that caused Dr. Lukens to send her away. She probably thought I didn’t mean a word of it when she didn’t hear from me for months—probably thought I just went back to my regular life, completely clueless to the pain she was going through all alone in that place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There’s a whole lot more to that story than I’m ready to tell you right now. A lot has happened since last summer. Not just at Dunstan. A lot has happened here. In my life. In fact, a lot has been happening in my life since the day I turned 18. That day was a real shocker for me. Not only did I learn that Gram had been keeping a whole lot of secrets about herself, my parents, and me from me, but it was also the day I suddenly realized I knew I loved Norah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gram knew it long before I had a clue, of course. She always knew stuff like that before anyone else, but that was the end of it once I knew. Every thought, every hope, every plan for the future was nothing without Norah Aven Lukens in it. Nothing about last summer changed that for me, despite Dr. Lukens’ best efforts. He could say all he wanted about getting too serious too fast and what not, but what I felt for Norah wasn’t going anywhere. Believe me, I’d tried to make it go away. Geez! Especially after everything Gram told me on my eighteenth b-day. But like I said, that’s a story for another day.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Would you believe me if I told you I was going through my own torture while Norah was locked up at Dunstan? And would you believe me if I told you I was painfully aware of her suffering in that place? Dr. Lukens might’ve even agreed to bring her home sooner if he’d been around to see the mess his interference caused for both of us. But he wasn’t, was he? Nah, the old doc took off for the year. He was in Iceland doing “important research.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now I’ll admit, after the conversation with my visitor, I now know that research was much more important than I realized, but still, Dr. Lukens was supposed to be like a father to Norah. Fathers are supposed to protect their daughters, right? Then why did it feel like I was more concerned about protecting her than he was?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Again, that’s what I thought before my visitor came. Can you tell I’ve struggled to make peace with this new information here? That I’m trying to give the old doc the benefit of the doubt? If he’d just been more up front about stuff, we could’ve avoided a couple of disasters.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For example, it was pretty obvious the conversation Norah thought she heard between me and my visitor that night and what was actually said were two very different things, since she was gone when I went to check on her after my visitor left. Whatever she thought she heard caused her to run off without a word or a note. Nothing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now, I’m not the panicky type, as a rule, but I don’t mind telling you the first thing that came to my mind was that she’d been taken. The helplessness I felt at that thought was worse than just about anything I’ve ever felt before. And that’s saying something because I’ve felt some really bad stuff since Gram died.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, Dr. Lukens had planned ahead enough to give me these clippings as clues to where she might run off to if she ever got the wrong idea about things. Dr. Anjou wasn’t such a bad guy—I’d visited his shop once or twice with Norah—but I’d never met this Maximillian guy. All I could do was hope to high heaven Norah stayed in Boston and didn’t run back to Dunstan.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only I’d tried harder to get her to trust me. If only Dr. Lukens had been more open and honest sooner about, well, everything. If only she hadn’t overheard my conversation that night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only…if only…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Man, I hate secrets! If it wasn’t for all the ones I had to keep from Norah, she wouldn’t have felt like she had to keep stuff from me. I definitely wouldn’t have been out traipsing all over Boston looking for her after my visitor left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And yet, as it turned out, it was a darn good thing I was out there looking for her. Because what Norah didn’t know yet—what I hadn’t gotten the chance to tell her before she ran off—was that I wasn’t the worst thing out there looking for her. Nah, not by a long shot.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"so":2,"title":"News: Nobel Winner","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"22d141807b0848b993be043c12c2b671"},{"so":3,"title":"News: From Globetrotter","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"64acdcb07a0c490b8e228ed0bd76f0bb"},{"so":4,"title":"Map of Harvard","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4852d104f4e143549245b1a5f05e5293"}]},{"n":6,"letterTitle":"The Curious Case of Norah Lukens","narrator":"Samir Anjou","numword":"Six","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Twin Brothers' Tale","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I suppose I should begin by introducing myself. It’s peculiar to write an account of one’s dealings after one is dead. The truth is, death is not the end, my friends. Now you know.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My name is Samir Anjou. I am…was…a professor of physics at Harvard University with Dr. Jack Lukens. I knew his ward, Norah Aven, quite well. She often came to my residence in Harvard Square for help with her physics education. She was quite a remarkable student, Norah. Her mind was like an extraordinarily absorbent sponge. She had a photographic memory, I believe. She understood almost immediately everything I taught her, and she was endlessly asking questions to fill an insatiable appetite for knowledge and an understanding of our world. In my experience, homeschooled students are often like that. Perhaps it’s the ability to explore learning at their own pace and according to their own interests. Such was the case with Norah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grew quite fond of her over the years of our acquaintance. Her guardian, for he wasn’t actually her uncle as I’m sure you’ve been told, was a rather negligent protector. At least that was what I thought until he visited me in the very early hours on the morning of the day he died. The truth is, I believe he was already dying when we spoke.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jack and I had a common interest in the metaphysical realms—in the possibility of parallel worlds existing in our universe. We discussed such things at length. You might say I was something of an anomaly in my profession because, ironically, I was a scientist who also owned a mystic arts shop—The Mirage—on the corner of Dunster and Mt. Auburn at Harvard Square. My shop no longer stands, as you’ll learn soon enough. When The Mirage did stand, it stood in a sea of intellectuals who were utterly sterilized of any imagination outside of what they could see, touch, feel, or prove with facts. My shop was one of the great controversies, as well as curiosities, at Harvard Square, and one I was quite proud to own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All this, I imagine is why Jack decided to come see me on the day he died. By choosing me, I believe he felt he wouldn’t have to waste precious time convincing me of things I already believed were possible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He came by taxi at about 4:30 in the morning. He didn’t waste time on niceties, just simply bid me take him to my lab so he could show me something he assured me would be well worth my time as a scientist and spiritualist. When we reached my laboratory, he immediately made himself at home, pulling out a microscope and glass slides. I didn’t mind, of course, as every visit Jack made usually ended most profitably if I just allowed the method of his madness to reveal itself in its own time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This time was no different, only the payoff was far more rewarding than on any of his previous visits.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everyone at Harvard knew about Jack’s famous dogril discovery. We all knew about the mysterious “energy element” he found in its DNA, so when he pulled out a vial of blood and began administering a drop to the glass slide, I was most intrigued. I certainly didn’t waste any time when he motioned me over to look under the microscope.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After I’d had a good, long look at one of the most remarkable things I’d ever seen in my thirty-two-year career, he asked, “Do you know what this means?” Of course I knew what it meant—at least I thought I did. It meant that somehow, in some way, the world-famous myth chaser Dr. Jack A. Lukens had gotten his hands on a real-life sample of dogril blood. When I told him as much, he shook his head and said what he thought was dogril blood all those years before was not. It was, in fact, the blood of the Cobbogothian the dogril had possessed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He then explained the reason he believed it was dogril blood and not Cobbogothian blood at first was because, until recently, he didn’t understand the nature of the dogril. He quickly explained—for he still seemed to be in a dreadful hurry—that a dogril did not initially have a body of its own. Instead, it was like a metaphysical parasite. It latched onto or “possessed” the body of another living humanoid, then used its power to transform that body to reflect the dogril’s metaphysical form.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, he showed me the included diagram and notes from his journal. They were of what he called the “Azura.” He told me this Azura was what made the ancient Cobbogothians so different from Homo sapiens—what qualified them as an elevated race.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After studying it for a moment, all I could think to ask was, “Where on earth did you find more Cobbogothian blood?” That’s when he told me a most curious story about an ancient crystal city buried deep beneath a volcano in Iceland. He told me about an ancient temple at the center of that city and how he had discovered a room there filled with urns of Cobbogothian blood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t yet understand why they regarded their blood as sacred,” he simply said, but then he gave me a folded piece of paper that looked as though whatever was written on it had actually been rubbed onto it from a stone surface, for the characters looked like engravings and were entirely unrecognizable to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jack said he believed the answer lay in this document. He then told me he had set a series of events into motion that would eventually lead to Norah Aven coming to see me. When that time came, he asked me to promise to show her the sample of the Cobbogothian’s blood and give her the rubbing. He said she would be able to translate it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gave him my word, and then he left me. The next time I saw or heard of Jack was later that night when his picture was plastered all over the news, reporting his untimely and most mysterious death. There was no evidence of struggle, no sign of foul play. There was no illness that could have caused it. Dr. Jack A. Lukens was simply dead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So when Norah came to see me the following morning and told me that the last person she thought she could trust—that handsome young James fellow I saw her with so often last summer—was no longer trustworthy, the urgency of delivering her uncle’s message was all the more pressing on my mind. I didn’t waste time. I simply delivered the message and was about to send her on her way when I heard my shop being broken into downstairs. My first thought was, of course, to protect the girl and the secret she now carried. Whether James had turned against her or not, who knew what other sort of villains might be looking for Jack’s latest discovery or what they would be willing to do to get it? I knew from years of conversations with Jack that Norah Aven was key to finishing his life’s work, and now I knew how vitally transformative his work would be to mankind and our often-conceited view of our superiority in the universe.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly guided Norah to a secret passage I had once discovered in the old pre-colonial building that housed my shop and living quarters. It was a passage that had been built by the Freemasons before the Revolutionary War. Thankfully, it led directly into Harvard’s sewer system. After instructing her to run and to trust no one with what she now knew and possessed, I closed her in, praying she would be able to safely find her way out and that I had sufficiently kept my word to Jack.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then, dear reader, armed with a clear conscience before my Maker and an insatiable, lifelong curiosity to discover what lay in the great beyond, I faced the advancing villains and my fate.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dr. Samir Anjou","i":false}],"narrator":"Samir Anjou"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: The Azura","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"fac80e3b4d2f431b945f1070aa75b5f1"},{"so":3,"title":"News: Fire on the Square!","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"79f30b30797c40a69ba0702171175e79"},{"so":4,"title":"The Tale of the Twin Brothers","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d10a0dad722a4c428c5de86026cbfe29"}]},{"n":7,"letterTitle":"The Vow","narrator":"James","numword":"Seven","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Betrayal","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Facts are funny things. Dr. Lukens used to say you’d limit your possibilities exponentially if you stuck with the facts. Me, I like the facts, and in this particular situation, I needed to limit the possibilities. If only I’d had all the facts when I followed Norah to Samir’s shop—if only I’d known how close she was to danger when I’d let her go into the Mirage alone—none of this would have happened. I never would have left her side. But this was Samir. Norah was supposed to be perfectly safe with him. That’s why I kept my distance and gave her some space to see if she could find out for herself some of the things I couldn’t tell her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But she wasn’t safe at Samir’s. Somehow, less than an hour and a half after watching her enter the Mirage, I was called into work to help put out a fire on the corner of Dunster and Mt. Auburn—the exact location of Samir’s shop.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew it had to be a big fire for the Cambridge Fire Department to call Belmont Station for backup. I was right. The fire was huge. In fact, it might’ve taken down more than the Mirage if crews from Newton and Somerville hadn’t shown up as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If I wasn’t scared for Nor’s safety before, I was then. There was no sign of her anywhere—nothing to indicate she’d even been at the Mirage. Nothing, that is, except for what I’d seen with my own eyes and the word of the bagel shop owner across the street describing the last person to enter the Mirage that morning as a pale wisp of a girl with platinum hair.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we found Samir’s body—what was left of it, anyway—and only Samir’s body, I let myself start hoping again that Nor made it out of there all right. I knew Samir personally, and if he had sensed Norah was in any danger, he would have protected her with his last breath. It was why I had thought she’d be safe going to see him on her own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t until one of the Somerville guys pulled back a charred bookshelf on the second floor of Samir’s shop and called me over to look that I started breathing easy again. The bookshelf had been hiding one of those old Freemason secret passages with a staircase heading right down into Harvard Square’s sewer system. There was no doubt in my mind Nor had escaped through there, especially when I saw fresh footprints in the dust on the stone stairs. You better believe I did my best to destroy that evidence when no one was looking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As soon as the fire was out and the chief gave me the go-ahead, I took the fastest route I could back to Barbara Road. Since she hadn’t taken any of her luggage when she ran off to see Samir that morning, I figured Nor would come back to my place eventually. I needed to be there when she did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After I got home, I headed to the back porch of my house and waited. I had plenty of time to come up with a plan before she showed up. The fact was, there was no way I was going to be able to keep Norah safe if things went on as they were. Like I said before, she didn’t know enough, and I couldn’t tell her anything more. As far as I could see, there was only one thing left to do if I wanted to avoid flat out lying to her or breaking any of the promises I’d made to Dr. Lukens. My plan would, however, require me to make some wickedly risky moves that might just end up causing Norah to hate me for the rest of our lives.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"No matter how many times I went over it in my mind though, I couldn’t see any other way. So I took the risk. I had just enough time to put my plan in motion before Norah came trudging up the hill on the backwoods path from Pleasant Street. We used to go that way all the time as kids because it was the quickest and most secluded way home.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Nor saw me on the back porch, she slowed down a bit. There was caution in those pale green eyes. It was hard to see the distrust in the look she gave me, but I knew it was better she be too cautious than too trusting right now. Besides, she definitely wasn’t going to trust me when I did what I was about to do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We both stood there looking at each other for a moment. She spoke first. It was just one word.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Samir?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dead,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It didn’t surprise me when a tear slid down her pale cheek.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew how she felt about Samir. A wave of suffocating grief crashed over me, nearly knocking the wind out of me. It wasn’t my grief. I must’ve been feeling it for her. I’d always been pretty plugged into Nor’s feelings. Boy, I wished I wasn’t about to add to that grief.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I heard the sirens start blaring down on Belmont Center, I knew they’d be turning onto Pleasant Street any moment. I kept my cool, made my way over to Norah, and took her in my arms. She let me hold her and buried her face in my shoulder. If I wasn’t about to sell her out, I would’ve relished the feel of her in my arms a bit more. Instead, I told her how sorry I was—and I was sorry. I told her everything was going to be all right. I told her everything I had done and would do was only to keep her safe. But I didn’t tell her that one day she’d understand because, quite frankly, I didn’t know if she ever would.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the cop cars turned onto Barbara Road and screeched to a halt in my driveway, I tightened my hold on her. My girl is no dummy. She sensed something was up and began to struggle to get free. I heard the pounding on my front door and hollered for the cops to come around back. They were swarming within seconds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then she was gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not even the look of complete betrayal she gave me as Narson and his men cuffed her and lowered her into the cruiser changed my mind about what I’d done. It was the only way. Thankfully, I’d had enough forethought to slip Dr. Lukens’ journal from her pocket while I held her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know what you’re thinking of me right about now, and I can’t say I blame you. I mean, isn’t this the girl I love more than air? Hadn’t I made a promise to protect her or something? Why had I taken the trouble to destroy evidence of her being at Samir’s if I was just going to turn her over to Detective Narson?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well, the answer to all your questions is simple, really. Danger had been on Norah Aven’s trail since the day she was born, and it was finally starting to catch up to her. If I wanted even the slightest chance of keeping her safe, it was time to call in the cavalry.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"so":2,"title":"News: Smell's Like Teen Trauma","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d3bbc621ca1940709dbd8fcbd1140b5d"},{"so":3,"title":"Map of Belmont","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"b398653efbc54c11b1d7c94ba45450c6"}]},{"n":8,"letterTitle":"The Funeral","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Eight","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Hoption Hole","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Did you know a 17-year-old can be tried as an adult for murder in the state of Massachusetts? I didn’t. Not until Detective Narson told me and implied that, while he still didn’t know how I’d done it, he planned to charge me once he’d proven I, and not “mysterious circumstances,” had killed my uncle. In fact, according to Narson’s half-baked theory, he was almost certain I was responsible because he was “certain” I’d killed Samir. Since I was at the Mirage before Samir’s death, he had a bit more of a case there, but how did he arrive at these other conclusions? I mean, I had a solid alibi where Uncle Jack’s death was concerned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, though, Narson was sounding alarmingly deranged while he hollered all his theories at me, so I wasn’t too worried when I first arrived at the Belmont Police Station. In fact, I was certain there was no way his theories would hold up in a rational court of law, especially since I’m only seventeen. Plus, if I had killed Uncle Jack, why on earth did he leave me clues in his journal—clues even Narson believed would lead to discovering the true cause of Uncle Jack’s death?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At the same time, I was reeling over the fact that James was the one who’d called the cops on me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My surprising lack of freaking out ended once I was searched and realized Uncle Jack’s journal wasn’t in my pocket anymore. I went into full-on panic mode after that. That’s right, I blacked out and everything. You’re probably thinking, “What is up with this girl and blacking out? Get a grip already.” Believe me, I’ve tried. Regardless, Officer Germani had to carry me back to my cell while Narson whispered to him about how he was sure I was faking it in order to get out of answering his questions. It was humiliating, and my slurred reply for him to go…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You know what, I’m just going to skip that part, as it has nothing to do with my story.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know what questions are probably running through your mind right now. Why would James do something like this? How could I ever trust him again? Wasn’t I angry at him? To answer all those questions honestly: I didn’t know, I don’t know, and yes, I was angry. Like I said, I was reeling. I was hurt and confused, and I felt completely alone again. But these were all the feelings I’d felt at Dunstan for the last nine months—my whole life, really, if you count all the times Uncle Jack had left me alone. I had numbed myself to these feelings, so while I can say I was definitely surprised by James’ betrayal, I had gotten used to being disappointed and was therefore able to talk myself out of blacking out a second time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The truth is, while I was feeling all those feelings toward James right then, I couldn’t help feeling like there was something going on with him—that everything would make sense if I just knew more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I have this note James wrote me last summer, you see. I guess you could call it a “love note.” I’d kept it with me ever since he gave it to me. I always kept it close because, despite all the days, weeks, and months that went by without hearing a word from James while I was at Dunstan, it reminded me that when I fell in love with him, I fell in love with the James who wrote that note, not the James he’d been since Gram’s funeral. Somehow, between losing Gram Riley and what happened afterward, the James I fell in love with had gotten locked away somewhere inside him—had vanished somehow. I’d only seen a few glimpses of him since. I suspect it has to do with whatever happened that night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Boy, the night of Gram Riley’s funeral was a doozy. She died after suffering from an illness no doctor could properly diagnose. Why? Because Gram refused to see any doctor. From what I gathered from bits and pieces of conversations, my own observations, and things Gram and James told me, Gram’s illness looked like cancer on the inside but acted like a strange form of leprosy on the outside. She was ravenously hungry all the time but looked like a walking skeleton by the time she died. Even when she was constantly in and out of consciousness toward the end, no one, not even Uncle Jack, insisted she see a doctor. I didn’t understand it and did everything I could to try to get them to do something for her. But Gram didn’t want it, and whenever I brought the subject up with James or Uncle Jack, they would change the subject. It was the worst.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Because of my homeschool schedule, I was free to help James care for Gram. I spent a lot of time sitting with her during James’ shifts at work. We talked a lot when she was conscious. She told me about James when he was little and a few things about herself. In a lot of ways, Gram had been filling a mom role for me since she and James moved in across the street, but never more so than during those last few months of her life. When she passed, it felt surprisingly like I’d lost the only mother I’d ever known.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, the sadness I felt the night of Gram’s funeral was so intense, so excruciating I was certain something was wrong with me. I didn’t understand it, really. Gram had been kind of like a surrogate mother to me but not enough of one to be feeling that level of grief. The intensity of it scared me so much I almost went down and disturbed Uncle Jack in his library just to talk to someone about it. Before I could, however, I heard a tap on my window, and there was James, looking just as traumatized and broken as I felt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He helped me out onto my cherry tree platform. Then we both burst into tears and sobbed our hearts out together while we held each other under a sky that seemed to be weeping for Gram as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The details of what happened after that are still pretty foggy. I do remember James kissed me. Our relationship had been accelerating toward that moment all summer, but when James finally kissed me after all that waiting, it was not the first kiss I expected. I mean, it was at first. It was almost too perfect. But then it was as though my entire body went up in flames—not the metaphorical flames of first love you read about or see in the movies—it felt like my very veins were on fire. Literally.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The pain was beyond anything I’d ever experienced, and from there it was pretty much game over for me. According to Uncle Jack, I sort of blacked out and went into a kind of seizure. Apparently, I also had my first vision, of which I can’t remember the details for the life of me. And according to what James told me, lightning struck my cherry tree that same night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was unconscious for three days after our kiss. When I woke up, there was no sign of Uncle Jack or James or anyone familiar. Instead, I found myself in the hospital wing at Dunstan Academy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pretty epic first kiss story, huh? It definitely wasn’t the “first kiss” I’d been daydreaming about since James had moved in ten years earlier. Strangely, after I regained consciousness and the initial burning feeling in my mind and body wore off, I couldn’t help wanting James to kiss me again, just to make sure it wasn’t all some strange, very painful dream. Well, that, and I just really wanted James to kiss me again. I know it sounds crazy, but with the pain came the euphoric certainty that James loved me. I mean, he’d whispered it to me three times before kissing me, as though he wanted me to know the kiss was meant to seal the deal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sitting in a jail cell, waiting for Detective Narson to scrounge up some evidence to convict me of two murders, all the while knowing I’d lost the one piece of evidence that might prove my innocence, you’d think I’d have more important things on my mind than that kiss. But I didn’t.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Have you ever been forced to await your own doom? It’s not very pleasant, especially if you have an anxious nature like mine. A lot of people see anxiety as a form of weakness, but that’s because they haven’t been forced to think about it as much as I have. My conclusion about anxiety, panic attacks, and all the things I’ve been dealing with over the last nine months is that people who deal with these kinds of things daily are far braver than people who don’t. Why? Because we have to conquer our fears multiple times a day in order to simply keep moving forward. Every day is a battle, therefore every night deserves a victory dance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was forcing myself to think about this—instead of that first kiss—and how much fear I was going to have to conquer to get myself out of the current mess I was in when I noticed something strange happening in my cell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, it just looked like a small heat wave in the air above my head, a slight ripple. After studying it for a moment to make sure it was really there, I reached out my hand to touch it. When I did, I felt a slight pulsing in my fingertips. I hurried to the edge of my cell and placed my face against the bars. I glanced down the hall as far as I could. No one was there. I turned back to the ripple and found it had turned into a funnel that looked like it was siphoning a mass of colorful particles from the air. I jumped back when those particles rapidly began gathering together and a gnarled hand suddenly formed and thrust itself toward me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now I may be the fainting type, but thankfully I’m not the screaming type because I definitely wanted to scream as that hand grasped at the air in front of me. It looked like it was searching for something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, the hand was followed by an extremely large arm, then a vaguely familiar looking head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, kyndie,” the head said, “are you staying or going?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I knew what I was doing, I took the hand. Then all I could do was scream.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Love Note from James","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a86809eafcac46a6a89713ac92cfb4e7"},{"so":3,"title":"Journal: Hoption Hole","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"83980eb1fe3f4b8489150383014ed4d5"}]},{"n":9,"letterTitle":"A Real Life Cobbogothian","narrator":"Lylend","numword":"Nine","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"A Real-life Cobbogothian","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Ten thousand years is a long time to walk any planet, especially this one. I’ve only actually spent 1,746 of my more than 10,000 years walking your Earth and only nineteen of them in the past several centuries, but it’s been enough.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the Gihara! You humans have created quite a mess, haven’t you? And I thought we Elementalists did a good enough job mucking things up. After all, that’s why the Gihara sent the ice age to wipe us out. Thankfully I had enough sense to follow Cobbogoth into the caves before it all hit. That’s right, I was there when the events of the Great Legend of the Cobbogothians took place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My name is Lylend. I am a real-life, 10,084-year-old Cobbogothian in the flesh. Not all Cobbogothians live as long as me, mind. Most are weaklings who die around 1,050 years old.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Only joking. I am an exception among my people—what we call a Naridi. I am a guardian of sorts, one of seven chosen by the Gihara to guard my people and their existence from, well, anything or anyone who might seek to harm them. Which reminds me, if you happen to be a potential threat to my people, I highly recommend you stop reading now because I will inevitably hunt you down and end you. The less you know, the better, and whatnot.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m joking again, of course. There’s absolutely nothing you could possibly do to harm my people. In fact, humans are at the very, very, very bottom of my list where threats to my people are concerned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I digress.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You may be wondering what a 10,000 plus year old Cobbogothian is doing in your realm in the first place. Well, to be clear, it is not “your” realm, human. It was actually ours to begin with, and if you’ve read the legend, it’s prophesied to be ours again one day. Also, it’s complicated. But, alas, I suppose I will tell you some of it—what I think you need to know. The rest I’ll keep to myself, thank you very much.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oh dear. My tyro just informed me that many of you might find my introduction offensive. Don’t mind me. I’m old, very old. And I quit worrying about coddling hyper-sensitive human feelings centuries ago. Indeed, the last time I didn’t offend a human was the time I ran into an Irish monk in Iceland. His name was Brendan of the Fosterling folk. You probably know him as St. Brendan or Brendan the Navigator. Anyway, the fellow happened to catch a glimpse of me in one of our communocite pillars when I was checking to see who’d landed on our island. Brendan was cut from a fine, jolly cloth, not the delicate gossamer the humans I’ve seen since I returned are swaddled in.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If it makes you feel better, I’m like this to everyone. I do, however, have enough regard for you humans intellectually to always be honest with you. As I mentioned, I will not tell you everything in these chronicles, but what I tell you will be straightforward and truthful to the best of my knowledge, however unbelievable it may seem.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of humans cut from fine, jolly cloth, I’ll get on with my part to tell in this story. I knew long before I saved Norah Aven from the dogrils in the woods by her school that she was an exceptional human. I knew it simply by listening to my friend Jack Lukens speak about her during our long hours searching my former home—or what Jack liked to call the Crystal City—together. Norah Aven was his favorite subject. After yanking her out of a prison cell on Belmont Center and into a hoption hole, I could see why. The kyndie didn’t even look peaky when the hoption hole spit her out on the other end, not even when it spit her out into the arms of myself, a massive stranger (by puny human standards, that is) who happened to be lurking down a dark alley on Belmont Center. My glowing eyes alone should’ve sent her running.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My tyro just informed me you probably don’t know what I mean when I say “kyndie.” “Kyndie” is the Cobbogothian word for “girl.” “Kynd” is the word for “boy,” and “kyndlin” is the word for “children.” Now you know.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So anyway, thankfully Norah remembered me from the woods at Dunstan Academy, and once I told her who and what I was and about my plan to lure the remaining dogrils that were tailing her out of hiding, she didn’t bat an eye—not even when I told her I wanted to use her as the bait to lure them. Bless the Gihara! All I could think to myself was if you humans could create more kyndies like Norah Aven, there might be hope for you yet. Of course, I didn’t know then what I know now…so more on that later.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kyndie was visibly shaking at the prospect of meeting another dogril, but she forged ahead anyway, making her way toward Pleasant Street. My plan was to have her appear to be heading back alone to her uncle’s house on Barbara Road. I assured her I wouldn’t be far behind, but even so, I knew it would bring little comfort with the prospect of encountering clouds of black, shimmery mist down every dark alley and in every tree looming over her. The fact that it was already a misty night from the pouring rain didn’t help either, so I gave her a piece of sygnocite. All she had to do was touch it to the ground where she stood, and someone would show up to aid her. She looked just as skeptical as I expected her to, but it did seem to calm her a little.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swear to you, I didn’t take my eyes off the Kyndie as she made her way up Pleasant Street, and even if I did, I knew my tyro was keeping an eye on her from a safer distance. She was well guarded, but there were more dogrils out there; I knew it. I just didn’t know how many, which was why putting her in danger like this was necessary. We already knew she was what they were after.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Norah Aven was about to reach the corner on Stella Road, she was only out of my sight for a split second, but that was all it took. When I saw her again, she was standing under a streetlight on the opposite side of the road, speaking to a man and a woman.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hung back to watch. They were too far away to hear what was being said, but the Kyndie pointed up Stella Road and nodded. I figured the couple was just asking for directions.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the woman looked back my way, however, I saw something that made my blood run cold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You can learn a great deal about someone by looking into their eyes. It’s the same whether you’re human, Cobbogothian, or dogril. I believe your William Shakespeare had a saying for it, something like, “The eyes are the window to the soul.” Well, this woman’s eyes were a window to her soul—or lack of soul, that is—and the color was bright, glimmering gold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Norah Aven had to have noticed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the man looked my direction and his eyes were a deeper shade of yellow—the color of ripe flax—I almost shouted at the Kyndie to run, but she beat me to it. I saw Norah dive over a stone wall into a patch of woods on the hill above. The next moment, the man and the woman vanished into clouds of black shimmering mist.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I began to run toward the patch of woods, but the dogrils, eyes as large as yellow moons, morphed and emerged from the mist. They dove into the woods after her before I could reach the stone wall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After 10,000 years as a warrior fighting off greater terrors than dogrils, it takes a lot to alarm me. But seeing that little kyndie tear off into the woods only to be followed by the dogrils was enough to frighten even me.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: The Naridi","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a511c890894e4dee88960bbe581ab892"}]},{"n":10,"letterTitle":"The Caves of Aegissida","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Ten","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Hunted","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"It’s one thing to read about being chased by fire-breathing demon bats or to hear stories about them from your myth-chasing uncle. It’s even one thing to have crazy psychotic visions about them, but ACTUALLY being chased by them? Not. Cool. At. All.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew I wasn’t having a vision when the man and woman strolling down Pleasant Street in the rain stopped and asked me for directions, not even when I saw their yellow eyes. I knew they were real as certainly as I knew Uncle Jack was dead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s why I dove over that stone wall into the trees, despite Lylend, the real-life Cobbogothian—let’s just take a moment to let that information soak in—instructing me to stay out in the open.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I remember, because honestly I’ve blocked most of it out, is running through the woods. They were dark and the ground was muddy from the rain, and I was slipping every other step as I tried to claw my way through the wet undergrowth. It probably wasn’t the best decision I ever made, but when it comes to fight or flight, I will always choose flight unless I’m backed into a corner. Either way, what chance of outrunning the dogrils did I really have?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My every thought became consumed with simply surviving long enough to take my next breath. My lungs and calves burned simultaneously, and I kept asking myself where the heck Lylend had disappeared to. Wasn’t he supposed to be covering me?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I heard the flapping of the dogrils’ wings above me, it became painfully clear I wasn’t going to outrun them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, I was on James’ and my secret path, and I knew there was a thick patch of trees up ahead. My plan was to find a decent hiding place and signal Lylend with the sygnocite stone he’d given me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Uncle Jack’s journal was filled with descriptions of different stones and crystals and what the Cobbogothians had used them for, so I wasn’t surprised when Lylend presented me with one before sending me off as bait for the dogrils.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once I reached the patch of trees, I dug the rock out of my pocket and did just as Lylend instructed me to, dropping it as soon as I reached the spot where I wanted him to come.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only Lylend had warned me what the stone would do next.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After taking cover in a thick bramble of wild berries, I watched in horror as the sygnocite began to glow brighter and brighter. When it started making a strange—not to mention extremely loud—whirring noise, I dropped to my stomach, covering my ears with my arms and prayed for the forest floor to swallow me up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The whirring only got louder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Panicking, I was about to leave my hiding place and make a run for it when I heard the dogrils flapping directly overhead. The stone was still whirring and glowing more and more, drawing their attention. I closed my eyes, willing it to shut up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Miraculously, it did stop. But then a strobe-like conduit of light burst up from its surface into the sky.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed a scream, wondering what on earth Lylend was thinking giving me that stupid rock!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A powerful gust of wind grazed my face then, and I hugged the ground even tighter, trying to make myself invisible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The forest floor shook, and I knew one of the dogrils had landed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The second dogril landed moments later, much closer to my hiding place. I heard a raptor-like clicking noise—what Uncle Jack used to call their “ignition spark”—and instinctively covered my head again. Seconds later, a bright ball of fire belched from the dogril’s mouth and landed on a cluster of dead bushes several feet from where I was hiding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As terrifying as the fire was, it didn’t hold a candle to the fear I felt at my first real glimpse of a living, breathing dogril. You’ve seen Uncle Jack’s diagram? Well, the real thing is SO much worse. All I could do was wait, paralyzed, as the creatures prowled the woods, getting closer and closer to my hiding place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At one point, I thought about trying to sneak away, but their fire breath kept the woods too bright. They’d see me for sure if I moved.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"However, when a fireball dropped, igniting a tree just above me, I didn’t care anymore. Burning to death was not the way I wanted to go, so I started crawling under the brush in a straight vertical dash to the top of the wooded hill. I knew it wouldn’t be long before the dogrils heard me but thought I might be able to slow them down long enough for Lylend to show up if I just stayed low.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Almost the moment I began crawling, a deafening screech ricocheted off the trees, and the ground shook as the dogrils clambered toward my hiding place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Branches scratched my cheeks and arms as I crawled, frantically dodging fireball after fireball when they shot out of the darkness. One whizzed overhead, catching fire to a bush to my right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I scrambled through mud and brush—thankful for the rain pouring down and slowing the spread of the fire. For every three steps I took, I slipped two, so I veered left, trying to get better footing by moving upward diagonally, but I soon found myself caught in a thick wall of ivy vines.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I heard my name.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I struggled against the vines. I thought I heard footsteps above me, as well. A root gave way beneath my left foot. I fell to my stomach, slipping several feet down the muddy hill. I turned just in time to see a pair of bright golden eyes waiting mere feet below me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was it! I was literally dogril meat.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I prepared myself to fight, digging through the mud for a rock or a tree branch as I slid, anything to use as a weapon. My hand grasped a gnarled branch and I gripped it with all the strenght I had left, skidding to a halt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Panting, I turned, trying to pull myself back up the hill and caught sight of the pillar of light coming from the sygnocite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I froze as a tall figure stepped out of the light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Finally!” I heard myself sob.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then the light vanished and indescribable pain seared through my left leg. I looked down just in time to see one of the dogril’s claws tearing through my calf muscle. I felt it scrape bone and then an intense burning engulfed my entire leg. I knew in an instant the burning was the dogril venom. I grasped my injured leg and jammed my other foot into the dogril’s face. It went sprawling down the hill.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend had moved away from the sygnocite and stood just a short distance away from where the dogril landed. The second dogril wasn’t far away. Lylend glanced my way, his eyes burning like blue flames beneath his hood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then everything happened so fast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just as Lylend started up the hill toward me, the dogrils reared back, spread their wings, and spewed enormous fireballs out of their mouths. Before I could warn him, they hit Lylend square in the chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I screamed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend stopped short, staggering a moment, his body on fire. The blue flames beneath his hood vanished for a moment, and his shoulders hunched forward. His body was almost completely engulfed in fire.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The dogril venom must’ve started affecting my brain at this point, because things start to get a little foggy from here on.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The orange flames engulfing Lylend’s body quickly faded to blue. Then they started gathering toward the center of his chest. He was no longer hunched over, and his eyes were open again, burning brighter than ever. Swinging his arms to one side, he lunged forward, but rather than lightning shooting out of his palms the way it had when he faced the dogrils back in Dunstan’s woods, a much larger conduit of blue fire erupted from his hands, hurtling straight for the dogrils.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The creatures heaved themselves into the sky, screeching like banshees. The hose of fire just missed them, striking the top of a tree instead, which instantly burst into flames.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I remembered was hearing the Belmont Fire alarm blaring in the distance and wondering if James would be on shift that night. Then it felt like a dark tunnel was closing in on me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I saw Lylend running toward me. I heard myself whispering “Thank you!” over and over again. Just before I lost consciousness, however, he drew up beside me, and that’s when I realized my mistake…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My rescuer was not Lylend.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"News: It's A Bird!","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"ea33f1098e2742d2a1d52642df06f0f1"},{"so":3,"title":"Sygnocite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"3350e4e486414639ae6312c5f2a77079"}]},{"n":11,"letterTitle":"The Rodaor","narrator":"Lylend","numword":"Eleven","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Rodãor","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I told you I’d never lie to you, and I don’t plan to now. If my tyro hadn’t shown up when he did, Norah Aven would’ve burned alive from the inside out after being scratched by that she-dogril. A dogril’s venom is potent and fast acting. Thankfully my tyro knows his medicinal stones as well as his battle stones and acted quickly. He slid a healing cuff on Norah’s wrist as soon as he reached her. The sipho stone embedded in the cuff began pulling the poison out of her wound immediately, while a remedecite stone kept the pain at bay. As for the blood loss on account of the gaping wound left behind in her calf, I knew I could take care of that once we were safely out of the woods.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When my tyro was satisfied the poison was out of her system, he left us to take care of other urgent matters. I woke the kyndie by slipping some vigilcite into the cuff around her wrist. A moment later those pale green eyes fluttered open, and I helped her to her feet. I knew she’d realized it was my tyro and not me that came to her aid, so I wasn’t surprised when she asked about him. I told her a “tyro” is like an apprentice. Bless the Gihara she was satisfied with that explanation, so I conjured a hoption hole and helped her limp inside. I needed to get her out of sight before the human authorities showed up to put out the orange and blue flames raging around us. The last thing I wanted was to weave my way out of all the yellow tape—literal and metaphorical—you humans put up during times like these.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few moments later, we fell out of the hoption hole onto another small, densely wooded hill along the banks of Walden Pond. I’d camped on these banks many times since meeting Jack Lukens. It wasn’t until I pulled out my rodãor and opened it in front of Norah Aven that I learned what an exasperatingly inquisitive kyndie she is. For those of you who happen to be equally inquisitive, I’ve included a page from Jack’s journal on rodãors to quench your insatiable curiosity since I’m still exhausted from answering all the kyndie’s questions. For those of you who don’t care, all you really need to know is a rodãor is the Cobbogothian version of a house. You may still want to reference Jack’s notes on the subject, as little of what I say concerning my rodãor will make sense otherwise.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once the kyndie and I climbed inside my rodãor, I set to work healing the rest of her wound. She was looking even paler than usual, if that was possible, and there wasn’t any time to lose if we wanted to save her leg. The she-dogril had sliced straight to the bone and beyond, shattering part of the tibia while she was at it. It would take much of my not insignificant healing skills to put her back to rights.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m sure many of you are wondering about my people and our curious use of stones. Well, you’ve proven yourself trustworthy thus far, so I suppose it can’t hurt to trust you a little more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It all started with the Earth Kneaders, Elementalist masters of the earth element, they were. And a right curious lot of them, too. Very secretive in their mines and caves, Earth Kneaders, always experimenting with minerals and whatnot. They discovered if they took bits and pieces of different minerals and combined them with the azura (or a drop of Cobbogothian blood), they could grow crystals that held what your primitive race might call “magical properties.” They were quite prolific with their experimentations, therefore, we Cobbogothians have a stone for just about any necessity you can think of.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For example, we have the ability to grow a vast array of healing crystals. We have crystals that can numb pain or can hold memories. There are crystals for combat and crystals for travel. We have crystals for communication and concealment and crystals—as you’ve seen with my rodãor—for shelter. We have very rare crystals that allow some of us to see the future, and even rarer crystals that enhance our innate powers as Elementalists. We grow crystals that are so fine and flexible they can be spun into cloth for clothing. There are even crystals that can alter our appearance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There are two necessities our crystals cannot provide us, one being sustenance. And while there are stones that can restart hearts and restore breath, there are no stones that can bring back the dead. Once the Gihara call us home, we are truly gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jack Lukens was fascinated with our crystals, so I told him about our communocite pillars. As a myth-chaser, he’d even discovered a few of our stones, the Skofnung Stone and Singasteinn, that were accidentally left behind when we entered our new realm. Thor’s hammer, Mjolnir, is made from some of our stone. You may also have heard of others in your own studies of Norse mythology. Jack hunted several of these mythological stones down until he found the genuine article—which of course always led him back to the Cobbogothians. In fact, I’m willing to wager that most, if not all, the magical stones talked about in human myths and legends can be traced back to my people.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was trying to distract the kyndie from the state of her leg by telling her all this. Unfortunately, it wasn’t working, especially since she’d already read about everything I was telling her in Jack’s journal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally finished healing Norah, the poor kyndie looked wrung out to the core. She was in definite need of a good long rest. It was clear that now wasn’t the time to tell her my true purpose for being in your realm, or that I’d needed her help if I was to succeed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, after assuring her that her leg was completely healed, I steeped her a strong cup of somnocite tea and sent her off into a dreamless sleep. The kyndie had seen more than enough for one night. Morning would come soon enough. Then she would finally know all.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: Rodaor","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d1a40a4136a249139c2f76b5aedf3c15"},{"so":3,"title":"Crystal Growing","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a92bb0c177f14ebbbd505de440b3d569"},{"so":4,"title":"Sipho Stone","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"1328a65d653642f7ba1a739d46c6636a"},{"so":5,"title":"Somnocite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"6a310fd6ebc24ec89f64f7b753ddc0d7"}]},{"n":12,"letterTitle":"The Lost Art of Qualdriny","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Twelve","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Lost Art of Qualdriny","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Have you ever heard of the Australian Aboriginal walkabout? Or the Native American vision quest? Both are traditional ceremonies where teenage boys journey into the wilderness to transition into manhood. Cobbogothians have something similar for both boys and girls. It’s called Yvaine-i-Gihara, which, according to Lylend, means “to walk with the gods.” I’d heard the phrase before, both in Jack’s journal and again when I translated The Twin Brothers’ Tale.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I woke up in Lylend’s rodãor the morning after being attacked by the dogrils, the first thing he asked was if I knew what Yvaine-i-Gihara was. There I was, hoping everything that had happened the night before was just one of my psychotic visions, but instead I woke up to a massive Cobbogothian warrior looming over me, asking me weird questions. Creepy, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"On the other hand, being healed by Cobbogothian healing stones—something I’d heard about from Uncle Jack all my life—was incredible. And there wasn’t even a scar left where the dogril had sliced open my calf. Watching Lylend force the blood back into my leg with a stone that looked like a giant raw diamond, then feeling my bone vibrate when he set another stone inside my wound and told me it was stitching the bone back together…well, there are no words on earth to describe it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, everything I’d experienced since Lylend pulled me from my jail cell and into that hoption hole—don’t get me started on the actual experience of going through the hoption hole—was otherworldly. It was just like the sort of stuff I saw in my visions. Only, once again, the red-haired woman wasn’t there guiding me the way she had been since my first episode last summer. I was actually starting to question what really happened last summer. In fact, I was kind of wondering about the red-haired woman, too. I mean, she sounded an awful lot like Totherma from Uncle Jack’s journal. Could there be some connection between it all?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before Lylend dug too far into explaining the details of Yvaine-i-Gihara, he served me breakfast. Part of it was just your regular run-of-the-mill ham and eggs; the other part was yet another novel experience.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The best way to describe it is it was like eating the Cobbogothian version of jam and bread. Lylend called it lyf and roogle. The roogle was like a sort of sweet bread, rolled up in a spiral. It looked like a cross between a crescent roll and a cinnamon roll, but the “roll” part spiraled up into a miniature tower. It tasted like chocolate sweet bread, but there was a spice in it that reminded me of smoky cinnamon.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The lyf, or “jam,” was made from something called lyfine berries, and while it was sweet like jam, it also had a spicy bite to it, not to mention some heat. In fact, when I looked down at the small clay pot and saw a flame flickering over the surface of the lyf, I wasn’t so sure about eating it. But Lylend assured me it wouldn’t hurt me and would just “warm me up a bit.” Since my hair was still damp from the rain the night before, I tried it. It too was delicious.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he finished eating, Lylend leaned back in his chair and waited until I finished. Up to this point, every time I’d come in contact with this strange, giant, hooded man, I’d either been blind with terror or it had been too dark outside to really get a good look at him. I took the opportunity now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the light, his eyes didn’t glow but were still a vibrant electric blue. His auburn hair was streaked with more white than I’d first realized. Like his hair, he wore his long beard in a neat but tangled twist of dreadlocks and braids with stone beads woven into them. A faint, jagged scar came up around his temple, down through his left eyebrow, and across his cheek along the side of his nose. It looked like someone had taken a thick white pen and drawn a rough question mark there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He’d removed his strange, hooded tunic, revealing a black sleeveless undershirt. I tried not to stare at his arms folded across his massive chest, but they were colossal and marked with several deep, jagged scars.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Lylend caught me staring, he shook his head and chuckled wryly, “I’ve seen many battles in my time, kyndie.” Lylend spoke with an accent. When I first heard it, I thought it was Icelandic, but after listening to him since, there was definitely a difference. His had more brogue and a musicality I’d only ever heard in Welsh.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s a good story for each of those scars,” he continued, “and I’m sure if I told them right now, they’d bore you to tears.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I had no doubt of that—not the “bore to tears” part but the “good story behind each” part. Can you imagine living for 1,000 years, let alone 10,000?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I set my fork down and finally asked Lylend the question I’d been dying to ask since I first saw him in the woods outside Dunstan.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So,” I said, clearing my throat nervously, “why exactly are you here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He answered with a question of his own, if Uncle Jack had ever told me The Twin Brother’s Tale. I told him how Samir had given it to me before he died. Lylend then proceeded to tell me something that, had I not just been attacked by dogrils the night before and had been in possession of Uncle Jack’s journal before James turned me in, I wouldn’t have believed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He told me that just as The Twin Brother’s Tale professed, his people really had passed into a new realm after Phitessnie paid his blood sum. They called their world Cobbogoth in honor of Phitessnie’s father, and they’d been living in a universe parallel to our own ever since.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Because of how quickly they had to leave our world for their new realm, however, the Cobbogothians had left the majority of their records behind. Consequently, many of his people’s ancient records and traditions—most particularly the art of something he called qualdriny—had been lost for several millennia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d already read about qualdriny and qualdrine stones in Uncle Jack’s journal but still had so many questions. Lylend patiently answered my questions, and when I asked if he had a qualdrine of his own, he unlaced the first three laces on his undershirt and pulled it back just enough so I could see a glowing, electric blue jewel set in a silver casing strapped to his chest with leather straps. I’ve drawn it for you on the next page.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As you’ll read from the page of Uncle Jack’s journal I’ve included (yes, I do get the journal back…eventually) qualdriny was a sort of alchemy practiced by the ancient Cobbogothians while they still lived in our realm. It allowed them to make the stones—qualdrines—that allowed the Cobbogothians to channel and master their elemental powers. Losing the art of qualdriny was a cultural tragedy of epic proportions for the Cobbogothians.","i":false},{"t":"img","src":"/chronicle-art/ch12-lylend-qualdrine.svg","alt":"Lylend's qualdrine"},{"t":"p","x":"The really horrific consequence of losing qualdriny, however, didn’t affect the Cobbogothians until long after they left our realm, in fact only recently. According to Lylend, after entering the Cobbogothian realm, the Cobbogothians hadn’t seen or heard of a dogril since Phitessnie paid his blood sum. Then, about 25 years ago, rumors of Cobbogothians with yellow eyes began circulating through Cobbogoth. A year later, rumors started reaching the Cobbogothians’ main cities—rumors of black mist hovering over small hamlets and villages for days and weeks before all the villagers there mysteriously vanished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"None of the Cobbogothians wanted to believe the stories, of course. Go ahead and read the Twin Brothers’ Tale again if you need to remember why. Consequently, it wasn’t until one night when a large army of yellow-eyed dogrils attacked Lylend’s own city that he knew for certain Cifer’s minions had somehow escaped their ancient prison and that Cifer most likely wouldn’t be too far behind them. Because of the lack of qualdrines—qualdrines being the only real protection the Cobbogothians had against the dogrils—Lylend’s people had no way of protecting themselves. Thousands of Cobbogothians were killed in Lylend’s city that night, and thousands more have been killed since, including Lylend’s “kinzura” or wife, Alobuenath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can only imagine the horrors my people have lived through in my absence,” Lylend said, his broad shoulders slumping a little as he said it. “If I didn’t know for myself there are still Cobbogothians resisting, still surviving, I wouldn’t believe it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a long pause as Lylend cleared away our breakfast dishes and set them in a stone sink full of water. I watched as each dish was somehow cleaned without Lylend even touching the water until it was time to retrieve each dish for drying…which of course he had a stone slab for.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Lylend returned to the table, I asked how the dogrils could’ve escaped from their prison, especially if they were buried in the Crystal City like the Twin Brothers’ Tale claimed they were. He simply shrugged and said someone had to have released them from their prison. He went to a nearby cupboard and retrieved a leather bag. He opened it and showed me several crystal shards that had once made up a strange casket like this:","i":false},{"t":"img","src":"/chronicle-art/ch12-crystal-crypt.svg","alt":"The crystal crypt"},{"t":"p","x":"“Jack found them lying on the altar in our temple,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If you were able to work out the Gihara’s warning at the end of the Twin Brothers’ Tale, kyndie, then you’ll know the only thing that could’ve released them from their qualdrine prison was another qualdrine, and that altar was nothing but.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Only the power that binds them is strong enough to unbind them,” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I asked Lylend if he’d come back to Earth’s realm to find whoever broke the casket, but he shook his head. “What’s done is done. I’m here because Totherma sent me back to find an ancient and vital record our people left behind.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Does this record have a name?” I asked. “Maybe Uncle Jack wrote about it in his journal.” Maybe if I introduced James to a real-life Cobbogothian, I could convince him to give the journal back to me. If that didn’t work, I could step aside and let Lylend convince him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The record is called the Haven,” Lylend said, shaking his head. “And I’m afraid your uncle and I searched the city for years without any luck…well, not until recently, that is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded for Lylend to go on. “A few months ago, your uncle discovered an obscure text in one of the record rooms of our temple. That’s when I realized I could pull that city apart stone by stone and never find the Haven.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I asked why not, he simply shrugged and said, “Because I am a man.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend could see his explanation hadn’t clarified anything for me, so he continued.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Haven contains the ancient secrets of qualdriny. What the ancient record your uncle found revealed, however, is that the making of qualdrines was only ever entrusted to a few very select women chosen by the Gihara, not unlike myself as a Naridi. Qualdriny is a very sacred art that was guarded fiercely by those women. Consequently, they wouldn’t have revealed its whereabouts to anyone unable to carry on the practice after them. That is where you come into the picture, kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Me? How so?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, the only one who stands a chance at finding the Haven today must be a kyndie, mustn’t she? Not only that, she must be able to read ancient Cobbogothian, as any clues to the Haven’s whereabouts will undoubtably be written in our language. I believe you’re the only one in this entire realm that qualifies on both accounts. Am I right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, yeah,” I said, slowly. “But I’m not a Cobbogothian woman.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend shrugged. “Your uncle didn’t seem to think it mattered. All the record said is you have to be female.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So,” Lylend said, leaning onto the table and pinning me with his electric blue eyes, “will you Norah Aven, a human kyndie, help me save the Cobbogothians?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Honestly, what did he expect me to say to that? I was human, not heartless.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Lyf & Roogle Recipe","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"90f4bdef685846ea80221265ca5cad94"},{"so":3,"title":"Journal: Yvaine-i-Gihara & Qualdrine Crystals","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"ac2b374d135a48e58734060319ee0cc9"},{"so":4,"title":"Secret Message #4","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"15242aab90264c708c4ee4cd09a842aa"},{"so":5,"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Come walk with Gihara, new child of power. Come walk with Gihara and prove yourself true. For you are our child, our precious creation. Come walk with us child, for we will e'er walk with you.*","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":13,"letterTitle":"The Naridi","narrator":"Lylend","numword":"Thirteen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Secrets of the Opalian Eye","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Things certainly could’ve gone worse when I told the Kyndie I needed her help to find the Haven. She only looked slightly paler than usual. I’ll admit, I was skeptical when Jack suggested Norah Aven might be the missing piece to my 19-year-old puzzle, especially considering she wasn’t even born when I first started searching the Crystal City.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was only a few weeks ago when Jack finally convinced me she could help and warned me the Dogrils might already know the Kyndie was the only one who could help us. That’s why I went looking for her at Dunstan in the first place. Jack asked me to check in on her for him. I could Hoption straight to her, you see, but he had to take the long, human way back to Boston. I’d have Hoptioned him along with me, but Hoption holes didn’t agree with Jack for some reason. He’d nearly died both times I tried to take him through one.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully I didn’t wait to keep my word to Jack about checking in on the Kyndie. If I had, the Dogrils would not only have destroyed Norah Aven at Dunstan but also, with her, my people’s final hope of defeating the vile creatures.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I don’t often give praise for you humans—as you well know by now—but thank the Gihara for Jack Lukens and his marvelous Kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I met Jack about four years after I began searching the Crystal City—not too long after he initially “discovered” it. I’d run out of food supplies again and needed to venture out into the human world to restock. I never wanted to run into any humans while in this realm. I was hoping I’d be able to find the Haven in a few weeks—months maybe—and then get back to Cobbogoth before any more harm came to my people. That is clearly not how things played out, now 20 years later.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, on the day we met, Jack was heading into the caves just as I stepped out of a Hoption hole near his grandfather’s farm. The first thing I noticed was that he wasn’t at all surprised to see me there, nor was he shocked at the peculiar way I arrived. When I tried to stop him from heading into the caves, he told me it was all right because he knew Totherma, and she’d told him about me too—that he’d come to help me find what I was looking for. Why Totherma hadn’t bothered mentioning him to me—or the fact that the Haven could only be found by a woman—is beyond conception, but there it is. When I later managed to contact her through one of the Communocite pillars, she only mentioned Jack to acknowledge the fact that they did indeed know one another.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could’ve been offended by that—and the fact that she never once mentioned I needed a woman in order to find the Haven or the fact that, unbeknownst to me, my Tyro had followed me through that Dark Matter Door into this realm only a few days after I had come through—but that was just Totherma’s way. I’d known her since I was a boy—yes, she’s even older than I am—and there was always a method to her madness. Ten millennia working with her to protect our people meant having to accept that fact.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, I accepted Jack’s help and even grew to enjoy his company. Jack and I searched the Crystal City together for 15 years before he discovered our first real clue concerning the Haven. It happened the week before his death. That was the first he or I knew anything about needing a female to help us find it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully I hadn’t given up on my belief in Totherma. After everything she’d gone through to get me here, I knew she’d do everything in her power to make sure it wasn’t all in vain. You see, Totherma made a great sacrifice in order to send me here. In fact, she opened a Dark Matter Door to send me through. The gravity of such an act, especially with Dogrils roaming Cobbogoth at the time, is beyond anything your human mind can comprehend. After watching what she had to do, I believed she wouldn’t be able to afford to send anyone else from Cobbogoth through to help me if she hoped to have enough power left to get me back once I had the Haven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet she did send someone else—a woman even—along with my Tyro. I didn’t discover this fact for many years. And even when I did, I still had no idea a woman was needed to find the Haven. As I’ve already mentioned, Jack did finally discover that clue, but by then it was tragically too late.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the Spark! My Tyro has just informed me that you won’t understand the term “Dark Matter Door.” I could waste precious time describing it to you, but spare me that infernal frustration and see the page from Jack’s journal I’ve included for an explanation. He was always better at illuminating things to your kind. I’ve rarely had the patience for it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, why didn’t Totherma simply tell the woman she sent through another Dark Matter Door with my Tyro about the Haven? Your guess is as good as mine, human. At the time, I comforted myself by continuing to assume there was a method to the Opalian Eye’s madness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully I was right. Aside from the help Totherma gave me in my search by describing the mark I was meant to look for, she had also been preparing another way for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You see, after I got the Kyndie to agree to help us, she began asking a lot of questions about Totherma. After I’d answered a few of them, she grew quiet for a long while. Then she asked if Jack ever told me why she was at Dunstan and what the past year had been like for her. I told her I didn’t know, and it was the truth. That’s when the Kyndie told me the whole story. When she began telling me about her “visions,” it soon became clear that Totherma was behind it all. From what the Kyndie revealed to me in my Rodãor after breakfast, it sounded like Totherma had spent the better part of the past year slowly revealing our world to the Kyndie. So when Jack and I did find the clue to discovering the Haven, everything Norah has faced since Jack’s death, including meeting myself, wouldn’t come as such a brutal shock to her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, like I said before, one never knows all the reasons behind Totherma’s actions. As the Opalian Eye, she answers directly to the Gihara. In my experience, there have been countless instances over the past millennia when the Gihara have entrusted her with grave secrets, forbidding her to share the burden with anyone. In the end, the secrets were only revealed when the Gihara willed it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As a reward for the Kyndie’s help, I treated her to one of my spectacular lightning shows—think fireworks but with electricity instead of fire—and then answered all her questions concerning the Cobbogothian power system, how each manifests a particular element, how their powers work, etc. I even gave her a mock Manifestations exam.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As a human who has thus far proven trustworthy, I suppose I can share these Cobbogothian secrets with you as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Carry on.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"so":2,"title":"Manifestations Test","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"70b38beedb7b4db7bd2136e7b20ab15e"},{"so":3,"title":"Journal: Einstein-Rosen Bridge","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d11e4031631d4f1c967f066c4194931e"},{"so":4,"title":"Journal: Elementalist Manifestations","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"886ad0de747749628b5938cff7155f98"}]},{"n":14,"letterTitle":"Fridrika Bjornsardottir","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Fourteen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Fridrika Bjornsardottir","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"First of all, I’m going to point out the very fascinating fact that Lylend has an iPhone. I didn’t see that coming. I mean, I would’ve thought he’d have some kind of stone for that, wouldn’t you?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Regardless, if Lylend hadn’t shown me the included text message from Uncle Jack, I don’t think I would’ve had the courage to return to my house on Barbara Road after we spoke over breakfast. Uncle Jack was right. I knew exactly where he would’ve hidden the “crystal scroll.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The night after Lylend healed my leg in his rodãor, we hoptioned to the hill just behind my house. It was late, and everything was dark. There was a light on in James’ house across the street, though. Between that and having a full view of my charred cherry tree and platform scattered across the back patio, I couldn’t help wondering if James knew I’d escaped from jail and, if so, was he bothering to look for me at all?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I also wondered what he’d done with Uncle Jack’s journal. Thankfully I’d already read it thoroughly when James slipped it out of my pocket before Narson arrested me. That’s all it took to recall every page like a photograph in my mind. But the journal was still the last thing Uncle Jack had given me, so I’d have to see about getting it back after I helped Lylend. Hopefully once he had the Haven, he and the dogrils would go back to Cobbogoth, and I could try to scrape together some sort of a normal life. Whether or not that life would include James Riley depended entirely on James’ explanation for why he’d turned me in.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The really good news that came from meeting Lylend was it turns out I wasn’t crazy after all. When the topic of my visions came up over breakfast and I described some of the things I’d seen to Lylend—which, of course, led to me to telling him about the red-haired lady—he told me the woman sounded just like Totherma. Then he told me that, as the Opalian Eye, she had telepathic capabilities. She had probably been causing me to have the visions so I would be prepared to help find the Haven when the time came.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ever since I’d returned home to Boston, I’d been thinking the reason all this sounded so familiar was because of my childhood being inundated with stories about the ancient Cobbogothians by Uncle Jack. But it all made sense now. Everything felt familiar because of the stories Uncle Jack had told me, yes, but mostly it felt familiar because I’d actually seen so much of it in my visions. After hearing more about the Crystal City and the Cobbogothian realm, I couldn’t help wishing Totherma had shown me more of those things instead of all the terrifying things my visions always seemed to be filled with. When I mentioned this to Lylend, he grew quiet, and then he told me that the visions I had described were most likely Totherma showing me the horror happening to his people back in Cobbogoth right now. If that was the case, his people were living one perpetual nightmare after another, which made me want to help him even more. I wouldn’t have wished the things I’d seen over the past year on my worst enemy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I headed inside my house to search Uncle Jack’s library, Lylend warned me that the dogrils might make an appearance. They’d caught the scent of my blood when the she-dogril tore into my calf. Because of that, it apparently wouldn’t be very hard for them to find me from now on. This wasn’t what I wanted to hear just then, but Lylend assured me he’d be circling the house while I searched. If he saw anything amiss, he assured me he’d hoption inside and get me out of there ASAP. It was more than I could’ve hoped for if Lylend was just a regular human, so I sucked it up and headed inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was weird being back inside my house after all that had happened. All the crime scene tape was still up. The house was dark and empty and creepy. However, I didn’t waste time spooking myself by peering into the shadows or focusing on anything other than heading straight through the kitchen into Uncle Jack’s library.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was still a disaster zone in there. Anything that had been on the bookshelves was on the floor now. When Narson led me in there the night Uncle Jack’s body was found, he told me he was pretty sure Uncle Jack had made the mess himself. I’d wondered what in the world Uncle Jack had been looking for. Tonight, however, after seeing Lylend’s text message from Uncle Jack, I knew exactly what he’d been doing. He hadn’t created the mess because he was looking, he’d created it to hide something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Due to his paranoid nature and multiple threats from jealous archeologists claiming he was nothing better than a “treasure hunter”—which is apparently a major insult in the archeological world—Uncle Jack had a safe built behind the paneling of the wall behind his desk when I was about 10 years old. The only way to open the paneling to get to the safe was to line up a set of ancient Etruscan vases on a specially made bookshelf. When the vases were lined up just right, their weight would trigger a lever hidden in the molding behind his desk, unlatching it. Then the panel would pop open, revealing the safe. It was all very Indiana Jonesy, but, like I said, Uncle Jack was paranoid and a treasure hunter—*gasp!*—so there you go.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I examined the room more closely, I could see that Uncle Jack had, in fact, turned the room upside down on purpose. He had been trying to avoid the possibility that anyone besides myself would discover his hidden door and safe. He’d even gone so far as to remove the Etruscan vases from their shelf. It didn’t take long before I located them in the rubble on the floor. I lined them up in the right order on his special shelf and then heard the familiar click of the hidden panel lever. Down on my hands and knees, I pulled open the panel and began twisting the combination dial of the safe inside. Thankfully I already knew the combination, my birthday: 6-20-92.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when it dawned on me. With everything that had happened since leaving Dunstan, I’d completely forgotten my birthday was only a day away. With Uncle Jack gone and James having just betrayed me to the police, I didn’t have high hopes for my 18th birthday. Even if none of that had happened, it was always Gram Riley who made birthdays special anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally had the safe open, I found three things inside. The first was a peculiar looking cylinder carved from stone with a handle on it—the crystal scroll, I presumed. There was also a photograph and some folded up papers, one of which was yellowing with age. The photograph was an older one as well—probably taken back in the ’60s or ’70s. It was of a woman—obviously Icelandic—standing on what looked like Reynisfjar Beach on the southern tip of Iceland. I knew it because the sand on that beach is black. I felt my stomach lurch and nearly dropped the crystal scroll when I realized I recognized the woman. It was none other than the woman I’d met on Pleasant Street last night—before she turned into the she-dogril. I was about to tear the folded papers to read and figure out why on earth Uncle Jack would have a picture of her in his safe, but before I could, I saw something out of the corner of my eye and froze. The room was growing strangely darker around me. I hurried and tucked the pages under my shirt and shoved the crystal scroll deep in my jeans pocket.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was about to head for the nearest window—to yell for Lylend—but then I saw that strange shimmer in the air, the same shimmer I’d seen the night before when the woman in Uncle Jack’s photograph morphed into the she-dogril.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I held my breath, bracing myself as a raptor-like clicking rang through the empty house.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Fridrika's Testimony","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4a765d9716b9452993a82c5f06e5f257"},{"so":3,"title":"Photo of Fridrika Bjornsardottir","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"84d6df751a9441f1b81c98889d7b6196"},{"so":4,"title":"Text Message #2","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"11a6dd81a972419ea8eabc26ab5100a3"},{"so":5,"title":"Secret Message #5","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"32a5d3503a4542c4a5276a8f737c68f7"},{"so":6,"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If you find this, contact Max Anderton. This is extremely important, Norah Aven.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Uncle Jack","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":15,"letterTitle":"The Letter","narrator":"James","numword":"Fifteen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Tyro","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Fighting fires is hard work, especially the kind of fires I’ve been fighting lately. First there was the fire at Samir’s shop. Then later that night there was that wicked fire in the woods down the street from my house. If I hadn’t already been in those woods searching for Norah, things could’ve been much worse.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As hard as it is for me to admit, I have Detective Narson to thank for being in the woods in the first place. He phoned me from the police station after Norah escaped, accusing me of helping her, which was why I was in the right place at the right time, otherwise I would’ve been too late and a lot of people could’ve lost their homes…or worse.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the chief saw my state after we finally got that second fire out, he ordered me to go home and get some rest. I did, and it was the first solid eight hours of sleep I’d had in over a week. Worry had been keeping me up—worry for Norah, worry for her safety, worry that she might hate me once she discovered the truth about me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was dark outside when I finally woke up. After showering, I was in my kitchen making a sandwich when I happened to glance out my window. At first I thought it was just the sunset reflecting off Norah’s house across the street, but when I leaned in closer, I realized there was another fire. Norah’s house was on fire!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I dropped everything and reached for my phone to call it in but then froze. I wasn’t so sure this was going to be the kind of situation we’d want the authorities getting involved in. Too many questions. The last thing I wanted was for them to have another reason to search Dr. Lukens’ place, especially if things didn’t work out the way I hoped they would. I ran out the back door and decided to try and put the fire out myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I reached Norah’s yard, every window on the northeast side of the house was filled with flames. I circled around back and found the patio doors locked. Yanking a branch off the blackened cherry tree lying on her patio, I was about to break the glass when I felt a hand on my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned and felt relief wash over me. “It’s you,” I said. It was my visitor from the other night. “She’s in there,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched him conjure a Hoption hole, then dropped my branch and followed him, ready to take on any dingbat standing between my girl and me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"(Surprise! My visitor was Lylend. Man! That feels wicked good to finally get that off my chest. I did warn you there was a whole lot more to my story than I could tell you before.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The smoke was thick when the Hoption hole spit us out inside the house. He handed me something cool. “Koolite,” he explained. You’ll need it to get her out of here safely. I placed it in my pocket and then dropped down to my knees. Being what I am has its perks, one of them being that fire and smoke don’t affect me like they affect other people. In fact, they don’t affect me in the least, not negatively anyway. You could say it was what enabled me to become the youngest fireman Belmont had ever hired.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Norah didn’t have my perks. By now she was probably down on the ground trying to find any oxygen left to breathe in all this smoke. At least I hoped she was still trying to breathe.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I didn’t see any sign of her, I called out. I could hear my visitor in another part of the house, drawing the dingbat away from the main room, which would hopefully give me a chance to get to Norah and get her out of there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I called her name again, and this time I heard a muffled response. It was coming from the piano in the corner of the room. I crawled toward it. As I did, I saw that the whole floor near that side of the room had been torn up by what could only be dogril claws. What if she was hurt again?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally reached the piano, there were claw marks across its shiny black surface, and the lid to the bench had been torn off. Burning sheets of music were strewn everywhere. The piano had one leg torn off and was tilted at a tenuous angle. I carefully removed the bench out of the way and, bracing the instrument with my shoulder, reached a hand underneath, shouting for Norah to grab hold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Her hand immediately grasped mine. I pulled her out. She was clutching something to her chest and coughing pretty hard. I examined her as best as I could through the smoke, praying she didn’t have a poison-filled scratch somewhere I couldn’t see.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I was satisfied she was okay, I pulled her into my arms and snapped the Koolite stone into the sliver cuff on her wrist. The moment it was in place, the smoke and flames all around us receded, and it was like we were standing in a bubble of fresh, cool air.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get the Kyndie out of here.” Lylend shouted, then tossed me something and threw a lightning bolt in the direction of the dingbat. “I’ll meet you at Walden,” he yelled as I watched him disappear into the next room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Norah stopped coughing long enough to look up at me. Man, I wanted to kiss that look of shock right off her face. Why? Because I had made it to this moment—this moment I’d wanted to happen for an entire year—and I didn’t have to lie to her once to get here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James?” she questioned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rather than answer all the questions I could see in her eyes, I conjured another Hoption hole and pulled her inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment we tumbled out of it onto a wooded hill overlooking Walden Pond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait…how do you…you know Lylend?” Nor asked between coughing fits.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sat up beside her, gently patting her back as she struggled to catch her breath. “Yeah, you could say that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait,” Norah froze, choking back a cough and staring at me. “Don’t tell me he was your visitor the other night.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, and…” I said, willing her to guess the whole truth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched her eyes slowly grow wider and knew the truth was finally fitting together in that wicked smart brain of hers. “No,” she said, shaking her head. “There’s no way. You can’t possibly be his Tyro!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s worse than that even,” I said. “The old man’s actually my dad.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What the? But that means you’re—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A Cobbogothian? Yeah.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Without another word, I dug into my front pocket and pulled out a sealed letter I’d been carrying around since the morning Gram died.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go ahead. Read it,” I said. “I don’t know what it says. She just told me it would explain a lot.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"so":2,"title":"Gram's Letter","kind":"webp","pages":4,"id":"ee03eca541954105af154f980fe00029"},{"so":3,"title":"Journal: Kinzura Bond","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"321363836e094db7abc34259e5fe905f"},{"so":4,"title":"Koolite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"dfa5ea134b9f48a78aa2735bd780c7fd"}]},{"n":16,"letterTitle":"The Ambush","narrator":"Lylend","numword":"Sixteen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Dappled Brown","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I’ve said before that fighting one dogril is a feat for any Cobbogothian, but fighting two is quite a harrowing event, no matter how much experience one has. My Tyro—which I assume you now know is Jamus—can take on two dogrils at a time, three if he’s well rested. He’s a Flame Thrower, you see. He absorbs fire, which makes the dogril’s fiercest weapon entirely obsolete against him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What’s that? Konklegrop! I informed you from the beginning I wouldn’t be telling you more than I felt you needed to know. You didn’t need to know until now that Jamus was my son or that he was a Cobbogothian. That, and that alone, is why I didn’t tell you before now, nosy human.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I am much older and wiser than my Tyro, but I am not a Flame Thrower. He takes after his mother. I’m a Spark Spindler, and while I can do a fair amount of damage to the dogrils myself, I am not fireproof. I have to keep my distance in a fight. I have to keep moving. I can’t make mistakes. I have to hit them with my lightning when they least expect it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is precisely what I did while fighting that she-dogril so my Tyro could rescue the Kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My first goal was to keep the she-dogril distracted long enough for Jamus and Norah Aven to escape. My second was to destroy her if I could. One less dogril in any realm is always a good thing. In the end, however, she morphed into mist and was gone before I could do any real damage to her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jamus made me promise to alert the Belmont Fire Department about the fire once he and the Kyndie were safely away. But before I brought any fragile humans on the scene, I wanted to make sure they weren’t in danger of becoming the next dogril victims. One more dogril in any realm is always a bad thing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly made my way around the house, looking into the rooms that weren’t on fire. It was dark when I stepped outside to do the same around the perimeter of the house, so I didn’t think I was in much danger of being seen by any of the neighbors.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After using Jack’s landline to place the call to the fire department, I stepped back out into the night, preparing to Hoption to Walden. Then I paused, remembering that the Kyndie had only just learned about Jamus. She must’ve been surprised out of her mind to discover that I wasn’t her rescuer. But I’d be lying if I said I wasn’t glad she finally knew. It had been harder than I thought it would be to keep the identity of my Tyro to myself, especially since I knew what the two of them meant to each other. I can only imagine what it must have been like for Jamus the past two years, after Gram finally told him what he was. It had all been part of Gramillia’s plan to keep Jamus safe and hidden from any dogrils lurking in this realm. Jamus didn’t find out who or what he was until I came to begin his training for Yvaine-i-Gihara just after his 18th birthday. His mother made me promise he be brought up in the old ways, and I intended to keep that promise. Her dying wish was for Jamus to inherit her Qualdrine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I first saw him all grown up, there was no mistaking the Kynd. Like I said, he takes after his mother. That’s when Gramillia told me she suspected Jamus and Norah might have a Kinzura bond. I doubted her then, especially since the Kyndie is human, but after seeing the two of them together the last few days—the lengths Jamus was willing to go to protect her—I believe Gram may have been right after all. I’d know if the bond was certain once I saw what lengths the Kyndie would go to for my Jamus.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t envy my Kynd the explaining he would have to do to make this right with Norah Aven. The least I could do was wait here a bit longer and give him time to do his explaining in private. I decided to give him until I heard the fire truck sirens pull up Barbara Road. Then I’d Hoption to Walden and join them. Anyway, Kinzura bond or no, there was little time to spare now that the Kyndie had the crystal scroll in her possession.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I settled myself down on that old, blackened tree on Jack’s patio and gave my ancient bones a rest. The fire blazing before me turned my thoughts to my own Kinzura. How I missed my Alo. Alobuenath was her full name, and I’m only telling you that because I’m feeling particularly nostalgic as I write this, human. That’s what happens when you stare death in the face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Alo had been gone nearly 19 years. Losing your Kinzura leaves an ache that never heals. We spent 500 years together before she became pregnant with Jamus. The dogrils attacked our city the night he was born. Had she not been weakened from childbirth, which is a much more dangerous and strenuous process for Cobbogothian women, she’d still be alive. Alo was an extraordinary Flame Thrower herself, you see, but for her the reward of having her own child always outweighed any risk she took to give him life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He’s a fine boy, Alo,” I was soon saying to the smokey sky. I often speak to her when I am alone. When you share a Kinzura with someone, they are always with you, human, whether they have gone home to the Gihara or not. It is a blessing, but it can also be a curse at times. The grief of losing Alo nearly consumed me for sixteen years. I became obsessed with finding the Haven simply so I could complete my task and finally see her again. It wasn’t until Gramillia contacted me through Jack and told me my son was in this realm that I began to live again. When I saw him as a grown man for the first time, I realized my Alo lived on through Jamus and that I couldn’t fail in keeping my promises to pass on her Qualdrine and the old ways to him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was lost in these thoughts and others when I heard the sirens begin to blare from the station.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Returning to the present, I suddenly realized I wasn’t alone. I heard the she-dogril before I saw her. When I turned toward the snapping of twigs and leaves in the woods to my right, however, I could see she’d brought company this time. Her sidekick from the night before stepped out of the woods beside her. I stood, preparing to fight again. I’d fought two dogrils plenty of times before, and while I wasn’t fireproof like Jamus, the arrival of the second didn’t worry me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then I heard a clicking noise from behind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned slowly, just in time to see an already transformed dappled brown dogril move around the side of the burning house.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Three dogrils at once would certainly be more of a challenge. But where had this one come from? I’d killed all but the she-dogril and her friend back at Dunstan. I knew this one must’ve been recently possessed. This posed a new set of problems, as we had yet to discover who the host body had belonged to. As long as this new dogril’s identity stayed anonymous, we were all in more danger than I was comfortable with.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The she-dogril and her sidekick transformed before my eyes, and they all began to circle. I’m not one to run from a fight, human. I wasn’t made for that. But this wasn’t meant to be a fight, and by the Gihara, can you imagine the horrors a dogril could summon if they ever got in possession of a Naridi body? It was unthinkable. There was really only one strategy to take.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I conjured a Hoption hole and dove inside but not before I felt the flames of the dogrils catch hold of me as every particle of me filtered through space and time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the hole spit me out on the banks of Walden Pond, every cell of my not insignificant form was on fire. The pain was excruciating. If there was ever a time the Gihara would call me home to see my Alo, I prayed this was it.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"so":2,"title":"News: The Lethal Trio","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"8f81b1617c6f491eb75ea4cce4e888a4"}]},{"n":17,"letterTitle":"The Trial","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Seventeen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"A Second First Kiss","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"After reading Gram’s letter, James and I went silently into Lylend’s rodãor. We stood facing each other for a long time while I focused on taking deep breaths, processing everything I’d just learned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know what you’re thinking. After ten years of living next door to each other and spending so much time together, how could I not have suspected James Riley was a Cobbogothian? Well, people don’t usually go around suspecting the boy they love is a member of a mythical race from an interdimensional realm, no matter how much evidence their myth-chasing uncle digs up to prove said race wasn’t mythical at all. So let’s just get that out of the way, shall we?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Secondly, I’m with you. I have no idea how I didn’t see it. I mean, not only am I pretty smart, but I’d known James for ten years, had been best friends with him for TEN years! I’d been raised by Uncle Jack, “world famous myth-chaser,” who was obsessed with the Cobbogothians—who knew Gram and James were Cobbogothians. How had the thought never even crossed my mind? All I can say is that up until meeting Lylend, despite all of Uncle Jack’s research and discovery, I never dreamed the Cobbogothians were still out there somewhere.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet here we were.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And what about this whole kinzura thing? I didn’t want to be the one to mess up James’ life’s mission. Yet I couldn’t ignore the fact that not messing it up meant I’d be living without him—not just not being in a relationship with him but being in a completely different realm without him—for the rest of my life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of all the horrible things I’d experienced during my time at Dunstan—all the horror movie visions, the idea that I was going crazy, the feelings of complete and utter abandonment—none of it was as bad as thinking I’d never see James again. I know it sounds dramatic and intense, but the way I feel about James, the way I’ve always felt about him, has been dramatic and intense from the start. Now I knew why. From the first moment I spied him moving in when I was ten years old, I felt the pull. Whether it was because of this kinzura cosmic bond or not, that’s just the way it had always been. Nine months in Dunstan, trying to convince myself it didn’t matter if he didn’t want me or that I could go on and live a normal, happy, fulfilled life without him, hadn’t changed that fact.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you okay?” James asked, after a very long time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know,” I said truthfully.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry I didn’t tell you, Nor. I’ve wanted to, but—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let me guess,” I sighed. “Uncle Jack made you promise not to?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Why, over the past three days, whenever I discovered some monumental secret from this no-longer-mythical world of his, did I also discover the reason I hadn’t found out about it sooner was because “Uncle Jack forbade it?” What right did he have to decide that for me?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now that I knew about the Haven, a lot of the secrecy made sense, but why keep James’ secret from me? This one felt different. It felt less like he was trying to protect me and more like he was trying to control me. Even Gram said in her letter that she didn’t agree with his choice to keep it from me. I mean, I could’ve protected myself a little more if I’d known sooner. I could’ve been honest with myself about the reality of the situation and the odds of James and me ever ending up together.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now it was too late. The incredible ache splintering through my chest, pressing down and nearly choking the air from my lungs, told me I’d never been more in love with James or Jamus—whatever his name was—than I was right now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When James reached up and touched my cheek, I realized I was crying. I swiped the tears away, feeling my face burn with embarrassment. I hated looking pathetic in front of him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to smile—to reassure him. The poor guy looked like someone had just punched him in the gut. If we shared a kinzura and he could really feel my strongest emotions, then he looked that way because he literally felt exactly how I felt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry,” I told him, taking a deep breath. “This has just been a lot to take in.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, I’m sorry,” James said. He moved toward me then, and I felt myself stiffen. I knew from Uncle Jack’s journal that a kinzura bond could only be sealed in a sacred ceremony—one where both parties made a blood pact. I didn’t have Cobbogothian blood, so I didn’t even know if that would seal the bond. But was letting him touch me going to make saying goodbye in the end any easier?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could decide, James pulled me in for a big hug—our first real, genuine hug since last summer. So much of the anguish I’d been feeling immediately seeped out of my body. Boy, I’d missed his hugs. He’d always been so warm. Now I knew it was because he literally had fire running through his veins.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s better,” James said with a sigh.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I instinctively squeezed him tighter around the waist, unable to shake the thought that this might be one of the last times he ever held me like this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then I cracked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I just thought we had so much more time, James,” I said, a sob escaping before I could stop it. “You are officially the only person I have left in this world, and pretty soon you’re going to leave and go back to your own.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hey, hey, hey,” James said, gently holding me away from him so he could look me in the eyes. “Now listen here, Nor. Do you honestly think I would set foot in that realm without doing everything in my power to get you to come with me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come with you?” I sniffed, wiping my eyes. “Is that even possible?” Gram hadn’t said anything about that in her letter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course it is,” he laughed. “I asked my dad about it and everything. I was just waiting to ask you to come until I was sure you’d forgiven me for, well, everything, and I was sure you’d actually say yes.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?” I asked, my heart suddenly expanding with hope. “You didn’t keep all this from me because you didn’t want me to know?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Are you kidding, Nor? You’re my best friend. It’s been everything I could do to keep myself from telling you sooner! I hated keeping you in the dark, but your uncle was right. Telling you would’ve only put you in unnecessary danger.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So, what would you call everything that’s happened over the past three days?” I asked, suddenly angry at Uncle Jack again. “Necessary danger?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James groaned, resting his chin on my head. “It could’ve been avoidable danger if you’d just cooperated.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? I cooperated,” I said, pulling back and looking at him again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, Nor,” he chuckled, his eyes crinkling at the sides. “You hid in a bathroom for over an hour to avoid riding home with me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, that’s true,” I said, leaning back into him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And you ran off to Samir’s without a word.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hid my face in his shoulder. “How was I supposed to know it was Lylend you were talking to outside my window that night?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You could’ve asked,” James said, simply.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And if I had?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I would’ve told you we were trying to get the dogrils to stop hunting you. That we wanted to get the journal away from you to see if that’s what they were after.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hmmm…and the whole getting me arrested thing?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James shrugged, as though I should’ve already guessed the answer. “How else was I supposed to get you to stay put long enough for my dad to properly introduce himself. Time’s running out to find the Haven, Nor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Fair enough,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So…you forgive me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked up at James again, this time looked him full in the face, and said, “Yes, and even more, I’ll say thank you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For what?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For keeping me safe and wanting me to come with you to Cobbogoth.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah, that’s just my pleasure,” James said, pulling me close again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We stood there in silence for a while, and I forced all worrying thoughts about the future as far from me as I could. I just wanted to be still and be held by James for a bit.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Too soon, James spoke again. “Listen Nor, I know it seems like the whole world has turned on its head right now, but I just want you to know that one thing hasn’t changed. In fact, it’s become even more definite since the last time I said it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The fact that I think you’re wicked awesome, and I’m in love with you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sighed. “I love you, too.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We were looking at each other again, and a warmth I’d never seen before pooled in James’ eyes. It looked just as warm as the feeling blooming in my chest. Was this because of our kinzura? Was I feeling whatever was causing that look in his eyes, or was the feeling only mine? When my stomach started doing somersaults, I figured it was probably a little of both.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I need to apologize for one more thing,” he said quietly, brushing a hair from my temple. His head was bending ever so slightly toward mine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. “For what?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For hurting you last summer—you know, the night we kissed.” His eyes grew serious. “I would’ve never kissed you if I knew it could hurt you like that. I asked my dad about it, and he said it was probably because I was still learning to control my flame throwing—that all the emotion I was feeling must’ve overridden my ‘elemental regulators’—his words, not mine—and caused a bit of fire to transfer into you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh! I mean, that’s okay,” I said, waving his apology away as though I hadn’t spent the last nine months at Dunstan Academy. Truth be told, even though my knees were getting a little shaky from just thinking about the pain I’d been in, I still hoped James was going to try what I thought he was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His face was inches from my own now. “I promise it’ll be better this time,” he assured me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “And if it isn’t, and I pass out or catch fire…or whatever…you can just revive me with one of your crystals, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James smiled. “That’s my girl.” A spark of blue fire jumped in his eyes. Something in my chest jumped in response.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was happening. James Riley—Jamus the Cobbogothain—was going to kiss me again, and I didn’t care a bit about the consequences.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he finally pressed his lips to mine, it was gentle. And so warm! The warmth carefully spread from my lips to my stomach and through the rest of my body. Unlike last summer, James was in control. I could feel it, and he stayed in control during our entire kiss.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just like he promised, kissing him the second time was so much better than the first. In fact, it was even more perfect than the “first kiss” I’d constantly dreamed about since spying on him from my cherry tree ten years ago.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: Kinzura Blood Binding","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"bb469fdba4744317bea56783df31f5fb"}]},{"n":18,"letterTitle":"The Crystal Scroll","narrator":"Jamus","numword":"Eighteen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"The Crystal Scroll (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Man! Kissing Norah was the best feeling I’d ever felt—even better than when I met my dad for the first time and found out I could shoot fire out of my hands. Now that she knew everything, the feeling between us, the “home” feeling that had been missing since last summer, was back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I would’ve been happy kissing her forever, and hopefully I would be kissing her forever since she’d just agreed to leave the only home either of us knew and go back to Cobbogoth with my dad and me once she helped us find the Haven. She agreed without even knowing what we might find when we got there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor and I had been kissing for a while—you know, making up for nine months of lost time—when I heard something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I forced myself to pull back from Nor and listen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no! Did I do it wrong?” Nor asked. I looked down at her, confused. Her eyes were soft and her lips extra red from kissing a whiskered fella, and she had the prettiest blush creeping over her pale cheeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nah, Nor,” I assured her, pulling her close again. “You did wicked good. Believe me! I just thought I heard something outside.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We both looked up at the ceiling. I’d left the rodãor door open so dad could get in when he finished with the she-dogril. It only just occurred to me that he’d been gone way longer than he should’ve been.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’d better go out and look for my dad,” I told Nor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll come with you,” she said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Actually, will you wait here in case he comes back?” Really, I just wanted her to stay safe, but I’d learned a long time ago that you didn’t tell Norah Aven what to do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She hesitated. “Okay,” she said and then fished something out of her pocket. It was a thin packet of papers and a small crystal cylinder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is that what I think it is?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The crystal scroll,” she nodded, handing it to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jerked back. “Actually, you better hold on to that,” I said and started climbing the rope ladder hanging from the rodãor entrance. “I don’t want it disintegrating on me or something ’cause of my man cooties.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I like your man cooties,” she laughed—a sound I hadn’t heard in nine months. I smiled as I pulled myself out of the rodãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll see if I can’t learn something from the scroll while you’re gone,” she called before I sealed her inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I slipped the rodãor in my pocket, I took a deep breath of the fresh air. Everything in my world finally felt right for the first time since Gram’s funeral.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just then something down on the banks of Walden pond caught my attention. A flaming figure had just burst out of thin air, plunging itself into the pitch black water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There were screams from a group of teenagers surrounding a bonfire on shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was tearing off my shirt, booking it for the shore, when I lost sight of where the figure had gone in. As a rule, I stayed out of large bodies of water—water was hostile to my element—but if I wasn’t mistaken, that was my dad’s head that had gone under the surface of the pond, and he looked unconscious.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The water was cold when I dove in. All water felt cold to me these days. I swam as hard as I could toward the center of the pond, every breath, every stroke, feeling like hours. When I reached the spot I’d seen him go in, it was too dark to see. I conjured a fireball. I knew it was a risk. The water could attack me any minute, but I was banking on the bonfire on shore to keep the water distracted while I searched.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took a minute before my eyes adjusted, and then the moment I caught a glimpse of his pale face sinking below me, I extinguished my fire and dove headfirst in that direction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I groped in the water until I thought my lungs would burst, and then, thank the Gihara, I caught hold of him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ten minutes later, I was dragging a sopping, smoking mass onto the shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dad!” I shouted, crouching over his steaming body. The smell of burnt flesh was strong despite the water. His face was puffy and blistered, but I patted his cheeks to bring him to.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he finally came to, he could barely open an eyelid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What happened?” I panted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Three...Dogrils,” he croaked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Another dingbat?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad nodded. “Didn’t...See...Coming.” His eyes flickered again, the tiny blue slits focusing momentarily on my face. “Re—stor—o—cite,” he wheezed, then pointed to his belt where his healing crystals hung.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right. Let’s get you patched up, old man.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Half an hour later, I was dragging the old man’s sorry bones inside the rodãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh my gosh! Is he okay?” Norah cried, running to help me as I lowered dad onto the loifer (cobbogothan version of a couch ).","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh sure. He’s in a ton of pain right now, but I’ve seen him in worse shape.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“YOU HAVE?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, but really I’d never been more worried for him and wondered whether I knew enough about Cobbogothian stones yet to heal him sufficiently. All I could think the whole time was what would’ve happened if dad had been unconscious this time and wasn’t there to walk me through each step of healing him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My hands were still shaking when I went to wash off the blood. Nor must’ve seen because she followed and, as I dried them, took hold of them. She looked me in the eye. “Are you okay?” She asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I said, putting my arm around her. “I am now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good,” she said, “because I think I might’ve figured out how to open the crystal scroll.”","i":false}],"narrator":"Jamus"},{"so":2,"title":"The Crystal Scroll (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Once Lylend was looking more like himself—a process that took an amazingly short couple of hours rather than the months or years it would take a human to heal—we sat down at Lylend’s table together. I pulled out the crystal scroll and placed it in the middle of the table between us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Am I right in guessing neither of you can see the carvings on this?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All I see is a cylinder about the size of a roll of quarters. It looks like it’s made out of a purple and yellow stone,” James said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, well, I see a tube covered in distinctly Cobbogothian carvings, and…” I held it up to the light, “…it looks like there could be something inside.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We can’t see it because the crystal scroll is carved from abscondicite—a concealment crystal,” Lylend explained. “If it’s forced open, the stone will disintegrate whatever is inside the chamber.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I told you my man cooties would ruin it,” James said.","i":false},{"t":"img","src":"/chronicle-art/ch18-crystal-scroll.svg","alt":"The crystal scroll"},{"t":"p","x":"I turned the crystal cylinder in my hand and squinted at the symbols. There were so many, and they were so delicately carved. I had been staring at them since James had gone looking for Lylend, and things were starting to pop out at me. Whoever had carved the scroll had cleverly woven several symbols together, making it very complicated but not entirely impossible to decipher.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is what I think it says,” I said, holding out a piece of thick parchment-like paper I’d borrowed from a nearby desk of Lylend’s.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let’s hear it then, kyndie,” Lylend said. “Just because you’ve translated it doesn’t mean we’ll be able to read it. I told you, only our women were allowed to have anything to do with qualdriny.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, clearing my throat. “This is what it says:","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Within this chamber, a treasure’s hid","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But first you must remove the lid","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For one to spy what lies inside","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right one shall touch the true fixed eye","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right two will make its place on wings","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Left three can reach for two true kings","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then back, left one shall press the kynd","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A cradle to thy key he’ll lend.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced up to see James with his brow scrunched in concentration.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, does it mean anything to you?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No. You?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We both looked at Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Perhaps left and right, with the number sequence, indicate which hand and fingers need to press where,” he suggested, gesturing to the scroll.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course.” I’d originally been thinking more along the lines of a map or something, but this made more sense. I held the stone scroll closer, squinting to make out the tiny carvings. They swirled and scrawled all over the chamber. It reminded me of posters librarians put up in libraries—you know, the illusion ones you stared at until your eyes went out of focus and could see a 3D picture hidden within the picture?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see it,” I began, my hands shaking a little. “Now, if Lylend’s right, there should be an eye, wings, two kings, and a boy on here. I think if I put my fingers on them right before I twist…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sensed Lylend wincing. “That’s the only clue we have to the Haven, kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t make her nervous, Pops,” James said from behind me. “You got this, Nor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed and carefully placed my right thumb on what, with a little imagination, looked like an eye. My right index finger fell easily onto a pair of curly wings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Two down, two to go.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Take your time,” James soothed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took me a minute to figure out which were the two kings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right there,” I said, spying two figures wearing halo-like crowns. I put my two middle fingers on the two kings. Finally, down around the other side of the scroll, my left thumb came to rest on a kynd, or a small boy. With my fingers in position, I took a deep breath and looked up. James had moved around the table to face me, his eyes tense.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right, on the count of three,” I said. My heart was pounding, my skin prickling with the reality of what I held in my hands—the only hope for an entire civilization. “One,” I breathed, “two, three.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I closed my eyes, held my breath, and gently twisted the tiny cylinder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nothing happened at first. Then there was a small click, and the pieces of the scroll slid easily against one another. When I turned them a full rotation, like I was screwing a lid onto a bottle, there was another tiny click.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, the chamber fell open in my hands, and a little rolled-up scroll the size of a cigarette fell into my lap.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All three of us breathed sighs of relief, and I picked up the scroll. When I spread it out in front of me, it wasn’t crackly and brittle like most ancient paper. Instead, it was supple and smooth, almost like cloth, and was covered with more of the same intricate Cobbogothian letters and symbols from the scroll’s outer chamber.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One symbol—a seven-pronged and bejeweled sunburst—was larger than the rest and sat at the top of the scroll. I held it out to Lylend. “Can you see this?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend shook his head. “Describe it to me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I described the bejeweled seven-pronged sun.","i":false},{"t":"img","src":"/chronicle-art/ch18-haven-symbol.svg","alt":"The Haven symbol — the qualdrine sunburst"},{"t":"p","x":"His face grew somber. “This is a good sign, kyndie,” he said. “That is the symbol of the qualdrine. When you start seeing that, you know you’re looking for the Haven in the right place.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked up to find James beaming down at me. “I told you she was wicked smart, didn’t I, Pops?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sure, sure,” Lylend grumbled, failing miserably to hide his own grin. “Now let’s get the scroll translated, shall we? We’re running out of time.”","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":3,"title":"Journal: Crystal Scrolls","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"12112f4df9f34a759e7e1dcf00a0fcc9"},{"so":4,"title":"Secret Message #6","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a8f14d778c894d4686abe9b30819472a"},{"so":5,"title":"Crystal Scroll Hide & Seek","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"17e21566f38a4ec3abf56662cec69243"},{"so":6,"title":"Restorocite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"83bd7fd07cc349e3bb0a4ddd3df3d7e4"},{"so":7,"title":"Abscondicite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9a447b73a60c4c5399e8ddce98c52145"},{"so":8,"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Let the woman wishing to bare kyndlin hasten to the Haven. There she will be instructed in the ways of qualdriny. If she knows not where to find it, let her follow the signs. Therein, a pouch of safety lies hidden 'neath Albine wings.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":19,"letterTitle":"A Cobbogothian Birthright","narrator":"Dr. Anderton","numword":"Nineteen","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"A Cobbogothian Birthright (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If you’ve read the Jack-man’s journals, then you know how I fit into all this. It’s been a pretty wild ride being the “myth-chaser’s” sidekick in crime, or whatever you want to call me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My professional name is Dr. Maximillian Anderton, but you can call me Dr. A. All my students do, one of which recently was Norah Lukens.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Jack-man and I met in grad school. Once we discovered at the beginning of our careers that we both had a sort of reckless “Indiana Jones” chromosome built into our DNA, we were somewhat inseparable. Our mutual need for adventure and discovery took us on some pretty wild quests, chasing obscure myths to the darkest corners of the globe. There was never a dull moment when Jack made up his mind to prove some wisp of legend was actually based on long-forgotten threads of truth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hunting for the Crystal City was different, though. The Jack-man was basing his search for it off the account of a single person—one who was most likely crazy. I’m just saying, that red-haired lady—Totherma, as she called herself—was beautiful, yes, but she was also gaga.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oh sure, the Jack-man was obsessed with the legends of the huldefolk Mama Aven told him when he was a kid (great lady, btw). And sure, his most famous discoveries were The Legend of the Cobbogothians and the dogril skeleton that backed up the possibility of the legend’s factuality. But come on! An entire city made of crystal located dangerously close to an extremely active volcano? Still, in the end it didn’t matter what I thought. Like all his other myth chases, the idea had taken root in Jack’s insatiable curiosity. I was used to it. It was something we had in common, and I’m not complaining. We had some wild adventures because of it. But meeting Totherma pushed him—both of us really—over the edge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, you know what people never seem to realize? All the Jack-man’s really significant discoveries—the ones that gave him his “myth-chaser” status—all happened before we went looking for that Crystal City. Sure, he kept his legendary career relevant with several minor discoveries over the past 18 years, but nothing ever compared to his discovery of the dogril skeleton. I’ve said it once and I’ll say it again: that fiery broad robbed us all of what he could’ve discovered had he never met her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In all the years the Jack-man and I weren’t on speaking terms, I wondered what was holding him back. When I heard from a mutual friend that he’d adopted a kid, I thought it was because of her—that he’d slowed down his career a bit to raise Norah. I could understand that, of course. I have a wife and kid of my own. Becoming a family man significantly altered my former globe-trotting ways. But when Norah showed up at Dunstan last August—in real bad emotional shape—and I found out from her therapist that Jack had been gone “myth-chasing” most of her life, I couldn’t help wondering what in the world he was searching for. Then it hit me—he was still searching for that ridiculous notion of the Crystal City under Iceland. That’s when I realized my old friend had gone just as gaga as Totherma.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then the Jack-man called me out of the blue a few days before his death, begging me to do him a favor for old times’ sake. This is where I’ve got to pause because before anything from this point on can make sense to you, there are a few things you need to know about the time Jack and I went looking for that Crystal City back in ’91.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First of all, Jack never made it to the Crystal City. If he had, it would have been in every archeological journal known to man. Secondly, when Totherma led us into that mess of tunnels, she abandoned us. At first, I thought it was because of me. She and I didn’t get on too well from the start. She didn’t like the attention I was paying her, but how could I help myself? I tried to make it right with her. I really did. But the further we got in those tunnels, the more she got under my skin. If you’d seen her, you’d understand. Sure, she was high and mighty and all, but even still, I haven’t seen a woman more beautiful in all my life. Jack and I aren’t the first guys whose friendship was ruined over a dame.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that was only the beginning. The real problems happened when Totherma reappeared in those tunnels six days later. We were on the brink of starvation by the time she finally came back. And what did she want us to do? Rather than getting the blazes out of that place, she wanted us to follow her even farther into the tunnels! I’ll admit, maybe I wasn’t thinking straight. Maybe the severe lack of food and water led me to blow up at her, but I wasn’t about to die in order to appease some lady who was obviously unhinged, no matter how pretty she was. But Jack? He’d already fallen for her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t mean to get violent, but when the Jack-man started taking her side, how else was I supposed to save him from himself? We were going to die down there if I didn’t knock some sense into him!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jack and I had argued often throughout our various adventures, but we’d never come to blows before. I’ll admit, I threw the first punch—well, the only punch really. Just after I did, Totherma put her hand on my shoulder, and I was suddenly in more pain than I’ve ever been in. I don’t know what she did to me or how she did it by barely touching me, but the pain was so intense I passed out. Being taken down by a dame is not my proudest moment. Believe me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, I was waking up in a hospital back in Reykjavik and didn’t hear from the Jack-man again. I went on to have my own successful career in geoarchaeology, and Jack went on with his career. But we never explored together again, and we never spoke again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not until three nights before he died.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the middle of the night when he called. He spoke to me like the last 18 years had never even happened. Without any explanation, he asked me to make sure Norah made it safely onto her bus home from Dunstan Academy when it was time for her to head back to Boston in a few days. When I asked why he couldn’t come pick her up and take her home himself—none of the students at Dunstan should be allowed to travel alone right away—he told me he was delayed in Iceland and wouldn’t make it back in time for her graduation or her birthday due to unforeseen circumstances. Because of this, he asked me to do him that big favor. He wanted to send me her birthday present, and then asked if, on her birthday, I would meet Norah outside his dogril exhibit at Harvard’s Peabody Museum. He insisted I give her the gift there. I asked why he didn’t just mail it to her himself or wait until he got back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was when the Jack-man got uncharacteristically frantic. He said it was paramount that Norah received her gift at that exact place and time, in person.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If he hadn’t sounded so desperate, I probably would’ve hung up on him. Instead, I agreed to do as he asked. When I saw the news of his death on T.V. a few nights later, I’ll admit I was glad I had agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A package addressed to Norah showed up in my office on the last day of classes. There was no postage on it and no return address. It had been hand delivered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the news reported Norah as a person of interest in Jack’s death, I called the Belmont Police Department to see if I could visit her there. Unfortunately, she’d already broken out. However, my call still led to a visit from a Detective Narson for further questioning about my connection to Jack and Norah. Let’s just say things got much more complicated and dangerous after that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is why when you finish reading my part to play in all this, I’m asking you to not judge me too harshly. Things aren’t always what they seem, you know? Sometimes we’re given a bad hand to play, and we have to play it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*Note to Reader: This concludes Part 1. Please proceed to Part 2.","i":false}],"narrator":"Dr. Anderton"},{"so":2,"title":"Norah's Perspective (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Did you know Lylend gets cell service in his rodãor? Four bars! Crazy, right? It’s a good thing too because I’d completely forgotten Uncle Jack’s instructions to contact Dr. A until I remembered to show James and Lylend the other things I’d found in Jack’s safe with the crystal scroll. You should’ve seen Lylend’s face when he learned poor Fridrika Bjornsardottir was responsible for breaking the dogrils free of their prison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This, of course, only gave Lylend one more reason to hurry and find the Haven. He was worried that, like the dappled brown, Fridrika or the she-dogril—whatever we were calling her now—and her partner were making even more dogrils.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It takes a few days for a dogril to take full possession of a host body, kyndie,” Lylend explained. “There could be dozens of hosts out there who’ve been possessed and are halfway through having their souls ripped out. We can’t waste any more time here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now that we knew the dogrils were possessing more hosts, the sooner we found the Haven and lured the dogrils from earth’s realm back to Cobbogoth, the better. If we left, the dogrils would definitely follow because they far preferred Cobbogothian hosts to human hosts.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before we could head back to Iceland to search the Crystal City though, I needed to go meet up with Dr. Anderton. Uncle Jack said it was important.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I stalled for time and finally convinced Lylend he looked too weak after his encounter with the dappled brown to hoption anywhere just yet. What if we ran into the dogrils again? I wasn’t going to be much help fighting them, so wasn’t it better to rest a little while and then go? Surprisingly, Lylend gave in almost as quickly as James did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I suppose you’re right, kyndie,” he’d grumbled. “No point storming the castle with no crystals in your satchel. Let’s get some sleep, and we’ll leave first thing in the morning.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I waited until I was certain James and Lylend were fast asleep and then opened the rodãor the way I’d seen both James and Lylend do it. I climbed out, sealed the door, and stored the rodãor in my pocket. I wouldn’t exactly be meeting Dr. A alone like he insisted, but what he didn’t know wouldn’t kill him. Besides, if our meeting took longer than expected, I could open the rodãor after he was gone, let a most likely very angry James and Lylend out, and then we could hoption to Iceland from a secluded alley in Harvard Square or something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was 4:00 pm when I finished calling a cab to pick me up at Walden St. and started walking the short distance from Walden Pond. The taxi was there when I arrived, and thankfully traffic was decent because we reached Harvard Square in under half an hour. When I finally reached the Peabody Museum, I took the stairs to Uncle Jack’s dogril exhibit on the second floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The museum had spared no expense for my uncle’s exhibit. The dogril skeleton was located in its own room, and the entrance had been made to look like one of the entrances to the caves of Aegissida.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The inside of the room was made to look like a man-made cave. I’d seen the exhibit in pictures before, but I’d never seen it in person for some reason. To be honest, I’d never wanted to. If you haven’t gathered by now, dogril skeletons are super creepy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dr. A was waiting for me at the entrance of the exhibit. He looked pretty frazzled in comparison to the cool, outdoorsy professor I knew while at Dunstan Academy. Before saying anything, he handed me a small package with my name on it. Then he opened the door to the exhibit and motioned me inside. He told me to meet him up on the roof when I was done and pointed at an exit sign over a camouflaged door a few exhibits away. I glanced at my phone. The museum was closing in ten minutes. When I told him this, Dr. A just shrugged and said he was only following Uncle Jack’s instructions.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once I was inside the dogril exhibit, Dr. A shut me in, and I was alone. A shiver ran through me as I stared up at the large dogril skeleton in its glass case. Live dogrils were infinitely scarier in person, but the skeleton in the dimly lit cave-room with ancient Celtic runes carved into the walls was adequately creepy. I sat down on the viewing bench situated across from the case and opened the small box Dr. A had given me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The box contained nothing more than a small flashlight, a small notebook, and a stubby little pencil. To the normal person raised by normal parents, this would seem like a pretty lame birthday gift. To me, a girl raised by a man who was so paranoid he’d kept all his research notes in code, I knew this was just the beginning of the gift.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I switched on the flashlight and shone it around the room. It wasn’t just a regular flashlight. It was a LED light. I stood, slowly spinning around in a circle, taking in the entire room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Something caught my eye—something in the runes etched on the cave walls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stepped closer, shining my flashlight. Inside one of the larger Celtic Runes, someone—Uncle Jack—had written a much smaller ancient Cobbogothian symbol in invisible ink. I shined the flashlight along the walls and up over the ceiling. It was the same with every rune; each one had an “invisible” Cobbogothian symbol inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sat back down on the bench, balancing the flashlight between my knees, and began to translate. The hidden words began to turn into a message—a sort of good-bye letter from Uncle Jack.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"* Note to reader: Pause here and read Part 3 before reading the rest of Part 2.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The deafening silence of the dogril cave wasn’t nearly loud enough to drown out the whirling of my mind. I wiped away the tears streaming down my face as I stared at the symbols making up my uncle’s signature on the wall in front of me. There was so much information to digest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Uncle Jack had chosen to die the moment he broke his Silentium oath—he had died for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A sob ripped from me. Knowing Uncle Jack’s sacrifice was a choice somehow unlocked the grief I’d been holding inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I’d gotten myself under control again, I unzipped my front pocket, ready to open the rodãor and tell James and Lylend everything when the flashlight rolled off my lap onto the floor. When it came to a stop, it shined on a particular spot as though it had been drawn there by a magnet. The light shined on a symbol on the wall, just below my uncle’s signature. It was one I’d missed because it was not hiding in a Celtic rune. I didn’t recognize it either. The symbol looked like it was just a simple rectangle about the size of a credit card drawn on the wall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stepped closer to examine the symbol, brushing the wall with my fingers. The moment my skin touched the rectangle, it popped out of the wall, revealing a tiny drawer made from a golden crystal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I carefully reached inside and pulled out the contents. The drawer contained a small velvet drawstring pouch. Inside the pouch was a medallion on a thin gold chain. A clear stone about the size of a quarter sat in its center, and the gold around the outside of the stone looked like a multi-symbolled dial.","i":false},{"t":"img","src":"/chronicle-art/ch19-qualdrine-medallion.svg","alt":"The qualdrine medallion"},{"t":"p","x":"“Holy Hades!” I whispered. I hadn’t recognized the rectangular symbol, but I knew what the medallion was. Uncle Jack had drawn a sketch of an identical medallion in his journal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked closer at the clear stone in the center of the medallion and realized that this particular qualdrine hadn’t found its owner yet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when it hit me; this qualdrine was mine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slowly drew the qualdrine medallion over my head. It fell to my chest, resting just below my collarbone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I picked up the medallion in my palm and brushed the stone with the tip of my forefinger just like I’d read in Uncle Jack’s journal. The moment my skin touched the stone, I felt a great whoosh from behind, and it was like my entire body ignited with light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The dogril cave vanished. All I could see was bright white. Then musical voices filled my mind, chanting a melody so beautiful my eyes filled with tears.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Al Yvaine-i-Gihara dol kindlin qualdere. Al Yvaine-i-Gihara dandandel la pluerer.”","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"My mind didn’t have to translate because my heart knew what the words meant: *“Come walk with Gihara, new child of power. Come walk with Gihara and prove yourself true.”*","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My heart stopped racing for a moment, and I felt like I was floating, like I was coming home for the first time in my life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At last, the wind, the light, and the voices all faded, and I was back in the cave room staring down at the medallion in my hand. It was still clear, but I knew once I was given the chance to complete Yvaine-i-Gihara, the stone would change to the pale green color of my eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, I met Dr. A on the museum roof. It was raining hard again. Dripping wet, he looked even worse than before. “What is it, Dr. A?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry, Lukens,” Dr. A said. “But they’ve got my wife and kid.” The next thing I knew, he stepped forward and pushed me off the roof of the Peabody Museum.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Careening toward the ground, a scream tore from my throat, and then I was flying. I didn’t have to look to know I’d been snatched from my fall by a dogril.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":3,"title":"Uncle Jack's Letter (Part 3)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"My Dear Norah Aven,","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If you’re reading this, I am gone. Please don’t mourn for me, my girl. If I am dead, rest assured I have passed on to the greatest myth-chasing adventure of all—the great beyond. I hope in spite of this fact you are all right, and Lylend and James have kept their promise to protect you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right now you may feel a great deal of anger toward me, both for 18 years of neglect and for all the secrets that have been kept from you. I assure you dear Norah Aven, it has all been done with very good reason, the most important of which—to me at least—is that whether you believe it or not, I have loved you like my own flesh and blood since the moment you entered my life. Everything I have done has been to protect you for as long as I possibly could.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I have never told you much about the day you entered my life, but I assure you, when I found you as a baby, it wasn’t at some random orphanage in Iceland. Nor did I accidentally stumble across you in a basket on one of my digs. In fact, there was nothing random or accidental about that day. Now the time has come to tell you everything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The day Totherma led me to the Crystal City, she told me she had been testing me for something greater, and when I chose to follow her deeper into the Caves of Aegissida instead of returning to Hella to save myself from starvation, I had passed the test to her satisfaction. Then she told me I had proven myself worthy to behold her people’s greatest treasure.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma then led me to a cairn in the heart of the Crystal City. Through the crystal pillar, she showed me her people’s greatest treasure—something that for the past 18 years has been my own greatest treasure.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That marvelous mind of yours has probably already suspected by now, especially after hearing James’ story and meeting Lylend, but I will tell you anyway. Totherma’s treasure was a child, Norah Aven—a baby girl. That child, my dear, was you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma told me that the day she received the prophesy that James was the one destined to return the Haven to her people, she was also shown another child—one who would come after him and who would inevitably prove to be the final hope of her people. Then she told me it was no longer safe for you to stay in Cobbogoth—if you did, the word of your destiny would spread, and you would be hunted by every dogril plaguing their realm. As you can see, the word has spread, and the dogrils have come to our realm to hunt not just the Haven but to hunt you, my dear girl.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma said she had been watching me through the cairn in Iceland—especially while I was working on the dogril site, and she knew that with my unique set of skills and knowledge concerning her people—your people—I was the right human to raise and protect you until you were finally old enough to receive your qualdrine, complete your Yvaine-i-Gihara, and begin harnessing the elemental power that would allow you to protect yourself from the dogrils. She asked if I would accept my own destiny to raise you. When I said yes, she asked if I would prove it by taking a Silentium oath—an oath that would prevent me from revealing what I knew to anyone but yourself, and only then when the time was right. When I agreed and the oath was complete, Totherma opened a Dark Matter Door, and you were brought into our realm and placed in my arms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So now you know the truth. You are a Cobbogothian, Norah Aven. Your real name is Noriah, which means “Final Hope” in ancient Cobbogothian. I also gave you my mother’s name because I wanted you to remember where your people really come from when you returned to Cobbogoth. They come from this realm—from Iceland. They are the true huldefolk—and, as the prophesy foretells, they will return to rule again someday. Because of that, I wanted you to feel a kinship here. And, of course, I selfishly wanted you to remember me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You may have wondered why I was so cruel as to tear you and James apart after Gram’s death last summer. Allow me to finally explain my actions on that point—although I believe Gram may have already beat me to it. However, if she has, she explained things without the significant knowledge that you too are a Cobbogothian, which, I assure you, significantly alters the way a kinzura bond works. Because you and James are both Cobbogothian, you are therefore two halves of a pair of souls. When kinzura occurs between two Cobbogothian souls, even if the bond isn’t officially forged with a blood bond, their two destinies can become intertwined in very complicated and unforeseen ways, meaning if the bond between the two of you grows too powerful too fast, it can alter the course of one or the other’s destiny. Because both James’ and your destinies are so vital to your people’s survival, when you demonstrated that your powers were already manifesting the night you absorbed James’ fire last summer and collapsed, I thought it best to separate you until your 18th birthday when you could finally understand who and what you are. I’m sorry for all I put you through on this account, but I hope this explanation will help you understand why.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As to the peculiar visions you’ve been having since last summer, Totherma promised me she would begin using her telepathic powers to communicate with you to prepare you for this moment when I could finally tell you the truth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Silentium oath I made to Totherma restricted me from being able to tell anyone about you, your destiny, or the Crystal City until after your 18th birthday. If I broke the oath, fatal consequences would occur. Unfortunately, Cifer and his dogrils learned about your existence sooner than we hoped, so it became necessary to break the oath. This, dear girl, is why you are reading this confession rather than hearing it from me face to face. I suspected this might happen and made necessary preparations so I would have no regrets. My only regret is any pain I may have caused you in the process. I only hope my death will have given you enough time to help Lylend and James find the Haven and return it to the Cobbogothians before it is too late.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, I pray you will believe me when I tell you what a privilege it has been to love you and raise you as I have been honored to do. My life has been consumed with searching for proof of your people, and, in the end, I was lucky enough to be entrusted with raising you. I know I have not been an ideal father, but you have been the greatest joy of my heart and the pride of my life. I’ve told you many times that I was preparing you to finish my life’s work. Now you finally know the truth, Norah Aven. You are my life’s work. I hope you take that knowledge with you to Cobbogoth, and even if you find you have a birth family there, I hope you’ll spare a thought for your old Uncle Jack once in a while.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All my love,","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jack A. Lukens","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*Note to reader: Return to Part 2 and finish reading.","i":false}],"narrator":"Uncle Jack"},{"so":4,"title":"A Cobbogothian Birthright (Part 4)","kind":"text","blocks":[],"narrator":null},{"so":5,"title":"Text Message #3","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a050a0e3d51f4660902aff1796eda27c"},{"so":6,"title":"Silentium","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d3948fd927ff4e09b1343037fb667c01"},{"so":7,"title":"Theaurusite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"c54d4e43d96143e39f24b755153f1452"}]},{"n":20,"letterTitle":"The Crystal Scroll","narrator":"James","numword":"Twenty","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Text Message #4","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"800fb6fa1e624e41aa3adb6ca64b495b"},{"so":2,"title":"The Man in the Glass","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"It was 6:00 a.M. When I woke up to Norah’s text. When she didn’t respond to my responses, I woke my dad.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do we do?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We go after her,” he said, already on his feet and dressing. “If the dogrils have her, they’ll take her straight to the crystal city—they’ll take her to him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Him? Him-him?” I asked, feeling a surge of panic. “As in Cifer?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My dad paused a moment to put a hand on my shoulder. “She’s tougher than she looks, my kynd.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But he’ll kill her!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not until he has the Haven. And if my judgement of the kyndie is correct, she’ll do everything in her power to keep it from him. Should buy us some time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How are we supposed to get to her though? We’re trapped in here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My dad stopped again and looked at me, only this time it was the look he’d given me any time I had made a really dumb mistake during elemental training.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My kynd,” he said. “You have a back door to your human-made house, yes?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I NODDED.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ve got windows, haven’t you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then please don’t insult my significantly greater-than-mere-human wisdom and intelligence by assuming I don’t also have another way out of my rodãor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I rolled my eyes at this. “You could’ve just said you had another way out. You didn’t need to insult an entire race of people—Norah’s people—while you were at it.” This had always been a touchy subject between us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad had the nerve to look confused. “I wasn’t insulting anyone, just stating things as they are. You do realize I have an average of 100 times the experience of the oldest human, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I groaned. “Can we please stop wasting time and get out of here?” I asked, motioning for him to lead the way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“THANK YOU.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, we were climbing out of a horizontally placed rodãor exit and stepped into what looked like an ancient, abandoned cathedral.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait, is this the altar room?” I whispered, only to have my dad silence me and motion me behind a pillar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since you, reader, probably won’t ever experience it, let me just say that entering a rodãor at one location only to leave it at a different place all together is a disorienting experience you never get used to. It’s like waking up from a dream and not knowing where you are.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once the disoriented feeling passed, I recognized my surroundings. We were in the crystal city—the altar room of their temple, to be specific. Dad had taken me here a few times when I was just figuring out who I was and where I came from. Unlike those other times when it was just dad and me, however, this time there were voices echoing through our new surroundings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hurry up, get her in here,” someone was saying. “We’re already late.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first voice was familiar. I turned to see the she-dogril, her companion, and Norah, enter the ancient altar room from an archway to our right. From what I could see, Nor looked windblown and a little paler than usual but unharmed. For the first time since getting her text, I breathed a SIGH of relief. Norah and the two dogrils were followed by one other person. When I saw him, I did a double take.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What the—? What’s he doing here?” I hissed in my dad’s ear. It was Detective Narson from the Belmont Police Station.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dappled brown,” dad murmured, unconcerned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mean he’s a—?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dogril,” dad nodded. “The kyndie did say he was making wild, irrational accusations when he took her in for questioning the other day. The dogrils must’ve just finished ripping his soul out before he helped ambush me yesterday.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt a twinge of guilt for disliking Narson so much. Turns out he couldn’t help being the absolute worst.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Looks like we’ve made it in record time,” Narson said once they reached the center of the room. “I don’t think the master will punish us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll allow tardiness this once since you brought along what I asked for,” said another, oily voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad gave me a significant look and pointed further into the room. I slowly turned toward his gesture, and I instantly recognized the mirror-like wall behind the altar for what it was, Cifer’s Qualdrine prison. I’d met the creepy dude before, but dad was always there to keep me from doing something stupid, like touching the Qualdrine barrier that had separated him from this realm—and all other realms—for the past 10,000 years. The girl I loved didn’t have that luxury.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The dogrils dragged Norah toward it. She struggled against them, and it was everything I could do to stay still.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We gotta do something,” I hissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not yet,” dad replied. “If they realize we’re here, they’ll start fighting us, and her chances of getting hurt are much higher.” I squeezed my fists in frustration and tried to see the sense in what he said. What I learned instead is that it’s a lot harder to see reason when the person you love is in danger. Why’d Nor have to go and run off again?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As the dogrils dragged her toward the glass wall, all four of their images appeared in the glass, but they were foggy. The only really clear image was of a black-haired, “pretty boy” man standing in the corner, and my old man didn’t have to tell me who this joker was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Go ahead and laugh, but I’m telling you, reader, your hunky hollywood stars have got a whole lot of diddly for looks compared to Cifer. Just one more reason to hate the guy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Master Cifer,” the three dogrils said in unison. Each fell to one knee, pounding a fist to the left side of their chests like a bunch of tools.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, Narson, who is this beauty you’ve brought me?” Cifer asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is Norah Lukens, master,” Narson said. “She’s the one we’ve been following.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer’s eyes examined Nor one inch at a time. The slow smile that spread over his stupid face made me want to punch him in the throat.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Would you mind coming closer?” Cifer asked, continuing to devour her with his eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Easy, kynd,” dad said, resting a hand on my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor stepped closer to the glass. She wasn’t struggling anymore, and warning sirens started blaring in my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer kept looking at my girl, a sick glimmer of fascination on his face, and Nor kept moving toward him, her eyes locked on his.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s wrong with her?” I hissed. “She’s gotta know who that is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad gripped my shoulder, keeping me in place. “It’s one of Cifer’s more astute powers,” he said. “He’s going to try and read her mind—see what she knows about the Haven.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. “Will it hurt her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad hesitated. “It could, but only if she knows something and puts up a fight. The good news is Cifer is a man and, therefore, anything the kyndie knows about the Haven will be inaccessible to him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I clenched my jaw, bracing myself. Nor was inches from the glass now, looking straight into pretty boy’s eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now tell me,” he purred. “Where is the Haven?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I-I don’t know. I…” Nor said in a weird, breathy voice. “I can’t remember.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Shall I help you?” Cifer pressed his hands to the thick crystal separating them. “That’s right, Norah. Just place your hands on mine.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was it. I had to stop her. I started to lunge forward, but my dad was faster. He had me in a chokehold before I could make it past our pillar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Under no circumstance whatsoever are you to move from this spot, Jamus,” he warned in a more deadly voice than I’d ever heard from him. “If you bust onto the scene now, he’ll kill her for sure.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tapped his arm to show I understood, and he loosened his hold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know you love her, kynd,” he whispered, “but you’ve got to master yourself. Look away if you must.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glared at him but nodded, keeping my eyes averted. I glanced up only one time more to see Nor’s palms pressed to Cifer’s through the glass.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After that it didn’t matter where I looked because the next moment my ears were splitting with Norah’s screams.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"so":3,"title":"Drawing of Altar Room","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e71ddb3483374f8986d8765b5d2d792d"}]},{"n":21,"letterTitle":"A Cobbogothian Birthright","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Twenty-One","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Neath Albine Wings","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Remember how I told you why Uncle Jack named me Norah Aven at the beginning of all this? Remember how I told you he named me after Iceland’s national flower, the Mountain Aven, because he wanted me to remember to go back to the beginning—to go back to where it all began—when I wanted to know who I was?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well, for the record, when he told me all that, I had pretty much zero idea that by “who I am” he meant a member of a legendary elevated race or that by “going back to the beginning” he meant going back to the Cobbogothians’ ancient Crystal City located beneath one of Iceland’s most active volcanoes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But you know…it’s not like Uncle Jack has left me out of the loop before.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Regardless, here I was. In the Crystal City. At the beginning of where all this crazy Cobbogothian stuff began—where I began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, I wasn’t too bent out of shape about being here. I’ve wanted to see the Crystal City since I read about it in Uncle Jack’s journal—even more so now that I knew who I was. I would, however, rather not have arrived via Dr. A’s betrayal, which inevitably led to being kidnapped by three very terrifying dogrils who took turns flying me here on their backs. But who’s complaining? (I am, that’s who! P.S. Dogrils stink. Literally.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the record though, let me just say that Uncle Jack’s journal entries didn’t do the Crystal City justice. Not. At. All.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only I’d been able to look around a bit—explore the city of my ancestors and maybe find the Haven—before the dogrils hauled me straight to the heart of the city. But somehow I ended up in just the right place anyway. I knew the moment the dogrils started dragging me up the stairs to the crystal pyramid, which according to Uncle Jack’s journal was the Cobbogothian temple, that perhaps greater forces than myself were at work. You see, I knew I was in the right place because, unlike the dogrils, I could see the Haven symbol carved onto every single step leading up to the temple.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we reached the top of the steps, there was even more proof I was on the right track. The crystal door leading inside the temple also had the Haven symbol etched into it. Once again, I was the only one who could see it and was, therefore, the only one who jumped and stumbled backward when the symbol suddenly burst with light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s wrong with you?” the she-dogril spat and shoved me forward as the door opened and let us through.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Inside the temple was the same. There were Haven symbols lighting a path that led directly to the altar room. However, once we entered the altar room, the symbols stopped. Well, not exactly stopped. I just lost sight of them because there were so many other symbols carved into the multi-colored stone slabs circling the altar like a psychedelic Stonehenge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I scanned the slabs as we passed by them, hoping to see the symbol or at least read some mention of the Haven in what was written there. But all I could catch as the dogrils dragged me along were a few snatches of sentences that sounded like a more elaborate version of the story Samir had passed along to me before he died—The Twin Brothers’ Tale.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we reached the glass wall at the back of the altar room—when I saw the man in the glass there—it didn’t matter if I was on the right track or not because all thoughts of the Haven (or anything else for that matter) slipped from my mind completely. From that moment on, all I could hear or think or feel—all I cared about—was that man.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Until, of course, our hands connected through the glass, and I felt like my entire being was on fire for the second time in nine months. The man was Cifer, of course, and this was where I learned firsthand that his manifestation was fire. However, unlike James’ fire—James who never meant to hurt me when we first kissed—Cifer’s fire felt controlled, intentional, greedy, and definitely focused on causing me as much pain as possible while it searched for something inside my mind. I don’t know if that makes sense, but it’s the only way I can describe it to you. It was like Cifer was using his fire to invade my mind in search of something—something that was almost certainly the location of the Haven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From then on, all I remember is pain—the most agonizing pain I’d ever experienced. I also remember screaming. Yep, I was definitely screaming before I blacked out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the connection between us finally broke, I came to, lying on my back on the ground. I expected to still feel the burning pain or at least to feel weak and broken the way I had after James first kissed me last summer. Instead, I felt a sensation unlike anything I’ve ever felt in my life. I felt strong—really strong. So powerful, in fact, it was almost like there was fire in my veins that was quickly gathering toward my chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For a second, I wondered if I had just manifested for the first time—if, now that I had my qualdrine, I’d just proven myself to be a Flame Thrower like James by absorbing Cifer’s fire. But just as this thought crossed my mind, the surge of power in my chest dissipated into my limbs and faded away as quickly as it had come.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The dogrils jerked me back onto my feet. To my amazement, I saw Cifer getting to his feet as well on the other side of the glass. He looked a little dazed. Apparently, our encounter had knocked him out too.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Detective Narson—or whoever he was now that he’d been possessed by a dogril—stood at the glass like a loyal dog, practically begging Cifer to assure him he was all right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m fine, konkle!” Cifer spat as he steadied himself by placing a hand to the glass. He looked visibly shaken, and I wondered what he was thinking as he glared down at me. “Bring her back,” he ordered. “We’re going to try this again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Scouring the altar room for any escape from feeling that pain again, my eyes landed on the archway of a passage leading away from the altar room. There it was! The Haven symbol, illuminated and beckoning me to follow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? What is it? What do you see?” Cifer panted as he glowered down at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The way to the Haven,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A hungry look sprang up in Cifer’s eyes, and I knew it was time to start taking risks, especially if I wanted to survive long enough to actually find the Haven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s what you’re looking for, right?” I asked. “When you burned through my mind, or whatever it was you just did, you saw for yourself that I don’t have the Haven yet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I did,” Cifer said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You also know I’m the only one here who can find it, which is why your dogrils came after me in the first place.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then I’m guessing it’s in your best interest to let me find it by following the symbols.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Symbols that only you can see?” Cifer asked skeptically. “How am I to know you’re not fooling us all?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I suppose you’ll just have to trust me,” I said with a shrug.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer didn’t look convinced.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look, this isn’t my fight, and I really don’t care who gets the Haven,” I lied. “I just want to get out of here alive.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer smirked at me through the glass. “I’m listening.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I get you the Haven, and you let me go…without letting one of your dogrils possess me first,” I quickly added. “Deal?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer scrutinized my face for a long time, and then to my surprise and uneasiness, he agreed. Of course, I wasn’t dumb enough to trust him to keep his promise once he had the Haven, but since I had no intention of giving it to him anyway, what did I have to lose?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ten minutes later, I found myself flanked by two dogrils, following more Haven symbols. They led me through the marked archway, down a dimly lit passage, away from the altar room. If I was lucky, I could lose the dogrils down one of these hallways and finally let James and Lylend out of the rodãor in my pocket.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But, luck wasn’t on my side. The dogrils tailed me at every turn, taking turns phasing in and out of their dogril forms in order to light our path with their smelly, flaming breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Several corridors, foyers, and staircases later, the Haven symbols had led me to yet another spiral staircase. This staircase looked slightly different than all the others, however, because not only was each step emblazoned with the symbol, but the staircase itself appeared to be carved out of qualdrine. We had to be close now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed the staircase up to a peculiar room. The walls were covered in the same mirror-like qualdrine crystal Cifer was imprisoned in, and the floor and ceiling appeared to be made from it as well. The room was empty except for a single qualdrine statue of a child standing in the center of the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sight of the mirror-like walls had captured the dogrils’ full attention. They kept calling out to their master, as though they were trying to summon Cifer into the walls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"While they were distracted, I examined the statue more closely. The child held out its hand, and on its palm was a concave, oval shaped dial. The dial looked very much like the dial encasing my qualdrine. I quickly slipped my medallion out from under my shirt and snapped it into the dial. It fit perfectly, and I wondered if it was some sort of key. I reached forward to try and turn my medallion, but before I could, the dials on both my qualdrine and the child’s palm began to spin as correlating symbols lit up each time they aligned. It reminded me of a combination lock.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Almost as soon as it had started, the spinning stopped. The dogrils were still trying to summon their master in the glass walls when the room suddenly lurched with a loud grinding noise. I braced myself against the statue. The dogrils shrieked in protest. Suddenly, the entrance to another but descending spiral staircase appeared in the floor at the child’s feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly slipped my medallion back under my shirt as we started down the stairs together.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We descended into what appeared to be a long room lined with shelves made of stone, filled with dusty urns of various shapes and sizes. I thought of what Samir had shown me in his lab and what Uncle Jack had told him about a room full of countless blood-filled urns. I wondered if this was the place he meant.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As my eyes adjusted to the dimness, I could just make out another statue made from qualdrine farther down toward the end. As I approached it, I realized it wasn’t just one statue but several statues entwined together. The closer I got, the more I could see that each of the various statues were winged women, and each appeared to be clothed in one of the six different elements: water, fire, earth, wind, metal, and wood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I suddenly remembered a phrase from the crystal scroll:","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If she knows not where to find it, let her follow the signs. Therein a pouch of safety lies hidden ’neath albine wings.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Take a look at Uncle Jack’s journal, and you’ll know how I knew these statues couldn’t be anything other than albine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I soon caught sight of a small opening at the center of all pairs of wings. The wings appeared to be crowded together as if sheltering something in that opening. It seemed pretty obvious I was supposed to put my hand inside the hole, but I couldn’t help worrying that this was some sort of worthiness test, like the Bocca della Verita—The Mouth of Truth statue in Italy. The idea of getting my hand chopped off if I didn’t pass definitely didn’t thrill me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unfortunately, I didn’t have time to worry about that. Taking a deep breath and making sure the dogrils were still busy searching the rest of the room, I closed my eyes, counted to three, and slid my fingers into the hole.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nothing happened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I did it again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Again, nothing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At last, I decided it was all or nothing and slid my entire hand inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Like thunder, the room began to shake just as the room above had. There was the sound of stone rubbing against stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I retrieved my hand, jumping out of the way as the combined albine statues burst with light, and the elements they were dressed in surged to life as they began to move. The statues unwound themselves from one another until they stood before me, straightened, wing to wing and shoulder to shoulder in a crescent.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Seven beautiful statues clothed in earth, fire, water, wood, metal, and air—the center statue clothed in all six elements—held out their combined hands to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced around at the dogrils. They continued to search as though something incredible hadn’t just happened here, and I gathered that, like the symbols, this too had been invisible to them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned back to the albine and saw that a single, dusty stone sat cupped in the palm of the center statue. I gingerly picked it up and proceeded to wipe away centuries of dust from the top with my sleeve. Etched into the surface of the smooth, white stone was the bejeweled, seven-pronged sunburst. The symbol of the Haven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of course! The Haven was a stone!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Again, part of a phrase from the crystal scroll came to my mind: “a pouch of safety.” Again, realization struck. The Haven wasn’t just a stone. It was a rodãor—a rodãor that contained the secrets Lylend was looking for.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Holding the cool stone in my palm, I slowly traced the outside of it the way Lylend did to open and close his rodãor stone. When my finger circled it once, the stone did as I expected and sputtered to life. The bejeweled symbol ignited, slid from the stone and turned itself upright, hovering in the air before me. Each jewel—ruby, emerald, sapphire, onyx, turquoise, gold, and opal—lit up one by one, casting a rainbowed shimmer on the walls around me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unlike the rodãor, however, the symbol didn’t unravel and stretch into a circle of light, becoming a doorway the way Lylend’s rodãor did. Instead, it began to pulse and vibrate, causing ripples in the air around it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I held my breath, waiting, unsure of what to do next.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when the symbol began to move toward me. I took a step back, giving it room, but it kept gliding straight toward me—toward my chest—until it lay atop the exposed skin just beneath my collarbone, looking like a golden-inked, gem-studded tattoo against my pale skin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, it vanished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is that it? Is that what we’re looking for?” the she-dogril hissed from behind me. I jumped, nearly dropping the stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think so,” I said, still bewildered. But now I wasn’t so sure. I scanned the room for anything else that might be the Haven, but I only saw more urns.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, give it here then.” The she-dogril reached out for the stone. I tried to pull it back, but her fingers brushed it before I could stop her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A loud bang blew through the room, and we were both thrown backward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Fire seared through my fingertips. The other dogril shot a pillar of flames, nearly blasting me, and I dove behind the albine statues.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get her,” the she-dogril cried. “She tried to blow us up. It’s all been a trap.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I scrambled around on the floor, looking for the white stone, but the air was so thick with dust I couldn’t see anything. I reached into my pocket for Lylend’s rodãor. If I could just open it and let him and James out, perhaps they could buy me some time while I figured out what had just happened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t get the rodãor free from my pocket before the dogrils grabbed me from either side, dragging me back to the altar room—to Cifer and pain.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: The Haven","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"19f0438cf43f45e9ba472eb09a999f85"}]},{"n":22,"letterTitle":"The Uprising","narrator":"Lylend","numword":"Twenty-Two","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Only a Qualdrine","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"As a Naridi, I’ve spent millennia trying to anticipate every possible scenario wherein Cifer and his dogrils might return to reign more death and destruction on my people. It has been the whole reason for my extremely long existence. In all my anticipation, however, I never once thought it would happen like this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Only a qualdrine can break a qualdrine.” This phrase has been used among my people since we first entered the Cobbogothian realm. After Phitessnie’s blood sum was paid, we gained our freedom from Cifer and his dogrils but at the cost of losing the greatest leader our people had ever known. This phrase has always served as a reminder to us that the fate of the Cobbogothians lies in our own hands—that only we have the power to unleash the evils and destruction of Cifer upon ourselves.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before returning to Earth’s realm to find the Haven, Totherma told me one of the reasons the secrets of Qualdriny had been left behind was to help prevent our posterity, those who hadn’t lived through the horrors of Cifer’s first attack, from having the power to unwittingly release him and his dogrils from their qualdrine prisons.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, did we bring these consequences upon ourselves simply for seeking the Haven in the first place? Perhaps. Read on and judge for yourself. But before you pass judgement, human, tell me how you could’ve done better. I dare you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The moment I saw the kyndie being dragged back into the altar room, I felt a shifting in the elements—something every Naridi feels when a monumental event is about to take place. I knew I wouldn’t be able to hold Jamus back from trying to rescue her much longer. And I didn’t want to. I heard the dogrils tell their master what the kyndie had done—saw the flame of rage ignite in Cifer’s eyes. I knew we didn’t have long if we hoped to save her from his wrath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I glanced over at Jamus, giving him the signal to be ready to move, the kynd was already moving. The dogrils had dragged Norah Aven toward Cifer’s prison wall and had smashed her hands back onto it so they were even with the devil’s behind the glass. I knew her wretched screams would begin filling our ears any moment—knew they would be torture for my kynd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jamus reared back, ready to throw his first fireball. I tried to call out to him, tried to stop him, but it was already too late.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I have replayed this moment in my mind again and again, human. I have tried to discover just how I could’ve acted a moment sooner—could’ve held Jamus back a moment longer—but no matter how many different scenarios I come up with, that fireball still leaves his hands. It still crashes into the qualdrine mirror that has isolated Cifer between realms for over 10,000 years. That mirror still shatters into a thousand pieces, showering down over Norah Aven’s head. And when all the dust settles, I still see, to my shock and horror, Cifer standing there in the middle of all that rubble, a free man for the first time in ten millennia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So now you know, Cifer is free. A qualdrine—my son’s qualdrine—set him free, fulfilling the Gihara’s warning that has kept the villain contained since Phitessnie’s blood sum.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I saw from the disbelief in his eyes that the kynd knew immediately what he had done. He rushed to pull Norah Aven free from the rubble, away from Cifer. Despite the fear and fury roiling up inside, ready to choke me off, I knew I had to act immediately.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And so the battle began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Fire and smoke and dust couldn’t keep me from doing everything in my power to try and help my son undo what he had done.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get her back! Get her back!” I yelled to Jamus. As soon as he and the kyndie were clear, I threw a bolt of lightning in Cifer’s direction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He brushed it away with the swipe of his hand, looking gleeful as he stepped out of the gaping hole where his qualdrine prison had once been. That wicked smile—how I hoped I’d never live long enough to see it again in the flesh. Yet there the villain was, gliding carelessly away from the shambles to fight beside his dogrils once more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced around the room to get my bearings. Jamus had placed himself between the kyndie and danger. Norah was crawling to safety behind a fallen slab. The dogril was scrambling after her. I shot a well-timed lightning bolt in his direction, and the creature disintegrated. One down, three more to go.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Fireball after fireball hurtled toward me, causing me to duck just in time. The Dappled Brown—who was once Detective Narson—was prowling somewhere to my right in the cloud of smoke, rubble, and dust. I sent another lightning bolt his way, blasting a pillar. It began to tip.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Move kyndie! Now!” I shouted. Just in time, too. The pillar crashed to the stone floor, barely missing the kyndie’s ankles.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I dove for cover against one side of the altar. Cifer fought alongside the she-dogril and the Dappled. Flame, spark, and fireballs erupted in every direction, blasting stone and sending shards of crystal all over.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I kept my eyes on Jamus. He was fighting Cifer. Fear gripped me, and yet I couldn’t help but acknowledge how phenomenal the kynd had become with his fire. I saw my Alo in his every move.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet Cifer continued to strike away every blow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The she-dogril sent a burst of flame my way, forcing me to my stomach, and I circled the altar on hands and knees for a better angle. Norah Aven was taking cover there. The she-dogril was aiming for the kyndie. I stood up and sent a bolt of lightning back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What did you find kyndie?” I called.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean?” she shrieked, covering her head as another stone slab crashed to the floor nearby.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Haven?” I said, coughing as dust swirled around. “What did you find about the Haven?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—I thought I found it,” she panted, uncovering her head, “but . . .” her voice cracked. “It exploded.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked down at her. “Exploded? What do you mean? Is that all that happened?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She shook her head, and between several more bolts of lightning, which resulted in the obliteration of the Dappled Brown, she told me how the bejeweled sunburst had risen off the stone and mysteriously disappeared into her chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help staring at her when she said this. “It vanished once it touched your skin?” I asked, to be sure she’d said what I thought she’d said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re certain?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now, Dad!” Jamus’ shout came from across the room. I turned just in time to send a bolt of lightning at the same instant he shot a ball of blue flames. They simultaneously hit the she-dogril full force in the chest. The electricity spread its spindly sparks all over her body while the flames devoured her hair. She screeched and thrashed in pain. Then her body was a giant ball of flame running through the altar room until it vanished through the gaping hole where Cifer’s mirror prison had once been.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The three dogrils were gone. Cifer stood outnumbered. He bowed mockingly in Norah Aven’s direction. “Until we meet again, fairest Norah,” he purred.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before either Jamus or I could act, the devil dove into the gaping hole after his she-dogril.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer was gone, but I knew he was heading straight for Cobbogoth. We needed to act fast. But my mind was still reeling from what the kyndie had just told me. If what she’d said was true, if the Haven had truly vanished when it touched her…but it was impossible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to face her then. I needed answers, and quickly. “By the Gihara, kyndie. You’ve got some explaining to do,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Norah Aven smiled at me sheepishly, then held up a pale green qualdrine by way of explanation.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was all the explanation I needed to fully comprehend what had happened when she touched the Haven stone. But the kyndie, she wouldn’t comprehend the significance of what had just happened to her until I gave her an explanation of my own.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: The Albine & Space Shifters","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"44e7573cb9d04a37aef8c061cddbbbcd"}]},{"n":23,"letterTitle":"The Battle for Cobbogoth","narrator":"James","numword":"Twenty-Three","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Blood Sum","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I think I’ve made it pretty clear by now that I hate secrets—even the good ones, like finding out my girl is a Cobbogothian like me. You’re probably wondering how in the world I could hate knowing that, but it’s not the knowing. It’s the timing. Knowing my girl is a Cobbogothian—that there was nothing standing in the way of us going back to Cobbogoth and to our people together—couldn’t have come at a worse time. Why? Because I still had one last secret to tell, a secret I was hoping I’d never have to tell. But now that it was necessary, telling this secret would change everything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You say the Haven vanished once it touched you, kyndie?” I heard my dad ask Nor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded as I examined her pale green qualdrine medallion. I was still grappling with the fact that Nor and I had lived across the street from each other for ten years and never once suspected the other was a Cobbogothian.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad took her by the shoulders. “I don’t want to seem forward, but will you show me your chest?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…” she said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dad!” I dropped Nor’s medallion, pulling myself back to the present, and stepped between the two of them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He moved me aside, rolling his eyes. “I mean her collarbone, kynd. I want to see the place where the Haven symbol vanished.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, sure,” Nor said, looking relieved. She pulled back her collar a little.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad’s eyes widened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hey! When did you get a tattoo?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tattoo?” Nor looked down. “Holy Hades!” The “tattoo” was a golden, seven-pronged sunburst, about the size of a silver dollar, and it was emblazoned on her chest just a little below her collarbone. I recognized the symbol of the Haven. There were even tiny elemental gems embedded into her pale skin at the tip of each of the seven prongs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara!” Dad breathed. “I had my suspicions you were too smart to be human, but . . .”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You did? Why didn’t you say anything to me?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No point in getting your hopes up,” he said, not looking at me. “Do you know what this means, kyndie?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor only shrugged. “That my entire life has been a lie?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad’s brow furrowed. “No. It means nineteen years of searching this city is finally over. You’ve found the Haven.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She shook her head. “But I told you what I thought was the Haven exploded before I could find out what was inside. And wait! How are you even able to see this mark?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t you see?” Dad grinned at us both. “It means you are the Haven. And since it’s Jamus’s mission to return the Haven to our people, the Gihara must be allowing us to see it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked from Dad to Nor and then back again. “I’m confused. How can she be the Haven?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because she’s a space shifter, my kynd! Her manifestation is space—she absorbs matter, which means she has absorbed the contents of the Haven.” He pointed at her tattoo. “That mark proves I’m right. Also, what happened last summer between the two of you—the incident that caused Jack to put you in Dunstan Academy, kyndie—makes perfect sense now as well. The reason why Norah reacted to your kiss the way she did last summer is because she literally absorbed the fire from your blood, but without a qualdrine to temper the effect, it nearly did her in.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor looked dumbfounded. “But I thought space shifters were super rare—like only born once every couple of millennia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right. Only in your case, there hasn’t been a space shifter born amongst our people in over 12,000 years. Don’t you see? That’s why Totherma sent you here as a baby. Not only were you the only one who could find the Haven, but being a space shifter, you’re also the only one who could bring it back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But I thought that was James’s mission,” Nor said. “He’s the one who was prophesied to return the Haven to your—er—our people.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The more they talked, the sicker I felt. Nor was right. I was the one prophesied to return the Haven to the Cobbogothians. It was my destiny to return it by whatever means necessary. The Gihara had told me as much during my Yvaine-i-Gihara, and then they’d sworn me to secrecy by placing me under a silentium oath—the very same oath that had taken the life of Dr. Lukens a couple of days ago as forfeit simply for breaking it. If I’d ever doubted whether the Gihara were serious about the oath, his death was proof they were.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James?” Nor asked, jarring me from my thoughts. “What’s wrong?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to her and, for a moment, just looked at her. She’d always been beautiful to me, but there was a subtle difference in her now—it was that “ethereal” quality that Dad said took over every Cobbogothian after they qualified for an activated qualdrine. I would’ve loved her as long as she lived, no matter what. But knowing I could’ve loved her my entire life—my very long Cobbogothian life—changed everything. If only I hadn’t been so reckless. If only I’d paid more attention when I was trying to save her from Cifer. If only I hadn’t broken his prison open with my qualdrine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nothing! I’m happy,” I said, pulling her into my arms. “How could I not be? You’re safe, and we have the Haven now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She glanced over at my dad, then back at me with a wink. Then she said, “I think it also means you can kiss me without putting me in a mental hospital.” She smiled at me then, but I didn’t feel like smiling back. Instead, I took her face in my hands and kissed her—kissed her like I’d never kissed her before—like I might never be able to kiss her again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She wrapped her arms around my neck and kissed me back. I heard my dad clear his throat—sensed him turn away. Bless him. But even if he hadn’t, I wouldn’t have cared.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally pulled apart, I pressed my forehead to hers.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa!” she breathed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I love you, Norah Aven Lukens. Whatever happens, always remember that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Why are you being so mysterious all of a sudden?” she asked, grasping my forearms to steady herself. She was starting to look worried, and I knew she sensed things were off.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, Jamus?” Dad asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned and saw him glancing back and forth between me and the hole Cifer and the she-dogril had just disappeared through. I knew it wouldn’t take him long to catch on.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We don’t have a lot of time,” I said. “The prison is broken. That’s just a gaping hole into the dark matter realm. Anything can come and go if we leave it. It’s got to be closed, and soon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad gave me a long, hard look. He looked paler as the realization of what I was saying settled in. Then, for the first time in my life, I saw the old man tear up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right, fellas,” Nor said. “You’re really starting to freak me out. Somebody better tell me what’s going on.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad gave me a stiff nod, and I took Nor by the hand, leading her toward the altar. When we got to it, I paused. How could I find the right words? How was I supposed to tell her that the future we both hoped for wasn’t going to happen because I’d messed up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, James?” Nor asked gently. “No more secrets. You can tell me anything now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I kissed her forehead and decided she was right. No more secrets.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did you know that ‘Jamus’ means something in ancient Cobbogothian?” I began. Might as well ease her into knowing the truth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, what?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It means ‘Deliverer.’ I was given that name to remind me of my destiny.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took her by the shoulders then and held her away from me so I could look her in the eyes. Those soft, searching, pale green eyes looked right back at me. “Nor, my destiny is to safely deliver the Haven back to Cobbogoth. No matter the cost.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, so let’s do it. I’ve got the Haven,” she said, pointing at the tattoo.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Unfortunately, it’s not going to be that easy. See, I just made things wicked complicated when I busted Cifer out of prison.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How complicated?” Nor asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well…I’ve got to seal up that hole to the dark matter realm. We’ve got to protect Earth from more dingbats coming in. Then I’ve got to open a safe portal into Cobbogoth’s realm so we can get you back there. In order to do both of those things as quickly as we need them done, I’m going to have to…” I cleared my throat. “I’ve got to pay…a blood sum.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry?” Nor blinked rapidly, probably mentally sifting through her uncle’s journal to try and understand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s the only way to keep Cifer from returning to Earth, Nor. Humans aren’t like us—they wouldn’t stand a chance against an army of dogrils. You saw how quickly they possessed Narson. The entire race would be extinct in a matter of months.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t understand,” Nor said, eyes wide. “Why can’t we just head to New Cobbogoth through that? Isn’t that where Cifer is going?” She gestured to the gaping hole in the qualdrine mirror. “Or couldn’t you just contact Totherma through a cairn? Have her open up a safer way or something?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That hole isn’t a portal, kyndie,” Dad intervened. “Think of it as a ‘gateway to hell,’ the way Icelanders have thought of the volcano this city is built under for centuries. Turns out they were right about a lot more than the rest of the human world believes. Cifer’s ill-begotten powers enable him to find his way through the dark realm and into Cobbogoth because that’s where his powers and his dogrils come from. But if you or I were to travel through the dark realm without Totherma’s or the Gihara’s help, we wouldn’t survive it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But I’m a space shifter too, right? Can’t I do that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad almost looked sorry for Nor. “Oh kyndie, entering the dark matter realm takes a great deal of training and practice. I’m afraid that isn’t an option.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So let’s get in touch with Totherma,” Nor said. “I saw plenty of communocite pillars on my way to the temple.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s no time for that, Nor. That hole needs to be sealed up now,” I added. “The only way to do that and open a portal into Cobbogoth is by asking the Gihara for help.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And the way you ask the Gihara for help is…?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“With a blood sum,” Dad finished, and his voice cracked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But what do you mean when you say ‘a blood sum’?” she asked, the seriousness of the situation beginning to set in. “Are we talking like a Phitessnie blood sum? The kind you don’t come back from? Or are we talking a pricked finger sort of blood tithe?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced at my dad for help.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s a lot we don’t known about a blood sum, kyndie,” he said. “But yes, it has to be like Phitessnie’s…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mean he won’t survive it?” she asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad gave a sharp nod but wouldn’t meet her eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” Nor said. “No way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m not asking for your permission, Nor,” I said it as gently as I could. But she needed to know the truth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’re talking about a blood sacrifice!” she cried, moving between me and the altar. “We’re talking about your life! There has to be another way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There isn’t another way, kyndie!” Dad said. “And we’re running out of time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She spun on him then. “You’re willing to sacrifice your own son?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dad suddenly looking all his 10,000+ years. “If I could pay the blood sum instead, I would do it right now with no regrets, kyndie. I’ve waited so long to see my Alo, and this sacrifice for my people would be an honor. But I can’t. When the Gihara made me a Naridi, they changed my blood.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then take me!” Nor cried. “If James dies, I won’t have anyone left either.” Tears were streaming down her cheeks now, and the sight was breaking my heart.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We can’t, kyndie. You’re the Haven.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A sob tore through her. “No!” she cried, shaking her head. “Just…no!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took her hand in mine then. She was shaking almost as bad as me. “This is what I was born to do, Nor. I’m just sorry we didn’t have more time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No!” she sobbed. “We’re supposed to be together! We share a kinzura like your dad and mom!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A kinzura?” I asked. I glanced over at Dad.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded and shrugged, helpless. “It’s true. Gram suspected it even before we knew the kyndie was Cobbogothian.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I remembered the letter Gram wanted me to deliver to Norah if she ever happened to find out what I was. Gram had never mentioned anything about a kinzura bond to me. I suddenly felt like I’d been kicked in the chest, but then I shook my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It doesn’t matter,” I said, more for myself than anyone else. “It will all work out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reached down and picked up a large, sharp shard of crystal from the altar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Norah’s eyes widened in horror. “Don’t you dare,” she cried, moving forward to take the crystal shard from me. “You said you love me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t think my girl was going to accept this, no matter how long we talked it out. And we were running out of time. “I do, Nor, more than I’ve ever loved anyone or anything. But that’s why I need to do this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She stopped suddenly, her eyes even wider with sudden realization. “That’s what Uncle Jack meant in his letter. ‘Trust that it will all work out,’” she murmured. “He meant this?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Another sob tore from her, and she dropped her hands, no longer trying to get the shard of crystal from me. Our eyes met, and I knew she was resigned to it. She would let me pay the blood sum. Thank goodness for Dr. Lukens and whatever he’d said in that letter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reached out and touched her cheek, then kissed her one last time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wherever you go, whatever happens, I love you, James.” It was the last thing she said to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then I did it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There are several ways to pay a blood sum. All of them are necessary in their own way, but I knew this one had to be different. It had to be complete. I couldn’t just cut my finger and call it good. I had to show the Gihara I was willing to give it all up to save my people.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I pulled the crystal shard out of my stomach—saw the amount of blood—I knew it would be enough.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Shock started to set in as my blood splashed down on the white stone altar. I couldn’t help thinking it looked like mini crimson fireworks exploding in a bright white sky. Nor steadied me as I climbed up onto it and laid down. I looked at my girl then. She was wringing her hands, tears streaming down her cheeks as she shook her head. The sight of it was more painful than my wound, but I couldn’t have been prouder. She was handling this like a champ—like a true Bostonian, even though neither of us actually were…Boston—er—was it Cobbogothian?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My thoughts were starting to scramble. My vision began to dim. I could just make out my dad, face twisted in pain and pressing the heel of one hand to his eyes. Then the altar room was gone, vanished without a trace. All I could see was darkness, but weirdly, I could still hear. It sounded like everything was coming to me from the other end of a long tunnel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where?” I heard Norah gasp in the distance. “Where did he go?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Take cover, kyndie!” Dad’s voice shouted, but it sounded like a whisper. I heard shuffling feet followed by deafening silence.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then the explosion came. It roared out from every direction, as time and space were torn apart. Blinding, excruciating light split the darkness around me, and the last thing I remembered was two silhouetted figures stepping out of the light to stand over me.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: Opalian Eye & Blood Sum","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"261e34fff0f24e348b1c95b6a3c040fc"}]},{"n":24,"letterTitle":"Yvaine-i-Gihara","narrator":"Norah","numword":"Twenty-Four","pieces":[{"so":1,"title":"Yvaine-i-Gihara","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Even if there were words to express what I felt when James vanished on the altar, I wouldn’t use them. Some pain is just too sharp, too deep, too all-consuming, to put into words. All I can say is that a very tangible piece of me tore away with him. Perhaps that’s what it means to share kinzura.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After the altar room exploded, it wasn’t until the dust settled that I saw the two figures—a woman and a man—in the slowly diminishing light. They stood in the rubble of the altar room in front of Lylend and me. I knew straightaway what they were, certain they were the most magnificent beings I had ever seen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend immediately dropped to his knees before the Gihara, and I averted my eyes. I somehow couldn’t help myself. I sensed the woman Gihara looking at me, so I peeked up through my lashes and met stunning but peculiar eyes. She held out a radiant hand, motioning us forward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, Gihara or not, I didn’t want to go. Whoever these beings were, they were the reason James—my James—was gone. But Lylend stood and beckoned me forward anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Gihara looked human in a lot of ways, but they were unlike any people I’d ever seen. Against the stark whiteness of their clothing, their skin looked like flawless, iridescent porcelain. Their eyes were fringed with white brows and white lashes, their hair just as white. The woman’s hair fell in undulating waves and intricately woven coils all around to her hips. The man’s reached his shoulders. He was dressed in a simply cut, gold-embroidered robe. The woman wore a white gauzy shawl—like spun glass—over a gown that shimmered like it was woven from a billion strands of diamond thread.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Come closer.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I jumped at a voice in my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*Do not be frightened*, she said. *We are friends to Cobbogoth*. Her voice was quiet and calming, but I hadn’t seen the woman’s lips move.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend must’ve heard the same thing because he moved closer. I followed him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Gihara looked beautiful and bright from a distance, but as we came up next to them, it was clear just how inferior we were in comparison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman’s eyes stayed fixed on me. They were so penetrating that I almost looked away. But I couldn’t. Her eyes were strange. They weren’t like human eyes or even Cobbogothian eyes when they glowed. It was almost like her eyes were made from precious stone—her lashes crystallized and the irises like perfectly cut diamonds. Even her pupils were different, clear and golden like the qualdrines on her clothing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Qualdrine eyes? Was that what it meant to be a Gihara?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*We come in answer to a blood sum*, the woman continued, still speaking in our minds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right, Great Ones. It was my son—” Lylend’s voice was rough, and he cleared his throat. Then he relayed everything that had happened, including Cifer’s escape and my becoming the Haven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All I could do was stare through fresh tears at the rubble near my feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is this true, Noriah?” the woman asked, this time speaking aloud.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Both of the Gihara’s eyes flashed to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman took a few steps, then paused beside the altar. It was the only thing still standing after the explosion. With one hand, she motioned upward, and a layer of fine crystal dust puffed into the air, hovering. The motion left the altar clean and glistening white, except for the still wet splashes of James’ scarlet blood on its surface.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Jamus has done very well,” she said, turning to her companion. “But we knew he would.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The man gave a faint, reflective smile. There was something strangely familiar about him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman turned back to me. “You have done exceptionally well too, Noriah. The Gihara are very pleased.” She smiled, and it was so bright, so beautiful, I couldn’t think straight for a minute.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you,” I finally said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Everything has played out precisely to its purpose,” she continued. “Just as it was always meant to play out. And you—you have saved everything, dear one.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mean James has saved everything?” I asked as I wiped my eyes and dared to glare up at her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No. You have,” she continued, unruffled by my contempt. She probably could have snuffed me out with the wave of her hand, but she didn’t. In fact, she continued to smile at me the way I’d seen mothers smile at their young children.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You were clever enough to find the Haven before it was too late, you see. Had you allowed the dogrils to find it first or even Jamus or Lylend to touch it before you did, it would have destroyed itself, and the secrets of qualdriny would have been lost to your people forever. But because you are a space shifter, you absorbed the Haven’s contents and will return the secret art of qualdriny to your people.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And James?” I asked feeling a bit humbler. “Do you know what’s happened to him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman’s perfect brow furrowed, and again she glanced at her companion. “You and Jamus share a particular kinzura that we have been watching for a long time, Noriah. It is a peculiar one, even among Cobbogothians. You see, even before you were born, your souls were ever gravitating toward one another, pulling the other into close proximity. Special care was often necessary to keep you from welding your souls together into one.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Welding?” I asked. “What do you mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We have always created souls in pairs to keep balance in the universe, but rarely have they bonded before their time as the two of you have.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why were James and I different than other kinzura?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman considered me a moment. “Because your destinies were already intertwined. Since your births, arrangements were made by the Opalian Eye and at our command to align yours and Jamus’s Yvaine-i-Giharas so the successful completion of one would mean the success of the other.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My Yvaine-i-Gihara?” I looked at Lylend. “But I haven’t gone on my Yvaine-i-Gihara yet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman smiled. “Oh, but you have.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ve been on Yvaine-i-Gihara since the moment you received your medallion,” the man said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And we’ve been walking with you ever since,” the woman added, touching my arm, causing an electric warmth to seep into me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Was it when I faced Cifer?” I asked. “Or was it when I found and absorbed the Haven?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Those were both a small part of it,” she said. “But we knew that neither of those would be a true test for you. Your Uncle Jack knew it as well, which is why he left you the counsel he did in his letter. He foresaw what we knew about you from your creation, that your test had to depend not on your life but upon Jamus’s.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t understand.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course not, but I shall try to explain it to you anyway. The moment you let Jamus pay the blood sum, Noriah, is the moment you passed your Yvaine-i-Gihara.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stared at her, not quite certain I was hearing correctly. “So you’re saying my test was whether or not I would let him die?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. Jamus’s life meant more to you than anything else, including your own. Therefore, allowing him to pay the blood sum was the greatest sacrifice you could make for your people.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was speechless. Relief and anger were warring inside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to face Lylend, confused to see him beaming at me, tears sliding down his scarred cheeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But what about James,” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t you see, kyndie?” he whispered, “Yvaine-i-Gihara is just a test.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A test, yes,” the man said, “but unlike any other Yvaine-i-Gihara before yours or Jamus’s, this one was also very real.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced from one Gihara to the other. “I’m confused. Was James’ blood sum a test or was it real?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A blood sum is a curious thing,” the man said. “It is a completely selfless sacrifice of love on behalf of another. In Jamus’s case, many others. Such selfless love is the most powerful force in the universe. And when a blood sum is paid so willingly there is always an excess of power. In this particular case, since the willingness came from both Jamus and yourself, there was an even greater excess of power leftover.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because of that,” the woman continued, “not only has Jamus’s blood sum afforded us enough power to close the dark matter to earth and open a safe portal to send you back to Cobbogoth, but there is enough power left over to spare Jamus’s life as well.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend drew in a sharp breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gasped. “Wait, what?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Jamus is all right, Noriah. Because you didn’t try to stop him from paying the blood sum—because you were willing to trust that all would work out in the end—the sacrifice wasn’t just his but yours as well. It was a twofold sacrifice that gave us enough power to save him. He’s alive now in New Cobbogoth,” the woman assured me. “You will find him there.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend scooped me up and spun me around. “Well done, kyndie!” he boomed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can’t believe it!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Believe it, for the Gihara do not lie,” the man said, smiling his own dazzling smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To say I completely lost it then and there is an understatement, reader. Never before in my life had I gone from feeling such complete and utter despair to inconceivable bliss. The only thing that could’ve made me happier is if James had suddenly reappeared, alive and unharmed, right there on the altar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And now it is time to open the portal and return you to New Cobbogoth,” said the man when I finally pulled myself back together again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” agreed the woman. “Lylend, Noriah, it is time for you to go home and help your people stop Cifer and his dogrils once and for all.” And then she paused, exchanged a furtive look with the man and said, “for the fate of more than one world may depend upon it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend bowed low to the Gihara. “Our deepest gratitude, Great Ones.” Then he paused, touching his heart, and I saw him look pleadingly from one to the other.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman smiled understandingly. “Your Alobuenath is well, Lylend. She sends her love and wishes you to give all her love to Jamus when you see him again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My Alo…” Lylend—the brave, stoic, sometimes ornery warrior I had been in awe of the past few days—pressed a hand to his mouth and squeezed his eyes tight against the emotion welling there. “Thank you,” was all he managed to say.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The woman nodded, stepping back and taking her place beside the man. She motioned, and Lylend and I turned to where Cifer’s qualdrine prison had been. Instead of the gaping hole he and his she-dogril had jumped through, we found an oozing waterfall of glowing molten lava. There was no portal in sight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Earth is safe from the dark realm once more,” the man said. “But for how long, I cannot say. The time may come when some of you will need to return and fulfill the ancient prophesy given to Cobbogoth of old.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But let us not talk of that now,” said the woman. “For today is a victory, and it is time for them to leave this realm.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The man nodded his consent, but his eyes never left my own. I would be lying if I didn’t admit that this peculiar exchange worried me a little.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The worry was gone an instant later however, as, standing side by side, the Gihara held out their hands. They each grasped a peculiarly shaped stone. Turning toward one another, they brought the stones together until they connected, creating what looked like a peculiarly designed Celtic knot. The joined stone began to rotate, and they lowered their hands, watching as the stone continued to hover in midair. After a few seconds, the knot unraveled itself and began to expand into a doorway of light, just like Lylend’s rodãor had done. The doorway grew rapidly until it hovered at least fifteen feet tall in the air in front of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come, Bandier,” the man called.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At his command, something moved into the massive doorway—something equally as massive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I inched closer to Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The something stepped forward, crouching through the ring of light, causing the altar room to tremble a little. Once inside the room, the magnificent creature spread his wings, dwarfing us all as they touched from one end of the room to the other. Towering over us all, the creature glowered down with fierce amber eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I realized what it was, my jaw dropped in disbelief.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A Basili!” Lylend breathed, taking the words right out of my gaping mouth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Uncle Jack’s journal had said the Basili were the special messengers of the Gihara.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Bandier will carry you safely to New Cobbogoth,” the woman Gihara explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?” I swallowed, my voice higher than usual. “Sure you can’t just zap us there or something?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Bandier shook his head and rolled his golden eyes. His dark silver braid slid across his back with the motion. His skin was a warm bronze, and the copper of his feathers made him look like he was made completely out of precious metals.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend chuckled, and it was then I realized I was using him as a human—er Cobbogothian—shield.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry about that,” I said, finding my courage and stepping out from behind him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara, I won’t eat you Noriah,” the creature boomed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Duly noted,” I croaked. “And thank you very much.” In the end, I supposed if this was the only way to get to James, I was just going to have to deal with it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on then, kyndie,” Lylend said, coming up beside me. “Let’s go find my tyro. The kynd won’t know his way around Cobbogoth without me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitantly took the hand Lylend offered as we stepped beside Bandier. To be honest, I’d had enough flying for a lifetime after being kidnapped by the dogrils. But this was my ride into Cobbogoth. So despite my shaking legs, I managed to let Lylend hoist me onto Bandier’s back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he was situated behind me, Lylend grasped a handful of the Basili’s feathers, and Bandier turned. I grabbed hold of some feathers as well to keep from falling off.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we reached the giant doorway of light, I glanced back toward Earth one last time. I felt a pang at leaving the only home I’d ever known. But what was there to go back to? Everything I wanted and loved lay before me, not behind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, at the Gihara’s bidding, Bandier stepped into the doorway of light. It was pitch blackness on the other side, but I could still see the Basili spread his giant wings. And then he began to run.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A scream tore through me as the ground fell out from beneath us. Soon we were soaring into the darkness. My heart beat loudly in my ears and my stomach clenched as I thought of the unknown dangers, adventures, and wonders ahead of me. But none of that mattered as long as each beat of Bandier’s wings brought me closer to Cobbogoth and to James—to my true home.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"so":2,"title":"Journal: Gihara & Basili","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4ff6f41345d6445194890e63f14c77d7"},{"so":3,"title":"News: More Truth in Icelandic Myths","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a44a6b0e9324450da92c560505b4db27"}]}]},{"part":2,"element":"Water","chronicles":[{"n":25,"letterTitle":"From Starlyt Sea to Drusy Path","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Origin & Myth","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2ea9e61e67d24947b4bc7370a4cb2c9b"},{"title":"From Starlyt Sea to Drusy Path","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I know what you’re going to ask, and unfortunately the answer is no. No, I haven’t found James yet. He wasn’t waiting for me with open arms on the other side of the portal like I hoped. Apparently when the Gihara said we’d find James in Cobbogoth, they literally meant we’d have to find him. But don’t worry, you’ll be the first to know when I do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A ton—and I mean seriously a ton—has happened since I woke up in Cobbogoth. (That’s right, I totally passed out on Bandier’s back going through the portal. Super embarrassing.) Thankfully the Basili didn’t eat me when he discovered I also puked all over his feathers, not that you’d want to eat someone who just puked on you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, it turns out Bandier is a pretty decent guy—er—Basili. He was there when I woke up. I was kinda freaking out, as I’m sure you can imagine, because I couldn’t find Lylend anywhere. I was all disoriented because the place I woke up in made it feel and look like I was suspended in the middle of outer space or something. For a minute, I honestly thought something had gone wrong and I’d fallen off Bandier mid-transport through the Gihara’s portal. My first thought was that I’d somehow gotten stuck out there between realms in the great unknown. Turns out the portal had spit us out into a place called the Starlyt Sea Cave—pretty much exactly what it sounds like. The Starlyt Sea is a relatively small underground sea in a black cave where the walls, ceiling, and seafloor are studded with an insane amount of white glowing stones. It was a trip waking up there, I’ll tell you, but I calmed down enough to actually appreciate my first encounter with Cobbogoth once Bandier explained where I was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It didn’t last long though because that’s when Bandier told me Lylend had abandoned me there. Okay, he didn’t use those exact words, but it definitely felt like abandonment. Thankfully, the Basili managed to calm me down again before I walked off the very tiny island we were standing on (which happened to be in the middle of the Starlyt Sea). He assured me everything would be okay, even if I fell in, because there wasn’t anything living in the water except something called Brawlo (whatever that is). Then he told me Lylend had been called away on “urgent Naridi business,” and I was to not only find my way out of the cave but to make my way down the mountain range to another sea where Lylend would inevitably meet back up with me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Confusing enough yet? My point exactly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t until Bandier pointed to a single communocite pillar located in the middle of our tiny island that I began to believe everything might actually be okay. He said cell phones wouldn’t work here but gave me the next best thing: a “com stone.” He told me how com stones were like Cobbogothian cell phones—more like phone booths really—and showed me how it worked with the pillar and how to use it to “chime” Lylend if I needed to speak to him before we met back up again. He explained there were communocite pillars all over Cobbogoth, so I could use my com stone to get ahold of Lylend any time, as long as I was standing beside one. Then he warned me that unlike cell phones and text messages on earth, I was only to use a communocite pillar to contact Lylend in an emergency. I thought about asking him if being abandoned by Lylend counted as an emergency but didn’t think he’d appreciate the sarcasm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To his credit, Lylend did leave a few things to help me on my way. One of those things was a change of Cobbogothian clothes called a shyntara and a pair of ranalin. Guess he didn’t want me sticking out among the locals. Unfortunately, he didn’t leave any instructions on how to put the dang things on.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The most useful thing he left was a map of Cobbogoth. He even marked where I was and how I was supposed to get to our meeting point, which I now know is called the Glindian Sea, a fact that would come in handy when I needed to ask the locals (that I wasn’t supposed to talk to) for directions.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, Lylend left me a list of rules to follow until we met back up again. Being raised by Uncle Jack, I wasn’t too thrilled about being told what to do, but thanks to his rule about steering clear of water, I thought to ask Bandier how I was meant to get to shore. The Basili pointed out a small boat and oar tied to the island. Then he pointed out something I hadn’t noticed before. There was a bridge not too far from our island that had been built into some of the larger stalactites and stalagmites of the cave. The bridge was only halfway across the small sea—maybe half a mile out. Once I reached it, I’d be able to climb onto the stairs leading up to it and walk the rest of the way to shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All in all, maybe things weren’t as bad as they originally seemed. Maybe.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, Bandier wished me luck and handed me a little satchel that contained Lylend’s rodãor and a days worth of food. Then the Basili conjured another portal doorway and went back through it to, well, who knows where.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After changing into the ranalin and shytara (hopefully I had them on right), I headed off in my little boat, intent on getting out of the cave before dark.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"About a mile out to sea, I discovered the Basili wasn’t kidding when he said there was at least one thing living in the water. It turned out to be something huge, white, furry, and fanged. The creature swam right past me, and I nearly capsized when it gave my boat a little nudge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully the nudge sent me straight toward the bridge. As soon as I reached it, I quickly scrambled out of the boat and onto the stairs as fast as I could. However, I didn’t even have time to process my encounter with the creature because as soon as I reached the center of the bridge and looked down over the water for a better view of it, I was distracted by something else entirely.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was one of the most incredible things I’ve ever seen, and by now I think we can agree that’s really saying something. At first it looked like an extremely intricate constellation had been designed right there on the seafloor. All the little white glowing stones were arranged into a giant spiral design. There was even a large pale orb out in the distance, looking just like a moon against a watery midnight sky.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It didn’t take long to realize the “constellation” wasn’t a constellation at all but a spiraled sort of message left there by what I assumed were ancient Cobbogothian hands because it was written in ancient Cobbogothian. I’ve included the translation on the next page.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This is where we entered","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Here Cifer finds his end","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When moon and royal blood arrive","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From olden realm again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They twain shall restore qualdriny","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Six keys they twain must find","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before the lost horn can return","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And elements combine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then prophecy shall be fulfilled","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which Cobbogoth foretold","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As we, united, rise again","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To save our home of old.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After translating the literal meaning of the runes, I decided to tuck it away in my mind to ask Lylend about once we met up again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I reached the seashore, I located the exit to the Starlyt Sea Cave and soon found myself in the largest and strangest grove of trees I’ve ever seen. Seriously. You think the redwoods on the northwest coast of America are big? They’ve got nothing on these babies with their white trunks like skyscrapers and roots as thick and gnarled as the Mass Pike running every which way. I stood there in shock and awe for at least five minutes solid, feeling like a tiny ant in a jungle of roots. The strangest part was that the trees had these weeping branches, heavy with broad, blood-red leaves that fell so low I might’ve been able to jump up and grab one. Yet weirdly no leaves had actually fallen on the ground.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Phitessnie’s Blood Grove is what the map called the place. There was a peculiar feeling there. It was obviously an extremely old forest, but there was something else to it, something intangible about the place—almost spiritual. It made me feel like whispering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"According to Lylend’s map, somewhere in this grove was the “invisible” path that was supposed to lead me down to our meeting place at the Glindian Sea.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Believe it or not, of all the things I’ve told you so far, none of them are the craziest things that have happened since arriving here. Not even close. In fact, it wasn’t until I stepped on a weird looking patch of purple light in Phitessnie’s Blood Grove that my whole body suddenly vanished before my eyes. The good news was I’d found the Drusy Path Lylend had instructed me to find. The bad news was it really does make you invisible. Kinda hard to avoid tripping when you can’t see your feet. I’m just saying. Whoever thought of this thing must’ve been infinitely more graceful than I am because I was pretty banged up by the time the sun started going down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After stubbing my toe for the umpteenth time, I was more than ready to call it a day, regardless of all the new and interesting things I was seeing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once inside the rodãor, I decided to visit the record room Lylend mentioned in his rules. It took me a while to find it, but sure enough, I found it down a long, narrow passageway, third pillar to the right of the sofa.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ve been in Lylend’s rodãor a few times already but only in the main circular room. Turns out it’s much bigger than I thought. The record room is kind of like a library—a library four stories high with a cathedral-like ceiling—with cubbies upon cubbies full of crystal scrolls and various stones instead of books. The cubbies are all labeled according to topics, covering anything Cobbogothian you can imagine. Uncle Jack would’ve loved this place—probably did. I was definitely going to be spending a lot of time in there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Or maybe I wasn’t.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From the moment I walked into the record room, I could tell something was off. For one thing, it was super cold in there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After looking around a bit, I made my way up a ramp to the second level. I was exploring some scrolls on the evolution of modern day Cobbogothian dialects when an icy breeze swept up the staircase behind me. At first, I thought it might be some Cobbogothian version of an AC unit—some cooling stone maybe—that was causing the cold breeze. Then suddenly the breeze became a gust and wrapped itself around me, whipping my shyntara and flogging me with my hair.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gasping, I backed up against the cubbies, wondering what in the world was going on. That’s when I caught sight of a mysterious purplish-grey cloud swirling against the ceiling above. I’ll be honest, my first thought was that a dogril had managed to slip into Lylend’s rodãor. Then I remembered I’d seen dogril mist firsthand, and this wasn’t black enough or shimmery enough.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everything grew really still after that, and I watched, holding my breath, as the cloud slowly began to sift downward. Cautiously, I followed it down the ramp until the cloud covered the entire record room floor like a dense fog.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few moments passed, and then the slight breeze picked back up again. I could just make out a disturbance at the center of the fog—a slight mound rising up out of the midpoint causing a rippling effect.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I saw him. At first he was nothing more than a column of purplish-grey, and then he began to take shape.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I scrambled back against the wall but not before an emaciated, four-armed creature materialized, looming over me like a Grim Reaper ready to claim his next victim.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You’d better believe I was out of that rodãor as fast as I could move, dark or not, booking it to the nearest communocite pillar. Whatever that thing was, it definitely qualified as an emergency.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Map of Cobbogoth","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"c5c92be7e35c47fe9fea071c8f13f198"},{"title":"Lylend's Rules","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"42e0fff91d044755b7b2c3f25b228766"},{"title":"Journal: Drusy Path & Shyntara","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0da1fe62600e42899a2f54708e9cd735"},{"title":"Com Stone","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"907e07a46c8b4ca38344ae0dfceb4858"}]},{"n":26,"letterTitle":"Bruxracium","narrator":"Lylend","pieces":[{"title":"Bruxracium","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Yes, it’s true. I left the Kyndie on her own during her first voyage into Cobbogoth. It may seem cruel to your weak constitution, human, but what Noriah Aven—we’re in Cobbogoth now, so let’s call her by her Cobbogothian name—needs to learn, and the sooner the better, is that she is a Cobbogothian. Being a Cobbogothian means being much more capable and resilient than even the most impressive of you humans could ever hope to be. Do you think one reaches 1,000 years old by being a tenderfoot? Of course they don’t! The Gihara made our bodies vigorous and sturdy, our minds firm and resourceful, our nerves as steady as our three moons. (Yes, there are three moons in Cobbogoth.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that wasn’t the only reason I abandoned her so soon. Almost the instant we reentered Cobbogoth’s realm—while the Kyndie was still unconscious from the journey—I received an urgent chime from Totherma through the communocite pillar located on the tiny island in the middle of the Starlyt Sea. The Gihara had warned the Opalian Eye of our coming, and action was required immediately. You see, time has already sped up in Cobbogoth. The Origin Legend is already coming to pass, which means that while we may have left Earth’s realm only a few hours after Cifer, he had already been in Cobbogoth for several months by the time we arrived. Don’t ask me to explain the logistics; time and space are tricky things at the simplest of times.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma’s reports of Cifer’s activities since arriving chilled me to my core. I knew as well as she that there wasn’t a moment to spare. There was tracking to be done, and there were records to be examined in the Naridi archives at Leborana Falls, especially if we hoped to have any advantage over Cifer’s significant progress.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But what about Jamus, you ask? What about my kynd? You may think me callous, but just like I said of Noriah, he’s a Cobbogothian. And unlike the Kyndie, he had two years of preparation prior to entering this realm. He will be all right, and we will meet again in the Gihara’s timing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In my rush to be off, however, I may have made one or two oversights in the little preparation I was able to give Noriah before parting. Initially, I was surprised and somewhat disappointed to receive a chime from her so soon. She had shown great spirit in your realm, which I had felt even before I knew she was a Cobbogothian. Needless to say, I thought she would last longer on her own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But there I was on the last leg of my journey to Leborana Falls, in a rather precarious position, dangling from the side of a waterfall, when her chime came. Frankly, I thought about ignoring it. I really couldn’t afford the interruption. In the end, however, I reasoned that my son would never speak to me again if anything happened to her—neither would Totherma, for that matter, considering the lengths she’d gone to keep Noriah alive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I answered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when she told me of her encounter with Shudder. Like I said, I overlooked a few things in my hurry to be off and neglected to warn her of his presence in my rodãor. It was an honest mistake, human. After all, I have been blissfully free of the old windbag for the past eighteen years. (You live with Shudder as long as I have and try not to call him names. I dare you.) Noriah’s chime reminded me of something I had failed to consider. Now that we were back in Cobbogoth, Shudder would be able to resume his elemie form, something I was wholeheartedly not looking forward to.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m sure you’re quite confused about the term elemie, so I have asked the Kyndie to lend me her excellent notes upon the subject to enlighten you. You’re welcome.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For now, let it suffice to say that Shudder would’ve merely made his presence known in my rodãor as nothing more than a subtle breeze in Earth’s realm. Noriah might have mistaken him as a gust of air conditioning or an unseen fan. Here, however, Shudder took his personified form, which was that of a sylphid. Horrifying looking creatures, sylphids, especially when one comes upon them unawares. I’m surprised the Kyndie forgave me as readily as she did for not forewarning her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the end, I was very glad for her chime. It afforded me the opportunity to give her some light reading assignments. Unlike you may have assumed about me, I do worry over the Kyndie being ill prepared for this realm. There is a great deal of history to understand before she will be able to fully comprehend the state of things here, which is why I suggested she visit my record room in the first place. After her chime, I assigned her to read up on Cobbogothian history, specifically an event called Bruxracium, or “Bruxrac” as it is more commonly known.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the Gihara! I’ll admit I felt a might guilty assigning her that as her first topic of study. There isn’t much written about the event, and what there is was written by an old acquaintance of mine, Arwyndon, “Empirical Historian of the Fifth Age,” as she referred to herself. If only she hadn’t been as dull as dishwater. (And I would know, as I courted her a bit before I met my Alo.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But her writing is impeccably accurate, even if it is dismal enough to put you right to sleep. Then again, in regular conversation she could drone on and on, making the most exciting subject seem mind-numbingly tedious…may she rest in peace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ah well, hopefully the accompanying letters I assigned the Kyndie—letters written and exchanged between the Naridi during the actual happenings of Bruxracium will more than make up for Arwyndon’s tedium.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Go ahead and read them for yourself, human. You may as well know the darker side of our history. I even wager you’ll be able to relate, as many of your current events strike some chilling similarities with Bruxrac.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As for me, I have a rather daunting waterfall to get back to climbing.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Journal: Elemies","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"fc32f6cfe21c410da2dfc955ca4aeb21"},{"title":"Bruxracium","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"7dd7b43191484b7b9ff820d1f1afcc8e"},{"title":"Naridi Letters","kind":"webp","pages":8,"id":"fae8ecf1b423442fb7522c1a0f5b4ae4"}]},{"n":27,"letterTitle":"Brawlo and the Glorian Pass","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Glorianite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"19812f86402e417e8dca5b48f876c821"},{"title":"Brawlo and the Glorian Pass","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So Lylend wasn’t kidding about the elemies. They’re pretty much the worst. Shudder is by far the most miserable and unpleasant creature I’ve ever encountered. Yesterday, I woke to him hovering over my bed with a breakfast tray. He’s never made me breakfast before. In fact, he’s not shy about letting me know how much he detests doing anything for me, especially cleaning up after me. So far that has only entailed one dirty dish and a pair of muddy footprints. Honestly, I didn’t grow up with Uncle Jack as my ever-absent guardian and not learn how to take care of myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Consequently, when Shudder brought me breakfast in bed, I was leery. Turns out I was perfectly reasonable to be so. “Breakfast” was a bowl of the most disgusting purplish-gray slugs I’ve ever seen. They were swimming in a gray gruel that reminded me very much of the cadavers Samir took me to see at Harvard when we were studying anatomy. I may have thrown up a little in my mouth. Shudder, of course, assured me it was a Cobbogothian delicacy called Yillancha. I may have just arrived in Cobbogoth, but I wasn’t born yesterday. I definitely wasn’t dumb enough to put anything in my mouth until I was certain it was edible first, especially when it looked like that and came from the likes of Shudder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Needless to say, I’ve since been cooking for myself and avoiding Shudder by staying outside as much as I can, which is actually what led to my worst encounter with the elemies yet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since I had been avoiding the rodãor as much as possible the past two days, I was really starting to smell bad. I can’t seem to find a shower of any kind in Lylend’s rodãor, and since he warned me to avoid water, I hadn’t bathed since—well, gosh, I think the last time I showered was the night Uncle Jack died, so we’re going on about a week now. Yeesh! Anyway, Cobbogothians and humans at least have bad B.O. in common, and it was time for me to do something about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Drusy Path happened to be passing along a little pond with a small hill that led up to it and an outcropping of crystal that created a little alcove with a ledge leaning over the water. I thought I could step off the path for just a little bit, conceal myself in the alcove, and give myself at least a sponge bath to tide me over until I met up with Lylend. I honestly didn’t think it would be a big deal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And it wasn’t at first. I’d just finished scrubbing with a bit of what smelled and looked like eucalyptus I miraculously found near the edge of the pond and had slipped back into my ranalin. As I finished buttoning my shyntara, I heard something in the water. I made my way back to the edge of the crystal ledge and peeked down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A cloud of brightly colored, feathery-finned fish caught my eye. Lylend honestly couldn’t expect me to not stop and get a closer look of at least some of these creatures I was seeing for the first time in my life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The fish dashed out of a thicket made by a bowing tree near the edge of the pond. I watched them trailing one another toward a crystal island near the center of the pond. Then one stopped suddenly, like it had snagged on something. Thrashing around, it struggled to free itself, making the pond surface ripple. It was only about a foot away from me, so I crouched down to get a closer look and see if I couldn’t help free it. Just as I reached for it, however, the fish got loose and scuttled away into a forest of what looked like giant lily pad vines.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was still looking after it when a pillar of water ripped through the surface of the pond and drenched me. Sputtering, I stumbled back from the edge. I wiped the water from my eyes just in time to see a wisp of a figure dashing behind a crystal cluster on the crystal island that feathery fish had been swimming toward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Stunned, I inched along the edge of the alcove, careful not to make a sound. I just wanted to see if I’d actually seen what I thought I had. I had to squint due to the glare on the shimmering surface, but sure enough, scattered between several other crystal crevices on the island were five or six little girls…girls made entirely of water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nymphs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Each little girl was clothed in a dress made of all sorts of plants, with flowers woven throughout their water hair. The sound of their tinkling laughter sounded just like a bubbling brook. They were playing a game that included tossing fruit into each other’s mouths before diving back into the pond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was absolutely mesmerizing. Before I realized how far I was leaning over the crystal ledge, my hand slipped on the wet crystal, and I hit the water with a splash. I didn’t even fall in, but in the end it didn’t matter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked back at the island to see if the nymphs had heard me, but the island was empty. Goosebumps raised up on my arms and neck like a warning, and I braced myself. The next moment, it was like I stepped into a watery war zone. I didn’t even see them coming! The first spear of water hit me right in the face. After three more, I had completely toppled off my crystal ledge into the shallow pond. So much for Lylend’s warning about staying away from water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, the little nymphs were on me, taking turns cannonballing on top of my head. Their bubbling laughter had turned menacing, and their attack became so relentless I couldn’t get my head above the surface.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to swim away from them. I’ve been a strong swimmer ever since James taught me how at Walden Pond our first summer as friends. But there were no demon nymphs out at Walden, and try as I might to get away and break the surface, there was always one more little water girl ready to cannonball my head back under.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right about then, I felt a large movement happen in the water around me. The sand from the bottom of the pond stirred up, clouding my vision even more. In panic, I let out the little air I did have, pushing back when I felt something soft like fur brush my arm. Then Something sharp—the weasel’s sabretooth?—slipped through the hood of my shyntara. The next thing I knew I was being catapulted up out of the water and onto the shore by a force so powerful it knocked every bit of water I’d just swallowed right out of me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Next, there was a loud hissing, followed by an ear-piercing trilling sound. I rolled onto my back and froze as a pair of the deepest, blackest, biggest eyes I’d ever seen looked back at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A giant snow-white weasel, as tall as and longer than a horse, stood on its hind legs, hissing and bearing sharp saber teeth at what now appeared to be over 50 water nymphs gathering along the shore of the pond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was on my feet and running before I had time to think about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t get far before what felt like the combination of a stampede and an earthquake came pounding after me. Before I could find one of the cool beams of light indicating the Drusy Path, the weasel had, well, weaseled himself beneath me, and I was no longer running but was riding the terrifying creature!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He was running so fast I couldn’t even think about jumping off. The trees blurred past me like I was riding the T back in Boston. I didn’t know what to do! We were heading away from where I thought the Drusy Path was. What if the weasel took me so far off track I couldn’t find my way back? What if I missed meeting up with Lylend? More importantly, what if this thing ate me?!?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just as I thought this, words as clear as if someone had spoken them sprang into my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"NO WORRY. NO EAT. TASTE TERRIBLE!","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I froze. Where had that come from?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"BRAWLO.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo? Brawlo as in the thing Bandier said was living in the Starlyt Sea? I wondered in response.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"NO LIVE IN SEA. ONLY LOOKING FOR NORIAH.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait, what?” I heard myself say aloud.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Suddenly the weasel slowed until he came to a stop. I glanced behind us. There was no sign of the stampeding nymphs. We’d outrun them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The weasel lowered its head, indicating I should get off. I did, gladly, and was considering how far I’d make it before he pounced and took my head off when the weasel nuzzled his giant nose under my arm, nudging until my hand rested on its snout.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*BRAWLO HERE TO HELP.* I heard the voice in my mind again and then felt an overwhelming sense of pride rush over me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Y-you are Brawlo?” I asked. The fact that I was even considering the weasel as the source of the voice was a testament to how weird things had been since arriving in Cobbogoth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The weasel pushed his nose into my hand again. *YES. MY LADY SEND TO HELP.*","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your lady? Who is that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*TOTHERMA.* The rush of love and worshipful devotion that came with the name was so intense it made my eyes sting and I was forced to blink back tears. Then I realized I wasn’t just hearing the weasel in my mind, I was feeling his feelings too.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Y-you know Totherma?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*MY LADY.* The weasel confirmed. *KNOW YOU TOO. SHE TELL ME OF YOU.* This time the emotion was one of curiosity and…awe?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help smiling at him now. In fact, the longer I stood there gaping at this giant, saber-tooth weasel, the more he went from terrifying to…dare I say, adorable?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*BRAWLO HERE TO HELP,* he insisted again. Then, without any warning, he leaned in, pressing his head against my forehead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dear reader, I don’t know how to explain it to you, but from that moment on, not only did I trust him, but I was completely smitten with him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, unbelievable as it sounds, there I was, empathically bonding and telepathically communicating with a giant, saber-toothed weasel named Brawlo. I told you things just keep getting weirder around here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After establishing the fact that Brawlo was not here to eat me, he informed me that Totherma had sent him to carry me through what was supposed to be the most dangerous part of my journey on my way to meet up with Lylend. According to Brawlo, this most dangerous part consisted of a mysterious canyon called The Glorian Pass. (See my included sketch.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took us a few more hours to get there, but when we did, I understood why I needed Brawlo’s help. The Glorian Pass was a narrow canyon made almost entirely of a peculiar, mottled crystal called glorianite. As we traveled through it, Brawlo explained that the place had once been an interdimensional center of travel in Cobbogoth, which, to be honest, sounded kind of like an airport terminal. (See my journal notes for more info on the Glorian Pass.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, several of the gates were now unstable to the point that it was no longer safe to even travel along the Drusy Path near the pass without taking precautions. I’ll pause here a moment to say if you haven’t gathered yet from what Lylend’s article about Bruxrac has to say about this place, remember it, because it’s important. That’s why I took the trouble of sketching it for you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As Brawlo and I passed by the various rickety gates and cliffs covered with hieroglyphs, a strange mournful whistling echoed through the canyon. It was almost like the canyon itself knew what had taken place here during Bruxrac. The sound combined with the memory of what I’d read sent shivers down my spine. I couldn’t help feeling a deep sadness well up inside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently Brawlo’s empathic abilities go both ways because he noticed my change in mood. After a while, he explained that there is something like memory infused in the crystal I’ve seen scattered everywhere since entering Cobbogoth. Whether it was the glowing crystals in the Starlyt Sea Cave, the various communocite pillars I’ve passed, or the countless other crystal clusters scattered throughout the landscape here in Cobbogoth. Geographically, crystals are decidedly one of Cobbogoth’s main characteristics. But according to Brawlo, those crystals are more than just part of the landscape. They have memory! In fact, they act like a sort of record keeping system, only rather than a record of words, they record a record of feelings—feelings that can be felt by anyone who happens to pass by them. For example, the Starlyt Sea cave? It felt ancient and important. Phitessnie’s Blood Grove felt sacred, to the point that I felt like whispering while I was under those massive roots and blood red leaves. The Drusy Path felt clandestine—cloak-and-dagger-like and exciting. And this place, the Glorian Pass…it felt sad. So very sad.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo felt it too. The same sadness I was feeling roiled off him. As we continued to pass by each dilapidated gate, he began naming them for me and describing as well as he could the realms each gate had once led to—water worlds with flying ships as big as islands, worlds as desolate as the moon on the surface but with amazon-like jungles underground. I wasn’t surprised to find that none of the gates led back to Earth’s realm. Suppose it would defeat the whole purpose of Phitessnie’s blood sum if one did, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, when we reached the final gate of the Glorian Pass, Brawlo’s sadness suddenly turned to deep longing. I asked him what this gate was called to see if I couldn’t make sense of the sudden change in him. A long moment passed before I heard his thoughts in my mind again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*THE WOOD WEAVER’S GATE,* he finally answered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then I understood.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"The Glorian Pass","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2e82e0f2f1424ea5aeb02f3bdd38ced1"},{"title":"Journal: Glorian Pass & Brawlo","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"16f6fd18fb594b1d8103c0673c11ca95"}]},{"n":28,"letterTitle":"The Water Walker and the City of Stones","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"The Water Walker and the City of Stones (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Have you ever been so tired of swimming, you couldn’t feel your fins anymore? No, you probably haven’t. You probably don’t have fins, do you? Well, I do have fins—part of the time anyway—and I am tired of swimming. So. Very. Tired. But I can’t stop, you see. My people depend on me for healing. They’ve been depending on me for almost 20 years now, ever since Melusian stole Mother’s trident and ran us all out of Adoloria. That’s when the Lamianza plague struck my people. We had no idea that when the ancestors altered our DNA so we could shift into sina, they also weakened our azura against such things as plagues.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hindsight, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I was especially tired the day I met the Lune-kyndie on the shores of the Glindian Sea. I was swimming back to Feg after healing the sick. My people are refugees, living in the City of Stones, you see. The Unda Swamp is just off the northeastern shore of the Glindian Sea, and since Unda trees are water trees—giant trees filled with water—it was the best home Mother could find for our people after Melusian’s attack.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My name is Zuli, by the way. I am the Crown Princess of Adoloria, or at least I was before Queen Citrine—my mother—disowned me for joining the Hood. But that’s a story for another day.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Like I said, I was especially tired on the day I met the Lune-kyndie, tired of swimming, never stopping, never able to rest. I am a refugee like the rest of my people, but unlike my people who have their Unda trees, I have no home to go to. Not in the traditional sense, anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet I can’t seem to stay away from my people, despite my mother’s banishment—not when I am the only one who has the power to heal them. That power was given to me a long time ago by one of the Naridi. She entrusted me with her qualdrine before she vanished from Cobbogoth almost 20 years ago. Before she left, she made me promise to use her qualdrine and do everything in my power to cure my people from Lamianza.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that’s what I have been doing. Every night until just before the sun rises, I visit the sick among my people, and I heal them. I was just finishing up a long night of healing when I first saw the Lune-kyndie on the shore. I was already in the water, ready to swim back to Feg, when I caught sight of her. She seemed to appear out of nowhere. She was a rather peculiar sight wandering out of the trees and onto the beach like that all alone. Just a girl, not much younger than myself—a decade or two at the most.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, I wondered if she was one of the Adolorians coming late to the healing, but she didn’t look Adolorian. Her skin was far too pale, and she was wearing her green shyntara in a very strange way. Her hair was partly covered by her hood, but I could still see that it was so white it looked like it had been spun from the light of our largest moon, Vyn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Who was this stranger, and what was she doing so near the City of Stones? The last thing my people needed was an outsider causing trouble. So, as tired as I was, I turned around and began swimming back to shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I drew closer, I could see she was pretty enough, completely lacking in style, of course, but not everyone grew up in Queen Citrine’s court.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The girl kept examining the rodãors embedded in the tree trunks. She didn’t seem to know what they were, which was strange since everyone knew what had happened to my people after Melusian invaded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I transformed in the water and walked up onto shore. I called out to her. She didn’t answer. I asked what her name was. She didn’t answer. She just stood there with wide, strangely pale green eyes looking…what…shocked?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried again, but still she didn’t answer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I don’t excuse myself for what I did next. I simply ask that you don’t judge me too harshly. Like I said, I was tired.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"The Water Walker and the City of Stones (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When Brawlo and I finally reached the Glindian Sea, it was nearly dawn on the third day. Lylend was supposed to meet me there after sunrise.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Extremely proud of himself for getting me safely to my final destination, Brawlo lowered his head, indicating I should slide off. Once I was standing in front of him, he pushed his nose into my palm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"GO NOW. TOTHERMA CALLS.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, surprised to feel my eyes stinging. I’m telling you, Brawlo’s whole empathic, telepathy thing really gets under your skin. Fast. Uncle Jack never allowed me to own any animals, but I’d always wanted a puppy. Brawlo was like a giant puppy, only he could talk and make me feel what he was feeling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He could also tell what I was feeling right then and responded by pressing his forehead to mine again. A rush of affection washed over me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"LOVE.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh…” I sniffed, wrapping my arms around his soft neck. “I love you too, boy.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After that, Brawlo licked my cheek and scurried off into the trees the way we came.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"According to the map, Brawlo had left me in a place called the Unda Swamp. The Drusy Path ended near a place called the City of Stones. Once I stepped beyond the trees, I was no longer invisible, but Brawlo said I would be safe walking to the beach to meet Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The City of Stones wasn’t a city at all but a shallow, swampy wood of very large squatty trees bordering the seashore. The trees weren’t enormous, towering giants though, not nearly as large as the red-leaved trees in Phitessnie’s Blood Grove. Their trunks were almost as big around, though. These trees were much shorter—the size of a two-story house instead of a skyscraper—and their branches stuck straight out, creating a thick canopy over the swampy forest floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The glowing stones embedded into the bark of nearly every tree in that wood made me want to get closer. When I was close enough to see that the stones weren’t just glowing stones but were rodãors, the whole City of Stones idea made a lot more sense. Later I learned the rodãors were doorways into the trees themselves, which is a pretty trippy idea.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I was on the edge of the wood by then, and my ranalin leggings were soaked to the knee from wading through the shallow swamp. Up until that moment, I was certain I was alone on the beach. It was a small beach, and there weren’t many places for someone to hide.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d finally gotten onto dryish sand and had just finished examining yet another embedded rodãor on the edge of the wood when I turned toward the Glindian Sea. It was the first time I’d had the chance to see the sunrise in Cobbogoth. Unlike Earth’s sun, Cobbogoth’s sun rose in the west and set in the east. It was just beginning to creep above the horizon, tingeing the sky with pink, when a fiery copper cloud bobbing out on the surface of the water glinted in the light and caught my eye.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first I thought it was a bit of sea plant, like seaweed or something. Then the cloud leapt, and not only did I see a face attached to the cloud, but I also thought I saw what looked like a tiger-striped tail slap down in the water behind it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I froze. “No way,” I breathed aloud. It couldn’t be! Wait a second, I’d just ridden on the back of a giant telepathic weasel on a path that made me invisible. Of course it could!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of all the Cobbogothian legends Uncle Jack had told me growing up, the one I loved the most was the sina legend. Sinas were kind of like mermaids. The Cobbogothians called them sina-yvaina because it meant “water walker.” Unlike regular mermaids, Cobbogothian mermaids weren’t mermaids at all. They were just Cobbogothians who could transform or shape-shift into mermaids.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Whether it was the fact that I was seeing a sina in real life for the first time or that I was watching her transform back into a girl right before my eyes, I was rendered completely speechless. When she stepped onto the shore and started talking to me, my mind went completely blank. I was even more starstruck than the time James and I ran into Ben Affleck and Matt Damon at the Bertucci’s in Newton.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sina was even more beautiful than the legend claimed. Even her voice sounded mermaidish, and the more she spoke to me, the less I knew how to respond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t that I was afraid of her. Not really. Not until she conjured a water whip out of the sea anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When she cracked it against my left temple and my head split with white hot pain, it’s safe to say I wasn’t so starstruck anymore.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Legend of the Sina Y-Vaina","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4d2378553a244d39bd3c22b110fe2462"},{"title":"Journal: City of Stones & Lamianza","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"64b64f394efe44ba9ef37fa7429e0e8c"}]},{"n":29,"letterTitle":"Awakening","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"Awakening","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"James here. Yep, I’m still alive. Yay. For the record, waking up from paying a blood sum isn’t the most fun thing I’ve ever done. The Gihara warned me it would be “uncomfortable,” but that was a major understatement, if you know what I’m saying.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently, I didn’t have to do the blood sum thing so thoroughly. I mean, I still had to fatally wound myself, but I guess I did a pretty good job of it because the Gihara felt the need to put me into some kind of comatose state for my body to repair itself. Guess I did some real damage to my digestive system.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I woke up in this place—whatever it is—about three days ago, and I guess I’ve been in and out of consciousness ever since.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It’s a weird room I’m in. The walls, ceiling, and floor are all made of some kind of crystal. Big surprise there. Rather than a bed, I’ve been trying to recover on a really uncomfortable stone table thingy. I imagine it’s carved from some kind of healing stone because I’ve been feeling some strange sensations since I started waking up more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the first two days, I wasn’t sure if I was a prisoner or a patient here. Didn’t care much either way, to tell you the truth. There were a few instances I was even hoping someone might come in and put me out of my misery. But I never saw anyone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Food—real good food—kept showing up on the table next to me, so I knew someone was there. I’d eat what I could manage when I was conscious, and the next time I came to, it would be gone or replaced by different food, depending on how long I was out that time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I still haven’t tried getting all the way up to check the door yet—see if I’m locked in here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Last night, I did finally get visitors. I was hoping Norah would start visiting, but no luck there. This group of visitors was an odd bunch, if I’m being honest. There were six of them, three men and three ladies, I think. They were all wearing these green robe thingies with hoods, so I can’t be sure. Between you and me, though, if there weren’t any ladies in the group, Cobbogoth has some really pretty men.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nah, they were definitely ladies.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, it was a guy who talked to me first. He told me the Gihara had sent me to them for two purposes: to remember who I am and to become who I’m destined to be.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not sure what any of that meant, but I asked them when I could see Norah or my dad. They said soon, but first I had to do some stuff. So I said, “Okay, whatever I gotta do, let’s get this show on the road.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then they gave me something to drink. They told me it was the first step in accomplishing the Gihara’s purposes. They warned me the drink might make me “uncomfortable,” and I would most likely begin to see things. I told them I’d walked through fire, fought Dogrils, and paid a blood sum, so I’d probably be able to handle it. They didn’t respond to that, which probably should have made me a little nervous. It didn’t.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know what you’re thinking; it probably doesn’t seem like such a smart move, you know, to drink something a bunch of strangers gave me, “stranger danger” and all that. But I figured the Gihara got me this far—had even let me live—so I might as well trust them a bit longer. So I drank the drink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And it was hot. Not hot as in temperature—hot like spicy. There was a bit of grit in it too, so I knew they’d added some kind of stone powder as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the first few minutes afterward, I just got drowsy, although that was happening a lot lately anyway, so I can’t be sure it was the drink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I closed my eyes though, I did see something. Something real strange.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In my mind, I was under water. There was this copper-haired mermaid, but rather than a regular mermaid, she had this weird tiger-striped tail. Never seen a mermaid like that before. Wait, what am I saying? I’ve never seen a mermaid at all. Well, unless you count that one time when Gram proved she was also a Cobbogothian by transforming into a…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sina! That’s what this “mermaid” thingy was. The Sina was dragging something behind her through the water. I tried to focus in on what she was dragging, but she was moving pretty fast, and things were blurry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt myself falling deeper under as the drink affected me more. What I was seeing became clearer, and I realized the Sina was dragging a body. And not just any body. She was dragging Norah!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to jerk myself back to consciousness, but it was too late. The next moment, the spiciness of the drink spread though my whole body like gasoline.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then it was like someone lit a match. So yeah…things got real “uncomfortable” after that.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Hallucite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"04be387c22ba4d35a9c015064d34cec7"}]},{"n":30,"letterTitle":"Lune-kyndie and the Island of Feg","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"Lune-kyndie and the Island of Feg","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Hello there! Levian Pieterskynd here. My friends call me Lev. We’ll be friends soon too if I have anything to say about it, so you might as well call me Lev as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Welcome to the Island of Feg, central location of Hood headquarters. Being from Earth, you probably don’t know what the Hood is, do you? Let me explain. The Hood is the name we use for the resistance here in Cobbogoth. The Naridi who survived Bruxrac, i.e. Lylend and Gram, formed the Hood 20 years ago as a means of fighting against the Dogrils when they invaded Cobbogoth. As you can imagine, with so few Cobbogothians in possession of Qualdrines left—especially when Lylend and Gram disappeared only a couple years after forming the Hood—the Dogrils have been wreaking havoc here ever since.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, everyone kind of abandoned the Hood when the Naridi left, which is understandable…I guess. All that’s left of us now is Pieter—he’s my very moody father, Iolyt—the smartest, sweetest lady you’ll ever meet, Zuli, and myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli’s been with the Hood longer than I have. She’s 17 years older, so she and her mom were part of the original members recruited by Gram and Lylend. Man, it would have been the best to be recruited by Lylend. Iolyt has told me so many stories about that guy. Instead, I got recruited by my dad, if you can call it recruiting. I guess I was just kind of born into it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Zuli’s had a tough year, but she’s still hanging in there. And thank the Gihara for that because, honestly, I don’t know what I’d do if Zuli wasn’t a part of the Hood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ve been worried about her lately, though. Being a princess, she puts a lot of pressure on herself. Zuli and her mom don’t get along. Neither do my dad and me, but Zuli’s situation is worse. Her mom won’t even talk to her. She’s been banished from the Water Kin-Klan because she won’t leave the Hood. It’s a long story for another time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I mean, Zu’s the sweetest girl in the world. Well, she’s actually a woman by your standard, but in Cobbogoth she’s barely grown, even if she is 17 years older than me. (The age difference isn’t a big deal here because we all live so long.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Where was I? Oh yeah, Zu’s been more worn out and irritable than usual. If the stuff with her mom wasn’t bad she’s the only member of the Water Kin-Klan left who has a Qualdrine, so keeping the Lamianza plague from wiping out her people is kind of up to her. You get the picture, right? I’m not making excuses for her or anything, but we can both agree that Zuli’s been dealing with some tough stuff, can’t we?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is why when I went out to meet her on the beach this morning and saw her dragging a lifeless body behind her, I got kinda nervous she might’ve finally snapped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I knew it was a bad week,” I teased, rushing to help her with the body, “but I didn’t think it was bad enough to murder someone.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli rolled her eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Seriously. Who is this?” I asked, helping her lug the body over a sandbank. “Not someone who refused Lamianza treatment, I hope?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This time I got a withering look.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Okay, message received. No teasing today.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So who is she?” I asked again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli shrugged. “I just found her on the shore. She was snooping through the City of Stones.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So you drowned her?” I chuckled, hoping she hadn’t. No one knew better than me how unpredictable Zu could be. She’d never murdered anyone, of course, but I’d learned over the years that the only way to escape her tempestuous moods unscathed was with humor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara! She’s perfectly fine, Lev. Look. She’s breathing. I just knocked her out with a water whip and put that Vatnanda around her waist.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I let out my breath and crouched down to get a closer look at Zu’s victim. “She looks a little different, doesn’t she?” I asked. “Almost like a—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lune-kyndie,” Zuli finished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, what do you plan to do with her now?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know, I thought your dad and Iolyt might know.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Honestly? It might’ve been better to just leave her there,” I said, knowing I was moving into dangerous territory. But a Lune-kyndie? “Bringing her here might put the Hood in danger.” The only reason the Dogrils haven’t tracked us down yet is because of Feg. Feg was a peculiar old island, the perfect location for Hood headquarters.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Listen, all I know is she didn’t answer me when I asked her name or what she was doing on the shore.” Zu knelt down beside me. “It seemed suspicious.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did you ever consider the fact she might not be able to understand you?” I asked, patting the Lune-kyndie’s cheek to try and wake her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why wouldn’t she be able to understand me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For one, she’s a Lune-kyndie. She most likely grew up in the wild with maybe only her deranged mother to talk to. She might not even know how to speak.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Lune-kyndie started to stir then. She groaned, grimaced in pain, and touched the goose egg-sized welt on her left temple. Slowly she began to open her eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good morning,” I said, smiling at her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She looked dazed for a minute, then confused. When she glanced over at Zuli, however, her pale green eyes filled with panic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s okay, it’s okay,” I said, putting my hands on her shoulders to keep her from moving too much. Zu must’ve walloped her good because the Lune-kyndie was having a hard time focusing in on me. She’d need to get checked out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on,” I said, scooping the Lune-kyndie up in one arm and taking Zu by the hand. “Let’s take her to Iolyt.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We hoptioned to Feg’s Fork, and I sent Zuli to go get Iolyt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once we were inside, I sat the Lune-kyndie down at the large banquet table spanning the length of what used to be the Hood strategy room and sat beside her. For some reason, she kept clutching at the hood of her Shyntara, right under her chin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You cold?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Lune-kyndie shook her head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah, so you do understand me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course, I do,” she said. I couldn’t place her accent. “Please tell me I’m still on the Glindian shore?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. “Nah, you’re on an island in the middle of the Glindian Sea.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no!” she gasped. “No-no-no-no…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? What is it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Lune-kyndie took her head in her hands, resting her elbows on the table. That’s when I realized she was going to be sick.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hold on,” I begged. “Let me find something. Pieter will kill me if you puke on this table.” I stood, looking around the room for something to give her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hurry!” she gasped, breathing slowly through her nose.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, but keep talking to me, okay?” I said, trying to postpone the inevitable.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I was supposed…” *deep breath* “…to meet…” *deep breath* “…someone on shore.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I finally found a stone basin and slid it between her elbows. “Meet someone? Who?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thanks,” she managed, before throwing up a stomach full of water. I grabbed her hair, holding it out of the way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No problem,” I said. “It’s carved from evaporite,” I gestured toward the bowl, not knowing what else to do. Her puke had already vanished. “Go ahead and let it all out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded her thanks this time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s going on here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jumped, turning to find my father—or Pieter as he insisted I call him—glowering down from the doorway behind us. Honestly, the guy has been in a bad mood since the day I was born.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I-I don’t know,” I said, standing up. “I was just about to ask her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean? Don’t you know who she is—what is she doing here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pieter, perhaps we should discuss this outside first,” Iolyt said calmly as she passed through the doorway behind him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t want to discuss it outside. What on Earth made you think it was a good idea to bring a stranger here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He didn’t bring her here,” Zuli said, hot on Iolyt’s heels. “I did.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My father ignored her—he always ignored Zu—circling the table until he was facing the Lune-kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when he froze. A visible change unlike anything I’d ever seen in him suddenly came over Pieter’s face. A thousand different emotions warred across his features as he looked at the Lune-kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pieter?” Iolyt asked warningly. “What is it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How did you get here?” Pieter hissed in a strained whisper. He was visibly shaking. Was it with rage? Fear? I couldn’t tell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pieter?” I stood up, suddenly concerned for the old man’s health. “Dad? What’s—” I was silenced by a gentle but firm hand on my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev, my kynd,” Iolyt said from behind me. She squeezed my shoulder, and I knew I was meant to obey her next command without hesitation.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter’s eyes had lost focus now, his knuckles were white from gripping the table so tight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Remove the Lune-kyndie from Feg’s Fork immediately,” Iolyt commanded. She was using the tone she only ever used in training, when it was vital I follow her instructions exactly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stood, taking the Lune-kyndie’s hand. “Come on,” I said. “Don’t ask questions.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt caught my arm, then. “Make sure it’s somewhere safe,” she whispered. “Where he can’t find her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded and hoptioned on the spot. Hopefully the crystal cells would be safe enough.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Journal: Lune Kyndlin & The Island of Feg","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d4361ab18f344e5fa22be88df8a51a03"},{"title":"Vatnanda","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"b6facbbedc594c22bcc1487cf62fa043"}]},{"n":31,"letterTitle":"Callowynder and Friend","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Callowynder and Friend","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So that guy Pieter has got some anger management issues, huh? You’d think I’d wronged him personally or something. To be honest, I’ve been puzzling over that one for days and still haven’t figured out a reasonable explanation for why he got so crazy livid toward me that first day. I mean, I’m a complete stranger.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Between him and Lev’s friend Zuli, I suppose I’m just lucky to be alive. Honestly, the only thing I can think of that might’ve made them both treat me like a criminal is if they saw the Haven tattoo. Lylend said there would be those who wouldn’t like it. I suppose it could also be the whole lune-kyndie thing, but as far as Pieter is concerned, it feels too personal to be something like that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oh well, thankfully I haven’t seen either of those crazies again since arriving on Feg. I’ve actually kind of lost track of time down here in Feg’s underbelly, which, as you’ll see from my last journal, is just a Cobbogothian way of saying “the dungeons.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I have learned a lot though. There are all kinds of creatures that swim past my cell window—creatures I’ve only ever heard of in myth, or never heard of at all. My favorites so far are the fairy prawns. They live in a large garden of giant, glowing sea flowers just outside my window. The sea creatures have been my main source of distraction down here. Zuli must’ve searched me while I was out and confiscated Lylend’s rodãor—either that or I dropped it while she was dragging me through the sea—because it’s not in my shyntara. The record room would have been nice to have right about now. Thankfully this place is fully stocked with ink and some kind of parchment, so I’m at least able to keep busy writing down everything I see.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev has been my only visitor since he put me down here. It turns out he’s actually a pretty cool guy. It’s taken me a while to warm up to him, especially since Pieter is obviously his father and the Sina is his friend. Possibly more than his friend, the way he talks about her all the time. Lev is the one who brings me food. Apparently, it’s important that he’s the only one who knows where I am right now. Who knows what could happen if Pieter finds out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev seems just as baffled by his father’s behavior toward me as I am…at least that’s what he claims. Either way, I’ve decided not to trust him with anything too important. I knew he and Pieter were father and son the moment I saw the two of them together. They look really similar but, as far as I can tell, are as different as can be personality-wise. Lev’s all golden wavy hair and the typical Cobbogothian Viking height (by typical, I mean he looks like the few other Cobbogothian males I’ve seen). He’s also got huge muscles—like WAY too big for someone who just celebrated his 18th birthday. Seriously, he’s ripped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter looks almost identical, except 30 years older (or whatever the Cobbogothian equivalent is), and his hair is more pale and sandy because of the silver creeping in. Aside from that, the biggest difference between them is their eyes. Pieter’s are such a pale, icy blue they are almost silver. Lev, on the other hand, has these peacock green eyes that are as warm as Pieter’s are cold. They draw you in like the first green grass of spring.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Despite my decision not to trust him, I’ve really had to work at keeping my guard up around Lev. He’s honestly the friendliest guy I’ve ever met. He showed up on my third day here with my dinner and what looked like a wooden bejeweled box under his arm. Turns out it’s this Cobbogothian game—THE Cobbogothian game, apparently. Lev said he brought it because he thought I might be bored, but after the first round, it was pretty clear he was desperate for someone to play with.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, the game is called Callowynder. I’ve included an “Earth friendly” version of it for you to try. It’s pretty fun once you get the hang of it. Apparently, they teach it to children here to help them better understand their world and their elemental powers and how to use them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As a matter of fact, Callowynder has actually been helping me better understand my own powers. After the first week of being trapped in this crystal cell—the nights are pitch black down here—I may or may not have disregarded Lylend’s rule about not using my fire power, and I actually managed to conjure a small green flame in the palm of my hand! Seriously. It was the only way I could think of to keep from losing my mind when the larger more sinister sea creatures began prowling past my cell window. But conjuring fire was amazing! After a few nights of practice, I was even able to keep the flame in my palm for most of the night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The most useful thing about this Callowynder game is the callowynder itself. See, the whole object of the game is to win a stone called callowynder. Whoever wins the stone gets to make a request of the game maker—Lev, in this case. As soon as I learned about this part of the game, I saw my chance for escape. I decided I was going to win the callowynder and use it to request that Lev take me to a communocite pillar and let me chime Lylend. I already tried telling him about Lylend, but he didn’t believe me. He said Lylend disappeared before Lev was even born. When I tried to explain the situation to him—that Lylend had been in Earth’s realm all this time—he just gave me a patronizing smile and said, “I’ve heard you lune-kyndies are odd.” Then he went right back to playing Callowynder. I wanted to chuck my dice at his head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Lev’s visits (patronizing smiles aside) and all I was learning from playing Callowynder with him might’ve made the blackness of the underwater nights almost bearable. That is, of course, if I wasn’t also having visions again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They started up a couple of nights after Lev first brought the Callowynder game. So far the visions are all the same. It’s just James lying on a stone table in a stone room, and he’s writhing in pain. Comforting, right? I honestly don’t know what to make of them. They’re not exactly like my Totherma visions—she’s not in them at all—but there is a “realness” to them, and I always wake in a cold sweat, bawling my eyes out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which leads me to the point of all this. Last night, Lev was late bringing my food and happened to enter my cell while I was in the middle of my latest vision. It was the worst one yet. Not only did I see James in pain, but I was finally able to get close enough to see what was causing the pain. There was something going on under his skin, almost like a fire surging through his veins. Every part of his skin I could see was almost translucent with a blue fire blazing just under the surface. I finally got close enough to try and wake him up, but when I touched his skin, it burned me so badly that I was the one who woke up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I woke, Lev was shaking me—probably freaked him out seeing me like that. I, on the other hand, could still feel scorch marks on my fingertips from touching James’s skin. That’s what worried me the most. If these visions were so real that I had burn marks on my fingertips, how was I supposed to know they weren’t actually happening just as Totherma’s visions did? All I could think about was the vision I’d had of Uncle Jack before finding out he was really dead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was the mindset I was in when Lev finally calmed me down. I was so desperate to chime Lylend—to tell him what I’d seen of James—that I challenged Lev to a game of Callowynder…and I won! I won the callowynder!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The thing about Callowynder is the game maker must honor the request. Something about the stone makes it impossible for them to refuse. I told Lev I wanted to be able to chime Lylend as my prize. When I mentioned Lylend’s name, though, Lev gave me that patronizing smile again. When I insisted I was serious, he finally gave in and told me he’d ask Iolyt and see what he could do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was better than I hoped for, and I was so relieved he was even willing to consider asking that I—very uncharacteristically—threw my arms around him and hugged him right then and there. I was thinking about James, okay!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But this is where the trouble started.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev was just fine with my sudden burst of gratitude. Like everything, he took it in stride and laughed it off. But not Zuli the Sina. “Coincidentally,” she just happened to be swimming outside my cell at the precise moment I threw my arms around Lev.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First there was a sharp pounding on my crystal cell. Startled, Lev and I pulled apart just in time to see her face—which was NOT happy. Next, she whacked the window with her tiger-striped tail, and then, after sending me a glare that pretty much froze my blood, she vanished into the seaweed, scattering a school of curious piskies in her wake.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First of all, when did she find out where I was? Did Lev tell her? And secondly, as innocent as the hug between Lev and I was, Zuli didn’t seem like the type of person to let someone explain before doing something crazy to retaliate—like, let’s see, knocking them out and dragging them off to an island as her prisoner.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Lev was so flustered that he left without playing another Callowynder match. Said he needed to track Zuli down and explain things before she did something stupid…like go tell Pieter where my cell is located.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was over three hours ago, and no one’s come back yet. I’ll be honest, I’m starting to panic a little. I’m just waiting for Zuli to attack me in my sleep or something. I guess if I don’t write again, you’ll know something really bad has happened.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Journal: Cobbogothian Sea Creatures","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d72230e54bee48c5b0befa6c55449c55"},{"title":"Callowynder","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"ffd6036a9bc74e57a66b56d9aaf1c3f6"},{"title":"Callowynder Game Board","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"81176f2f5cff4ba788052e9252c79a14"},{"title":"Callowynder Game Pieces","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9dc02f5ddb33437383136605f2f1a003"}]},{"n":32,"letterTitle":"A Sina Scorned","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"A Sina Scorned","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"My mother Queen Citrine always told me that Sina don’t get angry, they get even. The sentiment never sat well with me, particularly after I met Lev. He’s not the vengeful type. Tonight, however, after seeing that Lune-kyndie throw herself into Lev’s arms, revenge was very much on my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After finding the man I loved in another woman’s arms, I returned to my hiding place in the Garden of Blooms outside the Lune-kyndie’s crystal cell. Then I watched and waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By his frantic departure from the cell, I knew Lev was coming to look for me. He was a Verawyn and would one day master water as well as earth, but until then, without the help of Yatnanda, I knew he couldn’t find me down here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"No one knew it, but I’d been watching the Lune-kyndie for a week now, ever since I followed Lev and discovered where he was hiding her. I knew he was visiting her regularly. This was Lev we were talking about here, never one to let the opportunity to make a new friend pass him by. Even if they were a prisoner of the Hood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, his visits were just to bring her food. After a while, though, Lev was down there too often and for too long to just be dropping off a meal. So I followed him. It wasn’t long before I figured out where he’d put the Lune-kyndie after Pieter zyrked out at first meeting her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d seen the two of them talking together…sometimes even laughing together. Then Lev’s visits started getting longer and longer. When he started bringing Callowynder with him, I didn’t understand. I was upset. Sure, Lev was obsessed with Callowynder. He was always looking for a worthy opponent, and he isn’t picky about who that opponent is. He just wants to play, right? But to play the game with a complete stranger—a prisoner of the Hood?! I just told myself it was just Lev being Lev and that he couldn’t help himself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t until I started seeing the Lune-kyndie’s little green flames show up in her cell at night that I knew the type of Lune-kyndie we were dealing with. That’s when I knew she couldn’t be trusted. She was obviously a flame thrower, and everyone knows flame throwers are the children of traitors. Most of them aligned themselves with the Dogrils not too long after the Dogril invasion. So I started watching the Lune-kyndie’s cell all the time. Someone had to protect Lev from himself. He’d always been too trusting of people. He thought everyone was good, even his perpetually angry father. The Lune-kyndie was clearly worried, and the thought of Pieter and how he treated Lev only made me angrier as I watched her pace her cell. Pieter was always belittling Lev and had never shown an ounce of fatherly affection toward him, and he definitely didn’t like me. He’d made that very clear from the beginning. But he was the only parent Lev knew. Yet the way he treated him, sometimes I thought it would be better if Lev didn’t even have him. Iolyt said there was a complicated history there, but this was Lev we were talking about. It was impossible not to like him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which was probably why the Lune-kyndie had thrown herself at him in the first place. I wasn’t incapable of seeing things from her point of view. Lev was handsome and likeable and kind and…well, other things. He was also a Verawyn, which meant he was powerful and talented. Furthermore, he was almost always in a good mood, which was really great for our relationship because I was not. But Lev was also too trusting, too reckless, friendly to a fault, and most importantly, the only home I had left. I wasn’t about to lose him to some flame-throwing Lune-kyndie who decided to wander into civilization for who knows what purpose. Besides, how was I supposed to know she wasn’t trying to manipulate Lev by fawning over him like that? That she wasn’t trying to get him to fall in love with her so he’d let her escape—so she could run off and tell the Dogrils where Hood headquarters was?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I should have never brought her here. I had thoughtlessly put all our lives in danger. It was time to clean up my own mess, and whatever revenge I exacted on the Lune-kyndie tonight would inevitably only do the Hood a favor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At least that’s what I told myself when I goaded an electric eel into slamming its tail against her cell window a few moments later.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the easiest thing in the world after that. Once the eel got started, it only took three more giant whacks against her crystal cell with his tail and the crystal splintered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was dark, but my Sina eyes could see everything. I watched in satisfaction as water began to spurt into the Lune-kyndie’s cell, creating an ever-growing puddle on the floor. I saw the fear in her eyes—saw her conjure a small ball of fire to help illuminate the darkness. Didn’t she know fire and water didn’t mix very well? Her little green flames would only make the water angry—only get her killed sooner. Even without them, though, she was going to drown. There was no way out of that cell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swam out of my hiding place then. The water was already at her waist. I swam up to her window and waved my tail to get her attention. When her eerily pale green eyes met mine, they were so full of fear I almost felt sorry. Instead, I gave her a little wave before swimming toward the surface. After all, she had a right to know why she was about to drown in her cell. So what if I felt a few pangs of guilt when I glanced back, watching her scramble around in panic. This was for Lev. This was for the Hood. Seen in the right light, this was for all of Cobbogoth. Her death was a small sacrifice in exchange for the greater good of us all. If only I’d known how very wrong I was.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"Journal: The Power Wyns","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"ff845070bc88416588fce48d10e6194e"}]},{"n":33,"letterTitle":"Buried Alive","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"Buried Alive (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Just so you know, it’s not a great feeling to discover your girlfriend is a “homicidal maniac”—Lune-kyndie’s words, not mine. But when I hoptioned to Feg’s underbelly after searching for Zuli all day and then saw Lune-kyndie in her crystal cell up to her neck in water, I knew there could only be one person responsible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Did I mention Zuli is going through a rough time right now? She really is the sweet…*exasperated sigh*…anyway, we can talk about that more later.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First, back to rescuing Lune-kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unfortunately, there were some serious obstacles keeping me from saving Lune-kyndie from drowning. For example, I couldn’t just hoption in there, grab her, and hoption back out. For one, the cell was almost completely full of water. In case you didn’t already know, water is one of the elements that can’t be hoptioned into, out of, or through.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Secondly, these crystal cells weren’t just your standard crustaceans found on a giant turtle’s under-shell. They’d been grown over thousands of years and were made of Aguarianite crystal. You can’t hoption into, out of, or through anything made of Aguarianite crystal either. It’s what made these cells such good prisons.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully I’d thought to bring Iolyt along. Iolyt is the best, by the way. She’s the Hood’s resident healer and qualdrinist and the closest thing I’ve ever had to a mother. We look nothing alike, so I know she’s not my real mother. In fact, we look exactly the opposite in just about every way. Iolyt has skin as black as unda fruit, eyes as warm and soothing as Vyntara—our golden moon—and black hair, except for an awesome white streak that shoots out of her widow’s peak. Me, I’m all golden hair, golden skin and green eyes. Where I’m outgoing and friendly, and somewhat rash, Iolyt is quiet and brilliant and extremely patient. And…let’s not fail to admit the obvious; she’s also pretty much the opposite of Pieter in every way imagineable, which is probably why I love her so much.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, the point is, Iolyt always keeps her head in a crisis and always—I mean always—knows exactly what to do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is why when she told me to break Lune-kyndie’s crystal cell, I only questioned her once.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Break it?” I asked. “Like clean off Feg’s shell?” We were standing in a tunnel the early Hood members had burrowed into the outer rim of Feg’s shell. The tunnel acted as a sort of hallway to the crystal cells, allowing us to access them without having to go into the water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If we open Lune-kyndie’s cell right now, won’t the whole tunnel flood with water?” Since Iolyt wasn’t a water wielder and Zuli was just beginning to teach me how to control that element, I could see a problem here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Iolyt only nodded again. “We’ve got to get her out of there before Zuli returns,” she insisted. “You know she’s gone to find Pieter.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yep,” I sighed, preparing myself, despite any lingering reservations, to break the crystal cell free.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was pounding on the cell wall and I turned to see Lune-kyndie pounding as the water slipped over her head. With no time to lose, I stamped the ground and smacked the crystal wall with the heel of my hand. The cell broke away from Feg instantly. The water began pouring in on us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good. Now we have to work together to force the cell to the surface,” Iolyt instructed over the noise. She stood up on a jagged ledge of Feg’s shell, just above the water and reached for me. “As soon as it surfaces, we’ll hoption to shore and retrieve her from the cell before she drowns.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I climbed up by Iolyt where we joined our forces and pushed the cell to the surface. Then Iolyt took my hand, and we hoptioned just before the water reached us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The hoption hole spit us out on shore just in time to see the crystal cell bob to the surface of the sea.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The cell was completely full of water now and much heavier. Digging deep into my earth kneading skills, it was all I could do to keep the crystal sphere from sinking back to the bottom.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let me, Libkyn,” Iolyt said gently. “She’s already been in there too long.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew she was right and stepped aside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt extended a hand and raised it. The crystal cell mimicked her movement, hovering in the air over the water. She swung her arm back, and the cell flew to shore. When she made a fist, the cell shattered, spilling water and a limp Lune-kyndie onto the sand in front of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lift her up quickly,” Iolyt said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I moved Lune-kyndie away from the wreckage and set her down on the sand. Iolyt turned her on her side to help her expel the water, but she didn’t respond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked to Iolyt. She’d settled Lune-kyndie onto her back again and was checking her pulse. Iolyt wouldn’t meet my eye. This was a bad sign.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Open my crystal pouch, Lev. Hand me the three green stones.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I did as she said. Iolyt placed the stones along Lune-kyndie’s sternum.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"An even longer pause passed. What if Zuli had killed her? What if my Kyndie was a murderer? I hadn’t heard the entire conversation, but after Iolyt came back from sending that chime, she didn’t waste a minute rushing to bust Lune-kyndie out of her cell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This train of thought was broken by a ragged intake of breath and a fit of coughing. Then, for the second time since we’d met, I was holding Lune-kyndie’s hair back as she puked up water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d be lying if I told you I wasn’t more relieved than I’d ever been in my entire life at that moment…and thanking the Gihara my Kyndie wasn’t a murderer after all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thanks…thank you,” Lune-kyndie said with a shaky voice once she’d caught her breath. She was shivering like crazy, probably in shock, so Iolyt handed her a piece of Varmrine crystal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Place it over your heart,” Iolyt instructed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lune-kyndie did as she was told, and soon the trembling subsided.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all looked around at each other. It was the first time I’d been able to breathe normally since Zuli had spied Lune-kyndie hugging me in her cell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now what?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Scrambling to avoid talking about Zu for as long as possible—until she got the chance to explain herself—I jumped in before either of the others could speak. “So…good news!” I said, turning to Lune-kyndie. “I-uh…told Iolyt here your story, you know, about Lylend and being from the Olden Realm—about your Kinzura Jamer…Jamie…Jame, what is it, again?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James,” Lune-kyndie said, perking up a bit.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right, James. Well, turns out she believed me enough to at least try and chime Lylend. You know, see if you were telling the truth and all.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I had Lune-kyndie’s full attention now. She nodded, urging me on.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well…you’ll never guess who answered!” I continued. To be honest, I couldn’t believe it myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…Lylend?” she questioned as she shot us both a puzzled glance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well…yeah, but it depends on whether or not we’re talking about the same Lylend! Do you mean Lylend-Lylend? The Lylend? Lylend, the original founder of the Hood? Lylend, the captain of the Naridi?” I asked, unable to keep the excitement out of my voice. “Is that the Lylend you mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s the one,” Lune-kyndie sighed, tapping the side of her nose and winking at me. I got the sense she was being sarcastic, but she also looked relieved.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What Lev is trying to say,” Iolyt broke in, “is that we spoke to Lylend, and he confirmed your story. We believe you. He is on his way to Feg now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Finally,” Lune-kyndie mumbled, plopping back onto the sand in exhaustion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ll admit, I felt a little deflated that neither of them seemed more excited about this. Sure, Lune-kyndie had almost died a few minutes ago, but still. This was Lylend we were talking about here. The Lylend! I’d been waiting to meet this guy my entire life. Almost every story Iolyt told me as a kynd included Lylend and the other Naridi.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Zu would understand when I told her…that is if I even got to tell her. Who knew what Lylend would do once he learned she’d tried to drown Lune-kyndie.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Buried Alive (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"“From the frying pan and into the fire.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I think you’d agree that phrase easily describes my first few weeks here in Cobbogoth. Believe it or not, it was about to become even more accurate. I hadn’t been recovered from nearly drowning—again—for more than five minutes before more trouble arrived in the form of Zuli and Pieter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One moment the two of them hoptioned several yards up the shore from where Lev and Iolyt had just rescued me. The next they were storming down the beach toward us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Even with Iolyt and Lev there—even knowing Lylend had backed up my story—I felt some serious dread at the sight of Pieter and that psycho-sina. Not only did they both look livid, but I guess you could say the near-death experiences were starting to get to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Levian!” Pieter snarled the moment he reached us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev answered by situating his massive form in front of Iolyt and me. My respect for him instantly skyrocketed because his dad looked even more terrifying than the last time I’d seen him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is this?” Pieter shouted. “Iolyt, why are you helping him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s not what you think,” Lev said. He was directing his words at Zuli, completely ignoring his father. “If you would’ve just let me explain, you’d know what a huge mistake you’ve made by bringing my dad into this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the first time since meeting Zuli, I saw the cold suspicion she wore when looking at me slip from her face. A flicker of uncertainty flashed through her sea green eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The emotion was gone as soon as it came, though, and then she was right back to glaring at me. “I told you she’d tricked him,” Zuli hissed to Pieter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ve got it wrong, Zu!” Lev warned. “Trust me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why are you protecting her?” she asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because she’s innocent.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look at me, kynd!” Pieter boomed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could see Lev’s frustration as he reluctantly turned to face his father.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll give you one chance to contradict what the sina has told me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The lune-kyndie isn’t what you think she is, Pieter,” Lev insisted. “Iolyt, tell him. He’ll listen to you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It doesn’t matter who she is or isn’t,” Pieter fumed. His face was almost as red as Zuli’s hair. “You’ve disobeyed my orders—gone behind my back. You’ve compromised the safety of the entire Hood!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pieter, no—” Iolyt cried.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But the blast of dirt and rocks was already coming our way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stay behind me!” Lev yelled. He threw up a rock wall of his own to protect us from Pieter’s attack. We backed into a nearby cliff bordering the shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was an ominous cracking and a rumble at my back. I looked up just in time to see several tons of dirt and rock slip away from the cliff face, heading right toward us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look out!” I screamed, but not before my arms acted on their own. They flew up over my head, and I kid you not, green fire—more than I’d ever been able to produce during the pitch-black nights in my cell—burst through my palms. A shield of flame surrounded us, and the falling pieces of cliff melted into magma before it hit us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What the…?” I breathed, looking down at my hands. Where had that come from?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev and Iolyt acted like nothing out of the ordinary had happened. They just went right to pushing the molten rock away from us and toward the sea. As the lava touched the water, it hissed and bubbled. A thick cloud of steam engulfed us, momentarily hiding us from view.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter’s attacks stopped, and Lev took my hand. I knew he was preparing to hoption again. Before he could, the ground under our feet began to shake so badly he lost his balance. I grabbed Iolyt’s hand before she fell to the ground as well. The next thing we knew, the sandy shore was roiling around us like waves in a giant whirlpool, forcing us to all grab hold of a nearby crystal cluster just to keep from getting swept away in it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Has he lost his mind!” Lev yelled over the rumbling. He was looking to Iolyt. “What is wrong with him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mean he isn’t always like this?” I cried.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt only shook her head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then the ground split. A gulch expanded, running between the sea and the dune cliff, separating me from the others. There was more violent shaking, and I slipped over the edge, barely catching hold of a nearby tree root.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No!” I heard the sina scream. The curtain of mist was dissipating, and I could just make out Lev clinging to the edge of the gulch, Zuli’s copper locks dangling over his arms as she tried to pull him up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Iolyt first!” Lev shouted. “Help her first!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I risked a look down then. The drop wasn’t too far. If I fell, it probably wouldn’t be fatal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From the corner of my eye, I could see Zuli and Iolyt heaving Lev up on the other side of the gorge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hold on lune-kyndie!” Lev yelled once he had his feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then the ground shook again. I scrambled to get a firmer grip on the tree root. I’d just seen Lev vanish into a hoption hole when Pieter stepped up to my side of the gorge. He reached down and took hold of the back of my shyntara, ripping my grip free of the tree root.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who sent you?” he demanded through clenched teeth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you crazy?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What fire covyn do you hail from?” he yelled, giving me a shake until my teeth rattled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Covyn?” Wasn’t that a witch thing? “I don’t know what you’re talking about!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently, that was the wrong answer. Pieter’s eyes flashed, and I was falling again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t long before the bottom of the gorge came up to meet me. My legs buckled, shooting pain up my spine. I clambered to my feet, looking up. I could no longer see the others because the walls of the chasm roiled around me, sand and gravel circling like a whirlwind. The noise was like nothing I’d ever heard before.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When it all started pouring in on me from every side, panic took over. I felt a stirring of heat rising from my stomach, and for the second time, my arms acted on their own. They shot out at my sides, and soon a cocoon of flame surrounded me. The sand pouring in on all sides melted before it could touch my skin, and soon a glass barrier separated me from the skin-flaying tornado outside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter increased his efforts. The glass barrier broke, but my arms shot out again. The sand melted, creating a second glass barrier. It broke again, and I formed another glass barrier. This happened again and again until the pressure of the sand closed in so tightly, the only thing keeping me from being buried alive was one last glass barrier—a barrier that hugged my skin, the top extending to the surface like an air tube. I knew it wouldn’t last long—that this final barrier would either break or become my tomb.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just when Pieter began to force sand down the tube and over my head, I heard a familiar trilling sound.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then there was only muffled darkness.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Journal: Aguarianite Crystal Cells","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"768a3260ef2149b192df07817450a5c8"},{"title":"Varmrine","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"92be761dc3fb434d8e9bdf49b8a7773e"},{"title":"Aguarianite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"c24e7dbf927143c48990f68a045b2393"}]},{"n":34,"letterTitle":"Prophesy Fulfilled","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"Prophesy Fulfilled","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If I could tell you how…if you could’ve seen…I mean, by the Gihara! It was incredible! I literally have no words to tell you…utterly speechless.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sorry about that. On second thought, Lune-kyndie has just informed me she’s going to tell you about it herself if I don’t find the words, otherwise you’ll all think she’s dead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So…here goes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just when we saw Lune-kyndie disappear into that churning earth pit of death Pieter was trying to bury her alive in, guess who showed up on Feg? That’s right, Lylend. The Lylend. Lylend the legend! Lylend the Naridi!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend couldn’t hoption onto Feg—no one can—so he’d gotten a ride on Brawlo. They showed up just in time to stop my dad from making the biggest mistake of his life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The moment Lylend stepped ashore, all my kyndhood daydreams materialized right in front of me. Yet every bedtime story Iolyt ever told me about Lylend’s and the Naridi’s great victories paled in comparison to the man himself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then…*pause for very dramatic effect*…I got to see the greatest spark spindler who has ever lived in action.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First, he and Brawlo charged toward the churning pit of death. Lylend momentarily paralyzed my dad with a lightning bolt—so awesome—and then began to conjure a cyclone of epic proportions. Seconds later, Lune-kyndie was out of the pit. He even had her hover over us in the sky like some kind of moon spirit while he blew her clean of all the sand. Then he buried my dad’s chasm like it never even happened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally—he saved the best for last—I got to watch Lylend, the Lylend, Lylend the legend, Lylend the Naridi, cuss my dad out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pieter, you konkle!” Lylend advanced on my dad who was just recovering from the lightning paralysis. “Do you have any idea the damage you nearly caused—the lives sacrificed you nearly wasted?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lylend! I-I…you’re alive!” Pieter stammered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, and you would not be if this kyndie had come to any harm.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why?” Pieter demanded. “For all any of us know, she’s a spy from those treacherous flame covyns.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A spy? A spy?” Lylend laughed. “Gihara spare me your stupidity!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In case you haven’t heard,” Pieter spat, “the Dogrils are even stronger than they were when you abandoned the Hood 20 years ago.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They’ve been using children as spies for over a decade now, and if the rumors are true, that’s not the worst of it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One of Lylend’s brows rose in interest. “Go on.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’ve come up with a stone—something that’s allowing them to monitor all our hoption holes, save the ones on this island,” Iolyt explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, and lest you forget the Twin Brothers’ Tale, it isn’t the first time children have been used to get at the rest of us,” Pieter added.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m well aware of our history,” Lylend sighed. “But if I’m not mistaken, those are green eyes I see in the Kyndie’s head, not yellow, so it’s clear she hasn’t been possessed by a Dogril. Furthermore, if you’d taken the time to search her at all, you’d know who she is and why she is here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend reached out then, placing a hand on Pieter’s shoulder. “Had you searched her, old friend,” he said more gently, “by the Gihara, you would’ve gladly laid down your life before harming even a single hair on her head.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean?” Pieter demanded, suddenly looking frightened. “Who is she?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend beckoned Lune-kyndie toward him. “Show him,” he said, gesturing toward the collar of her shyntara. “Show them all.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Now?” she asked, clutching at her shyntara the way I’d often seen her do these past weeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, Kyndie, now,” Lylend assured her. “He needs to know what his rash stupidity nearly cost us all.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lune-kyndie let out a breath and slowly peeled the collar of her shyntara back from her collarbone. We all leaned in for a closer look. Just below the bone, on the surface of her pale skin, was a strange bejeweled star tattooed in gold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I heard Pieter and Iolyt take a synchronized gasp.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Eye of Gihara!” Pieter stumbled back. The blood had drained from his face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The prophecy!” Zuli whispered. I hadn’t heard her move up beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Prophecy?” I asked. “What prophecy?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Trio-Lune Prophecy,” Iolyt said. I glanced over to see her blinking back tears. “Never in my life did I think I’d live to see it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right,” Lylend said, still speaking to Pieter. “She is one of the kyndlin the prophecy speaks of. Her name is Noriah, which, as you know means that she is our final hope.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Final hope for what?” I asked. I’d seriously never heard of this prophecy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Our final hope of survival, Lybkin,” Iolyt explained. “Survival against the Dogrils, survival against Cifer when he returns.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right,” Lylend said. “Both kyndlin were sent to the Olden Realm by the Gihara for safe keeping. I was sent as well. We’ve only just returned, so you know what that means.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Cifer has returned,” Iolyt breathed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why wasn’t I told about this?” Pieter managed in a strangled whisper.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend drew himself up, looking every bit the legend he was. “You’re not a Naridi. There was no need for you to know. The fewer people who knew, the better.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—I…forgive me,” Pieter said, shaking his head. It was the first thing I’d heard him say since Lune-kyndie—er Noriah?—arrived that wasn’t laced with inexplicable loathing for her. But then I realized he wasn’t talking to her. He was still talking to Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s not my forgiveness you need,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all looked at Lune-kyndie then. She shifted uncomfortably. When I glanced back at my father, however, I was amazed to see the loathing had returned. What was it about her he couldn’t stand?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He and Lune-kyndie glared at one another for a long time. Finally, without a word, Pieter turned and stormed off down the beach.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt sighed. “That man. He’s worse than iron.” She tucked her white streak of hair behind her ear, about to go after him, when Lylend caught her arm. “A word, Lyttie?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara! I haven’t been called that in 20 years,” Iolyt said, beaming up at him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Forgive me. Old habits die hard. You were only a child when Alo and I recruited you to the Hood.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” Iolyt said, placing a gentle hand on Lylend’s arm. “Dear Alo, how she is missed.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“More than you know,” Lylend agreed, taking Iolyt’s arm and threading it through his.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched the two of them walk away from the rest of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once they were out of sight, my first instinct was to talk to Zuli. When I looked for her, however, I only just caught the last sight of her tiger-striped tail slapping the water as she swam away from Feg.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Embarrassed, no doubt. She’d need time to process before we could talk all this through.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to find Lune-kyndie then. She was leaning against Brawlo—cuddling him really—who had curled up protectively around her on the sunny sand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I missed you too, sweet boy,” she said, nuzzling her face into the fur at the weasel’s neck. “I don’t know what Pieter’s problem is,” she continued. “He was like that from the moment I got here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo answered her with a low trill.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hated to break up their reunion, but truth be told, I had a million questions for Lune-kyndie considering everything Lylend had just revealed about her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, I cleared my throat. “Well…that was exciting,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lune-kyndie squinted up at me. “Yeah, a real blast,” she said dryly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So…your name is Noriah, huh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, if you don’t mind, I think I’ll keep calling you Lune-kyndie. Sounds a bit less ominous, you know?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She shrugged. “Okay. Whatever.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Grand. Wanna take a tour of Feg?” I asked. “Maybe do a rematch for that last Callowynder game?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She bit back a smile then. I watched as she patted Brawlo’s neck, who trilled happily as he snoozed. Then she stood up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sure. Why not?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that was that. My first time meeting Lylend—the Lylend, Lylend the legend, Lylend the Naridi—couldn’t have been more extraordinary if I’d dreamed it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If that wasn’t enough, Lune-kyndie was still alive and had come to help us defeat the Dogrils. All in all—aside from my dad and girlfriend both trying to kill my new friend—this was probably one of the best days of my life.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Lev's Map of Feg","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"c0fec6bcbf55469d8f32e720a8fe0bb5"}]},{"n":35,"letterTitle":"Misery","narrator":"Jamus","pieces":[{"title":"Misery","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You know the line from that Bon Jovi song? The one about his guitar bleeding in his arms or something?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wait, what? You don’t know who Bon Jovi is? Dude, look him up. He may be vintage, but he’s one of the greatest classic rockstars of the 90s. Gram was a huge fan…listened to his stuff all the time. She even dragged me to a few of his gigs whenever he came to Boston.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, there’s this line from one of his songs: “Misery likes company, I like the way that sounds. I’ve been trying to find the meaning so I can write it down.” It’s a little melodramatic, I give you, but I was drowning in misery after Gram died and Doc Lukens sent Norah away to Dunstan. I listened to that song like a million times to cope. I even thought I knew what it meant. Heck, I agreed with it. All I wanted after Gram died was Norah by my side, grieving right along with me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that ain’t the case anymore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ve been in this place (I’ve dubbed it the Dungeon) for…who knows how long. Night and day seem to run together, like they’re just one real long, horrible day of pain. The people in the green cloaks (I’ve nicknamed them Greenies) keep coming to visit me—to give me more of that crystal concoction to drink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I haven’t been fighting them. Thankfully they’ve explained a few things that has this making a bit more sense. I think they’re right, too—that this is what the Gihara want me to be doing right now. Heck, I even let them weave these weird crystal “threadlocks”—not sure what else to call them—through my beard and hair every time I make it through the aftermath of the drink. It’s some sort of ceremonial thing they do. Dad has a few threadlocks in his own beard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I keep asking the Greenies how much longer I’m gonna have to do this, though.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They keep telling me it depends. When I ask what it depends on, they say it depends on me—that who I am has to decide to stop fighting against who I’m meant to be—whatever that means.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, they told me to start writing things down, you know, like logging my experience here for posterity or historical purposes or something. There’s not a whole lot to write other than drinking that stuff hurts like Hades, and I’m glad Nor’s not here to see me like this, even if these threadlocks do make me look wicked cool.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Come to think of it, I actually started worrying about Nor the other day. I even asked the Greenies if she could be feeling any of this, her being my Kinzura and all. You know what they said? They said it was hard to tell, but they didn’t think the Gihara would allow that to happen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They didn’t think? Hold the phone. My girl’s out there somewhere and could be in as much pain as I’m in every time I drink that stuff, and they “think” the Gihara wouldn’t let it happen? Not good enough, folks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, I let myself believe ’em, you know? I was in so much pain, the idea of Nor feeling all this as well was unbearable. I just kinda blocked out the thought in order to manage, right? But then, a couple of nights ago, I wasn’t so sure anymore. I had this dream, see. At least I think I was asleep. All I know is I was still in the Dungeon and writhing in pain when I heard someone say my name. I swear to you, it was Nor’s voice. The problem was, I couldn’t open my eyes to be sure.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not until she reached out and touched me. Once I felt her fingers brush my skin, I opened my eyes just enough to see Nor jerk her hand away in pain. After that, I either woke up or, well…something happened because when I blinked, she was gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when the Greenies and I had ourselves a real heart-to-heart, if you know what I mean. I let them know in no uncertain terms that they had to do better than “think” the Gihara were protecting my girl from feeling what I was feeling every time I drank that stuff. If they didn’t, I wasn’t gonna be drinking anymore of their rock potion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So…moral of the story? Bon Jovi’s song is wrong. Misery—at least Cobbogothian misery where Kinzura bonds are concerned—does not like company. In fact, the lonelier the misery, the better if it means protecting the girl I love.","i":false}],"narrator":"Jamus"},{"title":"Wyndaloo Craft","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"85b3335b13db4ff282e01cdc2ba4de16"}]},{"n":36,"letterTitle":"The Lemorian Crest","narrator":"Lylend","pieces":[{"title":"The Lemorian Crest","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Of all the bull-headed, idiotic, konkles I’ve met in my time, Pieter is the worst. “Act first, think later.” It’s always been his motto. This isn’t the first time I’ve had to intervene to save the Hood from his stupidity, although I never took Pieter for a kyndie killer…even if he doesn’t like them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"How sweet Iolyt has put up with him all these years is a mystery. Brilliant, thoughtful, Lyttie. Thank the Gihara she had the sense to chime me when she did. I shudder to think what we’d be facing if Brawlo and I hadn’t reached Feg and Noriah when we did. We certainly wouldn’t have without Lyttie’s coordinates.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I can feel you judging me, human, but I never intended for the kyndie to set foot on Feg without me. I knew the consequences would be disastrous if she did. However, something I wasn’t expecting prevented me from reaching our rendezvous spot on time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I suppose I should just tell you and get it over with. Before she passed, Gram told me of a contact she’d made during her time spent in Holictac. I’d sent her to fire territory to keep an eye on Holictrian before and during Bruxrac.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This contact was a spy who’d infiltrated Holictrian’s highest ranks and had somehow managed to stay deep undercover in the fire nation’s leadership long after. Gram called the contact the Phoenix because of the spy’s symbol. During Bruxrac, the information Gram received through this contact is how we knew Holictrian was killing any and all who opposed him before he finally fell. Much later, the Phoenix alerted us to the Dogril’s return, 25 years ago.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With her dying breath, Gram made me promise that as soon as I managed to return to Cobbogoth I would reestablish communication with her contact. If Cifer managed to return, Gram was certain he would go to the Flame Kin-Klan first. If that proved to be the case, the contact would be invaluable in helping us stay ahead of him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The reason for my delay was to retrieve information Totherma had left for me at Leborana Falls and to fulfill my promise to Gram. She told me of a place not too far from the falls where Leboria Wood and the Albinian Grove meet at a bend in the Opaline River.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“On the banks of the river stands a single Unda tree,” she said. “I had Emerida put it there so I could be near salt water and execute my Naridi duties in the Glindune Desert.” She told me to seek out this tree, and if it still stood, the chances were good that her contact was still deep undercover. The Phoenix may have also been there recently to report on Cifer’s movements.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was skeptical of finding the Unda tree still alive after all these years. For starters, there isn’t a body of salt water for miles. However, it was there when I arrived at the spot, human. It stood on the banks of the Opaline. There were even signs of recent activity. I entered its root system as Gram instructed—not an easy feat for a spark spindler, by the way. And it’s a good thing I did. In the largest saltwater pocket, I found the messages I’ve included, along with a peculiar looking stone, the likes of which I’ve never seen in my entire 10 millennia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As soon as I’d rescued Noriah from Pieter’s stupidity and had the chance to speak with Lyttie alone, I showed her the stone. She is presently hard at work trying to discover what the stone is used for. In the meantime, we’ve stopped using all hoption holes {excepting the ones on Feg, of course}, which is why I missed my rendezvous with Noriah in the first place. When one is used to hoptioning everywhere, one forgets how bad you humans have it. Traveling by foot is agonizingly tedious.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Between that and quibbling with a cantankerous old earth kneader over a well-deserved and desperately missed 4,000-year-old truckle of Jombula cheese, I had two very worthy reasons for not arriving on time. I would’ve arrived even later had it not been for Brawlo, Gihara bless the creature. Totherma—or his “Lady,” as he worshipfully refers to her—sensed something amiss. When Brawlo told her I wasn’t on the shore to meet Noriah, she’d sent him to find me. I was so relieved to not have to walk any further, I gave him the rest of my Jombula cheese. For the record, human, your cheeses on Earth fall devastatingly short when compared to Cobbogothian cheeses. You never age them properly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Alas, all’s well that ends well. That’s what your Shakespearean bard wrote, isn’t it? Wise fellow. Good plays. Thank the Gihara some of you humans found the sense to use your brains for something useful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, once we got to Feg and saved the kyndie, I spoke with Lyttie about the spy-o-lite, and then she briefed me on the current state of things with the Hood. When that was done—and I’d had adequate time to properly grieve our depleted numbers—it was time to gather the rest of the Hood members for a council (what was left of them} and tell them all about what I’d discovered at Leborana Falls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of numbers, do you know, human, there were 1,037 Hood members {not including Gram and myself} when I left Cobbogoth 20 years ago? Gram and I had been recruiting them since Bruxrac, and it had taken centuries. Pieter took charge in our absence, and what had taken centuries to build, he’d undone in two decades. If his interaction with the kyndie is any indication of his leadership capacity, it’s safe to reason the konkle drove them all away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Including myself and the kyndie, only six Hood members remained. Calling a council in the old assembly hall at Feg’s Fork seemed rather ridiculous for such a small group, but “old habits die hard,” I believe I’ve heard you humans say. When all six of us were seated at the old banquet table, I told them the legend I’ve included for you to read. I wonder what you’ll make of it.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Spy Notes","kind":"webp","pages":6,"id":"cb31b4ccbe574fe7a00161379f0c21bf"},{"title":"Legend of the Lemorian Crest","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"895a26da59b04b7ab2ea73fe8bf87d4c"},{"title":"Norah's Q&A","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"becf432635064014a10e059c9b7ae475"},{"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Sina,","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"It has been a long time, my friend. I am attempting to reestablish contact again. I have kept my place with the flame covyns as you asked, and what you predicted has come to pass.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Cifer has returned.","i":true},{"t":"brk"},{"t":"p","x":"Sina,","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"He is gathering a strong following among the flame covyns. Being outcasts since Bruxrac, they are the most susceptible to his influences. Keep your children close.","i":true},{"t":"brk"},{"t":"p","x":"Sina,","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"He is more powerful than last time. This stone is one of his first weapons. It is called spyolite. Do not use the hoption holes.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":37,"letterTitle":"The Sina Bracelet","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"The Sina Bracelet (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"By now you’re probably thinking I’m the villain in this whole tale, and that’s fair. I’m sure Lev has tried to tell you I’m really a “sweetheart once you get to know me,” that I’ve been having a “hard time of it,” which is why I’ve been acting so zerky lately. And he’s right, you know. I have had a hard year—several years, really. And I am a nice person once you get to know me—once I realize you’re not trying to harm my people or steal my man.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All that said, you can imagine the horror I felt when Lylend and Brawlo showed up and told us who the lune-kyndie really is. I mean, she is the key to saving my people from Lamianza once and for all, and I almost killed her. Until now it has felt like an uphill battle in Lunegalda sand. I was so angry with myself for doing what I always do—acting rashly and passionately before thinking—that I couldn’t bear to face Lev. I knew he’d understand. I mean, we’ve known each other for a long time, and while my being seventeen years his senior (the equivalent of barely a few months difference to you) may seem weird to you, Lev is actually much wiser than I am in a lot of ways.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that’s not important. What is important is I realize I was wrong. I’ve been spending the last week since Lylend arrived trying to make it up to Noriah. First I faced Lev and made up with him. Then I tried to improve my chance of gaining Noriah’s forgiveness by giving her my most fashionable weaving stones so she could do something about that drab little shyntara she’s been wearing since I found her at the City of Stones. She accepted the gift with more grace than I expected, but I have yet to see her wear any of them. I even selected the stones I thought would work best with her peculiar lune-coloring. I mean, if I’d been trapped in such dowdy clothes for as long as she had, you couldn’t keep me from using the stones, even if they had been given to me by someone who tried to kill me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I told Lev, he shrugged and said, “I don’t know anyone who cares about clothes like you do, Zu. Besides, she probably thinks you booby trapped them or something.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is exactly the gist of what he said after I gifted Noriah a Rodãor of her own only to find out she wasn’t using it. I gave it to her when I discovered she’d been sharing a living space with that monstrosity shudder. Yeesh! The creature makes even my blood run cold, and I’m a sina!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before you accuse me of trying to buy Noriah’s forgiveness with gifts, it was Iolyt’s suggestion. She even helped me choose a Rodãor from the castoffs left behind by former Hood members when Pieter’s temper forced them to abandon us. The Rodãor wasn’t even much to look at—just a main living/cooking/lounging room as well as rooms for sleeping and bathing. Iolyt, Lev, and I scrounged up enough furnishings from the other abandoned Rodãors to make some semblance of a home for her. But even Iolyt and Lev being involved wasn’t enough. The very next day, I entered Iolyt’s healing room to find Noriah asleep in one of the healing hammocks. When I asked Iolyt about it, she said the lune-kyndie hadn’t even bothered going inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All Lev did when I told him was shrug and say “booby trapped” in that irritating I told you so tone he gets when he ends up being right. Which isn’t as often as he thinks, by the way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"> “Give her time,” Iolyt soothed. “You did try to kil her, lybkin. It will take more than","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"a few gifts to win her trust. You’ll have to earn her trust—show her in no uncertain terms that you mean her no harm.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So that’s what I did. When Lylend proposed we begin looking for the Lemorian keys at the Adolorian ruins, I volunteered to partner with Noriah and give her the grand tour of our once great kingdom.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To say she was less than enthusiastic about the arrangement is a grim understatement, but I won’t waste time going into that. In the end, practicality won out, and it was settled by Lylend. As Noriah is one of a mere handful of people alive (Lylend included) who can read ancient Cobbogothian runes, he agreed to my plan. He and Lev would be traveling to Melusian’s Barracks in order to provide the distraction meant to keep the Rayves from discovering what the two of us were up to.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I greeted Noriah and Lev on Feg’s eastern shore the following morning, I knew Noriah and I still had a ways to go before “trust” replaced the guarded suspicion still very much in her behavior toward me. I’d spoken to Lylend the night before after she flat out refused to accompany me on today’s outing. Lylend told me how you humans are much slower to forgive attempted drowning than Cobbogothians. It wasn’t until he explained your shocking fragility that I finally began to understand. Lucky for me, Lev beat Noriah in a Callowynder match late last night, and as his Callowynder request, he insisted she accompany me today. I would have preferred it to be her choice—wanting to build trust and all—but I wasn’t about to waste a perfectly good opportunity, was I?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that is why today I decided to make my own gesture of trust—the grandest gesture of trust a sina can make toward a non-sina.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After Lylend gave us our final instructions, I presented my gesture of trust to Noriah. “Another gift?” she asked, grimacing more than smiling her thanks. She gingerly opened the bejeweled, stone box it was enclosed in and looked inside. Then she closed the box. “It’s beautiful, thank you,” she said, somewhat woodenly. I bit my cheek, willing myself to not be offended. It was clear she had no idea what I had just given her. Thankfully Lylend and Lev did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara, Kyndie! That’s a sacred honor, that is,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I thought you said sina bracelets were forbidden if you weren’t a born Adolorian,” Lev said, sounding both awestruck and hurt as he watched Noriah open the box again and then cautiously slide the bracelet onto her wrist. Okay, this was progress.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“These are special circumstances,” I replied, standing on my tippy toes and comforting Lev with a peck on the cheek. “If we can’t find the Lemorian Crest and whatever it contains, there won’t be any Adolorians left to care who wears a sina bracelet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are we sure this is a good idea?” Noriah was trying hard not to direct her question at Lylend—still angry at him, no doubt—but I could tell she intended the question for him. “Isn’t this kind of like ‘playing with fire?’ No pun intended.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend shook his head. “That bracelet neutralizes any threat the water could possibly pose for you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Any threat?” she asked meaningfully.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was just out of the corner of my eye, but I was sure I caught her nod ever so slightly in my direction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Any threat,” Lylend assured her with a wink, then gave me an encouraging smile. I, on the other hand, had to bite down on my cheek again. Honestly! What more proof did she need that I meant her no harm?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Again, I don’t blame her—or you—for thinking I am the villain in this story. If you still don’t trust me, trust in my love for my people. Like I said earlier, the lune-kyndie is the key to saving the Adolorians. I would lay down my life before I allowed any other harm to come to the “final hope” of my people.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"The Sina Bracelet (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So I’m not speaking to Lylend. I know what you’re thinking; didn’t he just save my life? Yeah, MY life. When it comes to his son’s life, though, he’s not willing to bother.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Remember those visions I’ve been having of James? The ones where he’s burning up? Well, they’re getting worse. James is still lying on that stone table, fire surging through his veins as he writhes in pain, and I’m still feeling the burning. Only it’s gotten so much worse.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, I was in Iolyt’s healing room the other day—I’ve been sleeping there for the time being—and she was treating me for several third degree burns on my arms I’d woken up with. Someone apparently told Lylend about it (Lev you traitor) because he came to see me there. It was the first time we’d been able to talk since he stopped Pieter from burying me alive. He asked what happened, and I told him about the visions. I told him how I didn’t even have to touch James to get burned now. I just had to be close to him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"How did Lylend respond to this? It seriously didn’t even phase the guy. Actually, when I told him his son seems to be burning up from the inside-out, he looked excited!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I get it, Cobbogothians are tough—like back-on-Earth-you-have-no-idea tough—but they aren’t heartless. Not all of them anyway. Lylend wouldn’t even consider the idea of going to look for James, let alone rescuing him from…well, whatever it is that’s happening to him. Instead, he just kept trucking on with this whole Lemorian Crest thing. Which is important. I get that, okay. I am the Haven. But James is his son!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that’s only one reason I’m not talking to Lylend. The other is because he’s paired me up with a homicidal maniac for our first mission to start tracking down these Lemorian Crest keys. That’s right. I get to follow Princess Zuli the sina into the depths of the Glindian Sea less than a week after she tried to drown me in my crystal cell. So not cool.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s going to be all right, lune-kyndie,” Lev said, before following Lylend down to Feg’s fin. The fin was more like a peninsular sandbank that served as a sort of dock and launching point for the Hood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unfortunately, those were Lev’s only words of comfort before going to join Lylend and wading out into the water on the other side of the sandbank.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the distance, I could see Lylend produce a ball of blue electricity. Lev followed suit with his own green one. I keep forgetting he’s a verowyn. It throws me off every time he switches between his manifestations.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It took me a long time to not be jealous of him for being a verowyn.” I jumped to find Zuli standing beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are they doing?” I wondered aloud.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’re catching their ride.” She pointed back out at the water. “See?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed her gesture. Lylend and Lev had turned their spheres of electricity into giant thick ropes with lassos at the end. Soon they were both swinging them over their heads like a couple of old-fashioned cowboys.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was just remembering how much Uncle Jack loved watching old westerns when IT happened. I jumped, certain I’d swallowed my tongue, when two enormous creatures burst out of the water and right into the lassos of both electric ropes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are—are those—?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sparking serpents,” Zuli replied. “They’re naturally drawn to the electricity.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend and Lev were whooping and hollering in celebration. They jumped onto the serpents’ backs and began wrestling them into submission. In no time, all I could see of the serpents were two great dark masses slithering just under the surface of the water. With the electric ropes wound securely around their Viking-sized forearms, Lev and Lylend made the creatures rear up out of the water, thrashing. Again, I was reminded of cowboys and bull riding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With a final whoop and holler from both of them atop their sparking serpents, Lylend and Lev dove into the sea, leaving only a ripple of waves in their wake.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was still somewhat awestruck when I felt a hand on my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We should get going,” Zuli said. “Their distraction won’t happen for a while now, but we need all the time we can get.” She glanced at me then. “Are you ready?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, swallowing a new wave of fear. Zuli’s behavior toward me had changed since Lylend told everyone who and what I was. She’d been giving me stuff—stuff I wasn’t about to use, in case it was one more way for her to off me. The fact that Lev and Iolyt trusted her—were involved in giving me the rodãor—was causing me to soften a little toward her. But that was part of what I was afraid of. What if she was just waiting for me to let my guard down?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good. Follow me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook away my doubts and followed. After all, Lylend may be willing to play roulette with James’ life, but I knew he wasn’t willing to risk my own. He’d said as much when we spoke—even gave it as his main reason for not going to James’ rescue, said he couldn’t risk Cifer finding out about me and the fact that I was the Haven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli led the way down Feg’s fin until we were waist-deep in water. “So where are Pieter and Iolyt?” I asked, in order to distract myself from memories of my last several encounters with water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lylend gave Iolyt a stone he found between here and Leborana Falls. It’s a new stone none of us have ever seen before. Spy-o-lite, he called it. Iolyt is staying behind to study it—see what it does.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And Pieter?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He’s staying behind to ensure she stays safe while analyzing it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We were up to our chests in water now, and I was struggling to keep my breathing calm. Zuli had instructed me to keep the sina bracelet above water until she gave the signal, so I was holding one arm above my head as I walked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” she finally instructed. “You can lower your arm now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ll be honest, I’m not now, nor have I ever been, the type of person who trusts easily. Having Uncle Jack always away from home and being mostly in charge of raising myself, trust doesn’t come naturally. Sure, Zuli had made a few gestures to try to apologize for trying to drown me, and I did appreciate them—if they were sincere—but this whole sina bracelet thing was a different story. This wasn’t something I could say “thanks but no thanks” to and not risk her going “zyrks” on me, as Lev liked to say. Buuuutttt…I really didn’t have a choice at this point. And we were wasting time. So I lowered the bracelet into the water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come a little further out,” Zuli coaxed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a strange buzzing in my ears now. I hesitated but then took another step…and another…and then another.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, my entire head plunged under the water. I’d forgotten the sandbank was actually a drop-off.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The buzzing stopped as my blood turned to ice. I could hear my pulse galloping in my ears. My eyes were blurry, and all I could see was a blob of yellow, orange, and black stripes coming toward me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m ashamed to say I panicked. Pushing against the water, I turned, trying to find the edge of the drop-off, but I couldn’t feel it anywhere. I tried to swim for the surface, but when I kicked, it was like my legs were glued together. I looked down to see what was wrong, but my vision was still blurry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A sharp pain ripped into both my sides, like someone had inserted a knife between my ribs and was trying to pry them apart. Had the sina stabbed me? I began clawing my way toward the surface with my arms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt Zuli take me by the shoulders. “Stop struggling,” she said. I heard her perfectly—but her voice was different. It was echoey and musical.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I struggled against her, but her grip was too strong. “You’re going to be okay,” she said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just then, as if on cue, my lungs expanded. My mouth wasn’t open and my nose was plugged, but I could breathe…under water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Realization dawned, and I stopped struggling. I reached down, touching my sides where the sharp pain was now dulling. Sure enough, just below my armpits, along the diagonal space between my ribs, there were two long slits.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gills!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My rushing pulse began to slow. I felt my eyes begin to harden—sharpen—and then everything came into focus.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli released her grip on my shoulders. She swam back, hovering in the water before me, her tiger-striped tail waving back and forth. Was she smiling?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” I said, then immediately clamped my hand to my mouth. My voice didn’t sound like mine anymore. It was high and musical like Zuli’s. It bounced around in the water, almost like an echo in a canyon but more…magical.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nothing,” Zuli said, holding a hand up and gesturing to my body. “I mean, you’re breathtaking and everything, but it’s going to be hard to camouflage you down here, lune-kyndie,” she said, emphasizing the nickname Lev had given me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Huh?” I looked down and immediately understood what she was talking about. My body, once clothed in a pale green shyntara and ranalin, was now covered in scales, copious angel-like fins, and a mermaid tail, and every inch of me was as creamy pale and platinum as my skin and hair had been.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“As hard as camouflaging the moon in a midnight sky,” I agreed.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Sina Coloring Page","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"231d9d3711e549db82c240fbb765a88f"}]},{"n":38,"letterTitle":"The Princess and Her Palace","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"The Princess and Her Palace","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I still couldn’t believe when I told Lylend about the fire raging like an inferno under James’ skin and he did nothing—didn’t even acknowledge it—just said, “Oh, that reminds me, kyndie. I was supposed to give you something.” After summoning Shudder to bring him that “something” from his rodãor, he handed me a rock. A rock! I’ll be honest, I almost threw it at his head. It was like we were back in the Crystal City again, and James was about to pay the blood sum. I understood why Lylend couldn’t intervene then, but this is different.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I asked him—pretty sarcastically—if the rock was some kind of forgetting stone to help me forget that his son was most likely being tortured somewhere, Lylend only chuckled and said, “Don’t be so humanly ridiculous, kyndie.” Then he explained that the stone actually did the opposite, which is the only reason I didn’t throw it at his head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend called it a memory stone. Apparently, James had made it for me before he knew I was a Cobbogothian. He wanted me to have some way of remembering him when I was left on Earth by myself after he and Lylend came back here. Thank the Gihara things didn’t turn out that way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Don’t get me wrong, I was ecstatic to have something James had made specifically for me. It was just like him to think of me at a time like that. Unfortunately, I still don’t know how to use the memory stone. Lylend was off with Iolyt making plans for finding the Lemorian keys before I could ask him. Honestly, I was too angry to ask him anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I don’t know, maybe the fact that Lylend has lived so long has deluded him into thinking James is just as resistant to death as he is. Or maybe it’s the fact that I just lost Uncle Jack—and thought I’d lost James—a few weeks ago that is causing me to worry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wait a second…what am I saying here? I was acting perfectly normal for a person who thought the boy she loved was being tortured. It was Lylend who was acting completely nuts-o! And I didn’t just “think” James was being tortured anymore; I was almost certain.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of course, I had to set all my worry for James aside once Lylend got our first lead on the location of one of the Lemorian keys. Apparently, he and Iolyt found some documentation of Adolor the water kin-chief mentioning a mysterious key. They discovered it in one of the crystal scrolls Lylend brought with him from Leborana Falls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Lev tried—not very hard—to prepare me for what we might face when we were under the Glindian Sea. It went something like: “Hey, lune-kyndie, watch out for the sparking serpents while you’re down there, huh? And, oh yeah, maybe stay away from any caves cuz that’s where the kraken live.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yeah, he was that helpful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In hindsight, maybe he could have added something like: “You might want to watch out for the Lunegalda sand. It’s the blue-black shimmery stuff that can kill you.” Or…“You’re going to want to avoid the Gihara blooms. They’ll give you away to the Rayves (who also want to kill you) faster than if you yelled your location at them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Honestly. I know he meant well, but he could’ve been a bit more helpful, especially since I was already super nervous about being partnered with Zuli.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know I sound a bit like a harpy in this chronicle, and to be honest, I felt like one for a while there. But that was all before everything between Zuli and me changed. The change was so sudden, unexpected, and drastic that I forgot to be angry anymore. And it was all thanks to Gram Riley—or Gramillia, as she was known here in Cobbogoth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The change happened after we’d been swimming for a while. Zuli just finished telling me about the bad blood between the Adolorians and the Rayves (see my journal notes) when we came upon what can only be described as an underwater mountain range. The following conversation followed, changing absolutely everything I thought I knew about Gram and Zuli the Adolorian princess.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We’d reached the base of the nearest mountain when she stopped and turned to me. “How much do you know about the Lamianza plague?” she asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, um…well…” I shrugged, a little taken aback by the shift in the conversation. “I guess only what I know from having watched someone I loved die from it.” I thought of Gram wasting away in front of us, no doctor able to save her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli looked surprised at my answer, then her brow drew down in confusion. “Wait. Who would you have known with Lamianza if you’ve been in the Olden Realm all this time?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Her name was Gram,” I said, as we started swimming again. We were nearing a cave entrance, and I immediately thought of Lev’s warning about kraken. “Sh-she lived across the street from Uncle Jack and me. Actually, she raised Lylend’s son James—er—Jamus while Lylend was with Uncle Jack doing…well, other stuff.” I’d stopped and was about to protest getting any nearer to the cave when Zuli stopped as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Gram?” Zuli asked. “As in Gramillia the Naridi?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “Yeah. You know her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli nodded her head slowly, eyes widening. “I should say so.” She examined my face as though she was seeing me for the first time. “Gramillia was my seventh great-grandmother.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait, what!?” I couldn’t imagine the youthful, lovely Gram (at least before she got really sick) being someone’s grandmother, let alone great-grandmother. But then she was Cobbogothian—and a Naridi. So…yeah. Totally possible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She was also my mentor,” Zuli continued, touching a turquoise jewel with a sad, distant look in her eyes. I’d never noticed the jewel before. It was set in a silver cuff she wore around her right bicep. I realized what it was, just as Zuli continued. “The last time I saw her, she gave me her qualdrine—told me to use it to keep my people alive. After that she told me to find and join the Hood.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now I was the one looking at Zuli as though I was seeing her for the first time. “I’m so sorry,” I said. “If I’d known, I wouldn’t have broken the news of her death to you like that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli shook her head. “No, it’s all right. I’ve known she was gone for some time now. You don’t inherit someone’s qualdrine and not sense when they’ve gone back to the Gihara. That, of course, and everyone in Cobbogoth knows when a Naridi has passed on.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right,” I said distractedly. My mind was still being blown by this sudden discovery, the fact that this tempestuous sina was a relative of Gram’s—strong, steady, soothing Gram. Maybe there was more to this princess than I originally thought.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Listen,” she said, turning to face me fully, “about trying to drown you the other day? I feel terrible…I honestly didn’t know humans were so fragile…I mean, what I’m trying to say is I’ve tried to apologize a few times indirectly, but you deserve a direct apology from me…and an explanation.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head then. “Don’t worry about it,” I said, and I realized I actually meant it. Something about knowing she was related to Gram had me seeing her in a whole new light—understanding her. “Besides,” I shrugged, “I might’ve done something similar if I thought you were trying to steal James from me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, but I’m so sorry—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look,” I said holding up my hands to stop her. “Lev is your kinzura, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then I’m over it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli hesitated uncertainly for a moment, then finally she smiled. It was an unmistakably genuine smile, and suddenly I saw the family resemblance. Gram was definitely related to this girl.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m not sure what came over me next, reader, because you’ve got to know by now that I’m not a big hugger. But something completely unlike, well, me came over me in that moment, and I reached out and awkwardly pulled Zuli in for a hug. “The girl who rendered you unconscious?” you ask. “Who tried to drown you less than a week ago?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The answer is: yes, that girl. I don’t know how to explain it; it just seemed like the fastest way to put the past behind us. This was Gram’s granddaughter, after all. Gram was practically James’ mom. She was the closest thing I’d ever had to a mother figure in my own life. The fact that Gram lived on in this hot-headed sina made me want to be her friend more than I’d ever wanted to be anyone’s friend before. And I was pretty sure she wanted to be my friend too because this wasn’t an awkward, one-way hug anymore. Zuli was hugging me back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ve never had a friend that was a girl before,” I said, more to myself than to her. “Or a sina.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli laughed. It was the first time I’d ever heard it. “Then it won’t be too hard for me to be the best one you’ve ever had,” she said with a squeeze. She held me away from her then and looked me straight in the eyes. “Because that’s what I’m determined to be to you from now on, Noriah.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now it was my turn to smile. Only I didn’t just smile; I’m pretty sure I grinned like an idiot. I mean, my first “girl” friend wasn’t only Gram’s seventh great-granddaughter, but she was also a princess and pretty much a mermaid. So, yeah…try and beat that back on Earth, I dare you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, enough sentimentality. Moving on…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli took my hand then. “Come on,” she said. “They’ll be reaching Melusian’s barracks by now.” We began to swim again. We swam inside the cave we’d been hovering outside of when all the above happened, and somehow it didn’t look so scary anymore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is a secret passageway that only the royals of my kingdom know about,” Zuli explained as we swam through the darkness. “The Adolorian ruins are at the other end. You’re going to love my home, Noriah.” Zuli seemed much more upbeat and happier than I’d seen her since meeting her. “In fact,” she said, suddenly quickening her pace, “now that you’ve reminded me of Gram, I think I might actually know where to look for Uncle Adolor’s key.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where’s that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s a legend,” she began excitedly, swimming faster. “It speaks of a secret chamber—a record room—hidden somewhere near the throne in the palace. Gram used to play a game with me in the throne room when I was just a kyndie. She would have me look at the throne and try to find shapes in the intricate carvings. Every time I saw a trident in the carvings—there were several—she would always say, ‘It’s the seventh one, Zuli. Always remember the seventh one.’ I never understood what she meant until now. I think she was trying to show me where the door to the room is. Why would she do that if it wasn’t important, and what could be more important than finding and unlocking the Lemorian Crest?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just as she finished, the black cavern suddenly grew lighter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa…what’s that?” I asked, pulling on her to slow down. The ground beneath our feet was beginning to glow. The luminescence appeared to be coming from the shimmering blue-black sand below us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh. That. I meant to warn you about that. It’s Lunegalda,” Zuli explained. “Moon sand. It’s only found in the Lunegalda mountain range.” She gestured to indicate that was where we were now. “It’s the reason this is such a good secret passage to Adoloria. No one wants to come near Lunegalda.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why not?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because Lunegalda sand is dangerous,” she said, beginning to move again. “If it gets in your bloodstream, it’s deadly. Just try not to touch it, and you’ll be fine.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Great,” I said, scooting closer to the center of the cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t worry,” Zuli said, squeezing my hand. “Stay with me, and we’ll be through here in no time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I can’t tell you how long we swam in that glowing cave before it finally spit us out the other end. Regardless, boy was I glad to be out of it. I discovered later that even a slight scratch from the walls of the cave, which were also made of Lunegalda, could’ve been deadly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let’s take a different route back to Feg, huh?” I asked when we came out the other end of the tunnel. Before Zuli could answer, we were both blinded by a brightly colored garden of enormous, slightly glowing flower buds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What in the world! Does everything down here glow?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Gihara blooms,” Zuli said. “Just be careful not to touch—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it was too late. My tail had accidentally smacked one of the flowers as I swam past. The next moment, it was like the Fourth of July. The entire garden burst to life and light as the flowers rapidly bloomed like fireworks in the sky. It wouldn’t have been such a bad thing if the garden didn’t go on for miles and miles over a seafloor of rolling hills.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Suddenly I was face down on the seafloor, with Zuli practically sitting on my back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get off, Zu—!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hush! Lay completely still!” she hissed in my ear. She was still on my back, but now she was scurrying around clouding the water with not blue-black—thankfully—but pearly white sand. That’s when I realized she was burying me with it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are you—!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She clamped a hand over my mouth as she quickly burrowed her own tail and body under the sand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A wild thrashing in the water caused us both to freeze. “Not a word,” Zuli mouthed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could argue with her, a mini tsunami of blurred color and movement burst over a hill not too far away from us. When the blurring finally slowed, several of the most peculiar looking sea people I’d ever seen swam toward us, riding a herd of kelpies.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Rayves!” Zuli hissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could feel dread rolling off her as she pushed more sand over her hair and shoulders to camouflage herself. I was lucky. I was already as white as the sand, so I reached over to help her when something cold touched my arm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jerked around. If Zuli’s hand hadn’t clamped over my mouth again, I would’ve screamed at what I saw.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A Rayve with milky, pupilless eyes and dreadlock-like octopus tentacles for hair blinked wide at me from less than a foot away. I shuddered as her webbed hand slowly slid from my arm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, thank the Gihara!” Zuli breathed at my side. “Mala! I’m so glad to see you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mala” nodded, and once Zuli removed her hand from my mouth, I shrank back into the sand. This Rayve was distinctly female, but so weird looking. Thankfully, though, she also happened to be a friend of Zuli’s who had shown up just in time to keep us from becoming Rayve bait.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After Zuli explained to Mala what we were up to, the Rayve swam to the top of the Gihara blooms looming over us and distracted her fellow kelpie-riding Rayves. In the end, Zuli and I were able to slip away unnoticed through an umbrella of countless Gihara blooms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I asked Zuli why Mala had helped us, she explained that Mala was Melusian’s favorite daughter but had become the Hood’s ally over a decade ago. When I asked why Zuli told me such a familiar mermaid story, I almost laughed out loud.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As it turns out, Mala fell in love with a guy up on land a while back—an earth kneader or spark spindler by the name of Romany—something like that. Anyway, the problem was that she was of the sea and he was of land (sound familiar?), so they couldn’t be together. Not without some sort of “magical” help. In the hopes of one day being able to be with him above water, Mala decided to begin helping the Hood in exchange for Zuli healing her from Lamianza long enough that Mala could periodically visit her boy Romany.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I asked Zuli what Mala hoped for in the end, she told me it was what they all hoped for: that the Hood would figure out a way to bring qualdriny back and defeat the dogrils—and now Cifer—once and for all. Then Lamianza would be gone for good, and Mala and her man could be together all the time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I guess it’s not word-for-word like the fairytale we all know and love, but there are some pretty stark similarities. So much so that I couldn’t help humming a song from a famous cartoon it reminded me of the rest of the way to the Adolorian ruins. “Sha-la-la-la-la-la don’t be shy…etc.” You know the one.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally reached the ruins, however, I was confused. “Um…where do ya’ll sleep?” I asked. All it looked like when we swam into the clearing where the city was supposed to be was a kelp-covered, albeit colorful, henge—like Stonehenge—with a giant, disk-shaped boulder at its center.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli smiled knowingly. “You’ll see.” Then she told me to take cover behind a small crystal pillar not too far from the henge. “You’d better hang onto something really tight,” she said before swimming back to the clearing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when things got impressive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From where I hid, I didn’t see the symbol light up on the boulder disk until after Zuli had done this crazy, spinny, whirlpool trick where she lifted the stone into the sea above the clearing. That’s when it was clear the disk wasn’t just a disk but a giant rodãor. The glowing rodãor symbol unraveled into a giant circle of light and dropped to the sand in the center of the henge. Suddenly, the seafloor began to shake, and an enormous glowing hole in the ground began to open. Zuli beckoned to me, and I joined her where she was swimming over what was now a hole in the seafloor about the size of a football field.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa!” I breathed. Inside the rodãor was an almost exact replica of the Crystal City from back in Iceland under Mount Hekla. Only this one looked like it’d been built by a bunch of mermaids instead of Cobbogothians.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli explained that Queen Citrine had pretended to blow up the Adolorian’s city after the Rayve rebellion in order to keep Melusian from overrunning it. Really she’d hidden it inside the giant rodãor in the hopes that the Adolorians would one day be able to return to it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli led the way into the rodãor. It felt like I was back in Mount Hekla again, only this time Mount Hekla was full of water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally made it to the throne room, it wasn’t long before we figured out that Zuli was right about Gram preparing her to find the secret record room behind the throne. The “seventh trident” turned out to be a lever that opened the door to the record room. Inside we found a blue stone tablet engraved with “The Final Words of Adolor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could translate any further, however, Zuli came rushing toward me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We have to go…now!” she said, taking the tablet. She tucked it under her arm and grabbed my hand, swimming toward the door.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We just got here,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s something wrong,” she explained. Then she stopped, holding perfectly still and closing her eyes. Then she gasped. “Did you feel that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. The entire record room had just shuddered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If it’s shaking in here, it’s going to be much worse out there. Ready?” she asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we stepped out into the throne room, it looked like an earthquake was raging.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s happening?” I yelled over the deafening noise.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before she could answer, Zuli pushed me out of the way as a giant pillar slammed down where my tail had just been.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We didn’t waste time talking after that. Instead, we swam through the record room, out of the city, and back toward Feg without looking back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hopefully Lylend and Lev had already done the same.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Secret Message #7","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"dcef918ed93143308150627be512feeb"},{"title":"Journal: Ray & Lunegalda Sand & Gihara Blooms","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"93b9ae0a10b045409489ba5e4aff7987"},{"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Final Words of Adolor","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"With my dying breath, I leave my final words for Our Final Hope: The Crest key shall henceforth be hidden in the ark of my royal ruling mark.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":39,"letterTitle":"Melusian’s Keep","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"Melusian’s Keep","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You know how sometimes you totally mean for things to go one way, and then, “surprise!” they go completely the opposite, and it’s really bad. You don’t? Well, I do. Surprisingly, that sort of thing happens to me a lot more than to most people. I mean, I’m a nice fella. I don’t mean anybody any harm, but when someone comes up to me and starts talking about my kyndie Zu like that? Well, I can’t just let that sort of thing go now, can I?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Visiting Melusian’s Keep was a bad idea. I saw that from the start, but what other choice did we have? Melusian is a tyrant. Has been since the day he invaded Adoloria and ran Zuli’s kin-klan out of the Glindian Sea. He runs his keep like a military zone. He sends his Rayves out on multiple perimeter checks every day, always at random times so no one can ever get familiar with the patrol schedule. Lylend and I both knew Zu and Nori—that’s what Zu and I decided to start calling the lune-kyndie—didn’t have a chance of searching the old ruins without some sort of distraction to keep the Rayves off their tails.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of tails, I wonder what Nori looks like as a sina. Bet she stuck out like a lunestone. Zu must’ve loved that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, what I’m trying to say is I should’ve seen this coming the moment Lylend and I rode into Rayve territory.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"See, Melusian is one of those paranoid, unstable guys who has squadrons of no less than ten Rayve gaurds at every possible lookout point surrounding his keep. Like I said earlier, he runs his territory like a war zone, which I suppose it still is, even though the Adolorian civil war has been over for a decade now. But that hasn’t stopped the sina from exacting revenge on their scalier brethren. You see, Lamianza-infected sina who are beyond saving have been organizing themselves into anarchist cells. They call themselves the Sinarene. Over the past decade, they’ve become infamous for volunteering for suicidal missions, launching fragorine stones and Lunegalda darts into groups of patrolling Rayves every chance they get. Zu has explained it all to me, and it’s one of the reasons she didn’t try to counter her mother’s banishment of her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Citrine openly condemns the Sinarene, but everyone knows she’s behind it all. She’s been funding their missions and turning a blind eye for years. Zu says her mother supports them because the missions not only end the Sinarenes’ suffering sooner, but they give the sick an opportunity to make their deaths count toward the greater good of their kin-klan. Don’t tell Zu, but I kinda think her mom is, well…zyrky, which is the equivalent of “crazy” in the Olden Realm, according to Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Zu’s been opposed to the Sinarene from the start. I’m with her on this. They are seriously getting in the way of the possible reconciliation she and Mala are trying to bring about between the Adolorians.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know what you’re thinking. If Zuli is so against the Sinarene and killing and stuff, why did she try to drown Nori. All I can say to that is sometimes Zu can’t make the connection. She just goes a little zyrky herself, you know? But aren’t all kyndies a little zyrky when it comes to their kinzura? Aren’t we all? I mean, that’s why I found myself in the situation I was in at Melusian’s Keep, after all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So the sparking serpents take us right up to the keep without being stopped by a Rayve patrol, right? We’re almost to the gate when, out of nowhere, at least 50 Rayves ride up on their kelpies and hold a bunch of coral spears to our throats. I’m thinking, “Man these guys are ugly.” But then Lylend tells them who he is, right? And if you thought I was impressed by the famous Naridi, you should’ve seen these guys. The locked down, ultra-rigid military base that is Melusian’s Keep pretty much instantly turned into the Triolune Festival. Apparently Melusian and Lylend go way back. Melusian was almost excited to see his long-lost friend, which is a lot of emotion for him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We get inside the barracks, and the party is just getting started. Lylend’s treating everyone to one of his legendary spark shows—which are incredible, by the way—when Mala, of all Rayves, comes up and starts talking to me. That is if you can call her way of speaking talking. It’s more like gurgling and bubbles.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, kraken-head (Melusian’s son) over here doesn’t like the idea of me, an earth kneader, talking to his sister, right? He’s not putting up with that at all. So what does he do? He and seven or eight of his other fishy friends come over and tell me they don’t like me talking to one of their kyndies. But Mala was telling me about running into Zu and Nori out by the Lunegalda mountain range. She tells me about the Gihara blooms drawing the attention of her brother and his patrol to the kyndies’ hiding places. Then she tells me how long ago she saw them, and I’m thinking there’s no way they’ll be able to search the ruins if we don’t buy them more time than we originally planned. But kraken-head doesn’t care about my dilemma. He’s got one thing on his mind, and that’s battle. Seriously, this guy won’t stop trying to pick a fight with me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So…I gave him battle. In hindsight, not the smartest decision I’ve ever made. Kraken-head didn’t realize he was dealing with a verowyn, see. Once he did, me against seven or eight Rayves turned into me against more than 20 Rayves real quick.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What was I supposed to do, though? Let some fishhead kick me around just for talking to his sister? I mean, I tried to make it fair. I offered to settle things with him over a good, clean Callowynder match, but he didn’t want anything to do with that. He pretty much left me no choice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d be lying if I said I wasn’t, well, thrilled to finally be able to try out some of the spark spindling Lylend’s been teaching me since he showed up. I just kinda wish the stakes hadn’t been so high.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, when the sand settled and the battle was over, I was the only one left standing. Nobody actually died, so there is that. But unfortunately, I might’ve, sort of, maybe broken Melusian’s entire keep during the battle. I mean, I’ve been trying to get my water wielding and spark spindling down for a couple years now, but it’s hard without a teacher. Needless to say, even with all the things Lylend’s shown me, it’s pretty clear I’ve got to work on my control.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the end, Lylend and I got kicked out of Melusian’s Keep with a crystal-clear invitation to never return. You can probably imagine Lylend wasn’t too happy about that. In fact, I’m pretty sure the only reason Melusian didn’t order his Rayves to kill us on the spot is because Lylend saved his life a bunch of times back in the day. So yeah. Way to be a legend, Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only we’d convinced Melusian to give us the trident—aka the water key to the Lemorian Crest—before we got kicked out, none of this would be such a big deal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"On the bright side though, with all the partying, battling, and getting kicked out of Melusian’s Keep, we ended up buying Zu and Nori the time they needed to search the ruins for Lemorian keys. And really, when all is said and done, isn’t that what matters here?","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Melusian's Map","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f48c272209594210a302d163034c2c27"},{"title":"Fragorine","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2f527cdab05944759d03219bfbff9265"}]},{"n":40,"letterTitle":"The Visitor","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"The Visitor","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If I were to take a guess, I’d say this is probably somewhere around day 25 of my time in the dungeon, give or take a week. It’s not like the greenies keep a calendar on the wall or anything. Consequently, everything sort of keeps blending together.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The further along I go in this whole “metamorphosis” process, the harder it is to remember stuff. Like Earth stuff: sports—the Red Sox and Patriots, Fourth of July fireworks with the Pops, Dunkin’ Donuts, pizza. That sort of stuff.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, it took me a full 15 minutes to remember what a cannoli was—what it tasted like—just the other day. You better believe growing up in Boston I ate my fair share of cannoli, so there’s no way I should forget that taste. But I did for a little while, and it was kinda scary.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This whole experience has reminded me of a weird story I once read. Can’t remember the title, but my senior English teacher, Mrs. Lazott, assigned it to our class. The story was about this guy who hates his job, you know, and one day he wakes up to discover he’s been turned into a bug and can’t go to work. The guy’s family is so grossed out by him, they eventually want to get rid of him, even though the reason he had the job he hated in the first place was to support the ungrateful freeloaders.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, the poor guy eventually begins to forget he used to be a man but not to the point that he forgets his family, and he still understands that he’s become a burden to them. Spoiler alert: in the end, the poor guy…bug…thing up and dies one day to spare his family the inconvenience of having to look after him anymore. Real depressing story.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There is a point to this tangent, by the way. I guess what I’m trying to say is I’m starting to feel like the bug here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The greenies warned me that some memory loss and confusion could happen. They said it’s all part of the process of surrendering who I am to who I’m becoming. I’ve been experiencing both. Thankfully, there are some precautions I can take where the memory loss is concerned—some of them I already took a while ago without even knowing it. I mean, I’m all about becoming whoever it is I need to become for the Gihara’s purposes, but I’d rather not completely forget who I am or who I was in the process. What if I forget more than that? What if I forget Nor?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of Nor, I was surprised to find my threat worked. You remember the one? When I refused to keep drinking the greenies’ crystal concoction if they didn’t make sure Nor was safe from feeling the side effects?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first the greenies said there wasn’t anything they could do to stop her from feeling things if that’s what the Gihara wanted, but I wasn’t buying it. In fact, I decided to show them I meant business and flat out refused to drink any more rock potion until they got things sorted out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, I thought they’d just knock me out and force it down my throat or something, but one thing I’m learning about these greenies is they’re irritatingly patient…and accommodating.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It’s true. Three days after I refused to take the potion, the greenies brought me a visitor. And not just any visitor. She wasn’t a greenie like the others. Nah, this lady was a bit “higher up the ladder,” if you know what I’m saying.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, I’m still kinda shocked my whole “potion strike” thingy actually worked. I didn’t even have to hunger strike to get them to pay attention. In fact, I pretty much ate constantly because I was finally awake long enough to be hungry. The food here’s not bad either. The sweets are especially good, particularly this candy they call maffony. I liked it so much I mentioned it to one of the greenies once. The next time he brought food, he gave me the recipe for it. Like I said, these greenies are accommodating, to say the least.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, it was surprisingly easy to convince “the Lady,” as they all called her, that my fears where Nor and our kinzura bond are concerned were pretty valid. In fact, she’d left the dungeon for only a few minutes after we talked—at least that’s what it seemed like—before returning with a solid plan to eliminate the possibility of Norah being affected by any of this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was resistant to the Lady’s plan at first. It wouldn’t be easy to make it happen. First of all, Nor would have to agree to it, and if I knew Nor like I do, that would take some convincing. But I had to try, right? Besides, Nor would do the same for me if the tables were turned. That and, the Lady and I agreed, Nor’s got much more important things to be getting on with than wasting time being in pain and worrying about my sorry carcass.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well, keeping these daily logs is exhausting. I’ve already written way more than I was supposed to about the Lady’s visit, so I’ll leave it here. All I’ll say is Nor’s gonna be pretty stoked to finally meet her after all this time.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Maffony Recipe","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"04b56bb94fca4cc08e5f6d64a5292688"}]},{"n":41,"letterTitle":"Dark Matter Doors","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Dark Matter Doors","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Contrary to what you might think after reading my last chronicle, Zuli and I did not find the water key to the Lemorian Crest while at the Adolorian Ruins. We only found the “Final Words of Adolor,” which gave us a clue to where the key was hidden. Zuli quickly figured it out, of course. Adolor had hidden the key in his “ruling mark,” which, as I’m sure you’ve already worked out for yourself, is his trident. Unfortunately, it was too late by that point. Why? Because Melusian stole the trident when he overthrew Zuli’s mom, Queen Citrine. Lylend and Lev could’ve tried to negotiate the trident away from Melusian if they had known it contained the key. Sadly, if you’ve read Lev’s account of what happened in Melusian’s Keep, you know that’s not how things went down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Poor Lev. I mean, when we learned he had cost us the water key by breaking Melusian’s Keep, no one was thrilled. But Pieter…he literally went psycho. In the end, he became so cruel that Lylend intervened. I’ll never understand how Lev turned out so great with Pieter as a father. Hearing Pieter rail on his son like that made me extra thankful for Uncle Jack, faults and all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All the drama aside, we are pretty much at a standstill as far as The Lemorian Crest is concerned. It’s not such a bad thing since it has given me time to try and figure out a way to find and rescue James…which Lylend still refuses to do anything about. Honestly! He just told me to go look at the memory stone James made for me. “Took the entire year you were at Dunstan to create,” he’d said. “Yes, that’s right, very thoughtful. Anyway, should help.” Then he rushed me out of his record room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t until I was standing outside that I realized Lylend never actually told me how to use the stone. Before I could return and ask him, Shudder blocked the door and refused to let me by. I swear that sylphid is the spawn of Satan.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That is how I ended up on a walk by myself. I’d had enough of Lylend’s evasion, so I decided to find James myself. I was trying to do just that when I had my first real Totherma vision since arriving in Cobbogoth. I honestly hadn’t even noticed until then that my daily “episodes” with the mysterious red-haired prophetess were MIA. Not that I’d missed them or anything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It all started when the sun began to set. I was about to head back to Hood HQ when I began to feel the telltale signs coming on. I blacked out a moment later, and suddenly I was flying somewhere over the Glindian Sea. Feg wasn’t anywhere in sight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, I was approaching a cluster of small glowing islands. They reminded me of Vestmannaeyjar—the Westman Islands just off the southwest coast of Iceland—looking like they all just decided to pop up from the seafloor one day. Because of this, I thought I was back in Earth’s realm, for a sec, but then I realized these islands were covered in what had to be hundreds of thousands of moving lights. Not electric lights like you’d see in a city. These were orangey and strange—like flames flickering as they moved in lazy, random patterns over, around, and in between the islands.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I caught sight of Totherma beckoning to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that was it. That was all I saw. I came to a few minutes later on Feg’s east beach.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since I was still upset with Lylend, I decided to confide in Lev and Zuli about what had happened. You see, in my vision, it felt very much like Totherma wanted me to meet her at these islands. The only problem was I had no idea where they were.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So she was just hovering over these islands covered in lights?” Zuli asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev, Zuli, and I were in my new rodãor, gathered around the map of Cobbogoth Lylend had given me. I’d finally moved into the rodãor after Zuli and I returned from Adoloria. For the record, having your own place—particularly one you can carry around in your pocket—is pretty great.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What kind of lights were they?” Zuli asked. “What did they look like?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know. They were big, and they just sort of floated around the island like Chinese lanterns.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Chinese lanterns?” Lev and Zuli asked in unison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh sorry, Chinese lanterns are these paper lanterns back in the Olden Realm. People light the centers on fire, and they float off into the air. They’re pretty magical.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So the lights were like fire?” Zuli asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know. They might’ve been burning,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What I mean is,” Zuli continued, “was the light warm or cool?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I couldn’t feel it,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She means the color,” Lev said. “What color were the lights?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, they were warm—a yellow-ish orange.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev and Zuli looked at each other. “The Flame Fleck Islands,” they both said. I pulled the map closer, and Lev pointed to a small cluster of islands surrounded with yellow dots. These islands, however, were labeled “Firefly Islands.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, that makes sense,” Lev said, reading the label. “I think that’s what they used to call Flame Flecks back in Lylend’s day.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I said, relieved to be making progress. Whatever the reason Totherma had for wanting to meet, it felt important. “How do I get there?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After quite a bit of arguing concerning the best route to take and if I should even go, let alone go alone, Lev, Zuli, and I agreed I should go, but only after I promised to let them come with me. It wasn’t an easy promise to make, but honestly, I knew they would follow me anyway, so why postpone the inevitable?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not long after that, the three of us were in a boat on the Glindian Sea, heading toward the Flame Fleck Islands.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can’t believe you’re actually going to meet up with Totherma,” Lev called over the wind as he and Zuli used their elements to move us along at speedboat pace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why?” I asked. “Don’t you guys meet up with her all the time?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev guffawd. “No.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Totherma hasn’t been seen—not in person at least—by anyone since Bruxrac,” Zuli explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli nodded. “She always communicates with the Hood either through chiming or sending a message with Brawlo.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev and Zuli looked at me like I was crazy. “Because she’s REALLY old,” Lev said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but so is Lylend,” I countered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They shook their heads. “Totherma was old before Lylend was even born,” Zuli explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They both nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh wow.” Apparently my visions had been deceiving me because ever since Totherma started coming to me, she’d always looked younger than Lylend…like a lot younger.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We spent the rest of the boat ride speculating on what could be important enough to warrant a personal visit from the Opalian Eye. The suggestions were pretty believable until Lev suggested she might be out of jombula cheese. Things just went downhill from there. By the time we docked at the largest, most central Flame Fleck Island, we were having to shush each other’s giggling to keep from attracting the attention of the flame flecks the islands were named after.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just stay away from their beaks,” Lev warned me through a final stifled chuckle. We were just passing under a trio of the creatures whose “beaks” looked more like serrated swordfish snouts.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What was more terrifying was their buzzing. It sounded like a bunch of industrial fans going all at once, which was probably why I didn’t hear Totherma call to me the first few times. It wasn’t until an image of her hovering in the middle of a small meadow on the main island entered my mind’s eye that I heard her voice for the first time. I nearly jumped out of my skin because, well, her voice was only in my mind. Lev and Zuli couldn’t hear it. (No wonder she and Brawlo got on so well.) And when I say “the first time,” I actually mean the first time. Up until that moment, I’d never heard Totherma’s voice before. In all my previous visions, she had never actually spoken to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was a nice voice, too. Low for a woman’s but still feminine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come alone,” was all she said. So I did. Leaving Lev and Zuli on the shore near the boat, I ventured into the thick trees covering the island until I came to the meadow she’d shown me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And there she was. In the center of the clearing, surrounded by a multi-colored crystal henge, the woman who’d been haunting my mind for the past two years hovered—yes hovered—about four or five feet above the clearing. She was dressed in an amethyst shyntara that matched the exact shade of her eyes. Her hip-length hair fluttered in the slight breeze and looked more like the color of blood than ever.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hello, libkyn,” she said with a soft smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“H-hi,” I said in return. To be honest, I’m surprised I got that much out. It was so strange to see her there in real life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ll forgive me if I call you libkyn, won’t you?” she asked with an apologetic grimace. “I’ve spent so much time in your mind, I feel as though I know you quite well by now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “I don’t mind. What does it mean, though?” I asked. I’d heard Iolyt call Lev “libkyn” several times since arriving on Feg.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma smiled again softly, but this time the softness reached her eyes. “It means child of my deepest heart. That’s the inadequate modern translation, anyway.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was my turn to smile. The closest Uncle Jack had ever come to using a term of endearment with me was “my dear.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, here we are at last,” she said. “How are you faring?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I drew a deep breath, feeling my brow rise. “Well…it was a rough start, but I think things are getting better,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s good news.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I suppose, yeah.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see you’ve already made some nice friends,” she gestured toward the shore where Lev and Zuli waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I agreed. Surprisingly, since Zuli and I worked things out, she, Lev, and I had become an inseparable trio of sorts. Having never had any real friends besides James, it was a new experience for me. And kind of awesome.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well then, good news all around,” Totherma said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Except where James is concerned,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah…yes, I wondered when that remarkable kynd would come up. If you will exercise just a little more patience with me, we will come to him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sure,” I said, feeling hope rise in me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now, you’re probably wondering why I’ve summoned you to this island, of all places,” she said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good, because we have a lot to cover, Noriah, and a very limited amount of time to cover it. Let’s get started, shall we?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With a wave of her hand, Totherma beckoned me into the henge. Then she stepped aside—or floated aside, really.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was when I realized Totherma wasn’t actually there. She was more like a shimmery sort of hologram image, or a reflection of herself, shining up from a strange glowing circle on the ground. It was much less Princess Leia than it sounds, and much more Tom Riddle after he’s escaped the diary. (If you don’t understand either of those references, you’ve been living under a rock.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, when Totherma waved her hand and stepped aside, another larger circle, about the size of a small picnic blanket, appeared on the ground between us. I kid you not, dear reader, it looked just like a miniature crop circle, only instead of having been burned or pressed into the grass, it was being shone there, shimmering like iridescent fairy dust. It had the same strange alien-looking maze-like design as a crop circle in the middle, only this was more swirly—you know, more “Cobbogothian.” (See my journal notes.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you know what this is?” Totherma asked me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I told her I didn’t, she clapped her hands, and the maze design shifted inside the circle. “This is a Dark Matter Door—a DMD if you like.” She clapped again and the image shifted again. “It represents a light path or a map through the dark realm.” Clap. Shift. “This is how space shifters travel through the dark realm.” Clap. Shift. “This is how I’ve been able to communicate with you, Jack, and occasionally Lylend, both here and while you were in the Olden Realm. Clap. Shift. Clap. Shift. Clap. Shift. As you can see, I am not physically here right now. This is only my soul you see before you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Only?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She chuckled softly. “Yes, it must be quite a shock to you, libkyn, but it’s true.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Honestly, at this point I’m beyond shock.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good, because I’m about to lead you through a DMD, and I’d rather not have to waste time answering questions while you get used to the idea.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re about to lead me through one of those things?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh yes, Noriah. This is one of the key powers of a space shifter. Consider this your first lesson in what I call Yvainedalandin, or ‘dark walking’.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. “Okay.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good,” she said. “Now, the most important thing to remember when traveling through the Dark Realm is that soulself travel—meaning traveling in your soul form—is best. Had I come to you through the DMD in my body, it would have been a much more difficult—and dangerous—feat. However, one can learn to navigate the Dark Realm quite easily and safely while in soulself form.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From there, Totherma walked me through the entire—and truly bizarre—process of summoning a DMD. (Again, see journal notes for details.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now, we haven’t much time,” she said when she’d finished demonstrating traveling from one door to another around the clearing. “Come.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come?” I asked. “What about James?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Precisely.” With that, she waved a new DMD into place and said, “Now quickly lie down inside the circle.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reluctantly obeyed. Was she going to be like Lylend and pretend like nothing serious was happening to James?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, that’s right, take the fetal position, libkyn. Perfect! Now,” she clapped her hands, and a new map appeared in the second DMD, “let’s go visit your Jamus, shall we?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Visit James! “Wait, what—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could even sit up, the henge, the meadow, and even the giant flame flecks vanished, and one of the strangest experiences of my life began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was suddenly standing in a thick, eerie darkness. There was a path of light—not unlike the Drusy Path—beneath my feet. I could see wavy, almost heatwave-like neon images of light—what I could only assume were the souls of things—flitting and vibrating all around me. The giant flame flecks. The trees. Lev and Zuli down at the shore. Wait. Were they kissing?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could really focus in on anything, a strange sensation reached into my stomach, hooked onto my bellybutton, and yanked. Then I was rushing at breakneck speed along the path of light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few moments later, I found myself—well my soulself—standing in the very stone room I’d been dreaming of James in. Sitting upright on the stone table was James himself, only this time he wasn’t writhing in pain.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If soulselves could cry, I would’ve broken down and bawled my eyes out right then and there. I’d never been so relieved to see anyone in my life.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Spy-O-Lite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"268b5e0469e9477ba4842213aeb2e7d8"},{"title":"Journal: DMD & Flame Flecks","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"7327a5a3326b482e9f99ec545469d5c1"},{"title":"Flame Flecks","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"73a934e0d6814e6d85bf5419ed4876d6"},{"title":"DMD Map Insert","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e9243f6085b0458a8c06d7bfd743d1cd"}]},{"n":42,"letterTitle":"Kinsyska","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"Kinsyska","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I had to rub my eyes several times to make sure I wasn't dreaming that Norah was here. She could've easily been an angel standing there, with her pale skin and platinum hair. Boy was she a sight for sore eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Shall we continue later, Jamus?” Glynt the Greenie asked. Before Nor and the lady appeared, he'd been giving me another grueling lesson in Callowynder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah…sure,” I nodded, still staring at Nor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt gathered up the Callowynder board and pieces and slipped soundlessly from the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You know Callowynder?” Nor asked once he was gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, Dad told me about it back in Earth's realm. Guess it's like a sport here…like baseball or the Olympics.” I gestured in the direction the Greenie had left. “Glynt's been teaching me the rules.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really? My friend Lev taught me the game, too,” she said. “He's obsessed with it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev?” I asked, a surge of jealousy hitting me in the gut. “Who's that?” It was the one thing I'd been afraid of during my time in the dungeon—that Nor would meet someone else in Cobbogoth and forget about me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He's just a guy I met,” Nor said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah. I'd introduce you, but I left him back on the island with my body.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. “You did?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mmmhmm.” She looked down at her nails. “He's really great. All sunshine and muscle, you know?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He is?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yep, and right before I left, he was on the beach with his kinzura, Zuli.” Nor's eyes met mine. “I'm pretty sure they were making out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nice.” I let out a shaky laugh.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor's lips broke into a smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So, nobody I need to worry about, then?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She shook her head, still smiling. Man, she was beautiful!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I'll leave you two alone then, shall I?” We both started when Totherma spoke. I'd forgotten she was there. After warning us not to touch, she summoned one of those weird glowy circles and disappeared.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She doesn't want us to touch?” I laughed in disbelief. “I haven't seen you in weeks.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know.” Nor winced apologetically. “Apparently I'll head straight back to my body if we do.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gihara help me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So…how are you?” she asked, changing the subject.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I'm good,” I shrugged. I was pretty sure I looked a mess, even though I had tried to clean up for tonight. I'd also arranged for the Greenies to hold off on the rock potion because I wanted to make sure I was completely lucid when Nor stopped by. Bringing her here was all part of the plan.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I like your…” She gestured below her chin, indicating my beard and wyndaloos.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh yeah, thanks,” I said, tugging on one of them. “I've been ‘earning’ them for good behavior.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Her brow crinkled. She looked troubled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How are you?” I asked. Now it was my turn to change the subject.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I'm-uh-I'm all right,” she shrugged, looking around the dungeon. “Just been getting used to Cobbogoth, you know?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, it's pretty wicked, huh?” What was I saying? I hadn't even been outside the dungeon yet. Man, why was this so awkward between us?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As if reading my thoughts, Nor stopped looking around and faced me straight on. “What's going on here, James?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And there it was. The question I was hoping she wouldn't ask. “I wish I could tell you, Nor, I do. But…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You can't,” she finished for me, not hiding the frustration in her voice. “At least tell me what I've been seeing you go through in this place isn't real.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So you have been seeing it?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And feeling it. James, seriously! What is going on? Are they torturing you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew I was right about our kinzura bond. It was that strong. No wonder Totherma believed me so easily.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They aren't torturing me, Nor. I swear. Not on purpose, anyway. It's all something I have to go through, something that's making me…better, you know?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not really, no.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I wish I could tell you more.” I wished I could do more than tell her. I wanted to show her. Last night's dose of rock potion had caused something pretty bizarre to happen to me. It was all part of the reason she was here—why it was so important we get this over with before she saw or felt anything else.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I've just been really worried about you,” she said softly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Man, I wanted to touch her right then. I wanted to grab her and hold her and tell her everything was going to be fine, even if I didn't know it myself. The worry I saw on her face was worse than any of the pain I'd felt so far.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I gotta tell you something, Nor,” I said. “And I want you to listen to me all the way through before you ask any questions, okay?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a deep breath. “Totherma brought you here for a reason—a reason I'm certain you're going to hate me for, but I hope you'll forgive me one day when I can finally explain it all to you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay?” Her pale green eyes were uneasy now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I told her the things I could tell her. I told her why she was able to feel what I was feeling in her dreams—that our kinzura bond was causing it. “Totherma says there's a way we can stop this from happening. It's called Kinsyska. It's like some ancient Cobbogothian ritual she can perform that will close the kinzura bond between us somehow. She didn't tell me much more than that, and I didn't really understand all the science-y stuff she did tell me—that's more your department—but I think it will work.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What exactly do you mean when you say ‘close the bond’?” Nor asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He means the channel that connects your ability to feel what he's feeling, and vice versa, will no longer be open.” Totherma was back. She'd summoned one of those glowing circles and hovered between us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Have you been eavesdropping on us this whole time?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait,” Nor interrupted. “You're saying I won't be able to know what's going on with him anymore? Like if he's in danger of dying?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah…yes to everything except the dying bit, libkyn,” she said. “If he dies, you will know it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait! What?” Nor cried.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I covered my eyes and dragged my fingers through my hair. Totherma wasn't doing me any favors here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Could we have a few more minutes?” I asked the lady.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, dear me, yes!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Without any eavesdropping?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I think she was blushing now. “Of course. My apologies. I only…it was really just that last bit…I mean, I guess I thought since the two of you couldn't touch, your salutations would be brief.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment she was gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned back to Nor. “Listen,” I began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, James, you listen,” Nor said, her eyes bright. “There is no way you're putting me through a whole blood sum experience again, okay? It's been hard enough thinking you were being tortured all this time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kinsyska won't be like that,” I assured her. “It's just supposed to keep you from worrying about me needlessly.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James, I love you. There's nothing in this world or any other that could keep me from worrying about you. And it's never needless. Besides, somebody's got to worry about you if your dad won't.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My dad? What's he got to do with it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nothing! That's the problem. Every time I've tried to get him to do something, he insists ‘the Gihara are in charge’ and ‘this is all part of some grand plan.’ That you're Cobbogothian and can handle whatever's going on…yada yada. I'm not very happy with him right now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Have I ever told you how adorable Nor is when she gets riled about something? I honestly almost grabbed her and kissed her right then—or would have if it wouldn't have sent her right back to her body.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What'd I do to deserve you as my girl?” I asked instead, watching her pace back and forth within her glowing circle.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor stopped and turned to me with a half-smile. “You can sweet talk all you want, but this Kinsyska thing isn't happening, James. Not unless you can promise me you aren't going to die.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Everybody dies,” I said, quoting a line from one of our favorite movies. (Ghost Town starring Gervais and Kinnear. You're welcome.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not funny,” Nor said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just trying to lighten things up a bit.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I'm serious, James!” she cried.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I held up my hands then. “I'm not gonna lie to you, Nor. I promised myself I'd never lie to you. I can't read the future, so I don't know what's going to happen. But I will promise to do everything in my power to not die. How's that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not great, but I suppose it will have to do.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I'm sorry to interrupt again, libkyns.” Totherma was back. “I'm afraid we're running out of time to perform the ceremony.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What if I refuse to do it?” Nor asked, folding her arms and sticking her chin out in that cute way she's been doing since she was 10 years old.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Noriah,” Totherma said with a soft smile, “I'm afraid this is something that must happen. I know you fear for Jamus' life, but it is not his life we're concerned about here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” Nor asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, libkyn, your life is in jeopardy if we do not perform Kinsyska.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really? You never said anything about her life being in danger,” I said, alarm shooting through me like one of Dad's lightning bolts.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, isn't that why you agreed to it?” Totherma asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I-no, I just didn't want her to feel the pain I was feeling anymore. I didn't know she could die from all this.” I waved my hand around the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then, my dear Jamus, wouldn't you concur that the risk of death is an even greater reason to ensure Kinsyska takes place?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…yeah,” I said. “Sorry, Nor, I don't like being a bully, but this is happening.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well then, let's begin, shall we?” Totherma summoned a different looking glowing circle near me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, Nor,” I said. “Please do it for me. It's all gonna work out for the best. I can feel it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After a long searching look as a thousand emotions flickered over her face, Nor finally nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good,” Totherma said. “Jamus, please lie down in the DMD here.” She gestured to a circle she'd just summoned. “Yes, that's right, in the fetal position if you please.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I did as she directed, feeling pretty weird about it too. When she told me to close my eyes, things just got weirder. One moment I was looking up at Nor from where I was lying on the ground. The next, I was staring down at my own body lying in the DMD.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What the…?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Isn't it wonderful?” Totherma exclaimed, clapping her hands. “In order to perform Kinsyska, the two of you must be able to kiss. It is just your soulselves kissing, of course, but that is what Kinsyska means, you know—to ‘soul kiss’—oh my!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You better believe I had Nor in my arms and was “soul kissing” my girl like I'd never kissed her before.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It only took a sec for Nor's shock to wear off. Then she was kissing me back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well—ehem—then I'll just proceed, shall I?” At least I think I heard Totherma say that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few moments later, I felt something distinctly different come over me, something distinctly, well…“Norah.” That's the only way I can describe it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor must've felt it too because we both pulled back at the same time, staring at one another.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa! Your eyes,” Nor said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know,” I said, seeing something strange happening in her own. The only way I can describe it is it was like a bit of deep blue ink had splashed into the pale green pupils of her eyes, right around the irises. When it stopped spreading, it had created a sort of deep blue starburst there. According to Nor, my own starbursts were the same celadon green as her eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There now. Kinsyska is complete. Your kinzura bond is now finished. Yes, that's right, you'll find the color of your qualdrines has changed as well,” she said, noticing me glance down at my right hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don't understand,” Nor said, examining her qualdrine medallion. Like my own, her's was now marbled with deep blue and celadon green.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The only way to close the kinzura channel between the two of you is to dam it by weaving your souls together,” Totherma explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But isn't that backwards?” Nor asked. “By weaving our souls, aren't you binding our fates more closely?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your fates have always been aligned, libkyn, ever since your souls were created. Kinsyska has not changed that. The reason the kinzura channel between your two souls existed in the first place is because you are and have always been two halves of the same whole. You feel for one another because each half is constantly reaching out to the other—restlessly searching for the other half. By weaving your souls back into one, the searching ceases, and your souls are finally at peace. You are whole once again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don't understand,” Nor said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My dear libkyn, I'm more than 10,000 years old, and even I don't understand it fully. I just know from much experience that it works. Will you trust me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nor hesitated only a moment before nodding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Very good. Then shall we return you to your body?” Totherma asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grabbed hold of Nor's hand, not wanting to let her go so soon.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You may as well kiss her again, Jamus,” Totherma said. “Only the Gihara know when you'll get to do it again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The lady had a point, so I gathered my girl up and kissed her again, hoping real hard it wouldn't be the last time.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Kinsyska Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e73815890c6d4697b4e9122a2228fd01"},{"title":"Dungeon Illustration","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"eb6ae6c315524fbb94153cdccb9f2c55"}]},{"n":43,"letterTitle":"The Legend of Boki’s Bow","narrator":"Lylend","pieces":[{"title":"The Legend of Boki’s Bow","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Well human, you'll probably be glad to know the Kyndie and I are on friendly terms once again. If feeling her constant disapproval follow me around like one of Shudder's wretched storms wasn't pure torture, I don't know what is. Alo used to get upset with me like that sometimes, but she never punished me with what I believe you humans call “the silent treatment.” I did not like the Kyndie's use of that treacherous tactic at all. I suppose it's one of the many downsides of being reared amongst humans.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Kyndie and I have been on the most amicable terms since our first meeting, so the last thing I wanted to do was cause her pain. But the truth is, she still has much to learn about the Cobbogothian way. Much of what she considers callous in me is actually the Cobbogothian way of demonstrating deep love and confidence in our kindlin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ah well, she will learn soon enough. Either that or she'll cease speaking to me altogether. Or she'll die. The latter would be a problem for the entire Cobbogothian race, so let's just hope she learns, eh?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, the Kyndie dying is not a worry I carry today, human. Today we are celebrating. Why? Because after weeks of searching for another clue concerning these Lemorian keys, we have found one!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Kyndie found it. It all came about due to a suggestion from Lyttie. She had just presented her findings on Spy-o-lite to me and mentioned she's noticed a change in Noriah that I may have been too preoccupied to notice myself. It's true, human. I may tend to get entirely absorbed in quests such as our search for these keys. I wouldn't have felt so guilty about it if I hadn't witnessed with my own eyes the neglect she suffered due to Jack Lukens' relentless absorption in his work.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Consequently, when Lyttie brought Noriah's melancholy state to my attention, I thought inviting her to browse my record room {free of Shudder, of course} might give me an opportunity to observe her more closely. After a few days, I realized Lyttie was right; the Kyndie did seem somewhat downcast. That, and there's something different about her. It's not her hair or her weight. I'm old enough to know that asking a Kyndie about her weight is unacceptable. Before I could get any further along in my investigation, however, Noriah stumbled across a crystal scroll. I've read this particular scroll more times than I can recall, as it directly relates to the legends of my own kin-klan. I simply never considered it relevant to our cause. However, bright as the Kyndie is, she noticed the importance of it right away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I have yet to get to the heart of her melancholy, but thanks to Spy-o-lite, we do have a long journey on foot ahead of us. There will be plenty of time to observe and talk then.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the meantime, human, rather than tell you what the Kyndie discovered in that crystal scroll, I'll let you read it for yourself. Let's see if your significantly smaller brain can pick up what mine did not.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Legend of Boki's Bow","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f9521e56bbcb4c839508505526cdfdc3"},{"title":"Journal: Creatures of the Dark Realm","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0cb1844adff745f28c67f2e7ace988b0"},{"title":"Iolyt's Spy-o-lite Notes","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"7833fed352004a51b256743e345f9bd1"},{"title":"Journal: Yihara Journal Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4e5efe09a3494a73b3847825b560eb6c"}]},{"n":44,"letterTitle":"TaraYvaine","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"TaraYvaine","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Have I told you yet how incredibly awesome Lylend is? Man, I wish he was my dad. I don't think Pieter would care if he heard me say that either. He's been trying to get rid of me since he found out I existed. And even more than usual lately, if I'm being honest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, we're all a bit on edge right now. We've been traveling for the past two weeks, making our way toward Lobokia, the Wind Nation. We would have hoptioned there, but it turns out that stone Lylend brought to Iolyt a while back—the one she was studying while I was busy breaking Melusian's Keep—is a sort of ambushing stone. Not sure how the Dogrils figured out how to make it, but it's called Spy-o-lite, and stones just like it—well dust from the stones anyway—is turning up in hoption holes all over Cobbogoth. You can't travel through a hoption hole without the stone telling the Dogrils exactly where you are so they can hunt you down and either kill you or worse…steal your body.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt figured out what Spy-o-lite was about the same time Nori found the scroll containing the legend of Boki's Bow. Consequently, Lylend and Pieter agreed it would be best if we went on foot to try and find Boki's Bow rather than risk letting Cifer and the Dogrils know what we were up to by hoptioning. Needless to say, traveling on foot takes much. Much. Loooooonger. When Nori told me that's how you humans travel as a rule, I teared up a little.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which brings me to the reason Pieter decided to humiliate me in front of Lylend…and everyone else. In my excitement to see the Wind Territory for the first time in my life, I forgot about the Spy-o-lite and almost hoptioned, putting the entire Hood in jeopardy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori said something when she came to my defense—a phrase you humans say when you mess up: “I'm only human.” The truth is I'm only Cobbogothian, and believe it or not, we make mistakes too. I would know. I've made more than my fair share.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt says I should cut Pieter some slack, and I think she's probably right, as usual. I mean, we have been in Wind Territory for a few days now, not to mention out on the Zephyrvana, which everyone knows is the most dangerous place in Lobokian territory. We are exposed like a raw nerve—no place to take cover during the nearly constant and unpredictable lightning storms happening there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It didn't used to be that way. The Lobokians used to take a lot more pride in their nation than they do today. Lobokia is a bit of a mess, you see. It has been since the Dogril invasion 25 years ago. It's actually where the Dogrils made their first attack on their capital city, Tarayvaine. Lobokians don't even recognize themselves as a united kin-klan anymore. They're more like a bunch of small kin-klans that don't like each other very much. Seriously. They're always fighting each other for more territory, better resources—that sort of stuff. The whole territory was carved up by the Spark Lords and Maidens about 10 years ago, and Tarayvaine has been a war zone ever since.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, we had to cross Roda Bay in order to avoid running into any Lobokians. Once we finally made it past the wild sylphids at Roda Bay and through the wide-open space of the Zephyrvana, I was sent ahead to scout out a safe route through the city to the old palace. That was when I almost hoptioned. Not one of my best moments.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter was so angry. I mean he's mad all the time, but this was a new level of rage. Once he finally calmed down enough to see some reason, Iolyt and Lylend convinced him to give me a second chance, and I was able to make my way to the city and find a safe route.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The truth is, no one really knows where Boki's Bow could be. It's been lost for hundreds of years—thousands, really. We're all just “shooting sparks,” as Lylend says, by traveling to Lobokia in the first place. The first part of our plan is to not get caught trespassing on any Spark Lord's or Maiden's territory, and the second part is to see if Boki had a record room like the one Zu and Nori found in the Adolorian Ruins. Lylend's hypothesis is that Boki might have built his own record room in the throne room at Tarayvaine—and if we're lucky, all the Kinchiefs did when they were given the keys. The chances are actually pretty good since all their keys are necessary to unlock the Lemorian Crest, and the fate of Cobbogoth pretty much depends on us being able to find them all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Boki had this legendary throne, see. Every Cobbogothian has heard of it. It's supposed to be made of Zephyrvite, which is this malleable stone, kind of like how Nori described a mineral from the Olden Realm called mercury. Only Zephyrvite is more of a cloud than a liquid, and it's not poisonous. I don't think.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The stories say Boki had the throne made by a Qualdrinist who created Zephyrvite from unique elements found only on the Zephyrvana, for the sole purpose of sculpting Boki's throne. Zephyrvite is supposed to mimic the mood of whoever's holding it, or in the case of Boki's throne, whoever's sitting on it. Since Boki was a Spark Spindler, the legends say the throne always took the shape of the wind, and if he was angry, it took the shape of a big storm, lightning and all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the record, I just asked Lylend about the stories I've told you. He said everything is true, so there you go.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Even so, as much as I admire Lylend the Naridi, I was still skeptical when we finally made it to the Lobokian throne room. When we walked inside, however, I learned for myself that it's true; the throne looks exactly like the legends say it does. Apparently, whenever the throne's empty, it's in a perpetual lightning storm due to the fact that Boki was assassinated while sitting on it by one of Holictrian's more zealous followers during Bruxrac.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Bet you believe me now, eh kynd?” Lylend asked while we all stood staring up at Boki's throne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could only nod, dumbfounded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He slapped me on the back. “Then get up there and sit in it before Pieter does and we're all exposed to one of his fowl moods again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn't wait to be told twice. Stumbling up the swirling mass of Zephyrvite, I eventually found myself sitting on the throne. The electric storm brewing beneath me stopped immediately, probably mimicking my complete awe.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well done, kynd!” Lylend called to me, circling to the back of the throne. By the time I was back with the rest of them, he was placing his qualdrine into a dial none of them had been able to see until the Zephyrvite stopped moving.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I spent a lot of time in council with Boki,” Lylend explained as he stepped back to let the dial spin. “When circumstances became dire during Bruxrac, he gave me access to the room I'm about to show you. I'd completely forgotten it existed until you kyndies told me what happened while you were in the Adolorian Ruins. But it wasn't until Noriah found the legend of Boki's Bow that I was certain we should start searching for the next Lemorian key here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once his qualdrine stopped spinning, Lylend pressed it, and a door appeared in the back of the throne. Moments later, we were all filing down a spiral staircase and into a royal record room like the Adolorian one Zu had described to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rather than scrolls, however, the walls were lined with rows and rows of large stone tablets, some as small as my hand, others taller and bigger than me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Everyone spread out,” Lylend instructed. “We're looking for anything that has the Lemorian Crest symbol on it. If you see it, anything else written on it will most likely be in ancient Cobbogothian, so just call out, and either Noriah or myself will come take a look.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all spread out and began looking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I'd only just finished pilfering through a stack of giant white crystal tablets leaning against a wall when I heard Zuli gasp behind me. I turned in time to see her coming out of her aisle. She was carrying a small chest in her arms. Nori joined us from where she'd been examining some writing on one of the walls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, libkyn?” Iolyt asked from another aisle.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli shook her head, as if too amazed to speak, and slowly opened the chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Eye of Gihara!” Iolyt breathed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, Lyttie? What has she found?” Lylend asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Qualdrines!” Nori said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara! Qualdrines indeed, Kyndie! There must be at least fifty in there.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where did they come from?” Zuli asked. I could see the brightness in her eyes and knew exactly what she was thinking. More qualdrines meant more help for her people suffering from Lamianza.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I haven't seen this many qualdrines since before Bruxrac,” Lylend said, running a hand over his beard. “Where did you find them, Kyndie?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Over here,” Zuli indicated, leading us down her aisle. “They were hiding between these two slabs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What should we do with them?” Nori asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I saw Iolyt and Lylend exchange a hopeful glance. “We'll take them with us,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend retrieved the chest from Zu and handed it to me. “With what's sure to come once Cifer gets himself organized, we'll need as many qualdrine wielders as we can get. These will allow us to begin recruiting to rebuild the Hood until we can find the Lemorian Crest and retrieve the Haven from Noriah.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend handed the qualdrines to me. “The sooner we get these safely stowed away, the better. Will you lend Lev your rodãor, Kyndie?” he asked Nori. “I've left mine back with Brawlo outside the city.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori nodded and handed me her rodãor. I took it and headed back up the staircase and outside the record room to the throne room where I'd have enough space to open it and stow the qualdrines inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was on my way back to the throne to help the others search the record room when I caught a flash of something from the corner of my eye. The Zephyrvite throne had resumed its stormy brewing again. I looked closer at the back of the throne where I'd seen the flash. At first I thought it was just a bit of lightning and was about to enter the record room when I saw it again. This time I was certain it wasn't lightning.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was the flash a third time, only this time it looked more like a “glint” or a glimmer, if that makes sense. I decided this was as good a time as any to try out a few of the spark spindling tricks Lylend had taught me during our painfully long journey here. I reached out my hand, and as I did, several spindles of electricity jumped out of the churning Zephyrvite, weaving themselves around my wrist. It tingled a bit. The next thing I knew, my hand was being drawn into the swirling throne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A moment later, I felt my hand grasp hold of something slender. I pulled slowly, snapping the spindles of electricity around my wrist. Bit by bit, I pulled my hand from the shifting throne, keeping a firm hold of the object.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When it was finally free, I blew any remaining wisps of Zephyrvite from the object and, for the second time since entering the throne room, stared in stunned silence. I couldn't believe my eyes. It couldn't possibly be this easy. Could it?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it was! I held a bow in my hands—a bow that looked exactly like the bow I'd seen in the crystal scroll Nori had found—the one that led us here in the first place. I blinked back tears of joy and let out a loud whoop! I was holding none other than the legendary Boki's Bow—the Boki's Bow! The one that allegedly shot bolts of lightning from it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could call out to the others, I hesitated. What was I doing? This was Boki's Bow! The Boki's Bow. What kynd in their right mind could possibly pass up an opportunity to see if it really did what legends claimed?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I did what any kynd in my position would do. I took the bow in both my hands, drew back the string, and let go.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A deafening crack, a flash of blinding light, and an instant later, I was flat on my back, staring at a blackened hole in the ceiling of the throne room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yep. This was definitely Boki's Bow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The last thing I remember thinking before blacking out completely was, “What a way to die!” That and the fact that it suddenly smelled an awful lot like burning flesh. At least this time—if I lived to see it—when Pieter humiliated me in front of everyone, I could definitely say it was worth it.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Zephyrvite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"baa8c9e4fad64983a355f3cc4fb744d1"},{"title":"Coloring Page","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"11ba397786c242e6a39d19ecc45e68e2"},{"title":"Map of Lobokia","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"99e4dc5f288b4d0b9a0968bea021dc1d"}]},{"n":45,"letterTitle":"A Wild Sylphid Storm","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"A Wild Sylphid Storm (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Have you ever had one of those moments? You know the kind. You’ve been focusing so shortsightedly on a problem that you haven’t been able to see how things all fit together? In fact, it isn’t until something forces you to take a step back, and you look at the whole picture, that you realize you had the answer all along.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s how it was when I saw the writing on the wall. And no, that’s not a metaphor. I literally saw writing on the wall. It was a verse. Just one verse, inscribed in ancient Cobbogothian on the wall behind the door to the Lobokian record room. The moment I saw it was like a moment of deja vu, and I realized I’d had the missing piece of the puzzle to this Lemorian Crest riddle all along. Between being captured by the Hood, almost dying twice, everything going on with James, and then hunting down Lemorian keys, I’d completely forgotten to tell Lylend what I’d seen at the beginning--when I’d first entered Cobbogoth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Seeing that verse on the wall of the record room brought it all back to my mind. It was almost like Uncle Jack was here reminding me to go back to the beginning, as he had when he gave me his mother’s name, Aven.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lylend,” I said, my voice shaky. “Lylend, over here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend finished sending Lev out of the record room with my rodãor and the chest of qualdrines Zuli had just found, and then he was at my side. “What is it, kyndie?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gestured to the verse carved into the wall. “I’ve seen this before. It was on the floor of the Starlyt Sea. I saw it right after I woke up and discovered you were gone.” The words poured out of me. “I meant to tell you, I swear, but with everything that’s happened, it just slipped my mind.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoah, kyndie! Slow down,” Lylend said. “Tell me from the beginning.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I did. I told him all about climbing that bridge in the Starlyt Sea cave and looking down at the Starlyt Sea—about seeing that strange “moon” in the distance with the constellation formations on the sea floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend turned back to the verse and read it aloud. By this time the others had joined us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is where we entered","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Here Cifer finds his end","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When moon and royal blood arrive","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From Olden realm again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They twain shall restore qualdriny","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Six keys they twain must find","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before the lost horn can return","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And elements combine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then prophecy shall be fulfilled","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which Cobbogoth foretold","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As we united rise again","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To save our home of old.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Triolune Prophecy,” Iolyt whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” Lylend agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mentioned that before,” I said, remembering the day Lylend arrived on Feg just in time to keep Pieter from burying me alive. “What does it mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Triolune Prophecy foretold Jamus’s and your births. It’s called the Triolune Prophecy because Totherma received it under the Triolune—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“One of Cobbogoth’s most sacred and significant celestial occurrences,” Iolyt finished for him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But what does it mean?” Zuli pressed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It means we’ve found our next clue,” Lylend answered. “Tell me about this bridge over the moon. Particularly about the moon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I opened my mouth to answer when a loud crack echoed from the spiral staircase above, sending a tremor through the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all froze, staring at each other.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter’s face melted into a scowl of disgust. “The konkle’ll bring every spark lord and maiden in the territory down on our heads,” he spat. He pushed his way through us to the staircase. “Lev!” he hollered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli and Iolyt rushed after him. Lylend and I brought up the rear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally rushed out into the throne room, we found a black hole in the cathedral-like ceiling and Lev lying flat on his back, unconscious. His skin and shyntara were blackened and smoking all over.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Iolyt!” Zuli cried, running to Lev’s side. “Hurry! Your healing pouch.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hurried over to see how I could help but paused when I noticed the object Lev was clutching in his left hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is this what I think it is?” I asked, gently taking the intricately carved bow out of Lev’s hand. I handed it to Lylend who took it and examined it carefully. Then he glanced up at the ceiling and back at Lev. “By the Gihara! It might just be, kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s that just there?” Pieter asked—demanded, really—pointing over Lylend’s shoulder at the handle of the bow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend held the bow closer, examining the handle more carefully. “Words. It says, ‘You hold my key…but what you seek still sleeps…under the bridge over the moon.’” Lylend met my eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Boki’s Bow!” Lev croaked from where he lay. “I found it in the zephyrvite.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We turned in time to see him sit up, clutching what I assumed was a cocktail of healing stones to his chest. The burned patches of skin where the electricity from Boki’s Bow had escaped his body were rapidly healing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of all the idiotic—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not now, Pieter,” Lylend said, cutting him off. “We haven’t time for one of your tantrums just now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli hid a snort of laughter in her shoulder as she and Iolyt—who winked at her—helped Lev to his feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The spark lords and maidens can’t have missed this,” Lylend continued, gesturing toward the ceiling. “We have to move!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With that, we were running.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was slow going on our way out of the city. Lylend insisted we travel in complete silence and take as many back passageways and tunnels as he could remember from his time living in TaraYvaine with his kinzura Alo.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally made it out of the city, it was turning to dusk and was safe enough to begin talking again. Brawlo was there to meet us, and we all climbed on. There were no encounters with spark lords across the Zephyrvana.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we could finally make out the spot where we’d all swam across Roda Bay on our way into Lobokian territory, Lylend shushed us. His thick brows drew down in concentration.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” Pieter asked. Zuli and I helped an almost completely healed Lev slide off Brawlo’s back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hadn’t noticed it before, but it was getting dark a lot quicker than it should have, and a slight wind was picking up. It grew stronger the closer we moved to the bay.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you feel that?” Lylend asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked at the others. They had similar looks of concern on their faces.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev shrugged. “It’s a bit colder, I guess.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli and Iolyt nodded in agreement.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But do you feel the wind?” Lylend whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I felt it once we spotted the shore,” I offered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*Felt it too.* Brawlo said in my mind, and I patted his side comfortingly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Exactly.” Lylend slowly held a finger up to his lips. “We’re not alone,” he mouthed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all took turns glancing around the area. I didn’t see anyone. No one else seemed to either. But then Lylend pointed upward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first the darkening sky above looked like it was full of wispy grey clouds. Then I realized the “clouds” were moving—stirring. And they had faces. Really creepy faces. Just like Shudder’s.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wild sylphids,” Pieter hissed through a clenched jaw. For the first time since meeting him, he looked genuinely terrified rather than angry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s the matter with him?” I asked Lev.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was surprised when he stifled a chuckle. “He doesn’t like sylphids.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev shook his head. “Spark trumps earth, see? Since Pieter is only a monowyn, he doesn’t have a backup power to protect himself.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, tucking that little bit of info on Pieter away in case he ever tried to bury me alive again. It also explained why he didn’t try to contend with Lylend’s authority when he returned and started running the Hood again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Everyone stay here,” Lylend said. “I’m going to try and talk to them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Being raised by Uncle Jack, a myth-chaser who loved legends and epic myths, I’d heard a lot of famous last words in my life, and I really hoped these wouldn’t be Lylend’s. They weren’t, thankfully, but his words didn’t do us any favors either. Lylend wasn’t two minutes into talking with their leader when the sylphid blew him back in our direction. Then the pack of sylphids started circling again, and a massive tempest sprang up on Roda Bay, the likes of which I’ve never seen. Not even one of Boston’s nor’easters could compare.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was impossible to take cover—there wasn’t time—so we did the only thing we could do before the storm and swirling sand tore the skin from our bodies. We grabbed hands, and Lylend hoptioned us out of there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But not before a feeling unlike any I’d ever felt before tore through me. It started from the crown of my head and shot to my fingers and toes, like white-hot fire was raging though my very bones. For a minute I saw the color blue—like the sky—and then it burst. It was a feeling of complete and utter freedom—of release, and it was followed by a vision. The vision was of James. He was soaring upward, toward heaven, and I felt the distinct impression he was finally and unequivocally free from all the pain he’d been suffering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Was this what Totherma meant when she said I’d know it if James was dead?","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"A Wild Sylphid Storm (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t think it would end like this. I thought the greenies would prepare me. They might’ve tried, but how could you prepare someone for this?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew I was finally free from pain—from the oppression of a mortal form. I’d felt like this once before.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My only regret was in leaving the girl behind. That pale-haired, pale-skinned, green-eyed beauty. I knew I’d loved her. Like really loved her. A part of me would never forget that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet every memory of her stayed behind, falling away from me like feathers, the higher I soared.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Journal: The Zephyrvana","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"639cbdf4cdfc470081ec54f81311403f"}]},{"n":46,"letterTitle":"The Bridge Over the Moon","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"The Bridge Over the Moon (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Hoptioning away from Roda Bay wasn’t ideal. If Lylend hadn’t insisted on trying to reason with the leader of the wild sylphids, we probably could’ve snuck back across the Bay. Storm or no storm, the Sina bracelet I brought along for Nori would’ve worked. But Lylend did speak to the leader, and the rest of us almost had our skin peeled from our bodies by the tempest that followed. One of the first lessons you learn as a princess is that diplomacy isn’t always the best answer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Even knowing what we knew about spy-o-lite, none of us could’ve anticipated what we’d find when the hoption hole spit us out into Phitessnie’s blood grove. I knew it was bad the moment I saw all the yellow eyes glaring at us from around the grove.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Because I’m Lev’s Kinzura, people often forget I was alive when the dogrils invaded Cobbogoth 25 years ago. I was ten according to your human years and, consequently, remember it well. Even so, I’d never seen so many dogrils gathered in one place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev,” I heard Lylend yell as fireballs started to fly, “get those kyndies on Brawlo’s back and out of here!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m not going anywhere,” I countered before Lev could respond. “Besides, Brawlo didn’t take the hoption hole with us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He didn’t?” Lylend sent a shaft of lightning that hit a Dogril Square in the chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I saw him disappear into the Bay while you were talking to the Sylphid,” Nori added.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend nodded, keeping his eyes on the dogrils. They were inching closer as their bodies phased in and out on the verge of morphing. “Very well, kyndie,” he said, taking his eyes off the dogrils just long enough to catch nori’s eye. “You know what you need to do then. Take the princess with you.” Iolyt and Pieter had joined the battle by then. “We’ll buy you as much time as we can. But hurry.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And be careful,” Iolyt added.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori nodded and grabbed my hand. “Come on,” she said, dragging me behind the trunk of the closest tree. When we were certain there were no dogrils hiding in the giant roots, she began scanning the ground for something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” I asked. “Where are we going?” I couldn’t help glancing back at Lev. He and the others were outnumbered at least ten to one. And that was only if more dogrils didn’t show up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There,” she said, pointing at a shaft of light indicating the closest entry point to the Drusy path. She pulled me toward it. I felt myself relax a little the moment we were on the path and invisible again. We’d traveled the path from the Glindian sea to Roda Bay on our way to Lobokia but had to leave it once we reached the Zephyrvana. I’d been on edge ever since.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now you might be asking yourself why the rest of the Hood didn’t jump on the path with us rather than stay and fight the dogrils. It’s a fair question. The simple answer is the others wouldn’t dare risk it for fear of alerting the dogrils to the Drusy path’s existence. The Hood worked tirelessly to keep the path a secret all these years so we could use it if, for example, something like spy-o-lite ever happened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Knowing all this, all I could do was hope that if it came down to it, Lylend would use the path to get Lev and the others out of there. I had no interest in losing another family today.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where are we going?” I asked again. Nori kept hold of my hand as we ran.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The verse on the wall back at Tarayvaine?” Nori panted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah?” I’d seen it and had overheard her and Lylend talking, but it hadn’t made much sense to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ve seen it before,” she explained. “In fact, it was one of the first things I saw when I arrived here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“OKAY?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It wasn’t all I saw, though,” she said. She was beginning to slow down. “There was something else…a bridge over the moon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“HUH?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry. It was a large, round, disc-shaped object. I thought it was supposed to be a moon—a bridge over a moon. But it’s not a moon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nori!” I cried, stopping her from going any further and taking her by the shoulders. “You’re not making any sense. If I’m going to leave Lev back there to potentially die, you need to make some sense!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Lemorian Crest!” She panted. “I think the moon is the Lemorian Crest.” She grabbed my hand again. “You’re going to have to trust me, okay? There’s not much time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, and we began running again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Phitessnie’s blood grove is an interesting place. Lylend and Iolyt told us all about it on our way to Lobokia. It’s this ancient forest of enormous white-trunked trees—trees with peculiar blood red leaves. The trees are the oldest in Cobbogoth, and as far as anyone knows, they don’t grow anywhere but in Phitessnie’s blood grove. The legend about this place says the earliest kin-klan chiefs created the grove as a homage to Phitessnie and his bloodsum. They created it before creating anything else in Cobbogoth. Another peculiar thing is that the leaves never fall. In fact, it’s believed they hold miraculous healing powers and will only fall if one is willing to make a pretty significant sacrifice first. According to the lore, one must be willing to sacrifice what one wants most in order to obtain a leaf.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What I wanted most right then was to be back fighting by Lev’s side, but if Nori was right and knew where the Lemorian Crest was, every member of the Hood would gladly put their life on the line to find it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We finally stopped running when we reached a small clearing in the grove. Without even checking the area, Nori pulled me off the path to our right and headed for what looked like a solid rock wall covered in vines. As we got closer to the wall, I could see that vines were camouflaging an entrance of some kind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori quickly glanced around and held back the curtain of vines, and we both hurried through. After moving through a narrow stone passage, it finally turned, and we both found ourselves standing in a giant cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Starlyt sea!” I breathed. It was a place I’d only heard about once or twice in legends Gram told me as a little girl. I never once dreamed the place actually existed.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"The Bridge Over the Moon (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"“This is where our ancestors first entered Cobbogoth after they left the Olden Realm,” Zuli said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes! You’ve been here before?” she asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “Follow me.” I would’ve liked to have given her more time to explore the place, but time was short. Instead, I did the next best thing and led Zuli up the stalagmite bridge I’d first crossed all those weeks ago after waking up on the tiny island with Bandier the Basili towering over me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we reached the center of the bridge, I pointed out in the distance. “Do you see that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The moon?” she asked. “Yeah, I see it. Wait, is that the—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think so.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How far out do you think it is?” Zuli asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“About a mile,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We better get swimming then.” Zuli handed me the sina bracelet. “Who knows how many more dogrils have shown up to fight the others.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew she was just as anxious for Lev’s safety as I was for James’, only I knew it might already be too late for James. What had that strange feeling and vision been all about?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed down the fear choking me and forced myself to focus on the here and now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I led the way to the other side of the bridge to the second staircase that was situated in the middle of the sea. With all the crystals and stalagmites haphazardly situated in the water, entering the sea from there instead of the shore would provide a more direct route to the “moon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, the two of us were in the water and transformed into sina. Zuli and I began swimming as fast as we could toward the moon. After a while, however, we halted as a small grove of Gihara Blooms burst to life around us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Here,” she said, picking one of the smaller flowers the size of an umbrella and handing it to me. I was surprised to feel how light it was. “We used to use these as torches before the Rayves took over the Glindian Sea,” she explained. “Since there’s obviously nothing alive down here but us, I think it’s safe to use them to guide us through the maze of stalactites, stalagmites, and crystal columns barricading our way to the moon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s almost like someone didn’t want us to get to it,” I said wryly after catching one of my copious fins on yet another jagged stalagmite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After we’d been swimming a while, I heard Zuli gasp from behind me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” I asked, spinning around.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are those what I think they are?” she breathed. I could hear fear in her voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” I swam back to where she’d stopped. Her head was close to the seafloor while her striped tail waved back and forth in the water like a flag. She gently brushed the dark sand away from something. The sand and something triangular in it immediately began to glow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait. Is that…?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli carefully pinched the glowing triangle between her thumb and finger and pulled it out of the sand. As she held it up to me, I could see it wasn’t a triangle but what looked like a glowing arrowhead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are they?” I asked, suspecting I already knew.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“These are lunegalda darts,” she said, carefully placing the glowing arrowhead back on the seafloor. It began to fade back into the blue-black sand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then that’s lunegalda sand?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded, then carefully swept her hand over the seafloor again and the sand began to glow. I realized I’d never gotten close enough to the seafloor during my first swim through the Starlyt Sea to notice any glowing sand or arrowheads. I’d only noticed the white stones that made up the constellation verse and that studded the seafloor and everything else in the cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How do you think they got up here?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“An earth kneader—Glip the Strong, I’m guessing—must’ve kneaded them all the way up here from the Lunegalda Mountains.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wow!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whatever you do from now on,” Zuli cautioned, “tread very carefully. Lunegalda darts are usually triggered by a certain amount of weight or by trespassing of some kind.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Trespassing? You mean they’re probably protecting the Lemorian Crest?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli nodded. “And if the border of whatever they’re protecting is crossed without some kind of sanction, they’ll go off.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean by ‘go off?’” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’re programmed to shoot at the trespasser, and they hardly ever miss.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. My mouth suddenly felt dry in spite of being surrounded by water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli’s eyes were still wide when she swam up to me. “Don’t worry, Nori,” she said, placing a comforting hand on my arm. “We’ll just go nice and slow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right,” I said, beginning to swim again. “Nice and slow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally reached the moon, the lunegalda darts were still resting peacefully on the seafloor. On the other hand, the moon-shaped disk appeared to be embedded in the wall of a peculiar little alcove made by a series of “star-studded” stalagmites shooting up from the seafloor, creating a half circle, like a cage around the disk. I could easily reach in and touch the disk, but instead I decided to carefully examine it first.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you see that?” I asked as Zuli swam up beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The seven keyholes?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “For seven elemental keys,” I said, pointing at the elemental symbols beside each key. If that wasn’t enough proof, the ancient Cobbogothian writing surrounding the bottom edge of the disc was covered in sand, but I could almost make out the word “Lemoria.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only there was a rewind button on life. If only I could reverse what happened next, I would in a heartbeat. But everything happened so fast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli was saying something next to me when I reached out between the stalagmite bars to sweep away the sand along the bottom of the crest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stop!” Zuli shouted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started, spinning toward her. But it was too late. Lunegalda darts began flying through the water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli threw herself forward, shoving me behind a stalagmite pillar, but not before I heard her suck in her breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to find her holding her right arm. Her eyes were wide with fear as a cloud of blood began to seep between her fingers.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Starlyt Sea","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"318ce995585542ba822eb4cc48665d9a"}]},{"n":47,"letterTitle":"Phitessnie’s Blood Grove","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Phitessnie’s Blood Grove (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Do you know what it feels like to have someone willing to die for you, dear reader? I do. If you’re wondering, it doesn’t get any easier to accept the more it happens. Each selfless act evokes a strange combination of humility, gratitude, complete unworthiness, and soul-crushing guilt. The sense of obligation in every ounce of my being to ensure their sacrifice wasn’t in vain is a burden of epic proportions. In all honesty, I wouldn’t wish it on my worst enemy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Don’t get me wrong; I’m beyond grateful for Uncle Jack…and James…and probably a few other people I don’t know about yet. And I know it wasn’t necessarily that they were putting their lives on the line for me but for the Haven—for the Cobbogothians. Regardless, it’s not in my nature…or nurture…to easily accept help from anyone. I suppose that’s what practically raising yourself does.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet here I was with one more person putting their life on the line for me when Zuli swam in front of a Lunegalda dart to protect me from my own reckless stupidity. If I just hadn’t touched the crest before thinking! If I’d just used my brain!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’ll only stop if you prove you’re authorized to retrieve the crest,” Zuli said after I pulled her inside the henge surrounding the crest. I positioned her against one of the stone pillars for protection as Lunegalda darts continued to fly all around us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Think, Nor,” I said, trying to stop the trembling taking over my whole body. This was not the time to fall apart. How could I forgive myself if she died? Think. Think. Okay, all I had to do to make the darts stop flying was…what? What was it? Lylend and I had gone over this several times on our journey to Lobokia, but my brain was frozen in panic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your medallion!” Zuli cried, gesturing to my qualdrine with a trembling finger. There was a note of exasperation in her voice, which was encouraging. If she could still get annoyed with me, the poison hadn’t hit her brain yet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gave my head a little shake. “Right, the medallion.” Until recently, none of us knew I had been carrying around the “space key”—or Totherma’s key—to the Lemorian Crest all along. It was my qualdrine medallion, the one Totherma had given Uncle Jack when she left me in his care 18 years ago. Lylend had come back from retrieving whatever new messages the Phoenix had left for him, only to tell me he’d been thinking it over and thought it might be the case. He mentioned the theory to Brawlo, who confirmed it with Totherma, and then Totherma chimed us on our way to Lobokia to tell us herself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently, when the kinchiefs had originally hidden the crest during Bruxrac, Totherma thought it best if she didn’t go with them—she didn’t want to know where it was in case Holictrian caught up with her and tried to torture the location of it from her. But she did give the others her key in case they needed it. When they returned it to her, all they told her was when the Lemorian Crest was found, her key would be the only way to unlock it from its hiding place. Which was, of course, why she had passed the medallion on to me. It had been hidden in the Olden Realm for 18 years, kept safe for this very moment.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No pressure, huh,” I could hear James teasing me if he were here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hurried to the crest and, with trembling hands, placed my qualdrine medallion into the keyhole at the center of the carved relic. The moment I did, the Lunegalda darts stopped flying, and the dials on both my medallion and the keyhole began spinning. Then the crest popped free from the seafloor, sending up a cloud of shimmery blue-black sand all around us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grabbed the basketball-sized stone disc and headed back to Zuli.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why did you do that?” I asked, finally able to think again. Hefting the crest in one arm, I caught Zuli with the other as she slid down the stone pillar to the seafloor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What were you thinking, jumping in front of it like that?” My throat was so tight I almost couldn’t get the words out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I was thinking two things,” she said weakly. The poison was rapidly moving through her system now. “I was thinking this was my chance to prove to you once and for all how sorry I am for trying to drown you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I already forgave—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And that you’re my people’s final hope for survival. I would do anything for my people, Nori,” she interrupted in a trembling voice. “And I would do anything for you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, Zu,” I hugged her close. I knew I was crying even without being able to feel the tears on my face. Zuli had become more than a friend to me since learning she was Gram’s several times removed great-granddaughter. We’d become more like sisters. In fact, we were practically roomies, since she’d started camping out in my rodãor after the Flame Fleck Island incident.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now go,” Zuli said, pushing me away. “Take the crest and go save the others.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not without you,” I said, repositioning the crest in one arm and wrapping the other around Zuli’s tiny waist. Even though I was several inches taller than her, I knew right away this wasn’t going to work.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s too heavy,” Zuli moaned in pain. “Get the crest to shore and come back for me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m not going anywhere without you,” I told her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli’s strength was fading fast. When she tried to protest, it wasn’t very convincing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My eyes scanned the seafloor frantically for something—anything—that could help me cart both Zuli and the crest back to shore. But there was nothing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What was I going to do?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then I thought of Totherma. I thought of the communocite pillar on the tiny island I’d first woken up on with Bandier standing guard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll be right back,” I said and swam to the surface. When my head broke above water, I looked around until I located the island. There it was, less than half a mile away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swam back to Zuli. “A com stone!” I said. “Please tell me you have one!” I’d left mine with my shyntara back on shore. Zuli produced one from somewhere in the skirt-like fins around her waist.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I said, propping her against a stalagmite. “I’m going to get help.” I left the crest propped beside her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took me less than five minutes to reach the island. Once I’d hefted myself ashore, I removed the sina bracelet and hurried to the communocite pillar. Three chimes later, I was thanking the Gihara as Totherma appeared in the crystal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Libkyn? What’s happened? Where are you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Over the next few minutes, I told her a very short version of everything that had happened since Tarayvaine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She willingly jumped in front of the Lunegalda dart for you?” Totherma clarified once I’d finished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, swallowing back fresh emotion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good? She’s going to die, Totherma!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not if you listen very carefully to me. It’s ‘good’ because now you have the power inside of you to not only save Zuli and retrieve the Lemorian Crest but to save the rest of the Hood from the dogrils as well.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, don’t argue with me,” Totherma said. “There isn’t time. You must do exactly as I tell you. Do you understand?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “What do I do?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“First, you must believe you can wield fire and water if you hope to succeed.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Water?” I laughed, mystified. “The water will kill me if I try to use fire down there.” I held up the sina bracelet. “This is the only reason it hasn’t killed me already simply for having fire power.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma held up her hand to stop me. “Have you ever wondered why the first element you have been able to wield is fire? Or why you were able to wield it so powerfully without even trying?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I thought of how my body had automatically responded on its own when Pieter tried to bury me alive. And all the other crazy things Lylend had been teaching me on our journey to Tarayvaine. I shrugged. “Because James transferred it to me before he paid the blood sum?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes and no. It all has to do with bonds, libkyn. You are able to wield fire because of your bond with a Flame Thrower.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. But these aren’t just any bonds. These are powerful bonds forged through selfless sacrifice on one another’s behalf.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But James paid the blood sum,” I said. “I didn’t do anything but try to stop him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But you allowed him to pay it in the end, didn’t you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A space shifter does not receive their power in the same way other Cobbogothians do, Noriah. A space shifter absorbs her power from those she’s bonded to, but only when both parties involved prove they are mutually willing to make the ultimate sacrifice for one another. That kind of selfless love is a mighty power in and of itself.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So you’re saying that since Zuli took a Lunegalda dart for me—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m saying that since you were both willing to enter these dangerous waters in the selfless pursuit of the greater good, and Zuli has proven as much by taking that dart for you, you are now a diowyn. And not only that, but you are now able to wield water to the extent that Zuli herself is able to.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So people always have to die in order for me to get my power?” I asked in disgust.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, not die,” Totherma countered. “But prove without a doubt that they’re willing to die for you and you for them. That is the key, Noriah. You must be willing to die for them in return. There is no greater act of love—no bond so strong.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma paused, her violet eyes seeming to pierce my very soul. “Zuli has just proven her willingness to die for you; how will you prove the same to her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—I am willing,” I said. “But how do I do it without actually dying?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Only you can know how, libkyn. And I have every confidence you will figure it out before it is too late.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With that, Totherma vanished from the communocite pillar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Frustrated, I put the sina bracelet back on and dove into the water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the time I reached Zuli and the crest again, however, nothing had come to me. I could pick up a Lunegalda dart and pierce my skin with it, but where would that get us? I could run out into the raging battle with the rest of the Hood and let the dogrils have at me, but that would take too long.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then it hit me, a plan that would allow me to prove my willingness to die for Zu and get us back to shore with the crest in time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After gathering Zuli and then returning again for the crest, I swam back to the surface. Then, after perching Zu and the crest on a nearby crystal beam, I removed the sina bracelet again and conjured a ball of fire in the water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Holding my breath, I waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are you doing?” Zuli asked, her speech slurred.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I held my fireball higher, but nothing happened. Not only didn’t the Starlyt Sea try and kill me, but, gathering Zuli and the crest in my arms, I was able to locate the water now flowing through my veins. Breathing a giant sigh of relief, I focused with all my might and miraculously mustered a giant wave out of the water. I body surfed it with Zuli and the crest close beside me as the wave catapulted all three of us back to shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pushing myself onto my forearms from where the wave had deposited me, I scrambled across the sand to Zuli. She was lying on her side with her eyes closed. I rolled her onto her back and pressed my ear to her chest. I could just make out a faint heartbeat. There wasn’t much time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After locating my things where I’d left them on the shore, I quickly dressed and safely stowed the crest in my rodãor. Then I made my way to the water’s edge. All Totherma’s talk about sacrifice had given me an idea of how I could save Zuli’s life. As we passed through Phitessnie’s Blood Grove on our way to Tarayvaine, Lylend and Iolyt had told Lev, Zuli, and me the legend of the grove and its sacred healing leaves. If their stories were more than just legends, getting one of those leaves was my best shot at saving Zuli.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But first I would need some cover if I wanted to make it out of the cave. If my idea worked, it would make things a bit easier for the rest of the Hood too. Even from inside the cave, I could hear the sounds of battle raging.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Taking a deep breath, I conjured another fireball in my palm. I cast a final glance back at Zuli; she was looking worse by the second. I lowered my hand into the water, and as soon as I saw the water begin to boil, I did the same with my other hand. I focused harder, sending as much fire as I could muster into the sea.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A thick cloud of steam soon began rising from the water. I started gathering it to me and then sending it out the entrance of the cave. When I’d forced so much heat into the water that the entire Starlyt Sea began to boil, I was very glad no creatures lived in it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I’d produced enough steam to create a heavy fog over the surface of the sea, I left the fireballs in the water and heaved Zuli over my shoulder. Gathering an especially thick cloud around us, I sent the rest of the fog out of the cave before me, then headed outside into the battle.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I reached the grove, all I could see was white. Thick fog clung to every surface, swirling through the roots of the giant trees, clinging to their weeping branches. The battle had paused in the confusion, and I took my chance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hurrying as fast as I could, I stayed low and hidden in my cloud. I knew I had to find a tree far enough from the battle but still shrouded in fog so I could retrieve one of the leaves without being noticed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I heard a familiar trilling sound close by.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Brawlo!” I whispered. “Brawlo! Are you there?” At first, I couldn’t see anything, but as I moved closer to the noise, I began to see a large shadow in the fog.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Brawlo! It’s me!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I found the giant weasel perched several feet above me on a large tree root. As soon as he saw me, he scurried down to help me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Can you carry her up?” I asked. Away from the tree roots where the battle was raging was probably the safest place, and it was closer to the dangling bloodred leaves I needed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we’d climbed to a safe enough height, I left Zuli with Brawlo and climbed a bit further up the tree to the closest sap collection. Remembering what Lylend and Iolyt had said, I plunged my finger into the crimson goop and scooped up a mouthful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sap was bitter and almost minty, but the aftertaste was sweet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I forced myself to swallow, and then waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A moment passed, and then a peculiar feeling began to rise up from my stomach. I was suddenly enveloped in a rushing wind. Then for the second time in my life, I heard the voices of the Gihara singing to me—calling. They sang of love and loss and healing. It was so beautiful and comforting after everything that had happened, I wanted to burst into tears.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the voices died down, I looked around but wasn’t sure what I was supposed to be looking for. According to Lylend and Iolyt, the tree was supposed to present me with a sacrifice—or something I had to choose to sacrifice in exchange for one of its precious leaves.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It didn’t take long before I saw it. The sight made me falter, and I nearly lost my balance where I still clung to the tree.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A Dark Matter Door blazed on a patch of thick, coppery moss on the ground just beyond Brawlo’s shoulder. I didn’t even have to climb down from my perch to recognize the map at the center of the circle.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sacrifice was clear, and it nearly stole all my resolve. This was the Dark Matter Door that led to the room with the stone table—the room where James was being held. All I had to do was step inside that circle, and I would know what that strange experience had been back at Roda Bay. I would know once and for all if James was still alive.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Phitessnie’s Blood Grove (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"There’s not a lot I can tell you about what happened in the battle at Phitessnie’s blood grove, other than the kyndie saved us all in the end.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not only did she figure out how to save the Adolorian princess with a pulp made from one of those bloodred leaves—a feat only possible through great personal cost, mind—but she also created a thick fog for the rest of us to take cover in. In fact, that fog ultimately enabled us to escape the dogrils without any casualties.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We were outnumbered to begin with, but we were holding them off successfully. Cifer must’ve known we were the ones his spy-o-lite had caught in the grove. The old devil never made an appearance himself, of course. How I would’ve relished the opportunity to fight him again. However, because he kept sending more and more dogrils to destroy us, we were heavily outnumbered. Every one of us could’ve been as battle-hardened and skilled as myself but it would’ve been the end of us had Noriah not produced that fog when she did. The fog gave us a place to hide and catch our breath and, inevitably, a means of escape.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, we’d just resumed fighting the dogrils when the kyndie appeared out of nowhere and began creating tunnels through the fog. An entrance to one would appear next to where a Hood member was fighting, and she’d yell for them to enter it. Once they did, she’d close the fog around them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I, with my military experience, and the kyndie, with her newly acquired water power and her fire power—a power that gave her a better chance at surviving the dogrils than any of us—remained to the last and covered the rest as they escaped through the fog. My Jamus would’ve been mighty proud of her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When it was only the two of us left facing an ever-growing Dogril squadron, the kyndie opened a tunnel near me. She told me to follow it until I found Brawlo guarding her open rodãor. She instructed me to jump inside—that she would follow shortly—and she and Brawlo would carry the rest of us safely through the Glorian pass, back to the Glindian sea where we could then make our way back to Feg.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Despite the handicap of being raised in the Olden realm, it was an impressive plan. I don’t need to tell you, human, that when I ran into that tunnel and lost sight of her as the fog closed in around me, I knew I’d have to answer to more than my kynd if any harm came to Noriah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yes, it’s true I felt some trepidation about leaving the kyndie. But I’ve said it before, and I’ll say it again: She’s a Cobbogothian. The sooner she learns what she’s capable of, the better. For all of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo was there waiting by the open rodãor when I reached the tunnel’s end. The others were all inside and accounted for. Several excruciatingly long moments passed. In fact, we were all preparing to head back out into the fog to find the kyndie when she suddenly poked her head inside, told us to “hang tight,” and closed the rodãor on us, trapping us inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the next several hours.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As we waited, the princess showed me the Lemorian Crest she and Noriah had successfully retrieved. She told me how Noriah had saved her life—what she’d sacrificed to get that leaf. I’m not ashamed to say I wept openly then, human. I’m not a weeper, but these tears were not tears of sorrow, as you might’ve assumed. These tears were tears of pride and, dare I say, gratitude. Had the kyndie chosen to take that dark matter door and discover Jamus’s fate, consequences far more severe than the princess’ death would’ve occurred.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the rodãor finally opened and let us out, we found ourselves on the shores of the Glindian sea. It was midnight, and Melusian’s daughter Mala was there. She was the one who opened the rodãor. She had no answers for us, at least none that we could understand. All Zuli could make out from her mumbling and gurgling is that she happened upon an extremely distressed Brawlo after meeting Romi, her earth kneader, for a forbidden midnight rendezvous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She directed us to Brawlo, who had refused to leave a crystal alcove near the shore. He was guarding it with considerable fierceness—I honestly didn’t know the gentle creature had it in him. We tried to calm him, as he was trilling and hissing like a wild animal—of which I had to firmly remind him he is not. When the confusion finally died down, we found what he was guarding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the kyndie. She was lying unconscious in an alarmingly large pool of blood. Lyttie ran to her, only to roll her over and discover one of her arms barely hanging on by a thread.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ve recorded Brawlo’s account of what happened after the battle while the rest of us were trapped in the rodãor. He was quite upset, human, so you, with your inadequate brain and inability to feel the emotions he communicated to us telepathically, may find it difficult to understand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Alas, you’ve stayed with us this long, so I thought I would at least let you try.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Phitessnie’s Blood Grove (Part 3)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"NORIAH CAME OUT OF FOG AFTER LYLEND.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"DOGRILS FOLLOWED CLOSE BEHIND.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"NORIAH CLOSED RODÃOR. CLIMBED ON BRAWLO’S BACK.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"MORE DOGRILS CAME. TOO RISKY TO GET ON DRUSY PATH. MUST KEEP SECRET.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"BRAWLO RAN INSTEAD. BRAWLO RAN AND RAN AND RAN DOWN MOUNTAIN. HEART ALMOST BURSTING. DOGRILS CHASING ALL THE TIME.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"AT GLORIAN PASS, DOGRILS CAUGHT UP TO NORIAH AND BRAWLO.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"NORIAH USED FIRE AND WATER TO FIGHT, BUT NOT ENOUGH.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"DOGRILS SCRATCHED BRAWLO WITH TALONS. BRAWLO VERY HURT, BUT LOVE NORIAH. BRAWLO PROTECTED NORIAH.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"NORIAH PROTECTED BRAWLO. NORIAH BROKE OPEN UNSTABLE GATE IN GLORIAN PASS. BIG WIND CAME FROM GATE AND THROUGH PASS. GATE SUCKED US TOWARD IT.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"NORIAH SAVED BRAWLO FROM BEING SUCKED INTO GATE. NORIAH FREEZED BRAWLO’S PAWS TO GROUND OUTSIDE OF GATE. NORIAH HELD ONTO BRAWLO SO TIGHT. DOGRILS HURT NORIAH BEFORE GATE SUCKED THEM INSIDE.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"BRAWLO AND NORIAH ARE NOT SAFE YET. ICE ON BRAWLO’S PAWS BEGAN TO MELT FROM OTHER DOGRILS’ FIRE. NORIAH BROKE OPEN OTHER GATE. OTHER DOGRILS SUCKED INTO NEW GATE.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"NORIAH HELPED BRAWLO ESCAPE GETTING SUCKED INTO GATE AND DIFFERENT DIMENSION. NORIAH FROZE BRAWLO’S PAWS TO GROUND THEN MELTED, THEN FROZE THEM, THEN MELTED THEM AGAIN AND AGAIN, ONE STEP AT A TIME UNTIL BRAWLO AND NORIAH OUT OF HARM’S WAY.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"WHEN FINALLY SAFE FROM GLORIAN PASS, BRAWLO SAW NORIAH VERY HURT. ARM NOT WORKING RIGHT. NORIAH BLED MUCH. BRAWLO LOVES NORIAH. BRAWLO NOT LET NORIAH DIE.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"MORE DOGRILS COMING.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"BRAWLO FOUND DRUSY PATH. BRAWLO GOT ON DRUSY PATH. BRAWLO RAN AND RAN AND RAN. BRAWLO FOUND MALA AND ROMI AT GLINDIAN SEA.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"BRAWLO PROTECT NORIAH WITH LIFE WHILE MALA OPEN RODÃOR.","i":true}],"narrator":"Brawlo"},{"title":"Journal: Blood Grove","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e124163e325a4b288a6a82ab7a603f8d"}]},{"n":48,"letterTitle":"The Wood Weavers’ Gate","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Iolyt's Qualdriny Lab","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4b09edc55b2a42daa12a12aa4aaa18a8"},{"title":"The Wood Weavers’ Gate (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I genuinely never thought I’d be able to say I lost an arm over something. Not that I thought about it a lot, but you know what I mean. I guess I technically didn’t lose an arm over Zu and the Lemorian Crest, thanks to Iolyt. But I almost did, and that’s going to be a wicked cool story to tell my grandkids one day.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In all seriousness, though, I really thought I was a goner for a minute. Apparently, everyone else did too when they found me unconscious on the Glindian shore. If not for Romi and Mala, I would’ve bled to death before anyone with opposable thumbs—I think Mala still has those—found me and let the others out of my rodãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The last thing I remember before passing out was the City of Stones in the distance. To be honest, I almost blacked out a couple of times after seeing my arm…or the lack thereof. The blood didn’t help. Thanks to Brawlo, who kept telepathically screaming at me to stay awake, I stayed conscious enough to hold on while he ran for the shore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s right! That’s because he’s such a good boy! Yes, he is! Yes, he is! Who’s a good boy? You are Brawlo! That’s right. Such a good, sweet…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ehem…sorry about that. Got distracted for a minute while petting Brawlo. The sweet guy hasn’t left my side since I woke up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I don’t think either of us realized how much danger we were really in. All I would’ve had to do was pick a different gate to break open in the Glorian Pass, and it would’ve been game over. As it just so happened, I picked the only two gates that led to Arctic dimensions. That wind sucking the dogrils and us toward the gates was absolutely freezing and, consequently, the only thing keeping the dogrils’ fire from melting the skin off our bones. That and it allowed me to use my new water power to freeze Brawlo’s feet to the ground (and me to Brawlo) so we didn’t get sucked into space along with the dogrils.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, according to Zuli, I didn’t wake up from the whole ordeal for two weeks. When I did, I found myself on one of Iolyt’s healing cots, with every stone and crystal imaginable dangling over me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, I actually thought I was in Samir’s lab back at the Mirage on Harvard Square. Iolyt’s qualdriny lab reminds me a lot of Samir’s lab, only it’s much more organized…and colorful. In fact, if you combined Samir’s mystic shop downstairs, with its bright boho vibe and crystal curtains, with his science lab upstairs, you’d come up with a pretty decent resemblance to Iolyt’s lab.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She has crystals of every color imaginable growing in all sorts of different sized beakers. Jars of colorful crystal powders line the shelves. She has different combinations of healing crystals hanging from bryothyniums, which are kind of like sun catchers, but they have crystals instead of pieces of colored glass. And instead of just shining pretty light into your space, bryothyniums shine healing energy down onto whoever they hang over. You’ll have to see what I mean in my journal notes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, thankfully I woke up just in time to bid Lylend farewell. Now that we have the Lemorian Crest, he was heading off to Leborana Falls again to see if he could find more clues concerning the rest of the keys. We’ve been at a bit of a standstill while my arm has been mending.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everyone has been great, though. I’ve had daily visits from Lev, Zuli, and Iolyt. I also discovered the Hood has a new member. Remember that Romi guy—Mala’s boyfriend? The one who helped Brawlo let the others out of my rodãor in time? Turns out he’s not just any old earth kneader. He’s a triowyn, and when Lylend found out, he invited him to come to Feg. Romi’s second and third powers are water and spark. He was more than happy to agree when Lylend and Zuli promised to make him their tyro and teach him how to use his secondary powers.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since Lylend has been away, the rest of us are keeping busy. Zuli’s been keeping her promise to Romi, and Lev has joined in. The three of them have been working night and day on their water wielding. I’ll be joining in once I’m strong enough.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt disappears into her lab in the morning and is there until well into the following morning trying to figure out an antidote stone for spy-o-lite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As for Pieter…well, I actually don’t know what Pieter’s been up to. Don’t really care as long as he keeps a healthy distance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As for me, I’ve been using the extra downtime to recoup and have been keeping myself from going crazy—or “zyrky” as Zuli calls it—by studying a large stack of scrolls on Dark Matter Doors Lylend loaned me before he left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is what I was doing this morning when it happened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev, Zuli, and Romi invited me to go with them down to Feg’s lookout point for a picnic so they could practice. Like I said, I’m still too weak to join in—apparently reattaching an arm takes a decent amount of time, even with magic stones. I have been watching and learning while I study my scrolls, though.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I was spread out on a blanket, crystal scrolls and various Dark Matter Door maps scattered around me. I’d been trying to memorize as many of the different DMD maps as I could before Lylend gets back. I was kind of, sort of, hoping he would agree to let me chime Totherma again (apparently when I chimed her back at the Starlyt Sea, I was committing a serious cultural faux pas…which she didn’t seem to mind that much) and see if we couldn’t start setting up some regular DMD lessons. After all, she’s the only other person in Cobbogoth who knows how to use them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Turns out I didn’t need to chime Totherma. In fact, I was just moving on to my fifth map when she was suddenly in my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jolted up from my blanket but kept my eyes closed so I would keep seeing her. She was beckoning to me again—like she had at Flame Fleck Island—like she wanted me to follow her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. “How?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Open your eyes, lybkin,” her voice echoed in my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I obeyed. The sand before me sparkled with a fully materialized Dark Matter Door. I didn’t recognize the map inside, but you better believe I wasn’t about to let this chance pass me by.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced to where the others were practicing. Their backs were turned to me, so I scrambled into the DMD and laid down in the fetal position.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, I was zooming through dark matter. Lights and sounds buzzed past me, sending chills down my spine. When I reached the end of the path, another DMD was there waiting for me. I drifted over to hover over the second door. The darkness faded, and I found myself standing in a very familiar place. It was the Glorian Pass. I started a little, glancing up at the sky for possible dogrils. When I saw none, I sighed and looked around. Why would Totherma bring me here?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The gates of the pass lined the canyon on either side of me, but I was situated in front of the Wood Weaver’s Gate—the one Brawlo told me the wood weavers had disappeared through during Bruxrac.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a sudden rustling behind me, and I froze, images of dogrils seizing my mind again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Someone cleared their throat. “Hello there?” It was a man’s voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slowly turned around to face him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He stood several yards away from me beneath a small tree—small for Cobbogoth—just at the edge of the pass. The morning light silhouetted him from behind, so I couldn’t see his face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You wouldn’t be able to tell me where I am, would you?” he asked. There was something about his accent that struck me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Um…yeah, this is the Glorian Pass,” I managed, gesturing around me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Glorian Pass?” He looked like he was rubbing his chin. “Never heard of it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did you come through this gate?” I asked, gesturing back toward the Wood Weaver’s Gate.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shrugged.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where did you come from?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Silence stretched out between us. Then he shrugged again. “I honestly can’t remember.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay…um…do you know where you’re heading?” I asked. Maybe that’s why Totherma brought me here. Maybe this guy was looking for the Hood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was about to pry some more, just to be safe, before mentioning the Hood, but then the guy stepped out of the shadows and into the light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first thing I noticed were his eyes. They were blue. Deep, soul-piercing blue.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James?” I whispered. I nearly choked on a sob. “Holy smokes, James! I thought you were dead.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slowly floated closer to him. His eyes didn’t budge from mine. They were searching my own, like he was trying to figure something out—something really important.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But the closer I got to him, the more confused I became. There was something very different about him. What was it?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally reached him, I stopped, hovering at arm’s length away from him. He looked different, yet it had to be him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James extended his hand to me, and for a long time I just stared at it, confused.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You all right?” he asked. “My name’s Jamus, but I’ll answer to James, too.” A dimple formed in his cheek beneath his copper whiskers. I teared up a little. I’d really, really missed seeing that dimple.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Without thinking, I reached out to take his hand. Only then did I remember the rule about Dark Matter Doors. Only souls can touch souls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No wait!” I breathed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James opened his mouth in surprise when his hand went through mine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then I was speeding back through the dark matter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I opened my eyes, Zu, Lev, and Romi were all hovering over me. Relief replaced worry on their faces when I opened my eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You okay, Nori?” Lev asked the moment I shot up from the ground.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He’s alive!” I said, swiping a tear from my cheek. “James is alive!”","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"The Wood Weavers’ Gate (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Would you believe me, human, if I told you I didn’t relish climbing raging waterfalls? At my age, it’s no “walk in the park.” I believe that’s a phrase you’ll understand. The truth is, this impromptu trip to Leborana falls was only part of the reason I left the rest of the Hood back on Feg. Did I truly believe I would find more clues pertaining to more Lemorian keys? Perhaps. But it was unlikely. I am not a stranger to the record room at Leborana falls. Only Totherma has scoured its cubbies more thoroughly than myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that was only part of the reason for my journey. And certainly not the most important. The real reason for my journey was to visit Gram’s Unda tree on the banks of the Opaline river. My journey was to see if the Phoenix had returned with more news of Cifer’s movements.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Even you, a human, will be able to see from the messages I’ve included that my efforts were not in vain.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Bryothynium Craft","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f1a1230f3bb241f58f8493a9daaecb3d"},{"title":"Spy Notes","kind":"webp","pages":6,"id":"ac91cebe057a43d09c20d3486221a857"},{"title":"Thank You & The End","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a970d29c70964c7f8afe5bce8a09a38a"},{"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Something is rotten in Rasili.","i":true},{"t":"brk"},{"t":"p","x":"Attend the Triolune Festival but keep your children close. There is a way to possess the metal key, but you must first win the Great Tournament.","i":true},{"t":"brk"},{"t":"p","x":"There is an unda tree on the edge of the Rocklin Marshlands. The one closest to Rasili and the River Troll. One of its roots is marked with my signet. Further intelligence, should there be any, can be found there.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]}]},{"part":3,"element":"Metal","chronicles":[{"n":49,"letterTitle":"Triolune","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Triolune Prophecy","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"75f4c5bd45284900b16dbbee989b033d"},{"title":"Triolune","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You’re probably wondering why I included this strange legend about dancing moons, tournaments, and true love with this chronicle. It’ll make sense if you keep reading.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"However, hardly anything else seems to make sense for me lately. I feel like I’ve kind of been free-falling through the Twilight Zone or something. Just to be clear, by “Twilight Zone” I don’t mean some strange realm where sparkly-skinned vampires play baseball in the woods. I mean the vintage cult classic TV series Twilight Zone, where creepy dolls keep showing up in random places, and gorgeous girls get plastic surgery to trade their perfect noses for pig snouts. THAT Twilight Zone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I mean, come on! James was right there in the Glorian Pass. I talked to him. I finally found him in a place I recognized, and then I lost him again. Why? All because of Dark Matter Doors and the fact that only souls can touch other souls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I suppose the fact that I couldn’t touch him without getting sucked back into my body is a small comfort. At least I know he wasn’t just a soul himself—that he isn’t dead. Right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Needless to say, ever since I talked to James at the Glorian Pass and then lost him again, I haven’t been much use to anyone. Thankfully nobody has needed me to be much use lately, especially since we’re still trying to find our next lead where the Lemorian keys are concerned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All this and more was spinning through my head as it always did when I went to talk to Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I found the old Naridi at his regular spot in his record room. As usual, he had heaps of crystal scrolls piled up around him—scrolls Shudder flat out refused to put away for him. Why the heck Lylend continued to keep that creepy dude around was beyond me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Actually…I take that back. I did ask him about it once. It was after one of Shudder’s more violent windy tantrums. Lylend answered me with a shrug, saying something about it having to do with 10,000 years of shared history with the “poor devil” and the fact that Shudder knew way too much to be wandering Cobbogoth as a free sylphid. As a reason for keeping creepy sylphids skulking around your rodãor, I suppose it wasn’t terrible. Not that it made my daily pilgrimage from my own rodãor to Lylend’s any more enjoyable.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I always stopped in to see James’ dad after I’d finished studying Dark Matter Door maps for a few hours and then doing elemental training with Lev and Zuli for a few more. And I came with the same question on my lips each time. “When can we start looking for James?” Today was no different.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend didn’t notice I entered his record room until I was standing beside him, saying his name for the third time. He glanced up, glazed-eyed, with the remnants of the crystal scroll he’d been studying still in his hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah, kyndie! Just the person I wanted to see.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really? What for?” I asked, trying to gauge from the pile of scrolls on his table what kind of mood he was in. Like I said, we hadn’t had much luck finding more Lemorian keys since Zuli and I almost died retrieving the Lemorian Crest from the Starlyt Sea.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well…I’ve found something, and I think, amongst other things, it will answer the question you were coming here to ask me,” Lylend continued.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How’d you know what I was gonna ask?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because you ask me the same thing every time, kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Which is?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whether or not we can go look for my kynd.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So can we…finally?” I asked, trying to keep things light by adding an exasperated laugh to my question.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your persistence would’ve been a golden asset on the battlefield during my military years, kyndie,” he countered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before either of us could say anymore, Lev and Zuli came wandering into the record room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There you are, Nori!” Lev bellowed brightly. “I’ve been looking everywhere for you. Zuli refuses to play anymore Callowynder today, but I know you won’t say no.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli rolled her eyes at me before glancing back at Lev with unmasked adoration.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m glad the two of you are here,” Lylend said. “Where’s Iolyt and your father?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Iolyt’s dragging the old man here as we speak,” Lev said, gesturing toward the door.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As if on cue, Pieter and Iolyt came marching into the record room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Watch your words, konkle,” snapped Pieter. He and Lev were the spitting image of one another except for Pieter’s silver-hair, silver eyes, and extremely unpleasant personality.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before Pieter could start in on Lev, Lylend invited us all to take a seat. When we were all situated at the long record room table, he began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I believe I’ve finally found another key to the Lemorian Crest,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You did?” several of us asked in unison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend pulled out a crystal scroll, unrolled it, and slid it across the table for the rest of us to see.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” Pieter barked, irritation etched in every crinkle of his face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s a poster,” Lylend replied, “and the perfect opportunity for us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Opportunity? What sort of opportunity?” Iolyt asked in her quiet way. The contrasting, white streak in her black hair looked less pristine than usual. She must’ve just come from working in her qualdriny lab. She was still dissecting the stone Lylend had given her a few months ago. It was called spyolite. Cifer had created it to boobytrap all the hoption holes in Cobbogoth, which made it take ten times longer to travel anywhere.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend proceeded to expound on the poster details. (See the copy I’ve included!)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Neither of you kyndlin have been alive during a Triolune Festival,” Lylend said, gesturing to me and Lev, “but you’ll most likely have heard of one by now—including you, kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. The prophecy Totherma made that changed the trajectory of my entire life happened at the last Triolune Festival 20 years ago. The prophecy was even referred to as the Triolune Prophecy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are you planning?” Iolyt asked Lylend, her black eyes glinting like shiny beetles.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m thinking we should take these kyndlin to their first Triolune Festival, Lyttie. While we’re there, we might try and win ourselves another Lemorian key—the metal key. If it’s anywhere, it will be in Rasili, and if anyone knows where it is—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It will be Rasili’s kinchief.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend nodded, a look of excitement in his eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“DonTon is not just going to hand over a Lemorian key,” Zuli offered. “How do you plan to get it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Callowynder,” Lev breathed, barely audible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right, kynd.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No. Way.” Lev jolted forward, grasping his golden curls with both hands as a giant smile spread across his sun-kissed face. “Are you zyrking me right now?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend shrugged, fighting a smile of his own. “You’re the best Callowynder contender I’ve seen in five millennia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no…” Lev’s face fell. “I can’t compete,” he said. “I’m a verowyn. They only allow monowyns to enter in order to keep things fair.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, my kynd, you’re in luck.” Lylend tapped the poster between us, pointing to a certain paragraph. “DonTon is holding a completely separate tournament for multi-wyns. I think we have a real chance at winning.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes! Yes! Yes!” Lev whooped, punching a fist into the air to punctuate each word. “I can’t believe this!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait, hold on,” I said. “Lev’s going to go win a board game? How is that going to help us find a Lemorian key…or James?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This isn’t a board game, Nori” Lev said, turning to me, his eyes blazing with excitement. “This is what the board game was created for—a Callowynder tournament…THE Callowynder tournament.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“O-kay? What am I missing?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The board game was only ever intended for kyndlin,” Iolyt explained, looking more animated than I’d ever seen her, “to help familiarize them with the way our world works and their powers. But it’s only a watered-down version of true Callowynder. The true Callowynder tournament is much, much more than that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Try thinking of the Triolune tournament as the Cobbogothian version of the Super Bowl or the Olympics back in the Olden Realm,” Lylend explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oooohhh!” I said, understanding settling in. “So…when you say Lev’s the best you’ve seen in five millennia, you’re saying he’s like the Tom Brady of Callowynder?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend touched the side of his nose. “Precisely.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wow.” I suddenly saw Lev in a new light. “Wow!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev turned to me. “Whatever this bowl thing is and oly-olympics—how do you say it? You’re telling me all about it later,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help grinning. Lev would’ve loved Boston and its sports scene.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all jumped as Zuli squealed suddenly. “I can’t believe this!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I spun to look at her. “Um…sorry, but did you just…squeal?” It was the first time I’d heard anything remotely squeal-ish from her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” she squealed again, somewhat apologetic this time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? What is it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I…have wanted…to go…to the Triolune…for-ev-er!” she said, mini-clapping her hands in excitement. “I was only twelve the last time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh my heck! Why? What IS this tournament? Don’t tell me you’re dying to play Callowynder, too,” I said, “because I won’t believe you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No way, not Callowynder,” Zuli guffawed. “The Triolune is in Rasili this time. It’s only the fashion mecca of Cobbogoth!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Like New York or Paris,” Lylend translated for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh. Yeah. That makes more sense.” One thing I’d learned about Zuli really quickly is that she loves—and I mean LOVES—fashion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I have dreamt of visiting the troll quarries since I was a little girl. Mother introduced me to Queen Jayda once too—DonTon’s wife, you know—and she was wearing one of the trolls’ famous weaving stones. By the Gihara! It was so gorgeous.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m guessing these weaving stones are the equivalent of Gucci or Dior?” I clarified with Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Save me from these konkles!” Pieter growled, standing up. “I have better things to do than listen to this giddy blather. When do we leave?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tomorrow,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter nodded and left the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I said, “so we’re going to Rasili.” I was starting to feel a little excited myself. But then I stopped. “Wait. Lev’s going to play Callowynder. Zuli’s going shopping. How is this going to help us get a Lemorian key? Or find James?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m glad you asked, kyndie,” Lylend said. “When Lev wins the Callowynder stone, you see, he’ll be able to request the metal key from Rasili’s kinchief DonTon as his prize. As for Zuli’s shopping, I’m sure it will serve some vital purpose by and by.” He winked at Zu, and she grinned. “As for Jamus, anybody who is anybody will be at this tournament, kyndie. It’s the most likely place to find him…at least while the tournament lasts.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How long is that?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“As long as the Triolune eclipse lasts,” Iolyt explained. “The entire dance of the three moons is roughly 30 days.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay!” I said, finally satisfied. “It’s a win all around! Why leave tomorrow? What are we waiting for?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Turns out we were waiting for Brawlo to show up. He arrived on Feg the next morning, and after everyone else rowed to shore—Zuli and I hitched a ride on Brawlo’s back—we were on the road to Rasili by mid-day.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"On foot, unfortunately. Despite Iolyt’s near constant time spent in her qualdriny lab the last three months, she still hadn’t been able to crack the code behind Cifer’s spyolite stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Listen reader, I know it sounds super whiny, me complaining about having to walk or travel in some other “normal” way to get places, but you’d understand if you’d ever traveled via hoption hole. It’s amazing how much time I wasted on public transportation back in Boston.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that wasn’t even the worst part of our trip to Rasili. Due to Lylend’s “over abundance of caution” in case we met any dogrils along the way—an abundance of caution that Iolyt seconded due to my arm being almost completely severed the last time we ran into them—I got to spend 90 percent of the journey cooped up in my rodãor while Brawlo carried me in a little pouch around his neck.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not. Cool. I missed traveling through the Unda Swamp for the first time—the northern part. I missed the Roklin Marshlands, which are supposed to look like you’ve stepped from a world of color into some otherworldly black and white fantasy movie. I even missed most of Magglyn Wood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Needless to say, by day seven of our trip we were all dirty, exhausted, sick of Shudder’s cooking and moaning, and ready to be in Rasili. Not even Iolyt’s assuring us she was getting closer to formulating an antidote stone for spyolite could cheer us up, although it was fascinating listening to all the other never-before-in-existence stones she was creating as a byproduct of her efforts. Apparently Cifer had used some really useful foreign elements when he created spyolite. The question worrying Iolyt most was how did Cifer acquire these foreign elements? Most of them were either extremely rare or just couldn’t be found in Cobbogoth’s realm. Period.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally made it over the Rocklin Marshlands and through the relentless ancient tangle of vines that is Magglyn Wood, Lylend finally agreed to let me out of my rodãor so I could see the first sight of Rasili shining in the distance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was unlike any city I’d ever seen. Carved into the side of an active volcano, Rasili spiraled up, up, up like a golden wedding cake topped with the most opulent domed palace that made St. Petersburg’s palaces look like children’s sandcastles.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Suddenly all the fuss over coming to the Triolune Festival seemed like an underreaction. Neither the Superbowl nor the Olympics compared to this.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Legend of the Triolune","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"8b1a5381bd5c41e2b4e96f8776abb9f0"},{"title":"Rasili & Donton's Palace","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"20e316e3aa34442b8b8fead8e69cbf63"},{"title":"Norah Lukens","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"6235d181bef84217ac435fc713fcf1e6"}]},{"n":50,"letterTitle":"The Way of Royals","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"The Way of Royals","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So you know I love my kyndie, right? Zu, she’s the best. There’s no one like her in all of Cobbogoth. She’s spunky and smart and tiny, and once you get past her initial distrust of pretty much everyone, she’s the sweetest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What I don’t love are her kind. You know…royals. To be honest, I haven’t had a ton of experience with them, but the ones I have met besides Zu, like her mom, are not my favorite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Yeah, not my favorite at all. Zu’s mom’s never liked me. Let’s just get that out of the way right now. I’m not good enough and all that…yada yada…I’m sure you’ve heard it before. I’m not broken up about it or anything, though. Heck, if I was going to care what people thought of me, I wouldn’t last two minutes as Pieter’s kynd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What does bother me, though…what really gets under my skin…is how Zu’s mom treats her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Yeah, I don’t like that at all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now, I haven’t seen Queen Citrine for a good three years or so, not since back before Zu was banished. And I’ll tell you, I could’ve gone three times that long without seeing her again. But I’d be naïve if I didn’t at least suspect we might run into her in Rasili. I mean, Lylend wasn’t kidding when he told Nori that anyone who’s anyone would be at the Triolune. Every 20 years isn’t nearly as long in a Cobbogothian lifetime as it probably is in yours, but this just isn’t something you miss.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So anyway, Zu and I are down at the Callowynder arena getting me all signed up for DonTon’s tournament, right? We had to go to a separate booth since I’m entering the multi-wyn tournament. Well, while Zu’s doing her thing—“schmoozing” is what Nori calls it—we find out that DonTon only made this second tournament happen because his son is a triowyn, and he wants to showcase him a bit to attract future queens and stuff. Poor princie-kynd couldn’t find his Kinzura on his own, I guess. Wonder what’s wrong with him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not that I’m complaining or anything. Believe me! I mean, I get to compete in the greatest Callowynder tournament of all time because of it, but come on. How’s DonTon’s kynd Sydian going to survive in the real world if papa’s always paving his way in gold? And that’s not just a metaphorical question, by the way. The streets in Rasili are literally paved in gold. I told Nori that before we reached the city, and she didn’t believe me, so I thought I’d make it a point to tell you. That way there’s no confusion about who won that argument.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right, so gold streets. Anyway, we’re walking toward the Wyn registration area on one of these gold streets. All the other kin-klans have their candidates there—I’m representing the Hood as a separate organization, which is allowed in the multi-wyn tournament, I guess—so we arranged to meet Lylend and Nori there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After we get my stuff taken care of, we run into Mala and her brother jet. You remember Mala, right? She’s Melusian’s daughter—the Rayve kyndie that saved nori’s life when she let all of us out of nori’s rodãor to find her arm practically torn off? Yeah, well, her brother jet is the guy who picked a fight with me when I broke Melusian’s barracks, so let’s just say things were a bit tense to begin with. But thanks to Zu’s and Mala’s blossoming friendship, we were handling it. First Zu offered to help Mala keep her Lamianza at Bay while she competes. She’s representing the Adolorians in the regular tournament, I guess. Thought it would be jet, but you never can tell with water wielders, them being notoriously unpredictable and such.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, after Zuli’s offer, jet stopped staring me down with his sharky eyes like he wanted to take my head off or something. I wouldn’t have let him, of course, but despite what happened in Melusian’s barracks, I respected this guy. He really held his own down there, and I actually side with Zuli on the state of the Adolorians; I think a unification of their kin-klan is the best thing for them. So, needless to say, I didn’t want to mess anything up there either. At least no more than I already had.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, we’re talking to Mala, and I’m avoiding eye contact with jet…because he looks weird…when who comes around the corner in all her royal glory but Zu’s mother. Yep, her highness Queen Citrine. The moment she sees my kyndie talking to Mala—“the enemy”—she comes straight for us, and she just starts laying into Zu like she’s done something more along the lines of blowing up a city. Not that I’m pointing fingers, but the only person I know who’s been zyrky enough to blow up a city—even if it was just a ruse—is the royal highness herself. So…yeah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Queen Citrine is accusing my Zu of being a traitor…again…but then she starts insisting Zu enter the regular tournament before Mala could in order to stop “her kind” from representing the Adolorians in the Triolune tournament. Now if Zu wanted to enter, fine. It’s her right as the princess to take that honor, but Zu didn’t want to compete. First off, she doesn’t like playing Callowynder like I do. She’d rather just watch me. And second, she’s worked hard to build the fragile relationship that exists between her and Mala. Like I said, the two of them are trying to create some kind of unity for the future of their people. Zu wasn’t about to jeopardize that just to appease her mother’s spiteful whim.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By this point, Mala and jet had taken off to find the regular Callowynder arena in the lower level of the city. That’s when Queen Citrine really started in on Zu. It got to the point where she was actually threatening my kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well the least you can do is use the distraction of the festival to try and steal back what’s rightfully ours,” the queen said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And what’s that, mother?” Zu asked, a bite to her words.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The trident, of course.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu let out a guffaw. “If I was going to steal the trident, I wouldn’t give it to you,” she said. I could tell she was about to lose it by the tick in her jaw.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And why is that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because I would give it to the Hood where it will actually do some good for all of our people and not just one group or another.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Citrine took my kyndie by the arm then, and on instinct I stepped in. Of course, the queen’s guards were on me the moment I budged. But I had to do something, even if Zu, not to mention the rest of the Hood, would kill me if I got myself thrown in jail or disqualified from competing in the tournament.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully Zu has more patience for her kind than I do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If you’re not careful, daughter, I will have you hunted down and tried for treason,” Queen Citrine hissed at Zu. “As it is, the only thing protecting you is the fact that you’re my daughter.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Forgive me, mother,” Zu said politely, “but last I was told, you didn’t have a daughter.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With that, and as much dignity as I’ve ever seen in her, Zu jerked her arm away from the queen and proceeded to stare down the two blowfish holding me back. I didn’t blame ’em for letting me go on the spot. I’ve seen that look in Zu’s eyes before, and what follows isn’t pretty.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Citrine opened her mouth—she was actually going to keep pushing this thing—when who should come around the corner but Kinchief DonTon of Rasili himself. He’s a lot bigger in person, by the way. He was followed by his wife, Queen Jayda, and his two kyndlin, Sydian and Sylva.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Citrine, my dear! Here you are!” DonTon opened his trunk-sized arms in greeting.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kinchief DonTon, how nice to see you again.” The queen had her royal mask back in place as if she hadn’t just threatened to hunt down her own daughter!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"See what I mean?! These royals!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori and Lylend joined us after that, and it appeared we might avoid a royal conflict after all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s going on?” Nori whispered beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tell you later,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh wow! Is that…?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Zu’s mom? Yeah.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“YOWZA!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yowza?” I turned to look at her. “What does that—wait…something’s different about you.” I almost didn’t recognize her. “What happened to your face?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Gee. Nice to see you too, Lev. Why? You like it?” Nori crossed her eyes at me, giving me a zyrky face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently she’d spent too much time cooped up in her rodãor by herself on the way here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Careful, your eyes’ll get stuck like that, and James won’t recognize you when you see him again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hey!” She playfully punched my arm. “Mean.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, okay, I’m sorry.” I cleared my throat and looked at her again. “You look…nice.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“THANK YOU.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“One of Iolyt’s new stones?” I asked. Lately Iolyt had been recruiting Hood members to try out various new stones she was creating with the elements she’d discovered in Cifer’s spyolite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah. It’s called shimmerite.” Nori said the last word in a mock-dreamy sort of voice while batting her unusually thicker or just darker eyelashes at me. “She wanted to test them out, you know, see how they work. I volunteered, with Lylend’s blessing, of course. Anything to keep him from sending me back to my room—er rodãor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He’s just worried Cifer might turn up at the festival and recognize you,” I explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know,” she sighed. “But it’s super lame having to hide like this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, well, as one of your best friends, I gotta tell you the truth. That shimmerite isn’t enough of a disguise. You still look like you, just…enhanced,” I said. “It makes your face kinda…well, it looks like…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Is it bad?” She asked. I could see her starting to worry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No-no. You just kinda shimmer a little more, I guess.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Shimmer, huh? Well at least Iolyt chose a good name, right? Do I look weird though?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nah, you look…pretty,” I shrugged. “Not my type, of course, but pretty. Anyway, whatever.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori laughed. “Thanks, Lev.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A second later she seized my arm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Gihara! What?” I gasped. Queen Citrine was no longer bullying my kyndie, but her presence had put me on edge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yowza!” Nori said, her voice dropping instantly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You keep saying that word. What does it mean?” I looked at her, and her mouth was open and her eyes were sort of glazed over. Before I could see what she was looking at, she reached across me and grabbed Zuli’s arm. “Who is that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the first time since her mom had arrived, Zu broke into a smile. It was a small, knowing one, but it was a start. It usually took me hours to get her to smile again after an encounter with her mother.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s Prince Sydian,” Zu whispered. There was something in her voice I didn’t like. “He’s not even wearing a vanity collar.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Vanity collar?” Nori asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah. See those?” Zu discreetly pointed at all the Rasilians running the registration process for the tournament. “See the collars the kynds are wearing and the breastplates on the kyndies?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the first time I’d noticed them—not that I went around noticing what people were wearing much. That was Zu’s thing, but she was right. Each of them wore either a gold, silver, or copper bejeweled breastplate or collar. Even some of the other non-rasilian contenders and their entourages had them on.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“See all those stones decorating them?” Zu continued. “They’re called vanity stones. The trolls make them in their quarries. They work like the weaving stones, only instead of changing the way a shyntara looks, they alter the way a Cobbogothian looks.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Like their faces and stuff?” Nori asked, her eyes widening.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu nodded. “Nose size, eye color, jaw shape, age, you name it. You wouldn’t recognize any of these people without their vanity stones,” Zuli said with a knowing look. “It goes clear back to Rasil the beautiful’s time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori and I both glanced around us with new comprehension, and again, it was the first time I’d noticed it, but Zu was right. Compared to the rest of us, every person wearing one of those vanity things was ridiculously better looking than those of us without them. Almost too good looking. Like they were all trolls or something. I’d always heard the Rasilians were obsessed with their looks, but this was next level stuff.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Huh. What do ya know,” Nori laughed. “Cobbogothian plastic surgery.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” Zu asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s just something from the Olden realm,” Nori explained. “Humans get their healers to perform actual surgery on them in order to change the way they look.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Permanently?” Zu and I asked in unison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, and a lot of times it doesn’t go so well.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew Zu’s horrified expression only mirrored my own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Anyway,” Zu said, shaking off this disturbing revelation, “see how Prince Sydian isn’t wearing any vanity stones? He’s just naturally that gorgeous. And he should be. His mother was the seven times great-granddaughter of Rasil the beautiful.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Was?” Nori asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, she died a little over twenty years ago—before the last Triolune festival.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori shook herself a little, reluctantly looking away from princie-kynd. “Oh…we…I mean, he’s not my type, of course, but he is gorgeous.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu giggled. Really. It’s the only way to describe the sound that came out of her. Since it wasn’t directed at me, my hackles were up. I mean, I’m not one of those jealous, possessive Kinzura, but now that I was looking the princie-kynd over, all these questions started rushing through my mind. For instance…was this part of the reason Zu was so excited to come to Rasili?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Argh! Why was I so angry all of a sudden? By the Gihara! I felt like my dad right then.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We courted a bit back in my 20s,” I heard Zu explain, “before I met Lev.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait, what?” I said, “you courted?” That was it. I didn’t like this guy!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It was my mother’s idea,” Zu whispered. “He was sweet, but, like you Nori, not my type. I prefer the rugged, athletic type.” She gave me an appreciative once-over and a wink that set me at ease…sort of.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then princie-kynd over there started heading our way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Zuli! It’s so good to see you again,” he called. When he reached us, he took my kyndie in his arms, and I swear, once again, if Nori hadn’t put her hand on my arm to stop me, I might’ve done something to jeopardize my spot in the tournament. When he kissed Zu’s cheek, I wished I had.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori’s nails dug further into my arm when Zu kissed his cheek in return. What was going on here?!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Calm down, Lev,” Nori whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How long has it been?” The slimy troll asked. Then he dropped his voice, and I had to lean in a bit.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry I didn’t get here sooner,” he said, glancing over where DonTon and Zu’s mother were “schmoozing.” “The moment I saw her take hold of your arm, I started steering my father this way. Are you all right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Fine,” Zu said, beaming at him. “Thank you for coming when you did.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hasn’t gotten any better with your mother, then?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nope.” Zuli let out her breath. “But what about you? You’ve gotten taller, I think, and Sylva is a beauty. No surprise there.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She looks just like mother,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced back at Queen Jayda. Even without knowing princie-kynd’s mother had passed, it was obvious the queen was a stepmother. She looked nothing like the royal siblings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You must miss her,” Zu said, “especially during the festival.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I do. I do. Very much.” Prince Sydian kissed Zu on the other cheek, and before I could say anything, he released her and turned to the rest of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now,” he said, “please introduce me to your friends. I, of course, know who this is.” He offered me his hand, and I grasped it…pretty hard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The legendary Levian of gliptrainia—Lev the verowyn—that’s a strong grip you’ve got there. To be expected, of course, if the rumors are true.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…” was he mocking me?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ve heard tales of you for some time from the goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Goldabungs?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The earth kneader kyndlin who come to gather the gems we find in our quarries,” he explained. “I’m not even disappointed you’ll probably throttle me in the first level of the tournament. It’s just an honor to compete alongside you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you?” I said, still unsure if he was mocking me, but I did loosen my grip a little.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu slipped her hand in mine as princie-kynd moved on to greet Lylend. “See?” She whispered, “nothing to worry about.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I kept my eye on the prince, just in case, until he reached Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that, human, is when all the anger, and yeah, I’ll admit it, jealousy surging through my veins bled right out of me. Why? Because from the moment he laid eyes on her, I knew Zuli was right. I didn’t have anything to worry about.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The poor princie-kynd had just fallen for Nori. And by the Gihara, the kynd had fallen hard.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Character Legend","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"aeea9026d12143348071d7eff6b9d8a1"},{"title":"Journal: Triolune Festival & Vanity Plates","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e6295616982e41829e45963b79ced948"},{"title":"Shimmerite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9f9e2353453a4847b2b9368f85819c63"},{"title":"Triolune Tournament","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"1f43ebe6215b4bb08c072e6a1064c89a"},{"title":"Lev's Character Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"99056d0310b944e6875db8c7275f0909"}]},{"n":51,"letterTitle":"Background Checks and Beauty Pageants","narrator":"Lylend","pieces":[{"title":"Background Checks and Beauty Pageants","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Being invisible is incredibly liberating, human, I must say. You may think a little spying done while invisible in a situation such as the Triolune tournament is cheating, but believe me, every other contender will be doing something similar to what Brawlo and I have been doing. Unfortunately, these other contenders don’t have the greatest qualdrinist in all of Cobbogoth on their team.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Little Lyttie and her marvelous brain! Gihara bless her for it! She’s been creating all sorts of useful stones since I gave her Cifer’s spyolite. Luckily, she needs volunteers to try them out before deciding what to do with them. Thanks to her latest stone—an invisibility stone she’s calling huldecite—Brawlo and I spent the morning gleaning invaluable information from the lower, common streets of Rasili. We were able to eavesdrop on all five other contenders and their entourages with no one the wiser. The only contender we weren’t able to track down is a self-proclaimed quintowyn—an undeniable fabrication, I’m sure. But since no one has seen hide Nor hair of this “Seraphyna,” she is probably just as mythical as her proclaimed array of manifestations.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Prince Sydian, a verifiable triowyn, is the only real competition Lev faces in the Wyn tournament. As the only verowyn ever heard of in Cobbogoth, he’ll sail through to claim the Callowynder stone with little trouble.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, I was feeling more and more confident in this plan of mine the closer I got to the Hood encampment—a little clearing in the woods just outside of Rasili. The only thing left to do was discover what the metal key was so Lev knew what to request as his prize.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you think, Brawlo?” I said aloud, not that it was necessary. The beast knew my every thought when I was riding him like this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rasil the beautiful was a close friend of my lady. Even she can’t remember.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As it happened, Rasil was a close friend of mine as well—one I spent a significant amount of time courting before Alo came along. Of course, what man didn’t? She was the most beautiful woman anyone had ever seen. Period.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And I’m not being unfaithful to alo’s memory by thinking it, my friend,” I explained to Brawlo, who had become very acquainted with memories of my Kinzura during our time together these past weeks. “Everyone knew it, you see. But she wasn’t vain about her looks. And she didn’t need one of these vanity plates either. She was breathtaking on her own merit.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*AND KIND.* Brawlo added, a tinge of melancholy to his thoughts.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, my friend,” I said with a consoling pat. “She was very kind.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We rode in silence from then on. I was still racking my considerable memory for what possession rasi valued more than anything as we trotted into camp.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I immediately felt myself tense, and Brawlo came to an abrupt halt the moment we arrived. I knew he sensed my uneasiness. I couldn’t see anyone but knew from centuries of covert warfare that we were not alone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cautiously dismounting the weasel’s back, I removed lyttie’s huldecite from a silver cuff on my arm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Show yourself,” I demanded. I’d expected the others to be back from the lower market by now, but they knew better than to hide from me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo hissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is a friendly warning,” I called again. “My four-legged friend is not to be trifled with.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a scuffling noise to my right, and suddenly a young kynd about nine or ten years old popped his head out from behind a crystal cluster.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo trilled his delight and charged. *FRIEND!* I heard as he brushed passed me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kynd drew back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa, Brawlo!” I called. “Wait ’til the kynd knows you’re friendly, eh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo plopped down on his haunches and cocked his head, watching our guest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kynd stepped out from the crystals, and the moment I saw his uniform, my interest grew. He was dressed in a silver shyntara with the gold and black embroidery of DonTon’s insignia on his chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re from the palace?” I asked, already wondering how I could make this work in our favor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kynd shuffled from foot to foot, but he didn’t speak, only stared.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you DonTon’s page, kynd?” I asked a little louder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Still, he just stared at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you have a name?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"NOTHING.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo trilled beside me, then pushed his forehead against my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"TOO NERVOUS. CAN’T SPEAK. NEVER SEE NARIDI BEFORE.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Eye of Gihara!” I mumbled. “Spare me from my celebrity.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo trilled again. It sounded like a laugh.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"BRAWLO HELP.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you, my friend.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The giant white weasel lowered his head, slinking closer to the kynd. Soon he was sidled up behind him, pressing his forehead into the kynd’s back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“F-forgive me, your ancientness,” the kynd finally croaked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Call me by my name, kynd,” I sighed. “I don’t need reminding of my age.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Forgive me then, Lylend the Naridi.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just Lylend will do.” I busied myself opening my rodãor. It was the worst when they got like this. One of the few great things about being among you humans—just shy of a grilled ham and cheese sandwich {what I wouldn’t give!}—Was that your people left me alone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“F-forgive me, Lylend…my n-name is Tolliver. I come on behalf of p-Prince Sydian.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned back to see him holding out an official looking crystal scroll. There was DonTon’s insignia again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It is an invitation for Princess Zuli and…the other kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took it from him. “I’ll see that they receive it. How shall we respond if a reply is required?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kynd finally smiled at me. “Send for darlyck in the lower palace smithies. He’s my cousin. He’ll get it to me, and I’ll return it to the prince.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Very well.” I turned back to my rodãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo trilled again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced over my shoulder at him. The kynd still stood there, staring at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right,” I sighed. “Out with it, kynd.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His eyes brightened. “Is it true you recently blew up Melusian’s barracks with one of your lightning shows?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, kynd,” I said. “That was Levian of gliptrainia’s doing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The verowyn competing in the Wyn tournament?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I NODDED.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But you were there, weren’t you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I WAS.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is it true you fought Melusian and all his Rayves with one arm while wrestling a krackyn with the other?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That one deserved a chuckle. “No, kynd.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is it true you—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Last question?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded “—you brought back our final hope from the Olden realm?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This question took me by surprise. “Who told you that?” I asked, keeping the alarm out of my voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’ve been hearing Tale of it for the past six months. I promised darlyck and Oriette I’d ask.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“DID YOU NOW?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He grinned, then leaned forward a little. “We’ve also heard Barakyne was spotted twice last month.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could think, I had the kynd by the shoulders. I loosened my grip when alarm tinged his eyes. “Forgive me, kynd? But where? Where was he seen?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“H-he was spotted near t-trolly row last month and…and again while flying over rasi falls earlier this week.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo chirped in agreement.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Realizing I still had hold of the kynd, I patted his shoulders but didn’t release him. “What would you say to making a deal with me?” I asked, lowering my voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What kind of a deal?” Tolliver replied, squinting up at me with interest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How about from now on, you come and deliver word to me here if Barakyne is ever spotted again. In return, I will tell you and this cousin darlyck of yours one of my finest, never before heard, real-life adventure tales?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kynd’s face broke into a wide grin. “Can I bring Oriette too?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who’s Oriette?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She works at the palace—helps with the kyndie stuff.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By all means,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tolliver bowed in the traditional Rasilian way of agreement. “You have yourself a deal!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right then,” I said, bowing in return. “But remember, the next time you hear mention of Barakyne, you come find me here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Consider it done, your ancient—er—Lylend.” Tolliver excused himself then, and I watched him trot off through the trees in the direction of DonTon’s palace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I spun toward Brawlo. “Barakyne! Can you believe it? By the Gihara!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo chirped again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was so deep in thought over the implications of a Barakyne sighting, I didn’t notice the sound of voices approaching until Brawlo bounded off in their direction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hello, boy!” I heard Noriah call. “Oh, you’re such a good, sweet, handsome boy! Yes you are, Brawlo! Yes…you…are!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to see Zuli, Lev, and Iolyt enter the clearing. They were loaded down with parcels from their shopping. I spotted Noriah a few yards back where she’d dropped to her knees, scratching Brawlo’s giant exposed belly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Such a good, sweet, handsome boy!” She continued.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kyndie spoiled the beast to an unhealthy degree. I regularly worried if she’d domesticate him completely and “love” the dangerousness right out of him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As if in response to this thought, Brawlo trilled gleefully as the kyndie continued to coo at him, then he pressed his forehead to hers.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re a terrible lookout, Brawlo,” Lev laughed. “A few flattering words and a belly rub and you’re useless to us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo hissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He just missed me,” Noriah scolded as she led Brawlo back into the clearing. “Didn’t you, boy? Of course you did! And I missed you! That’s right! That’s right!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who was the kynd?” Lyttie asked, approaching me. “And how did the huldecite work?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help smiling at her. “It’s genius, Lyttie, true genius.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?!” She wrung her hands excitedly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “Brawlo didn’t even need to wear a piece of his own. He was invisible the moment I got on his back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who knew Cifer’s boobytrap would end up being so useful, huh?” Lev said, taking a bite out of a spicy ellaprava {Rasili’s traditional dessert; you humans might mistake it for a hand pie}.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The kynd was a messenger from the palace. Which reminds me, he brought this.” I held the crystal scroll out to the princess.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?” Zuli asked, taking it from me. “It’s for both of us, Nori.” A slow smile spread over her lips as she twisted it apart and examined its contents. “It’s from Prince Sydian.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s princie-kynd want?” Lev asked, suddenly very interested in rearranging the pile of parcels near a boulder he sat on.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh Lev, we’ve talked about this,” Zuli said, handing the scroll to Noriah and going to him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched discreetly as the kyndie opened the scroll. Any connection to the palace could be useful to us for multiple reasons, not the least of which was figuring out what Rasil’s metal key was. Since I had no previous acquaintance with Kinchief DonTon, this invitation was likely the only window of opportunity we might have to identify it before the tournament.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, Nori?” Zuli asked. She’d thrown herself onto Lev’s lap. She looked like a doll in his arms. I found myself glancing around for Pieter on their behalf. He hated their “pda,” as you humans say.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Come to think of it, I hadn’t seen Pieter all morning, and he’d turned in early the night before.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s an invitation from Prince Sydian,” Noriah said. “He’s inviting us to something called a soireenya, hosted by Queen Jayda.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was better than I hoped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Judging by the root word of that, I’m assuming it’s like a royal high tea or something?” The kyndie looked to me for confirmation.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I NODDED.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And then there’s this,” she held up a poster that had also been in the scroll.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The tri-o-lune tab-lon-ia,” Lev read slowly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli screamed, and Lev shot to his feet. “What? What is it?” He had her stowed safely behind him in an instant, shielding her with his body. He’d been on edge since running into Queen Citrine the other day.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry!” The princess panted. “There’s no need for alarm.” She moved around him and crossed the clearing to Noriah. She took hold of the poster. “I just, after everything with my mother—being banished—I just never thought this day would actually come.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What day?” Noriah asked, peeking over her shoulder. “You are acting so weird lately.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara, Zuli!” Lyttie chuckled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli continued to devour the poster. “I know! I’m just—I’m so—I mean, it’s the Triolune Tableunia, Iolyt.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know, libkyn,” Lyttie said in a soothing voice. She was standing beside Lev now. “But you’re going to send poor Lev to an early tomb if you don’t stop this uncharacteristic squealing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli looked abashed and turned to Lev. He was sweating. “Sorry, levy. It’s just…I’ve dreamed about being part of the Tableunia for…well…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“As long as I’ve dreamed of being part of the Triolune tournament?” Lev sighed, settling himself back on the boulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help chuckling a bit. I could already see the hand of the Gihara in this new development.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ve got to do it, Nori!” Zuli said, clasping her hands in front of her. “Please, for me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Me?” Noriah looked confused.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Perhaps you should tell her what she’d be agreeing to first,” Lyttie nudged. I had a strong suspicion how the kyndie would respond to this. You humans have an equivalent to the Tableunia, I believe. It’s not favorably looked upon by kyndies like Noriah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, okay,” Zuli began, failing to suppress the glee in her voice. “The Triolune Tableunia is this amazing opportunity for kyndies of all walks of life to experience what it’s like to be a true princess—like me. But it’s like a competition as well, you see. The Tableunia even has rounds like Callowynder. There’s the diplomacy and debate round, the poise and polish round, the talent and technique round, the forte and fitness round, and the enchantment and elegance round.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The more Zuli explained, the more horrified the kyndie looked. When the princess finally finished, Noriah was shaking her head vehemently. “No. No way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Why?” Zuli asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Um…because what you just described to me is a beauty pageant, Zu!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh! But it’s so much more, too! Please, Nori! You have to do it. It’s my lifelong wish—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You be in it then!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli looked bewildered. “I don’t want to be in it. I’m already a princess. I want to coach the kyndie who wins it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well…I’m definitely not interested. Besides, look at me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course you are! Lev, tell her! You’re beautiful and in the most unique way…which actually gives you an edge over everyone else.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are you talking about?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re a Lune-kyndie, Nori! Imagine the triumph—the novice—of having a Lune-kyndie be my first Tableunia winner?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, Zu. Just…no! Besides, I thought I was supposed to be laying low in case Cifer shows up?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I could help with that,” Lyttie chimed in, her eyes twinkling. “I actually need someone to try out several new stones I’ve created, and this would be the perfect opportunity.” She winked at me, and I knew she saw the potential of this opportunity as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes!” Zuli gushed. “Iolyt was telling me all about them on the way back from shopping.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m thinking of calling them beauty gems,” Lyttie said, a deep flush touching her coffee-colored cheeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I love it!” Zuli clapped her hands. “You could even start selling them while we’re here. When Nori wins, everyone in Cobbogoth will be clamoring for them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Noriah turned to me in exasperation. “You can’t possibly think this is a good idea?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I cleared my throat, forcing the laughter down. “Looking at it in the right light, kyndie, I actually do. This could be the perfect opportunity to discover what the metal key is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh. My. Heck,” Noriah said, plopping down on a boulder beside Brawlo. “You’ve already decided. I’m going to get stuck doing a beauty pageant!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shrugged. “What’s the point of Lev winning if he doesn’t know what to request as his prize? If you and the princess do this, you’ll have unlimited access to the palace, which is the perfect opportunity to discover what we’re looking for. Furthermore, you’ll be surrounded by DonTon’s guard, and that’s the best possible protection I can offer you here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched as the kyndie mulled things over a bit. “So, doing this beauty pageant would be like an undercover sort of thing?” She asked. “Like a spy mission?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Precisely,” I said. “You’d be our ‘eyes on the inside.’”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But it would be a really fun and glamourous spy mission, right?” Zuli clarified. “And we’d still get to win?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course, princess,” I said. “And even more than that, I’ll pay for everything you need to give Noriah her best shot.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who said anything about winning?” The kyndie gasped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But the princess had already taken the bait. “You’ll pay for everything?” She asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Everything.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli glanced pleadingly at Noriah.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kyndie guffawed then. She looked around at all of us in exasperated panic. Even Brawlo spurred her on with a chirp.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I did feel a pang of guilt for playing my part in her distress. I’m old, human, not heartless.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At last, she surrendered, throwing her hands up in the air. “Okay, I’ll do my part, but I’m warning you, I have no idea how I do in front of large groups of people. I was homeschooled, remember?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli smiled triumphantly and ran to her. “You’ll be amazing!” Zuli began crying and hugged Noriah tightly. “I’ll be your coach, remember?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you seriously crying?” Noriah asked in bewilderment.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can’t help it. I’m just—*sniff*—so happy!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara!” Lev laughed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the Gihara, indeed. Two sightings of Barakyne only confirmed their hand was in all of this.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Lylend Character Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"06c909471f8248c6b0ecef2bde979456"},{"title":"Lylend's Contender Notes","kind":"webp","pages":4,"id":"b5662a3290334c55ab59616dcfcc85cb"},{"title":"Ellaprava Recipe","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"17d80f876cf84726b7806648c6f8646b"},{"title":"Soireenya Invite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4b6151f1f2cf48569b0781ce0dfa7fd9"},{"title":"Tableunia Rules","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0d704a608510417590ef0ef0ab96f548"},{"title":"Huldecite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"33c4af9f5b35408b9260fe6a9f57b884"},{"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The Tournament is a trap. Enter with caution.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":52,"letterTitle":"In the Forest of Dag","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"James Riley Character Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"7224faf20a824b0a8574e5ea104cf1f9"},{"title":"In the Forest of Dag","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Soooooo, I’m just going to go ahead and say it. Being lost isn’t the most fun thing in the world. I feel like I’ve been lost forever, you know. But I know I’ve been lost for at least the past three months. Turns out nobody around here wants to even talk to a flame thrower, let alone give him directions anywhere.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All in all, not a great start to my Cobbogoth adventure.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At least it wasn’t until a couple of days ago when my old buddy Glynt showed up. That’s when I actually started getting somewhere. Before he found me, I was just wandering. And apparently, I was wandering in circles because I kept ending up right back where I started.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"According to Glynt, we’re in a place called the Forest of Dag, which I’m told is a pretty sacred place to Cobbogothians. To me it feels mostly like an abandoned place, but every once in a while I sense what he means. The only living things I’ve come across in here that weren’t animals—not entirely anyway—are the dryads. Nice folks and all—at least they don’t give me the evil eye—but not much for talking, you know.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I was camping out near a pile of rubble—wood weaver ruins, I’m told—when my man Glynt shows up and is like, “Hey pal, you lost?” And I’m like, “Brother, you have no idea.” So we’ve been traveling north for the past two days. The thing with this forest is it’s incredibly dense, which means we haven’t gotten very far. I mean, you can’t even see the sunlight coming through the treetops way, way, and I mean way above us. It’s also really hard to breathe in here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But we’re cracking on. And it’s so much better with someone to talk to. I mean, Glynt’s no talker, but he’s a real good listener. And I’ll tell you, I’ve definitely needed someone to talk to over the past while—had a lot on my mind, or actually missing from my mind, needing to be sorted out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I’ll cover that another time. First, I better tell you what we found today.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first thing we did after he found me was locate some water. According to Glynt, the River Dag runs straight up through the forest, and if you follow it north, it’ll eventually lead you out of this place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, we’re making our way north along this river, right, and we come to this spot where the River Dag almost touches the shores of the Glindian Sea. I was so happy to finally be able to see the sky again, I almost ran out onto the beach and jumped into the water. Thankfully Glynt stopped me before I could, explaining that water and fire don’t mix too well here. According to him, the sea would’ve tried to kill me the moment I dipped my big toe in. When I told him I hadn’t had any trouble bathing, he said there must not have been enough water, which is true because I’d only had access to brooks and springs before he came along and led me to the River Dag.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, we’d just started following the river again when we could hear something happening not too far up the bank. Glynt gestured for me to keep quiet, and we carefully began weaving our way through the thick vines, keeping ourselves safely hidden in the trees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were finally close enough, I heard the dingbats before I saw them. You don’t forget that sound, no matter what you’ve been through. We kept moving along until, sure enough, we could just make out several dogrils screeching and belching flames in the distance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt gestured for me to get down on the ground. We both slowly and quietly started to army crawl toward them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally reached the source of the noise, we hid behind this giant crystal pillar covered in vines and looked out onto a spot that had obviously been recently cleared of several old trees. In fact, just then seven or eight dogrils were clawing away at one of the trunks, shaving off ribbons of the grey-green bark. A few others were using those ribbons to weave what looked like large, crate-sized baskets shaped like jars with lids. The last of the dogrils were arranging the baskets into stacks three high onto a giant net that was woven from tree vines.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you hear that?” Glynt whispered beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Listen,” he said, and pointed back toward the clearing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I closed my eyes and concentrated. Sure enough, barely audible above all the racket the dingbats were making came noises that sounded like crying.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Those are grimcages,” Glynt said, gesturing toward the basket cages. “You see that there?” he whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed his gaze to a bright yellow stone woven tightly into the lid of each basket cage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Fanglesite,” he explained. “It’s the stone prison chambers are made with. They’re using it to turn those baskets into grimcages, or transportable prisons.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you think’s in those grimcages?” I asked, a sick feeling rising in my stomach.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt gave me a knowing look, and I knew he had the same suspicion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right then,” I said. “Let’s get this over with.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt started doing his thing, and I, having fueled up on the coals from our campfire this morning, reached deep to gather as much fire as I could from my blood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then it was on like Donkey Kong, whatever that means.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I love baggin’ me some dingbats, I must say. It was a wicked way to blow off some of the steam I’d built up being lost in those woods for weeks on end. Glynt wasn’t too bent outta’ shape over having to root out some dogrils either.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the black mist of what was left of the dogrils drifted away on a slight breeze, we made our way toward the baskets.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let’s run a perimeter check first,” Glynt suggested.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt and I both had qualdrines, so we weren’t in any danger of dogril possession. Even so, we didn’t want any rematerializing on us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were both satisfied the dingbats were all gone, we hurried toward the stacked cages. The crying sounds had stopped, so we approached cautiously. They were so tightly woven you could barely see into them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt carefully lifted one of the top grimcages down to the ground, and I blasted the fanglesite stone off the top of it. We pried off the lid and peered inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Two kyndies were huddled inside, pressing themselves into the back of the grimcage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s okay! It’s okay! Look at my eyes,” I said, reaching out a hand to them. “There’s no yellow here. You’ve got nothing to be afraid of.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced over at Glynt. There was a pained expression on his face. I kept my hand open, outstretched to the kyndies, waiting.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Another cautious moment passed, and finally a little hand, cold and trembling, slid into mine. The little kyndie it belonged to couldn’t have been more than ten years old. Her companion soon followed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The situation was the same in each of the cages—young kids in every single one. Some cages held two or three, depending on the size of the kid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where do you kyndlin come from?” Glynt asked once all the cages were open. The kids were huddled together on the ground around him, and he was handing out the little food we had. They were devouring it like they hadn’t eaten in days.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Rasili,” an older boy said around a mouthful of roogle. He looked about twelve or thirteen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How did you end up with the dogrils?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kyndlin stayed silent, looking at one another, terrified.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s all right,” I insisted. “You’re safe now. We just want to get you home.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, a girl about four or five piped up. “It’s the red-haired lady.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A lady?” I asked gently, crouching down in front of her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded. “A pretty lady.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She told us if we followed her, she would give us food and a place to sleep.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But she led us to a cave instead,” the older boy added. “That’s where the dogrils were waiting.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why did you follow her? Couldn’t you get food and beds from your parents?” Glynt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One of the kyndies shook her head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We don’t have parents,” an older kynd said. “We’re goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked at Glynt for translation. “Orphans?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Orphans with jobs in the quarry and other places in Rasili,” he explained. “They help the trolls extract precious metals from the earth. From the looks of it, some of these goldabungs came over from Gliptrainia to extract gems as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m a glip,” said a young boy proudly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Me too,” said one of the girls. Looking them over—there were a little over two dozen of them total. Glynt explained which ones were “glip” and which were true Rasilians. The glips tended to be bigger, with a stockier build, and fair-haired. The rest were slenderer, with dark, fine features. Rasilians.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is that where you’d like us to take you?” I asked the glip boy? “You want us to drop you off on our way to Rasili?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All the glip children shook their heads decisively.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You wanna go back to the place the dogrils stole you from?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You don’t think getting kidnapped by dogrils would keep us away from the Triolune Festival?” an older, strawberry haired glip girl asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Besides,” said the first glip boy, “we’ve got to warn our friends about the red-haired lady.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked to Glynt again. “You ever heard of this festival?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded. “I don’t believe it’s something you’d want to miss, either. Their kinchief must be warned, anyhow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And there’s food there?” piped up a little boy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, lots of food!” said several all at once.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right then, little pals,” I said, tousling a small dark haired boy’s hair. “Since you’ve eaten all our food, we better go check out this Triolune thingy.”","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Journal: Grimcages & Goldabungs","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2b018e43c2ac436a8ded592a82b76275"},{"title":"Goldabung Rescue & Troll Quarries","kind":"webp","pages":4,"id":"67654f82d9064b548ba17d746fc64607"},{"title":"Fanglecite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2eccc72db3d24c488770e28dc804a177"}]},{"n":53,"letterTitle":"The Gorgeous Gloriole","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"Zuli","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"47b63e59b40845eaa23005dfd93a49b9"},{"title":"The Gorgeous Gloriole (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Even if Nori has no concept of them, I’m sure you have your own fashion philosophies in the Olden realm. From what she’s told me, you don’t look that different from us, so you must have something, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just in case I’m wrong, I’ve included a few simple tips and tricks to get the fashion conscious juices flowing for you humans. I mean, every “intelligent” species has to start their fashion evolution somewhere, right? And trust me, whether you’re competing in a Tableunia like Nori or not, you wouldn’t believe what the right color can do for your complexion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of Nori, do you have any idea how long it took me to get her to stop wearing that hideous green weaving stone Lylend gave her with her shyntara when she first arrived here? Too long, honestly! It made her look like…well, a Lune-kyndie. Whatever the Olden realm’s beauty standards are, that is not a good thing in Cobbogoth, especially not if one is competing in a Tableunia…particularly the Triolune Tableunia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But praise the Gihara, I finally got her to give that ugly thing up. I even tossed it into the Glindian sea to celebrate…and to make sure she could never wear it again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And now, after a week of relentless, grueling hours preparing Nori for the Tableunia, Iolyt and I finally figured out a way to make nori’s peculiar looks work for us rather than against us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I am a fashion genius!” Was all I could say while admiring our handiwork. “I’ve suspected it for some time, Nori, but now I’m certain of it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori laughed. She stopped short, however, once I spun her around to look in the reflection crystal. “Holy moly!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“See what I mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"How could she not?! Creamy pale hair braided and wound into traditional Cobbogothian coils and a new ceremonial shyntara with not just one or even two but three weaving stone settings! That was key. With three casements, I was able to switch weaving stones in and out to create an entirely new and unique signature color for Nori to wear throughout the competition. And that wasn’t even the best part. The three stone casements also enabled me to create a new design for her ceremonial shyntara. I was even able to make it with a new, airy, luminescent, never-before-seen weaving technique of the shyntfish skin. And finally, I designed an exquisite embroidery pattern for it by hand. I’m not usually one to trumpet my own conch shell, but that alone was sure to steal the magistrates’ breaths away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re sure Lylend agreed to pay for this?” Nori asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Every golden terra of it,” I assured her. “Who knew he had such deep pockets?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“WOW.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wow was right, and she didn’t even know how many terras he’d forked over in advance. Not that it would’ve mattered; Nori was still trying to figure out the Cobbogothian currency system. For the record, a terra was a lot.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I never thought of Lylend as being rich,” Nori said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Me neither,” I replied. “For one thing, he always wears the same boring, black shyntara. I’d give my second-best weaving stone just to see him in some cobalt!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hold on! What did you do to my face?” Nori interrupted after taking her eyes off her shyntara long enough to take in the rest of her reflection.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, I can’t really take credit for that,” I said, fingering the stone encrusted cuff on her wrist. “Iolyt and her beauty gems are responsible.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wow,” was all she could say again. Then she swallowed and whispered “I look…I look…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Beautiful,” I finished for her. “Iolyt did have to take things a bit further than I would’ve liked,” I said, securing a braid in her hair with one final pin. “Unsettling as your looks may be to some,” I began delicately, “lune-kyndlin stand out. And since I’m almost certain there won’t be another Lune-kyndie entering the Tableunia, I’ve decided to use it to our advantage. I think if I can keep you fairly close to your original coloring but tone it down a bit like this, you’ll still stand out, but in a good way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but Iolyt definitely did more than ‘tone down my coloring,’” Nori insisted. “I barely recognize myself.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, that’s the point, isn’t it? I simply had her thicken and darken your brows, change the color of your eyes and the shape of your nose slightly, and give you a heart-shaped face instead of a diamond-shaped one. Oh! And it was her idea to change the height and shape of your hairline.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s it, huh?” Nori said wryly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it really was simple. Once nori’s hair and shyntara were absolutely perfect, I had slipped Iolyt’s carefully designed cuff of individualized beauty gems on her wrist. Just like that, she went from strangely-ethereal-but-still-attractive Nori to absolutely-stunning-more-able-to-blend-in (but not too much) Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is that really all you did?” Asked a soft voice from behind us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette, the little hostess who’d been assigned to us upon arrival at DonTon’s palace, sat perched on the window sill. (In an extinct, renovated volcano we were one of the lucky contestants that actually got a window.) Oriette was a small girl of eleven, with the characteristic Rasilian blue-black hair and light eyes. Hers were wide, endearingly innocent, and a deep jade. She was meant to stay close by to help us maneuver the labyrinth that was DonTon’s palace during our stay.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded at her reflection. “It’s really the tiny details that make the biggest difference, lybkin.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette leaned forward excitedly. “It’s much better than the vanity plates and collars they wear around here, isn’t it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, retreating to the window to sit beside her. The view was incredible. “It allows her to look more natural. Less disfigured.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I wish I had a cuff like that,” Oriette sighed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You don’t need one,” I said, tweaking her nose. “You’re already so pretty.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She blushed and smiled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You can have this one,” Nori said, almost pleading.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s too young to compete,” I reminded Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, yeah. All right. Well, let’s get this over with then,” she sighed, making her way toward the door to the corridor outside. “Thank you for making me unrecognizable, Zu,” she said with a pat on my arm as she passed. “At least I’ll be able to show my real face in public once this whole thing goes south.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, Nori! We talked about this!” I said, kneading her shoulders a few times. She continued to walk as if to her doom. “You’ve got to believe you’ll win.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know. I know. ‘Wear the crown. Be the crown. You are the crown.’”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…I never said that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“True,” Nori laughed. “But it’s from this great movie—miss congeniality. I’ve been quoting it to myself since this whole Tableunia nightmare started. It’s the only reason my sanity is still intact.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t think I ever realized how dramatic you are until this moment,” I told her with a grin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori turned to me. “I know. I’m sorry, Zu. You’ve worked so hard, and I appreciate it. I really do. But I’m scared out of my mind right now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gave her hand a reassuring squeeze. “You’re going to do amazing. And remember, I’ll be right beside you every second.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded, and we left our room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As we made our way out into the corridor—or opulently gilded lava tube, really—and toward Queen Jayda’s banquet hall, I continued to analyze my work. It was a bold choice to embrace a simpler sophistication and elegance as nori’s signature look, but I had a gut feeling about how the judges were going to respond. They were always on the lookout for the unique or peculiar. Nori was that on her own. The last thing I wanted was to detract from that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stop fidgeting,” I told her as we reached the banquet hall. We followed Oriette as she found us a place in line with the other, much more elaborately made up kyndies. Each was waiting her turn to be introduced to the judges. “Just remember, we’re only making an impression today. That’s why I chose to go with the softer shade of your signature color. You need to stand out but not dazzle. Not yet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh…I’m gonna stand out, Zu,” Nori said, looking around at the other kyndies nervously. As expected, most of them were the dark-haired beauties of Rasili.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know,” I assured her, “but thanks to me, tonight you stand out in a good way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori snorted. “I’m glad you feel our friendship is strong enough for brutal honesty.” Then she sobered. “But seriously Zu, I don’t want you getting your hopes up too high. I’ve lived eighteen years—almost nineteen now—and the only person who ever thought I was pretty was James.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s only because you didn’t know how to make the most of what you’ve got,” I assured her. “And thanks to Iolyt, you really don’t look like ‘you’ right now, so trust me and stand up straight. Everything is going to be perfect.” I reached over and smoothed one of the folds of her shyntara. “You ready?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“NO.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Great. Let’s go.” It was our turn next. I gave nori’s detail scroll to the Tableunia master. He looked it over carefully as we took our positions. A few tense moments passed, and then the Doors to the banquet hall opened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"An aurethryn—to you humans it would look like a large tent-sized crystal dome with a door—stood in the doorway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your royal metallicans (Rasilian way of saying magesties) and distinguished Tableunia magistrates,” the Tableunia master began, “I present to you from the mysterious flame Fleck isles of the Glindian sea, Lunalyn Opalinya. She enjoys Qualdrine crafting, elemental meditation, wind dancing, stargazing, lunar rituals, and archery of any elemental persuasion.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori rolled her eyes at the fake name and persona Lylend and I had concocted for her. I could see the little color she had draining from her face as I gave her a gentle push toward the aurethryn. “Remember the curtsy I taught you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think I’m gonna puke!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well don’t,” I said, “deep breaths. You can do this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I actually don’t think I can,” she said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For one brief moment, I thought I was going to have to force Nori into the aurethryn. But then, Gihara bless her, she took one last shaky breath and stepped in on her own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the door closed and the aurethryn slowly began to rotate, she shot me a final panicked look. I reminded her to smile, and then she spun away from me. I quickly turned my attention to the ceiling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori had struggled all week with the curtsy I choreographed for her, especially with the flourish I added to showcase her flowing sleeves. I held my breath as her image slid into place on the ceiling, magnified several times, and watched. For a moment, she looked like a nymph caught in the hungry gaze of a salamander, but I nearly cried with pride when she pulled herself together and completed the entire routine somewhat woodenly but without a hitch.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt myself letting out a breath I didn’t realize I’d been holding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She did it!” Oriette whispered. “She remembered every step.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, still blinking back tears.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the scene on the ceiling changed to the magistrates’ table set up on a platform at the far end of the banquet hall, I carefully watched each magistrate’s reaction as a smaller aurethryn on their platform focused in on them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the aurethryn at our end of the room stopped spinning, allowing Nori to step out onto the massive staircase leading down to the main floor and her seat, my muscles began to relax again. Overall, the reception of Lunalyn Opalinya had been exactly what I was going for: Delighted surprise.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori was waiting at the top of the steps, and I was about to join her when the final magistrate’s face slid into view.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I froze. Suddenly I felt like I was going to be the one to “puke.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My own mother’s magnified face gazed down haughtily at me from the ceiling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori glanced back at me, a worried expression in her eyes. I nodded at her encouragingly, but for the first time since beginning to plot out our Tableunia, I felt my dreams of victory rapidly slipping away. Mother’s presence on the magistrate panel could jeopardize everything if she knew I was coaching Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All at once I made a decision that might jeopardize nori’s and my friendship forever—a friendship I’d come to depend upon. I thought of Lylend, of what he’d already spent on the new wardrobe for her. I thought of what was at stake if we didn’t win. Then I thought of Nori. I’d convinced her to do the Tableunia—begged her to do it. But even if the rest of the magistrates fell in love with her, nobody knew the Tableunia better than Queen Citrine of Adoloria. She’d be able to sway enough of them to vote Nori out just to spite me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stay with her,” I said, quickly turning to Oriette. “Help her however you can, but don’t break any rules. We can’t give the magistrates any reason to disqualify her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could see Oriette was confused, but I didn’t stay long enough to explain. Instead, I ran.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Princess!” I heard Oriette hiss behind me. “Princess Zuli! Wait, I—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I turned a corner before she could finish her sentence.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"Journal: Aurethryns Journal","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9e50d7fa7a0641f6b19302d502d18ba4"},{"title":"The Gorgeous Gloriole (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"“How did I get myself dragged into this mess, Oriette?” I hissed as she discreetly led me to my table—the table I was supposed to be sharing with Zuli. Zuli, who’d promised to have my back during this whole excruciating experience.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew why she ran. One look at Zu’s mom’s giant 3D face on the banquet hall ceiling, and I was ready to take off myself. Sheesh! Nobody pulled off the ice queen persona quite like Queen Citrine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go back to our rooms and check on her, will you?” I asked Oriette before she sat down at my table.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She smiled sympathetically but shook her head. “The princess told me to stay with you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course she did.” I breathed out through my nostrils, my mind racing as to how I was supposed to pull off a good first impression without Zu’s relentless (but useful) instructions on how to act, who to smile at, and what to say.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once I settled into my seat just the way Zu taught me, I scanned all the other tables in the banquet hall. I’d never seen anything like it in my life. Whoever paid for this event was loaded. The hall looked like it was made entirely of pure white marble, dripping with gold filigree in the spirals and coils common in Cobbogothian artwork. It was adorned with every possible precious gem you can think of and then some. Even the centerpieces on the tables and the tables and chairs themselves were cast from pure gold, or at least gold leafing. With all the gilded lava tubes we’d taken to get here, it was safe to say, Queen Jayda definitely liked her gold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And she wasn’t alone. Nearly every other girl in her entourage appeared to be frosted with jewels and dipped in gold, silver, copper, or rose gold. I suddenly felt very underdressed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s the only way you’ll stand out,” Zuli had said. “Trust me, every other kyndie there will be dressed like a walking treasure chest.” I reminded myself I was supposed to be emanating the “subtle sophistication of the royals.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sitting up a little taller, I tried to look more confident than I felt. I could sense myself being watched by some of the girls at nearby tables.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lune-kyndie?” I began to hear the question uttered in the hushed whispers swirling around me. I’d been in this position several times before back in Boston—the subject of curious looks and awkward questions about my hair: “Is it natural?” Or my skin: “Don’t you ever go out in the sun?” An older lady once asked if I wore contacts to make my eyes so pale. I didn’t mind the curiosity; I was used to it. But the looks I was getting now? These weren’t curious; these looks were bordering on disgust. I’d gotten those back on Earth too, but I didn’t remember them coming with such obvious malicious undertones.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was safe to say I probably wasn’t going to make any lifelong gal pals in this place. Thankfully, I was okay with that. I was here to figure out what the metal key for the Lemorian Crest was so Lev knew what to request when he won the Callowynder tournament. The sooner I found it, the sooner I could ditch this bedazzled beauty pageant.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was doing my best to ignore the looks and stares when a long golden hand slid a gilded dish of creamy plumb-colored soup in front of me. Startled, I glanced up to thank them, but the words lodged in my throat.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A featureless face, half-masked from where the nose should’ve been up to its widow’s peak, “stared” down at me. (How else do you describe something you know is looking at you, but they don’t have any visible eyes?) The creature looked and moved like a golden robot, but I’d never seen a robot move so fluidly or look so beautiful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Troll. The word finally popped into my momentarily dazzled brain. Lev and Zuli had warned me about trolls, but no description they’d given me did justice to the flawless metal elemie hovering near me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shifted away from the troll but got the distinct impression I was being scrutinized to my very core by this cold, calculating creature. I shivered a little, glancing around the banquet hall at the other trolls that suddenly appeared from doors along the elaborate walls to wait on tables.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you ever get used to them?” I whispered to Oriette.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She glanced over at the troll from the corner of her eye. Then she gave her head a little shake. “Not if the troll isn’t your own,” she whispered. “My father always said never trust a troll that doesn’t work for you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why is that?” I whispered, leaning toward her. Something told me this troll could hear everything we were saying despite the whispering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because a troll’s sole purpose is to serve its own master, no matter the cost.” The way she said it sent a shiver down my spine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once everyone else had received their bowl of plumb soup, Queen Jayda stood up at the head of the magistrate table, stepping into a smaller replica of the crystal polygon at the top of the staircase—Oriette called it an aurethryn. Queen Jayda’s face was suddenly magnified on the ceiling of the banquet hall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She was tall and slender with long, silky rose gold hair. If her stepdaughter hadn’t been beside her at the magistrate table, I might’ve said she was the most beautiful woman I’d ever seen. But Princess Sylva outshone everyone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The metal queen welcomed us and introduced the magistrates beside her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now, my beauties, before I leave you to dine and mingle amongst yourselves, I will begin this Tableunia the way every Tableunia has begun since its inception. I will tell you the legend that started it eons ago—the Legend of Rasil the Beautiful and the Gorgeous Gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A buzz of excitement hummed through the air, and then it grew very still as Queen Jayda began her tale of a magical gloriole that was first owned by none other than Rasil the Beautiful. As the story unfolded, it sounded like the gloriole was a precious ancient artifact that had been passed down for generations in DonTon’s family.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Excitedly, I opened my mouth to speak but then remembered Zuli wasn’t there. Could it really be this easy? Could I have already discovered my ticket out of here? Could the gloriole be the metal key?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Jayda finished the tale and pointed to the gloriole she wore on her own head. “This is the gloriole of Rasil the Beautiful.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All signs were pointing to yes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I am very pleased to announce,” the queen continued, “that this Triolune Tableunia is quite a special one. This celebration happens to mark the thirty-fifth birthday of my stepson, Prince Sydian.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The room exploded with excited whispers and muffled giggling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It is difficult to believe how much he has grown since I married his father twenty years ago. Those who are old enough to remember will recall that DonTon swore to make the winner of that festival’s Tableunia his new queen. I stand before you today because I was the winner of that Tableunia.” She reverently touched the gloriole once more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And now it gives me great pleasure to introduce Prince Sydian to you all. He would like to make his own very important announcement.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With that, Queen Jayda sat down. A door behind the magistrates’ table opened, and the ridiculously good-looking Prince Sydian stepped out of it. There was more giggling and whispering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you, Queen Jayda,” Sydian said, stepping into the smaller aurethryn. His gorgeous face magnified on the ceiling in 3D was blinding. I decided to stare at my plate instead of ogle him the way every other girl in the room (and her mother) was doing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could still see the prince’s reflection in my golden flatware, however, and when he smiled, another wave of breathless muttering fluttered over the room. Heck, my own heart fluttered a little at the sight of it. He was flawless.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Continuing to ignore the ceiling, I glanced back at the aurethryn encompassing the head table. When I caught the prince looking back at me, I quickly looked away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Honored candidates,” the prince continued in a deep, velvety voice, “I’m thrilled that so many of you have turned out to grace us with your beauty, poise, elegance, and talent at this Triolune’s Tableunia. As you know, Rasili hasn’t hosted the festival in over a century, and because of that, my father and I wanted to make this Tableunia extra special for everyone.” He paused for dramatic effect, flashing another glittering smile. “Which is why it gives me great pleasure to follow in my excellent father’s footsteps and announce that the candidate who wins this Triolune’s Tableunia…will be crowned using none other than Rasil the Beautiful’s gorgeous gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a moment of stunned silence…the calm before the storm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To say the banquet hall exploded once the prince’s words fully sunk in would be a major understatement. It was so deafening I had to cover my ears.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then the hall collapsed into utter chaos.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I knew what was happening——before I could fathom what this announcement even meant——I turned to Oriette, but she was gone. Where had she gone?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced back at the head table. Contestants were thronging to get to the prince. He narrowly made his escape back through the hidden doorway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I caught sight of Oriette. She was speaking to someone at the head table. When she moved aside, I could see it was Princess Sylva. The next moment, Oriette was heading back my way. Catching my eye, she mouthed for me to quickly follow her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to hurry toward her, but the crowd was impassable. When we finally reached one another, I took her hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is going on?” I mouthed, trying to make sense of everything. “What does it mean?” I knew from the legend Queen Jayda just told that the gloriole was a prize worthy of excitement, and it would definitely make getting ahold of it a lot more challenging for the Hood. But honestly!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was no point in talking here, though. I’d only seen frenzied madness like this once in my life when Uncle Jack showed me a documentary on the Beatles after I asked what “Beatle Mania” meant. The hysteria that greeted them everywhere they went in the 1960s was pretty similar to what was happening all around me now. In fact, multiple trolls were currently performing crowd control just so Queen Jayda and the princess could make their escape. Maybe Prince Sydian was Cobbogoth’s equivalent to the Beatles?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette eventually led me to the side of the banquet hall. She pushed what had appeared to be a seamless slab of gilded marble, and a secret panel like the door Prince Sydian had come through popped out of the stone. Oriette motioned me to follow her inside, and I found myself in a dark passage before she closed the panel behind us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Despite the sudden silence, I felt like I had a fishbowl over my head. I knew Oriette was trying to talk to me, but my ears were still ringing and everything was muffled. She gave up and motioned me farther down the passage and into a small room off to the right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I knew what was happening, she led me inside, turned around, and left, closing the door behind her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait…Oriette?” I called, returning to the door. “This isn’t my room.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t worry,” said a smooth, deep voice. “She’s waiting for you just outside.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I spun around, and an electric shock unlike anything I’d ever felt shot through me. None other than Prince Sydian stood against the opposite wall, smiling that mania-causing smile at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How did you get out of that circus so fast?” I asked, taking a cautious step back toward the door.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Okay, so this guy was insanely good looking, but I didn’t know him from Adam—er—Cobbogoth? Whatever. The point was, he could’ve been a total psycho, and Oriette had just trapped me in a room with him. And I was in love with James.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry to be so clandestine, Lunalyn,” he said, taking a step toward me. “I just couldn’t let the opportunity pass without wishing you the very best of luck in the Tableunia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh…,” I let out my breath. “Thank you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then I must’ve blinked, because the next thing I knew he was standing mere inches away from me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I should really get going,” I said. “Zu will be worried.” But before I could step back from him, the prince seized my hand, and I felt my heartrate rocket. I began summoning whatever fire I had in my veins, knowing water would be useless against a metal melder. But then he stunned me by gently placing my palm to his chest…his warm rippling chest. Think of James, Nor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I hope with all my heart you win the Tableunia,” he said, this time with what looked like genuine feeling in his eyes. Then he pressed my hand to his lips…his warm velvety lips. James, Nor! James!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A moment later, I was alone in that tiny room, wondering what the heck had just happened.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Cobbogothian Currency","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"3a68bd75d0e949f2a064f44eb13344b8"},{"title":"Zulis Fashion Guide","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"75f3eedccd874f718476d19ef0f39b39"},{"title":"Gloriole Legend","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"dcc28aa45b694e1ca98d85ce9a596a63"}]},{"n":54,"letterTitle":"The Lunevergence","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"The Lunevergence","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You know, I’ve never really been the nervous type. I’ve always been able to keep my cool under pressure. I don’t take myself too seriously, not even when I break things like Melusian’s barracks and ruin—maybe forever—the Hood’s chances of ever getting the water key for the Lemorian Crest. I’ve just never really worried too much about the small—or maybe not so small—stuff. For better or for worse. Sure, having a dad like Pieter, who’s always making such a big deal out of little things, probably made me this way. Who knows?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All I know is standing on the edge of the biggest arena I’ve ever seen in my life, waiting my turn to show the crowd what I can do, makes me real glad I don’t sweat the small stuff…and the not so small stuff.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It’s called Lunevergence by the way—the opening ceremonies for a Triolune Callowynder tournament. There had to be over 100,000 Cobbogothians packed into the spiral stands of the arena tonight. The Rasilians even added four more stories of stands to accommodate everyone. That’s double the crowd there was for the regular Callowynder tournament last week, even during its final round.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As Callowynder tournaments go, last week’s monowyn tourney wasn’t bad. Mala actually won. Who knew such a quiet little thing could whip water like that? The request she made in exchange for the Callowynder stone was no surprise though. She asked DonTon for his two best qualdrinists to come to Melusian’s barracks and study her people and their symptoms until they found a more practical cure for Lamianza. Until we gather all the keys to the Lemorian Crest and open it, all the Adolorians have is to keep sharing the key in Melusian’s trident—or keep visiting Zu on the shores of the Glindian sea in order to avoid the inevitable.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tonight felt completely different than the Lunevergence for last week’s tournament. There were all the extra people, sure, but they also added a giant crystal aurethryn over the field. As a result, everything happening down here was being magnified at least 100 times up in the sky. I mean, you couldn’t even pick your nose without 100,000 people knowing about it. In my opinion, it took away all the fun of being at the actual event—you know, being in the crowd, cheering on your contender in real time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"According to the game magistrates, the aurethryn enabled a more “personal connection” between the audience and the contenders. But really they just didn’t want that many people storming the field and messing up the tourney. If someone gets trampled or tampers with anything in the game, Triolune law says they have to shut it down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To me, though, there’s nothing personal about it. One of the best things about watching a Callowynder match is when a player catapults themself into the crowd to celebrate a victory with their fans. You can’t do that when there’s a crystal dome standing between you and the crowd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So naturally I got Iolyt to help me out. That’s right. I’ve got a little something planned for “connecting” with the fans tonight. If all goes well, they won’t be forgetting me any time soon. That’s the thing with Callowynder; you don’t want the audience forgetting about you. You want them rooting for you—cheering you on because all that energy directed at you can really save your skin during the tournament, especially if you ever run out of a certain element. Which was why this was definitely gonna be a Lunevergence to remember.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grabbed several ellaprava from the refreshment table and shoved one into my mouth. Kynd! I loved ellaprava. So far they had them at all the major initiation events for the tournament. We never got stuff like this on Feg, so I was definitely partaking. If I wasn’t careful, I’d end up walking away from the Triolune tournament looking like a zyrker.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re going to be amazing, levy,” Zu whispered, coming up beside me and squeezing my hand with both of hers.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I picked her up with my free arm for my good luck kiss. I knew the fate of the entire realm kind of depended on my ability to win Callowynder and get us the metal key, but I wasn’t too worried. I’d seen the competition. Lylend had shown me his notes. Prince Sydian was my only real competition. Thanks to Nori, we were all now pretty certain Rasil the beautiful’s gloriole held the metal key. It had all the hallmarks. If I didn’t win the tournament at the end of these next two weeks, nori’d have to win the Tableunia. It’s the reason Lylend wouldn’t let her drop out of it now that she’d discovered the key. Even so, I knew by the way she’d been fidgeting all night in that fancy shyntara Zu had her in, nori’d never forgive me if I didn’t win.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I was gonna win. Period. And it all started tonight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Lunevergence was all about showing the audience what you were capable of in order to win elementum—or elemental sponsorship from the crowd for when the tournament actually began. It was also about intimidating your opponents. This proved a little tricky when you had four different elements to show off. You had to figure out a way to prove your mastery of all four in the same amount of time the regular contenders showed their mastery for one. It was tricky but definitely worth it. You never knew when you’d need some elementum and for which element during a Callowynder tournament. You wanted as many sponsors for as many of your elements as you could get.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Three of the five contenders had already done their introductory stints for the crowd. The Fourth—princie-kynd—was just finishing up, and I’ll be honest, I wasn’t too impressed. I mean, creating a rotating spiral staircase (Nori called it an “escalator”) out of elaborate gold filigree and then blowing yourself into the arena on a mildly impressive storm in order to descend said staircase like the worst kind of poser out there didn’t impress me. Even if every kyndie and her mother had swooned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s nearly time now, kynd.” Lylend entered my tent rubbing his hands together. The Naridi came over and held out a hand to me before giving mine a firm shake. “May the eye of Gihara be upon you, kynd,” he said. “The hope of the Hood is in your hands.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook his hand in return—a strange custom from the Olden realm—and noticed the tightness behind his eyes had lessened. It was probably due to a combination of nori’s news from the Tableunia and the fact that my only real competition in the tournament was only as threatening as his “dreamy” smile. Since he was way more interested in Nori than my Zu, not even that was a real threat to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You have your routine all set?” Lylend asked. “Iolyt gave you the illusion stones we tested out last night?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I NODDED.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All week, Lylend had worked with me, helping me choreograph an epic lightning show to go along with the mini storms I’d be conjuring. It all came down to control. With Iolyt’s help, I was even able to form the giant golden disk I’d be using as the foundation for my icy grand finale.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Has anyone seen Pieter?” I asked nonchalantly. Honestly, I didn’t want to care whether he came to see me compete or not. But…*deep SIGH*…I did ’cause he was my dad. That and I hadn’t seen very much of him the past week. Not that it was a bad thing. But again…*deep SIGH*…he was my dad.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend glanced around at the others. Zu and Iolyt both shrugged. Every member of the Hood—except Pieter—was there. They were all in the tent with me, except Nori and Brawlo. They’d stayed behind in the private box reserved for contenders’ entourages—thought it would be too awkward to be in such close proximity to princie-kynd after he cornered Nori the other night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right, kynd,” Lylend said, resting a heavy hand on my shoulder. “You’re up.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to Zu, who was grinning proudly as she held a small bottle out to me. I drank the liquid mixture of gold and water, replenishing my blood’s supply for the show. The last thing I wanted was to run out of element halfway through my introduction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ll be wonderful, libkyn,” Iolyt said before giving me a hug.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princie-kynd’s music was winding down. I stepped back, glanced around at all of them, and winked at Zu. Then I charged out onto the grounds to take my place on the field.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m not one to brag generally, but imagine what it did to my confidence when the audience kind of went zyrky as soon as I took my place in front of them. I mean, I knew word traveled fast around Rasili, especially in the lower markets, but I hadn’t expected to hear 100,000 Cobbogothians suddenly begin chanting my name around the stands. My best guess was the goldabungs who’d hung around the arena to watch us practicing the past two weeks were spreading the word about each contender’s capabilities. It was clear I’d impressed at least some of them enough to start chattering about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev-I-an! Lev-I-an! Lev-I-an!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gihara! I thought. I could get used to this!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rolling up the sleeves of my shyntara, I took a deep breath and prepared to give the people what they wanted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started by forming a golden disk beneath my feet. The golden disk was where I would stand during my entire performance. This had been Lylend’s idea, but I hadn’t liked it in the beginning.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hear me out, kynd,” he’d said. “You don’t want them watching you scurry around the arena like you’re chasing after something you can’t seem to catch. You’re the master of your elements. You’re in control. Show them you are. Stand still and deliver!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the end, I had to agree with him, especially when he demonstrated. For the record, there’s nothing more mesmerizing than watching Lylend the Naridi stand in one place and deliver a “wicked cool” (to use one of nori’s phrases) spark spindling show.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When my disk was about the size of a baby crakyn, I stepped onto the center of it, then used my earth kneading to propel myself to the center of the arena. The crowd cheered louder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I kicked off my intro with a bang—a lightning show that cracked through the sky, lighting up the whole arena! Sparks flashed above like fireworks, and thunder rolled in perfect sync with the beat dropping in the music. I’d been pumped for this all week, especially after Lylend showed me some of his trickiest moves to try. So I figured why not start things off with a gust of wind? I whipped up a little cyclone that whipped around the arena, ruffling hair and flags, sending everyone’s cheers up a notch. They were hooked already, and I was just getting started.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right as the beat hit its sweet spot, I swapped the wind for earth and dug deep, summoning up a huge, spinning dust devil that whirled around the arena like it had a mind of its own. Then, just for fun, I split it in two, sending Twin dust devils in different directions. One of them charged right up to the edge of the aurethryn—the one thing between me and the crowd—making it shimmer like it might just give way. I commanded the earth to propel my golden disk up to the barrier, and with a single rap of my fist, the entire aurethryn dome shattered, crumbling to the ground like millions of diamonds. (I told you I had something special planned.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the first dust devil finally spun itself out, it exploded into a glittering rain of gold dust—all Iolyt’s idea, a brilliant way to show off my metal-bending skills. By the Gihara did it get the crowd going! I could see people jumping up and down, laughing and cheering as gold dust sparkled down over them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Three elements down. One more to go.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a deep breath, pumped my fist in the air to the beat of the music, then threw my hands toward the remaining dust devil, infusing it with a blast of water. The thing transformed on the spot, swelling into a wild, churning hurricane right in the middle of the arena. I could feel icy forms taking shape inside, and I grinned. This was it. With a flick of my wrist, I called forth three massive ice giants, each bigger than the last, rising out of the storm like legendary creatures. Thanks to Iolyt’s illusion stones, they looked like something from another world, glimmering and ghostly. The biggest one lifted me high on its palm, holding my golden disk and me like I was royalty, then swept me across the stands as the hurricane whipped around us, snow and ice flying everywhere. The crowd went totally nuts, and I couldn’t stop grinning—just raw elemental energy up there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could feel the elements from everyone watching, and I knew they wouldn’t be forgetting me any time soon. Honestly, I don’t think it could’ve gone any better. I brought the hurricane to a clean stop right as the music hit its final notes like it had never even been there. Flashing my most disarming grin, I gave a huge, theatrical Bow and told my giant to set my disk and me back onto the field. With a last flourish, I made the three giants burst apart into giant snowflakes, dusting the entire field and crowd with a shimmering layer of snow. People were laughing, reaching up to catch the snowflakes as they fell. I topped it all off by pulling up the shards of the aurethryn barrier and reforming it again, sealing everything back in place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took another Bow, and just as I started to straighten up, a bright splash of color caught my eye. I looked up, and—bam!—A big, aqua-colored chunk of plauzenite hit the barrier, bursting with color. I was frozen for a second; then more pieces of plauzenite started pelting the aurethryn, each one exploding into bright colors. It was like a full-on rainbow had erupted, and the stadium lit up in shades and sparkles like one of Lylend’s craziest light shows.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t believe it! Plauzenite was a rare crystal, and no one had tossed any yet—not even for princie-kynd and his “dreamy” smile. To get hit with a whole wave of it? That was next-level applause.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced over to see Zuli, Lylend, and Iolyt going zyrky from my tent.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I let out a breath I didn’t know I’d been holding and glanced up at my own reflection in the sky as my face broke into a grin. Everything worked perfectly. I had dominated the Lunevergence, hands down. Not a single thing had gone wrong!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Until suddenly it did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I should’ve known when I passed the strange lady on my way back to the tent that my luck was about to change. She was tall and regal, hair the color of rust on iron, eyes the color of a copper patina. But it was the challenge in her smile that made me take note.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This strange, wild beauty could only be Seraphyna—the one contestant nobody knew anything about but couldn’t stop whispering about. All we knew was that she was rumored to be a quintowyn. A quintowyn! She still hadn’t officially registered by the start of tonight’s ceremonies, so even that was speculation. The chatter among the contenders and their entourages was whether she’d be a no-show.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As usual, Lylend seemed to know better. He’d suspected it was all part of her strategy. “Stay vigilant,” he’d warned me. “Take nothing for granted.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I hadn’t. I’d been checking for any updates on her since the first contender opened the Lunevergence. Most of the contestants in the Wyn tournament were diowyns or triowyns. But if the rumors were true, aside from princie-kynd’s smile, Seraphyna was my only real competition.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I made it back to the tent, I found the rest of the Hood lined up at the entrance, preparing to watch. I sidled up next to Zu, and she took my hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You did amazing!” She said, beaming up at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really well, libkyn,” Iolyt agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thanks,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now let’s see if it was enough, kynd” Lylend said, with a nod toward the arena.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Seraphyna’s music—music just as strange and wild as she looked—started up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu squeezed my hand. Then Seraphyna began to move. And for the first time in a long time, I got nervous. Really nervous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the most spectacular display of fire, water, metal, wind, and earth I’ve ever seen. Not even Lylend’s best spark show held a candle to her skill.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You really think Noriah’s got a chance at winning that crown?” I heard Lylend mutter to Iolyt as Zu and I awkwardly retreated to the food table.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It stung to hear Lylend—Lylend the legend, Lylend the Naridi—doubt me. But when all was said and done, for the first time in my Callowynder career, I was doubting myself. My chances of winning the Callowynder tournament had just dropped significantly.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Mala","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"ba0c00f286064c2aad758236fc4282cc"},{"title":"Lunevergence Illustration","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"b5498499643f4fae868954300a0ee7a2"},{"title":"Journal: Lunevergence & Elementum","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d96693e0360445a9acf831546aff31f0"},{"title":"Plauzanite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"6e7ee7cd0f9d4506a4b4c23b2d5bb5ab"}]},{"n":55,"letterTitle":"The Siryn","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"The Siryn (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You know, Rasili doesn’t have much going for it as far as first impressions go. Once you get past the Roklin marshlands, though, it’s actually not that bad.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt and I made the mistake of not listening to the goldabungs’ advice to follow the Dag river north instead of the river troll, and we ended up in the thick of the marshlands before we even knew we’d reached Rasilian territory. Consequently, rather than being able to beeline it for the city, we had to lead the goldabungs through the wettest parts of the marshland, all while listening to them say “we told you so.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This hiccup in our plans ended up putting us several miles northeast of DonTon’s palace when we finally made it out of the last of the mud and sand traps and into trolly row pass or the eastern valley between DonTon’s volcano-turned-palace and the lake the goldabungs called trolly row.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Does this look right to you?” I quietly mumbled to Glynt. The last thing I wanted was to give the goldabungs the idea we’d made another wrong turn. We’d just entered a small forest near the lake. Glynt answered by turning to the right and pointing up. Sure enough, there was an unmistakable volcano towering over us just beyond the line of trees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There’s something about volcanos that really humbles you, you know? Not that Glynt and I needed any more of that. But this volcano was especially humbling. There were no foothills leading up to it. It was just valley and then a sharp, steep, vertical rise all the way to craggy black cliffs looming ominously like earth giants against patches and stripes of acid green moss and grass.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sky around the cliffs was littered with soaring, bright-colored birds who’d built their nests in the cliffs. As they prowled the sky looking for food, they circled multiple plunging, white waterfalls of various sizes, some cascading from as high up as the concaved summit where the top of the volcano had obviously blown off the last time it erupted. For Rasili’s sake, I hoped it was a long, long, long time ago.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not a bad place to take a rest, eh?” Glynt said, motioning for the goldabungs to take a load off.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grunted my agreement as I scanned the jagged, black cliffs closer, realizing they were riddled with gaping caves of every shape and size. It was hard to see how deep they were because all the waterfalls kept us in a perpetual hazy mist.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The kids had done surprisingly well on the journey. None of them—not even the littlest ones—complained when we ran out of food halfway through the marshlands.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As if reading my thoughts, one of the boldest of the goldabungs—a curly-haired kynd named Mica—came up beside me. “We’re all getting pretty hungry about now,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right,” I said. “What are our options?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mica just patted my arm and winked at me. “You and Glynt just go catch some fish out at the lake there. Leave the rest to us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I raised my eyebrows at Glynt for an explanation. He just shrugged and started leading the way toward the shore of trolly row.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The goldabungs, on the other hand, scattered. From where Glynt and I rigged some makeshift lines for fishing, we could see them scurrying up and around the lower cliffs of the volcano.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are they doing?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My guess is they’re looking for signs of flame Fleck covyls.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew from my time in Cobbogoth so far that these were like a combination of fireflies and honeybees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is one of the best times to harvest the fylyla,” Glynt explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pretty soon there was a whooping of celebration from the cliffs. They’d obviously found some covyls. I could just make out the older kids carefully retrieving several combs of blood red fylyla from one of the covyls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A little while later, a few of the older goldabungs came to us onshore, asking to borrow a rope.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What for?” Glynt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Watch and see,” said a tall girl named cambryte.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Curious, we gave them two ropes we’d found at the Dogril site back in the forest of Dag. The kyndlin took them, and we saw them scramble back up to the volcano, but this time it was to some of the higher cliffs. The older goldabungs secured the ropes to sturdy-looking rocks up top. Then we watched in half-horror, half-admiration as several of the smaller tykes took turns jumping and then swinging from the ropes along the cliff face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’re they doing now?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara!” Glynt breathed. “They’re sprangling! See how they’re swinging and then stopping at certain spots on the cliffs? They’re gathering eggs from the birds’ nests.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You ever seen anything like that before?” I asked Glynt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shook his head, wrestling with a bite on his line. “But I’ve heard of it before. It’s a Rasilian and gliptrainian tradition.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Creative,” I ground out while helping Glynt reel in a giant, iridescent shyntfish. It was big enough to swallow one of the smallest goldabungs whole.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally regathered, it was getting dark. Each of us showed the other our “catch” for dinner, and it was clear the goldabungs were the winners. Compared to our three—albeit rather large—shyntfish, the goldabungs contributed four dozen eggs, seven weeping fylyla combs, at least eight pints of lyfine berries, and a stack of large, stiff, flat leaves to use as plates. All we needed now was a sheltered place to set up camp and start cooking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt and I found a gully where the trees skirted the base of the volcano. It was somewhat sheltered, so we sent the goldabungs to gather something to cover the ground where they would sleep while we prepared the food.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt rigged the shyntfish on a sort of spit, and I used my fire to carefully roast them without burning them. It took a bit of the fire reserve in my blood, but who knew when the last time these kyndlin had a hot meal was?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the goldabungs returned, they brought a sort of clear, crystal-like tuber as well called starynth root to roast with the fish.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I decided to make things a little more fun for the goldabungs by letting them order their own eggs for dinner. They’d tell me how many they wanted and how they wanted them cooked, and I’d crack the eggs over one of the leaves in my hand and use my fire to cook them up. Most just wanted scrambled, but a few of the older kids wanted to test me and asked for poached instead. Rascals.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It could’ve been how hungry I was, but the foraged food was the best meal I’d had since arriving in Cobbogoth. Even the starynth root was good—it tasted like a mix between pineapple and yams with a minty aftertaste. Mixed with the savory, fatty shyntfish, it reminded me of thanksgiving dinner or something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thanksgiving dinner?” I mumbled to myself. “Huh.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s that?” Glynt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah nothin’,” I said, waving the fragment of memory away. “Just something I remembered.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You remember what to do with those, right?” Glynt asked, watching me closely. He’d been doing that a lot lately. Any time I mentioned some bit of memory out of the blue, he’d remind me what to do with it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I shrugged. “I’ll set it aside until I’ve got more to work with.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good,” he said. “It’s important not to force it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, pushing the last of my food around on my leaf plate.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt turned to the kids then. “All right. How’s everybody feeling now?” He asked. The goldabungs practically cheered. I was sure they were better fed and watered with this one meal than they’d been in days…maybe weeks. Something told me the dingbats weren’t too concerned with feeding them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where’d you guys learn to forage like that?” I asked, knowing the answer already. Goldabungs were street urchins. They were survivors.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s a whole lot more where that came from,” said one of the older kynds, folding his arms behind his head as he settled down for the night on the beds the goldabungs had made. They’d used the same kind of leaves we’d used as plates. “We just don’t want to give away all our tricks yet. You’ve gotta earn it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see,” I said with a chuckle. “Remind me to bring you along the next time I get lost in the woods, huh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right,” Glynt said with a Clap of his hands. “Time for sleep.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt had taken on a sort of paternal role for these kyndlin since we’d rescued them from the dogrils. The goldabungs took to him immediately. That was the thing with Glynt; he was extra likeable but in a quiet, unassuming way. Yet when he needed to take charge, it came just as naturally to him as blending into the background did. As much as I didn’t like his occasional microscopic analysis of me, he was a pretty useful fella to have around.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the goldabungs were all hunkered down for the night—they preferred a dogpile sleeping arrangement with older kyndlin on the outside to protect the little ones on the inside—Glynt and I sat by the fire to keep watch.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The goldabungs said the dogrils smuggled them from the city through one of these caves,” Glynt reminded me in a low voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you think it’s possible there could still be some dingbats around?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You keep calling them that,” he said. “Do you know why?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I thought about it for a bit but couldn’t think of a reason. “I don’t know,” I shrugged. “I think I just always have.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hmmm…” Glynt surveyed the trees while I took my turn watching the cliffs. When I didn’t see anything, I glanced up to the sky. The three moons were closer together than I’d ever seen them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pretty aren’t they?” Glynt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah.” Usually, it was just the big gold one and the smaller silver one close together. The blueish-gray one had been clear across the sky when I first trekked out of the Glorian pass. Tonight it was almost touching the other two.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In twenty days, they’ll have all come together into an eclipse before Corynty, the blueish-gray one,” Glynt clarified, “starts his journey back to banishment.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was about to ask why Corynty was banished in the first place when a sound—a strangely familiar sound—startled us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did you hear that?” I asked, my eyes on the cliffs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt nodded, glancing back at the goldabungs. They were all still sleeping, safe and accounted for.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sound came again—this time like an echo bouncing off the gulley we were in.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll go look in case it’s dogrils,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt nodded, knowing I was best equipped to take them on. “Signal with two flames if you want me to follow,” he said. It was a soundless system we’d worked out while trekking through the rest of the forest of Dag.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I crouched down, crawling up the small embankment. When I reached the top of the gully, I stayed low, pausing for a bit…concentrating.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There it was again! This time the sound was even louder. I scanned the caves and cliffs above us. Was it a bird?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head, as if to answer myself. It sounded more like…like…I closed my eyes as the sound rose and fell again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Like…singing? Like a person singing, and there was something really familiar about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I debated whether to signal Glynt. He’d almost certainly know what it was. In the end, I decided to.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whatever it is, it’s coming from up on the mountain,” Glynt whispered, when he’d army crawled up beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “It’s like it’s echoing out of one of the caves.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but which one?” Glynt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know, but it doesn’t sound like dogrils.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That doesn’t mean it couldn’t still be dangerous.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but…” I shook my head. The familiarness of it wasn’t setting off any alarms in me. There was actually something comforting about it. But it was sad, too. I wanted to get closer to the cliffs to see if I could get a better listen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just as I thought this, the singing stopped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt and I looked at each other. He shrugged and started back to check on the goldabungs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced back at the cliffs before following him. As I did, a sudden burst of vivid red erupted from one of the larger caves up in the higher cliffs. For a second, I thought it was a lava ball because it was so bright. But as I squinted into the night, I could see it was just a large, bright red bird of some kind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What was that?” Glynt hissed from halfway down the gulley.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A bird, I think.” I shrugged, finding it in the sky again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was much closer now, and I could see it was a really large bird.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And it was flying toward us!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started pulling the fire from my blood. There was hardly any left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get back to the goldabungs,” I called to Glynt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He disappeared down the gully.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced back at the approaching bird, almost expecting to see plumes of fire burst from its mouth. Just when I was ready to throw my own fire, the bird made a sharp swoop upward, exposing long, unmistakable tail feathers…and feet? People feet…with toes?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head, sure I hadn’t seen what I thought. It had to be a trick of the light. It was too dark to see anything clearly anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The strange bird continued flying off into the night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Relieved, I watched it until it was out of sight. Then I waited, listening hard as my eyes scoured the caves a while longer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The singing never returned.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"The Siryn (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So…I’m not the most talented girl on the street, okay. Let’s just get that out of the way right now. To be fair, I do have talents, but they are more subtle…hidden talents. Like my ability with languages or my vault-like memory. Those are talents, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unfortunately, they aren’t really the kind you can get up on a stage and wow an audience with. This particular fact was causing Zuli and me quite a bit of stress. Why? Because, unbeknownst to me before I signed on for this Tableunia train wreck, there’s a “talent” portion Zu failed to mention.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It was all on the poster, Nori!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but how was I supposed to know that’s what ‘talent and technique’ meant in Cobbogoth?” I replied. “I was thinking it had something to do with how fast I could crack a water whip or escape from a dogril.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, no, that’s forte and fitness,” Zuli clarified with a look that told me even the smallest child in Cobbogoth knew that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but when you said this was a competition, you didn’t mention anything about having to perform something in front of people.” I sighed in exasperation, my voice echoing around us. “I just thought it meant something like what Lev did in Lunevergence.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We’d taken refuge in a cave on the backside of the volcano DonTon’s Palace was built into. Since it was an extinct volcano, there were plenty of caves to choose from back here, but we only had direct access to this one from inside the palace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The cave was about the size of a small chapel and accessible through one of the “secret” tunnels in the palace. Only Oriette, who presently sat in the corner watching us, and her goldabung friends knew about this one. As far as everyone else in the palace was concerned, all the tunnels on this side of the volcano were sealed off when the palace was originally built on the other side.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, Nori!” Zu pleaded. “It’s the Tableunia! Everything about it is a performance.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but had I known there was even a remote possibility I’d have to dance or something, I never would’ve agreed to compete,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not even for Rasil’s gloriole?” Zuli coaxed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not even for that,” I insisted grouchily. She knew I wasn’t serious, of course. I’d do anything within reason to get my hands on it. After all, the sooner we got the Haven out of me, the sooner we could go find James. But I was still trying to decide if it was within reason to humiliate myself in front of the biggest audience I’d ever seen. Heck! The biggest audience Cobbogoth had ever seen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I still refuse to believe you don’t have at least one talent we can work with.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not that I can think of,” I shrugged.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you play an instrument at all?” Oriette asked. She’d led us to the cave when Zuli found out the other contestants were sending around their own hostesses to act as spies. They wanted to know what kind of talent they were up against. Which, of course, is what led to my current state of stress and enlightenment.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Maybe you could paint something?” Oriette suggested.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry, no,” I said, genuinely sad to disappoint the cutie pie. “I’ve never played an instrument in my life or done anything artistic.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What about dancing?” Zuli asked. “You’re good at memorizing. Maybe we could get someone to teach you a dance to perform.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. “No coordination. That’s why I only ever run for exercise.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, Nori! Help me out here!” Zu pleaded. She leaned her copper head against the cave wall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Zu, look at me. My goal growing up was to draw as little attention to myself as possible. Stages were a ‘no go’ zone for me. It’s why being homeschooled actually suited me pretty well.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli let out a defeated sigh.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If the stakes for winning Tableunia weren’t so high—if Seraphyna hadn’t shown up and blown us all away at Lunevergence the other night—I would be teasing both Oriette and Zu for getting so worked up about this whole thing. Better yet, I would’ve dropped out by now. Since Seraphyna had shown up, though, Lev was actually facing real competition in the Callowynder tournament, which meant the Tableunia was the Hood’s backup plan for getting the gloriole. Gihara help us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette got to her feet and began pacing. She was so tiny. It made me smile even in my grumpy, panicked mood to see how serious she was taking everything for us. After pacing a while, she started humming as she paced up and down the deep cave, periodically calling out possible talent ideas. Her humming soon turned to what sounded like quiet, primal beatboxing or guttural chanting. I found myself getting distracted by it more and more. There was something about her chanting—something really familiar. I started digging deep into what I’d started calling my “Olden Realm memories.” To be honest, even with my stellar memory abilities, it was getting harder and harder to recall them. Anything that wasn’t Cobbogothian-related was starting to get foggy. Thank goodness James was Cobbogothian.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d just shot down Zuli’s suggestion that I learn to juggle fireballs to music—again we had a coordination problem—when the memory finally resurfaced.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh! I do have one!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” Zu asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette stopped singing and eagerly trotted over.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you have?” Zu prodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t get your hopes up okay?” I said. “It’s probably nothing, but Oriette just reminded me of something that might work.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, let’s hear it?” Zuli urged.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ve never done it in front of an audience or anything,” I explained nervously, “unless one or two people count as an audience. Also, I’ve only ever had three other people say I was any good at it.” I winced. “One of them was dying at the time though, so…you know what, never mind. I think…I think it’s too weird.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just tell us!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What was I thinking bringing this up? Just showing Zuli and Oriette to see if I truly was any good at it would be humiliating.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nori!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I said, taking a deep breath. “So there was one time Uncle Jack took me on a short dig to the Faroe Islands. I was like eight or something at the time because it was before he started leaving me home on my own. Anyway, the dig was near this old farm where artists and hippies used to meet to create together. There was a particular singer there that he was friends with. Her name was Eva or Eivor Palsdottir. Anyway, she was performing at the farm one night, so Uncle Jack took me along to hear her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go on,” Zuli pressed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated. Think of the gloriole, Nor. Think of finding James. “Her ‘singing’ was really unique, unlike anything I’d ever heard before or since…except for just now, when Oriette started singing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli beamed at Oriette, whose jade eyes brightened adorably.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That singing always stuck with me because it felt…familiar, you know? Like it spoke to some deep, ancestral part of my soul or something. I’m only now just realizing why that might be.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, but what does this have to do with your talent?” Zu pressed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m getting to that part,” I said, sliding down the cave wall to sit on the cold floor. The girls slid down next to me. “That music stuck with me so much that I eventually taught myself how to do it. Just for myself, you know?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, one night when I was sixteen, Uncle Jack came home early from a dig and walked into the house while I was in the middle of a song. I didn’t know he was there until I’d finished. For reasons I didn’t understand at the time, he excitedly insisted I cross the street with him right then and perform the same song for Gram Riley—er—Gramillia. At first, I absolutely refused, but then he reminded me how unwell she was, and I was willing to do anything to get her mind off her pain. So I went with him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go on…” There was reticent hope in Zuli’s eyes now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, James was at home, so I was extra nervous, and my performance came off a little stiff and shaky, but I suppose I must’ve been decent because afterward, when I stopped singing and opened my eyes, Gram just stared at me. Then she nodded at Uncle Jack and said, ‘You’re right. She is a ‘siryn.’’”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli and Oriette both gasped and slapped hands over their mouths.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” I asked. “Did I say something bad?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No. No,” Zuli shook her head in emphasis. “You’re sure Gramillia called you a siryn?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “But it wasn’t the kind of siren we have in folktales and legends back in the Olden Realm,” I said. “I checked with Gram because sirens aren’t a good thing back on Earth.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A siryn. Wow!” Oriette looked at me with a new sort of reverence that made me feel awkward. What had I gotten myself into now?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Gramillia wouldn’t have called you a siryn lightly, Nori.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shrugged. “I’m pretty sure that’s what she said. Of course, I didn’t know she was Cobbogothian at the time, so I didn’t ask her to explain, not even when she asked me to come back and sing to her again and again after that—usually when she was in the most pain. On those nights, she did something really strange. At least it seemed strange then.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s that?” Zu asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She asked me to hold a small white stone with black spots while I sang for her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli and Oriette gasped in unison again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu slid around to face me then, taking me by the shoulders. “Nori, this could be a very, VERY good thing for us right now,” she said. “Show me exactly how you sang for her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” I asked, my throat suddenly feeling very tight and dry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Show me. Sing for me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, now,” Zuli said. “There’s no better place to sing than a cave. The acoustics are perfect.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but just because Gram and Uncle Jack—and maybe James—thought I was good doesn’t mean I actually am.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli huffed impatiently. “Listen Nori, being a siryn in Cobbogoth means you’re not just a really great singer; it means you’re a great storyteller too—a storyteller who, with the help of a siryn stone, can transport an entire audience to a different time and place in their minds…lit-er-a-lly. The stone Gramillia asked you to hold sounds like a siryn stone to me. She probably kept asking you to come sing to her because you helped her escape her pain for a little while.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette nodded, her corkscrew curls bouncing every which way. “Siryns are really rare.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I breathed, nodding. “So just give me a day to gather myself and prepare something. Then you can tell me for sure.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The talent and technique portion of the Tableunia is in two days, Nori. There’s no time for that.” Zu squeezed my shoulders. “You’ve got to sing for us now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed hard…then swallowed again and again, hoping I wasn’t about to be sick. I closed my eyes. For James and the gloriole, I reminded myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally I let out all my breath and, willing the sudden trembling in my limbs to stop, slowly rose to my feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu and Oriette both clapped excitedly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sing whatever you want,” Zu said, eyes bright. “If we have to, we can choose something more suitable later.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, took a deep breath, and closed my eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I decided to sing Gram’s favorite song—The Sacred Sleep—a song Uncle Jack had translated from an ancient Cobbogothian poem he’d found in the Caves of Aegissida at the same time he’d found The Legend of the Cobbogothians. After I’d been singing to Gram for a while, he’d contacted his friend from the Faroe Islands and had her put the poem to what he called “huldefolk music” just for Gram. After hearing Oriette chant and sing something similar, it was clear Uncle Jack was trying to give Gram the opportunity to revisit Cobbogoth one last time in the only way he could before she passed. It was just now that I was beginning to realize how much he’d really loved her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started out by slapping my leg to set the rhythm. I did a few ancient Scandinavian kulning or herding calls, which Zu was right, echoed perfectly off the cave walls. Then I began to sing The Sacred Sleep in ancient Cobbogothian.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I finished with what Uncle Jack called “the huldefolk chants” and then opened my eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As my voice continued to echo off the cave walls, I found Zuli and Oriette wiping tears from their cheeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s true,” Zuli sniffed. “You are a siryn. With the right song, right look, and a siryn stone, you’ll win this part of the Tableunia, Nori. I’m certain of it.”","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Trolly Row Pass","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"fb5fc94562a74da1b3a53ede3ed48e90"},{"title":"The Sacred Sleep","kind":"webp","pages":4,"id":"113cb1bfee5c4ef388da2d0a50dc991f"},{"title":"Siryn","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"3adc0ec73ee5426e9201bc107cc9184e"},{"title":"Gramillia","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"65f56e24fcb04767831be8ae6159118f"},{"title":"Journal: Siryn & Goldabung Foraging","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"8b083a11993c4ab2affb3170b887aa46"}]},{"n":56,"letterTitle":"The Goldabung Code","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"The Goldabung Code","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I’m just gonna say it. Palace guards are a real pain. Sure, I get that they’ve got to up the security for this Triolune thingy. I get that there are hundreds of thousands of strangers roaming the streets of Rasili right now. But do they have to be such jerks about it? I mean, we went to the palace to report that dogrils are stealing goldabungs from the streets of their city. The least they could do is say “thank you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As it was, we didn’t even get the chance to state our business before we were being carted off to the dungeons. I suppose it didn’t help that we only had three goldabungs with us out of the two dozen we’d started out with when we finally reached DonTon’s palace gates. The rest had scattered into the crowded streets on our way there. The guard on duty took one look at us “foreigners” and tried to turn us away lickety-split.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We probably would’ve been okay if he hadn’t picked my little buddy Mica up by the collar and threatened to sentence him to a life in something called the troll quarries simply for having missed his duties the past two weeks. Like it was the little guy’s fault or something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I decided I’d had enough, and my fire came out. I mean, mica’s only ten. What would you have done?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unfortunately, I’ve been learning something pretty important about these Cobbogothians during my short time here. They do not like flame throwers. And they definitely don’t trust them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Listen, dude,” I said to the guard as he shoved Glynt and then me into a high security rodãor he’d been carrying in a pouch around his neck. “I know you’ve got to do your job, but we’ve got some pretty important information for your kinchief.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The guard didn’t respond, just simply left the rodãor and sealed us inside for the unforeseeable future.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sheesh! Are all metal melders like this?” I asked Glynt who was taking everything in his usual calm, collected manner.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You know, I don’t know,” he replied distractedly. “I don’t have much experience with them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well,” I shrugged. “At least the goldabungs got away.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt didn’t respond this time. He was too busy examining our prison cell. “Fascinating. I’ve always wanted to see the inside of one of these,” he said. “They’re called fangledãors.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced around for the first time and was pretty shocked. The only way to describe it would be to call it a windowless, golden box of a room. I gave a low whistle. “It’s definitely a lot of gold.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Apparently, they combine the gold with fanglecite, the same stone the dogrils were using in the grimcages. It creates a virtually impenetrable rodãor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stellar,” I said. “Just where I hoped we’d end up when we decided to help those kyndlin. Why’d they run off on us like that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’re goldabungs, Jamus,” Glynt replied with a shrug. “They look after themselves first. It’s part of their code. The only reason Mica and the other two came with us to the palace is because they work here. But fear not, I don’t think our young friends will leave us in here for long.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean?” I asked. “I thought you said this place was impenetrable.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I did, but repaying debts is also part of the goldabung code.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How do you know so much about them anyway?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because I was one of them. Not here in Rasili, of course, but orphans are relatively the same no matter where you go in or out of Cobbogoth.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Huh…well,” I said, hunkering down on an intricately carved golden bench along one of the walls of the room, “I’m sorry to hear that. What happened to your parents?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt waved away my condolences. “Like so many of our goldabung friends, I don’t know. I never knew my parents. I was raised by the elders of my kin-klan, which led to our friendship. So you see, it all worked out in the end.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I said, returning his smile. I got the sense he didn’t want to talk about this part of himself anymore, so I changed the subject. “Well, I suppose as prisons go, this one ain’t terrible.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” Glynt agreed. “Your last one was much worse.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll have to take your word for it,” I said. “All I remember is drinking a lot of rock potion…and feeling a lot of pain.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We also played a lot of Callowynder,” Glynt chuckled. “You actually became quite good—almost beat me once or twice.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nah…I definitely beat you,” I said. “The memories are foggy, but I’m certain of that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt chuckled again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What were you talking to the goldabungs about on our way here?” I asked after a while. “The older kids seemed to trust you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I was asking them questions about what happened to them here,” he said. “There’s something about this red-haired lady that warrants attention. What were you talking to the little kyndlin about? Mica seemed especially worshipful.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know,” I shrugged. “I guess I just told them funny stories.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt shook his head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? They thought I was a riot.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before Glynt could respond, a ring of light appeared on the far wall, and the rodãor was opened again. We both tensed, looking at one another.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That was quick,” I said as the same guard who’d imprisoned us came into the gold prison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kinchief DonTon demands your presence,” he said. With a flick of his right hand, our wrists were encircled by fancy looking handcuffs. Then he escorted us out of the fangledãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Any ideas how we might get ourselves out of this fix?” I asked Glynt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Possibly,” he murmured. “Let’s see what we’re facing first.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few moments later, we found ourselves entering a much larger, much more golden throne room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“These guys really like their gold, huh?” I muttered as the guard marched us down a long, heavy, brocade carpet of silver, gold, and copper. It took a few minutes before we finally reached the throne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A big, black-skinned guy with metallic tattoos crawling up his neck and part of his face, looking like he could throttle Glynt and me in one go, sat on the throne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“DonTon, I presume?” I whispered to Glynt when the guard was far enough ahead of us. He gave a quick, sharp nod.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started to feel a little uneasy until I spotted my little pal Mica standing at the right arm of DonTon’s throne. I winked at him, and he grinned, giving me a little wave. Maybe Glynt was right about these goldabungs and their code.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were situated in front of DonTon’s throne, the guard pushed us to our knees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The metal kinchief didn’t speak right away. He just sat there observing us for a long time. When my knees started to ache from the hard, golden floor, DonTon finally spoke.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who are you?” He demanded. His larger-than-life voice boomed through the opulent hall, and under different circumstances, I got the sense he was actually a pretty jovial guy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let me,” Glynt mumbled to my right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“BE MY GUEST.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your honorable Metallican,” Glynt began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My brain stumbled on his last word. Did he just say metallica?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“May the eye of Gihara ever be upon you,” Glynt continued.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A familiar chorus automatically started in my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your names,” DonTon demanded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sleep with one eye open. Gripping your pillow tight.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Glynt and Jamus,” Glynt replied. “We come to you with disturbing news, your Metallican.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Exit light! Enter night! Take my haaaaand.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. Mica, my page here, tells me you rescued him and several other goldabungs from a pack of dogrils?” He inquired.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We’re off to never-neverland. Boom-ba-do-do-do…boom-ba-do-do-do…","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Oh man, this was great news! I’d have to tell Glynt about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of…I jerked back to the present when Glynt elbowed me in the ribs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Er—yeah, I mean yes, your highness—er—metallica, sir,” I fumbled. “We found them being caged up in grimcages in the forest of Dag.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“CAGED UP?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” Glynt said. “It appeared the dogrils were preparing to haul them away somewhere. To Holictac, most likely.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Holictac?” DonTon asked, examining Glynt more closely. He turned his eyes back on me. There was a suspicious look there I didn’t like very much.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You two don’t sound like you’re from around here,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, your metallica,” I said, hoping he didn’t ask for details.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s metallic-an,” Glynt corrected me out of the corner of his mouth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right, Metallican, forgive me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Boom-ba-do-do-do…boom-ba-do-do-do…","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Where are you from, then?” He pressed. “I know from what my guard tells me that you,” he pointed at me, “are a flame Thrower. This, of course, makes it very hard to believe a word you’ve said.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I really didn’t have a comeback for that, so I didn’t say anything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Jamus is a good man,” Glynt said. “He’s new to these parts—not from one of the southern covyns.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You may be right, master Glynt, but I’m afraid I don’t know you either. You’ll forgive me if I’ve learned not to trust strangers while there are dogrils smuggling kyndlin out of my city,” DonTon said. “How am I to know you’re not both in league with the culprits?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just then Mica leaned over and whispered in DonTon’s ear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It was a red-haired lady who lured the children from Rasili and into the dogrils’ traps?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Neither of us have ever been to your city,” I said, “so we can’t confirm or deny that. But yeah, that’s what Mica and the other goldabungs told us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They said they were taken to a cave at the back of your volcano,” Glynt added. “And that’s where the dogrils collected them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you know this cave?” DonTon asked Mica.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The older goldabungs said they were blindfolded before they were taken there,” Glynt interjected.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mica nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who blindfolded you?” DonTon asked the kynd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mica hesitated. He actually looked pretty scared to say more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Was it this red-haired lady?” DonTon pressed gently. It was clear he liked the kynd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mica shook his head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then who?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The little tyke stood on his tippy toes and whispered in DonTon’s ear again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon sat, watching us thoughtfully for a long time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Father, if I may?” A voice asked from behind us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Until then, I was pretty sure there was no one else but DonTon, Mica, the guard, Glynt, and me in the throne room. But from behind a heavy golden curtain at the back of the throne room came a third person—obviously the prince.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yeesh! And a pretty boy prince, at that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah, syd!” DonTon boomed. “Glad you’ve come, kynd. Join us. Join us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pretty boy approached the throne and crouched down beside Mica.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, that’s right, kynd, tell him everything you’ve told me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mica retold the whole story, from him and the others being captured—apparently they’d been stolen in their sleep, blindfolded and then taken to the cave—to our finding them in the forest of Dag and escorting them back home.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mica here tells me these two can be trusted, but one’s a flame Thrower and the other is…what are you?” DonTon asked Glynt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nothing of consequence, I’m afraid,” Glynt answered cooly. Which was definitely not true.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You have no manifestation?” DonTon pressed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not any I’d care to disclose to you after the prejudice I’ve witnessed against my friend here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I must assume you are a flame Thrower too, then,” DonTon said impatiently. “Or something more dangerous.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If you must,” Glynt said with a smile. “Assume away.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Take it easy, pal,” I murmured under my breath. The last thing we needed was to tick this Metallican guy off even more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Boom-ba-do-do-doom…","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, ‘innocent until proven guilty’ is what you’ve always taught me?” Pretty boy said to his father.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, but how can I be certain they too aren’t part of this trafficking of the goldabungs?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pretty boy shrugged. “Why not give them the opportunity to prove their innocence?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon stroked his beard, fingering a few of the metallic beads strung through his wyndaloos. “What did you have in mind?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We need to discover who’s behind all this, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” DonTon agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But we can’t let them go in case they themselves are in league with this red-haired lady.” Pretty boy began pacing in front of his father’s throne, surveying us with impartial interest. “They’re obviously loyal to one another, so why not use that to our advantage?” He lowered his voice then, so we couldn’t hear anymore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As pretty boy spoke, DonTon’s brow furrowed, and he continued to stroke his beard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced over at Glynt. He was eyeing me as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It is a good plan, syd,” DonTon finally said. He sounded pleased as well as proud of his son. Then he rose to his feet. If I thought he was big before, it was nothing compared to what I thought now. This guy was enormous!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon picked up a mallet and struck a golden gong…thingy. Two more guards entered the throne room from hidden passages in the walls. The Metallican pointed at me. “Take him to captain kham. Provide him with whatever resources he needs to discover who has been stealing the goldabungs. But tell kham to watch him carefully.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The guards nodded, approaching and hoisting me to my feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was good they were on both sides of me because my knees almost buckled from kneeling for so long.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And this one?” Asked the guard who’d originally captured us, gesturing to Glynt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Return him to the fangledãor.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait, what?” I said, as the guard immediately seized Glynt. I looked at him. “Say something, will ya!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Glynt just shook his head. “It’s up to you now, my friend.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon looked directly at me. “I hope you have a plan, flame Thrower. Fangledãors have been known to get lost in the troll quarries if they stay occupied too long.” He nodded, and I watched as Glynt was escorted back into the rodãor prison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You have until the end of Triolune to discover who is behind this,” DonTon said to me. “Try to flee Rasili before then, and your friend dies. Fail to identify and apprehend this red-haired woman…and you both die.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, the guard who’d escorted Glynt returned to the throne room, sealing the fangledãor and placing it back into the pouch around his neck.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon nodded to the two guards flanking me, and they began to escort me across the throne room toward the wall panels they’d come through.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So…as days went, this wasn’t the best I’d ever had. Then again, it wasn’t the worst either. Thankfully Glynt knew that. He also knew I wouldn’t leave him in prison longer than absolutely necessary. I glanced back, taking one last look at the fangledãor guard’s face, memorizing it. If all else failed, I’d be paying that guy a visit in the middle of the night some time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As for right now, I was gonna meet this kham fella and get what I needed. Then I was going hunting for goldabungs. It was time to call in their debts.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"DonTon","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4a0dfd7c8cb04686a67b336d63db58d5"},{"title":"DonTons Inner Palace","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"32de8324eaa24ebaae15dc03530e99d4"},{"title":"Journal: Fangledaor & The Goldabung Code","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"52486e79ac0643a194e3c4b68804c3bd"}]},{"n":57,"letterTitle":"Round One: Froymeffyn and the Blood of Phitessnie","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"Round One: Froymeffyn and the Blood of Phitessnie (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Lev, here. I keep forgetting you have no idea what an arena-level Callowynder match looks like. I bet you’re hoping I’ll fill you in a bit.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well…I don’t mind if I do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, I’m just wrapping up the first round, called froymeffyn right now. The goal here is to gather as much elemental energy as possible to see me through rounds two through five. What I accomplish in this stage is crucial. Sure, I got a lot of elementum donations from the crowd after Lunevergence, but froymeffyn is still vital. The main objective is to capture and bond with as many elemies as I can. Since this is the Wyn tournament, I need to connect with at least four elemies, one from each of my four different manifestations.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter’s been my mentor for four years now, right? So it’s no shock he hasn’t let me have my own elemie yet. He insists I need to master all my powers first. What he doesn’t get, being a monowyn, is having an elemie would actually make it easier for me to control my other three manifestations. But trying to convince Pieter of anything he didn’t come up with himself is like trying to ride an electric eel across the Glindune desert.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ah well. Speaking of Pieter, he’s been acting real strange since we got to Rasili. I’ve spent my whole life trying to avoid him—just to keep him off my back—but now I can’t seem to find him anywhere. It’s pretty zyrky, honestly. If there’s one thing I know about my father, it’s that he never misses a chance to criticize. I’m his favorite target, so with all the ways I’ve messed up lately, you’d think he’d be around to comment. But I don’t even know if he showed up today.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since my dad’s nowhere to be found, Lylend has been coaching me instead, which is incredible, but I can’t shake the feeling he’s holding back—maybe trying to spare my feelings or something? The upside of being my father’s apprentice (can’t believe I just wrote that) is the endless stream of “ways to improve” he points out. I suppose I’m just used to Pieter, who never really worries about hurting anyone's feelings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend definitely has feedback though. He’s just keeping it to himself. Or at least from me. Two days ago, I overheard him asking Iolyt if she had any stones that could help me out today. Not exactly a vote of confidence. Iolyt didn’t have any…yet. So today might be my last shot to prove I can do this without extra help. I know Callowynder inside and out; I've been playing since I could set up the board.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I’ve got this, you know? I mean, I’ve only been in the arena for froymeffyn four and a half days and have already captured and bonded with three of my four elemies: A troll named myr from a smelting forest—unpleasant and sweltering; a nymph named lyraville, lured from a sorrowstream—depressing and muddy; and a zyrker named tyloryn, wrestled down a stampelanche—zyrkin' amazing! In the end, I just have to keep trusting that eighteen years of obsession with Callowynder will kick in every time I step into the arena. Sometimes you just have to dive in, you know?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The only downside to this round is I’ve saved the worst for last. Right now I’m waiting to capture and bond with my final elemie…a Sylphid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I bet you’ve never tried to capture a Sylphid. Nori tells me humans don’t believe sylphids are real. If only that were true. If you haven’t figured it out since being introduced to Shudder, sylphids are pretty much the worst. And I’m a mostly imperturbable guy, so you know that’s the truth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First off, they're always ornery. Shudder isn’t a rare exception among his kind, you know. Cantankerousness defines sylphids. Unless, of course, you’re dealing with a zephyrphyid. If I’m lucky, I might encounter one of those today. What’s a zephyrphyid, you ask? Just a more hyperactive and mischievous type of Sylphid, usually female. So if you're me, you've got a better shot at charming one of those into behaving over the regular kind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I wouldn’t say zephyrphyids are cheerful exactly, but they’re bearable at least. Even if their constant fidgeting can drive you zyrky. Thankfully these are only temporary elemies, and I’ll only be bonded to them for the next few weeks, although I’m gonna look into keeping tyloryn 'cause he’s something else. But as far as the sylphids go, I’d much prefer a hyper Sylphid over an ornery one.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So here I am, perched on the edge of a vast fairy canyon unlike anything I've ever encountered. It’s deep and narrow, shrouded in mist with acid green moss carpeting the rocks from cascading waterfalls. Birds flit in and out of the canyon, settling into their nests while I wait for the thunderveil to sweep by again. I know the Sylphid is lurking inside there. Lylend's advice echoes in my mind: To catch a Sylphid, I need a sparklash—a lightning lasso that lets me ensnare it without entering the thunderveil. Things can get pretty dicey in there, especially if multiple sylphids are at play.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As the thunderveil looms closer, all I can think about is how much I wish Zu had arrived in time for my good luck kiss. She stayed away to dodge her mother, who seems to be everywhere these days.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A gust of wind tousles my hair, sending a jolt of electricity up my spine. I glance down the canyon, where a massive waterfall splits into two and a Mirage shimmers in the rainbow mist. Lylend warned me to watch for this—a sure sign the thunderveil is gathering more power.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Time to prepare.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I focus on the Mirage, pulling electricity from my blood to my left arm, which sparks with energy as it snakes down my wrist into a thick sparklash. I’m still riding tyloryn—my favorite elemie: Headstrong, fast, and seriously defiant, which is awesome. Our bond is strong; I’ve secured lyraville and myr safely in my rodãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As the wind howls through the canyon, I urge tyloryn to dig his feet deep into the rock beneath us. He snorts and tosses his head but complies, anchoring us against the cliffside. That’ll hopefully keep me grounded until it’s time to move with the thunderveil.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The mist from the waterfall is rapidly forming into a thick fog. I can hardly see the canyon beyond. Soon, flashes of light and deafening cracks echo off the canyon walls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knot my sparklash.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You ready for the ride of your life, tyloryn?” I ask, feeling the zyrker tense beneath me. He responds with a snort that sends up a cloud of dust—this is shaping up to be an epic moment in my Callowynder career.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I can’t help but smile; these are the moments I live for, the ones that make Callowynder the greatest game ever played.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rotating my wrist, I start the swing and flick motion Lylend had me practicing all week. Soon, my sparklash spins so fast it becomes a solid disk of light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The thunderveil is really roiling down the canyon now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I breathe in the musty air of dust and rain and moss.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then a massive flash of lightning spiders like veins through the storm cloud. There’s a deafening crack of thunder, and I catch my first sight of a sylpid’s shadow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I whoop and press my knees into tyloryn’s sides. “Here we go, kynd! Let’s catch us a wind bag!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The solid rock beneath us starts to roil like the clouds ahead, and tyloryn begins gliding along the cliff face, churning the earth and rapidly gathering speed the closer we get to the thunderveil.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Again, the cloud erupts with lightning, and just as we reach a breakneck speed, tyloryn leaps off the cliff and into the void, and we soar toward the storm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I let my sparklash fly, then dig deep for the earth in my blood. With all my might, I pull from the heels of my boots, willing tyloryn to do the same.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We begin losing altitude then, freefalling toward the chasm below, but not before a pillar of rock thrusts up from the canyon floor, catching us. At almost the exact same instant, I feel my sparklash catch on something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Like a shyntfish on a hook, a struggle erupts on the other end, and I know I’ve got one.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dig in, tyloryn!” I shout, grasping the sparklash tighter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then I start reeling in the Sylphid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hand over hand, muscles straining, I pull. Tyloryn digs in deeper, until his belly grazes the top of the pillar. The Sylphid thrashes from inside the thunderveil, straining to break loose.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This is where all my training with Lylend really kicks in. Leaving just enough give, I pull on the sparklash, then let it go. Then I pull again. The trick is to coax the Sylphid out of the thunderveil without breaking the sparklash’s connection or hurting the creature. No point starting off on the wrong foot if I can help it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s patience and wrist technique, kynd,” I hear Lylend say.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I repeat this process over and over while the thunderveil swirls around me, threatening to drop a funnel on the ground and become a full-blown tornado. I have to avoid that at all costs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no you don’t!” I grunt, gripping tyloryn tighter with my aching knees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn snorts and groans, and I feel stone creep up my legs as he braces me to his side.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good, kynd!” I manage to grind out through clenched teeth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then, after putting up one of the better fights I’ve had with a Sylphid, the creature screams like a banshee, and it’s free of the thunderveil.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Immediately, the storm begins to diminish. I’ve caught a female. And boy is she angry! So much for hoping for a less cantankerous one.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I wrack my brain for the names I prepared before she can break free. The two female options sit on the tip of my tongue, when a second banshee cry pierces the air, and a second Sylphid breaks free of the storm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I give another whoop! “Will you look at that, tyloryn! We caught two of 'em!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It’s unheard of to catch two elemies during froymeffyn! Unheard of! And yet here I am; two of them in one sparklash! Even Pieter might be impressed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pointing at the first Sylphid, I yell the first name, “aelyra!” Then I point to the second, “and you’re feylisse!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Instantly, the sylphids calm. The thunderveil vanishes, and the canyon disappears. After nearly a week in the arena, I suddenly find myself surrounded by the arena filled with countless cheering Cobbogothians. The aurethryn is all that separates us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To say the crowd is going zyrky is a massive understatement. I dismount tyloryn and open my rodãor. I summon the other elemies I’ve captured, preparing to—I don’t know, take a Bow and a victory lap around the arena—something to rile my fellow Cobbogothians up even more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I can, though, I happen to glance up at the aurethryn. What I see confuses me, but only for a moment. Then the horror settles in. It’s not my face grinning proudly down at the masses from the sky above. Instead, the face is terrible and beautiful…and positively leering down at us all. It’s the magnified, wild face of Seraphyna—of Seraphyna and her ten elemies—that’s right, ten! (Two for all five of her manifestations.) I don’t think the crowd even realizes I’m here with my measly five. All they can see is Seraphyna.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I realize they’re chanting her name.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My heart feels like tyloryn is standing on it as I skip the victory lap and make my way back to my tent—back to the disappointed faces of other Hood members. Even Pieter’s there to greet me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I told you all that time fiddling with Callowynder was a waste of life,” he spits out. “Maybe next time you’ll listen to me before humiliating yourself in front of all of Cobbogoth.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Good old Pieter. When the whole world’s crashing down on you, you can always count on him to stand on your head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I feel a hand squeeze my shoulder. “Don’t mind him, libkyn,” Iolyt says softly. “I’ve never been prouder of anyone in all my life.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reach up and squeeze her hand in return. Thank the Gihara for Iolyt.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Round One: Froymeffyn and the Blood of Phitessnie (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"“Must you bound so enthusiastically, old friend?” I asked Brawlo as we soared over the Roklin marshlands, heading toward the Glindian sea.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"YES. MUST. IN CITY TOO LONG.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I groaned as he bounded again, my ancient bones taking the full impact of each unbridled leap. Yes, 10,000-year-old Cobbogothian bones do tend to manifest wear and tear, human. Particularly bones that have been broken and mended as many times as mine have.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was one benefit to riding this giant weasel, though; while hoptioning was still off limits to us, it was a much faster way to travel. And if there was one thing we needed on our side right now, it was speed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After Lev took second in the froymeffyn round of the Triolune tournament, having happened hot on the heels of his being outshone by Seraphyna during Lunevergence, I knew it was time to do a bit more research. Thankfully, I had a valuable source, courtesy of Gramillia and her sacrifice during Bruxrac when she posed as a flame Thrower in the hostile Glindune desert. The Phoenix was a mysterious source, true, and possibly a dangerous one, but beggars can’t be choosers, and the Phoenix hadn’t failed me yet in providing vital information where events smacking suspiciously of Cifer’s tampering were concerned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before heading to Rasili—before I’d even planned to travel to the metal kingdom—the Phoenix had advised me to do so. Her final messages to me after finding the Lemorian Crest—nearly losing Princess Zuli and Noriah in the process—urged me to head to the Triolune festival but to proceed with caution. It was what inspired me to begin looking at the metal kingdom for keys in the first place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of course, we would’ve turned our attention there eventually. We were certain there was a metal key. But DonTon wasn’t the easiest kinchief to gain access to, Nor was he considered the most cooperative. So Rasili wouldn’t have been my first choice to go looking for Lemorian keys. The fact that the Phoenix suggested it and even set up a new Unda tree for us to exchange messages from at the edge of the Roklin marshlands made it seem like the most viable option. Of course, the fact that I’d had no luck finding clues concerning any of the other keys yet inevitably left us with no other options.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it was the phoenix’s specific warning that things at the Triolune festival weren’t what they seemed that had me galloping over the Roklin marshlands in search of more information now. It was time to locate this second Unda tree and renew my correspondence with the mysterious Phoenix.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As soon as the boy Tolliver from DonTon’s palace told me Barakyne was sighted multiple times throughout the northeast country, I’d made up my mind to find the tree and ask the Phoenix what she might’ve heard about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Phoenix seemed to have a peculiar omniscience when it came to what was happening in Cobbogoth. Hopefully she would have information concerning this Seraphyna.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After all, a quintowyn had never been heard of in Cobbogoth before, not as far as I knew in all the history of our realm. All the ones who claimed to be ended up actually being something else entirely, even during Phitessnie’s days in the Olden realm. Back then, one was simply either a monowyn or the Opalian eye. There was nothing in between.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And yet, Gihara! Wyns were such a new phenomenon, only appearing after Bruxrac. Who knew what else was possible?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But there was something more about this Seraphyna—something ancient and familiar I couldn’t quite put my finger on. That’s what I had to get to the bottom of. In the long run, the fate of Cobbogoth could depend on it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally reached the forest of Dag, thanks to Brawlo’s “enthusiastic bounding,” I was more than exhausted. To be honest, I hadn’t been sleeping well the past week or so. The boy Tolliver had been to the Hood’s camp outside Rasili to see me twice since our first meeting. He’d reported hearing of two more Barakyne sightings from visitors to the city. Goldabungs were blessedly resourceful rascals.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"During his second visit, however, Tolliver reported on more than a Barakyne sighting. He shared a little palace gossip as well, and it had been disturbing my sleep ever since. He spoke of two men—strangers who had arrived at the palace recently. They’d come with several dozen goldabungs that had gone missing and came bearing remarkable tidings. They’d reported coming across a pack of dogrils in the northern forest of Dag, building several grimcages, where dozens of goldabungs were being held captive. The two men believed the dogrils were almost certainly preparing to fly the goldabung kyndlins back to Holictac.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If young tolliver’s tidings didn’t take me back ten thousand years to when Cifer first became a menace to the Cobbogothians, I don’t know what else could. Unfortunately, when Phitessnie paid the blood sum to stop his brother the first time, we’d all been ushered into this new realm so quickly, there was no time to properly investigate what Cifer had actually wanted with all those kyndlin he stole. So while these tidings felt eerily familiar, I had no more insight into what Cifer was planning than I did 10,000 years ago.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hence, the third reason for Brawlo’s and my “bounding” down to the northern edge of the forest of Dag to visit the phoenix’s Unda tree.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took a few hours of searching, but when I finally located the tree marked with the phoenix’s symbol, Brawlo stood guard at the base of the tree while I, wearing a Sina cuff Princess Zuli had loaned me, entered the tree’s water-filled root system.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once inside the most bulbous part of the roots—a hollow tuber about the size of the main room in my rodãor—I located a familiar, small shelf that had been carved out of one of the walls. It was also marked by the phoenix’s symbol. Surprisingly, there was already a crystal scroll waiting for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After leaving my own scroll full of inquiries about Barakyne, Seraphyna, and the kidnapped goldabungs, I made my way back out of the root system to where Brawlo was waiting.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As soon as I was fully dry again, I opened the phoenix’s latest missive and read it. Curious, Brawlo leaned into my back to discover its contents as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the end, I wished I’d kept the phoenix’s seer-like news to myself until after we’d returned to Rasili. If I thought the enthusiastic bounding on the way down was jarring, it paled in comparison to Brawlo’s unbridled jubilation on the way back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet having seen the crystal scroll’s contents with my own eyes, human, how could I possibly blame him?","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Froymeffyn","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2cf11ecc52164c308a3d320e823222d9"},{"title":"Journal: Froymeffyn","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"1b00187d0f3e4fc589be7c92fb352102"},{"title":"Spy Note","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"5941011ff1b245c280ae16a128f4782f"},{"title":"Brawlo","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"c8201fcb2995446c9c089bd03b730633"},{"title":"Decoded Message","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Have you heard the news? Barakyne has been sighted near the Triolune Festival. My sources also report the following sightings:","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"West of the Gliplin Range","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"West of the Unda Swamp","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"It would appear the blood of Phitessnie has returned.","i":true}],"narrator":""}]},{"n":58,"letterTitle":"The Siryn’s Setback","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"The Siryn’s Setback","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I wish you could’ve been there to hear Nori sing. *Squeal* and to see her in the shyntara I designed for her. No doubt about it, the kyndie is a siryn. There wasn't a dry eye in the arena. Of course, being human, you wouldn't have experienced it in quite the same way, but it still would’ve been an unforgettable experience for you.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the sake of time, we decided to stick with the sacred sleep for nori's song. Lylend hired some musicians to accompany her, and with the help of a siryn stone from Iolyt, it really couldn't have gone any better.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since you've never experienced a siryn's song—a Cobbogothian siryn's song, not one of your Olden realm siren songs; they sound terrifying—I'll try to paint the picture for you. Hearing a siryn sing is like an out-of-body experience. I had to hide in the back of the crowd so mother wouldn't see me, but since I was in hearing range, even I got to experience it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Nori stood up, she looked absolutely paralyzed with terror. Thanks to Iolyt and me, she also looked absolutely fabulous (see my design for her shyntaras for the first two categories of the Tableunia). Once she opened her mouth, however, the music was all anyone cared about. It transported all of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sacred sleep is traditionally sung at memorials and death celebrations. I know those two words together—death and celebration—are an odd combination for you humans. Nori has told me that for the most part you fear death. Which is understandable, I suppose. You have so little time to do and learn and grow and experience. That, and the fact that some of you actually believe this life is the end. Preposterous notion, of course. But alas, you are human and therefore must be nearsighted in the scope of…pretty much everything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, the reason the sacred sleep is so…well, sacred to us is because it allows us the chance to see our loved ones again. We don't get to speak with them or interact, of course. We just get to take a peek at them—at where they are now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hearing a siryn sing the sacred sleep—or any song, for that matter—is an individual experience for each person, hence the deep emotions it evoked for so many the other night. Even I was able to see my seven times great-grandmother, grammillia, again. Lylend saw his Alobuenath. Iolyt saw her parents. Every member of the audience was able to catch a glimpse of a late loved one, well and happy in the realm of the Gihara. And all because of Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To say Nori impressed the magistrates is a vast understatement. After all, we haven't had a known siryn in Cobbogoth for centuries. Certainly never in my lifetime. They'd practically faded into legend. The only reason I even knew what they were is because I'm an Adolorian princess, and as such, we've historically had more siryns born into our kin-klan than any other. Something to do with DNA tampering, I guess.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After the talent and technique portion of the Tableunia was over, we used our newly acquired—thanks to Sydian—hearthurite shards (see stone card) to return to our quarters in donton's palace, and you would not believe the crystal scrolls that began pouring in from every corner of Rasili. They came from royalty (Prince Sydian, of course) all the way down to goldabungs, relaying how nori's singing had transported them. It was remarkable, really.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Consequently, nori's been invited to sing at everything from weaving ceremonies (I believe you humans call them weddings) to soul gate ceremonies (birthdays), to the very rare yvanie-I-giharas that occasionally take place among those fortunate enough to inherit a Qualdrine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I absolutely zyrked about it. Not a single other contestant came close to Nori in scores for talent. She is leading the others by eons. And Gihara willing (and as long as my mother doesn't discover I'm involved), she'll continue leading them by eons.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tonight is the diplomacy portion of the Tableunia, and if I said I wasn't starting to get a bit nervous, I'd be lying. Nori was supposed to show up nearly an hour ago so we could go over questions, but the arena is beginning to fill up already, and she's nowhere in sight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She'll be here,” Lylend assured me. “She's personally got too much riding on winning this thing not to.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, but tonight is different,” Iolyt interrupted. “Singing a memorized song is one thing, but giving candid answers to questions she's never heard before and then performing a manifestational demonstration to support her answers is quite another entirely. Are you sure you prepared her well enough, Zuli?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, believe me,” I assured them all, “an exorbitant amount of pain, sweat, and tears—hers and mine—have gone into preparing her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So as long as we don't have another Seraphyna show up out of the blue, she'll be fine,” Lev said beside me. There was a hint of forced cheerfulness in his tone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced over at my kinzurine. I was really starting to miss the old, imperturbably optimistic levy. He hadn't been the same since froymeffyn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I put my hand in his and squeezed. I couldn't help feeling guilty for not being at the first round of the Triolune tournament. He understood why I wasn't there, of course, but my kiss was his good luck charm. It always had been, and I knew things hadn't gone the way he'd hoped. He'd done amazingly, of course. Seraphyna had just done better. A lot better. It wasn't fair really. Seraphyna had to be old enough to be lev's mother. It was horrible watching him beat himself up like this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter was no help at all, either. Thankfully he wasn't around much lately. Any time he was, he made it a point to discourage Lev from continuing to compete. It was almost like he wanted his son to fail. As for me, I just wanted to claw pieter's and seraphyna's eyes out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the meantime, all our hopes rested on Nori. If she would just show up already. She was cutting it so close; even Prince Sydian had come down to our cell to inquire after her. She'd missed the banquet he'd hosted for all the contestants beforehand. Leave it to syd to take every opportunity his crown afforded him to “get to know” Nori—or Lunalyn Opalinya, as he knew her. His presence now only confirmed my suspicions that he was zyrky for her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We huddled in a contestant cell by the entrance of the Tableunia arena's aurethryn, eager to catch Nori when she arrived. This arena wasn't as grand as callowynder's—fewer people flocked to Tableunia—but it still held around 50,000 Cobbogothians, and it was filling up fast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where is she?” I whispered to Brawlo, who was sitting on his haunches beside Lev and me. I leaned my head against his shoulder. I didn't speak loud enough for the others to hear. No point in sending out a search party yet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo responded by infusing me with calm. *SHE COME. NO WORRY, PRINCESS. PROBABLY LOST.*","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But she's supposed to be with Oriette,” I whispered. “And now she has a hearthurite shard—a gold one. What if something happened? Will you go find her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"WOULD. NOT ALLOWED. DONTON NO LIKE “GIANT RODENTS” ROAMING PALACE.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo's hurt feelings nearly choked me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah, sweet kynd,” I said, hugging his front leg. “You're not a rodent. You're the sweetest, fastest, most loyal, smartest, handsomest…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hey! I'm standing right here,” Lev interjected with a guffaw.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo trilled at this, and I felt his spirits lift.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look!” Iolyt cried. “There she is!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned, and sure enough, Nori came rushing past our cell looking like…well, she definitely didn't look the way I'd left her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kyndie, wait!” Lylend hissed before she could charge blindly out into the aurethryn. “Not yet!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori spun, and by the look on her face, I could tell she'd had a time of it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What happened?!” I asked, as she rushed into our cell. She was out of breath, a portion of her diplomacy shyntara looked like it had been singed, some of her hair had escaped its coils, and she was sweaty. Extremely sweaty.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I got lost, Zu! Like really lost!” She panted, unsticking some of her hair from her damp forehead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lost?” Syd asked, a flash of alarm in his eyes. “Where is Oriette?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And why are you so sweaty?” I added.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori avoided meeting syd's gaze and headed toward me. She'd been avoiding him ever since he cornered her after the soireenya. “She disappeared! She was leading me through the tunnels when I ended up having an episode—you know, one of my Totherma visions?” She glanced at syd and lowered her voice so only Iolyt and I could hear. “Boy, was that terrible timing. It's been so long since she came to me in a vision. Why not just use a DMD instead? Isn't that why she's been drilling me so hard on them since flame Fleck island?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori barely paused to catch her breath. “Anyway, I finally came to, and oriette's gone. I have no idea where I am because I must've wandered while I was having the episode—probably scared the poor thing to death—and I ran into this really creepy dude down there. A guard, I think, because he at least told me I was in the guard's quarters. When I asked him for directions or to show me the way here, though, he was completely useless—didn't even seem to know what an aurethryn was!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The guard's quarters?” Syd stepped forward, taking nori's arm, as if to help her to a seat. “Did they do this to you?” He gestured to all of her. “Give me a description, and I'll see they're dismissed immediately.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Um…long ponytail, bushy eyebrows, sallow skin…” Nori began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he looked my way, I could only give syd an “I told you so” shrug. My mother had been trying to convince DonTon to domesticate the lower lava tubes of his volcano-turned-palace for decades. He never listened—didn't think the guards were worth the expense, and instead let them live in the raw lava tubes. Mother always said he'd regret not treating them better if his palace ever came under attack.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did he give you his name?” Syd pressed, oblivious to the fact Nori had gone back to ignoring him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was pretty sure she was in shock, though, so I took charge. “Okay. Okay. Take a deep breath and calm down,” I soothed, giving syd a warning look to stop talking. It wouldn't help calm her down. The last thing we needed was a riled up Nori heading out into the aurethryn for her first ever public interview. “Come sit down over here so we can fix you back up. Lylend, how many contestants are there to go before Lunalyn is up?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think there are two to go.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay. Iolyt?” Iolyt stepped forward, rifling through the shimmery leather pouch she'd begun carrying with her everywhere these days. Among other incredible new stone creations, it held her “beauty gems.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gave Nori a piece of aquafina to help her cool down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oooooh. That's nice,” she breathed as the sweat on her brow and upper lip instantly vanished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right,” I said, while repairing and rearranging her shyntara. “Now remember what's going to happen out there. After you get into the aurethryn, you're going to step into the crystal chamber with the magistrate. He's going to present you with a problem, and you have to demonstrate and explain—don't forget to explain—just how you would go about solving it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori nodded, but I could tell she wasn't really hearing me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced at Iolyt, who was lifting nori's chin and looking closely at her eyes. Her brow was furrowed in worry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt shook her head. “Later.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She was right. Now wasn't the time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly fixed nori's hair, tightening the stone beads in her wyndaloos. She earned her first when we retrieved the Lemorian Crest and a second after she saved us all, nearly losing her arm in the process. Just having two wyndaloos was impressive enough for the magistrates, so I always made sure they were prominently displayed when I styled her hair.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just trust your instincts, okay,” I continued, trying to give her a final boost of confidence. “You've got good ones, so if the answer feels right, go with it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori nodded, but again I could tell she wasn't hearing a word I was saying.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn,” I said, taking her face in my hands. “You're far enough ahead in the Tableunia that you can make mistakes, okay. Don't worry. It's going to be fine.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It's time,” Lev said, approaching. “I'll escort her out into the arena.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gently squeezed lev's arm. I'd been dreading this moment. “I actually think it would be best for Lylend to do it.” The last thing I wanted to do was give Lev any indication that I too lacked confidence in him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It's not what you think,” I explained when his face fell. “If my mother sees you with Nor—er—Lunalyn, she's much more likely to connect me to her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev nodded and stepped aside, but I could tell he didn't entirely believe me. Poor kynd. I'd make it up to him somehow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend stepped forward and offered Nori his arm. She stood and took it. She was put together again. She really did look beautiful…in her way—not as pale as usual, but still pale…er than Tableunia usual—but beautiful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just pretend you're having a regular conversation when you get out there,” I rushed. “And only use the techniques you're most comfortable with. Demonstrating fire and water will be pretty impressive though, so if you're feeling it, go for it. Just remember you're already ahead. And remember, the more natural you seem, the more the audience will like you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori nodded, but the look in her eyes still had me worried. “Don't forget to smile,” I reminded her as Lylend led her out of our cell and into the arena.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not unexpectedly, the crowd responded the moment they saw her face on the aurethryn ceiling, recognizing her as the siryn. Unlike Lev who relished the crowds roaring over him, Nori seemed to Sink a little further into herself. The poor thing did look a bit tired. It was a good thing she had three days after this to recover before the next event.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend left Nori standing with the magistrate near the crystal chamber and then returned to our cell to watch. We all turned our attention to the ceiling of the aurethryn and held our breaths.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The magistrate was a man about my mother's age. He greeted Nori, and she woodenly greeted him in return. He introduced her as Lunalyn Opalinya, the siryn of the Hood, and then stood aside for her to step into the chamber before him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The aurethryn was focused only on her face now. If it was possible, it looked like even more color had drained from her face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you think she's quite all right?” Iolyt asked beside me. “Do you think we should’ve stalled a little longer to make sure nothing more serious happened to cause her to get lost?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Her pupils were very dilated, and I'm sure I've never seen her so distracted.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mmm,” Lylend said once he returned. He came up beside us. “I agree. Something's off, Lyttie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The audience quieted, and the magistrate presented Nori with her challenge. In this portion of the Tableunia, she would be presented with an elemental problem a diplomat might face while executing their duties.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Clearing his throat, he began. “A volcano has erupted along the border of fire and water kin-klan territories, thanks to the fire nation's meddling. They aimed to harness lava for security after the water kin-klan reneged on a peace treaty to reroute glacier runoff into their arid desert. Instead, the eruption has triggered catastrophic flooding as the lava melts the nearby glacier. It has also ignited wildfires that are consuming the Unda trees and racing toward the city of stones, where queen citrine's Sina reside. How can both kin-klans emerge from this disaster better off than before?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Bless the Gihara! That was the perfect question for her,” I said, tears of relief welling in my eyes. All she needed to do was recount how she saved us from Cifer and his dogrils in phitessnie's blood grove a few months back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I returned to watching nori's face, however, I was shocked to see her staring blankly out into the crowd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, kyndie,” Lylend said under his breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori looked around distractedly, blinking several times as though she was wondering how she got there.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Something seems wrong with her,” Sydian said, concern scrunching his brow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You don't think she's about to have another episode, do you?” Lylend whispered to Iolyt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Episode?” Syd asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nothing to be concerned about, your Metallican,” Iolyt insisted with a tight smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Syd?” I said, taking his arm, “wouldn't you be more comfortable in your cell? Isn't it unfair to the other contestants for you to be here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Syd considered it, still watching nori's face on the aurethryn, then nodded slowly. “Just let me stay close by until I know she's all right. I'll stay just outside to avoid speculation.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded and led him outside before returning to the others.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don't know. She hasn't told me very much about her visions,” Iolyt was saying. “I thought she was done having them now that Totherma has taught her to use dark matter Doors.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Back in the aurethryn, the magistrate repeated the challenge for Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, this time she seemed to regroup.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Um…okay. So you've got two kin-klans angry at each other because a big volcano decides to erupt, melts a glacier, and now everybody's either on fire or drowning, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The magistrate's smile stayed frozen on his face. “That's one way of putting it, yes.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Great. Okay, so naturally, they're going to blame each other cause they are each other's antithesis elementally, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“YES.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori reached up, touching her forehead, and I swear I saw her give her head a little shake.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So, here's the move: You don't pick a side—you pick the steam. Fire plus water equals steam, and steam? Steam is power, right? You tell both kin-klans to quit bickering and start working together, channeling the forest fire and the melting glacier toward each other to start boiling the water and creating steam. Geothermal energy. They do it in Iceland all the time. Have this giant pipeline running all the way to reykjavik from their geysers.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The magistrate looked confused now, so Nori conjured a celadon icicle in one hand and a pale green fireball in the other. She brought them together and began to create steam, just the way she had when we were in the Starlyt sea cave…when she saved my life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara!” Sydian whispered in awe. “She's wonderful.” He was back in our cell and standing with his mouth open, eyes wide.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Syd!” I said. But he only had eyes and ears for Nori.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In the end,” Nori finished, as the crystal sphere they were in began to fog up, “flame throwers get to harness the volcano's power without burning everything down, while the water wielders get to control the floods and fulfill their peace treaty by making it rain for the fire nation. And all without throwing the whole southern hemisphere into atlantis mode. Everybody wins.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Perfect!” I said, clapping my hands. “That was perfect!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“SEE…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She's not done yet,” Iolyt whispered, looking worried.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uncle Jack once told me,” now Nori was beginning to slur her words, “politics is fifty percent problem-solving and fifty percent theater. You stage a big dramatic ritual—fire elders, water elders, you get 'em all together to summon a massive fog thingy…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"THINGY*?* Brawlo trilled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And not only does this literally cool things down, but it also makes everyone feel like they did something mystical and important. Which means egos will be soothed, and they'll stop fighting long enough to realize they can do much more together than they can apart.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Despite the slurring, that actually makes pretty good sense,” Lev said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“End result? Everybody wins. Fire gets fertile lands without having to go to war with the water wielders for breaking their peace treaty…and water gets infrastructure and flood control, and at the end of the day, physics does its thing and nobody dies.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is she drunk?” Pieter barked, having just stormed into the crystal cell. “Or is she just zyrky?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think she's actually doing well,” Lylend said coolly. “That steam cloud is quite advanced for someone with so little training.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The magistrates will overlook a great deal in favor of skill,” Sydian added with a scowl toward Pieter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The magistrates will take it into account,” I agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But her nerves are much worse tonight,” Iolyt conceded, biting her lip.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just then, Nori followed the magistrate out of the crystal sphere, but not before stumbling over her new shyntara.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, kyndie,” Pieter growled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori stumbled again. This time the magistrate reached out to catch her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a communal intake of breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When she'd balanced herself, she looked around the arena, almost like she just realized the crowd was there again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She opened her mouth as if to say something more, but the panic in her eyes didn't bode well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then…it wasn't words coming out of her mouth that was the problem.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oooooh!” Lev moaned in unison with the crowd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no. Oh please no,” I heard myself say.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah…poor kyndie,” Lylend sighed covering his eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get her out of there now,” Sydian said to me. “It's the only way to minimize the damage to her score!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo chirped and darted for the door. Sydian was hot on his heels, arriving just in time to catch Nori before she collapsed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In all honesty, as diplomacy portions of tableunias went, this wasn't the worst I'd heard of. During my mother's time, one of the contestants accidentally burned the magistrate to a crisp because of her nerves.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But being sick all over the magistrate? That wasn't good either.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I should’ve seen the signs from the moment she entered the cell,” Iolyt said as we rushed Nori back to our chambers.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Poison?” Lylend asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A stiff nod from Iolyt, and there wasn't a moment to lose. The Tableunia had just become deadly.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"Hearthurite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"5a4390ddd19a4624a54c3c41c45875af"},{"title":"Zulis Shyntara Designs","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"75e6465bd87d4304b10973d5ada03a0e"},{"title":"Urns Craft","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"51a37b9f5b9140159ac61181e4c48f35"},{"title":"Iolyt","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9b13305a139d4bcca95f1fa15a920d01"}]},{"n":59,"letterTitle":"Sydian’s Secrets","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Sydian’s Secrets (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"“You’re saying I actually barfed all over the guy?” I whispered when my head stopped threatening to explode.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yep, ’fraid so,” Lev said with a grin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And you’re smiling, why?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because now I’m not the only one who embarrassed myself in front of all of Cobbogoth,” he said with a smack on my back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I groaned, gripping my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev winced. “Sorry.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You never embarrassed yourself, though,” I countered. “Seraphyna is just really good. I, on the other hand, threw up all over a guy in front of 50,000 people.” I actually wanted to cry, and I would’ve if I didn’t think it would hurt my head more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stop teasing her, kynd,” Lylend said, coming forward with Brawlo. “Truth is, you were poisoned, kyndie. Once the crowd hears about this, they’ll be rooting for you more than ever.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“At least there’s that,” I said, feeling genuine relief. “At least it wasn’t my nerves that made me sick.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The good news is it wasn’t enough poison to do much more than make you sick for a few hours,” Iolyt assured me, reaching up and changing out some of the stones in the bryothynium hanging over me. We were in her qualdriny lab rather than my own rodãor, and I was lying in one of her healing cots.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In the meantime, there’s someone here who’s been waiting to see you,” Zuli said in a warning voice. “And please be extra nice to her. She feels terrible.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll think about it,” I groaned, lying back in the cot. “Who is it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli stepped aside, revealing a very sheepish looking little figure.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shot back up. “Oriette!” I cried, grabbing my head again. “Oh! I was so worried about you. Where’d you disappear to?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s what she wants to talk to you about,” Zuli said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I coaxed. “Go ahead, Oriette. Don’t worry. I’m not mad.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette trotted over to me and sweetly took my hand in hers. I could tell she’d been crying, and it made me feel even worse.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it libkyn?” Iolyt said gently.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s just…” She hesitated and tears welled up in her big, soulful eyes. “I was just so scared when she came out of the walls.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That wasn’t what I was expecting. “Who came out of the walls?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The lady,” Oriette’s voice quivered. “The scary lady with the red hair.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked to Zuli for some help as to what Oriette was talking about.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your vision,” Zuli explained. “I don’t think it was a vision after all because she saw it too.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You did?” I asked our little hostess. “Can you tell me what you saw?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette nodded, her curls bouncing around her little head. “We were heading toward the aurethryn when she came out of the wall. I’ve never seen her before, but I’ve heard the other goldabungs talk about her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What did she look like?” I asked. “Aside from the red hair.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She was tall and pretty…in a scary way. Her shyntara was green, like her eyes. Her eyes glowed, not like other Cobbogothians’ eyes. They glowed with something that made me think of fire.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Green eyes? Totherma’s were violet. How had I missed that detail? “What did she do?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She…” Oriette swallowed. “She scratched you. She used her fingernail, or something that looked like one, and scratched your chest. Right there.” Oriette pointed to a spot on my chest. “And then she disappeared back into the walls.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked down. Sure enough, slashed across the Haven tattoo just under my collar bone, was a scratch. It wasn’t terribly deep, but it did look angry, despite a clear shimmering salve Iolyt must’ve put on it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“After she did that, you started acting funny,” Oriette continued. “I tried to get you to come with me for help, but you wouldn’t come. You kept saying you had to follow her, so I ran for help on my own. But when I came back,” Oriette swallowed again, her little lip quivering, “you were gone. I looked and looked but couldn’t find you anywhere. But I was so scared I hid until Tolliver found me and told me what happened at the Tableunia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so very sorry,” she finished. “This was all my fault.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s just crazy talk,” I said, tapping Oriette’s little quivering chin with my thumb. “This is that lady’s fault—whoever she is—and nobody else’s.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette sniffed and managed a smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s right, libkyn,” Iolyt assured her. “She’ll make a full recovery by tomorrow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette managed to smile, then kissed the top of my hand adorably, and trotted over to take the hand Zuli held out to her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A moment later, the two of them were gone, and Lylend stepped back up to my cot. “There’s someone else here to see you, kyndie,” he said gingerly, “and I really think you ought to see him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For a moment, my heart stopped. Had it finally happened? Did Lylend finally find James?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why? Who is it?” I asked, closing my eyes to the sudden spinning.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s Prince Sydian,” Zuli said, returning to the room. “He’s waiting right outside.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not him,” I groaned. “I don’t mean to be rude, but isn’t it kinda weird he’s been following us around ever since I sang?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could just make out Lylend shooting a sideways glance at Iolyt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” Lev interjected, “it’s pretty weird.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev!” Zuli hissed, swatting him on the arm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? It is,” he shrugged. “You’d think princie-kynd would have better things to do.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you know what he wants?” I finally asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sure he just wants to know you’re okay,” Zu explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And I imagine he’s curious to ask you some questions so we can get to the bottom of who poisoned you,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“See,” Zuli said to Lev, “he’s just attending to his royal duties.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Either way,” Lylend continued, “it can’t hurt our cause to be hospitable—perhaps even welcoming of his attentions.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…sorry? Welcoming?” I said, turning slightly to my side to face him. “Why would I do that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because being in the good graces of the prince can only help our chances of securing the gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He’s not the one giving me the gloriole if I win.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Perhaps not,” Lylend said, but then he and Iolyt exchanged another look.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Something is up with you two, but my head hurts too much to make you tell me,” I said, fighting the urge to throw up again. “Is there any way to get rid of him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He needs answers, Nori,” Zuli said. “What if this lady is out poisoning anyone she comes across? What if she’s not? What if she specifically meant to poison you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She was probably just one of the other contestants,” I said, knowing immediately that wasn’t the case.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Or their mom,” Lev guffawed. “Who else would it be?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You could be right,” Zu said, “There’s a lot more riding on this Tableunia than usual. But it is Syd’s kingdom. He has a right to know what’s going on in it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right, fine,” I sighed. “It’s not like he hasn’t already seen me barf all over that poor magistrate.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re lucky you did,” countered Iolyt, leaning in to check my eyes with a clear, pink stone. “If you hadn’t, there’s a strong possibility the poison might’ve done much worse to you if I hadn’t been able to leach the rest of it out of your system in time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Leave it to Iolyt to spin throwing up in front of thousands of people into something positive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, the prince was questioning me while I pretended it was too painful to open my eyes to look at him. Just kidding, I wasn’t pretending. However, closing my eyes to his looks did help me keep my train of thought.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the peek that I did take, I could see he’d traded in his golden, armor-like shyntara from the Tableunia for a titanium colored one. (Hopefully it wasn’t because I’d thrown up on him too.) This shyntara was embroidered with a metallic sun and three moons, and DonTon’s crest in gold, copper, and silver thread. He looked like he’d just stepped out of the fantasy edition for a GQ ad.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m glad you’re awake,” the prince said gently. “I was starting to worry Iolyt might make me wait until the next portion of the Tableunia to see you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t tempt her,” I muttered, peeking at him through one eye. “She likes her rules.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His responding smile blinded me in my open eye. I closed it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I just wanted to let you know my guards are finishing up canvassing your quarters for any clues as to who might’ve done this to you. As soon as they are finished, you can return and rest up. We’re presently searching the tunnels for any sign of this red haired woman that Oriette described to me. When she is found, I assure you there will be consequences, Lunalyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He said my name—my fake name—like a caress, and I didn’t like what it did to my stomach. Imagine if he knew my real name.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you,” I said, feeling guilty for the fluttery feeling. I mean, James is absolutely my guy, but don’t tell me you haven’t fantasized about a ridiculously good-looking prince saying something similar to you? No? Huh…maybe that’s just a “homeschooled kid” thing?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And now I’ll leave you to rest, Lunalyn,” Sydian said. “You have a way to go yet in the Tableunia, and I’m dedicated to helping you win…without breaking any rules, of course.” I could hear the smile in his velvety voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a snorting sound and Lev discreetly cleared his throat from where he stood in the corner.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Before I go, however,” Sydian continued, “I was wondering if I might persuade you to join me for dinner and a stroll later this week. It would be my honor to give you a thorough tour of my father’s palace.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, I—uh…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I have a plan that will help you avoid getting lost again,” Sydian smoothly continued.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I opened my eyes to see Zuli grinning at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wait. Was this what it sounded like? Was this gorgeous prince asking me out on a Cobbogothian date?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed several times, stalling while I panicked, wracking my brain for a polite way to refuse him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I came up with something, Lylend piped in. “That sounds like a perfect idea, your Metallican. We were just saying, weren’t we kyndie, that what you need more than anything is for someone to give you a comprehensive tour of the palace—show you the landmarks.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Isn’t that right, kyndie?” This last question was accompanied by a look that squashed any hope I had of saying no. I could hear exactly what he was trying to say with it. “Think of the key, kyndie! Think of Jamus! It’s all for the sake of the Crest and my son.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grimaced at him, then turned back to the prince. “That would be very helpful, thank you,” I said. All the while, my entire being screamed in revolt like the old crone from The Princess Bride. “Boo! Boo! Rubbish! Filth! Slime! Muck! Boo! Boo! Boo!” But she was ad libbing for me: “Your kinzura lives! And you go on a date with another?!” (By the way, if you’ve never seen that 1987 cult classic movie, DO IT! So great.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As soon as Sydian left, Iolyt approached and slipped something into my hand. I glanced down slowly, my head throbbing worse than before. “What’s this?” I asked. “A new stone?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt nodded. “It’s a compass stone. I call it navagate,” she said. “It should help you maneuver the tunnels of the palace.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, in case princie-kynd has other motives for giving you a tour,” Lev guffawed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I groaned. “Thank you, but you couldn’t have given me this before I agreed to ‘dinner and a stroll’ with him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt grimaced an apology.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Everything will be fine, kyndie,” Lylend chimed in. “Just remember, it’s all for the sake of the Crest and Jamus.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, okay,” I breathed. I was too nauseous to argue about it anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I drifted back to sleep, vaguely aware of Lev chuckling in the background.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I woke much later, I found myself back in the palace quarters Zuli and I shared. Iolyt and the others must’ve returned to the Hood’s camp outside the city once she was certain I was out of harm’s way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was dark in our rooms. I could hear Zu breathing softly in her curtained cot across the room. I was feeling a lot better than before, so I slowly sat up and made my way to the window.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Outside, the city of Rasili was teeming with light and life, just like all the previous nights we’d been here. Rasili and the Triolune Festival felt like one continuous celebration. Cobbogothians seemed to party as hard as Bostonians did when the Sox finally won the World Series. I couldn’t help smiling at the thought. I was barely a toddler at the time, but I did remember Uncle Jack flew home from Iceland just to watch the game. He let me stay up all night and even took me downtown for the parade.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Boy, that seemed like such a long time ago—another lifetime altogether. It was quiet times like this, when I was by myself and had a chance to remember, that I missed Uncle Jack the most. What he would’ve given to see a Cobbogothian celebration like the Triolune Festival.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I stared down into the crowded, noisy streets, lost in thought, I felt a sudden shiver creep up my neck. From the window, I could see the bustling streets of Rasili filled with colorful crystal lanterns and people dancing and laughing. The buildings were adorned with decorations and silk streamers, adding to the celebratory atmosphere. I could see the excitement on their faces, but I was more concerned with the shadows and lurking figures in the corners and back alleys watching from afar. I couldn’t help the sudden feeling I was being watched.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, screeching unlike anything I’d ever heard before startled me from my thoughts. I scanned the crowds, looking for the noise. Nothing. Only people looking around, trying to discover the source of the sound as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I saw it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Several rooftops away from DonTon’s volcanic palace, I saw two eyes glowing at me, bright like phosphorescence—like emerald fire behind glass.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I froze.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The rest of the owner of the eyes was hidden in darkness, but those eyes didn’t blink. They didn’t flicker. They just stared straight at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The room spun for a moment, my legs like soggy sticks beneath me. I braced against the edge of the windowsill and carefully opened the sash.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The moment I moved, the watcher shifted too. The eyes rose…and then a face emerged ever so slightly from the shadows.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It rose a little higher. And then I saw…feathers? Yeah! Feathers. A smooth, crimson crown of feathers like liquid fire on top of its head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are you doing?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I squealed and spun, nearly tumbling to the ground.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Zu! Sheesh! You practically gave me a heart attack.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli took my arm, steadying me. “Are you okay?” she asked groggily.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, turning back just in time to catch the last glimpse of scarlet and gold tail feathers vanishing behind a much more distant rooftop.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Oriette","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"47f0853d6030403ab3edb9adf0f5a44a"},{"title":"Sydian’s Secrets (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I’m not a suspicious guy by nature, right? I don’t like thinking the worst of people automatically. I usually let them prove their character to me one way or another before I decide how I feel about them. But with kids going missing and my boy glynt’s freedom on the line, I was having to look at the world through a whole new lens. So for now, everyone was guilty until proven innocent.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You should’ve seen her throw her dinner like that,” one of the palace guards relayed to the group. We were in one of the lowest levels of DonTon’s palace—the least “unfinished” levels—and several guards, including me, were gathered around a giant stone the guards used as a sort of drinking/gaming table. Which was weird because, I don’t know if you’ve heard or not, Cobbogothians can’t get drunk.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The prince should stop serving such rich foods beforehand,” chimed in another. “At least that’s what the magistrate must’ve thought…once he was swimming in it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a whoop of laughter from the group. I joined in heartily, matching my enthusiasm to the fella’s standing nearest me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was the third group of guards reliving the “incident” from the latest Tableunia—some kind of beauty pageant thingy?—Round. I wasn’t there, but I might as well have been with how often I’d heard it recounted. Apparently, some poor kid let her nerves get the better of her while she was up on stage. She ended up puking all over the magistrate. I try not to laugh at those kinds of things, as a rule—nobody wants to be that kid—but I was trying to win over the trust of these guys. That wasn’t going to happen if I didn’t at least pretend to think their ring leader was a riot.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All in all, this group of guards weren’t my preferred companions, but you work with what you’ve got, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The thing I was learning about DonTon’s palace, particularly his palace guard, is the lower you got into the volcano, the lower the quality your company got. This sort of fun-making was mild in comparison to what the folks thought was funny two levels down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The guard next to me lifted his stein of lyfine berry wine with one fist and slapped me on the back with the other. These guards were mainly in charge of guarding tunnel entrances in and out of the palace. This was my third evening “drinking.” I was doing my best to glean as much information on the comings and goings of servants in the palace as possible. My hope was to identify any suspicious patterns or characters who might be involved with goldabung smuggling. So far, nothing was too noteworthy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was another whoop from the group as some poor schmuck near an open lava flow started imitating the puking event by dramatically dry heaving. He nearly pulled himself and his buddy over the edge into the lava. I scrubbed my forehead to hide the need to roll my eyes. How much longer did I have to hang out with these morons?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just then, a figure stepped out from behind one of the tapestries the guards were responsible for patrolling. A hush came over the group as Prince Sydian moved into the light of the cluster of sunstones hanging from a nearby stalactite. The tunnels on this level of the palace weren’t finished, so the guards had to rig their own light sources.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your Metallican,” said the leader of the group, as he shot up from a giant worn down chair made of lava rock. “How can we serve you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For starters, you can immediately stop entertaining yourselves at Lunalyn opalinya’s expense. If word hasn’t reached you yet, the kyndie was poisoned before she stepped on that stage. That’s why she behaved the way she did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Bad food?” Guffawed a guard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” Sydian said, looking more dangerous than I’d seen him. “Foul play.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"An awkward silence came over the group.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Furthermore,” Prince Sydian continued, then gave a heavy SIGH, “there’s been another disappearance.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who, Metallican?” Asked a female guard. I’d spent enough time with the group to know she was the only mom among them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The prince’s expression softened. “It’s little Mica, my father’s paget.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mica?” I said before I could stop myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Prince Sydian’s eyes fell on me with recognition. “As you know, he only just returned to the palace. DonTon’s wrath is kindled. He demands double shifts guarding all tunnels down here until the end of the festival or until the culprit is caught. Understood?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All the guards stood and saluted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I cleared my throat, recomposing myself. “What else can we do?” I asked, maintaining the accent Tolliver (tollie to his friends)—a goldabung I’d recently met in the palace—had helped me master for this disguise. The kid was a genius when it came to disguise stone cocktails.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian studied me for a few beats, then turned to stride back the way he came, pausing to add, “instead of wasting time drinking and mocking Tableunia contestants, you can start combing every tunnel on this level and below for weaknesses in our fortress. I want answers about why this kynd and so many other goldabungs keep disappearing. As far as we know, we’re looking for a red haired woman. I presume you haven’t forgotten who else’s life depends on getting to the bottom of this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of course he was referring to Glynt, and as much as I respected the guy for standing up for the poor puking girl, the veiled threat in it rubbed me the wrong way. Besides, how could I forget about Glynt? The guy’d saved my bacon out there in the forest of Dag. I’d still be lost and wandering if he hadn’t shown up when he did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As the prince retreated behind the tapestry again, I remembered what the goldabungs from the forest of Dag had told me when I finally tracked them down in the lower city after they left Glynt and me at the palace gates. They claimed that whoever was stealing them must have some wicked powerful ties to someone at the palace because they were using secret passageways the goldabungs didn’t know about.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently, the goldabungs knew the tunnels in DonTon’s palace better than DonTon himself. But maybe someone in the royal family did know them better.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Consequently, I’d been thinking this fact over the past few days, wondering if the culprit could be a member of DonTon’s family. This was the first time I’d run into one of the royals since Glynt was imprisoned, so following the prince might be my only chance to find out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once the guards resumed their storytelling—this time placing bets on who could’ve poisoned the Opalinya girl and why—I snuck away from the group and behind the tapestry the prince had used. In DonTon’s palace, I was learning there were “public” passages and tunnels and then there were a whole lot of “secret” unmarked ones. How did I learn about them? By getting hopelessly lost in them more than once. For the record, it ain’t Pleasant running into those creepy trolls at any time, let alone when you’re lost in some labyrinth of tunnels.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Careful to keep my distance, I followed his Metallican until we reached another tapestry. I waited for him to pass through before creeping through myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, I’d been issued a shard of silver hearthurite, which allowed me access to most of the tapestries—but not the royals’. This first tapestry led to one of the public corridors I recognized. After ensuring he was far enough ahead of me, I snuck through the passageway and continued following him. Sure enough, the prince was heading toward the royal quarters of the palace. If I stayed close enough, I could slip in after him. I’d have to figure out how to get back out once I was inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I ducked behind a decorative stone column when Prince Sydian paused to speak with another guard. Their conversation was brief, but I caught something about “increased patrols” and “the mid tunnels.” Once they parted ways, I continued trailing him, careful to keep to the shadows.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The closer we got to the royal quarters, the more everything gleamed with polished metalwork—platinum, gold, silver, and copper inlays that would make any goldabung’s fingers itch. Even the stones in the detailed mosaics of stern-faced metallicans, battle scenes, and Rasilian lore covering the walls and ceilings grew more precious. The gems winked at me in the sunstone light as I crept along.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, Sydian stopped at an ornate tapestry embellished with the royal Crest—three moons converging. He glanced over his shoulder, and I leaned back into the shadows, barely daring to breathe. After a moment, he pushed through the tapestry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I counted to five, then slipped in before the tapestry could close. The tunnel that greeted me on the other side was short and forked. I glanced both ways, barely catching sight of the prince’s sleeve before he disappeared into the room beyond the tunnel to my right. I hid myself in the tunnel to my left until the prince returned a few minutes later and left the way we’d come.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was my chance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The prince’s chambers were as opulent as you’d expect—walls embedded with veins of precious metals—furniture carved from volcanic stone and encrusted with an obscene amount of jewels. More mosaics depicting great Metallican triumphs. But what caught my attention wasn’t the wealth on display; it was the “flowery-ness” of it all. Not the kind of look I was expecting for the prince, I’ll be honest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly scanned the room. For what, I had no idea. What kind of clues would indicate smuggling kids? That’s when the tapestries in the room caught my attention. There were three besides the one I’d come through. Each had a design I recognized—except for one. This was good news. It meant I’d be able to leave without help. Apparently, the royals only cared about people getting into their quarters, not getting out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hurried over to the corner of the room where the tapestry I didn’t recognize was. It was almost hidden near a gaudy ivory and gold-framed mirror. It was tiny in comparison to the other tapestries I’d seen. There was also a unique symbol on it—much smaller and simpler than any I’d seen so far. Maybe it was older?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I dug my shard of hearthurite out of my pocket, only to find the tapestry was already open a crack. I carefully slid it open a little more to find a small staircase plunging straight down into darkness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just before I could step through and investigate, however, I heard something. It sounded like rushing water—and muffled… crying?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d heard that sound before. It sounded an awful lot like what Glynt and I heard in the forest of Dag before we found the grimcages.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could investigate, I heard footsteps—and the prince’s voice—coming from what I assumed was the forked tunnel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slid the small tapestry shut and darted to one I recognized, quickly slipping behind it just in time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before heading down the tunnel I knew led to DonTon’s kitchens, I let the darkness swallow me—but kept the tapestry open just a crack. That’s when I heard Sydian’s voice, accompanied by a higher, muffled voice. A voice that distinctly said the word “goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yeah. I was definitely coming back here when I had more time.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Navagate","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"5062f5e4976440079043bb36bd5532eb"},{"title":"Journal: Tapestries","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0a90eb353f3e4cab8970723491dd6aaa"}]},{"n":60,"letterTitle":"The Legend of Seer Stone Sink","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"The Legend of Seer Stone Sink","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Word to the wise, never spar with a sina princess who has just had words with her mother. It’s not pleasant, even if you do have one more manifestation than her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That one really hurt, Zu,” I said, rubbing my left ribcage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry,” she said, grimacing as she snapped back to the present. I could tell she was still arguing with Queen Citrine in her head from the moment we entered Lylend’s sparring arena.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What’s that? You didn’t know Lylend had a sparring arena? Well, he does. It’s down one of the passageways leading away from the main room in his rodãor. Apparently, everyone had one before Bruxrac.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’d she say to you this time anyway?” I asked, hoping that talking about it might help.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just the usual,” Zu said. “Expressed her infinite disappointment in me as her daughter, then threatened to disown me again if I didn’t figure out a way to help Marbelina win the Wyn Tournament.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Marbelina was the only Adolorian competing against Lev in the multi-wyn Callowynder tournament. “She does know Lev is your kinzurine, right?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” laughed Lev, striding onto the area floor, “to her lifelong shame.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hey, Levy,” Zu said, giving him a quick peck on the cheek when he reached her side.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Another rough day with your mom, huh?” he asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Heeeeeey Leeeevvvvyyy!” I mocked good-humoredly. I’d been trying all afternoon to get a smile out of Zu, and all he had to do was walk in the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry, lune-kyndie,” Lev countered. “I know you’re missing your own kinzurine, but I’m not gonna let you kiss me too.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Ich! Gross!” I said. “You’re practically my brother.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But,” Lev grinned, waggling his eyebrows, “I know someone who will.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stop,” I said, rolling my eyes. He’d been making the most of the whole Sydian situation, which meant he’d been teasing me relentlessly ever since the prince asked me out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Alright,” Zu said, shaking herself. “We need to focus. Your fitness and forte part of the competition is in two days.” Her eyes narrowed as she drew up another water orb between her palms. “Again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sighed but took my stance. My own water powers felt sluggish today, like I was trying to stir honey instead of liquid. I was probably still hungover from the poisoning. Iolyt assured me it would pass by the time I had to go back into the aurethryn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Still, I managed to pull a decent-sized sphere from the practice barrel in the corner, and we began to circle each other on the smooth stone floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ready?” Zuli asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could answer, she sent her water orb flying toward me. I barely had time to deflect it with my own, and the collision sent droplets spraying across my face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re getting better,” Zu said, already forming another attack. “But you’re still telegraphing your movements.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? No I’m—” I started.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry to break this up, kyndies,” Lev said, moving between us, “but speaking of princie-kynd, he’s waiting outside to take a certain Lunalyn Opalinya on a…what did you call it, Nor? A date?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no!” I cried, dropping my water orb with a splash. It soaked my practice shyntara and slippers completely. “I forgot all about that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Conveniently forgot,” Lev mumbled to Zu.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I rushed over to the water barrel and began splashing my face and arms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nori! Nori! Stop,” cried Zu, running toward me. “What are you doing?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you think I’m doing?” I said. “I just finished sweating like a pig.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s a pig?” Lev asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Never mind that. Sit,” Zuli commanded, motioning toward the front row of arena style stone seats circling the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once I was sitting, Zuli expertly pulled her pouch of stones out of a pocket in her salamander-scaled shyntara. She laid them all out on the seat next to us and began to work her magic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You want something subtle but sophisticated—Syd loves understated beauty.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m not trying to impress him,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sure, but you don’t want to embarrass yourself either.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Can’t I just wear a normal shyntara?” I asked as she began to fiddle with the weaving stones on my current water soaked outfit. She changed the color and texture multiple times before settling on a pale gold with iridescent pearl and creamy accents. “You may not want to impress him, Nori, but he’s still a prince, and you need to hold your own in his presence.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because other contestants in the Tableunia are going to see you together and—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t tell me they all know your mom and will tattle on me?” I asked. I immediately regretted it as soon as I saw her face stiffen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev scowled at me. I winced and shrugged my apology.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli cleared her throat. “You just want the other contestants to see you have an edge outside the aurethryn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I shrugged. “Work your magic.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And she did. By the time I left Lylend’s rodãor, I was once more unrecognizable. I had to admit, my confidence definitely took a boost I’d never experienced before Zu started decking me out like this. When I met Sydian in the parlor of our palace rooms, I was even glad. The prince looked amazing, and thankfully I didn’t look like a drowned Siamese kitten or something when I approached him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn,” Sydian said, rising from where he was sitting to meet me, “you look…you look like Vyn herself.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh…thank you.” I’m ashamed to say it, reader, but I blushed. I know. Lame. And believe me, The Princess Bride crone was booing at me again. The truth is, I’d been likened to the moon my entire life but never to a notoriously beautiful character from Cobbogothian history or lore…or whatever Vyn was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Shall we?” Sydian asked, holding out his hand to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a deep breath, reminding myself that James and I weren’t married, even if kynsyska—the night Totherma welded our souls together in James’ dungeon—meant we were almost as good as. Then I told the old crone to shut it and took hold of the ridiculously handsome prince’s hand. All self-loathing thoughts of betraying the only boy I’ve ever loved aside, it was a bit of a moment for me. I mean, if all those nonexistent friends I had back in the Olden Realm could see me now, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Prince Sydian and I walked down the main corridors of the palace for a while in silence. I was wracking my brain for things to say, but it was my first date outside of hanging with James before…well…Cobbogoth happened. What did one say to a ridiculously good-looking prince?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I hope you’re feeling fully recovered,” Sydian said, beating me to it. He took a left turn down a corridor I’d never been in before. The surroundings became decidedly more…golden. Which I was beginning to learn probably meant Queen Jayda was directly involved in the decorating. After a few more turns, we came to a door that looked a lot like my own, only, like I said, more golden. Prince Sydian pushed open the door and motioned for me to go before him. I did and immediately grew uneasy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The rooms we entered obviously belonged to a man—a man with clean, sophisticated taste and what Zu called “understated elegance.” From the looks of it, Sydian had taken me back to his own royal rooms. And I thought things had been going so well!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Can I get you anything?” he asked, removing the more formal outer “jacket” (for lack of a better word) of his shyntara, replacing it with a more worn, comfortable looking version.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Um…yeah,” I said. “You can get me out of here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pardon?” Sydian said, turning to face me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look,” I said, hearing my own voice shake. “I think you got the wrong idea about me or something. I’m not this kind of a gir—er kyndie.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What kind of a kyndie is that?” he said, a subtle smolder touching his eyes as he smiled and began to move toward me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The kind you take back to your…royal chambers,” I said with more confidence than I felt, but then ruining it by retreating. Much too soon, my back hit up against something solid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If I don’t take you back to my chambers,” Sydian said in an especially sultry voice, or at least that’s what my frantic mind was hearing, “it would take a whole lot longer to show you this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He reached up then, and I was certain he was going to do that “leaning thing” guys do when they’re going in for a kiss. Which is why I did what I did next.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the record, “Oooph!” is the sound a handsome prince makes when he’s been punched in the gut without warning.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not to be rude,” Sydian groaned, “but may I ask why you did that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—I’m so sorry!” I cried. “I—I just thought you were gonna—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do this?” he said, with a quizzical grimace. He reached up again with a platinum piece of hearthurite in his hand and opened a tapestry behind me. I hadn’t noticed it until then.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh,” was all I could say, not to mention my face was on fire.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The tapestry revealed a tunnel behind it. A golden tunnel. “What’s this?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian cocked an eyebrow mysteriously and grabbed a large sunstone wand sitting on a nearby table. “This, my dear Lunalyn, is the tunnel that leads to all tunnels…tunnels…tunnels…” He mimicked an echoing sound with mock solemnity, all while holding the wand up to his face to cast it in spooky light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help smiling at this sudden shift in mood. I had to hand it to the guy, he was quick on his feet. And charming. Really charming.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Shall we?” he said, sweeping me into the tunnel with an exaggerated bow. “I promise not to kiss you or try anything untoward if you’ll only hold my hand again. Not because I like it, of course,” he said with a wink, “but because you wouldn’t believe how terrifying it is to run into trolls down in these tunnels.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He extended his hand to me again, this time with a friendly smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took it, feeling a little perplexed. This was definitely a different version of the person who’d cornered me in that little room after the soireenya.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian led the way into the tunnel and along the golden corridor, using the sunstone wand to light our way. He told me the history of the tunnel and how it was made by his six or seventh great-grandfather who had, get this, fifty—that’s right, fifty!—children. Apparently they kept getting lost in the tunnels.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So he made a special tunnel that leads to all other tunnels?” I asked when we’d been walking for a while. “How did that help his kyndlin from getting lost?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not exactly,” he said with a laugh. “More like a tunnel that leads to a room that leads to all tunnels. Heartfire Hall. That’s what we call it because it’s very near the heart of the mountain. If you always enter the tunnels from Heartfire Hall, due to the Vegvisiri core it’s built on, you can always find your way back there.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Vegvisiri?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Think of it as a sort of magnet. Once you enter the hall, it attunes itself to your hearthurite frequency, and from then on, no matter where you go in the palace, it will always lead you there and back again. That’s the simple explanation anyway.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So it’s like the North Star?” I said before thinking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Pardon?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Er—a compass.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes! That’s it exactly. Heartfire Hall is like a compass. But you have to have a platinum piece of hearthurite to use it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All the royal suites in the palace have their own tunnel that leads to Heartfire Hall, so we can get where we need to go quickly without being stopped multiple times by goldabungs or trolls or servants or guests along the way. There’s only so many minutes in a day, you know, and for a royal—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Each minute is precious,” I finished. I’d learned as much from Zuli.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The prince smiled and nodded as the tunnel began to lead upward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, we stepped out of the tunnel into what looked like a grand, cathedral-like version of Lylend’s record room. It spiraled upward through multiple levels, but instead of cubbies filled with crystal scrolls, the walls were lined with hundreds of elaborately fashioned tapestries, each bearing a symbol engraved on it. The doorways climbed in a graceful upward spiral, accompanied by a winding ramp that rose level by level. Between each doorway, sunstone sconces bathed the space in warm light, and from the ceiling, a stunning Cobbogothian chandelier spiraled down in a coiling design, stopping just feet above our heads.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa!” I said. “I guess there are a lot of tunnels in your dad’s palace, huh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” Sydian agreed, “but the most important ones—the ones that will get you where you need to go while you’re here are all on the bottom row. See?” he said, taking my hand and leading me to a nearby tunnel. “See this symbol here?” he asked, pointing to the symbol on the tapestry. It looked very similar to the symbols on the tapestries throughout the palace. “Each of these symbols tells you where it leads,” he explained. “For example, this one leads to my mother’s banquet hall, where she held the soireenya. And this one,” he pulled me to the tunnel to our right, “leads to my father’s throne room.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is great,” I said. “But is there any way to get here from my side of the palace? I mean, I don’t imagine DonTon and Queen Jayda would want me wandering around in the Royal quarters looking for the tunnel that leads here?” If not, I was definitely going to be utilizing Iolyt’s navagate stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Unfortunately, there’s not,” said Sydian too innocently, “but you’re welcome to use the tunnel in my chambers any time you like. And…” he said, handing me a piece of platinum hearthurite. “I’ve even secured authorization from my father to issue you a piece of our highest level hearthurite, which, like I said, is the only way to access Heartfire Hall.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Navagate it was, then. But I took the hearthurite and thanked him anyway. He may not have been trying to kiss me earlier, but I wasn’t about to give him the chance again by frequenting his chambers any time I needed to use Heartfire Hall to get around.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So,” I said, tilting my head to look all the way up to the ceiling, “which of these tunnels leads to the kitchens? You promised me dinner.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I did, didn’t I,” he said, nearly blinding me with a smile. “But first, would you let me show you my favorite tunnel? I promise, you won’t regret it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I said, shaking free of the spell. “Then dinner after?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He laughed and nodded. For the record, dear reader, that laugh, in all its velvety smoothness, should be banned. Banned I tell you!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lead the way,” I managed, embarrassed by how breathless I sounded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Boo! Boo! Boo! Sheesh! Snap out of it, Nor!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian led the way up the winding spiral ramp leading to each level of tunnel entrances. As he did, I pondered this weird predicament I was in. Any other girl in Cobbogoth or the Olden Realm would’ve died to be in my shoes…well except maybe Zuli, since she already had her chance with Prince Sydian and chose Lev instead. But here I was with this incredibly good-looking, charming, funny, if a little stalker-ish at times, prince, and all I wanted was James. Ruggedly—if not devastatingly—handsome, self-deprecatingly good James. And yet in order to help me get closer to finding James, I was forced to go on a date with said incredibly good-looking, charming prince. I guess life was funny like that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we’d climbed about six levels, Sydian led me to a doorway that looked ordinary in every way when compared to the rest, except for the symbol carved on it. This tunnel’s symbol looked nothing like the whirly swirly Cobbogothain style of the other ones and more like it had been made a long, long time ago and by someone more akin to the Norseman. In fact, the symbol was crude by comparison. It looked just like a somewhat geometric, crystallized heart.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not a good omen if I intended to keep this date platonic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is it,” Sydian said. “Are you ready to see the oldest, most magical part of our volcano?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And yet, I was intrigued. “Lead the way, your Metallican.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian held up the sunstone wand and entered the tunnel. As we walked, we didn’t walk in silence. Instead, he began telling me the legend I’ve included with this chronicle—the Legend of Seer Stone Sink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Consequently, as we neared the end of the tunnel, I wasn’t surprised to hear the sound of rushing water. But when we finally rounded the last bend and the tunnel opened into a giant cavern, I still found myself stunned by what I saw. An enormous heart-shaped ruby colored stone really sat in the middle of the floor at the base of a thundering waterfall. Just like the legend described, it poured in from a sinkhole in the cave’s ceiling at least three stories above us. Of all the places I’d been in Cobbogoth so far, Seer Stone Sink felt the most magical.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian made his way to the ruby seer stone and motioned me forward. When I reached it, I gave him my hand, and he helped me up to stand with him in the center of it, just like the legend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking very close to my ear, he said. “Would you like to see the face of your one true love, Lunalyn?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The truth? You bet I did, because if this really worked I knew whose face I’d see, and boy did I miss the sight of it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What were the words again?” I shouted, barely able to hear myself over the thundering water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let the memory of this water and the echo of this stone show me a love just as true,” Sydian recited in his velvety voice next to my ear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This time it didn’t have the effect on me it usually had. As I peered into the white sheets of water pouring down from above, there was only one person on my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Half hoping, half doubting, but still eagerly, I said the words of the legend and waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I wasn’t really expecting it to work, of course.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is why when the waterfall turned to glass, my whole body responded by going numb. When I saw James’ face flicker over its surface, his soul-piercing eyes peering back at me, I could hardly believe it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who do you see?” Sydian asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could take advantage of the opportunity to gently but definitively tell him that unfortunately it wasn’t him, the image in the waterfall changed. And I don’t mean it just vanished. James’ face changed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If my body was numb before, now it was paralyzed. I watched in horror as James’ face warped into something dark and beastly with a different kind of piercing eyes. In the time it took me to shake myself out of it, I took in enormous black lion-like paws, a black feathered mane, and black feathered wings—wings that seemed to go on for miles.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jumped back, slipping on the wet stone. Prince Sydian caught me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you all right?” he asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—uh—yeah,” I said, a shiver running through me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“By the Gihara, Lunalyn! You’re as pale as ash…and trembling!” Sydian took off his shyntara overcoat and wrapped it around my shoulders.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I must be tired,” I said woodenly. “I think I’ll skip dinner, if you don’t mind.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, of course. You’re still recovering from the poison. I’ve thoughtlessly kept you too long. Forgive me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t protest when he put his hand on the small of my back, leading me out of the cave and through the tunnels. I didn’t even protest as he kissed my hand just before leaving me at the door of Zuli’s and my rooms. I was too distracted by the image of the beast, his piercing eyes and massive black wings, and what, if anything, it had to do with James.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Prince Sydian","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"3bc8be7151ed40ed9d5ed586f4a58a82"},{"title":"Legend of Seer Stone Sink","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"b931d1be8701483a8c3d9c880ac26dda"},{"title":"Journal: Heartfire Hall","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"fec59a9c5f4443f3b4bab2e60540850f"},{"title":"Heartfire Hall","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9da1aca0d1d74f7ab88eefa06f28fdd9"}]},{"n":61,"letterTitle":"The Myrkramaer","narrator":"Lev","pieces":[{"title":"The Myrkramaer","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So…you’re probably wondering what in the chipstrike a myrkramaer is. Am I right? Of course I am, ’cause Nori told me last night that word doesn’t exist in the Olden realm. The good news is, I think you’ll get the picture of what myrkramaer are and what they do pretty easily.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To lava it down for you, myrkramaer are pretty much shadow creatures, kinda like dark creatures from the dark realm, but they’re not stuck in the dark realm Nor are they metaphysical. They live here with us. And they’re not really bad or good, they just…are. That’s why we call ’em shadow creatures. I mean, your shadow isn’t good or bad, is it? It does what its creator does. Same with myrkramaer. They’re made of one of the six elements, and they behave like their element. Nori wrote it all down when I explained it to her, if you’re curious. I’ll see if I can get a copy of her notes for you. You know…to explain things better.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All you really need to know about myrkramaer is if you capture ’em, they’ll do exactly what you want ’em to do. They come in pretty handy during a battle.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Today I’m in the thick of the myrkramaer round of the Triolune tournament. I’m hunting hollokyn. I guess I figured if I’m gonna beat Seraphyna at her own game, I gotta stick with what I know best. And as a native gliptrainian, what I know best is earth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Besides, I grew up hunting hollokyn. They’re the cutest of the myrkramaer, but don’t let that fool you. I learned that the hard way. One time when I was a kynd of six, Pieter had me out riding his zyrker, digging tunnels for the Mines near moloth’s keep. I hit a colony of ’em, and they nearly carried me off into their tunnels. I never would’ve been seen or heard of again. Now that I think about it, that was probably what Pieter was hoping for. I probably could’ve charmed them into keeping me in the end—raising me like some wild hollokyn cub or something. Zyrk, who knows? Maybe they’d have liked me better than Pieter does.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, speaking of zyrkers and Mines, the Mines the Callowynder magistrates set up for myrkramaer are pretty convincing. They went all out with free-flowing lava and everything. Tyloryn has been digging for just short of three hours now, and I already have seven burns from the lava seeping through the ceiling of the tunnel we’ve been digging the last two days. We left the pre-constructed mine shafts behind yesterday.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I guided tyloryn for the first twenty-four hours of our burrow into the mountain, but now I’m just giving him his head, and he’s letting his nose take charge. That’s the thing with hollokyn; you need a zyrker to find ’em or else you’re just digging into a mountain with no idea where you’ll end up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that’s also what makes zyrkers so great! One minute you’re talking to a really smart zyrker in his totem form (like people, but with animal heads) and the next you’ve got a raging beast on your hands who can bite and tear through earth and stone like it’s water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Last night me and tyloryn devised a plan to catch as many hollokyn as we can. With Iolyt’s help, we even stayed up late crafting a fangledãor to hold ’em in when we do find ’em. Let me tell you, fangledãors are much heavier around the neck than regular rodãors. I’ll need Zu to rub my neck and shoulders down after all this. If not, I’ll be too stiff to celebrate when I show Seraphyna that having just one more power than me doesn’t guarantee she’ll get the Callowynder stone in the end.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ve had a lot of time to think about how I’m gonna beat Seraphyna in the end since I’ve been down here. I know it won’t be easy, and I kinda like that. But I’ve just got to do it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I mean, you ever feel like you’re on the brink of something monumental—like one breath away from becoming a legend? I’ve felt that way since I discovered I was destined to play Callowynder. I was about ten years old. Without that feeling pushing me, I’d never have what it takes to prove to Lylend, Pieter, and everyone else that I can do this. I was born to do this. It’s something I’ve just always known deep in my gut. It’s the main thing that’s kept me from believing what my father believes about me for all these years—that I’m just some screwup destined for failure.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, that “legend” feeling washed over me again the moment tyloryn struck something. The tunnel walls trembled—maybe even the entire mountain. We exchanged glances, holding our breaths.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then the stone in front of us cracked open, crumbling away, and we peered into a gaping chasm of glowing dimness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn let out a low growl.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We were deep in the bowels of the mountain now, steam hissing from vent shafts, the rock around us glowing like the embers of a dying fire. My shyntara clung to my back, and the walls oozed with magma sweat. Tyloryn’s breath came out in steamy bursts, his nostrils flaring.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, kynd?” I whispered, sliding off his back and coming up by his head. “What’d you find?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I patted his crusty exterior, and tyloryn jerked his head forward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s something ahead?” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I crouched down, pressing a hand to the ground.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was faint, but there—beneath the occasional aftershock rumbling through the mountain—was a cadence I’d only ever read about. Rhythmic tapping. Metal on stone. And laughter—sharp and cindery like fire crackling in a cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I crept forward, peering over the lip of what was left of the crumbled tunnel wall and into a chasm below.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sight was something else. I barely dared to blink, ’cause there they all were.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hollokyn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dozens of ’em. Maybe a hundred. Knee-high, goblin-esque creatures with glowing lava eyes, cracked obsidian skin, and those sharp pointy heads. They moved like a colony of ants—carrying rough stone tools, hauling chunks of glowing ore, and carving tunnels with picks made of rough hewn metal. Flames licked between their knobby fingers as they chattered in some deep-earth dialect, more grunts than language.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One of ’em laughed and dove into a lava pool like it was bathwater.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to tyloryn, grinning. I couldn’t believe it. We’d just struck the motherlode!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If we can pull this off, kynd, we’re not walking away from this,” I whispered. “We’re riding away from this in myth songs and festival legends.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He snorted his agreement, slow and deliberate, as if to say “finally.” Of all three of my elemies I’d bonded with in froymeffyn, tyloryn got me the most. He wanted the same thing I did…immortalization. I wanted people seeing me and saying the same kind of things they said about Lylend. Things like: “there goes Levian Pieterskynd—Lev the greatest Callowynder player who’s ever lived—the Lev.” To make that happen, I had to make this happen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn and I stealthily crawled through the opening and backed up into the shadows and to the highest ground we could find. That’s where we hatched our plan.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I only had one fangledãor. It was enough to trap a colony like this. But the conditions had to be perfect: The fangledãor had to be open without the hollokyn noticing, and it couldn’t take longer than a couple of minutes or they’d swarm. Everything would come down to timing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Piece of ellaprava, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I pulled a pair of blekkingstone clusters from my stone pouch. Iolyt had given me the stones to try out in this round. Blekkingstone was a new illusion stone she’d created. If I used it just right, I could create the illusion that I was a lavalanche flowing rapidly toward the hollokyns’ little colony from the opposite side of the cavern. If I ran at them from that end, then tyloryn could use the other stone to corner them from this side, herding them straight into the fangledãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Alright, here’s what we’re gonna do…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn sat on his haunches, stone ears flicking back as I explained the plan.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll come down the northwest side over there, okay. This hollow is shaped like a bowl until you hit the southern point here.” I’d drawn the shape of the hollokyns’ cavern in the sand between us. “If you hit the southern bend right here and then herd them into this chokepoint, we may not get ’em all, but we could get most of ’em.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn nodded his agreement.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whatever you do though, don’t let ’em see you.” I held out a piece of blekkingstone. “Hold onto this the entire time, understand?” Tyloryn nodded and opened his stony mouth. I placed the cluster on his tongue.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll set up the fangledãor over there first.” I pointed to a dark corner of the cavern. “Once they’re inside, we’ll close the fangledãor and…we’ll be legend status in no time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He blinked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s your cue to get zyrked up and pretend this isn’t zyrkin’ dangerous,” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He blinked again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Whatever. Let’s go.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With a dash of stealth almost being seen twice, we reached my side of the hollokyns’ cavern. I carefully opened the fangledãor. Signaling to tyloryn, he left me behind, skirted the colony, then slid into place on the opposite side near the chokepoint.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Keeping to the shadows and gripping the blekkingstone so tightly my palm throbbed, I prayed I wasn’t about to get us both pounded to dust or thrown into a lava stream by a horde of angry hollokyn. The key was surprise; if they spotted the lavalanche Mirage too early, they wouldn’t take the threat seriously. If we didn’t move fast enough, they’d know something was up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a deep breath and signaled to tyloryn. With the image of the lavalanche vivid in my mind and the blekkingstone clenched in my hand, I sprinted down my side of the little hollow. Then tyloryn charged down his side. It sent tremors through the colony. At just the right moment, he activated his blekkingstone and instantly resembled a rapidly descending wave of molten lava racing toward the largest group of hollokyn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dust and screeching, like metal scraping rock, filled the cavern as the colony scrambled toward the chokepoint.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was working!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly opened the fangledãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The hollokyn scampered, their eyes shining like the ring of light indicating the prison they thought was their only escape. Soon they were pouring into it like the lava they thought was chasing them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I ran toward it. I’d wait until the last possible second to seal the fangledãor up, but not a second longer or they’d have time to figure out they’d been duped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Like liquid being sucked through a pinhole, the hollokyn flickered, their little stone bodies vanishing one by one into the fangledãor. The screeching calmed down, tyloryn stopped chasing, and within seconds—","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Silence.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the last hollokyn disappeared into the fangledãor, I dropped the blekkingstone, whipped the fangledãor stone from around my neck, ran my finger around the outer edge, and watched, panting, as the ring of light recoiled into its symbol and slid back into place on the stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I scanned the cavern for any sign of hollokyn stragglers, but all I saw was an empty hollow, the river of real lava flowing groggily with clouds of steam shooting up here and there from thermal activity. The piles of smoldering rubble where the hollokyns had been gathering their spoils for the day were abandoned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We did it,” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn lumbered over and dropped beside me, steam rising from his back. I laughed and slapped his shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We actually did it, kynd! We caught an entire colony!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tyloryn let out a triumphant roar that shook the cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next minute, a ring of light similar to the open fangledãor appeared, large enough for both tyloryn and me to step through. When we did, we emerged from the inside of the mountain and into the Callowynder arena.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced up at the aurethryn ceiling, and I gotta admit, with our clothes singed and our faces black with soot and sweat, we really looked like a couple of legends stepping out onto the field. Especially when the entire mountain collapsed in epic fashion behind us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To say the crowd outside the aurethryn lost their minds is an understatement. Screams. Color-bursts. A chant—my name, of course—all greeted us. The only thing I could do was climb on tyloryn’s back and take a victory lap. You gotta give the people what they want, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally got a chance to look up at the aurethryn, the scores for the round were glowing under our images.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"LEVIAN—#1.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"SERAPHYNA—#2.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"PRINCE SYDIAN—#3.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stood there stunned, blinking at the sky. “We beat her, tyloryn,” I said aloud. “We beat Seraphyna!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli was the first one to charge the aurethryn, throwing herself into my arms. I caught her up and kissed her in front of all of Cobbogoth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tonight there would be some serious celebrating happening.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rasili was buzzing when we hit the lower city.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Streets burst with color, music spilled from balconies, and the air smelled like my new favorite drink—firefruit fizz with a sprinkle of dried starynth root—and fresh, warm, fylyla crescents, the official Triolune festival pastry. (Nori helped me convert the recipe as close as we could for them into Olden realm terms. I mean, you just gotta try ’em!)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of Nori, Zu and I bumped into her and the rest of the Hood on the lower streets just before sunset. Having missed my victory, she threw her arms around me when she heard the news, and then Zuli dragged the three of us into the celebration. I left tyloryn to celebrate in his own way, being fed roasted starynth root by a gang of awe-struck goldabungs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You really beat her?” Nori asked as we ducked beneath a rigged-up string of floating crystal lanterns. “I’m so sad I missed it!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, where were you?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I was stuck doing my poise and polish portion of the Tableunia. You know…it’s like the ‘do-you-have-what-it-takes-to-be-royal’ individual test thingy. Queen Jayda inconveniently scheduled mine during myrkramaer.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How’d it go?” Zu and I asked in unison.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori hesitated, then shrugged. “Well…I don’t think I have what it takes to be royal.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’d you have to do?” I asked, pretty confident whatever she had to do didn’t include capturing an entire colony of hollokyn. Not that we were competing against each other or anything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I just followed Queen Jayda and Princess Sylva around for a few hours and performed whatever royal tasks they gave me. I assume I was being judged on…all of it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu nodded. “It’s the portion of the pageant where they judge your royal potential.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori sighed dramatically. “Alas, I have none.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t say stuff like that,” Zu scolded. “You’ve got to believe in yourself, Nori.” Then she hesitated. “Wait. You didn’t get a bad score…did you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Who knows?” Nori shrugged. “By the time it was over, I just wanted to get out of there.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You didn’t stay long enough to find out your score?” Zu squeaked, anxiety all over her cute face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s not a whole lot I can do about my score now anyway,” Nori laughed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nori—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m only teasing, Zu,” she said, putting her arm around Zu’s shoulders. “I did fine.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How fine?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Fine enough to slide myself into third place.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu visibly relaxed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not too shabby after my puking incident, eh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I couldn’t help chuckling at that. Thankfully, Nori was able to laugh about it now, too. ’Cause let’s face it—","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Puking on stage is just funny.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We met up with Brawlo and the other members of the Hood near an outdoor food tavern. Stone tables were arranged in a small courtyard under rows of crystal lanterns crisscrossing above.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We entered the courtyard, and Iolyt and Lylend grinned at me as we reached the table they’d commandeered for our group.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well done, kynd!” Lylend said. “By the Gihara! What a round!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so proud of you, libkyn!” Iolyt said, giving me a motherly peck on the cheek.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked around the rest of the courtyard. Brawlo was lying in the nearby corner, pawing a string of lanterns that had partly fallen from above. When he saw me, he gave a cheerful trill.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No Pieter, eh?” I asked Iolyt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She pressed her lips together and shook her head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t worry, levy,” Zu said, wrapping her arm around mine. “He’ll hear about it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m gonna go ahead and say thank goodness he’s not here,” Nori chimed in.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A shocked hush fell over our table.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, come on,” she continued. “We’re all thinking it. Pieter’s just about the worst killjoy there is. This is your victory, Lev. He’d just try to ruin it for you. Don’t let him steal your moment of triumph.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She was right, of course. But the fact that Nori was the one saying it was the shocker. It wasn’t just a reflection of how the Tableunia was changing her—she was more confident than I’d ever seen her—but her saying it made it make more sense to me. Why did I want my dad here? She was right. He was never satisfied with anything I did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I brushed his absence off and resolved to enjoy myself tonight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“More lyfine berry wine!” Lylend called out to a nearby tavern keeper vendor. “In fact, wine for everyone! We have a champion in our midst!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At his words, the entire street erupted in cheers and toasts from all sorts of Cobbogothians. This was gonna cost Lylend a terra at least. I’d be lying if I said it didn’t feel zyrkin’ good to be the one being celebrated.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After crystal goblets of wine were passed around and we were halfway through our second platter of fylyla crescents, there seemed to be some kind of commotion moving down the street from the direction of the palace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh-oh.” Nori froze, then crouched down. Her eyes darted over my shoulder and then over to Zuli.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” Zuli said. We both turned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Citrine and her entourage were moving through the streets, parting the crowds as they went. It looked like they were headed our way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hurry, Nori! I don’t think she’s seen you yet,” Zu hissed. “Brawlo, take her! Hide!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori jumped on Brawlo’s back, and they darted off in the opposite direction. The rest of us braced ourselves for what was sure to be…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Unpleasant.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Princess Zuli of Adoloria,” her mother called as she finally reached our table.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hopefully she wouldn’t ruin the whole night for Zu. I really wanted to celebrate with my kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli rose to curtsy. “Mother, what are you doing here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Citrine looked as if she’d just stepped in some shyntfish dung. “Oh, I don’t know. Perhaps coming to see why my only daughter completely disobeyed my orders and now marbelina—the last chance we had at getting DonTon to help me take my throne back—is out of the tournament.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I had no intention of doing what you asked, mother,” Zuli said, “and I told you as much during our last conversation.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And you call yourself the princess of Adoloria?” The queen said, her lip curling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, actually. I haven’t formally used that title since you stripped me of it when you banished me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Citrine huffed, and Lylend stood. “Citrine, won’t you sit and join us? Perhaps I can explain to you another way to get your kingdom back—to reunite your kingdom.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” she said. “I know what your plan is, and I don’t want any part of it—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mother—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because it won’t work.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What could Lylend say to that? Nothing. So we all just waited there, uncomfortable.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zu’s mom glanced around haughtily until her eyes fell on me. “You, Levian of gliptrainia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your majesty,” I nodded, more cheerfully than I probably should’ve, I’ll admit.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If you think that today’s victory gives you any hope of winning the Callowynder stone, you’re mistaken.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stood up, but Lylend stepped between us. “Everyone, lower your voices,” he murmured. “Let’s avoid a scene.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But the queen wasn’t finished. She smiled at me, the look in her eye cold as ice. “You’re talented, kynd. Charming, even. But you have no idea who…or what you’re up against in that arena.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to keep my voice steady. “I’m playing the same game as everyone else in the aurethryn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you?” She glanced sideways at Lylend, her expression unreadable. “Tell me, master Naridi. You have eons of wisdom. I thought you, at least, would see through her to what she really is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend frowned. “Pardon? Her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh come now,” Citrine arched a brow. “Seraphyna, of course. Really, Lylend. When have you ever met a quintowyn who wasn’t something else entirely?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stopped chewing my fylyla crescent. “What do you mean?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before anyone could say anything else, Queen Citrine looked back at me, and I saw something there I’d never seen before.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was fear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then she turned on her heel and disappeared with her entourage back into the crowd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What did she mean?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She was just trying to rile you up, levy,” Zuli said soothingly. “She’s trying to get back at me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But when I glanced at Lylend, it was clear that wasn’t it at all. Lylend, Lylend the Naridi, the Lylend, looked scared, too?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” Iolyt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend shook his head and smiled. A smile that didn’t reach his eyes. “It’s nothing. Zuli’s right. Lev, my kynd, tonight is about celebrating your victory.” And with that he shoved an entire fylyla crescent into his mouth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I decided to join him. Whatever Queen Citrine meant, it could wait until tomorrow. Tonight—I was going to celebrate.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Blekkingstone","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"b3dfb8dac12942b082687ae4f02dd0d1"},{"title":"Journal: Myrkramaer","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0476fb5bad2a4de88a53c9d05c1d91c6"},{"title":"Fylyla Crescents Recipe","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"5089ef49f6e345aa95fd7de2ccb31f41"},{"title":"Queen Citrine","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"690297e8dc014b8d8e0d8ad2a4cbe468"}]},{"n":62,"letterTitle":"Pieter’s Prowling","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"Pieter’s Prowling (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"So, I really thought I knew Nori pretty well. I mean, we’ve practically spent every day—almost all day—of the last six months together, and yet she pulled this one out of the oyster shell like she’d been sparring all her life with Elementalists who’ve spent years training.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The fitness and forte trials are the most brutal portion of the Tableunia. It’s not just about strength or stamina. It’s a test of elemental synergy, agility, reflexes, showmanship, and sheer willpower. And you’ve got to look amazing while you’re doing it. I think you’ll agree when you see my design for nori’s sparring shyntara, that she had no problem there. Competitors are pitted one-on-one against each other in five timed matches. There is only one elimination round, and the ten contestants with the best overall scores go on to the final round. Nori still had a slight edge because of her siryn song, but she needed to win at least three matches to have a fighting chance. If we hoped to win the gloriole, she had to leave this part of the competition in second place over all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first match was flawless. Nori flowed through it like a song—fire in one hand, water in the other, weaving the elements into elegant arcs of steam and ice, pressure and flame. She used her opponent’s own momentum against them, redirecting blows with spiral-pulls and steam-shoves. Her final move, a crescent wall of boiling mist flared into a pulse of heat, brought the crowd to its feet. Decisive and chic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t worry, Zu,” Lev said, watching me pace the circumference of our crystal cell like a prowling electric eel. “If she keeps this up, she’ll move into second place. It’ll get her one step closer to taking back her first-place lead.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I paused in my pacing next to Brawlo, hoping Lev was right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo leaned his forehead into my shoulder. *NO WORRY, PRINCESS. NORI STRONG.*","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I leaned my head against his. “I know,” I said. “It’s just she hasn’t been herself since her outing with Sydian the other day.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"SYDIAN VERY HANDSOME. BUT NORI IN LOVE WITH LYLEND’S SON. CONFUSING.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re right. It must be incredibly confusing for her.” I had Lev right here, and even I couldn’t help getting a little fluttery when syd came around to visit. Even though things hadn’t worked out between us, he was still just as dreamy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But he’d never pursued me the way he was pursuing our Lunalyn Opalinya. My heart went out to Nori, really. I felt a little guilty continuing to deceive syd about her identity. With so much on the line, though, it really couldn’t be helped. One day he’d understand. However, my experience with syd told me his feelings wouldn’t change if he knew everything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Including what she looked like without Iolyt’s beauty gems. As it was, we’d upped the level of disguise in them, and due to the increasing goldabung disappearances, nori’d been instructed by Lylend to wear them day and night until we figured out who’d poisoned her. These days she only very slightly resembled her old self. She was still fair and pale but in a very fashionable way. Iolyt and I had seen to that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right everyone, here’s her second match,” Iolyt said, turning my attention back to the ceiling of the aurethryn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Here we go,” Lev said, rubbing his hands together. “Nothing like watching a couple of kyndies battle it out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“At least pretend your head isn’t full of pebbles, kynd,” Pieter spat from where he sat in the corner.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ugh! When had he come in? I saw Lev’s smile slip, and I glared back at Pieter. I couldn’t help doing an immediate double take. He looked awful! Like he hadn’t slept in weeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I caught Iolyt’s worried expression before we both returned our attention to the aurethryn ceiling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori walked onto the arena floor. She looked a little ruffled from her last round. I’d have to send Brawlo to bring her back in after this match for touch-ups. The kyndie she was sparring with was from Rasili, and it showed. Not a hair out of place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori made the first move. The Rasilian countered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I saw it. The flicker in nori’s stance. The half-second delay in how she responded. The sweat beading on her forehead far too early in the round.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The poison isn’t gone,” Iolyt said beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“NO,” I AGREED.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew this could happen. Nori never wanted to share during practice—she always gritted her teeth and insisted she was “fine.” But “fine” didn’t mean recovered; it meant she refused to burden anyone, convinced we all had enough on our plates. I was starting to see this as one of her defining traits, likely a product of practically raising herself in the Olden realm. What she struggled to accept was that the Hood was a family—an imperfect, dysfunctional one (ahem…Pieter), but still a family. For most of us, it was the only semblance of one we had, so we looked out for each other. I was still waiting for Nori to fully embrace this fact.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which was why seeing her struggle like this made me feel so bad, especially since I was the one who begged her to enter the Tableunia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The match seemed to end sooner than the last with Nori barely scraping by with a win.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sent Brawlo out to fetch her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why is it so important how I look for a sparring match?” Nori panted, while Iolyt and I worked our glamorous magic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do I really have to say it again, Nori?” I huffed nervously. “This is the tab-leu-nia!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, but Lev did great the other day.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“NO!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all jumped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter was suddenly there beside us. “You will not stop trying because Lev had a good myrkramaer.” He said it with surprising earnestness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori blinked up at him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then, as if not knowing how he got there, Pieter glared at all of us and stormed from the cell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What the?” Nori breathed. “He looks terrible.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is there anything we can do about him, Iolyt?” Lev asked, looking after his father, genuine concern wrinkling his brow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Actually, yes,” Iolyt said, pulling a small pouch out of her pocket. She poured its contents into her hand. There were five tiny, sphere-shaped stones, each a different color. “These are called nymuriel. As soon as you can, I want you to slip them in with his other healing crystals.” Iolyt returned them to the pouch and handed it to Lev.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll do my best, but he’s hard to track down these days,” Lev said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know, but please try,” Iolyt said. “I’m worried about him too.” Then she turned back to Nori. “That being said, libkyn, Pieter is actually right. Lylend’s still worried. Especially after what Queen Citrine said the other night.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What did she say exactly?” Nori asked. “And where is Lylend? He should’ve been here by now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She said something about Seraphyna not being what she seems,” Lev said. “Lylend’s gone off to Gihara knows where to figure out what she meant by it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But you don’t need to worry about any of that now,” I said. “You just need to focus on looking fabulous while you show the magistrates and audience what you can do.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah, Lune-kyndie,” Lev said, slapping her on the back so hard she stumbled forward. “These last three matches are yours.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori stood, touched her four fingers to her forehead, and gave us all what looked like a salute? Probably something from the Olden realm. Then she returned to the aurethryn. But I could see the drag in her, and I felt another pang of guilt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you think we’re pushing her too hard?” I asked Iolyt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The look she gave me was commiserating. Then she shrugged. “What other choice do we have?” She whispered, so Lev couldn’t hear. “We have to have a backup plan to get the gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. She was right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori lost the third battle. Decidedly. It didn’t help that her opponent, celestia of gliptrainia, was a giant of a kyndie, and her lean, muscular form towered over Nori like a looming mountain—a graceful, elegant, gorgeous mountain—but still a mountain. Thank goodness I added the bustle to her shyntara for extra padding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the Fourth match, nori’s hands were shaking, and Iolyt and I had to pull her aside to reassess her look. It wasn’t working for her anymore. We changed the color and cut of her shyntara and restyled her hair into something sleeker and more sophisticated. Finally, I finished her look by adding a coral comb from my own hair. It had just enough sparkle to elevate everything else.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Despite her new look, the Fourth round was an unmitigated disaster. Poor Nori. Agatta of Lobokia was notoriously good at spark spindling. And you couldn’t beat her fashion sense, what with that silky blue-black hair, edgy tribal inkings in silver and gold and her amethyst eyes. Unlike her Rasilian competitors, she didn’t even use a vanity plate.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the fifth match, Nori was absolutely spent. I could see it in every movement. But it all came down to this match. One more win would put her in the top seven—barely. But she had to win or none of her scores from the other rounds would count! It all depended on this match.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Here goes,” Lev said, wrapping his strong arm around my shoulders. “This is gonna be good, Zu. I can feel it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The aurethryn focused in on the arena. Nori stood across from her final opponent, a water wielder from my own kin-klan named fleuretine. She was obviously good, judging from the way she sent water droplets dancing across her fingertips. But having trained her myself, I knew Nori was better, if she could keep up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She looked pale, and her legs were shaking. But her gaze—her gaze—was steady and unflinching as any royally brought up cobbogothian’s. I’ll admit, it was a proud moment for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then the match began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori didn’t rush. She waited.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She waited for the first water whip strike—then turned it to steam before sweeping in with a wall of flame to blind her opponent. Good. It wasn’t about overpowering. It was about outthinking. And again, looking great while you were doing it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thanks to the wardrobe refresh and my coral comb, she looked amazing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The battle went on for the designated half-hour. Both opponents holding their own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, with a final burst of scalding mist followed by a sweeping, arcing spiral of ice that caught fleuretine by surprise (thanks to the shyntara sleeves I designed for just that purpose), Nori swept her feet out from under her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that was that. Very clean. Very classy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The arena filled with deafening cheers and colorful plausenite in nori’s favor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The ceiling changed to Nori. She stood in the center of the arena as the champion, trembling, chest heaving, a water burn across her right shoulder and water dripping from her sleeves—but she was still standing and triumphant. And she still looked amazing!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Three wins. Two losses. Just enough. The scores adjusted on the ceiling of the aurethryn: Of the ten going on to the final round, Nori was in ninth place. Ninth place in this category put her firmly in second over all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She did it!” I cried. Literally cried. And threw my arms around Lev. He spun me around before setting me down to hug Iolyt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go get her, kynd,” Lev said, and Brawlo darted from our cell and into the aurethryn. The only person that beat him to nori’s side was Prince Sydian. He’d come down from his own cell to congratulate her personally.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could see my mother looking down from the ceiling of the aurethryn as the magistrates’ pleased faces were magnified for the audience to see their responses. She might be in second place, but it was clear our Lunalyn Opalinya was still the favorite. Even mother wore a pleased expression, which thankfully meant she still didn’t know I was involved.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt and I turned to each other then. We were both fighting back tears of pride. Rather than try and speak, we wrapped our arms around each other and watched the ceiling of the aurethryn as Brawlo paraded Nori around the arena for a victory lap.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The gloriole was within our grasp. I could taste it!","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"Pieter","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d5a3bbeb892147ad973cdeb7c5f52918"},{"title":"Sparring Shyntara","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"38aef29db7d247cf8709779741186580"},{"title":"Pieter’s Prowling (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I woke choking. My pulse pounded in my throat like I was still in Seer Stone Sink looking into the waterfall at the face of the beast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Palace, I remembered. I’m in the palace. Rasili’s upper district, where the ceilings are gold-plated and the floors are too. Kinchief DonTon’s guest chambers, to be exact.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Yet I still felt the terror I felt after seeing the beast in the waterfall the other night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sat up in my cot, trying to shake myself back to the present. I peeked out of my curtains. Zuli wasn’t back yet. After my Tableunia win tonight, she and Lev had gone to see some traveling Cobbogothian Crystallum in the lower city—a sort of Cobbogothian circus was what it sounded like. Apparently, there were flying flamebirds and harmonic cave bears or something. I was so exhausted from my sparring matches and drowsy from the tangarite tea Iolyt had given me to make me sleep, I told them to go on without me. After dreaming of the beast for the third night in a row, I kind of wished I’d gone to the Crystallum.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I rubbed my eyes and exhaled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d told Lylend everything that had happened at Seer Stone Sink, of course. But his reaction hadn’t been what I expected.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He’d gone very still. “Describe it to me again, this beast,” he’d said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I did, and then with a seriousness I couldn’t argue with, Lylend said: “You can’t tell anyone about this, kyndie. Not yet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why not?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know I ask a lot of you, and a lot of it is on faith, but this is something you’ll just have to trust me on. For now. If what you say you saw was real and not just some trick of the seer stone, it’s more important than you can possibly imagine to keep it secret.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I hadn’t told anyone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not even Zuli, which had been really hard because she wanted to know exactly everything Sydian and I had done on our “date.” When I had to tell her we’d just roamed the tunnels in order to avoid breaking my promise to Lylend, she was quite possibly more disappointed than I’d ever seen her. Which made me start to wonder if she still has at least a tiny crush on Sydian and was living vicariously through me. Not that I could blame her. I mean, don’t tell Lev I told you that, but just because she’s as committed to him as I am to James doesn’t mean Prince Sydian is any less gorgeous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The silence in the room shifted suddenly, and I froze, listening.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Music?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everything was making me jumpy these days. Even music. It was faint and strange—rising from the lower city like mist curling up the cliffs. It was off-kilter, beautiful, winding through the air like smoke. Fiddle-like strings. Something rhythmic beneath it. The traditional Rasilian golden timpani. And then distant laughter. This city never seemed to sleep, at least not during the Triolune Festival. It kind of reminded me of Times Square in New York.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But how was I hearing music in here?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I realized—the window. It must be open.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shoved back the curtains of my cot and padded across the cold stone floor. A breeze tangled in the hem of my shyntara, lifting the long braid down my back like someone had just exhaled against my skin. Zuli must’ve left the window open? As a water wielder, she was often complaining about how dehydrated she felt inside the volcano, so far away from her native territory.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The window was opened. As I reached up to close it, I couldn’t help wondering how Zuli ever thought a relationship with Sydian could work—before her banishment and meeting Lev, of course. But she was raised in the sea, while he grew up in his father’s volcano.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I saw it again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Outside the window, sweeping low over the rooftops of Rasili—the red, bird-like creature was back. Only this time it wasn’t hiding in the shadows. I saw it clearly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And it wasn’t a bird at all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, it almost looked—human?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Slender. Lithe. Wings like massive scarlet fans tucked close to its back. Feathers trailed from its arms, shoulders, and even its crown. The moonlight caught them like oil-slicks, red edged in gold, moving like living flame. And it was dancing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"No—more than dancing. It was moving to the music as though it was the music. Every note, every chord rising from the city below seemed to pull it closer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched, mesmerized as it stepped lightly from one rooftop to the next, twirling, leaping, bowing. The way it moved was almost like fire. Erratic and hypnotic. I couldn’t look away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pressing a hand to the windowsill as my beating heart drowned out the music, I held my breath somewhere between awe and unease. The creature came closer. Rooftop to chimney. Ledge to lantern post.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then it stopped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It turned its head toward me and I was able to study it—to really get a good look.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Its face and head looked human, but there were feathers strewn through its hair, and its eyes were strange. More like a bird.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Up to that point, I hoped the darkness of my room was keeping me hidden from it. That hope vanished when the creature suddenly jerked fully in my direction, its strange eyes pinning me where I stood. For a moment of sheer panic, I felt like a butterfly in a display box.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then it beckoned to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not a gesture exactly. More like an invitation. A ripple of movement through its feathers, a tilt of its wings, as if saying: You. Come.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I had time to think, I’d already stepped forward. One foot on the sill.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The wind tugged gently at my shyntara. I wasn’t afraid. I wanted to go.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Knock. Knock. Knock.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gasped, stumbling backward from the window for the second time since seeing this creature.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What was I thinking trying to climb out the window? I wasn’t a bird.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As if agreeing with me, the scarlet creature turned sharply, wings flaring, and vanished in a blur over the rooftops.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Another knock.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I pulled out my hearthurite and opened the tapestry to my chambers. Tolliver, one of Kinchief DonTon’s pagets—and another worshipful admirer of Lylend’s—stood behind it, pale and twitchy with his little paget cap clutched in both hands.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tolliver,” I said, voice still thick with sleep. “What is it? Have you found Oriette?” Oriette had gone missing again two days ago. I’d asked Tolliver to keep me updated about her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The boy shook his head miserably. “Still no word. Nobody’s seen her. Not even the kyndies in the kitchen.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt a cold chill slip into my chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry, Lunalyn,” he said. “But I—I think…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” I asked, kneeling down to his height. “What do you think?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He grimaced. “I think it was the lady in the walls.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stared.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She comes out of the walls,” he whispered. “Takes goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ve heard of her,” I said. “Oriette talked about her.” I kept the part about her potentially poisoning me to myself. No point frightening the poor kid more than he already was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you know about her?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tolliver wrung his cap. “My cousin was taken by her. And I…I think she took Oriette too.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My mouth went dry. “Why would you think that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shifted on his feet. “Because…a few weeks ago, two men rescued my cousin. He and several other goldabungs from the Forest of Dag. They’d been taken there by a bunch of dogrils—dogrils that put them into grimcages.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend had mentioned hearing something like this before he left to find answers about Seraphyna.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The men found them in the forest. They fought the dogrils off, and brought them home.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And the woman?” I asked gently. “Was she there?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He swallowed hard and shook his head. “But I’ve seen her since. The same lady. Most of the palace goldabungs have. Only now she’s not hiding. She watches me. I saw her yesterday, just before she slipped through a tunnel tapestry.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was about to ask him more about the men who’d rescued his cousin—maybe I could talk to them, ask them for help finding Oriette.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tolliver, I—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Tolliver was suddenly shoved inside as Pieter tripped across the threshold. If it was possible, he looked worse than he had at the forte and fitness rounds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Galena!” Pieter roared, his eyes wild and face slick with sweat. “I know you’re here!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tolliver yelped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I got to my feet and went to him. “Pieter!” I cried, tucking Tolliver behind me. “What are you—?!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s here, isn’t she?” he bellowed. “She’s watching you! I know it—knew she would the first minute I saw you! Show yourself, witch!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tolliver,” I said, grabbing the boy’s shoulders. “Go. Now. Get help.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He bolted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter spun, eyes scanning every corner of the room. There was an ugly purple vein protruding from one of his temples as he began tearing open cot curtains, yanking open drawers, and leaning out the window.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s here. I know it. I can feel her! Can’t you, kyndie?!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re not well, Pieter,” I said, trying to sound calmer than I felt. “You need Iolyt.” Where were the nymuriel stones Iolyt had given Lev? I could’ve sworn he left them here before he and Zuli went to the Crystallum.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But before I could look for them, Pieter seized me by my wrists. “Shut your window!” he thundered. “If you do nothing else, Noriah—shut your window at night.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Stop!” I yanked free just as the guards arrived—Prince Sydian at their head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Enough, Pieter!” he said, voice cold as obsidian. “Get him out of here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He’s not well,” I tried to explain. “If you could send for Iolyt, she’ll take him away.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter struggled against the guards, eyes still blazing into mine. “You’re fools. All of you. She’s been in this palace—in this room! I can feel it!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They dragged him out, and the room fell silent.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you all right, Lunalyn?” Sydian asked, gently touching my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned toward him. “Yeah,” I said, but I was shaking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Would you like me to have him locked up for the night?” he asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” I said. I didn’t dislike Pieter that much. “He’s not well. He hasn’t been himself since we arrived here. That’s all.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is there someone I can fetch for you then?” he asked. “Or I could stay? I hate to leave you alone after an encounter like that.” There was a hope in his eyes I didn’t understand—didn’t want to understand, if I was being honest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” I said, assuring him with a smile. “Don’t worry about me; I’ve seen Pieter in a rage before. Never quite so unhinged, but he’s not one to hide his feelings, if you know what I mean. Just ask Lev.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I will,” Sydian said, his perfect brow furrowing in anger. Honestly, it made him look even better—more mysterious and dangerous. “If you don’t mind, I’ll be leaving a guard outside your door from now on in case he comes back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I looked up to see he’d brought the creepy guard with him. I wondered if he recognized me from the night I was poisoned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s okay,” I said. “I’m fine, really. The goldabungs need looking after more than I do.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At this comment, Sydian looked somewhat deflated, but this was my chance if I wanted to help find Oriette.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You remember Oriette?” I began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, she’s gone missing again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. I heard about that. We’re doing everything we can to find the missing goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I haven’t seen her in two days, and Tolliver just came to tell me he thinks ‘the lady in the walls’ took her. Do you know anything about that?” I asked. “Is it just a ghost story the goldabungs tell each other?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know,” Sydian said honestly. “I used to think it was, but now I’m not so sure.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded. “My sister Princess Sylva told me recently that she’s seen her, and if there’s one thing I know about Sylva, she would never lie about something like this. She couldn’t bear to add to the pain this is causing my father.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your father?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes.” Sydian ran a hand over his flawless face. “For all his bluster, his heart is quite tender. Particularly toward my sister and her goldabung friends.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is she friends with them?” I asked, only having seen her with Queen Jayda a couple of times during the Tableunia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded. “Yes. With Tollie, and Oriette’s sister Elaris, actually. Sylva and Elaris were raised as sisters practically.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see. Has anyone asked Elaris about Oriette?” I asked. “Maybe she knows where her little sister is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian shook his head. “Unfortunately it was Elaris who first alerted us that her sister was missing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And now I’ll bid you goodnight, Lunalyn,” he said. “But you’ll forgive me if I insist on leaving a guard outside your door despite your protests. Between this and your poisoning, I don’t want to risk your safety again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded reluctantly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With that, Prince Sydian left while giving the creepy guard instructions.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he was gone, I locked the tapestry after him—Zuli would be able to get back in with her hearthurite—then I crossed to the window and shut it, bolting the latch tight. I stood there for a long moment, forehead pressed to the cool pane.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The creature was gone. The window blocked the music out. The silence in the room felt too large as I thought about the creature and Pieter’s weird behavior, and then of Oriette gone missing. Something sinister was happening here at DonTon’s palace—something that scared me almost as much as seeing that beast in the waterfall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I pulled back the curtains of my cot and reached for my rodãor under my pillow. I kept it there for convenience’s sake, but tonight—from now on actually—I was going to be sleeping inside of it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only I’d had an extra rodãor to lend Oriette before she went missing.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Sparring Codex","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4a4074f51ae0468d87f381c28f088076"},{"title":"Nymurel","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a1bef5490e704430b127a4820d678023"},{"title":"Tangarite","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"12c0025392a7485f8805ff4c19545da0"}]},{"n":63,"letterTitle":"A Princess’s Plea","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"A Princess’s Plea","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Of all the disguises tollie had come up with, this one was by far the best. The kid was a genius with the details. Like a regular sherlock hollander…or was it sherlock holmes?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After we met, Tolliver insisted I call him tollie—a rare privilege, apparently—when he realized I was one of the fellas who rescued his cousin from the dogrils and their grimcages. Ever since, he’d been checking in on me regularly, helping me find the right stones to change up my disguise when the need arose. He’d even been sneaking me the good food from the royal kitchens so I didn’t have to keep eating the gruel they served in the lower levels of the palace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After all his help, it was only right I return the favor when he asked me to meet someone for him—someone “special” who needed my help.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently, this person was so “special” that we had to meet up outside the palace. And not just anywhere. We had to meet up at goldabung Hub, which was located in an abandoned smeltery just outside the city—an ancient one by the looks of it. I had to use my goldabung—a.K.A. Most uncomfortable—disguise to even get in here. I mean, you try shrinking several inches in every direction and tell me how you feel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The place looked dead from the outside—just another slag-covered shell with its chimney snapped in half like a broken tooth. But once I was inside, it was like I’d entered goldabung hive. The little tykes made it hum in here—like it was its own little world with its own set of rules—like ants in a giant’s ribcage. They’d made the ruins breathe again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everywhere I looked was evidence of goldabung ingenuity. Even down to the tarp-like tents and hammocks strung up between the rusted girders and rafters. They lit the place with sunstone shard lanterns hung on frayed rope. Sorting piles lay everywhere with their spoils from the day—rock, dust, maybe gold dust if they were patient enough. There were also hundreds of peculiar painted stones all over the floor and stacked into cracks and makeshift cubbyholes in the walls, some kind of goldabung graffiti stones or something.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They’d made this old drafty building kinda homey with the old smelting vat serving as their hearth. Fire crackled in it like it belonged there. They even had their own delivery system made up of old copper pans and had buckets strung up near their tents and hammocks to hold each goldabung’s belongings. From what I’d picked up the past half an hour, they were living true to their code, freely sharing what they had, passing items back and forth between tent and hammock by their pulley system.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d been taking it all in, admiring this scrappy little tribe of street urchins and their creativity as I sat at their makeshift table, keeping myself to myself. Tollie and his special guest were running late.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sat off to the side where they’d cleared a stretch of old conveyor belt for a table and had set up crates for stools. This was their mess hall—crowded, loud, full of stolen bread and laughter. The goldabungs ate like every crumb was gold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I knew I’d already been here too long. Tollie should’ve been here an hour ago, and I was starting to get suspicious looks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How goes the flame, golda?” Yet another goldabung said as she passed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Bright, golda. You?” I said. It was the only goldabung lingo tollie had taught me, which I’d gathered was the equivalent of, “how’s it going?” Or “what’s up?” You could only say that so many times before someone wanted to sit down and have a conversation with you and ask you more questions than you wanted to answer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, one of the older goldabungs—about fifteen or sixteen—was heading my way now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, tollie decided to appear in the doorway at that moment. A silhouette barely visible beneath a dark cloak followed him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he spotted me, the kid grinned and started my way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He was bypassing anyone calling out to him, moving fast, and I might have missed my first glimpse of the face of the will-o’-the-wisp trailing behind him if it weren’t for a draft coming down from the broken chimney. It blew the Hood off her head, revealing that unmistakable trail of gleaming white gold coils on her head spilling down like winter sunlight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What in the world was tollie thinking bringing Princess Sylva outside the palace gate?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was pretty clear I wasn’t the only one stunned. In fact, the whole room went eerily silent as every goldabung in the place stopped dead in their tracks—like someone had hit pause.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just as suddenly, every goldabung came to their senses and rushed into motion again. They fell to their knees, lowering their foreheads to the floor. Some didn’t stop there and went all the way down on their bellies, touching their noses to the dusty planks and holding their breath like they were afraid to breathe the same air as the princess of Rasili.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, please,” Princess Sylva said, “please get up. You’re my friends.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Several of the younger goldabungs responded right away and jumped back to their feet, running to her. The older goldabungs were a bit more cautious. Especially the fellows.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And I don’t blame ’em. I’m not lying when I say I’ve never been one who cared too much about a girl’s looks…I mean, I’m a guy, so I notice, but I don’t go crazy ’til I’ve had an actual conversation with her. So when I say Princess Sylva was the most beautiful girl I’d ever seen, you should take it to heart.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It actually didn’t seem right, someone like her standing there in all that ruin—like liquid gold glistening in a pile of ash. I’m sure tollie did his best to disguise her, but look what he had to work with! Every bit of her seemed to sparkle. From her shyntara of some silvery gauze to her flawless bronze skin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And don’t get me started on her eyes. They glittered more than any of her—rose gold, like copper under frost. I mean, I’d seen pretty before. Fancy too. But this…she was something else. Beautiful in a way that made your chest kinda ache.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hey Tollie,” I said, then cleared my throat. I did an awkward Bow thing to the princess. “Your Metallican.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hello,” she said. Then she smiled, and I kid you not, I actually had to look away. No wonder tollie was sweet on her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry we’re late,” tollie said. “I had to come around the back way for the princess’s safety. As you can see, my disguises were useless.” He grinned at the princess, who was actually blushing. So she was at that magical age where beautiful girls haven’t discovered their beauty yet. Fourteen? Fifteen maybe? But this was Cobbogoth, so who knew how old she was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What can I do for you, princess?” I asked, motioning for them to join me at my end of the conveyor belt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were situated, Princess Sylva leaned in. “Tollie told me what you did for his cousin in the forest of Dag,” she said, then hurried on. “I’m so sorry my father has your friend locked up, but I’m hoping you’ll still help me find someone else who is missing. Well…two someones actually.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right,” I said. “Who are they? Where’d they go?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Their names are Elaris and Oriette,” she said. “Elaris is my best friend, and Oriette is her little sister. They work in the palace. Oriette has been missing for nearly a week, but three nights ago Elaris disappeared. They both left all their things and no note as to when they’ll be back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d heard Tolliver and even the prince mention this Oriette. “Do they have family close by?” I asked. “Could they have gone away to visit a sick aunt or something?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sylva shook her golden head. “They’re goldabungs,” she explained. “They only have each other. And Elaris wouldn’t have left without telling me. She’s like my own sister. We’ve known each other since we were three, when she and her baby sister were left at the palace gate.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Left?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” tollie explained. “Same thing happened to me. There’s a law in Rasili that any parentless child left at the palace gate must be taken in by DonTon. As kinchief, he’s obliged to take them in, care for them, and teach them a craft until they reach the age of fifteen.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Most of the goldabungs who come are sent away when they come of age,” explained the princess, “but some, like Elaris, Oriette, and Tolliver are trained for palace work and stay.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, so could Elaris have changed her mind and decided to leave the palace and have taken her sister with her?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She would never do that. Besides, she’s only my age. She has another year before she’d be able to leave.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched the princess for a minute, thinking she looked even prettier with tears in her eyes. But she was fourteen. Way too young to think about like that. I shook the thought away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right princess,” I said. “Why don’t you tell me what you think happened to her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Have you heard of the lady in the walls?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I said. “I remember the goldabungs’ stories.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, they aren’t just stories,” Princess Sylva said. “I’ve seen her with my own eyes. That’s why I insisted we meet here so there wasn’t any possibility of her overhearing us.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a rumor going around among the guards at the palace that the Tableunia contestant who’d thrown up on stage had been poisoned by a mysterious red haired lady.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t tell me you still don’t believe she’s real?” Tollie asked. “Don’t you see how frightened we all are of her?” He gestured around at the floor and walls covered with the goldabungs’ graffiti stones.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“These are grythstones. They hold a goldabung spell to keep danger away—to keep the red haired lady away from the Hub.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do they work?” I asked, doubtful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I suppose it depends on what you mean by ‘work’,” the princess said. “They make them less afraid, and that is a kind of magic, isn’t it?” Then she glanced around and lowered her voice. “Elaris and Oriette both had one by their cots though, so do they really protect them? I don’t know. Most considered it a form of folk magic.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think they work,” Tolliver shrugged. “I’m still here, aren’t I?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princess Sylva gave him a patronizing smile. Poor kid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But there wasn’t really any point in wasting time arguing about goldabung superstitions. “Okay,” I said, steering the conversation in a different direction. “How do you think I can help?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princess Sylva hesitated. “I’ve spoken to my father, so I know you’re already trying to discover who’s doing this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All I ask is that you help me find Elaris and Oriette as well. Help me find them and don’t tell my father they’re missing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why not?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because if he found out they’d been taken by the lady, even if they’re found, he wouldn’t allow me to be friends with Elaris anymore. He’d think they’d brought me too close to danger and were too big a risk.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If the stories around the palace were true, she was probably right. It was no secret that if you wanted to get to DonTon, you did it through his children, particularly his daughter. Apparently, Rasili’s kinchief was a romantic and had been so in love with his first wife that after she died giving birth to Princess Sylva, he never let the princess out of the palace unchaperoned by a heavy, special guard he’d trained himself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All right princess, I’ll help you look for your friends,” I said. Of course I was going to help, but I saw an opportunity here as well. “But I’m going to need something in return.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course! Anything!” She said, another blinding smile making her face all glowy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh boy, let’s try that again,” I said. Now that I knew how young she was, my brotherly instincts reared their head. “I want you to answer that question as if I could be a bad guy, because technically you don’t know that I’m not.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t understand.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m trying to be delicate here, princess,” I said. “Someone as pretty as you should never agree to give ‘anything’ to anyone in return for a favor. You never know what they’ll ask for.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh? Why not?” She asked. And then understanding set in. “Oh!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tollie grinned his approval at me. He got it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What I need from you, though, is help getting into your Brother’s quarters.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Syd’s? Why?” She asked, her eyes turning cautious.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Good. She did have a danger radar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because I have it on pretty good authority that whoever this ‘lady in the walls’ is, she’s not working alone.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And you think syd could be helping her?” She asked, her voice rising in anger. “He would never—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Now hold on to your horses there, princess,” I said. “I never accused anyone of anything. All I know is in order to access the tunnels, this lady’d have to be working with someone pretty powerful back at the palace.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But my brother wouldn’t—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And you’re probably right,” I tried to soothe. “I just need to cross him off my list of possible suspects. That’s all.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princess Sylva still looked angry, the color in her cheeks only adding to her glow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You can trust him, sylv,” Tolliver coaxed. “He really did rescue all those goldabungs. Why would he do that if he was bad? And you and I both know Prince Sydian isn’t at the bottom of this. Just let him prove it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She continued to eye me suspiciously. “I can’t betray my brother…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But I can’t abandon Elaris and Oriette to the fate you found in the forest of Dag either.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So what’ll it be?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the end, clearing her Brother’s name and finding her friends won out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"An hour later, she and tollie were leading me through tunnels we’d entered through a cave in the back of DonTon’s volcano. Apparently this was a secret passage only the princess and the goldabungs knew about. The more I learned about this strange palace, the more I worried about its security.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How many of these tunnels are there?” I asked them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Three besides this one,” tollie said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Could this lady be getting into the palace through one of these?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why not?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because of those.” Tollie pointed down at the floor of the tunnel. Every yard or so, there was a piece of dark blue crystal. I’d have missed it if he hadn’t pointed it out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Those are ovynir crystals. For our enemies.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do they do?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tollie gave me a sinister little smirk that made me a little nervous. “Never become our enemy, and you won’t have to find out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Booby traps. Got it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After several miles of uphill walking, we finally came to a gilded tunnel that expanded out and up. I hadn’t seen this tunnel before. We entered, and after another half mile of hiking, Princess Sylva produced a platinum piece of hearthurite and opened a tapestry. Behind it was a room that looked like a spiraling cathedral. Only instead of stained glass windows and angelic murals, there were rows and rows and level after level of tapestries with symbols carved into them. I recognized some of the symbols from the tunnel tapestries throughout the palace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This is Heartfire hall,” the princess explained.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Whoa!” Tollie breathed. “It really exists? I thought it was just an old goldabung Tale.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, it’s real. This hall holds every entrance to every tunnel that leads to any room in the entire volcano—even ones no one uses.” She turned to me then. “I hope you’ll consider how vulnerable I’m making my family and myself by showing you the heart of our palace.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I just want to get to the bottom of this and get my friend his freedom back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She smiled, then reluctantly handed me a shard of platinum hearthurite. “This will give you access to any tapestry in the palace.” She pointed behind me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned to see an unfamiliar tapestry on the bottom level of the hall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That leads to syd’s rooms,” she said. “Please send for Tolliver if you find something about Elaris. He’ll be able to get a message to me any time of day.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, and she and tollie left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once inside the tunnel leading to the prince’s quarters, it didn’t feel familiar at all. For one, there was no sign of the forked tunnel I’d passed through last time. But then again, I’d seen three other tapestries in his rooms besides the one that led to the staircase—the one I’d heard the crying behind. This had to be one of the other tunnels that led to his rooms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally reached the end of the tunnel, I waited and listened for any sign of life on the other side of the tapestry. If the prince was in there, I guess I could just tell him I’d gotten lost. I still looked like a goldabung, after all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Using the hearthurite, I slowly opened the tapestry, peeking around the edge before entering the room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once again, what I saw was not what I expected.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everything was different.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stepped into the empty suite to find a very different set of rooms than I’d followed the prince to last time. First of all, the air was cooler, cleaner. The furnishings still looked expensive, but in a simple, sophisticated way. The walls were dark, rough stone veined with copper rather than the polished gilded marble of the others. They weren’t plastered with gold leaf and jeweled mosaics—there were only a couple of pieces of art, and they were simple. One was of a gorgeous woman who looked like an older version of Princess Sylva, and the other was a landscape mosaic that covered most of the wall opposite his hanging cot.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A desk of blackened ironwood sat beneath a narrow window with papers stacked in neat but flexible piles. The cot frame was forged from distressed copper—no carvings, no velvet, just clean lines and high-quality linens. Even the sunstone lanterns hanging here and there in strategic places looked like something chiseled by hand—elegant but solid, like everything in here had a backbone. Like the prince. After watching him compete in the Callowynder arena the other day, he definitely had one. These rooms made way more sense for the prince than the last set.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But the most important difference in these rooms was what wasn’t there. The tapestry to the stairs? The one I’d come for. There was no sign of it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If these were Sydian’s rooms, then either he’d done some major renovations in the last couple of days or I’d been in someone else’s rooms the last time I followed him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So whose were they? DonTon’s? Queen Jayda’s? Was there a royal family member I didn’t know about?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"OR…","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Was Princess Sylva less naïve and innocent than she seemed?","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Goldabung Hub","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"080c86dc406f42e2bcd35f7f47557adb"},{"title":"Journal: Grythstones","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"dbd3790c63f44bc5966b56a7e2c4ea7f"},{"title":"Rock Craft","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2e286150d49e4514a877805178d9e884"},{"title":"Princess Sylva","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f734d6aa5a704913a4ff653612abd0f3"}]},{"n":64,"letterTitle":"The Banquet and the Beast","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Tolliver","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"7c2df2f23c614be183eed56720637a5b"},{"title":"The Banquet and the Beast","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I should’ve known something was off the moment Zuli insisted I wear my “Extracurricular” shyntara. That’s what I called the extra event shyntara Zuli designed for me. This one was the nicest Lylend’s money could buy, other than the one I’d be wearing a few days from now in the final round of Tableunia. But I didn’t even like to think about that one.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ll thank me later,” Zu said, sliding the final of five—five!—weaving stones into place like they were armor instead of luxury. “You’re going to look like a queen.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But I’m not a queen,” I mumbled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re a finalist. It’s close enough. Now hush and let me fix your hair.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took twenty minutes, a half dozen crystal beads and combs, and at least one Adolorian curse word I’d never heard her say before to get every hair in its Zuli-approved place. Then she put what I was calling “the feather headdress” on my head. When I finally caught sight of myself in the looking glass, I no longer recognized the girl blinking back. I was getting used to that by now, but this was next level. Even my collarbones looked “elegant” and polished. I even had eyelashes…black ones.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You look like you belong in a story,” Zuli said, standing back satisfied.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t argue. Not because I agreed but because I was going to be late. The banquet was being held in Queen Jayda’s banquet hall. It was several levels up, which meant I was going to have to go the long way if I didn’t want to get this extremely delicate-looking shyntara dirty by taking the back tunnels shortcut. Now that I had Iolyt’s navagate stone, I knew the back tunnels were the fastest way to get to any part of the palace—without going through Sydian’s Heartfire Hall, that is. When Zuli had placed the final stone in Iolyt’s Beauty Gem cuff around my wrist, I was pronounced finished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Grabbing a piece of huldecite on my way out in case I needed to hide from Sydian afterwards, I closed the door, only to turn and run right into someone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ooph!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I looked up and froze.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the creepy guard from the guard’s quarters.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, excuse me,” I said, making to skirt around him as quickly as possible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn Opalinya?” he asked in a gravelly voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m meant to escort you to his Metallican, Prince Sydian’s banquet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“His banquet?” I asked, my mind addled. “Never mind. It’s okay. I know the way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry, but the prince insists.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Great. This was going to be a super uncomfortable walk through the tunnels. In the end, I nodded and let him lead the way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We walked on in silence for about fifteen minutes, passing several people along the way. Since we were taking the main tunnels, I relaxed a little and let my mind slip into thought. A lot had happened in the last few days—a lot I still couldn’t talk about—and my brain was trying to make sense of it all. Hopefully Lylend would get back soon, and we could talk some more about what I’d seen at Seer Stone Sink and why he didn’t want me telling anyone about it. Then I could tell him about the strange bird creature outside my window and about Pieter—Pieter who’d gone missing again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I looked up and realized I didn’t recognize where we were. “Hey,” I said. “Hey, I think we’re going the wrong way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hay is for horses, miss. My name is Karyk.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, Karyk. I think we’re going the wrong way.” After he failed to help me locate the Tableunia aurethryn when I was poisoned, my trust in his navigation skills wasn’t high.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The prince changed the location of the banquet,” Karyk said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, to where?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Heartfire Hall, miss.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Heartfire Hall? Really?” I’d gotten the impression from Sydian that Heartfire Hall was some sort of family secret or something. It was why I hadn’t bothered using it since he showed it to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I immediately felt the nerves gather behind my ribs like caged birds. What if Zuli was wrong and I’d overdressed? Heartfire Hall was impressive, but it wasn’t Queen Jayda’s opulently gilded banquet hall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We entered a tunnel along the grand corridor that led to the kitchens. When we reached a side tunnel, we followed it until we reached a tapestry. Karyk opened it for me, and I stepped inside alone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The spiraling, cathedral-sized room looked different than it had the other night. It glowed in soothing light—low, steady sunstone sconces set between each symboled tapestry. The sconces were on every level, but tonight only the bottom rows were shining. The ceiling arched high above me, glittering in the dim light with old runes and constellations—like a night sky. It was breathtaking. And strangely…intimate? Not the setting I would’ve expected for a banquet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I realized there were no other guests. No crowds. No table set for ten. No sign of the other contestants. There was only one small low table set in the middle of the floor. It was surrounded by sumptuous pillows and cushions and was set for two.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lounging amongst the luxurious sitting arrangement was none other than Prince Sydian. He was immaculately dressed in a midnight-blue shyntara adorned with curling celestial symbols embroidered in silver, copper, and gold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He stood as soon as he saw me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn,” he said warmly, as if everything was exactly as it should be. “You look…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He finished the sentence with his eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt heat crawl up my neck.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This…” I started, then stopped. “I thought this was for the finalists.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His brow furrowed gently, not unkindly. “It is,” he said. “Or at least…one finalist in particular.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was it? That was all the explanation he was going to give me?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Instead, he offered his hand to guide me to the table.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took it, still off balance by it all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All I could think was if Zuli knew this was just a banquet for two and didn’t tell me, we were having words when I got back. Strong words.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We sat, and Sydian removed the copper covers keeping our “banquet” warm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I thought after our last outing, I’d try something a little less…mysterious,” he said. “You seemed troubled last time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh that?” I waved away his concern. “I was just cold and tired. It’s been a long few weeks.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I understand.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And yet, you don’t look the least bit phased from competing in the Callowynder tournament.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please,” he said, motioning for me to begin eating first. “Perhaps it’s because I enjoy it so much. Forgive me, but aside from your siryn song, I get the sense you don’t enjoy the Tableunia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How could you tell?” I asked, making a subtle puking motion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He laughed, charmingly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The food? It was disgustingly perfect. Which was great because I was starving. It was delicate and fragrant, arranged in swirls and blossom designs. I spent most of the first course trying to figure out if I was supposed to eat the flower garnish or compliment it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian, of course, seemed perfectly at ease—like he hosted surprise romantic banquets all the time for startled competition girls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which I still wasn’t sure about. Sydian was a mystery to me. I knew he liked me. He wasn’t pulling any punches there. What I couldn’t figure out was why. Pretty much every other girl in this competition was eons prettier than me. Even with all of Iolyt’s beauty stones. Sure, Zuli and Iolyt had completely transformed me—and there were even some aspects about it I liked more than I cared to admit—but it wasn’t my beauty that made me stand out. It was my strangeness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried not to fidget. Or to think about James or what I was going to tell him if my playing a part as Lunalyn Opalinya and allowing Sydian to court me ever came up between us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian, on the other hand, didn’t seem to have any such distractions.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’ve surprised me,” he said, lifting his goblet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I do that,” I replied. “Usually by falling down or throwing up all over magistrates.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” he said, smiling, “by winning—by not giving up after what you’ve been through.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I blinked. “Oh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You went from being everyone’s pick with your siryn song to having to claw your way back after being poisoned. It’s inspiring, Lunalyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well,” I shrugged, uncomfortable, “poisoning builds character, and all that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He laughed again. Honestly, it was a little unfair how nice his teeth were.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Somewhere between dessert and the lyfine berry wine that tasted like toasted starlight (which also might as well have just been juice, since apparently Cobbogothians can’t get drunk), the conversation somehow drifted to Cobbogothian dancing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m a liability,” I confessed. “My last attempt at dancing involved an awkwardly enthusiastic entrance into a mosh pit and a table full of New Year’s Eve punch.” (All you need to know, reader, is James threw a New Year’s Eve party his senior year. It was my first. I showed up just long enough to humiliate myself. The end.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mosh pit? New Year’s? I’m not sure what any of that means, but you’re in luck,” Sydian said, “because Rasilian dancing is very forgiving.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not with my coordination.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He leaned in, eyes glinting. “Have you heard of the Weaving Dance?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “Zuli mentioned it once. She made it sound like interpretive staring.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He threw back his head and laughed, exposing his strong jaw and Adam’s apple. (Note to self, ask Zu if they call Adam’s apples something else in Cobbogoth.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s more than that,” he said. “It’s a conversation without words. Fire for passion, air for trust, water for memory, earth for promise.” With each word, his voice grew more velvety. “Every couple performs it for the first time on their weaving day.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. “Sounds intense.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It is. Want to try it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I choked on my wine-juice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I—no! I mean—why would I—? It’s a weaving dance for couples.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Exactly,” he said, rising and offering his hand. “Let me show you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stared at his hand like it might catch fire. “I don’t think that’s a good idea.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s just a dance, Lunalyn,” he said, voice coaxing now. “It only means something if you want it to.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For a moment I froze. What was the right move here? After all, I was playing a role, and the more I leaned into it, the closer it seemed to bring me to winning Rasil’s gloriole—the one thing in all of this that was sure to bring me closer to finding James. But was it possible to lean in too far? Could I lose myself in it?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well, reader, I was about to find out because I stood up. For the record, I blame the wine-juice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian led me beneath the scrawling sunstone chandelier that hung from the ceiling and spiraled light all the way down to a few feet above our heads. The sunstone sconces cast everything else in dream light. No music. Just footsteps. Breath. Flame.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We began.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, it was simple. A step forward. A mirrored turn. Our hands brushed. Then held. Then his palm was on the small of my back, guiding me through a spiral that brought us inches apart.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt the heat of him through my shyntara, on my skin. My breath hitched before I could stop it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His eyes searched mine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The steps changed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A deeper rhythm now—slower, closer. Each movement asked a question. Each one expected an answer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And I—er, Lunalyn—was answering. With my feet. With my hands. With the fluttering in my chest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He spun me once. Twice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then caught me—one hand at my waist, the other still holding mine—until my back brushed the wall. And I realized I was cornered again. And this time, it wasn’t a mistake.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian’s eyes held mine. He didn’t rush. He didn’t force anything. He just looked at me like I was a secret he was about to kiss open.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn,” he whispered, and this time it wasn’t velvet—it was grit, soft and raw.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He leaned in.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And reader, here’s the truly terrifying part. I wanted him to. I wanted Prince Sydian to kiss me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just for a second, just long enough to forget that I wasn’t actually Lunalyn Opalinya, I came back to myself. I was Norah Lukens, whose heart didn’t just belong to James but was welded to his through kinsyska.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nope!” I said, ducking under the prince’s arm just in time. “Holy moly that was close!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn, wait!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I was already through the nearest tapestry and bolting down the tunnel. Who cared where it led?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I don’t know how long I ran. Long enough for the hem of my shyntara to tear, for my breath to start hitching, for the echo of my own footsteps to become the only thing anchoring me to the present.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What was I thinking?! How had I let this happen? And why was my mind still back in Heartfire Hall, still twirling, still dangerously close to kissing a really, really good-looking prince, while my body was in full-on panic mode, barreling through Rasili’s back tunnels like I had fire snapping at my heels.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Get it together, Nor!” I muttered, slowing my pace. So what if I almost let myself get kissed by a prince at a banquet I didn’t mean to attend? Like Sydian had said, it only meant something if I wanted it to.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that was the question. Did I? I’d spent so much time trying to convince myself Sydian wasn’t adorable or that I wasn’t flattered by his attention that I was pretty sure I felt the opposite. So what did that mean?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The tunnels grew narrower. Darker. Colder. Any recognizable tunnel had long since vanished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I remembered Iolyt’s navagate stone and the huldecite I’d brought to help me out of a potential tight spot with Prince Sydian. (If only I’d thought to use it before he tried to kiss me.) When I reached into the pocket of my shyntara, though, there was nothing there. The stones must’ve fallen out somewhere along the way. And why not? I’d blitzed through who knew how many tunnels like a crazy person.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I was pretty much lost. And I really, really didn’t want to run into a troll down here. As I wandered through the dim tunnels, I couldn’t help thinking back to the lava tubes that led to Uncle Jack’s Crystal City. Was that really only less than a year ago? And now I was competing in beauty pageants and dodging prince’s kisses.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the tunnels began to slope downward, I knew something had shifted. I heard it before I saw it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was only the faintest rush and echo of it somewhere ahead. But it was unmistakable. If water could get in, it could also probably lead me out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please,” I whispered. “Please be what I think you are.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed the sound until the tunnel widened again and the walls began to gleam faintly, reflecting what little light there was from the moss-veins in the stone. Moss was a good sign.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then—just like that—I found myself back in Seer Stone Sink. I nearly cried from relief.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The long oval chamber yawned before me, mist curling through the open sinkhole above. Far below, the underground river roared where it cut through the cavern floor, probably rushing toward some exit waterfall on the back side of DonTon’s volcano. The massive heart-shaped ruby seer stone stood in its place, unmoved, still pulsing with quiet power.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew the way home from here. Just down the tunnel to the right would put me back on course toward the main tunnels of the palace. But I paused, almost tempted to go stand on the seer stone again. Would I see the beast a second time? Had I really seen it the first?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started for it, but something else caught my attention—something far beyond it on the other side of the cave. Like the seer stone, it was red. But it was a different red, a bright, punchy crimson. Like a flame caught in midair.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I crossed the cavern only to find the object was on the far side of a small natural land bridge, spanning the river below. I hadn’t noticed it last time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gingerly, I stepped onto the stone bridge, each footstep sliding out, testing its stability.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It held, and when I finally reached the other side, I knelt slowly, heart pounding, because there—caught in a crack in the stone—was a single, vibrant red feather. Longer than my forearm and curved like a blade, it glinted with a faint, golden iridescence when I turned it in the dim light.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I should’ve turned back. I almost did. But I couldn’t help thinking of the bird creature from the rooftops.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then, just past where the feather lay, I looked up and saw a section of the cavern wall that…shimmered. Not in the usual way. Not like water or heat or quartz light. It shimmered like a veil being held over something real. Like a mirage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stood, walked slowly to it, and reached out a hand. Just as I expected, my fingers went right through. The wall wasn’t there. It was like a hologram, but not in a Princess Leia glitchy kind of way. This was seamless.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated. Was this the smartest thing to do? Probably not. But I was making mistakes left and right tonight, so what was one more?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a step forward, through the mirage, and found myself in a dark tunnel. This one wasn’t lit with the strategic sunstone sconces from the palace nor was it smooth with centuries of wear and tear. This tunnel looked fresh, like it had just been dug.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The air shifted the moment I passed through—thicker, colder, tinged with moisture and something sooty. I lit a small flame in my palm, cradling it low so the shadows didn’t stretch too far ahead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The tunnel sloped downward. Just as I began to wonder if I was going to hit a dead end, I heard it again: water. Rushing, pounding really, like this water was coming from a much bigger source than the waterfall at Seer Stone Sink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a few more measured steps forward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then the tunnel opened into a massive cavern, hidden from the outside world by an even bigger—and I mean bigger—wall of rushing water on the opposite side. It reminded me of Skogafoss in Iceland and unfolded before me like something torn from a Tolkien novel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The cave was a peninsula of stone walls. Glittering stalactites hung from the ceiling, stalagmites shot up from the floor, while pools of glimmering runoff were scattered throughout. Acid green moss covered much of the walls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then I saw them. The cages.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My stomach knotted. Tolliver and Oriette had called them grimcages. There were dozens of them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cages woven like baskets with a yellow glowing stone in the top. They were in a darkened corner on the far wall of the cavern, stacked in uneven towers like crates in a warehouse. Most were empty.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But not all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I carefully scanned each stack until I found any sign of life. Sure enough, there were several goldabungs trapped inside the grimcages toward the back wall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then I found her. Little Oriette. I’d have recognized those corkscrew curls anywhere. She was curled in the corner of a grimcage, her hair tangled, her little hands tucked beneath her armpits to stay warm in such a damp cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My heart stopped. Was she alive?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then there was movement. Yes! Scared but alive. And she wasn’t alone. Two other goldabungs, one who looked like Oriette but older and a small boy I recognized from the palace. They were curled beside her in the same cage, eyes hollow, faces drawn. There were several others too. As I scanned a little further down, I saw another familiar face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No!” I breathed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it was true. There was Tolliver, locked in one of the grimcages as well.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I started toward them, forgetting everything else—","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"FWUMP!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sound made my whole body seize. I knew that sound well. Those were wings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dogril wings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Three of them burst through the waterfall in a tangle of leathery wings and furry, fanged bat faces. Their screeches were deafening, even with the rushing water. I ducked behind a stalactite, breath caught in my throat.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One of them sniffed the air.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I had to go. I had to get help. I turned and ran, retracing my steps up the hidden tunnel as fast and silently as I could, the red feather still clutched in my hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I made it back to the bridge, I threw a frantic glance over my shoulder, making sure I hadn’t been followed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ooph!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slammed into him before I saw him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Miss Lunalyn? Looks like we keep bumping into each other,” Karyk said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I backed up. “What are you doing here?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The creepy guard shrugged. “I might ask you the same thing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My hand tightened around the feather.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He took a step toward me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Here, give me your hand. I’ll help ya cross.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I wasn’t about to trust anyone down here. I retreated backwards, but this time it was too fast—too frantic, and I slipped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Watch out!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to catch myself, even scraped my knuckles on a nearby crystal pillar but overcorrected instead. Before I knew what had happened, I was falling—falling through darkness toward the black, rushing river below.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first thought that came to my mind as the bitter cold water rushed in around me was of Oriette and Tolliver. Who would help the goldabungs now?!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I distinctly felt large paws snatch me around my waist, lifting me from the water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the briefest moment, I wondered how on earth Brawlo could’ve found me here. He was supposed to be with Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then my head hit stone, and the last thing I saw before the blinding pain took over were black wings, spreading out before me and the terrifying, piercing eyes of the beast.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Shyntara","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e8d209d83ab0408e924920e9c850b1e9"},{"title":"Weaving Dance","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"56c37b62753a413488e7cceee4ee949a"},{"title":"Feather","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4368adeb7be849e49a12d08e0626f166"}]},{"n":65,"letterTitle":"Leborana Falls","narrator":"Lylend","pieces":[{"title":"Leborana Falls (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Well, human, who’d have thought we’d be climbing a waterfall together again? And so soon? I try to limit my scaling of Leborana falls to once a century, but unfortunately queen citrine’s insinuating barbs from the other night warranted another brush with death.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I don’t believe I described Leborana falls to you last time. Let me oblige you now, shall I? That way, perhaps you’ll understand the urgency of the matter. I’ll make it easy for you. Get on the internet, where so many of you are wasting your puny lives these days and look up glymur waterfall in Iceland. That pretty much sums up Leborana falls. It’s at the end of a vast canyon, with a raging river that must be swum first. And then we climb. Leborana’s sacred record room is located three-quarters of the way up within a rodãor stone hidden in a small cavern behind the falls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Why do we keep it there if it’s just a rodãor, you ask? Because, human, it contains every vital, sacred prophecy, historical account, legend, and encyclopedic account our realm has ever known, not to mention every record we managed to bring with us from the Olden realm after Phitessnie’s blood sum. They are all contained in the sagar stones and are faithfully kept and curated by the sagarine. See my included notes on both, for I haven’t the time to explain them to you here.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, the faithful steed Brawlo has accompanied me. Lyttie is so close to creating a safe hoption hole substitute to help us travel faster, but, as brilliant as she is, she hasn’t quite worked out all the kinks yet. I don’t know about you, but I don’t particularly relish the idea of my soul being torn from my body mid-transport. That’s what happened to the poor troll she sent through on her last attempt. Consequently, until she gets it right, I’ll be traveling by foot. I may as well be riding your subway system back in the Olden realm, though. By the Gihara! I’ve seen fylyla drizzle faster than Boston’s T.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That is why riding Brawlo is my preferred mode of transportation these days. Not only is he much faster than your Boston’s T, but he’s cracking good company to boot.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now back to Leborana falls. Of course, Brawlo couldn’t accompany me up the waterfall. He’s a remarkable beast with a fearlessness I’ve yet to see in other creatures. However, scaling raging waterfalls seems to be his limit, so I left him splashing around in the river while a school of water nymphs insisted on adorning him in flowers. I headed up alone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Leborana’s rodãor was right where I left it the last time. Once I was safely inside, I was greeted—albeit silently—by the sagarine, whom I had unknowingly offended five thousand years ago by insulting her favorite legend. She still hasn’t forgiven me for it. In my defense, it is the legend of how flame flecks got their name. It’s interesting enough, but when compared to the vast tomes of the far more fascinating legends we have, that’s the one she picks?! By the Gihara!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Alas, we are on “nodding terms” now, so that’s something. She will at least nod to acknowledge my presence or, if she’s in a good mood, to answer a question.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mycan-tahl wore the traditional silver shyntara of the sagarine. It trailed behind her like mercury as she led me to the correct record room and then to the category of sagar stones I came to see. Unfortunately for me, the stones were agitated.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What’s that? Never heard of an agitated stone, human? Well, if you ever have the privilege of beholding a sagar stone, you will know it is agitated if it hums. That’s right. The sound is unmistakable and quite unnerving, if you must know. It means it senses that something is amiss—something in the prophecy it contains is either coming to pass or is being deliberately frustrated. I suspect the latter, which means my journey to Leborana falls was warranted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I found the particular sagar stone I was looking for, I carried it to the sloothing stone {again, no time to explain…see my notes} and placed it in the smaller basin. After inserting my Qualdrine into the key dial, I spun it once, and thousands of images, words, ideas, and events flashed before me in the larger basin. I adjusted the stone dials various times until I began to see what I was looking for.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Using a sloothing stone has just as much to do with intention as it does with selecting the proper sagar stone, so I held the question I needed an answer to firmly in my mind, waiting for the whirlwind of images and ideas to stop on the most likely answer.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when something happened—something that in all my time coming to Leborana falls has never happened before. The sagar stone cracked. Then it shattered into several shards in the smaller basin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, the answer to my question flashed across the large basin. I stopped the dial from spinning and held it still, freezing the image, words, and ideas into place. I stared at it all for several minutes, dissecting every detail of not just a prophecy from long ago, but so much more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I finally came to understand enough of what I saw, I felt a sense of dread that I haven’t felt since I saw Cifer standing in the shattered shards of his Qualdrine prison back in the crystal city. I genuinely wished I hadn’t come to Leborana falls this day.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mycan-tahl stood staring at me with her white glowing eyes. In all my millennia visiting Leborana falls to seek answers, human, I have never once heard a sagarine speak—let alone mycan-tahl.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Today was the exception.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Three words. That’s all she said to me, yet she might as well have pushed me from the falls into the raging river below for the effect it had.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She is Náfenaeri.” {Now-fen-a-I-ri}","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Do you understand what that means, human?! No, of course you don’t.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A Náfenaeri is so rare—so unnatural—I haven’t heard the word, let alone spoken it, since the albine disappeared after Bruxrac. Why? It is because a Náfenaeri has everything to do with the pheneria. A Náfenaeri is the corpse of a pheneria, a cobbogothian’s most sacred evolution—its purest form. A Náfenaeri is a being forged in light only to be shattered in shadow, just as the sagar stone had shattered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The closest similarity I can think of in your realm, human, is that of a fallen angel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But a Náfenaeri is worse because she doesn’t just fall from the light. She falls and then she shatters. And that makes her very, very dangerous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Mycan-tahl refused to say more. She signaled for me to gather the fractured sagar stone, its surface raw and glistening with what I can only describe as crystalline tears. She then motioned for me to keep them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After carefully stowing the weeping shards away in my own rodãor, I descended Leborana falls to where Brawlo lay napping on the shore as the nymphs placed an elaborate water lily crown on his head to accompany a robe woven from Gihara blooms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come, my friend,” I said, mounting him after shooing away the nymphs and blowing away the robe with a gust of wind. “We’ve no time to waste.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*NAPPING NEVER A WASTE*, Was his groggy reply as the nymphs cursed at me for ruining their masterpiece.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I settled in for the ride back to Rasili, however, Brawlo tensed beneath me. I knew I couldn’t keep what I’d learned from him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"SOMETHING VERY WRONG.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” I agreed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"NEED TO STOP LEV.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Again I agreed. “If we don’t stop him from competing in the rest of the tournament, his very life could be forfeit.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo lurched forward, pausing only to shake a massive Gihara bloom from beneath his left front paw.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A moment later, we were galloping down the canyon without so much as a backward glance at Leborana falls.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Leborana Falls (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I woke up with the taste of gritty river water and stone in my mouth. My eyes burned, and my body felt like a troll was standing on my chest. Not that I really knew what that felt like, but I could imagine it wasn’t pleasant. And neither was this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, I thought I was dead, like Frodo Baggins sailing to the Undying Lands. But then the pain came. It was dull at first, then sharp and pulsing behind my eyes like someone was hammering at the center of my skull.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sky above me was heavy with clouds and brooding, like rain would fall any second. I was on a shore, curled into the fetal position. For a second I wondered if I had just traveled through a DMD and passed out in the dark matter only to wake back up in my body—hey, it had happened before—but my clothes were stiff and soaked, my hair clinging to my face, and there was no DMD map on the ground beneath me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sat up and looked around. Across the water, a mist slithered over the surface of the lake—Tarn Sils—just a few miles from the Hood’s camp outside Rasili. I recognized the trees on the other side.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took a minute before memory slammed back into place, jerking me fully awake.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The cave. Seer Stone Sink. The ruby heart-shaped altar. The waterfall feeding into the black river. Then dodging Karyk, only to slip and fall off the narrow land bridge into the surging river below. That moment of weightlessness before I should’ve been gone forever.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then—the beast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I blinked and stood up too fast. The pain split through my forehead where I’d knocked it on a ledge when I fell—or was it when the beast rescued me? Was it a rescue? I supposed it had to be because here I was…alive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I heard a branch snap. I spun around, and there it was—the black-feathered beast. It was standing just on the edge of the lake, the fog swirling around its massive paws and body like smoke rising from the deep.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was my first real look at it. It was as tall as two men, shoulders broad as a gate. It had to be male—there was nothing feminine about this creature. His wings, black as ink and iridescent in the dim light, rippled like silk thread stitched into velvet. His eyes were the color of heated, white-hot stone—molten, piercing, ancient.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We stared at each other. Me, gasping and shaking. Him, still as the lake.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then, he turned and vanished into the mist.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What in the world!” I breathed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My legs were shaking like they never had before when I started for the Hood’s camp. Thankfully the urgency kept me upright. I made my way toward the opposite side of the lake. My mind was so full of the beast, I arrived at the campsite much sooner than I anticipated. It was a mystery how I had made it that far without passing out or throwing up due to the pain searing through my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally arrived, however, no one was there. The site was empty. There wasn’t even a glimmer of an open rodãor on the ground or against a tree—nor was Brawlo lolling around on one of the giant crystal boulders.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt a panic rising in my chest—well, MORE panic rising in my chest. Not just because I’d just been face to face with the beast but because I needed to tell someone about it. I needed someone to tell me what the heck was going on—that I wasn’t as crazy as Fridrika Bjornsardottir was when she set Cifer’s dogrils free.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sat down on one of the boulders, determined to wait no more than fifteen minutes before heading up to DonTon’s palace to talk to Zuli about it. Something told me Lylend wouldn’t love the idea of me walking the streets of Rasili unchaperoned—not that I wanted to, not with the red haired lady (who was definitely not Totherma) out there. And then there was the beast. I mean, he may have saved my life, but what for?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ten minutes later, I was still sitting on my boulder. I was shivering now, and it was starting to get dark.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when it hit me. Of course! Why hadn’t I thought of it when I first woke up on the shore!? True, Totherma said to use them only in emergencies, but if this wasn’t an emergency, I didn’t know what was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I opened my own rodãor—I never went anywhere without it these days. After retrieving a calming stone from Iolyt for my headache, I grabbed my crystal scroll containing the DMD maps Totherma had given me. I hadn’t dared open one since arriving in Rasili, which wasn’t great because I was getting rusty. But if anyone could answer my questions about this mysterious beast, it was Totherma, and the only way to talk to her these days was either to wait for her to contact me or to summon a Dark Matter Door.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I found a more secluded spot in a tighter grove of trees nearby. It was still close enough to hear if a member of the Hood showed up but dense enough with foliage and heavy enough with shadow to hide me from anyone or anything that might wander by.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knelt down, opened my crystal scroll, and spread the DMD maps out in front of me. My breath was beginning to fog in the sudden chill of the evening. I studied the maps until I found the one I was looking for, the DMD that would lead me straight to Totherma.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I closed my eyes, visualizing first the glyph that would allow my soul self to leave my body so it could travel through the dark matter. I traced the familiar knotted maze design in my mind. It had been a while, but sooner than expected, the mandala-like design of light appeared beneath me. It scrawled over the forest floor, like light-filled veins of the earth and like a human-sized crop circle glowing with energy.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t waste any time. I laid down in the fetal position inside the DMD, visualizing the second DMD map that led to Totherma, and waited for the world to vanish around me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It did, and then my soul self was standing in the second circle, looking down at my body in the fetal position. The first door would keep my body safe until I returned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment I was racing along a path of violet light, like ley lines drawn with an invisible hand in the pitch-black of the Dark Realm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed the path until it opened into another small grove of trees. And then I was standing in the middle of an identical DMD, but instead of being in the middle of a dense grove, I was suddenly in a familiar room—a record room. Unlike Lylend’s, however, this record room was more like a deep pit in the ground with a sort of skylight or breathing hole dug out in the ceiling between two massive roots.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There were still cubbyholes crammed full of crystal scrolls of every size and shade imaginable, but rather than being carved from stone, the cubbies were carved from earth and lined with some sort of crystal that looked more like milky glass than stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the middle of it all sat Totherma, radiant, with hair the color of blood, and eyes the color of twilight. She was seated on the floor. Her legs were crossed as though she was meditating, but her eyes were open, and she was examining something against the dim light pouring in from the breathing hole. It was a thin geometric crystal that looked like it could be some sort of Cobbogothian monocle.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I noticed a peculiarly carved hanging worktable beside her where several other similar geometric monocle crystals lay. Totherma had stacked them into strange lattices and patterns that I soon realized were conjuring images in the air. Each time she lifted a stone to the light or shifted a stone here or a stone there, the images above her did the same. I cleared my throat, and she turned, smiling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Ah, libkyn,” she said, “it has been some time. I wondered if I might be seeing you soon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know I was only supposed to use the DMD to contact you in case of emergency,” I said apologetically, “but something crazy—well, crazier than usual—has happened.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can tell,” she said, pursing her lips. She stood and approached me. “By the looks of you, you’ve recently had an adventure. One which involved water, perhaps?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait. How did you—?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I didn’t, libkyn,” she said with a warm smile. “You look like you’ve just nearly drowned.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She took my hand, and that’s when I realized she was in her soul self form as well. She led me to a fire—amber and weightless, crackling in an earthen fireplace at the corner of the record room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sit,” she said, taking one of two strange whirling stools and situating them in front of the blaze. “Tell me everything.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I told her about the cave, the goldabungs in cages, the dogrils, the black river, the ruby seer stone, the fall, and the beast—including his inexplicable rescue of me. All the while, Totherma never interrupted. She listened with a faraway look in her eyes—like she was seeing everything I was describing, as though it was happening right in front of her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finished, she leaned back, tenting her fingers and pressing them against her lips.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’re still there? Oh, I’m so glad,” she said thoughtfully. “Malyk and Helna—the couple who created Seer Stone Sink—such devoted ones. I wondered if it had survived the earthquakes the zyrkers caused a few decades back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I blinked. “You knew Malyk and Helna?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She smiled. “Of course. I knew all the early shepherds. I was the one who assigned them to their posts.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But more importantly,” she continued, leaning closer, her voice suddenly low, “I know what saved you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You do?” I asked, swallowing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course. That wasn’t just some spirit or demon of the deep. Like you, that was prophecy fulfilled. He’s called Barakyne.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My skin prickled. “Barakyne?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The sign that Phitessnie’s blood has returned to his people—that Cifer’s end is in sight.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Phitessnie’s blood?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. In fact, I gave that prophecy myself.” She stood, continuing to tap her lips. “Now where did I put it?” I watched her begin searching through the cubbies filled with crystal scrolls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t understand. How could Phitessnie’s blood ‘return’?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well—ah! Here it is,” she said, reaching deep into a medium-sized cubby, her arm disappearing all the way to her shoulder. Then she pulled out a black marbled scroll. “No one really knows, libkyn. That’s why it’s called a prophecy. Prophecies are notorious for being ambiguous until they’ve come to pass.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So what’s the point of having them?” I asked as Totherma opened the scroll.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I suppose the Gihara send them out of love—to help the wynn who are paying attention prepare as much as we can for what’s to come—so we aren’t constantly living our lives in fear of what’s to come.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay.” I shrugged. “So why would this sign—this Barakyne—save me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I imagine it was because he couldn’t help it.” Totherma looked at me over the scroll that was now open in her hands. There was a suspicious twinkle in her eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“He can’t help going around saving kyndies who accidentally launch themselves off of bridges in caves?” I guffawed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma chuckled. “No, libkyn. From the sound of it, he wasn’t there for you,” she said. “If you take your whole sequence of events into account, he was most likely there hunting the dogrils.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In the meantime, shall I read you the prophecy?” she asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. (See included copy of the Barakyne prophecy.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma finished reading, but before I could respond, she changed the subject.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So how’s the festival going? Have you thrown up on any sacred relics yet?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I groaned. “You heard about that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I have my ways,” she winked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course you do.” I shook my head. “Thankfully no more puking has happened, but I did nearly get kissed by an extremely handsome prince.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma’s eyebrows rose slowly. “You are determined to win Rasil’s gloriole, aren’t you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well…it’s more that Lylend and Zuli are determined for me to win. Since it gets us one step closer to opening the Lemorian Crest and finding James, I’m going along with it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I wouldn’t mind kissing a handsome prince,” Totherma said wistfully. “I’ve heard tales that DonTon’s son is breathtaking.” Then she suddenly grew thoughtful. “You know, libkyn, it’s been a millennium since I’ve been kissed by a handsome man.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t exactly know what to say to that, so I didn’t say anything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She patted my cheek then and changed the subject. “Now tell me about Levian. How is he faring in the tournament?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated. “He’s doing well…I think.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You think? Don’t you know?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. “He’s got a real challenger. A quintowyn named Seraphyna. That’s why I’m still in the Tableunia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The change in Totherma’s expression was instant. “What did you say—about his challenger?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s a quintowyn named Seraphyna?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All warmth drained from her face. “You must return now, libkyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Why?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There’s no time to explain. You must stop Levian from entering the next round.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? Totherma—we need to win! If we don’t win Rasil’s gloriole—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Listen to me, Noriah,” she said, taking me by the shoulders. “Listen to me very carefully. If the Hood is going to get the gloriole, it must be because YOU win the Tableunia. But under no circumstances must Levian be allowed to continue competing in the tournament.” She began leading me back to the DMD. “Do you understand?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She grimaced an apology. “I need you to trust me in this, libkyn. All that matters right now is that you don’t let him face her again. When Levian is safe, summon another DMD to me, and I’ll explain everything.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, she guided me into the DMD. “Now, Noriah, go.” And her soul self began to fade.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, I was back in my body. I gasped awake on the forest floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could fully orient myself, the muffled pounding of paws on dirt sent me scrambling from the glowing circle of the DMD. I darted for the Hood’s clearing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo charged into the glade at the same moment as I did, flanks shining, trilling and chirping. Lylend was on his back. He scanned the campsite until his eyes met mine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kyndie! Thank the Gihara! Where’s Lev?!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hurried toward him. “He must be at the arena by now. He’ll be getting ready for the Giants’ Round.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend’s weathered face paled. “Then we’re already too late.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No!” I cut in, breathless. “Totherma says we have to stop him. We have to stop him from competing against Seraphyna again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He didn’t hesitate. He reached down. “Get on!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grabbed his hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, Brawlo was thundering toward the Callowynder arena, the wind whipping around me like Barakyne’s wings.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Leborana Falls","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"fdd3eabf54994de4b9e2a227b4fbaff9"},{"title":"Journal: Stones","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"e93f2c0aab724fa888080cd493c78aa9"},{"title":"Barakyne Prophesy Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"dd22e2eb2fc9429a88905f1509f510e9"},{"title":"Phitessnie","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"1c1af68bbc734132a0caec1a46f22089"}]},{"n":66,"letterTitle":"The Giant’s Maze","narrator":"Zuli","pieces":[{"title":"The Giant’s Maze (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If you’d told me a month ago that I’d be standing in a crystal cell inside the Callowynder arena, watching levy get ready to enter an epic-sized maze filled with elemental giants the modern world hasn’t seen since the last Triolune festival, I don’t think I’d have believed you. Oh, I would have had no doubt Lev would jump at the chance to do such a thing, and I have full confidence in his capabilities when it comes to Callowynder. He truly is the best player I’ve ever seen…except for Seraphyna, unfortunately. But after myrkramaer, I’m not so sure she’s any better than Lev—maybe she just got lucky those first two rounds. I’ll be honest, when he captured that entire hollokyn colony, I had never been prouder. Really. Not even when Nori won the first round of the Tableunia with her siryn song.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of course, since levy and I share a Kinzura, part of the pride I felt was his, but who’s paying attention to that?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Needless to say, I was extremely excited that I was free to attend the Giants’ round today, especially after having to constantly take a back seat to avoid my mother finding out about the connection between Nori and me. But if I’m being honest, I wasn’t sure if I felt more excited or nervous. I mean, who out there loves the idea of watching the kynd they love more than life head into mortal danger? And I’m Cobbogothian, so you know I mean it when I say this maze is dangerous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But you wouldn’t hear me breathing a word of this to Lev, of course. I’d die first. The last thing he needs from me is any shadow of doubt about his capabilities.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So when Lylend and Nori rushed into our crystal cell at the aurethryn, telling me I had to stop Lev from entering the arena, I was both relieved and terrified—because he’d already left, and there was no way of calling him back! Once a Callowynder round of this magnitude begins, there’s almost no way of stopping it until it’s over. But who’s to say Lev would even listen if we did call him back? He needed to win this round or at least take second if he wanted to progress to the battle rounds. And my levy is not a quitter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There has to be some way to stop it,” Nori said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But there isn’t!” I insisted, feeling the panic beginning to creep up inside me. “To stop the round would put everyone in the arena and the aurethryn in danger. Elemental giants aren’t something you mess around with.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s right, kyndie,” Lylend sighed, worrying his beard as he paced the cell.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But Totherma said we have to stop him! She said he was in danger because of the quintowyn—because of Seraphyna!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Totherma said?” I asked, my alarm rising even more. “When did you speak to her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s a long story,” Nori said. “I’ll tell you later. Right now we have to stop Lev!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend suddenly stopped pacing. “What if we went to your mother, princess?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“MY MOTHER?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. The queen has connections. Perhaps she could help us stop the round just long enough to pull Lev out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Doubtful,” I said. “The last time my mother helped anyone was when she still had adolor’s trident.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Isn’t it worth a try?” Nori asked, then turned to address the only one of us who hadn’t spoken yet. “What do you think, Iolyt?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt sat motionless in the corner of the cell. The fact that she looked at Lev as a son made her the only other person in the room who could possibly understand the fear I was feeling.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What did you say Seraphyna was?” She asked, turning to Lylend.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A Náfenaeri,” he whispered, looking around to ensure no one besides Hood members could possibly hear him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A pheneria corpse!” Iolyt gasped, her eyes suddenly tight with worry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s the direct translation, yes,” Lylend said. “But what it means is a broken pheneria—a shattered pheneria—meaning Seraphyna could blow at any moment.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If she’s as powerful as she’s led everyone to believe,” Nori interjected, “couldn’t the consequences be catastrophic?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt worried her lip. “Yes. And if Lev is nearby…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Precisely,” Lylend finished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then that settles it. If it’s a choice between the possibility of unleashing giants but saving Lev, or the certainty of leaving Lev to face a Náfenaeri in a contained arena, I go with releasing the giants,” I said. “Let’s go see Queen Citrine.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We were halfway out the door when I grabbed nori’s arm. “Oh no you don’t. You’ve got to stay here,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What? No! I should come with you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” I insisted. “You can’t. Not after all the work we’ve done for the Tableunia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But this is Lev, Zu.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Exactly. If we’re able to stop this, we’ll most likely destroy his chance of winning the Callowynder stone, which means you’ll be our only chance at getting the gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori opened her mouth to respond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, Lylend stepped between us then, hand raised. “That’s enough, kyndies.” His voice was quiet, but it shut us both up. “Time is of the essence.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nori looked at me, then Lylend, then at the ceiling of the aurethryn where Lev was now shown pacing beside the maze entrance. “You’re right. And I hate this,” she said, grimacing. She stepped back into the cell with Brawlo. “Hurry, then, and good luck!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My mother was already seated in her private cell when we approached her. She was cloaked in her finest ceremonial shyntara, a set of purple, milky silver, and pearl weaving stones creating a fluttery, iridescent masterpiece. Her crown of sea glass and coral was woven into her copper hair with a lifetime’s worth of wyndaloos holding it in place. Instead of acknowledging our entrance, she simply stared down at the arena like it was something beneath her notice. Her guards—silent, obsidian-eyed sentries, some of them looking a little too much like Rayves for my liking—stepped aside as we approached, but I felt the weight of their stares follow us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My mother didn’t look up until we were nearly at the foot of her dais.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I approached and bowed. Best to start off on the right foot if I could.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mother,” I said. “We need you to help us stop the Giants’ round.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The queen laughed, her eyes flicking toward me like I was a mildly interesting breeze. “The aurethryn is already sealed, daughter. You’re aware there are protocols.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” I said, “but isn’t there a clause for extenuating circumstances?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What sort of circumstances?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev’s in danger,” I said, hearing my voice crack. But this was not the time to show weakness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My mother could sense weakness like a troll senses gold.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt stepped beside me, placing a calming arm around my waist. “There’s a contender in the match, Queen Citrine. The one called Seraphyna. She’s not just a contender. She’s a quintowyn,” Iolyt said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’ve spoken of her before,” Lylend said, a meaningful look passing between himself and my mother.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For a moment, I saw something like fear flicker across her expression. But it vanished behind that infuriating queen-mask she always wears. “And what have you discovered about her?” She asked haughtily, “that she isn’t really a quintowyn at all?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s precisely what I’ve discovered,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And?” Citrine asked, eyes narrowing. We had her attention now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I visited Leborana falls after you spoke to us in the lower city. I visited the sagarine. What I discovered is not what I think you meant when you hinted Seraphyna is not what she seems.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My mother leaned forward, no longer hiding her interest. “Tell me,” she whispered, the seriousness clear in her voice.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She is a Náfenaeri.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My mother hissed, bored skepticism sliding back onto her face. “Myth and nonsense.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s true, Citrine,” Lylend said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where is your proof?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He stepped forward, carefully producing a leather pouch. From it he retrieved something wrapped in shynt fish skin. He unwrapped it to reveal several pieces of a peculiar-looking stone. It was cracked into multiple shards, but it was clear it had once been a single stone. Each of the shards was beading with what looked like crystal tears.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mykan-tahl gave this to me. It split when I received the answer to my question—when it told me what Seraphyna is. You know this only happens when the truth becomes too heavy for the sagar stone to contain.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s all? Just a shattered sagar stone?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, Citrine. The sagarine actually spoke to me. She’s the one who called Seraphyna a Náfenaeri.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A fractured pheneria?” The queen said. She still sounded skeptical, but she did look at the stone then. Not dismissively—truly looked at it. For a second, I saw the worry touch her features again. But then she exhaled and leaned back like the stone had told her exactly what she didn’t want to hear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s not enough.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt my chest tighten. “Not enough? Are you listening to us?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I hear you perfectly, daughter,” she said, voice as thin as glass. “But hearing you and being able to help you are not the same thing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a step closer. “Able or willing?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are you implying?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That you never approved of Lev, and now you’ve seen your chance to dispose of him. That’s what you do, isn’t it, mother. If people don’t live up to your expectations, you abandon them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tread carefully, Zuli. You may be my daughter, but your accusations are trying my patience.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt’s grip tightened on my arm. “Then what would be enough, your grace? Do you need to see him torn apart first? Will that satisfy the court? Or the council? Or you?” Iolyt asked. It wasn’t like Iolyt to speak so vehemently. This was something my mother knew.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That got her attention. Barely. Her jaw tightened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m begging you,” I whispered, throat raw. “Please. Just this once. I’ll never ask you for anything again. Lev is everything to me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The queen stood—slowly, regally, but not coldly this time. She descended a single step toward me and spoke so softly the sentries wouldn’t be able to hear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This isn’t the Tableunia, Zuli. Even if I did have influence here—even if I could convince one of the Callowynder magistrates to listen to me—they still couldn’t stop it. Not without proof Seraphyna intends to act maliciously.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean, Citrine?” Iolyt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There are over fifty elemental giants in that arena,” she continued, her gaze never wavering. “If even a tenth of them escaped—and if they happened to be the right combination of elements—they would level Rasili before we could sound an alarm.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What about the shepherds who brought them down from the mountains?” Lylend asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’ve all gone home. They won’t return for the giants until the festival is over.” My mother looked at me with something suspiciously like pity. “I’m sorry, but they cannot open that arena. Not for me. Not for you. Not for anyone.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That silenced us all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For a moment, I saw it—the woman, not the queen. Not a glimmer of cruelty. Just weight. And sorrow.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But he’ll die,” I whispered, tears brimming, my throat too tight to breathe.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Perhaps,” she said. Her reply was matter of fact, but for the first time in a long while, I sensed she didn’t take any pleasure in being right.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She turned away then.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And just like that, we were dismissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Numbly, I stumbled back to our cell on the bottom level of the aurethryn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I have given Lev one of my latest stones,” Iolyt said, squeezing my hand as we returned to our crystal cell. “It’s called solivayne. It will give him an added measure of courage, luck, and cleverness. It may just be what he needs to see him through this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew she was trying to comfort me, but then I heard her whisper to Lylend. “My only fear is it might make him needlessly reckless.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was nothing left to do but watch and wait. All was silent except for the sound of the maze shifting into place. On the aurethryn up above, giants could be seen moving in the shadows—massive silhouettes outlined within the even larger crystal walls of the maze.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev stood at the gate, looking forward, unaware.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I pressed the heels of my hands to my eyes. A moment later, arms were thrown around me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Let’s pray, Zuli,” Nori whispered after the others had told her everything. “We can still do that, can’t we?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I prayed to the Gihara like I had never prayed before—not like a princess or a strategist or even a Cobbogothian. I prayed as a kyndie in danger of losing half of her soul.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please,” I whispered. “Please protect him. Please bring him back to me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The only reply was the blaring of a horn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then the entrance to the maze began to open.","i":false}],"narrator":"Zuli"},{"title":"Solivayne","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f27e5760173e4d338c55d19d571f3960"},{"title":"The Giant’s Maze (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"If you’d told me a month ago that I’d be standing in the middle of an arena maze made of moving rock and wild weather, prepping to face down ancient elemental giants with only my wits, four elemental manifestations, and a very questionable starynth root sandwich in my pack…I would’ve probably believed you. Because let’s be honest—it’s me, Lev. And it’s the Triolune tournament. I’ve been dreaming of this scenario my whole life.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But still, walking into the Giants’ maze felt…different. Heavier. Like the air itself was bracing for something no one was ready for. Maybe it was the new stone Iolyt had given me to try out during this round. I reached into my shyntara pocket and touched the stone where I’d stashed it. She’d said it would make me cleverer, luckier, and braver. I wasn’t gonna turn down an extra boost of any of those things at this point in the competition.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The aurethryn dome shimmered above us like the inside of a gemstone, flashing images of each remaining contender as we entered the maze. The towering crystal walls were shifting all over the maze. It was like they were alive and listening. Even the light was strange in here—thin and coppery, like it was being reflected off our smallest moon Corynty.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could tell already this wasn’t going to be your average Callowynder maze. For one thing, I could hear the thundering of a waterfall in the distance. And before even entering, I could see I’d be required to climb at least one mountain at some point—probably to get to an earth or wind giant.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Only four contenders remained. There was me, of course. There was princie-kynd, who as it turns out is good for more than just looking pretty all the time. No really, despite Zu’s obvious approval of his looks, he isn’t a bad guy or a bad Callowynder opponent. Then there was Seraphyna, who somehow always seemed to be three steps ahead of me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And the other contender…well, I really should remember her name by now, but I’m pretty sure she communicates with sea creatures of every kind imaginable and smells like fylyla twists and sadness. It was probably ’cause I had to break up a fight between her and some kynd down in the market over a plate of fylyla twists. It was a perfectly good plate of pastry too, just lying in the dirt like that. It makes me tear up a little just thinking about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Deep sigh.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, the goal for the Giants’ round was simple in theory: Tame as many giants as you can. The more giants you tame, the stronger your \"army\" will be for the next round—the battle round, where each contender and our assembled elementals fight each other until only two contenders remain. Then it’s the final round—a one-on-one match for the Callowynder stone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Once the giants have been tamed, the maze transports them from inside to a special fangledãor prison each of us was given when we started the tournament. They were to be used to collect our army as we progressed through the rounds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So how do you tame a giant? I’m glad you asked. Let me enlighten you. You tame—which is just another way of saying you persuade them—by perfecting the “rite of echo”. Have you never heard of it? Well, all you really need to know is that each element has its own sequence of sound or “echo” it responds to. (You can read more about that in the journal page I swiped from nori’s notes.)","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But this round is special. Why? Because this one lets us team up with other contenders. This alliance is temporary, of course. It just helps to have someone watching your back when you’re going after giants, you know?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So naturally, princie-kynd and I decided to make a truce right away and team up to tame our weakest elemental giants together. What’s my weakest element? Well, don’t tell Iolyt, but it’s metal. I promised to help princie-kynd catch a wind giant (his weakest element) if he promised to help me catch mine. He agreed, but only if I stopped calling him princie-kynd. So I told him I’d think about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, we made our way into the air quadrant of the maze. Everything looked like someone unleashed a herd of sylphids and a covyn of salamanders on one another while some zyrkers played a game Nori calls baseball with the surrounding cliffs—a game I would very much like to see played in person one day. The result was a small valley where tornado pillars spun lazily above jagged, floating cliff islands dripping lava down their sides to the ground until it cooled into thin string-like pillars. Everything smelled faintly like ozone and something charred. This was probably due to all the scorch marks where the air giant we were hunting had unleashed lightning bolts on the surrounding earth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“After you, princie-kynd—er syd,” I said as we reached the foot of a floating cliff. He raised one of his perfectly groomed eyebrows and grinned. Then he jumped for one of the closest cooled lava pillars attaching the floating cliffs to the ground. When he was safely on top of the mass of floating earth, I followed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We made it up two more floating island cliffs, dodged a nest of sky-sylphids (one nearly bit Sydian’s perfect eyebrow clean off), and finally found a pair of air giants pacing in the distance along a glass bridge. Like the wind islands, the bridge floated at least five hundred feet above a roaring gorge below.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We started heading their way when, much sooner than expected, we reached a skyspine—a set of wind-hewn shifting bridges suspended in midair. According to Lylend, this was where the echo needed to happen. This was the easy part.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To perform the rite of echo, syd and I were both gonna have to perform the elemental chant of wind. If we hit all the pitches and rhythm just right, it would summon the wind giants.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then came the hard part. After performing the chant, we had to prove ourselves worthy of the Giants’ trust. By doing that, we had to, well, you know, demonstrate we were willing to trust them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And how does one demonstrate one trusts a wind giant, you ask? Why, by jumping into the wind and praying like mad to the Gihara that one of the giants decides to catch you, that’s how. And it’s not just a random jump either; you have to jump in sync with the wind. This meant we had to trust the air in our blood to tell us the exact moment to leap.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I went through each of the steps of the sacred ritual, just like Lylend showed me. My pitch and rhythms for the echo were perfect, and the execution of my jump was perfectly timed. Before I knew it, I had tamed my wind giant.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Now it was syd’s turn. He performed the elemental chant into the chasm below and then leapt into the wind. I had to hand it to him. The prince had iron nerves.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, he was caught mid-fall by an air giant, and I knew his echo had been accepted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Easy-peasy, as Nori likes to say.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Next it was time to tame the metal giants. We found them in the depths of a quarry. This taming was a bit more dangerous because it required us to perform the echo while dodging boulder-sized golden nuggets being hurled at us by the giants.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Syd was over and done capturing his before I’d taken cover from the first onslaught of golden boulders.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then it was my turn. I closed my eyes, breathed the air deep into my lungs, and performed the elemental chant. When I finished and it felt right, I stepped out of my hiding place, closed my eyes, and demonstrated my trust in the metal giants by walking straight toward them, golden boulders hurtling my way with each step.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It felt like hours passed before I finally felt myself being lifted in the air by one of them. When I reached his face, we stared into each other’s eyes in challenge. The next moment, I was back on the ground, and my metal giant was transported from the maze into my fangledãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just when princie-kynd was really starting to grow on me, our partnership came to an end, and we split.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Try not to lose your other eyebrow,” I called after him as he headed south to who knows where.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He chuckled in response.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I headed north toward the mountain, when I caught a glimpse of and the sound of the waterfall. My next goal was to tame an earth giant. Since I was making such good time, and since earth was my primary manifestation, I’d try for two if I could manage it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The maze shifted when it sensed me coming its way, trying to throw me off course, but I was a born-and-bred earth kneader. Peiter’d had me hunting earth creatures all my life. All I had to do now was follow the tremors.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The farther north I went, the stronger the stone beneath my boots trembled. It was coming in a rhythm too heavy for normal earth movement. That’s when I spotted the mist.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I rounded a wall in the maze when a huge, deafening waterfall, bigger than any I’d ever seen, spread out before me. It was tumbling down an enormous black stone cliff.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I approached and sure enough, at the base of the cliff, I found earth giant footprints, deep as wells and filled with water from the waterfall. This was my sign. I scanned the cliffside until my eyes shifted focus and I found what I was looking for. I let my eyes relax until they went out of focus, and then, like a hidden puzzle, a staircase that had been camouflaged, probably by some illusion stone, emerged from the cliff face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jogged toward it, scanning the clearing for any sign of the other contenders. When I reached the staircase, I started climbing what had to be a 100-foot cliff.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The stairs were slick from the waterfall’s mist, so the going was slow and steep. I nearly lost my footing two times before I reached the halfway point.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I saw her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was Seraphyna. She was three steps ahead of me, as usual.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A flash of rust-colored hair against the black stone caught the light as she vanished up the narrow switchback staircase.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Of course she was headed for the same target. Like me, earth was one of her strongest elements, and she was faster than she looked. I decided to follow her. Why? So I could let her sniff out the giants for me—do the hard part—while I swooped in, performed the rite of echo, and walked away with a new earth giant for my roster. Maybe it would shake her up a bit in time for the battle rounds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We climbed the stairs. And climbed. And climbed. I had to keep ducking out of sight so she wouldn’t see me each time she glanced back. The wind off the falls stung, and the spray started to freeze in my eyelashes and nostrils the higher we got.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I finally reached the top, the staircase leveled out into a flat path that led into a high river valley.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hiked in along the side of a rushing river that fed the waterfall below. I could just make out Seraphyna about a quarter mile ahead. As long as I hung back far enough, she wouldn’t know I’d followed her until it was too late for her to retaliate.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Almost as soon as I finished that thought, the river started to change. It got wider, shallower, and slower. It became scattered with massive boulders of every shape, color, and size. Finally, it began to rise in layered tiers, zigzagging in a gradual incline up the valley.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After a while, the boulders weren’t just boulders anymore. Not really. Some of them appeared to have arms. And shoulders. And sleeping faces carved by time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Giants! Earth giants. Half-submerged in the riverbed. They were not dead but sleeping, their snores causing the rumbling beneath my feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew I could stop right here, perform the rite of echo, and be on my way. But there was something about the way Seraphyna was moving along the path—almost like she hadn’t noticed the giants at all. Weren’t they what she was looking for? Or was there something else? Something better she was after? Did she know something I didn’t?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I paused for a moment, deciding what to do next. Was it worth risking not getting one of these earth giants in order to see what she was up to? Were there more powerful earth giants ahead? If there were, and she got one but I didn’t, then nothing else I’d done during this round would matter much.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reached into my pocket and pulled out the piece of solivayne Iolyt gave me. Now was the time for luck. Now was the time to be braver and cleverer than any other contender. I squeezed the stone in my fist and decided to find out what Seraphyna was up to. Besides, the giants were fast asleep. If it turned out there was nothing better ahead, they’d be here when I got back, right?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I kept my head down and continued to follow Seraphyna at a distance. She was graceful. She leapt from crystal cluster to boulder, around this outcrop of rock, and into that narrow canyon. She seemed too confident, but was still doing that strange gliding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I followed her, my excitement rose. The sleeping giants were getting bigger!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Seraphyna still didn’t stop.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Up ahead, the valley turned into another narrow canyon. Here giants slept standing in rows along the river. They were bigger than any I’d ever heard of—not even the ones described in the oldest crystal scrolls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I crouched down even lower, easing around a particularly large crystal pillar that jutted up from the river’s edge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I realized I’d lost sight of Seraphyna.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned, scanning the canyon for any sight of her. But she was nowhere in sight. She was simply gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I decided to cut my losses and head back. Besides, these giants were bigger than any I’d hoped to catch, and they were asleep, which meant taming two, or maybe three of them, would be a piece of ellaprava.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I moved back down the canyon, looking for the perfect spot to perform the rite of echo.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I began to notice the feathers. They were bright red and veined with gold, floating on the river like drops of blood.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The hair on the back of my neck stood on end.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I jumped as a crack of thunder ricocheted through the canyon. Lightning and flame came out of nowhere, slamming into me before I could run. My whole body seized. My spine felt like it had been struck by a massive current of electricity.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Next my thoughts shattered, and I was suddenly unmade. I didn’t fall so much as I crashed into the river. Every nerve was screaming as I sucked in ice water. My lungs revolted. I tried to summon the water from my blood, but I was paralyzed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then I was sinking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She came next.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was Seraphyna. Only I was sure my brain had been fried, cause she didn’t look like Seraphyna anymore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Instead, a bird-like creature—more animal than woman—stared down at me from the shore. Her head cocked to one side as she spread her scarlet and gold wings. Then, with a screech that could shatter glass, she lurched into the air, flying away as the river current carried me the way we’d come—back toward the thundering waterfall","i":false}],"narrator":"Lev"},{"title":"Journal: Rite of Echo","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"529c7a70e6b349089b2e2ef26435af11"},{"title":"Giants Maze","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"f05f20d018ee4df487a9a8494a6771fa"},{"title":"Seraphyna","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a0caa146d14a4dc5902513de465c0072"}]},{"n":67,"letterTitle":"Another Crimson Feather","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Another Crimson Feather (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"You’re probably wondering what happened to Lev during the Giants’ round. I wish I could tell you. It’s been three days, and we still don’t have any answers. None of us actually saw what happened. One minute, Lev was following Seraphyna to capture an earth giant, and the next minute he was rounding that crystal pillar. That’s when the whole aurethryn went dark. We could only hear something—a terrible something, unlike anything I’ve ever heard before. The closest thing I can come to describing it in terms you’d understand is that it sounded like the combination of a freight train and one of Boston’s Nor’easters. Before we knew it, a swarm of healers burst out carrying Lev’s body—soaking wet, unconscious, and looking as though he’d been severely burned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The healers were going to take him and sequester him in one of DonTon’s healing rooms, and we might never have seen him again if it hadn’t been for Queen Citrine.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I know. I was as surprised as you are. Apparently, the lady has a heart after all. At least that’s what I thought at the time. And maybe she does. But the excuse she used when she showed up at the Hood’s camp with her guard carrying Lev in a healing pod was that there wasn’t a more gifted healer in Cobbogoth than Iolyt. Why waste Rasilian resources when Iolyt could look after him better than anyone in her own qualdriny lab?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She might’ve said more, but I had to watch the whole thing take place with Brawlo from our hiding spot behind a cluster of crystal boulders. Zu banished us there as soon as she heard her mother’s voice. Afterward, though, when Zu burst into tears and gratefully flung herself into the queen’s arms, I hoped I’d see the queen’s royal mask slip and that she’d actually show up for her daughter. What happened instead was the queen set Zu aside and demanded Iolyt give her what she promised.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt didn’t bat an eye. She simply handed over a large pouch of what I was pretty sure were some of her new stones. Apparently, Queen Citrine hadn’t brought Lev to us out of the goodness of her heart or to simply be kind to her daughter. She’d come because Iolyt had made a deal with her: Lev in exchange for a set of her Beauty Gems.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All I can say is Queen Citrine blew it. Don’t get me wrong. I’m super grateful for what she did for Lev. But this was the perfect opportunity to patch things up with Zu. I mean, I’ve never seen Zu so broken or so willing to set her own stubborn pride aside. All the queen had to do was use Lev as a peace offering. Zu would’ve forgiven her for anything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, with Lev under Iolyt’s care, I figured Zu wouldn’t want to leave his side until he woke up. Boy, was I wrong about that. Turns out she’s more determined than ever to see me win the Tableunia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is why she was drilling me so hard on my “elegance and enchantment” techniques. We’d been at it all afternoon, and I was TIRED.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All you have to do during this final portion of the Tableunia is shine,” Zuli said, after demonstrating for the umpteenth time the choreography she’d created for me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We were in Lylend’s rodãor, utilizing his sparring chamber again. The enchantment and elegance category was the last of the Tableunia, and then it would all be over. Finally! Even the moons seemed to be celebrating, as they were presently nearing their eclipse in the sky, performing their “dance” before they’d separate again for the next twenty years.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The last Tableunia category was all about being and creating beauty. Not only would I be wearing the fanciest of all the shyntaras Zu had fashioned for me (and Lylend had paid a fortune for), but Iolyt had also made a special necklace to ensure I looked even less like myself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was the “elegance” part of the category. The “enchantment” part was where the choreography came into play. While looking the part, I was also supposed to enchant the audience with a sort of elemental “dance,” mimicking the lunar eclipse happening above. Hence Zu’s choreography.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right,” Zuli said, as I went over the final water steps of the dance. “Flick your wrist just a little. That will make the water arch more.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I followed her instructions without complaining. Believe me, it was a struggle. But the moment I’d seen Lev inside that high-tech Rasilian healing pod, I knew I’d do anything to try to make Zu feel better. Winning the Tableunia was the most obvious way. In fact, winning Rasil’s gloriole was sure to make everyone happy and more hopeful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Right now, hope was what the Hood needed more than anything. Why? Well, because Lev wasn’t improving. Not even Iolyt knew if he was going to pull through this ordeal. We all felt the weight of it. Lev had always been the sunshine of our little makeshift family. Consequently, it was like we were all walking around, trying to go about doing what we could in a world of overcast and shadow. There was no Lev hunting you down, cajoling you into playing yet another Callowynder match before completing the next chore, mission, or training session.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lev’s condition was also the reason I hadn’t managed to get back to Seer Stone Sink. On our ride to the Callowynder arena to stop Lev, I’d told Lylend everything I’d seen when I came across the red feather that led me to discover Oriette and the other goldabungs in those grimcages. As soon as Lev was safely under Iolyt’s care, Lylend and I returned to the site, only to discover that the grimcages were empty and there was no evidence a second cave had ever existed. There was no longer a mirage camouflaging the entrance of the cave. It was simply a dead end.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unfortunately, due to Lev’s “accident,” DonTon had upped security around the palace. As a Tableunia contestant, I was still allowed in, but there was no way I’d be able to get Lylend back to the cave to search again. It would have to wait until after we’d won the gloriole, which I was now more determined than ever to do.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lylend or not, though, as soon as the gloriole was secured, I was going after them. There was no way I was leaving little Oriette and her friends to the fate of having their souls torn from their bodies by a dogril.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is there any change in him today?” I asked Zuli during a break. I sat down next to her to take another drink of water. The water supply in my blood was running low after dancing all afternoon.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli shook her head. “It’s everything Iolyt can do to keep him comfortable. The healing pod helps, but those aren’t regular burns, Nori.” Zuli’s voice cracked then. “I don’t know if they’re even burns. Whatever happened in there—whatever Seraphyna did to him—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I wrapped my arms around her. It was true. No one had ever seen injuries like Lev’s. The only person who knew exactly what had happened to him—Seraphyna—had gone missing. As soon as Lev was retrieved from the Giants’ round, the guards began searching Rasili for her. There was still no trace.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What if he doesn’t wake up, Nori?” Zuli whispered. “What will I do without him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please don’t say that,” I said, holding her tighter. “Your mom said it herself; there’s no better healer in Cobbogoth than Iolyt. She loves Lev like her own son. She’ll find a way. I know she will.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Even as we spoke, Iolyt was hard at work in her lab, continuing to dissect Cifer’s spyolite, testing all manner of combinations of crystals, new and old, to see if she could find something—anything—to heal him. I was sure she hadn’t slept more than a couple of hours since the Giants’ round.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Zuli blinked back her tears and nodded. “Of course,” she said. Then she stood. “I think that’s enough practice for today. We’ll go again tomorrow—and finalize your music in the morning—before it’s time to get you ready.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sure,” I said. “You go be with Lev. I’ll clean up in here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Zu was gone, I began to go over the steps again. I had the water steps down pretty well. As a water wielder, Zu was able to show me exactly how they were meant to look. The fire steps, on the other hand, were a bit trickier. If only James was here to show me how a “spinning inferno” or a “kaleidoscope of chaos” looked. After all, these weren’t sparring moves. These were all for the sake of show—for wowing the audience.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I spent the next forty-five minutes practicing both steps, all the while racking my brain for some way to make my performance “shine” brighter than the other nine contestants left in the Tableunia. It wasn’t like I had anything they didn’t. They were all decidedly prettier, and while most of us were diowyns, there were two contestants who were triowyns, which gave them an edge. But there had to be something I could do better than all the rest of them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you about finished in here, kyndie?” Lylend’s voice startled me from my thoughts.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh hey. Yeah, sorry. You’ve got to go, don’t you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes. Brawlo and I are just making a short trip to the Unda Swamp,” he said. “I’m hopeful the Phoenix will have answered my questions about Seraphyna by now. It could help Lyttie with bringing Lev back. We’ll return in time for your final performance tomorrow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grimaced.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” he said. “What’s wrong?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nothing, it’s just that for the first time since beginning this thing, I actually kinda want to win it. Unfortunately, I still have no idea how.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?” Lylend’s brow furrowed. “I would’ve thought this category was the easiest for you to win. Well…second easiest, anyway.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really? Why?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Because there are really only two rules in this category. Correct?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shrugged. “I think so.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And what are they?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Look elegant and enchant the audience?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, and I think it’s safe to say Zuli and Iolyt have the first covered. As to the second, well, I believe you accomplished that in your first category. What’s stopping you from doing it again?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mean singing?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not just singing, kyndie. Siryn singing. There’s no rule that says you can’t add that to your dance, is there?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, no.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then why not? Consider it a third manifestation,” Lylend said, crossing his arms and looking down at me seriously.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kyndie, you know better than anyone what’s at stake if we don’t secure the gloriole. Can you really afford not to utilize such a powerful gift to your advantage? Who’s to say that isn’t precisely the reason the Gihara gave it to you in the first place?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I snorted. “You think the Gihara made me a siryn because I’d be performing in a beauty pageant?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My dear kyndie. This was never just a beauty pageant,” he said. “And when will you finally accept that the Gihara know, see, and are deeply involved in even the smallest details of their kyndlins’ lives—especially when there’s so much at stake?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I exhaled, feeling the weight of his words.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And now I really must be off,” he said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, gathering my things and climbing out of his rodãor. Before I had even left the clearing, Lylend had stowed his portable house away in his crystal pouch, climbed on Brawlo’s back, and was off.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If only I’d thought to ask him how he would suggest I bring this gloriole-winning siryn performance to pass. After giving it some thought, I realized there was one person who might be able to help me, though I risked him requesting a kiss in exchange for his help.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I found Prince Sydian in the palace armory. He was sparring with some of his father’s guards, and if I thought he was attractive before, well, let’s just say watching Prince Sydian metal meld up close was nothing short of knee-weakening.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The soldiers Sydian was sparring with were the guards trained by DonTon himself to protect Princess Sylva. Since Lev’s accident and Seraphyna’s disappearance, the prince was expected to win the Callowynder tournament. He was doing his best to prepare out of respect for his father and the game, but it was clear he didn’t take any pleasure in winning it like this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Prince Sydian saw me come in, he smiled, then motioned for the guards to take a break.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn. What a wonderful surprise,” he said with less enthusiasm than usual. “What brings you to the armory?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were alone, I told him my dilemma and asked for his help. The prince glanced around, making sure his guards had left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know just the band for this,” he said, taking me by the hand as we rushed toward Heartfire Hall. “You’re going to love them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A half an hour (and one very long tunnel later) he led me into an old ruin on the outskirts of Rasili. He called it Goldabung Hub and rightly so. The place was crawling with them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A black-haired goldabung ran up to the prince as we entered. Prince Sydian stepped aside with him, and the two spoke in low voices while I had the chance to look around. It was truly the worst place for kids to be living, but there was an ingenuity and coziness to it that was fascinating. It was obvious every goldabung in Rasili spent their time here when they weren’t up in the city or out at the quarries.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the prince returned to my side, the black-haired goldabung led us to a small makeshift stage just off to the right of what looked like a mess hall or cafeteria. On the stage, a small group had assembled with some of the strangest contraptions—what I assumed were instruments—I’d ever seen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know they don’t look like much,” Sydian said, “but you won’t find better musicians in Rasili.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are they all goldabungs?” I asked, looking over the scrappy bunch of kids ranging from as young as six to at least sixteen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded. “They’re street performers from the lower city. All you have to do is hum or sing the song you’d like them to play, and they’ll take it from there. By tomorrow night, they’ll have a complete, unique rendition ready and polished to accompany you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you wanna sing, kyndie?” one of the goldabungs asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was a good question, and one I’d given a lot of thought to all the way here. “I think I’ll try a song called ‘King and Lionheart,’” I said. It was by a favorite Icelandic band that Uncle Jack and I liked. The song was ethereal and had a sort of transportive quality to it that could pass as something vaguely Cobbogothian. It wouldn’t be as sentimental to the audience as “The Sacred Sleep” had been, but I wanted to enchant them, not make them cry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’re ready when you are,” a tall goldabung girl said, positioning her instrument that looked like a cross between a lute and an accordion. The others followed suit, and I began to sing it for them from memory.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The little band picked up every note and even added a section that took the song to a whole new “Cobbogothian” level. Maybe this plan would work out after all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Will you use the siryn stone?” Sydian asked after we’d finished and left Goldabung Hub.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I said. “I need all the help I can get.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I doubt that very much,” Sydian said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We followed the tunnel back to the palace in awkward silence for a while. I’d expected some weirdness after our almost kiss the other night.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were beginning the incline in the tunnel, I knew we were almost back at Heartfire Hall. I decided this was the perfect opportunity to tell Prince Sydian what I’d seen down at Seer Stone Sink. I glanced around for any sign of guards or trolls. When I opened my mouth to speak, however, Prince Sydian stopped and faced me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn,” he said, glancing over my shoulder and off into the distance. “I—I hope you’ll forgive me for presuming the last time we met.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Presuming?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You know? That you would welcome a kiss from me,” he continued, this time looking down at his boots. He nudged a stone with his toe. “You see, I’m accustomed to being the one who turns kyndies away. In my arrogance, I never once considered that you might not welcome my…er attentions.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh,” was all I could think to say. I cleared my throat awkwardly.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If I may ask now,” he began, turning his full gaze on me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My heart began to race and my palms grew sweaty.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Would you welcome a kiss from me in the future?” His question was sincere. His voice wasn’t confident and velvety like it had been before. This time it was…vulnerable?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He swallowed, then continued, “Would you welcome—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before he could finish, pounding footsteps echoed in the tunnel. They were coming our way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your Metallican!” a voice shouted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A figure approached, and I soon recognized the creepy guard from Seer Stone Sink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, Karyk?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re wanted up at the palace.” There was an urgency in his voice—almost a command. “Immediately.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it? What’s wrong?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk shook his head. “I’ve been forbidden to say.” He glanced at me. A puzzled look flickered over his face before vanishing into the regular stony expression the palace guards wore. He then leaned in to whisper in the prince’s ear, and I barely caught the word “princess.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian responded immediately. Without even saying goodbye, he darted off at full speed toward Heartfire Hall. Creepy guard Karyk was hot on his heels.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Another Crimson Feather (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I gotta tell ya, as pretty as he is, I felt sorry for the prince the moment I told him the bad news. I’d only just learned it myself, and it took the wind right out of me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean my sister is missing?” He’d demanded, and rightly so. As far as I’d seen since being in Rasili, he was a decent big bro. He and DonTon spoiled and protected Princess Sylva, some might say to the point of suffocation, but it was obvious they did it out of love.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d discovered she was missing when I went to inform her I’d found her little friends like she’d asked. Just to be sure she hadn’t left the palace to join the ongoing celebrations in the lower city and had forgotten to take her guard, I checked in with the palace goldabungs. Sure enough, they confirmed it for me—several of them said they’d seen the red-haired lady in the night—that she’d been spotted very close to the royal quarters, but none of them believed she’d be bold enough to steal DonTon’s precious daughter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The one positive thing to come out of Sylva’s disappearance was that it put my suspicions about the princess to rest. If she’d gone missing, she certainly wasn’t the one helping this phantom lady sneak in and out of the walls of the palace to steal her friends and other goldabungs. So that put both the prince and princess in the clear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d had a hunch for a few days now about who the goldabung snatcher might be. There was one place left to check just to be sure, but I was gonna need the prince’s help.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was why I was presently standing in Heartfire hall, having a staring match with the palace tapestry that led to his rooms. I don’t know what I was waiting for, exactly. Maybe it was the fact that if I was right about who the culprit was, the royal family of Rasili would never be the same again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was all because of tolliver’s note. He’d left it for me before he’d gone missing. It had only confirmed my suspicions. The sad truth was there were always gonna be people ready to sell out those closest to them to save their own bacon. I knew it like I knew the sky in Cobbogoth had three moons. In this case, though, I wished I didn’t. Sure, DonTon had taken my pal Glynt captive, but I understood the position he was in. Not only that, but I’d been in his palace long enough now to really respect—even admire—Kinchief DonTon. His kids were growing on me too. The last thing I wanted to do was tear their family apart over a half-baked hunch.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Glynt wasn’t getting any freer the longer I waited. So I pushed the tapestry aside and headed into the tunnel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My least favorite part about DonTon’s tunnels are the trolls. Thankfully, I’d found a solution to that problem in the most unlikely place—well, not that unlikely really, since this was Cobbogoth, and the solution was a stone. But unlikely because I’d found the stone just lying on the ground in the tunnels where that Lunalyn Opalinya kyndie—the one from the Tableunia that the prince was trying to kiss the other night—had dropped it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When she’d run away from him, he had sent me after her—to make sure she made it safely back to her room. While I was chasing her, the strange crystal fell out of her pocket. I stopped to pick it up and was gonna give it back to her, but it was one I’d never seen before. That, and as soon as it touched my skin, I disappeared. That’s right. The crystal was some sort of invisibility crystal.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Boy, you better believe I’ve been getting some wicked good use out of it since then. In fact, I think it’s safe to say it’s the whole reason I was able to track down the princess’s little friends in the first place. Nothing like invisibility to help you sneak up on some dingbats. My only regret is that I wasn’t able to rescue the goldabungs as soon as I found them. Tolliver was with them, and I happen to like that kid. But now, every time I go back to the cave where they were being held, there’s no cave. It’s just a solid rock wall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Speaking of that Lunalyn kyndie, tonight was the final round of the Tableunia, and there is definitely something about that girl. I was completely mesmerized by her. She sang during her performance—I guess she’s a siryn or something—but man, there was something so familiar about the song she sang…and about the way she sang it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What wasn’t familiar was what happened inside my brain when she sang. It was like I was somewhere else entirely—like I’d suddenly been sucked out of the arena and into a dreamland, a fantasy world where she and I were running away from all kinds of dogrils and dark matter creatures. Only I wasn’t scared or anything because I was this king, you know? And she was the “Lionheart” who was going to help me take back my kingdom.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Her flame-throwing, water-wielding dance thingy wasn’t too shabby either. I would’ve made some minor suggestions about her flame-throwing technique, but sure, if I was a judge, I would’ve voted for her. I mean, fantasy world or not, she did get my kingdom back and away from the dark creatures safe and sound.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And I wasn’t the only one who liked it. When the song was over and I came back from—well, wherever her singing transported me—the crowd erupted, throwing every color of plausenite you can imagine at the aurethryn. There was no need for an official announcement. It was obvious she was the winner…of this portion at least. If the gossip circling the guards’ tunnels is right, she’ll take the whole thing. I guess a siryn is pretty much the trump card in these kinds of competitions. Whether she wins or not, though, as far as I’m concerned, there’s something about her—something, just, I don’t know…magical. Ethereal. Illusive as moondust. And again, familiar. You know?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I arrived outside the prince’s rooms, I slipped the invisibility crystal inside my shyntara. I was about to knock this time—no point sneaking around when it was in his and the princess’s best interest to work with me. That’s when I heard a lot of commotion coming from inside. It sounded like a flock of birds had invaded Sydian’s bedroom.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I retrieved the invisibility crystal from my shyntara again and quietly slid the tapestry aside. The commotion immediately skyrocketed several decibels.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Prince Sydian’s rooms were packed wall to wall with a lot—and I mean a lot—of really pretty girls.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slipped inside and began skirting my way along the outside wall. I’ll be honest, I was kinda disappointed in the prince. Was this really the time to be entertaining the ladies? He had just found out his kid sister was missing for crying out loud. And didn’t he have a thing for the Lunalyn kyndie?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I finally spotted him at the center of it all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It’s also when I realized I’d just misjudged him. The frenzied panic in his eyes was on a primal level. This dude was in over his head, and he was in full-on fight-or-flight mode. But was he in danger? Only one way to find out. I slipped the invisibility crystal back into my shyntara and discreetly approached the prince.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The relief that came over him when he saw me coming his way was palpable. It was as if he was saying, “hey, fellow dude, get me outta here!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I called in backup.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The trolls were on the scene before the girls knew what hit ’em. In no time, enough of them had been escorted from his rooms to enable me to finally reach him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Can you get me to the tunnel without being seen?” He asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, handing him the invisibility crystal, and began clearing a path through what was left of the kyndies. Funny enough, these kyndies were so self-absorbed, none of them seemed to realize their prince had just vanished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we were back outside the tapestry that led to Heartfire hall, Sydian gave me back the stone and pulled me down a side tunnel and out of sight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Where did you get that?” He asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I found it in the tunnels a few nights back,” I said. “By the way, before I forget, sedi and yanth have been stealing food and silver from the kitchens since they got here. If they announce their retirement soon, you’ll know why.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian looked concerned for a second, then shook his head. “Why are you here?” He asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I need your help,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He watched me with a guarded question in his eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I need you to help me get into the queen’s chambers.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He responded exactly as I thought he would. “Why in the name of Gihara would I do that?” He guffawed, then quickly lowered his voice as the trolls passed the tunnel. They were ushering even more girls out of his rooms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you want to find your sister or not?” I asked after they’d passed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course. But what does the queen’s quarters have to do with it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I let the silence speak for itself.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The moment it clicked in his mind, I knew his own suspicions had been growing in that regard as well. If he was worth his salt, finding his sister would take precedence over protecting his stepmother.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll take you there myself,” he finally said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"We slipped back into the tunnel leading to Heartfire hall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So what was that all about?” I asked, gesturing toward the last of the trolls escorting kyndies away from his rooms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He sighed. “Would you believe me if I told you they’d all offered to comfort me during this ‘trying time for our family’?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wowza! That’s bold.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tell me about it. They don’t even know Sylva’s missing. Even if they did, though, the only kyndie I’d want comfort from is the only one who’d never show up like that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah?” I asked. “You got it pretty bad for that siryn, don’t ya?” Something surly uncurled in my chest, surprising me with an urge to do the prince bodily harm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian shrugged. “I’ve never met anyone like her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grunted my response, shaking myself and hoping the sudden urge to punch him would pass before he said too much more. Yeesh! What was wrong with me?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t long before we were back in Heartfire hall. The prince chose another tapestry—one I’d never explored before—and before he could expound on his feelings for Lunalyn Opalinya any further, we were heading toward the queen’s rooms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For what it’s worth,” I said, turning to the prince as we reached the exit of the tunnel, “I really don’t want to be right about this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded his acceptance of my offering, but when we were finally standing at the tapestry, I knew I was right. How? Because despite what I thought back in Heartfire hall—about never exploring this tunnel before—I was wrong. I had seen this tapestry before. And I knew that the rooms beyond it were the ones I’d originally mistaken as Sydian’s before Princess Sylva set me straight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You ready?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian answered by opening the tapestry. Before he could open it all the way, however, angry voices reached us from the other side, and he drew back, keeping the tapestry open just enough for us to hear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I raised a finger to my lips and pulled the invisibility crystal back out again. As soon as I had vanished, I slipped into the chambers, leaving the prince behind. Sure enough, there sat her Metallican, Queen Jayda herself. But while I couldn’t see who she was talking to, it was clear she wasn’t alone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We agreed, Galena!” The queen said. “No kyndlins from the palace. Only street goldabungs are fair game. It’s the only way to not pique DonTon’s suspicions or provoke his wrath.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And yet you were the one who allowed me access to your lovely palace,” Galena replied.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The second voice wasn’t quite as commanding as the queen’s, but there was a note to it that had the hairs on the back of my neck standing up.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That was only so you would have easier access to the new cave,” the queen exclaimed. “But you took the princess’s little friends, Galena! Of course she was going to follow you and go looking for them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Galena laughed. It wasn’t a nice sound. “What I don’t understand is why she was wearing goldabung clothes in the first place,” she said, unbothered. “If looked at in the right light, it really is her own fault the dogrils mixed her up.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t care whose fault it is! You must return her. And her friends. Unharmed. I demand it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You demand it, do you?” Galena mocked sweetly. “My dear queen, I don’t think you understand who you’re dealing with here. My master isn’t used to being told what to do, Nor will he take kindly to it. Not even when it comes from a queen as beautiful as yourself.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“If you expect me to persuade DonTon to pledge his allegiance to Cifer, you must return her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, in that case, I’ll just fly to the blood caves now and tell Cifer there’s been a mistake.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a crash as something shattered against the floor. “Sh-sh-she’s already at the blood caves?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“N-n-n-no,” Galena mocked, “but she’s d-d-d-definitely on her way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Jayda stood threateningly. “Get her back, Galena! Return her and the rest of the palace goldabungs. Fix this, or our deal is off.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, Jayda, Jayda, Jayda, do you really think you can afford to threaten me?” Laughed Galena. “I think not.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a commotion then, a noise that sounded like a window being unlatched, some rustling…and then there was silence.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The queen collapsed into her chair. She covered her eyes with a trembling hand, her face wickedly pale.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that’s when I caught sight of it. A large, bright red-and-gold feather fluttered into the room and landed silently on the floor at the queen’s feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I barely managed to grab the prince, throwing my hand over his mouth before he burst forward to confront the queen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Take it easy, Metallican,” I urged, once we were back outside in the tunnel. “How much did you hear?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All of it,” he said through clenched teeth. The anger radiating off him was intense. Not that I blamed him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good,” I said. “This way I don’t have to waste time explaining or trying to convince you of anything.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shook me off, trying to make his way back to the queen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Cool your jets, will ya?” I asked, barring his way. “We need a plan first.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s my sister!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know,” I said. “And if you really want to help her and her friends, which I promised her I’d do, we can’t just barge in there guns blazing, okay?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Guns? Jets?” Sydian asked, still angry but confused.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t worry about it. I just mean we need to think this thing through.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The prince relaxed a little. He knew I was right. Finally, he gave a sharp nod. “Midnight, no later. Plan or no plan, we’re going after my sister.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Deal,” I said, holding out my hand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Instead of taking it, he spun on his heel and left me there, wondering what in the world guns were.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"Elegance Shyntara Designs","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"572a3f93ffcb4ecca916631aca39f6d6"},{"title":"Queen Jayda","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"bc20bb5cb96142039653cfb0f4257967"}]},{"n":68,"letterTitle":"Totherma’s Daughter","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Totherma’s Daughter","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Well, I did it. I won the Tableunia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At least I think I did. DonTon won’t name the official victor until the closing ceremonies. I honestly can’t believe it. Zu and Iolyt are both certain I won, especially after my final performance. But we’re not out of the woods yet. There’s still a chance I won’t win if Queen Citrine discovers Zu and I are working together.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But aside from keeping a low profile, there’s nothing more I can do to convince the judges.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the record, the goldabung band crushed it. The King and Lionheart song was by far the coolest thing I’ve ever done. The goldabung arrangement was much more primal, and I got to do all sorts of cool Cobbogothian things with my voice that I wanted to try, so that was awesome.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But now it’s time to focus on more urgent things. Unfortunately, we just received word from DonTon’s paget—a new one that’s temporarily filling in for Tolliver and Mica, who are still missing. He told us the palace is now on full lockdown as they try to find Seraphyna. He couldn’t give us any particulars, but I was sure glad I packed up and headed to the Hood’s campsite as soon as the Tableunia was over. I would’ve been trapped inside the palace for who knows how long.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then again, maybe that would’ve been better. Maybe I could’ve helped, because I don’t think this is just about capturing Seraphyna. I have a feeling we’re dealing with more goldabung disappearances, which is why I sent a note with the paget to deliver to Prince Sydian.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everything was so crazy the night before the Elegance and Enchantment round, I never had the opportunity to tell the prince what I saw in the caves. I tried to tell him, but then creepy guard Karyk showed up. It was almost like he knew I was about to reveal his part in the whole thing—whatever it was—and showed up just in time to stop me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By that point, we’d been back at camp for five days, and Lev was still unconscious. Hood members were not taking it well. I was pretty sure Iolyt had stopped eating. She wasn’t coming out of her lab anymore. I was starting to worry about her, so I’d been bringing food to her every morning, only to discover she’d barely touched it when I brought her dinner in the evenings.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then there was Pieter, who I’d never heard utter a single kind word to Lev. Of all people, he had stopped disappearing all the time and instead started lingering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He showed up two days ago, after my final Tableunia performance, as if summoned by some paternal alarm bell he hadn’t responded to since, well…ever.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter was always sitting with Lev these days, and I still didn’t trust him, so I was keeping an eye on him from behind one of the cots in Iolyt’s lab. The guy still looked like a walking disaster. His hair was unbrushed, his jaw shadowed, and his eyes bloodshot. But so far he’d just sat there beside Lev’s healing pod not saying a word.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then, this afternoon, he just up and left. Not a word to any of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I would’ve sent Lylend after him, you know, just to make sure he didn’t do anything crazier than when he’d stormed into my room back at the palace. The problem was, Lylend still hadn’t made it back from the Unda Swamp like he promised. He even missed my final performance.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If that wasn’t enough to worry about, now that the Tableunia was over, Zuli wouldn’t leave Lev’s side. Not unless I hauled her out of Iolyt’s lab for a daily swim in Tarn Sils and a chance at some fresh air. I had to keep reminding her and Iolyt they were no use to Lev if they didn’t take care of themselves. For the most part it had fallen on deaf ears, so I decided to take matters into my own hands. I was tired of feeling helpless as I watched my friends sink into despair. Today I was going back to see Totherma. It was time to find out what she knew about Seraphyna.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I opened another Dark Matter Door.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This time, Totherma was sitting in the center of a space that looked like a simple stone cottage from fairy tales Uncle Jack or my nanny (when I had one) told me as a kid—the kind of cottage the witch lives in out in the forest. In a lot of ways, it reminded me of Iolyt’s lab, but there were also all kinds of colorful herbs hanging from the ceiling, some still living in pots and others bound and drying. There were peculiar creatures in cages and jars, all living. They were making strange, muffled noises. Where Totherma sat, there were dozens of floating, white crystals lazily drifting above her head and around her. There were also strings of light running from her fingers and attached to each one. She was reaching up and adjusting about half a dozen of them with her hands—like she was weaving threads. Each time she touched one of the crystals, it responded by glowing and pulsing at her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hello, libkyn,” she said softly…distractedly. “Welcome back. How is Levian?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Not good,” I said, leaving the DMD and moving toward her. “We were too late to stop him, which I’m guessing you already know.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She pursed her lips. “Yes.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did you know this was going to happen to him?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma continued fiddling with her crystals. “Once you informed me Seraphyna was involved, I knew it could happen—especially if he angered or startled her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, he did something, and she responded. Although we have no way of proving it was Seraphyna. No one outside the aurethryn was able to see what happened.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I see. Has anyone asked her if she harmed him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sure they would if they could. She’s missing. We’re unlikely to get any account of what really happened until Lev wakes up. In the meantime,” I sighed, “the entire palace is locked down. The city too. You can’t go anywhere in Rasili right now without being stopped and questioned by DonTon’s guards.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And yet you don’t think it’s just about Seraphyna?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. I thought of the goldabungs trapped in their grimcages, and my stomach clenched with worry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hmm…you could be right,” Totherma said, tapping her chin. “But let’s take one thing at a time, shall we?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"First, let me ask, does Lev have any wounds?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wounds? Oh…yeah,” I said, wincing a little at the memory of Lev’s charred and cracked skin.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are they burns—unlike any burns you’ve ever seen before?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” I said, moving closer to her. “The skin keeps on disintegrating to what looks like crystallized ash.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Has Iolyt discovered a way to heal him yet?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, but not for lack of trying. I’m worried she’s going to work herself to death.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma nodded decisively. “It was definitely Seraphyna then. She attacked him, and her ‘explosion,’ for lack of a better word, must have interfered with the aurethryn. We won’t know whether or not it was on purpose until Lev wakes or Seraphyna is found.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean on purpose?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I mean she can’t always control her powers. Sometimes they’re too powerful for anyone to control.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Even you?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma sighed heavily, and her eyes looked suspiciously bright. “Even me, libkyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t want to pry or anything,” I said, hesitating.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go on.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m just wondering how you know so much about her?” I cleared my throat. “It’s just…you did say you’d tell me about her when I came back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, I did,” Totherma said, standing and motioning for me to sit on a stone bench situated under one of two tiny windows in the room. She finished what she was doing by stilling the floating crystals, then with a flick of her fingers swept them aside, snapping the light threads. They shuffled over to hover in a nearby corner, making a tinkling noise as they bumped up against one another.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, Totherma came to sit beside me on the bench. “When I knew her,” she began softly, “she was called by another name—one I gave to her myself. Her true name is not Seraphyna, but Galena.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My heart stuttered. “Galena?! Pieter said that name,” I said. “He stormed into my room at the palace the other night, hollering at someone named Galena. He was acting like a complete maniac.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma nodded once. “Yes, Pieter would know that name.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But why?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s Pieter’s story to tell.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay, then what happened to her? How did she become the corpse of a náfenaeri, or whatever it was Lylend called her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The same way all náfenaeri are made,” Totherma said. “Galena was once like you, libkyn—brilliant, intuitive, capable. She was even supposed to be a space shifter like you. More importantly, she was supposed to be the next Opalian Eye.” Totherma sighed sadly. “Alas, her powers weren’t fully formed when something, well, when something—something she wasn’t prepared for—broke her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean ‘broke her’?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma didn’t answer right away. Instead, she stood and retreated to the corner where her crystals hovered. She reached out and adjusted one. It flickered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There was an event,” she said. “It happened nearly twenty years ago. I won’t say much about the particulars without causing harm to innocence. Only that it shouldn’t have happened the way it did.” She turned back to me. “It changed everything inside her. What should have developed into something beautiful, complex, and powerful—something to be mastered—instead shattered. She was still powerful, of course, but that power—the power meant to enable her to fill my place one day as the Opalian Eye—instead turned inward. It fragmented and festered until she became something dangerous and dark…even poisonous.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This last word struck a chord in me, and I thought of the lady who had scratched me—poisoned me. “She’s unstable,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh yes, and extremely combustible,” Totherma said, turning to face me again. “Lev is lucky to be alive.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed. “If you knew what happened to her, wasn’t there something you could do to help her? To heal her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I tried,” she said, her voice tight. “I tried to guide her. To anchor her. I neglected my duties as the Opalian Eye for a season just to watch over her. But it was longer than I should have, and many people were harmed because of it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then what happened?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma shrugged. “One day…she just vanished. I spent years looking for her, with no trace. It wasn’t until your return to Cobbogoth that I heard a rumor about someone who sounded very much like her. The rumor came out of Holictac—out of the Blood Caves.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What are those?” I asked, wrinkling my nose instinctively.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The Blood Caves are where Cifer has been operating from since his return.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She’s working for Cifer?” I gasped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma nodded. Again, a deep sadness seemed to have taken over her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So what was she doing at the Triolune Festival?” I asked. “Is she involved in the missing goldabungs?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma’s look sharpened. “What missing goldabungs?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The ones from Rasili. They’ve been disappearing ever since we arrived…I assumed you’d already know about it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then all at once it hit me. “The red-haired lady coming out of the walls!” I cried. “Of course!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, libkyn?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can’t believe I didn’t see it before,” I pressed a palm to my forehead. “I’ve just been drowning in the Tableunia competition. And then there are the disguises everybody’s wearing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Explain please,” Totherma urged.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Right. Sorry. It’s the goldabungs. They’ve been talking about this lady ‘coming out of the walls’ like the boogeyman or some kind of phantom, stealing away the kyndlin. I’d say she’s like a ghost, only I’ve seen her myself. Actually…I thought she was you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is that so?” Totherma said, a wry expression on her face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That was until she scratched me and poisoned me. It happened right before my diplomacy category at the Tableunia.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“When you were sick all over the magistrate?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “Yeah, but I haven’t seen her since. At least I thought I hadn’t, but I think I’ve been seeing her every time Lev’s gone into the Callowynder arena. I think the red-haired lady from the palace and Seraphyna are the same person.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And you didn’t recognize her as soon as you saw her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. “No one in Rasili looks like themselves from day to day, Totherma. Not even me. They all have vanity plates or collars…or Beauty Gems.” I held up the cuff around my wrist that was altering my own looks, though not beyond recognizability now that the Tableunia was over. “Iolyt even recently invented a disguise stone she’s planning to have Lylend try out when he gets back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lylend isn’t with you right now?” Totherma asked, brow furrowed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Uh-uh. He and Brawlo went to the Unda Swamp to see if the Phoenix left him a message about Seraphyna.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma began to pace. “How long ago was that?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Day before yesterday.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Eye of Gihara! I’m sorry to do this to you again, Noriah, but you need to return to the Hood.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” I said, standing. “I need to tell Sydian about Galena—that she’s the one most likely behind the missing goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Libkyn,” Totherma said, taking me by the shoulders. “Listen to me very carefully. You need to stay as far away from Galena as possible.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know if I can do that,” I said. “Some of my friends are goldabungs who have been taken.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma smiled, but it was strained. “All I ask is that you don’t go looking for her—that none of the Hood will go looking for her.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated. This was a promise I couldn’t make. If Galena had taken Oriette and Tolliver, I had to go after her to get them back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma sensed my dilemma. “You must remember you still carry the Haven inside of you,” she said. “But to be honest, you’ll be lucky if Galena doesn’t come looking for you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Me? Why?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Aside from doing Cifer’s bidding, she’ll try to hurt you to get to me. She’s never forgiven me for not healing her—for not stopping her from becoming what she is. She will try to exact her revenge by seeking to stop you from becoming who you need to be in order to defeat Cifer.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I still don’t understand,” I said. “How are you responsible for what happened to her?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma looked at me then, and for the first time since I’d met her, I saw the smallest flicker of vulnerability in her eyes. “I’m responsible, libkyn, because Galena is my daughter.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The words hung suspended in the air between us like a fog—a fog that caused a delay as my mind tried to compute what she’d just said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your daughter?” I finally said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma nodded. “My only child.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wow, Totherma, I’m so sorry…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She only cleared her throat and crossed the cottage to her multitude of herbs hanging from the rafters. “Just promise me you won’t go looking for Galena.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shook my head. “I still don’t think I can promise that,” I said. “She’s stolen some of my friends from the palace. I have to help them if I can.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Totherma pursed her lips and sighed as if she understood. “Yes, I suppose you do. Take this then,” she said, handing me a bundle of what could only be described as pale blue crystallized mushrooms with very long, slender stems. “You won’t be able to carry them with you through the dark matter, but take a close look at them. They’re called thassalyth. They grow in volcanic caves, so you should be able to find some in Rasili. If you can, have Iolyt add them to what she’s already giving Levian. They might help wake him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you!” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Totherma only ushered me back to the DMD.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t remember traveling back through the dark matter or even walking back into Iolyt’s healing room. My mind was still fixated on the fact that Seraphyna or Galena was Totherma’s daughter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thassalyth shrooms! Of course!” Iolyt said, once I told her about the mushrooms. “I think I might know how to gain access to them.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“This came for you while you were gone,” Zuli said. There was a glimmer of hope in her eyes as she handed me a crystal scroll. “It came from the palace just after you entered the dark matter.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took the scroll and opened it. Sure enough, Prince Sydian’s handwriting greeted me. It appeared the paget had successfully delivered my note.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Norahs Note","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0c029d366a614e22a58307570ca5f50d"},{"title":"Totherma","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"d8f311dcde1f4a13acacaf7bb40c663b"},{"title":"Tothermas Cottage","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"36f8aa2e50ea40f7b566216feba587f1"},{"title":"Journal: Shrooms","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"6e6eebae1fca4d45b884067e0306628d"},{"title":"Sydians Note","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0891747eb9f941fe8f5033ac34152387"}]},{"n":69,"letterTitle":"Unexpected Allies","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Unexpected Allies","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I’ve always wondered what it would be like to sneak out at night and meet a boy. I’d read about it in books growing up and seen it happen in movies and TV shows. I even heard about a girl out west who once snuck out to meet a boy and nearly killed herself by jumping out of a barn window to avoid getting caught by her parents. It seems extreme.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As for me, though, sneaking out was a rite-of-passage experience of my youth I never got around to experiencing. Why? First, what boy? And second, why sneak out when Uncle Jack was never there to catch me in the first place? I could just walk out the front door. There was never any need since James was the only boy I really knew growing up, and we spent all our time together by the time I would’ve even wanted to sneak out and meet him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But who says you can’t compensate for the “coming of age” experiences you missed out on as a homeschooled kid? All you have to do is find an ancient portal to a fantastical world like Cobbogoth, and before you know it, you’ll be sneaking out of your own rodãor to meet a sinfully good-looking prince who has begged you to help him figure out what’s happened to his little sister. Compensation complete.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, I decided to borrow as many of Iolyt’s new stones as I could get my hands on before sneaking out to meet Sydian at Seer Stone Sink. No one from the Hood was in any state of mind to come with me (nor would I have asked, all things considered). Lev’s unconsciousness was taking its toll. Lylend wouldn’t like it, but from the moment I read Sydian’s note, I knew I had to go alone. The goldabungs—and now Princess Sylva—were counting on us to save them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I had to successfully get past the others unnoticed. Thankfully, Iolyt and Pieter were currently out scouring the caves of Rasili for wild thassalyth shrooms. The shrooms were our last hope for bringing Lev back. Lylend and Brawlo still hadn’t returned from the Unda Swamp, so really, the only person who might stop me was Zuli.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt left her rodãor open so Hood members could keep an eye on Lev while she was gone. When I entered her healing room, I found Zuli inside Lev’s healing pod with him. She was snuggled up to his side, fast asleep. Good. She hadn’t slept more than two hours at a time in about a week.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tiptoed over to Iolyt’s worktable. There were several urns lined up along the back of it. Each was filled with samples of her new stone creations. They were labeled with the name she’d given each stone, along with a list of the properties they held. Thankfully, Iolyt was as organized as she was brilliant because we were going to need all the help we could get rescuing the goldabungs. Who knew what we’d find when we got to the dogril cave—or if there would even be a cave when we got there?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grabbed a small pouch from one of Iolyt’s cubbies and began filling it with any stones I thought might help me out in the caves. I grabbed a piece of navagate (since I’d lost mine in the tunnels) and four huldecite stones in case invisibility was necessary. I grabbed three blekkingstones—who knew when the ability to produce a good illusion would come in handy—and a handful of the solivayne she’d given Lev before the Giants’ round. Courage was always in short supply, so I took a few nymuriel. If we did find the goldabungs, chances were some of them were going to be pretty freaked out. Calming them down would be necessary if dogrils were still around.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"With my pouch full of crystals, I paused a moment to look at my two best friends—one badly burned and in some kind of náfenaeri-induced coma and the other completely exhausted from keeping constant watch over him. I really wished they were both coming with me this time. Then I left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reached Heartfire Hall without any issue. Huldecite came in real handy, I had to admit. Sure, a troll or two might’ve sensed me when I finally entered the palace from the Goldabung Hub tunnel, but my platinum hearthurite enabled me to dash into any nearby tunnel whenever a troll was around.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sooner than expected, I found myself alone inside Heartfire Hall. The tapestry to Seer Stone Sink was easy enough to locate again, having the simplest symbol. Once again, I used my hearthurite to open it, and then I was on my way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The deeper I got into the palace, the denser the troll patrols got. DonTon had ramped up security on account of the missing princess, no doubt. Careful to avoid brushing against any I encountered, I finally reached Seer Stone Sink just as the reflections of the moons neared each other in the pool of water around Helna’s ruby, heart-shaped altar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was surprised, however, to find the cave empty. I returned the huldecite to my stone pouch, regaining visibility in case Prince Sydian was hiding until I showed up. When he didn’t come out, I began exploring the cave for any sign of him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"About fifteen minutes later, there was still no Sydian. I decided to cross the bridge to the other side of the raging river below. Maybe he was waiting for me at the entrance to the dogril cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The last time I was in the cave, I was with Lylend, and the hidden entrance to the freshly dug cave filled with grimcages had been nowhere to be found. As I crossed the bridge tonight, however, I returned to the dead end, only to find there was the slightest ripple of a mirage again. I inched closer, and sure enough, the mirage rippled and the dead end morphed into the dogril tunnel.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I glanced back at Seer Stone Sink, knowing I should wait for Sydian. I just couldn’t get Oriette and the other goldabungs out of my mind. They’d already been imprisoned for too long. Waiting for the prince left them in the hands of the dogrils longer—if they were even still in the cave anymore. I decided to peek into the cave while I was waiting, just to see what we were up against tonight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I reached into my pouch, ready to retrieve the huldecite again, when suddenly the passage to the dogril cave burst with light. A flame erupted from somewhere inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stumbled backwards. As I turned, ready to scramble back across the bridge, I was suddenly grabbed from behind, and a rough hand clamped around my mouth. Before I could react, I was dragged backwards behind a nearby crystal boulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t make a sound,” a gravelly voice breathed in my ear.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I could hear the dogril getting closer—could see its shadow creeping along the passage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I swear I won’t hurt you,” the voice said. It definitely wasn’t Sydian’s, but I decided to be still anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The dogril never came around the bend. Instead, there was a distant screech. The dogril screeched in response, then retreated back into the cavern beyond.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The hand that was clamped around my mouth slowly loosened its grip.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I spun around.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You!” I gasped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the creepy guard, Karyk. “What are you doing here?” I hissed, backing away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What-d’ya-mean?” he asked. “I hang out in damp, dogril-infested tunnels all the time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You do?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His brow furrowed mockingly. “No, I’m here at the prince’s request.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How was I supposed to know that?” I said, backing up toward the land bridge. “His note said he’d come himself.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, he had to stay and watch Queen Jayda,” Karyk shrugged.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh,” I said. “Right. She must be devastated.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah,” Karyk said. “Something like that. Anyway, after I told him what we found the last time I ran into you down here, he knew the two of us could figure it out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Found?” I said. “You expect me to believe you’re not working with them?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You can believe whatever you like, kyndie. I’m here to do a job, which is to rescue the goldabungs—particularly Princess Sylva—from the dingbat—er—dogrils?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I froze. “What did you just call them?” It was a word I hadn’t heard in months.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Dogrils?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No. Before that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He didn’t say anything. Just shrugged.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You were going to call them dingbats, weren’t you?” I asked. “Where’d you hear that word?” I rushed toward him this time. “There’s only one person I’ve ever heard call them that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I don’t know what you’re talking about.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do you know him?” I asked, suddenly frantic. “Do you know James? Or…or…he might also go by the name Jamus?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk grew very still and eyed me suspiciously. “I might. How do you know him?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Can you take me to him?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Maybe. He’s a flame thrower. You know that, right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course I know that. But he’s a good one.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The creepy guard watched me stone-faced for a long time before answering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Tell you what, Miss Opalinya,” he said, stroking the whiskers on his rough, pockmarked chin. “You go into that cave with me—help me rescue as many goldabungs as we find there—and I’ll tell you everything I know about this Jamus.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Really?!” I almost squeaked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’ll even take you to him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated. What if this was a trap? But how else would Karyk know the word dingbat? James was literally the only person I had ever heard use that word. It was an Earth word, for crying out loud!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated, continuing to scrutinize Karyk for any sign of deception. Even as I did, I knew this was the best shot I’d had of finding James since the Wood Weaver’s Gate incident. “Alright,” I finally said, “deal. But if you’re really working with the dogrils, I have some pretty powerful people who will come looking for me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He grinned at me, baring several gray teeth, and shook my hand. “Good. Let’s go.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grabbed his arm. “Wait, what’s the plan?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Plan?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yeah. If there are goldabungs still in there, how are we going to get them out?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk reached up and pulled at something around his neck. “My instructions from his Metallican are to capture as many dogrils as I can in here.” He held up a round, golden stone attached to a cord. I knew it was a fangledãor. It looked just like the one Lev had worn during the Callowynder tournament.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What about the captured goldabungs?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’ll bust ’em out of their grimcages and lead ’em back to the palace to testify.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How do you propose we do that?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shrugged. “You got a qualdrine, don’t you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“A qualdrine can bust open a grimcage. From there, we’ll just have to wing it,” he explained. “Now, do you mind if we stop wasting time and go see what we’re up against?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was more of a plan than I’d arrived with, so I nodded, and a few minutes later we found ourselves halfway down the new tunnel, both of us keeping to the shadows, taking turns playing lookout for any dogrils that might be lurking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we reached the end, the tunnel opened into the cave, just as I remembered, only this time the situation we found was worse—much worse. There were twice as many grimcages as before—maybe more—and every last one of them had anywhere from one to two goldabungs huddled inside. I squinted hard, trying to catch a glimpse of a familiar face. I was sickened to find several, both from the palace and from the streets of the lower Rasilian markets.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But where was the princess?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I scoured each grimcage, searching for her white-gold hair. I went over each cage twice before I finally found her. She was curled up in the farthest grimcage, hair tangled, cheeks smeared with ash, and dressed like a goldabung, which was why I’d passed over her before. Oriette was confined in one grimcage on one side of the princess. In another stood an older girl who had to be her sister, Elaris. On the princess’s opposite side, Tolliver occupied a grimcage of his own, and beside him a smaller cage held a little boy I recognized as Mica, DonTon’s favorite paget.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"These five were in grimcages set apart from the others. From the looks of the bruises and burns on their skin, they had been used as some kind of example to keep the other goldabungs in line. DonTon was not going to be happy when he saw the state of his daughter.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I crouched behind a cluster of stalagmites with Karyk.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’re going to need a better plan,” I muttered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk grunted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I remembered Iolyt’s stones. Reaching into my pouch, I started pulling them out one at a time. “I brought a few things that might help.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He looked skeptical.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Listen, these were made by Iolyt the qualdrinist,” I said. I’d since learned Iolyt and her reputation were well known in Rasili. I placed the stones in a line between us and gave Karyk a quick background of each stone and what it did.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He picked up one of the blekkingstones and turned it over in his calloused fingers. “Illusion stone, huh?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “If you use this to become a dogril for a little while, you know, and distract the others,” I suggested, “I can sneak in with the huldecite and start breaking the goldabungs free. Once you’ve captured all the dogrils in your fangledãor, we can get out of here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He hesitated. Then, having decided something, he said, “Try breaking the cages a few at a time. The waterfall should cover the sound, but you don’t want to spook the little ones or alert the dogrils.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded. “I’ve only got three extra pieces of huldecite. They’ll have to escape in shifts of three. You think you can keep the dogrils distracted that long?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shrugged. “We’ll both do our best I guess.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I handed him a solivayne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He snorted. “The courage rock?” But the moment he touched it, he stopped smirking. “Oh. Okay! Let’s do this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He slipped both stones into a silver cuff he wore around his upper arm. Then he gave me a grim smile. “Ready?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gathered the rest of the stones and slipped all except the pieces of huldecite back into my pouch. “Let’s go.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk took on the illusion of a dogril, and I was invisible again, so the first dash in was easy enough. I slipped through the maze of firelit shadows, doing my best to avoid anything that would leave evidence to put the dogrils on my trail.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Halfway across the cavern, I nearly tripped over a sleeping dogril lounging in a pile of half-woven grimcages. Thankfully Karyk was there to kick some loose pebbles at the groggy dogril and told him to get back to work.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After I was safely out of reach, Karyk wished me luck and headed off in the direction of the waterfall where the majority of the dogrils were occupied with either building more grimcages or sitting around a fire they had to keep relighting due to the damp air in the cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A few minutes later, I reached the goldabungs without anyone seeing me. I knew I was going to need someone to vouch for me in order to win the goldabungs’ trust, so I headed straight to Oriette’s and Tolliver’s grimcages.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I was hidden enough in the shadows, I slipped the huldecite into my shyntara pocket and showed myself to them. They both jerked in surprise—Tolliver let out a very Tolliver-sounding squeak—but Oriette crawled over to me right away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s Lunalyn,” she whispered to Elaris. “She’s here to help.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was all it took.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Princess! Princess!” Elaris hissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princess Sylva stirred, pressing against the side of her own cage. I’d seen the princess from a distance here and there around the palace during the Tableunia, but never up close. Even with the filth, bruises, and burns, up close she was just as breathtaking as her brother.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No,” she said when I tried to break her free first. “Please free the others first. Start with the youngest.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The first blast went unnoticed, covered by the sound of the waterfall. Then another. I handed the three huldecite stones to Tolliver. If the princess refused to be first, Tolliver was the best choice for leading the others to safety.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Take them to Seer Stone Sink,” I whispered. “Then send them on to DonTon’s throne room and come back with their stones,” I said. “Keep leading two at a time until they’ve all gotten out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“They’ll want to scatter,” Tolliver warned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know. That’s why I need you to make sure they don’t. If they scatter and there’s no one to testify, goldabungs will keep disappearing.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tolliver nodded and led two girls off toward Seer Stone Sink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I checked on Karyk. He was using the blekkingstone, pretending to be three dogrils at once now. He had even somehow convinced the real dogrils to join him, carnie-style, for a flame-throwing contest in an “arena,” the entrance of which was actually his open fangledãor in disguise. It was working, too. There were at least a dozen dogrils lined up to enter the fangledãor and participate. If you hadn’t realized it yet, most dogrils are pretty dumb.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was only a few moments, but it felt like eternity before Tolliver returned with the extra huldecite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I broke two more grimcages open and sent him off with two more goldabungs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And so it went, two by two, led by Tolliver, until half the goldabungs were safely on their way, hopefully to DonTon’s throne room.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I returned to my invisible state with my own huldecite and started on the second half of the cages. One of the goldabungs in this group was shaking too badly to move, so I gave her a piece of nymuriel while Oriette whispered something encouraging. Another refused to leave until I handed her my last solivayne. She squeezed it so tight her knuckles turned white. But once the stone took effect, she straightened her spine and followed Tolliver like a champ.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Each time, the noise got louder, the air got hotter, and I was pretty sure one of the dogrils started to smell me. But then Karyk would change tactics and come up with another illusion or challenge to lure them into his fangledãor. He really did remind me of a circus master persuading his lions through a flaming ring of fire.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When all the grimcages were empty, we’d made twelve trips in total. Including Tolliver, that made twenty-five goldabungs to safety. I only had two goldabungs and the princess left.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Oriette and her sister, Elaris, refused to leave the princess behind. It wasn’t until the princess commanded them to go that I was finally able to send them off with Tolliver. When they were safely gone, he would return with the huldecite and then leave. There would be just enough pieces for myself, the princess, and Karyk to escape with.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When Tolliver made it back to the shadows and pocketed his huldecite, his breath was ragged. “It’s done,” he panted. “They’ve all agreed to go to DonTon’s throne room.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Great work, Tolliver!” I whispered as he slipped the extra huldecite into my hand. “Now go on ahead,” I told him. “Don’t wait for us. Go make sure the goldabungs get there safely. Prince Sydian should be there waiting for you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tolliver resumed invisibility, and I heard, rather than saw, him scamper off over the wet stone floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned back to the grimcages and slipped the other piece of huldecite to Princess Sylva, but it slid from her fingers and clattered onto the cave floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Don’t worry,” I said. “We’ll get it when you’re out. Just move back.” I prepared to blast open the final cage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just as I unleashed the power of my qualdrine on the grimcage, the thundering of the waterfall stopped suddenly. No warning. No slowing. Just silence followed by the blast of my qualdrine breaking open Princess Sylva’s grimcage. It echoed like a gunshot throughout the cave.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I spun just in time to see what looked like a scarlet ball of fire hurtle through the opening where the waterfall had been—then, just as suddenly, the thundering waterfall returned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it was too late. The dogrils had heard. All eyes were on the princess since I was still invisible.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Another burst of scarlet fire lit up the cave, swirling into a shape too large, too graceful, and too strange to be anything natural.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then, suddenly, I was stunned to see the red-winged creature from my rooftop land in the middle of the cavern, her feathered body blazing like a comet made of wildfire, soot, and gold dust.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was Seraphyna—Galena, the lady in the walls, and now the creature. They were all the same being.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Eyes ablaze, she started toward us. Then every dogril in the chamber was heading our way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I snatched the other piece of huldecite from the cave floor, shoved it into Sylva’s hand, and heaved her forward. “RUN!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I heard her footsteps slap against the wet stone in the direction of Seer Stone Sink.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Karyk!” I shouted. “We have to go.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Karyk had initiated a more elaborate blekkingstone illusion between us and the real dogrils. This time, he didn’t just look like one dogril or even three, but a whole army of dogrils, spewing fire and flapping wings like a flock of fiery fiends.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"How was I going to get the last piece of huldecite to him?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The real dogrils were confused by the illusion, and more and more of them were retreating, only to find themselves retreating right into Karyk’s open fangledãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Galena, on the other hand, was unconvinced. Before I could decide what to do next, she screeched and dove in my direction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then she was everywhere. One moment she was winged flame; the next, water lashed through the cave, dousing the few real flames the remaining dogrils had managed to conjure. The walls cracked with lightning; the ground began to heave beneath us like it was trying to swallow us whole. I wondered if I stood a chance with a quintowyn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I went inward, pulling all the fire and water I could from my blood. I threw everything I had at Galena, but it only gave away my location to her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk’s illusion was beginning to slip. He turned toward me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Go, Lunalyn!” he shouted, slapping at a sleeve of his shyntara that was on fire. “Make sure the princess is safe!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But your huldecite!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“GO!” he bellowed again. “I’ll hold them!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I didn’t wait to argue. I threw one last blast of fire and water at Galena and then ran. As I did, I released as much fog into the air as I could to give myself and, hopefully, Karyk some cover.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Galena kept throwing her power. It was like she’d lost control of it somehow. Debris was falling from the ceiling of the cave. I had to dodge it as I ran. I was certain I’d feel fire on my back before I made it out of the tunnel. But, amazingly, I got all the way through without a hitch.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My relief didn’t last long, though. I heard their screams before I saw them. They were crying out for help just ahead. I waved my hand, clearing the fog in front of me. When I finally located them, all I could see was Princess Sylva clinging to the side of the land bridge—the same one I’d fallen from before Barakyne caught me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I dropped my huldecite.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hurry! She slipped. Tolliver has gone for help, but he’ll be too late,” Oriette sobbed when she saw me. The princess was barely hanging onto the edge of the bridge, her fingernails scraping desperately for grip. Elaris and Oriette were much smaller than the princess, and while they had her by both arms, she was still slipping.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I slid the last few feet and grabbed Sylva’s arm just as Oriette lost her footing. I pushed her back to safety.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hold on!” I grunted, reaching down, bracing my foot against a sharp outcrop. In that moment, I was grateful for all the sparring Zuli and I had done to prepare for the forte and fitness portion of the Tableunia. Without it, I wouldn’t have been strong enough. But with one coordinated pull, Oriette wrapped her arms around my waist, and Elaris and I heaved the princess back onto the bridge.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She collapsed in my arms, gasping.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry,” I panted, hefting her back to her feet. “There will be time to process this later, but right now, we have to run. Go!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Together, the four of us raced through Seer Stone Sink toward the tunnel that led back to the palace. When we reached it, I pushed Oriette and Elaris into the entrance. I was just reaching back to pull the princess ahead of me when I was yanked backwards.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hit the ground so hard the breath left my lungs. My back screamed in pain.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Run, Princess!” I forced out at last.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Galena—the woman now—stood over me, rusty hair tangled in wild braids and coils, face vivid with feral beauty, eyes glowing with something that didn’t feel like fire but barely contained rage. She wasn’t breathing heavily. She hadn’t broken a sweat. To be honest, I had never seen someone look so powerful and so unstable at the same time. It was equal parts terrifying and mesmerizing all at once.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She tilted her head. “How’s your brother?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I blinked up at her. “What?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lev,” she said softly, almost mockingly. “He was really doing so well in the tournament. He shouldn’t have followed me to the earth giants, though.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What did you do to him?” I spat.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She crouched down, smirking. “He never told you? No, he wouldn’t remember, I suppose. But I do. I remember everything.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A surge of fury lit up in my chest. I threw a fireball at her point-blank.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She laughed as it exploded harmlessly against a stone shield she summoned without blinking. “You really are brave,” she said, standing. “Cifer told me you were.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to scramble to my feet, but I didn’t get far. The woman was gone, and the winged creature—the náfenaeri—had returned. She unleashed everything.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Earth wrapped around my legs. Water smacked me down. Metal wrapped across my chest like a vice. Fire curled beneath my skin as lightning crackled through my hair.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The “burning” started slowly. But it wasn’t burning, and yet that was the only way to describe it. It wasn’t heat on the surface—it was inside. Every bone felt lit on fire, as though my spine had been replaced with molten metal. I screamed, because it was the only way to relieve the pain.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This was what Lev felt! This was what had put him unconscious in a pod?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Galena’s shadow swelled above me. She wasn’t smiling anymore. She was shaking. Or was it me? Every part of her trembled, as if she might scatter in every direction. It reminded me of the first time I met Lylend, when he pulled me from Belmont Police Station and into a hoption hole.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then—just when I thought I might black out from the pain—it eased.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When my vision steadied, I saw a sharp, silver spike jutting from Galena’s arm, then another thrust through her left thigh.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I blinked through the haze and saw Princess Sylva standing in the entrance of the tunnel back to the palace. Her palms were glowing with red-hot drawn blades.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Galena shrieked—a horrible, high sound that fractured into something not human.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I saw it clearly then. The fracture in her. The unraveling of a person who had once been whole. Her form flickered—one moment glowing, the next cracked with shadows. She melted the blades right into her body and began moving toward us again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Run, Sylva,” I managed. “Run.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m not leaving you,” she said, and knelt beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to sit up—tried to push her toward the tunnel, but my bones were still on fire.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She struggled to lift me, but we both knew it wouldn’t be fast enough, so we braced for Galena’s blast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Something else hit instead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It sounded like distant thunder at first. Then like a lion’s roar. A moment later, it ricocheted again and again all over the cavern.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A great wind blew through the cave from the sinkhole above, and the next thing we knew, a giant black shadow descended.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"No. Not a shadow. Black feathers. Piercing eyes. Wings wider than the ruby altar below.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was Barakyne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sylva and I scrambled back toward the tunnel as the black beast swooped toward the cavern floor. He didn’t roar again, but his aim was true.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"One moment Galena was splitting at the seams with unstable power. The next, she was in his talons—shrieking and thrashing—then disappeared through the sinkhole above, swallowed by wind, wings, and the light of the eclipsing Triolune.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Battle Seer Stone","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"fd6d4ec9a8fb4bfdb36c8a9ff178b416"},{"title":"Cifer","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"7c37d7fa52bd44abbda6e3ad68bd949d"},{"title":"Feather","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"def74982d6594ca5aab967bbbf1724cf"}]},{"n":70,"letterTitle":"The Queen’s Sentence","narrator":"James","pieces":[{"title":"The Queen’s Sentence (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I’ve never walked a queen anywhere before, let alone one who moved like she was doing us all a favor just by breathing. In my weeks at the palace, I hadn’t had much interaction with Queen Jayda, but I wasn’t impressed. There was something false about her. Sure, she was beautiful, but was that all there was to her? Her step kids were much more impressive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Jayda’s heels clicked beside me like clockwork, every step with the purpose of one who knows her place in the world and that it’s at the top. Prince Sydian walked at her other side, eyes forward, expression carved from marble. Poor guy. As days go, this was gonna go down as a pretty bad one for him. If he was nervous, I couldn’t tell. But who wouldn’t be? He was about to tell his father that the woman he loved had allowed his precious daughter to be captured by dogrils and almost carted off to Holictac where Cifer planned to do who knows what with the goldabungs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I, on the other hand, was pretty stoked to finally have this whole fiasco in DonTon’s palace solved. I was trying not to look too relieved out of respect for the prince. ’Cause I did respect him. Prince Sydian, turns out, is a pretty stand-up guy. But there was something about this whole plan of his—tricking the queen into coming to the throne room—that I wasn’t sure about. Apparently he had it bad for that Lunalyn Opalinya kyndie—the one who puked but made up for it by being a siryn?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anyway, Sydian told the queen that despite Lunalyn pretty much winning the Tableunia in the end, DonTon is refusing to let him marry her simply because she’s a Lune-kyndie. That’s why DonTon postponed announcing the winner right away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Apparently, it’s a given that the winner of Rasil’s gloriole automatically becomes the next queen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Who knew, right? Turns out, despite lunalyn’s attempt to disguise it, one of the other contestants figured out she was a Lune-kyndie and brought it to DonTon’s attention. The kinchief doesn’t have anything against lune-kyndies as a rule—not like he does with flame throwers—but when it comes to the next queen of Rasili, I guess it’s another story altogether.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hence all the pretense with the queen. Sydian had convinced her to come “beg” DonTon to let the prince marry the winner of the Tableunia as originally planned, even if she is a Lune-kyndie.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"To be honest, though, the idea of Prince Sydian marrying that siryn girl didn’t sit quite right with me either. I can’t say why exactly. Who the prince married was none of my business. Still, there was just something about it, and the more I thought about it, the more it bothered me—so much so that I wanted to punch him every time the thought crossed my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But I kept my mouth shut, right? No need to screw things up when I was so close to finally busting Glynt out of jail.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When we finally reached the throne room, the Doors opened, and the queen swept in like she expected the usual enamored greeting her beauty earned her everywhere she went. Instead, she stopped short.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon was already seated on his throne. He looked terrible, if I’m being honest. And dangerous. But I was willing to bet the whole 2004 red Sox team that wasn’t what caught the queen’s attention. Nah, I was pretty sure it was the sight of Princess Sylva—alive but bruised and burned—sitting on the steps next to DonTon’s throne that let her know this summoning had nothing to do with who Prince Sydian wanted to marry.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princess Sylva looked bruised, burned, and tired, but steady. Her eyes flicked to mine. She gave the smallest nod of recognition and gratitude. I smiled back at her, just like a goldabung would.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As if he heard what I was thinking, DonTon made a motion to the guard standing behind his throne. The guard moved to a nearby tapestry, pulled out a shard of gold hearthurite, and slid the tapestry aside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, goldabungs came pouring into the throne room, about two dozen in all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"They stood in two rows on the right side of DonTon’s throne, like a jury. They weren’t cleaned up yet. It was clear DonTon wanted the magnitude of the queen’s crimes on full display before her eyes, so there was no question—not even in her mind—of her guilt.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"During the entire procession, the queen didn’t move. She just stood there, trying to save face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then Sydian went and stood beside his sister, and the queen cracked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A second later, she burst into what I was pretty sure were forced—if not completely fake—tears. Then she pushed past me and the guards who’d come to stand around her. “Oh Sylva! Libkyn! Thank the Gihara!” She sobbed, rushing toward the throne and her stepdaughter with outstretched arms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The princess held her ground, though, impressive kyndie. She refused to even flinch at her stepmother’s phony relief.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon, on the other hand, looked like the fires of a volcano were raging inside of him. He flicked his hand, and several blades of metal immediately shot up from the floor around the queen, bending into a sort of prison cell before she could reach the throne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The queen shrieked, looking genuinely confused.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are these tears of joy, my love?” DonTon seethed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Or are they tears of fear?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The queen blinked rapidly, her whole body shifting back into the smooth elegance she wore like perfume. “I don’t know what you mean, Metallican?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then perhaps,” DonTon said, his massive form leaning forward, “we should hear from those who do.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He waved his hand, and one by one, the goldabungs stepped forward and began to speak. Their voices were small—some quiet. Some of the older kynds’ were laced with false vibrato. But all were condemning of the queen and her involvement in their kidnapping. They spoke of a red-haired lady who moved through walls and of being taken in the middle of the night. They testified to being smuggled, half drugged, past guards and through the queen’s own rooms into the tunnel leading to the Dogril cave, where they woke in grimcages, surrounded by dogrils. They also spoke of Queen Jayda, holding torches, whispering orders, and helping the red-haired lady disappear into the dark.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Each testimony pierced a hole through the queen’s “innocence” like the blades holding her captive now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"By the time Lunalyn Opalinya was escorted into the throne room—it honestly hurt to look at her all scorched like that from her battle with the red-haired lady—the queen had gone utterly still.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lunalyn gave her own account of how she found the goldabungs and freed them. How she fought the Náfenaeri known as Galena—also known as Seraphyna. Despite the shape she was in, her voice didn’t waver once. She was definitely impressive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Unfortunately, I was unable to detain the creature, your Metallican,” she said, “but I do have these.” She reached into her shyntara and pulled out a long, bright red feather, and an even longer blue-black one. She handed them to a guard who delivered them to DonTon.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon reached out and held the scarlet feather, spinning it in the light. As he did, threads of golden fire seemed to flare up on it. “Let us hope Barakyne—if that is who it was—finished off the fowl creature. Was she working alone?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That was my cue.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stepped forward, cradling the fangledãor prison. I had to use both hands since I was in my goldabung disguise.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, your Metallican,” I said. “She had help, but not anymore.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knelt on one knee before DonTon and set the fangledãor on the ground. It shimmered in the light, but the golden prison stone was locked up tight. I’d seen to that after the final Dogril from the cave had been captured inside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“She had an entire pack of dogrils backing her,” I said. “Every last one of them is inside this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon’s eyes narrowed. “You’re certain?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian came to stand beside me. “I saw them myself, father.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The room held its breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then DonTon called for another guard who’d been lingering along the edge of the throne room. I recognized the man with his scarred jaw and sharp eyes. This was the guy I wanted.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He approached the throne. DonTon signaled to him, and the guard pulled another fangledãor from around his neck—this one was familiar to me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon took it and held it out for me to see.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“My kingdom and my family owe you a great debt of gratitude,” he said in a lower voice. “Come to the closing ceremonies tomorrow, and I will return your friend. Perhaps some flame throwers can be trusted after all.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grinned at him, slipping my own fangledãor back into my shirt. Tollie or the princess must’ve given my disguise away to him. “Then I’ll be sure to bring this along to hand over to you there,” I said. There was no way I was handing over the dogrils until Glynt was completely free.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon looked impressed—like he’d been waiting to see if I was dumb enough to hand over my only leverage before I had what I wanted. It looked like I passed. In fact, I felt like I’d just won the respect of a very valuable ally. He was an ally sure to come in handy sooner or later.","i":false}],"narrator":"James"},{"title":"The Queen’s Sentence (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Firstly, why in the world had Karyk entrusted a goldabung with a fangledãor filled with dogrils? I mean, given that goldabungs were scrappy, mature-beyond-their-years, and resourceful little tykes, it didn’t seem like the most responsible decision to me. Then again, all his heroics from the goldabung rescue aside, Karyk was an odd, creepy sort of dude. So…whatever.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Secondly, I’ve seen some strange things since arriving in Cobbogoth. But nothing—and I mean nothing—prepared me for watching the queen stripped of her crown.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Or rather, Rasil’s legendary, gorgeous gloriole.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rasil’s most famous treasure wasn’t a crown exactly, not in the traditional sense, but it reminded me of one of those halos from old angel paintings. You know what I mean, right? They look more like a rising starburst—part golden halo and part glittering gems—than an actual crown. Rasil’s gloriole appeared to hover just above Queen Jayda’s head, like the sun rising over a hill. It wasn’t the first time I’d seen her wear it, but it was the first time I’d seen it up close. For the record, it really was gorgeous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The second DonTon motioned for it to be removed from the queen’s head, a low hum filled the room. The guards stepped forward and reached through the queen’s prison of blades. The gloriole was removed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then she was gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Well, not gone, gone. The queen still stood there, of course, but gone was the flawless skin. The ethereal posture. The almost supernaturally youthful glow. What stood in her place now was a beautiful but average-looking, middle-aged woman in a simple shyntara who looked as though she’d wandered in from the lower market of Rasili after somehow losing her way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’m ashamed to say my first thought was extremely shallow—proof I’d been in this image-obsessed city way too long. I actually wondered if Iolyt’s beauty gems could help her win DonTon back now that she didn’t have the luxury of the gloriole. As if her lack of beauty had anything to do with losing DonTon’s affection in the first place. Sheesh! What was wrong with me?!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Instead, I watched as the queen collapsed to her knees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please, DonTon! I did it for you,” she choked, looking up at Rasili’s kinchief, her hands trembling. “For your son. For the kingdom he’ll soon inherit. DonTon, please—you have to understand.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon’s face hardened in a way that made the room drop five degrees.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I would’ve stopped there, but the queen continued. “Cifer has returned. You know this. He is rapidly growing in power. If we don’t bow now, we won’t have a throne left.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Never!” DonTon boomed. “He has not won. He won’t win! Even now, sightings of Barakyne are rumored all over Cobbogoth.” He held up the black feather I’d given him as proof.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The room erupted in excited whispers. The queen only scoffed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And there is the difference between us, Jayda. It always has been. You would hand Cifer our future to ensure your own comfort. I will fight him to the death so my children know we do not bow to evil—that we trust in the ancient ways—no matter how hopeless the future seems.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If I’m being honest, until that moment I hadn’t been able to decide if I liked DonTon, kinchief of Rasili, or not. But as he spoke, I saw him clearly for the first time. And I liked what I saw. I liked it a lot. Now I knew where Sydian got his unexpected depth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Queen Jayda tried to speak again, but DonTon had already motioned several trolls forward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The large, regal, and faceless elemies crossed the chamber like gorgeous floating robots. Prince Sydian came to stand beside me just as they reached her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry,” I said. “I had no idea your stepmom was behind all of this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Would it have changed anything?” he asked, sorrow and anger warring in his eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He accepted my answer with a nod, but his face gave nothing away. “They’re from the Magglyn Mines,” he explained quietly as the trolls circled the queen’s prison. “Summoned last night. When I heard the goldabungs’ testimonies for myself, I knew Father wouldn’t allow for a less severe punishment.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I managed to lift my stiff arm enough to touch his elbow. “At least Sylva is safe.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded. “And for that, I am grateful to you—grateful beyond words, Lunalyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The trolls removed Queen Jayda from the bladed prison without a word. She didn’t scream. She just stared, tears streaming down her face as she looked at her husband for what was most likely the last time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon gave a single nod. “Jayda of Rasili, you are sentenced to life in the Magglyn Mines for treason against your people, against your family, and against the throne. Let your shame serve as a warning to those who would trade sovereignty for safety, courage for cowardice, and the Eye of the Gihara for a master of the Dark Realm.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that was that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As the queen and the trolls disappeared through a sinister-looking tapestry at the far corner of the throne room, everyone else seemed to exhale. So did Sydian.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I squeezed his elbow one last time, and then he left me to return to his sister’s side.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please come forward, Lunalyn Opalinya,” DonTon said as he scrubbed a hand across his brow. It was the only sign of what sentencing his second wife had cost him personally. I stepped back in front of the throne, and he turned his weary gaze on me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I understand,” he said, “from what Princess Sylva has told me, that it is you I have to thank for returning my daughter to me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I straightened as best as I could. Every bone in my body felt like it had been steamrolled.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Truth be told, your Metallican, I’m pretty certain the princess saved my life as well,” I said. “And I didn’t act alone. One of your soldiers—Karyk—helped me. None of us would’ve made it out of that cave without his help.” I glanced around the room, but there was still no sign of the creepy guard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon frowned slightly. “That name is unfamiliar to me,” he said. “But if what you say is true, I’ll have him sought out and rewarded accordingly.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He stood then, stepping down from his throne, and walked directly to me. He unexpectedly placed a hand over his heart. “You have returned to me what I prize above all else. Tell me, Lunalyn Opalinya, what may I grant you in return?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For a second, I forgot how to speak. Everyone’s eyes were suddenly fastened on me. My instinct was to wave his recognition away as no big deal, if only to make everyone stop looking. But what had it all been for? What did the Hood come to Rasili for in the first place?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My next words came out hoarse but clear. “Um…I would be really grateful, your Metallican, if you would give me Rasil’s gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a commotion around the throne room. DonTon blinked and then laughed—not mockingly, but like someone genuinely caught off guard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, my dear kyndie,” he said, “I’m afraid I can’t give it to you outright. The gloriole has already been promised to the winner of the Tableunia. I’ll be announcing that winner—along with the Callowynder champion—at the Triolune closing ceremony tomorrow night.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried not to deflate visibly. It was hard since it was already taking so much energy just to stay standing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then DonTon placed a finger under my chin, lifting my eyes to meet his. In a low voice, he said, “Between you and me, there’s a very good chance it’s already yours—among other treasures that I, and Rasili, value very highly.” With that, he glanced at his son who was looking at me in a way that made me feel confused and…warm. I felt really hot, like my cheeks were on fire.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In the meantime,” he continued, dropping his hand and turning back toward his throne, “ask me for something else. Something I can grant you now.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I hesitated. What else was there? The only other thing I really wanted, he couldn’t grant me, but Karyk could—in fact he promised to bring me to James. That is if he meant to honor our deal. But maybe there was something else—something I could ask for to benefit my friends.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then it hit me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thassalyth shrooms!” I blurted out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon returned to his throne and sat, his brow furrowed. “Thassalyth shrooms?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes,” I said, swallowing my excitement. “You know my friend Lev who was hurt in the Giants’ round of the Callowynder match?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon nodded. “Of course. My deepest condolences.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thank you. But Iolyt the qualdrinist thinks thassalyth shrooms might help wake him up.” I had no idea if she and Pieter had any luck hunting some down last night, but if not, maybe I could bring her some.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon raised a brow. “Thassalyth shrooms are extremely rare,” he said. “They only grow in the most particular conditions.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Yes, I heard a rumor that your healers grow them in Rasili,” I said, my excitement deflating. “Did I hear wrong?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"If so, maybe I could figure out a way to go see Totherma in person—get the thassalyth shrooms she showed me when she told me about them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You heard correctly,” DonTon continued. “We do, in fact, have a cave reserved by our healers specifically for cultivating thassalyth shrooms.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My heart leapt again. “I’m happy to purchase them,” I offered. Lylend would spring for them, wouldn’t he?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon shook his head. “You shall have as much as you need. Where should I send them?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I let out a breath and almost cried. “Thank you, your Metallican. Could you send them to Iolyt the qualdrinist, down by Trolly Row? Tolliver knows where our camp is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gestured toward Tolliver, and he grinned at me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Very good. They’ll be there before you are,” DonTon said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then we were all dismissed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As I made my way out of the palace, feral goldabungs darting this way and that, my bones could still feel Galena’s fire. Hopefully, there would be some thassalyth shrooms left over after Lev was healed to relieve some of my own pain.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’d just reached the front door of the palace when I heard footsteps behind me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn,” Prince Sydian called, catching up easily. “Lunalyn, wait!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I turned slowly, my joints stiffer than I realized.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Did you mean it?” he asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Mean what?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That you want the gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I met his eyes, surprised. “Yeah, I meant it. Didn’t I just go through a whole beauty pageant fiasco to prove it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He flashed a smile so dazzling that all the pain momentarily left my body. Something about that smile made me feel like I’d just handed him the winning move in a game I didn’t know I was playing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I can’t tell you how relieved I am to hear it,” he said, voice quiet and low—sincere. “I should’ve asked you, you know, before I tried to kiss you at our private banquet.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I waved his words away. “Please don’t worry about that anymore.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s just the weaving dance, you know? I thought my intentions were clear and…” He looked like he wanted to say more, but then I winced in pain, and he thought better of it. “I should let you get back and rest. Tomorrow’s closing ceremony will be…” But then he shook his head, a grin spreading across his lips. “Can I help you back?” he finally offered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh no, the walk will do me good,” I lied, but then added truthfully, “I have a lot to think about.” That and I really didn’t want to encourage him any more, especially since I was pretty confused about what I was encouraging in the first place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He startled me then by taking my hand in his and gently pressing a kiss to it. “Then I will say farewell and thank you, Lunalyn—from the depths of my heart—for restoring my sister to me. And for what you will give my people tomorrow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please don’t mention it,” I said. “I really did mean it when I told your father she saved my life at Seer Stone Sink.” But the prince wasn’t really listening anymore. He was just looking at me. I was sweating again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The silence lingered half a second too long, so I cleared my throat. “Congratulations, by the way. On winning the Callowynder tournament.” No one at the Hood had bothered watching the final rounds with Lev still unconscious, but we all heard about it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian waved it off like a mildly annoying fly. “It’s not much of a victory when the rightful winners are either rendered unconscious or disqualified for being Cifer’s smuggler.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gave a tired huff of a laugh. “True.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He paused, then turned slightly as if to leave, but I stopped him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh! Before you go—have you seen Karyk?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian tilted his head. “No. The fangledãor with the dogrils arrived late last night, delivered by the goldabung you saw present it to my father.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hm…” I said, suddenly uneasy. “Do you think Karyk is all right? I didn’t see him get out of the dogril cave.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sure he is. I’ll swing by the lower tunnels and check on him. He’ll definitely be at the closing ceremonies tomorrow,” Sydian offered. “The whole court will be. Why do you ask?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I shrugged. “It’s nothing too urgent,” I said. “We just made a deal before we went after the goldabungs.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian’s brow lifted slightly. “Then I’ll be sure to see to it that he keeps his word.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He gave me one last scorching gaze, then bowed slightly. “Until tomorrow, Lunalyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Okay,” I said. “Tomorrow.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Halfway down the palace steps, I was cursing myself for not accepting Sydian’s help back to camp. But just as I reached the gates, I heard a familiar chirp and trill. To my bones’ indescribable relief, an adorable giant white rodent was eagerly waiting at the gates of the palace to carry me back to our camp.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Brawlo!” I cried—literally, because I didn’t have to walk anymore. “I’m so glad to see you! When did you and Lylend get back?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo waited until I had managed to climb on his back before answering.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"SWAMP EXTRA MUDDY. SWAMP NYMPHS NOT NICE.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“What do you mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"ATTACKED. HELD HOSTAGE. LYLEND SET SHUDDER ON THEM.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“Wow!” I said. “And that worked?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"NOBODY LIKE SHUDDER MUCH. NOT EVEN STINKY SWAMP NYMPHS.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I could feel the satisfaction of defeating a foe roil off him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"CAME TO FETCH YOU FOR OTHER REASON.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s that?” I groaned in pain as Brawlo began to run.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"LEV AWAKE!","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"When Brawlo and I arrived at the camp, despite my entire body being in twice the pain due to Brawlo’s gallop, I hobbled as fast as I could into Iolyt’s lab.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh Nori!” Zuli cried, throwing her arms around me. “They just came! Thank you! Thank you!” she said, kissing both my cheeks. Then she released me, spinning me around to find Lev sitting up, looking like death warmed over, shoveling pale blue, crystallized-looking shrooms into his mouth like popcorn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Taste like burned tree bark,” he said, grinning with his mouth full. “You want some?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a handful while swiping a tear from my cheek. He was right. They did taste like burned tree bark. But it also felt like the sun had risen on our little makeshift family again at last.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Everything was going to be all right.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Queen Jayda Sentence","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"c0df6eda053e4f8fa9972a7f7a6b73f6"},{"title":"Glynt","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"44d9edee06584841921ccfb0d201f90b"}]},{"n":71,"letterTitle":"The Closing Ceremonies","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Lemoria","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"253532d7505d43479119056b37c61a2e"},{"title":"The Closing Ceremonies","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"We were finally on our way to the closing ceremonies of the Triolune Festival. The whole Hood was going. Even Lev and Zuli. After eating at least a third of a barrel of thassalyth shrooms, Lev was feeling well enough to be up and about, so he and Zuli had gone ahead separately to avoid the worst of the crowds.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As one of the Tableunia finalists, Lylend insisted I ride Brawlo to the closing ceremonies, which was no small feat in my current, heavily beaded shyntara made of something called moffyloth silk. Lylend had brought it back from the Unda Swamp, and Zu just couldn’t resist using it to make her piece de resistance of Tableunia ceremonial shyntaras. It was absolutely gorgeous, but it was hard to feel very inconspicuous sitting on Brawlo in it. But I was also pretty sure Lylend had ulterior motives for making me ride him. The masses of people cramming the streets were parting for Brawlo like the Red Sea parted for Moses. At this rate, we’d make it to the arena in record time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The streets were extra crowded today because Vyn, Baryn, and Corynty were finally merging fully for the complete Lunevergence everyone had been waiting for. This was the most sacred part of the festival, and it showed as we climbed the spiraling streets of Rasili. The celebration that had been happening pretty much nonstop since our arrival in Rasili was restrained, and the decorations had changed too. Rather than streets exploding in vibrant colors, like Oaxaca, Mexico during Dia de Los Muertos, everything was covered in more hushed silver, gold, and copper to honor the moons up above. The combination of their light cast a metallic glow over DonTon’s palace—like someone had poured liquid gold over the spiral city.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So this was the “dance of the three moons.” It was pretty impressive by anyone’s standards, but for someone who grew up in Earth’s realm, it was pure magic.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The city was subdued while the eclipse reached its climax. It still had a few hours yet, but it was as if everyone—including the scrappiest goldabungs—was holding their breath.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"People whispered blessings in the streets as we passed them. Metallic gong-like instruments rang out at random all over the city. I’m not a fan of incense, as a rule, but whatever they were burning not only smelled like a combination of vanilla beans and honeysuckle, but the smoke gave off a golden glitter, causing the air to sparkle like we were strolling through stardust.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Somewhere, someone released a flock of golden birds into the sky. Brawlo paused to watch, calling them emberfowl, which was actually a pretty good name, considering each time they gave a cry, a burst of golden spark exploded from their mouths like fireworks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After passing through the lower streets, Lylend led the way into a sunstone-lit tunnel reserved for the VIPs of the Triolune Festival. It fed directly into the enormous aurethryn that would hold the closing ceremonies.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hopefully there would be no puking on stage tonight.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"As we finally reached the end of the tunnel and entered the aurethryn, the surrounding arena shimmered like a jeweled snow globe. It was already packed floor-to-ceiling with people. According to Lylend, one hundred thousand Cobbogothians could be in the arena for the closing ceremonies. It sure looked that way. They lined the inner sanctum and outer terraces, the arena rising level after level into the sky to house them all. I’d never been anywhere with so many people at once. The mood inside was much different than what we’d experienced in the streets below. Random spectators kept throwing crushed shards of plausenite against the outer dome of the aurethryn. Each explosion was met with cheers, chanting, and sometimes even singing, depending on which kin-klan was doing the throwing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Are you ready?” Iolyt whispered beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Does it matter if I say no?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She gave me a commiserating smile.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We’re very proud of you, kyndie,” Lylend said beside me. “What say you Pieter?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Pieter, whose behavior had calmed down considerably, which I assumed was due to Iolyt’s nymuriel stone, only grunted noncommittally.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, I knew you had it in you anyway,” Lylend countered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the ceremony finally began, it opened with a presentation of each kin-klan, according to their own traditional customs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Lobokians did air acrobatics, their spark spindling almost good enough to rival Lylend’s. Almost. The Gliptrainians entered like an earthquake, riding zyrkers. They reminded me of a cross between dwarves and cowboys at a rodeo, the way they whooped and hollered. Now I knew where Lev got it from. The Adolorians came in two parts. Queen Citrine led the sina as they swept into the aurethryn on a wave she’d conjured. Melusian and Mala’s brother Jet led a submerged procession of Rayves of all encrusted shapes and sizes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a very small showing of flame throwers—apparently formed by the combination of two covyns from the Glindune Desert. Their pyrotechnics display was mesmerizing, especially the part the salamanders played.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, there were the Rasilians. Being the host kin-klan, they were the best represented and, of course, the flashiest in their representation. The men arrived decked head to toe in diamond-encrusted armor, moving like blades in their formations. Each move was meticulous, and the adjoining procession of trolls that followed played on gong-like instruments. The women trailed in, wearing the most elaborate metallic costumes you can imagine. Think Vogue Fashion Week meets the Bedazzler and they’re both on steroids. Yet somehow it really worked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When all the kin-klans were in place, a low harmonic tone rang out through the aurethryn, and everyone grew silent.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"From our crystal cell, I watched in awe as a lone dryad, dressed in various shades of mossy green ceremonial garb, came to the center of the aurethryn and blew a horn shaped like a cornucopia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s this?” I asked, leaning over to Iolyt, who stood beside me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s the Emerald Honor,” she said, “to pay homage to the memory of the Emeridites—the Wood Weavers.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Not a sound could be heard as the horn blew again. It was then followed by the saddest melody I think I’ve ever heard. As I looked around the crystal cell and then out into the crowd, I saw tears streaming down people’s faces, each mourning the loss of Cobbogoth’s sixth and most peaceful kin-klan. When the dryad finished his homage, a moment of ceremonial silence passed for the Wood Weavers. Then the dryad left the floor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Next the procession of Callowynder contenders began. They were led by Prince Sydian—winner of the Wyn tournament—followed by Mala, who had won the regular tournament. The Callowynder contenders entered the aurethryn to thunderous applause. When they reached the center of the floor, Sydian raised a hand, quieting the crowd, and then signaled to a magistrate on the dais. The next thing we knew, the aurethryn ceiling changed focus, and Lev, who sat beside Zuli in a special crystal cell one level up from ours, came into focus. Their cell was surrounded by Rasilian guards, no doubt arranged and provided by DonTon in the event Seraphyna decided to return and finish what she’d started in the arena.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The crowd went absolutely berserk when they saw him. Lev still looked pale, but he was upright, and Zu looked as proud and radiant as ever. He grinned like a little boy, then slowly stood and waved to everyone. The cheering only got louder, and then the plausenite started.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The tribute lasted for a solid five minutes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the cheering finally died down, the contenders showed off their powers a bit and then gave their final bows and left. Except for Prince Sydian and Mala, of course, who were declared the official winners of their respective tournaments and took their places on the dais with DonTon, Princess Sylva, and the other royals and magistrates of the festival.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Cheers, once again, shook the dome.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then it was my turn. The Tableunia contestants entered in tiers of ten, from the top finalists all the way down. Each contestant was arrayed in a new, if not their finest, ceremonial shyntara. It was certainly their most exotic, which was meant to represent their kin-klan of origin or some other piece of their heritage. I was no exception. I’d tried to convince Zu not to bother with yet another shyntara.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re not being crowned in anything but the most fabulous shyntara my brains and Lylend’s moffyloth silk—and money—can create,” she’d said. Last night she’d presented me with the heavily beaded, green-tinged, pearly-white concoction I was wearing today. To be honest, I felt uncomfortably like a bride at a wedding. But Zuli said it was the crowning jewel of the “lunar-like” persona she had been carefully crafting for Lunalyn Opalinya all month. The embroidery and beadwork were above and beyond anything she had created so far. It even came with a matching headdress.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I walked alongside the ten finalists at the front, head high, legs only slightly shaking. I’d gotten a lot better in front of crowds, but one hundred thousand people were a lot more than twenty thousand. Needless to say, when we finally reached the golden chairs—more like thrones—that had somehow been conjured out of the center of the aurethryn floor, I was grateful to sit.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon stood from his much more elaborate throne then, with Princess Sylva on his arm instead of the queen. The crowd went feral when they saw the princess. Word had spread fast about Queen Jayda’s treachery. Golden plausenite, reserved entirely for the Rasilian royals, burst across the dome like golden fireworks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Princess Sylva waved at her subjects and then returned to her throne.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, DonTon positioned himself at the dais and raised his arms to quiet the crowd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good people of Rasili, honored guests of our brother and sister kin-klans. Callowynder warriors. Tableunia champions. All who have come to Rasili representing your kin-klans with skill, dignity, and heart at the Triolune Festival, I welcome you. The time has come to honor our champions and conclude the joint celebration of our customs, both varied and alike, before we part ways until the next Lunevergence.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then he turned toward the Tableunia contestants. “But before our final night of festivities can begin, we must first crown the winner of our Tableunia.” He turned, motioning Princess Sylva forward again. This time she was accompanied by a troll carrying a large crystal box in its hands.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The winner of this festival’s Tableunia and of the gorgeous gloriole of Rasil herself, is…” DonTon pulled out a crystal scroll and twisted it open. The crowd was so quiet, the tinkle of crystal could have been heard in the rafter seats.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn Opalinya! Siryn of the mysterious Flame Fleck Isles!” he finished.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"True, I’d been anticipating the win, but even still—one hundred thousand people, folks! For a split second, it didn’t compute. My brain just…glitched. I barely registered a commotion around me, until the girl beside me finally elbowed me in the ribs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s you,” she hissed. “Go up on the dais.” I looked up to see a smile pasted on her face while silver daggers shot at me from her eyes. Yeah, I hadn’t made a lot of friends—or any—among the other contestants. You could say I felt right at home in that regard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I forced myself to step forward, one foot in front of the other. Having never been in anything like this, I had no idea how this was supposed to play out. My only reference was the beauty pageants I’d seen on TV back in Earth’s realm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I reached the stage, a palace guard stepped forward to help me up the stairs. Princess Sylva met me next, glowing like a morning star, and before I knew it, she was placing Rasil’s gloriole within the headdress already on my head—the headdress Zuli had fashioned for this exact purpose. I could feel its weight immediately.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then a peculiar sensation of my features shifting began to take effect.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew the aurethryn was zoomed in on my face when I heard the entire crowd suck in its breath. Then murmurs of what I hoped were approval began to circle the arena.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ll be straight with you, though, reader. I avoided looking up at myself. I’d already gotten way too used to Iolyt’s beauty gems and what they did for my face. I wouldn’t be sorry when I left Rasili and the temptation to tweak my looks behind. It was getting way too addictive.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m very glad it’s you,” Princess Sylva said after securing the crown and kissing my cheek. “Syd’s so happy.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Her last comment should’ve given me a heads-up of what was coming, had it registered. But instead, I felt completely detached from my body as the crowd erupted around me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next thing I knew, Prince Sydian was offering me his hand and leading me back down the steps of the dais and into the center of the aurethryn. Music swelled from somewhere unseen—strings and elemental wind—and Sydian began leading me in a vaguely familiar dance before I knew what had happened.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Okay…a victory dance with the prince of Rasili made sense. I guess.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I realized what dance we were dancing. It was the Weaving Dance—the dance he’d taught me in Heartfire Hall during our “exclusive” banquet. Which seemed kind of weird. Why were we dancing the Weaving Dance here? Now? Wasn’t it specifically for weddings?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian led me through each step like a dream. Smooth, sure, and somehow…reverent? His hands never fumbled. His eyes were warm. Too warm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then he started talking.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"About the future. Our future.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And that’s when the alarm bells started clanging in my head.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I hope you’ll feel like you can revisit your islands whenever you like,” he was saying when I glanced toward the crystal cell where Iolyt and Lylend were watching. When I glanced up to Zuli’s and Lev’s cell, I was surprised to see they were both on their feet, frantically trying to get my attention. Zuli was miming something vaguely crown-shaped. Lev pointed at Sydian, then at me, then made a circle with his hands and mimed an explosion. It was not helpful.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Zuli’s last gesture—miming two fingers interlocking—clicked it all into place.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Prince Sydian was going to propose?! Here? Now? In front of all of Cobbogoth? We hardly knew each other!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian raised a hand, and the music faded. I’m not sure what I was expecting. Did they propose the same way in Cobbogoth as they did in the Olden Realm?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Turns out they did. Sort of. Sydian turned toward the crowd and took both of my hands in his.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Lunalyn Opalinya,” he said, loud enough to be picked up by whatever voice-amplifying stone he was using.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I swallowed as time stopped in the horror of it all. Out of the corner of my eye, a movement caught my attention. I looked toward it and found Karyk standing off to the side of the dais. He was watching us with an unreadable expression. I wanted to run to him and demand he take me to James immediately.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Instead, I looked down at the prince, still clutching my hands. “Will you?” he asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I-uh-what?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a smattering of good-natured laughter in the crowd, and Sydian grinned. “Will you weave your life with mine? Will you be my queen? Will you rule Rasili with me when the time comes, and let me love and honor you like Baryn of old loved his Vyn?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My lungs constricted, and I’m pretty sure my heart stopped for a few beats. I’d never been proposed to before, but this one wasn’t bad. I started to get lightheaded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Across the aurethryn, I caught the expressions of Iolyt, Lylend, and even Pieter. They all looked guilty. Which meant they had known a proposal was coming and hadn’t warned me. We were definitely having words after this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I…I’m so sorry, Sydian,” I finally managed, stepping back from the prince. “But I can’t.” A low gasp ran through the crowd.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The other Tableunia contestants erupted in excited whispers. Then DonTon was beside us, his intimidating form made only slightly less scary by the confusion in his expression. “You don’t want the gloriole, Lunalyn Opalinya?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, flustered. “Yeah, I do. Of course, your Metallican.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian raised an eyebrow hopefully. “Then you understand the arrangement?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Actually,” I swallowed. “I don’t think I do. I thought I did,” I said. “But maybe you’d better explain it to me in more detail.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"DonTon’s voice was solemn now. “In Rasilian tradition—it is an ancient and obscure tradition, mind—whenever there is a prince of marriageable age in line to rule, the winner of the Tableunia not only wins the gloriole,” he said, “but…the hand of the prince as well.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In that moment, reader, so many things suddenly made sense. The queen’s soireenya, when all the contestants and their mothers went nuts after Prince Sydian’s announcement. Prince Sydian cornering me in that small closet off the banquet hall, making a point of encouraging me to win. It was probably his only way of having some kind of say in the matter. Then, when it did look like I had a good chance, him seeking me out and trying to court me. Even Lylend’s insistence that allowing the prince to court me might increase my chances of winning the gloriole took on a new light. I had been royally duped—no pun intended—by people who had come to be like family to me. At least it was clear Lev and Zuli hadn’t been in on it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But why hadn’t anyone told me? Had they assumed I’d catch on soon enough—me who had never been on a date in my life!? Not a real one anyway. How was I supposed to recognize the signs?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Or were they just afraid I’d refuse to compete in the Tableunia if I knew? Well, they were right! I hadn’t known it in the beginning, but even setting sharing kinsyska with James aside, Sydian was far too nice a guy to lead someone on like this.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The ripple of confusion moving through the crowd was only making things worse. I turned back to Sydian. “Can we talk about this?” I asked. “In private?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He glanced at his father. DonTon nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Offering his arm, I took it, and he led me away from the dais. Music began playing back","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"in the aurethryn for the crowd. We bypassed Karyk, still standing there, watching everything expressionless. Then we retreated to a small tunnel behind a tapestry only a platinum piece of hearthurite could access.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry, Sydian!” I began. “I honestly had no idea.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No idea of what? That I loved you? Or that I meant to propose?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“All of the above. I-I sensed you liked me, sure,” I stammered, “but I had no idea about the Rasilian tradition.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But you know now,” he said. “Do you not find me pleasing?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian waited, the picture of sophisticated elegance. How did he seem completely unperturbed by this whole situation?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course you’re pleasing,” I said. “I mean, look at you. And you’re actually pretty cool, too, so there’s that bonus. But it’s just not that simple.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian smiled, but there was a question in it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sighed. “I think it’s time I told you something,” I said. The truth was the only thing that could clear up this misunderstanding.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Slowly, I reached up and removed the gloriole from my head, then unclasped Iolyt’s latest beauty gem necklace from around my throat. I could feel the “beauty” draining from my face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian’s eyes only widened slightly. His smile, on the other hand, widened a lot. “I had a feeling the rumors were true.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Rumors?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That you were a lune-kyndie!” he said with uncharacteristic enthusiasm. “You know, some believe Rasil herself was a lune-kyndie. I knew there was something special about you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s not all,” I huffed, somewhat exasperated. I pulled back the collar of my shyntara to reveal the Haven tattoo. “Do you recognize this?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This time the prince’s eyes not only widened but grew serious. “I know that mark, yes,” he said. “What does it mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I took a deep breath. “It means my real name isn’t Lunalyn Opalinya. It’s Norah. In ancient Cobbogothian it means—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Our final hope,” he breathed. “The Triolune prophesy. I’ve studied this.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry I lied to you—to everyone,” I said, “but I hope showing you this explains why I had to.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course,” he said. And amazingly he didn’t seem angry. “But what does all of this have to do with you refusing my proposal?” he continued. “Are you afraid I won’t be able to protect you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stared at him. “What? No…” After everything—seeing me, the real me—this prince still wanted to marry me?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then what is it?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I let out my breath. Time to bring out the big guns. “I-I already have a kinzurine,” I said as gently as I could. “And not just that, but we’re bonded. Kynsyska. I think that kinda makes it a done deal?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian closed his eyes. “Ah…now things are beginning to make sense.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry, Sydian,” I said again. “I really had no idea this was a marriage competition. I only entered the Tableunia in the first place because we need the gloriole.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The gloriole? Why?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“We believe it’s one of the keys to defeating Cifer.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He was quiet for a long time, his handsome face unreadable. “I suppose it would be futile to ask you to explain further?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I grimaced and nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I understand,” he said finally.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You do?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course, Luna—er—Norah. If there is one thing I understand, it is duty.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For what it’s worth,” I said, feeling like a complete jerk, “if I didn’t have a kinzurine, I would’ve been easily smitten by you—kinda was, if I’m being honest. And I am incredibly flattered you singled me out.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He smiled then. It wasn’t a dazzling smile but a humble, genuine smile. “I’m not sure if that makes this easier, but I do appreciate your objection isn’t personal.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No! Not in the least.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian reached up and touched my cheek. “Then may I at least kiss you goodbye, Norah?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Taken aback, I hesitated. If I was being honest, I did kind of want to know what it felt like to be kissed by a prince—well, this prince anyway. If only just once. Under these extremely unique circumstances, I was pretty sure James would understand.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So I nodded.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Prince Sydian smiled softly. He stepped forward, took my hands in his, and leaned in.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I closed my eyes, only to open them again when I felt his warm, full lips brush my cheek. Softly. Respectfully. Nothing like the kiss I’d feared after that banquet in Heartfire Hall.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When he pulled back, I smiled at him. “Thank you for understanding,” I whispered.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It is my honor,” he said in his velvety voice. “And I believe it’s best your true identity stays secret for as long as possible. I hope you trust me when I say it is safe with me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I do,” I said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"His smile was relieved. “Good. Put your necklace back on then, Lunalyn Opalinya. Time to return and face the cymborynths. And by the cymborynths, I mean my father.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I secured Iolyt’s necklace back around my throat but didn’t replace the gloriole. What was the point? I knew I’d have to return it anyway.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Shall we?” he asked, tucking my hand into the crook of his arm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, and we reentered the dome.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Wait!” I whispered, stopping him. “What will you say?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shrugged. “The truth.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But I thought—”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Trust me.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And right then I realized I did trust Prince Sydian. He’d proven he could be trusted, so I let him lead me to the dais. He left me standing by Princess Sylva, who took my hand in hers. Then he crossed to where his father now sat on his throne. The two leaned together, heads bent in whispered discussion for several moments.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I carefully handed the gloriole back to Princess Sylva and then took my place back with the other Tableunia contestants.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, with a nod from his father, Prince Sydian stood at the podium and raised his voice. “After interviewing Lunalyn Opalinya at length, I am convinced she was not familiar with Rasilian customs; therefore, she did not understand the full nature of the Tableunia. She was good enough to inform me that she had already entered into a kynzura bond with another. This occurred before she entered the Tableunia. She has therefore graciously acquiesced to relinquish her claim to the gloriole and my hand.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The crowd let out a collective sigh of disappointment, but thankfully it didn’t sound outraged. Just…resigned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I decided to avoid looking back at the crystal cell for as long as possible. I would suffer the Hood’s disappointment over losing the gloriole when we were so close later. I didn’t think I could do it right now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Unlike everyone else, the Tableunia contestants surrounding me only grew in excitement. Who would receive the gloriole now? And how were we going to convince them to give it to the Hood?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I watched as the prince returned to his place with the other Callowynder contenders. Then DonTon stood up to speak again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He raised his hands, quieting the crowd. “People of Cobbogoth!” he said. “This night is for the honoring of two winners, the Tableunia winner and the champion of the Callowynder tournament. We appear to no longer have a Tableunia winner. As you know, my son, Prince Sydian, is the champion of the Wyn Callowynder tournament. As such, he must name his prize. He has just done so, and his request is twofold. First, Lunalyn Opalinya, despite these recent developments, should remain the winner of the Tableunia, maintaining her claim to the gloriole, but be released from her claim on him. Second, that the prince may, when the time comes, have the freedom to choose his own queen and not have it chosen for him at the next Tableunia. In light of recent events, I see the wisdom in this request and will grant it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At this announcement, the crowd went wild.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My eyes met the prince’s. He winked at me. Then he bowed slightly, his hand on his heart, and smiled, a soft, wistful smile. The kind that says I wish things could’ve been different.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"For the next five minutes, the crowd chanted his name, peppering the aurethryn with golden plausenite the entire time. Prince Sydian was definitely one of the good ones.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the crowd died down, Princess Sylva motioned me back to the dais. The gloriole was placed back on my head. Then the crowd began chanting my name.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, DonTon raised his hands for quiet. “Before we close the Triolune Festival,” he said, “there is one final matter I would like to attend to. Unbeknownst to many of you, a great service has been rendered to this kin-klan—quietly, bravely, and without recognition—and to myself personally during the course of this festival. I would be remiss if I did not publicly honor those responsible.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The crowd fell into a murmur.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I would like to ask Lunalyn Opalinya to step forward once more and for Karyk of the Palace Guard to join us on the dais.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My head snapped up, nearly dislodging the gloriole. Karyk. I’d almost forgotten about him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I stepped forward. A few moments later, Karyk was standing on the other side of DonTon. He looked just as creepy as ever, like he’d been yanked off a pirate ship somewhere. But there was something different about him, too.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"A palace attendant stepped forward carrying a crystal box.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“These,” DonTon said, lifting the lid, “are honorary platinum hearthurite shards. The highest emblem of trust one can receive in Rasili. Forged from the converged metals of the Heartfire Hall by the trolls, they mark you both as trusted friends to Rasili.” He lifted one of the shards from the crystal box by a black velvety ribbon it was attached to.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He turned to me, carefully placing it over the gloriole on my head until the hearthurite rested against my ribs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How did the thassalyth shrooms work?” he mumbled quietly as he adjusted the shard.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Very well, your Metallican,” I said. “As you’ve already seen, Lev is alive and well.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Good. Good.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then he turned to Karyk.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“For you,” he said, “the same honor…and something more.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After he placed a second hearthurite shard over Karyk’s head, DonTon lifted a third cord out of the crystal box. I recognized it immediately as a fangledãor.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk took it, eyes unreadable. Then he bowed. “Thank you, your Metallican.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When the cheers of the crowd finally died down and the ceremony was over, Cobbogothians began filing out of the arena, heading for the lower streets of Rasili to celebrate.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before joining the others, I went looking for Karyk. He hadn’t gone far. I found him in a tunnel just off the aurethryn.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I could call to him, I watched as he opened the fangledãor and called into it. A few moments passed before a figure, tall and sturdy but a little gaunt and pale, stepped out.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Glynt,” Karyk said. “I’m sorry it took so long, old friend.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Do I know you?” the prisoner said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Karyk didn’t answer; he only smiled, then reached up and removed the vanity collar from around his neck.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The change wasn’t dramatic. Not right away. First his shoulders pulled back and broadened. His posture lifted. Then he shot up a good six inches, all the while, his looks improved…significantly. Why would he have worn a vanity collar to look worse?","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That’s when I saw his face rearrange from a creepy stranger into someone very familiar.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“James!” I gasped. Without thinking—without caring that I still wore the gloriole—I ran to him, throwing my arms around him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I waited for him to respond—for the familiar squeeze around my waist, my feet leaving the floor. For the months of aching in my chest to melt. But it never came.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I have been searching for you since that day at the Glorian Pass!” I said, blinking back several months’ worth of emotion.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James just stood there, stiff as a board.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I pulled back, looking up into his face. He’d changed a lot. His hair and beard were longer, still red, but now he had a few wyndaloos woven in. Like me, he also had a peculiar tattoo that scrawled out from under his shyntara on one side of his collarbone, just touching his neck. It was copper. He’d been completely “Cobbogothified,” as Zuli liked to call it. I’d expected it, of course.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"What I hadn’t expected was the startled look on his face.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s wrong?” I asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Hood members had found me and were beginning to gather in the tunnel now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Before I realized what had happened, James was holding me at arm’s length. Carefully. Gently. But firmly pushing me away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m sorry, Lunalyn,” he said, voice steady. “I know you said you knew me last night in the dogril cave, but I think you’ve got me mixed up with someone else, sweetheart.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The careful words, the gentle tone, and the guarded look in his eyes, it all hit like ice water.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What?” I laughed—too sharp. “Don’t mess around, James. It’s me. It’s Norah.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Kyndie,” Lylend said from behind me. “Perhaps we should…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But that’s when I remembered the gloriole and Iolyt’s necklace. Of course! He didn’t recognize me!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I removed them, handing them to Zuli. The glamour melted away.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“See, James,” I said. “It’s me. Norah. Your kinzurina.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He blinked. But there was still no recognition. “I’m sorry,” he said, shrugging apologetically. “I don’t know you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Jamus?” Lylend said, stepping forward.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James looked at his father for a long time—the same confusion he’d given me. But then recognition slowly dawned in his eyes. “Dad?” he said, as if he wasn’t certain but was at least willing to consider it.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James moved around me then, and father and son embraced.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James’ companion—Glynt, I think he’d said—stepped forward, placing a tentative but steadying hand on my shoulder. He looked at me with an unreadable expression, and I realized I recognized him.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I know you,” I said. “From the dungeon.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He nodded. “Yes.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What’s going on?” I asked. “Why doesn’t he recognize me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"He shook his head. “We don’t know. Something happened during his transformation. He remembers some things here and there, but not much from his former life. I was sent to keep an eye on him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Transformation? What do you mean? Will his memories come back?” I asked, swallowing the urge to puke for the second time since arriving in Rasili.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Glynt shrugged, regretful. “Perhaps. In time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tunnel vision hit hard and fast then.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nori?” I heard Zuli’s voice from far away. “Nori, are you all right?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Has she been poisoned again?” Iolyt asked.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I felt someone grab my elbow, steadying me—heard Sydian’s voice in my ear. “Follow me,” he said to all of us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But it wasn’t poison. It was shock. I knew it as certainly as I saw James looking at me—the same confusion in his eyes. Deep, stormy blue eyes—eyes with pale green starbursts around his pupils, the evidence of our kynsyska bond. It was still there, and yet he didn’t know me.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"James—my James—the only person who’d ever truly known me…just…didn’t anymore.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Perhaps he never would again.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The room was spinning now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I think I’m going to puke again,” I said to no one in particular.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Someone answered anyway, scooping me up into their arms.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Well, we can’t have that now, can we, Lunalyn?” Prince Sydian said gently. “Not when you’ve come so far since the last time.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to laugh in response, but it came out as a strangled sort of sob.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Sydian gave a pained groan. “Let’s get you out of here.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And he did—carrying me toward the tapestry that led to the very tunnel where I had just broken his heart.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was settled. Sydian was definitely one of the good ones.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Closing Shyntara","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"0525153f293b4b16a1a9c9d82da21125"},{"title":"Gloriole Craft","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"1f6b19573f1a41b198af02e72ed8c592"}]},{"n":72,"letterTitle":"Battle at the Unda Swamp","narrator":"Norah","pieces":[{"title":"Uncle Jack","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"9d10771006114c92b6c1ceb61bbbab82"},{"title":"Battle at the Unda Swamp (Part 1)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"I’ve seen Iolyt work a lot of miracles with crystals. I’ve been the recipient of those miracles a few times myself. But tonight, I didn’t need a miracle. I just needed sleep.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come see me when you’re done, libkyn,” Iolyt said, as Zuli helped me get out of the beautiful moffyloth silk shyntara I’d been wearing for three days now.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I’m so sorry, Zu,” I said again. I’d only just noticed how filthy it was.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please don’t,” she said, pausing to look at me. “It will clean perfectly. And even if it wouldn’t, Lylend has more moffyloth silk where that came from. I’m already planning your shyntara for your weaving ceremony when James gets his memories back.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You mean if he gets them back,” I said, completely numb—and if he still wanted me when he did. I really didn’t know what else to do. James not recognizing me felt like being trapped in a nightmare.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, I mean when he remembers,” Zuli said, leaning down and signaling for me to step out of the shyntara, then setting it aside.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She stood and pulled me toward her, wrapping her arms around me. “We’re all here for you, Nori. No one is going to stop until we help you both figure out whatever this is.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded my thanks, but I was too exhausted to think about any of it anymore. It had been seventy-two hours of back and forth between Lylend and me and James and Glynt, trying to see if we couldn’t jog some hint of a memory of me in James’ mind. In the end, Glynt cautioned us that pushing James too hard might have the reverse effect. So we stopped.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“To be honest,” I said to Zuli, “all I really want to do right now is sleep.” And maybe not wake up again, I added silently.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"After changing into a much simpler shyntara than I’d worn in over a month, I left my rodãor and went to find Iolyt, who’d promised me a few days of blissful unconsciousness.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I entered her lab, she took one look at me, and that was it. I burst into tears and cried my heart out for the first time since…well, I don’t actually remember ever crying like that.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Oh, libkyn,” she tutted. “Don’t do this to yourself. There’s still so much we don’t know about James’ condition. That should give us hope.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She was right. But she hadn’t seen what I had seen. Even after Prince Sydian led all of us to a place where we could discuss things—even after Lylend explained to James who I was and that we were kinzura for the gazillionth time, even after showing him the evidence of kynsyska in his eyes—James didn’t remember. And worst of all, he didn’t say anything. He gave no explanation, no reason for why he might not remember me. To be honest, it was like he didn’t even want to try.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then there was the most unbearable part—the look in his eyes. No warmth or love or twinkle from a shared memory or joke born of our entire nine years growing up across the street from each other. There was no recognition. Nothing. He was my last link to Uncle Jack and the Olden Realm—to my past. And it was gone.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Anything helpful from the past two days discussing the whole matter was said by Glynt, which wasn’t much.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“There were things that happened in the dungeon,” he said, pity in his eyes, “things even the elders were unable to anticipate.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Elders?” I asked. “What do you mean?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“The elders were the ones entrusted by the Gihara with Jamus’ care while he healed from his blood sum.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“But how could they not know what’s happened to his memory?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Then there was the most aggravating answer of all, the one I’d heard so many—too many—times: “There is much we don’t know about what happens to one who pays a blood sum.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Arrrggghhhh!!!!","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Other than telling Lylend and me that James’ memories of me had to come back in their own time, there was no other explanation.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt pulled me to my feet as I sobbed, helping me climb into a healing cot.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“That’s right, lie down,” she said, soothingly. “You’ve had quite the week—or month, really.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, Iolyt,” I corrected. “I’ve had quite the year.” Years, really, if I was considering everything that happened after Gram’s death—after I “broke.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You need a good long rest, then,” she soothed. “Everything will look better to you when the tincture wears off.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I doubted it, but in my current state, staying awake meant facing this new reality.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I was situated, Iolyt gave me a small cup of warm liquid.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Here, sip this,” she said, “slowly.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it?” I asked through a hiccup.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just a special tincture of my own. Nymuriel and tangecite and some thassalyth shroom paste. Oh, and a little fylyla to sweeten it for you. It will help you sleep uninterrupted for a few days. Some of your injuries from Seraphyna haven’t healed completely, and this is the least painful way to do that.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I tried to gulp down the hot liquid, wanting to be asleep as soon as possible, but burned my mouth.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Slowly, libkyn,” she said softly, taking the cup from me. “You only need a few sips. We do want to be able to wake you again.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Why?” I sniffed.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You don’t mean that,” she gently scolded as she helped me lie down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Sorry,” I mumbled. I looked around me. Iolyt’s lab was the opposite of how I felt. It was glowing with warm light from the sunstone sconces set in the corners. It smelled faintly of melted honeycomb and a cinnamon kind of spice and something akin to toasted marshmallows? (Sorry, that’s the closest description I could come up with.) Everything about Iolyt and her surroundings was soothing.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She fluffed a blanket over me. “You’ll stay here tonight,” she said. “I want to keep an eye on you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Is that Cobbogothian for ‘I don’t trust you to be by yourself tonight?’”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"She smiled. “It’s healer for ‘You’ve just had quite a shocking blow’, and someone needs to care for you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"This only brought more tears to my eyes, so I let Iolyt tuck the blanket around me—a motherly gesture that felt so foreign to me it ached. Then I turned my head to watch as she resumed working at her bench. I watched as she sorted stones and mineral powders by type, whispering over them like they were old friends she spoke to all the time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I knew the tincture was beginning to take effect when I felt like I was floating just above my body.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“How could he forget me, Iolyt?” I asked. My voice didn’t even sound like my own. “We did kynsyska, didn’t we? That was…seven months ago. I thought that meant something. He has a piece of my soul inside of his. I saw it in his eyes. How could he not recognize me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I squeezed my eyes shut as more tears slid down my cheeks.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt didn’t answer right away, just kept sorting, calm and quiet, like the question was too heavy to answer carelessly. “Perhaps he’s only protecting the part of your soul that’s inside him. Perhaps, because of all he has been through and how deeply he cares for you, his instincts took over somewhere along the way in order to protect your bit of soul from being harmed by it all. Cobbogothian souls are mysterious things, and so is the kynsyska ritual. You heard Totherma, not even she understands it completely.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was true. When I’d gone to see Totherma again after realizing James didn’t recognize me, she said just that…and to be patient.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So what you’re saying is James could be protecting my bit of soul so well that he doesn’t remember me?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“In a way, yes. Consider the possibility that the pain he was in while in this dungeon you speak of—the pain that you were feeling because of your bond to him—perhaps he subconsciously came to see himself as a danger to you.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“So he hid me from himself?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Or the memory of you, at least.” Iolyt shrugged. “Just as a Cobbogothian soul is a mysterious thing, a Cobbogothian mind and heart are equally so. All three are far more powerful than any of us can comprehend. Only the Gihara know.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was the best explanation anyone had given yet, if I’m being honest.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Think of all you know about James,” Iolyt continued. “Which seems more likely: that he would simply forget about you, or that he is protecting you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I rolled onto my back and stared at the ceiling. “James has been protecting me for as long as I can remember. Even when I didn’t know it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Then trust in what you know, libkyn,” she said firmly. “There is a lot of hope in it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was really beginning to surrender to the tea now. The effect settled over me like a weighted blanket. I felt the edges of the world blur.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But then something I remembered pulled me back.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I never told you,” I murmured. “Your stones. They helped so much…when we rescued the goldabungs. Solivayne…nymuriel…huldecite. Wouldn’t have made it out without them.” I let out a long yawn, then removed a cuff from my arm that I’d been meaning to give her. “You’re…a…genius,” I slurred, handing it to her.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt’s face softened. She returned to the cot and took the cuff.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“It’s beautiful,” she said. “Where did you have it made?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"My eyes drifted shut. “Nowhere. I made it.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“You made it?” She sounded surprised, which reminded me that in all the chaos of the closing ceremonies, I’d forgotten to tell everyone what I’d discovered since rescuing the goldabungs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I made it with my metal power,” I said, cracking one eye just to see her reaction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Your metal power?” Iolyt asked, brows raised. “And when did that manifest?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Princess Sylva.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Of course. When you saved each other’s lives from Seraphyna.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Just like Zu and me in the Starlyt Sea.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“And now you have your metal power.” I could hear the smile in Iolyt’s voice. “Well then, that’s some very good news indeed. Congratulations, libkyn. You’re a triowyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Thanks,” I said, closing my eye again. “I was hoping you’d agree to train me…when I wake up.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“I would be honored,” was all she said.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I was just settling back into the bliss of unconsciousness when Iolyt’s voice returned, pulling me back once more.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Nori?” she said, sounding curious.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Hmm?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Didn’t you tell me once that Lylend gave you a memory stone? One James made for you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I cracked my eye open again. “Oh yeah. Said it had memories…from the Olden Realm…never figured out how to use it though…”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Iolyt looked genuinely surprised. “He didn’t teach you?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I gave the tiniest shrug I could manage.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was silence for a while. Then Iolyt leaned down and brushed a light kiss across my forehead.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“When you wake,” she whispered, “I’ll show you how to use it, libkyn. Until James gets his memories back, you can cherish them for him.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I nodded, too tired to hope out loud but awake enough to let a little enter my heart. “Thanks…Iolyt. Lev’s right…you’re the best.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"There was a soft huff of laughter. “Go to sleep now, libkyn.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The last thing I remember was the quiet sound of Iolyt humming, low and steady—a Cobbogothian lullaby—as I surrendered my weary mind to sleep.","i":false}],"narrator":"Norah"},{"title":"Battle at the Unda Swamp (Part 2)","kind":"text","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"First of all, human, not all water nymphs are created equal. Oh, they’re all little she-devils at the best of times, but not all of them simply try and drown you or interrupt your nap by decorating you with Gihara blooms like the ones at Leborana falls. No. Some of them are dirty little swamp nymphs of the worst sort.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The deeper and darker the swamp, the more devious the nymphs. Why? Well, two reasons. First of all, no one chooses to live in a swamp. Not really. They’re usually forced to live there due to not having anywhere else to go. Like Queen Citrine and her people. But even she stays on the very edge of the swamp with the Glindian sea as a means of occasional reprieve from the suffocating marsh. That, and the Unda trees down by the shore have salt water in their root systems, which Sina need to survive—especially those infected with Lamianza.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But the deeper you go into the Unda swamp, the more stagnant the water gets, and that becomes the perfect gathering place for disgruntled and banished water nymphs. Only the ones who’ve been cast off by their own or their Cobbogothian masters end up in the swamp. And the only reason to get cast off by a Cobbogothian master is if there is no hope for that nymph to evolve into a Cobbogothian in their next life. You remember the stages of elemie evolution, don’t you? If not, check your journal page on them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In short, to put it in terms your puny human mind can understand, the Unda swamp is like the wild west of water nymphs.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The truth is, nothing ever happens in a swamp. And because of that, there are plenty of opportunities for these feral water demons to ponder and practice their devious ways.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"All of which, of course, Brawlo and I became targets for when we got a little turned around in the swamp on our recent and painfully prolonged visit to the phoenix’s Unda tree. Interestingly, a battle with mangey swamp nymphs wasn’t all that delayed our return to Rasili.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"You see, it all began when Brawlo got distracted by a moffyloth. Think of a mix between an eagle-sized lunar moth and an axolotl, but with feathered ears, luminescent downy wings, and an enchanting humming noise that completely hypnotizes rodents of all sizes. Hence, Brawlo’s distraction.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I should’ve been on the lookout for the elusive creatures, what with the Triolune happening above, but more pressing matters than moffyloth cycles, such as Lev’s condition, had me preoccupied. Regardless, by the time I resurfaced from my latest dive into the phoenix’s Unda tree roots, Brawlo, whom I’d left napping at the base of the tree, was nowhere to be found.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Thankfully, he left giant—if erratic—paw prints in the mud for me to track. Unfortunately, said paw prints led me deep into the swamp.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It took me two days of very careful {and quiet} tracking before I finally found the old kynd. He was dead still on his paws, waiting patiently at the base of an Unda tree on the edge of the river troll that splits the Unda swamp from the forest of Dag.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"When I got close enough, I could see why. The moffyloth had spun itself into its luminescent cocoon high above the swamp. Brawlo was waiting for her to wake up again. Since she hummed while she slept, however, he was trapped until either she woke, or I figured out some way to block the sound.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was easy enough. I simply gathered some moss from the base of the tree and used it to plug up the poor kynd’s ears.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"THANK LYLEND. VERY GRATEFUL. SO TIRED.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"“You’re welcome, old kynd. Now let’s get out of here, shall we? We’ve already missed Norah’s last round of the Tableunia, and I’m anxious to hear what’s happened.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"But Brawlo wouldn’t move.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“What is it, friend?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"MOFFYLOTH SILK. VERY RARE. EXTREMELY VALUABLE. PRETTY LIKE NORI.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"I squinted up at the moffyloth cocoon, and sure enough, it was situated just above a nest of several discarded cocoons from the elusive creatures’ previous cycles.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo immediately pushed thoughts of the kyndie and her many shyntaras for the Tableunia into my mind. Finally, an image of a triumphant Norah at the Triolune closing ceremonies being crowned with Rasil’s gorgeous gloriole in a shyntara made entirely of glowing moffyloth silk came into my mind.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I sighed. “You want me to climb and retrieve the silk for Norah?”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"AND PRINCESS ZULI. MAKING NEW SHYNTARA IS HAPPINESS. NOT THINK OF INJURED LEV AS MUCH.","i":true},{"t":"p","x":"Well, he wasn’t wrong. Making new shyntaras for Norah seemed to give the princess an inexplicable amount of joy. To the detriment of my retirement nest egg. Not that I was complaining. After ten thousand years, said nest egg was enormous.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"So, human, for the sake of the kyndies, I roused these tired old bones and began to climb the Unda tree. It was a very high Unda tree, by the way.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It wasn’t until I’d reached the moffyloth nest, retrieved the discarded cocoons, and safely stored them in my rodãor that I realized we were under attack.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The Unda tree was so high and the swamp so muffled that I only just made out Brawlo’s hissing and trilling below when I was halfway down.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then, dear human, a three-day battle with cursed swamp nymphs ensued. By the third day, I was even forced to enlist a very disgruntled Shudder to our cause.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I’ll spare you the fiendish details of the torture those little gremlins extracted upon us. Let’s just say I spent an awful lot of time regrowing Brawlo’s fur with a special crystal cocktail I’ve been using on myself for millennia.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"In the end, however, neither side was the victor. In fact, it was a third party altogether—one that blasted very unexpectedly right through our battle scene—that finally brought the fighting to an end.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"At first, it looked like a comet had fallen from the sky, blazing a path right through the swamp and into a bog just beyond our current battlefield. The impact caused an explosion so great, it left a steaming crater in the swamp floor. The nymphs scattered every which way, of course—little cowards—and Brawlo and I were knocked completely off our feet.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Come on, kynd,” I said, once the shock of it wore off. I mounted Brawlo, pulling ourselves out of the mire of the swamp, and we started for the crater. We were both so filthy with mud and moss and the stench of stale bog that we blended into our surroundings beautifully. If it was someone rather than something that had caused the crater, they wouldn’t notice us.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The closer we got to the crater, however, the harder it was to see anything. The steam from the impact clung to the swamp in a thick fog, but we could hear a monumental battle raging as we reached the edge of the crater.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"I quickly dismounted. Brawlo and I got low, inching our way forward. Suddenly, a blast of blue fire erupted from the crater, devouring the fog, and revealing all.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Two creatures, one as black as the mud of the swamp, the other in stark contrast, with feathers of bright crimson and gold, were locked in a battle so fierce it could only end in death. As a military man all my very long life, I could see that they were exquisite in their technique and astonishingly expert in nearly all six elements.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Which is why I knew who they both were in an instant: Seraphyna—nothing but a Náfenaeri could fight with such explosive abandon—and, though I could hardly believe my eyes, Barakyne. The legend himself, locked in battle with an equally elusive creature.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo and I watched, entranced as the fighting raged for several more minutes, Barakyne gaining the upper hand each time.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*SHOULD HELP?* Brawlo asked, leaning into my shoulder.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“No, kynd. We’d only be in his way.”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"It was true. The longer they fought, the more obvious it became that Barakyne was the master in this battle. His ability to control his powers was unlike anything I’d ever seen.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just when I was certain he was about to finish Seraphyna for good, however, something inexplicable happened. The Náfenaeri let out a screech that sent ripples over the water around us. In an instant, her wings and feathers were gone, and Seraphyna the woman cowered before the great black beast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"“Please!” She panted. “Please!”","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then she did something very unexpected. Turning her head away, she slowly reached out her hand to the black beast.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Barakyne circled majestically, caution in every step. But then, in all his noble power, he finally stepped forward, pressing his feathered forehead into her palm.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*DANGER!* Brawlo’s alarm and dread bled through my body, matching my own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Several seconds passed as we sat frozen, watching the two, woman and beast, locked in that position. To say every nerve in my body was about to snap is an understatement. But still, neither moved. Some sort of communication seemed to be passing between them.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Finally, just as strangely as it had started, Barakyne stepped back, pawing the ground with a mighty snort. Seraphyna stood, resumed her Náfenaeri form, and with another ear-shattering screech, shot up from the crater and into the sky.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brawlo and I sat motionless, completely stunned.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"The next moment, Barakyne turned his head in our direction. His strange eyes—eyes I’d only ever seen once before—locked on my own.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"*Go*, a voice said in my mind. *Tell no one what you’ve seen here.*","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"And then he vanished into the fog of the swamp.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"That, human, is when I realized where I’d seen eyes like his before. It was back in the Olden realm—in the crystal city. Barakyne had Qualdrine eyes.","i":false},{"t":"p","x":"Just like the Gihara.","i":false}],"narrator":"Lylend"},{"title":"Journal: Moffyloth","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"4dae79b29e354d1b9caec929d938dbc8"},{"title":"Spy Note","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"a30f2a9d98544cd4ba33868a01496867"},{"title":"Thank you Card","kind":"webp","pages":2,"id":"2cbf409cf88748edbf0f5aec76818e42"}]}]}]};
